window.LEARN_DATA = {"config":{"label":"Final","key":"fin-learn-v1","map":"Final Map.html","test":"Final Mastery Loop.dc.html","drugs":"Exam 3 Drug Cards.dc.html","masteryKey":"fin-mastery-v1","scope":"Provisional final-topic path: authored notes for chapters 13–17, 22–25, 45–53, 55–56. This includes the Exam 3 lessons and respiratory review. The syllabus calls the final comprehensive; no detailed final blueprint has been provided. Use the earlier-exam links for the rest of the cumulative review.","gaps":"The Final Map also lists chapters 1, 2, 4, 7, 8, 10, 11 and 12 as planned foundations. Their full chapter notes are not present, so this path does not claim those chapters are covered.","weightNote":"Equal chapter balancing for this provisional path; these are not professor exam-question weights.","page":"Final Learn.html","chapters":["13","14","15","16","17","22","23","24","25","45","46","47","48","49","50","51","52","53","55","56"]},"weights":{"13":1,"14":1,"15":1,"16":1,"17":1,"22":1,"23":1,"24":1,"25":1,"45":1,"46":1,"47":1,"48":1,"49":1,"50":1,"51":1,"52":1,"53":1,"55":1,"56":1},"lessons":[{"id":"fluid-balance","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Fluid balance: compartments, volume and safe replacement","notes":["13-overview","13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","13-rehydrating-safely","13-fluid-overload","13-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why sodium and potassium matter so much: Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium; K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in; 4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change","Heart and vessels: HR ↑, pulses weak, thready; BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand); Flat neck/hand veins even supine","Safety first: critical rescue: Assess q2h for pulmonary edema; Bounding pulse, ↑JVD, crackles, ↑edema, ↓urine → call","Why: Protected volumes: plasma (BP) and cells; Interstitial space = buffer; Judge hydration by perfusion signs + weight, not edema alone"],"checks":["Explain why sodium and potassium matter so much in Fluid balance.","Explain heart and vessels in Fluid balance.","Explain safety first: critical rescue in Fluid balance."],"recallAnswers":["Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium; K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in; 4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change","HR ↑, pulses weak, thready; BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand); Flat neck/hand veins even supine","Assess q2h for pulmonary edema; Bounding pulse, ↑JVD, crackles, ↑edema, ↓urine → call"],"requires":[],"terms":["hypovolemia","hypervolemia","isotonic","osmosis"],"questions":[{"id":"13|trad|1","sourceId":"13|c|1","q":"A nurse is caring for four clients being treated for dehydration. Which client is at greatest risk for a fall?","o":["BP 126/78 lying and 100/60 standing; says the room spins on standing","Urine specific gravity 1.028","Mucous membranes dry; asks for ice chips","Weight down 0.5 kg since admission; alert and steady on the feet"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Orthostatic hypotension with symptoms — standing drops the BP enough to cause a fall.","Incorrect. Concentrated urine, but no direct fall risk.","Incorrect. A comfort and hydration need.","Incorrect. Steady and alert with a small loss."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Low plasma volume → blood pools in the legs on standing → BP falls → the brain is briefly underperfused → dizziness and falls.","rule":"Dehydration: check orthostatic BP before walking; fall precautions; rise slowly; sit at once if lightheaded."},{"id":"13|trad|5","sourceId":"13|c|5","q":"For which client should the nurse question a prescription for IV 0.45% sodium chloride?","o":["A client with a head injury and rising intracranial pressure","A client with mild hypernatremia from water loss","A client with cellular dehydration","A client with no swallowing problem who is drinking well"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Hypotonic fluid moves water into cells — in the brain that worsens swelling.","Incorrect. Replacing water fits water-loss hypernatremia.","Incorrect. Hypotonic fluid is used for cellular dehydration.","Incorrect. No contraindication is described."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hypotonic fluid makes the ECF more dilute than the cells → water enters cells → brain cells swell inside a closed skull → ICP rises further.","rule":"Hypotonic fluids (0.45% NaCl): rehydrate cells; avoid with brain swelling or a low BP."},{"id":"13|trad|6","sourceId":"13|c|6","q":"Which instruction should the nurse give the assistive personnel (AP) caring for an older adult at risk for dehydration?","o":["Offer 60–120 mL of a fluid the client likes every hour and record the exact amount","Limit fluids after 1800 so the client isn’t incontinent overnight","Offer coffee often because the client enjoys it","Estimate the client’s intake at the end of the shift"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Small, regular amounts of preferred fluids, measured exactly.","Incorrect. Never withhold fluids to prevent incontinence.","Incorrect. Caffeine increases fluid loss.","Incorrect. Intake must be measured, not estimated."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Older adults feel less thirst and their kidneys conserve water poorly → a steady scheduled intake keeps plasma volume up without depending on thirst.","rule":"Teach AP: offer 60–120 mL hourly, preferred fluids within diet limits, exact recording, never withhold fluids for incontinence."},{"id":"13|trad|11","sourceId":"13|c|11","q":"A client without kidney disease has had a urine output of 350 mL in the last 24 hours. Why is this a concern?","o":["It is below the obligatory output of 400–600 mL needed to clear the body’s wastes","It shows the kidneys are concentrating urine normally","Insensible water loss will make up the difference","It matters only if the client says they are thirsty"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Below the obligatory output, wastes, acid and electrolytes build up to dangerous levels.","Incorrect. Concentrating is normal, but this volume is too low to clear wastes.","Incorrect. Insensible loss is water lost, not wastes cleared.","Incorrect. Thirst is unreliable, and the low output is a concern either way."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The kidneys must excrete a daily load of wastes and electrolytes → they need at least 400–600 mL of urine to carry it → below that, toxins, acid and potassium accumulate.","rule":"Obligatory urine output = 400–600 mL a day; in dehydration, under 500 mL a day (without kidney disease) is a concern — report it."},{"id":"13|trad|12","sourceId":"13|c|12","q":"Which client has the greatest increase in insensible water loss?","o":["A client with a fever of 102.6 °F (39.2 °C) who is breathing 28 times a minute","A client recovering from knee surgery with a temperature of 98.8 °F","A client on bed rest with an indwelling urinary catheter","A client who drinks 2 L of water a day"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fever and fast breathing both raise water lost through the skin and lungs — each 1 °C of fever costs at least 500 mL more.","Incorrect. Normal temperature; no added loss described.","Incorrect. Urine is measured, not insensible.","Incorrect. Intake, not insensible loss."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Insensible loss through the skin and lungs is uncontrolled (500–1000 mL a day) → it rises with fever, fast breathing, burns, trauma, thyroid crisis, mechanical ventilation and GI suction.","rule":"Insensible loss: 500–1000 mL a day, more with fever (≥ 500 mL per 1 °C), fast breathing, burns, ventilation and suction — add it when planning intake."},{"id":"13|trad|13","sourceId":"13|c|13","q":"A client starting lisinopril asks how it lowers blood pressure. Which explanation is accurate?","o":["“It lowers the amount of angiotensin II, so your vessels relax and your kidneys keep less sodium and water.”","“It makes your kidneys keep more sodium.”","“It blocks ADH, so you make more urine.”","“It works by replacing aldosterone.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. ACE inhibitors make less angiotensin II: less vasoconstriction, less aldosterone, more sodium and water excreted.","Incorrect. The opposite.","Incorrect. ADH isn’t its target.","Incorrect. It lowers aldosterone."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"ACE converts angiotensin I to angiotensin II → blocking it means less vasoconstriction and less aldosterone → the vessels relax and the kidneys excrete more sodium and water → BP falls (and potassium can rise).","rule":"ACE inhibitors (-pril) make less angiotensin II; ARBs (-sartan) block its receptors; both lower BP and can raise potassium."},{"id":"13|trad|14","sourceId":"13|c|14","q":"A client is receiving 3% sodium chloride. Which fluid shift does the nurse expect?","o":["Water moves out of the cells and the interstitial space into the blood vessels","Water moves from the blood vessels into the cells","Sodium moves into the cells while the water stays in the vessels","No shift — the solution stays where it is infused"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Hypertonic fluid makes the plasma more concentrated, so water follows by osmosis into the vessels: plasma volume expands while the cells and tissues shrink.","Incorrect. That is what a hypotonic fluid does.","Incorrect. Sodium stays mostly outside the cells; water moves, not sodium.","Incorrect. That describes an isotonic fluid (and even it spreads through the ECF)."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hypertonic saline raises plasma osmolarity above 300 mOsm/L → water moves by osmosis from the interstitial space and the cells into the blood → plasma volume rises and swollen cells shrink.","rule":"Hypertonic (3%, 5% NaCl): water out of cells into vessels — small volumes on a controller. Hypotonic (0.45%): water into cells. Isotonic (0.9%, LR): stays in the ECF."}],"studio":"fluid-balance"},{"id":"potassium","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Potassium: cardiac risk before a reassuring appearance","notes":["13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it matters: Low K⁺ → cells less responsive; Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic; Kills via heart + breathing muscles","Heart and ECG: Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic; ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave","Safety: Gait belt, fall precautions; Severe: breathing hourly (SpO₂, cough, ABGs)","Why: Insulin pulls K⁺ into cells fast; Insulin drips → check K⁺ often"],"checks":["Explain why it matters in Potassium.","Explain heart and ecg in Potassium.","Explain safety in Potassium."],"recallAnswers":["Low K⁺ → cells less responsive; Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic; Kills via heart + breathing muscles","Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic; ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave","Gait belt, fall precautions; Severe: breathing hourly (SpO₂, cough, ABGs)"],"requires":[],"terms":["hypokalemia","hyperkalemia","potassium chloride"],"questions":[{"id":"13|trad|3","sourceId":"13|c|3","q":"A client’s serum potassium is 2.7 mEq/L. Which assessment is the priority?","o":["Rate, depth and effort of breathing","Bowel sounds","Hand-grip strength","Capillary refill"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Respiratory muscle weakness and dysrhythmias are the main causes of death in hypokalemia.","Incorrect. Hypoactive sounds are expected; not the life threat.","Incorrect. Weakness matters, but breathing comes first.","Incorrect. A perfusion check, not the life threat here."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Low potassium makes skeletal muscle cells less responsive → the diaphragm and chest muscles weaken → shallow breathing → hypoxemia and CO₂ retention.","rule":"Hypokalemia: breathing first (at least every 2 hours; hourly if severe — SpO₂, cough strength, ABGs), then the heart (cardiac monitor)."},{"id":"13|trad|9","sourceId":"13|c|9","q":"The nurse finds swelling and coolness at a client’s IV site where potassium chloride is infusing; the client says it burns. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Stop the infusion","Apply a warm compress","Slow the rate to 5 mEq/hr","Flush the catheter to check that it’s patent"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Stop the potassium reaching the tissue; then remove the catheter, notify the provider, and document with a photograph.","Incorrect. Not before the infusion is stopped.","Incorrect. Any potassium leaking into tissue causes damage.","Incorrect. Flushing pushes more fluid into the tissue."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Potassium leaking into tissue is caustic → it irritates and kills cells → every minute of infusion adds damage.","rule":"Potassium infiltration: stop the infusion, remove the catheter, notify the provider, document with a description and photograph."},{"id":"13|trad|10","sourceId":"13|c|10","q":"The nurse teaches a client about oral potassium chloride. Which statement shows the client understands?","o":["“I’ll take it with breakfast.”","“I’ll take it on an empty stomach at bedtime.”","“I can use a salt substitute instead of the pills.”","“I’ll crush the extended-release tablet into juice.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Taking it with or after a meal reduces its bad taste and nausea.","Incorrect. On an empty stomach it causes nausea and GI irritation.","Incorrect. A salt substitute is not a measured, prescribed dose.","Incorrect. Crushing an extended-release tablet releases the dose at once and irritates the gut."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Concentrated potassium irritates the stomach lining and tastes bitter → food buffers it.","rule":"Oral potassium: with or after a meal; don’t crush extended-release forms; foods prevent further loss, supplements correct it."}],"studio":"potassium"},{"id":"sodium-calcium-magnesium","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Sodium, calcium and magnesium: brain, muscles and airway","notes":["13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it causes problems: ↓ Excitability (slower depolarization); Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema","Brain and muscles: Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma; Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs","Treatment: Deficit: IV saline; severe: 3% saline, small, controller; Excess: conivaptan, tolvaptan; SIADH: lithium, demeclocycline; Mild: ↑ salt, ↓ fluid; check hourly","Why: Symptoms = brain swelling inside the skull; Correct slowly, on a controller"],"checks":["Explain why it causes problems in Sodium, calcium and magnesium.","Explain brain and muscles in Sodium, calcium and magnesium.","Explain treatment in Sodium, calcium and magnesium."],"recallAnswers":["↓ Excitability (slower depolarization); Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema","Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma; Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs","Deficit: IV saline; severe: 3% saline, small, controller; Excess: conivaptan, tolvaptan; SIADH: lithium, demeclocycline; Mild: ↑ salt, ↓ fluid; check hourly"],"requires":[],"terms":["hyponatremia","hypernatremia","hypocalcemia","hypermagnesemia"],"questions":[{"id":"13|trad|7","sourceId":"13|c|7","q":"A client with cancer has a serum calcium of 13.2 mg/dL. Which prescription should the nurse question?","o":["Lactated Ringer’s at 125 mL/hr","IV 0.9% sodium chloride","Furosemide","Calcitonin"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Lactated Ringer’s contains calcium.","Incorrect. Saline helps the kidneys excrete calcium.","Incorrect. A loop diuretic increases calcium excretion (it replaces a thiazide).","Incorrect. Calcitonin keeps calcium in bone."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Cancer can release calcium from bone → the level rises → treatment stops every calcium source, dilutes and excretes it (saline, furosemide) and holds it in bone (calcitonin, bisphosphonates).","rule":"Hypercalcemia: stop calcium (LR, antacids, vitamin D), IV 0.9% saline, furosemide instead of thiazides, calcitonin or bisphosphonates, cardiac monitor."},{"id":"13|trad|8","sourceId":"13|c|8","q":"How does the nurse check a client for Trousseau sign?","o":["Inflate a BP cuff above the systolic pressure and watch the hand for 1–4 minutes","Tap the face just in front of the ear and watch for twitching","Tap below the kneecap and watch the leg","Ask the client to squeeze two of the nurse’s fingers"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A positive sign is a spasm of the hand into palmar flexion.","Incorrect. That is the Chvostek sign.","Incorrect. That tests the patellar reflex.","Incorrect. That tests grip strength."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The inflated cuff makes the arm’s nerves briefly short of oxygen → with low calcium they fire on their own → the hand muscles spasm.","rule":"Trousseau: cuff above systolic for 1–4 min → carpal spasm. Chvostek: tap in front of the ear → facial twitch. Both suggest hypocalcemia (or hypomagnesemia)."},{"id":"13|trad|15","sourceId":"13|c|15","q":"A client with a sodium of 126 mEq/L says their legs feel weak. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Check how effectively the client is breathing","Offer a high-sodium snack","Help the client walk to see how weak the legs are","Recheck the sodium level in the morning"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. When a client with hyponatremia has muscle weakness, check breathing at once — the respiratory muscles may be weakening too.","Incorrect. Diet may help mild hyponatremia later; it isn’t the first action.","Incorrect. Unsafe — weakness raises the fall risk.","Incorrect. Waiting ignores a possible breathing problem now."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Low sodium slows depolarization → muscles respond less → weakness can reach the diaphragm and chest muscles → ventilation fails.","rule":"Hyponatremia with muscle weakness: check respiratory effectiveness immediately (the book’s action alert); then neuro checks, cause-based treatment and safety."}],"studio":"sodium-calcium-magnesium"},{"id":"abg","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Read an ABG as a relationship","notes":["14-overview","14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","14-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days; Metabolic problem → lungs compensate; Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)","History: COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses; Opioids/sedation; Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin","Priority problems: Respiratory acidosis: reduced gas exchange; Acidosis: dysrhythmias (K⁺), falls; Alkalosis: injury (falls, tetany, seizures), weak breathing","Why: pH = result; PaCO₂ = lungs; HCO₃⁻ = kidneys; The value that matches the pH is the cause"],"checks":["Explain why in Read an ABG as a relationship.","Explain history in Read an ABG as a relationship.","Explain priority problems in Read an ABG as a relationship."],"recallAnswers":["Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days; Metabolic problem → lungs compensate; Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)","COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses; Opioids/sedation; Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin","Respiratory acidosis: reduced gas exchange; Acidosis: dysrhythmias (K⁺), falls; Alkalosis: injury (falls, tetany, seizures), weak breathing"],"requires":[],"terms":["acidosis","alkalosis","bicarbonate"],"questions":[{"id":"14|trad|4","sourceId":"14|c|4","q":"A client with diabetic ketoacidosis is breathing deeply and rapidly. What does this breathing pattern most likely mean?","o":["The lungs are compensating by blowing off CO₂","The client has developed respiratory acidosis","Anxiety that calls for a sedative","Hypoventilation from muscle weakness"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Kussmaul respirations: the brain drives breathing to remove acid as CO₂.","Incorrect. Respiratory acidosis is CO₂ retention; Kussmaul breathing lowers CO₂.","Incorrect. It isn’t voluntary and shouldn’t be sedated.","Incorrect. Breathing is deep and fast — the opposite."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Ketoacids raise H⁺ → central chemoreceptors are stimulated → deep, rapid breaths blow off CO₂ → pH rises toward normal.","rule":"Kussmaul breathing = metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation; treat the cause (insulin, fluids for DKA)."},{"id":"14|trad|7","sourceId":"14|c|7","q":"Which laboratory finding does the nurse expect in a client with acute acidosis?","o":["Potassium 5.8 mEq/L","Potassium 3.0 mEq/L","Calcium 11.5 mg/dL","Sodium 150 mEq/L"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. H⁺ enters cells and K⁺ leaves, raising the blood level.","Incorrect. Low potassium goes with alkalosis.","Incorrect. Not a feature of acidosis.","Incorrect. Not a feature of acidosis."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"To keep cells electrically neutral, every H⁺ that moves in is matched by a K⁺ moving out.","rule":"Acidosis → hyperkalemia; alkalosis → hypokalemia. Monitor potassium in every patient with acidosis."}],"studio":"abg"},{"id":"acidosis","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Acidosis: ventilation, metabolism and the underlying cause","notes":["14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","14-recognizing-acidosis","14-caring-for-acidosis"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Acid made or kept?; Bicarbonate not made or lost?","Signs it is working: pH >7.2 → toward 7.35; PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission; PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission","Treat the cause: Fluids; Insulin for DKA; antidiarrheals for diarrhea","Why#2: All roads: CO₂ in > CO₂ out; Fix = better ventilation, not a pH drug"],"checks":["Explain why in Acidosis.","Explain signs it is working in Acidosis.","Explain treat the cause in Acidosis."],"recallAnswers":["Acid made or kept?; Bicarbonate not made or lost?","pH >7.2 → toward 7.35; PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission; PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission","Fluids; Insulin for DKA; antidiarrheals for diarrhea"],"requires":[],"terms":["respiratory acidosis","metabolic acidosis"],"questions":[{"id":"14|trad|2","sourceId":"14|c|2","q":"A client with kidney failure is admitted with metabolic acidosis. Which assessment should the nurse perform first?","o":["Heart rate, rhythm and blood pressure","Skin color and temperature","Bowel sounds","Orientation to time and place"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Acidosis brings hyperkalemia, which can cause cardiac arrest — the cardiovascular system comes first.","Incorrect. Useful but not first.","Incorrect. Not the life threat.","Incorrect. Mental status matters, but the heart comes first."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"H⁺ enters cells and K⁺ leaves; failing kidneys can’t excrete it → potassium climbs → conduction slows → bradycardia, heart block, arrest.","rule":"Any patient with or at risk for acidosis: assess the cardiovascular system first; report cardiac changes immediately; monitor potassium."},{"id":"14|trad|3","sourceId":"14|c|3","q":"For which client with metabolic acidosis would the nurse expect a prescription for IV sodium bicarbonate?","o":["pH 7.15 with HCO₃⁻ 10 mEq/L","pH 7.33 with HCO₃⁻ 20 mEq/L","pH 7.36 with HCO₃⁻ 19 mEq/L and PaCO₂ 34","pH 7.48 with HCO₃⁻ 31 mEq/L"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Bicarbonate is given when HCO₃⁻ is low and the pH is under 7.2.","Incorrect. Mild; treat the cause.","Incorrect. The pH is in range — compensated.","Incorrect. That is alkalosis; bicarbonate would worsen it."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Below a pH of about 7.2, enzymes, hormones and heart muscle work poorly and buffering can’t keep up → added base buys time while the cause is treated.","rule":"IV bicarbonate: HCO₃⁻ low and pH < 7.2, kidneys unable to compensate, or rhabdomyolysis not responding to fluids. Otherwise treat the cause."},{"id":"14|trad|6","sourceId":"14|c|6","q":"A client was admitted with respiratory acidosis: pH 7.24, PaCO₂ 70 mm Hg, PaO₂ 55 mm Hg. Which later result shows the treatment is working?","o":["pH 7.31, PaCO₂ 54, PaO₂ 66","pH 7.22, PaCO₂ 72, PaO₂ 58","pH 7.24, PaCO₂ 70, PaO₂ 75","pH 7.18, PaCO₂ 78, PaO₂ 60"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. pH above 7.2 and rising, PaO₂ at least 10 higher and PaCO₂ at least 15 lower than on admission.","Incorrect. Slightly worse.","Incorrect. Better oxygen, but the CO₂ and pH haven’t moved — ventilation hasn’t improved.","Incorrect. Worsening."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Better ventilation clears CO₂ (PaCO₂ falls), which raises the pH, and improves oxygen uptake (PaO₂ rises).","rule":"Respiratory acidosis improving: pH > 7.2 and toward 7.35; PaO₂ > 90 or ≥ 10 above admission; PaCO₂ < 45 or ≥ 15 below admission."}],"studio":"acidosis"},{"id":"alkalosis","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Alkalosis: CO₂ loss versus acid loss","notes":["14-alkalosis"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Usually taken in or lost out; Often the nurse can stop the cause","Heart and breathing: ↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP; Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity; Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)","Respiratory — CO₂ blown off: Anxiety/fear hyperventilation; ventilator settings; Fever, brain lesions, salicylates, altitude; Gas: pH ↑ + PaCO₂ ↓","Why#2: Looks like low Ca²⁺ + low K⁺ together; Nerves overfire, muscles weaken"],"checks":["Explain why in Alkalosis.","Explain heart and breathing in Alkalosis.","Explain respiratory — co₂ blown off in Alkalosis."],"recallAnswers":["Usually taken in or lost out; Often the nurse can stop the cause","↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP; Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity; Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)","Anxiety/fear hyperventilation; ventilator settings; Fever, brain lesions, salicylates, altitude; Gas: pH ↑ + PaCO₂ ↓"],"requires":[],"terms":["respiratory alkalosis","metabolic alkalosis"],"questions":[{"id":"14|trad|5","sourceId":"14|c|5","q":"A client is breathing fast after hearing bad news and says the fingers tingle. SpO₂ 99%; ABG pH 7.50, PaCO₂ 29 mm Hg. Which action is best?","o":["Stay with the client and coach slow, relaxed breathing","Give IV sodium bicarbonate","Apply a non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min","Encourage the client to breathe deeper and faster"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Calm reassurance and slower breathing let the CO₂ come back up; oxygenation is already normal.","Incorrect. Adds base to an alkalosis.","Incorrect. SpO₂ is 99%; high-flow oxygen isn’t needed.","Incorrect. That blows off more CO₂."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Anxiety speeds breathing → CO₂ is exhaled faster than it is made → pH rises → ionized calcium falls → tingling.","rule":"Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety: check SpO₂ (rule out hypoxia), stay, reassure, coach slow breathing."},{"id":"14|trad|8","sourceId":"14|c|8","q":"A client being treated for metabolic alkalosis from vomiting is weak and has a BP of 92/58. Which nursing action is the priority?","o":["Institute fall precautions and help the client whenever getting up","Encourage the client to walk in the hall","Give an antacid","Restrict oral fluids"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Hypotension and muscle weakness make falls the nursing priority during correction.","Incorrect. Unsafe with weakness and a low BP.","Incorrect. Antacids add base.","Incorrect. The client needs fluid and electrolyte replacement."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Lost volume lowers BP; low potassium weakens muscles → standing up can cause a fall.","rule":"Alkalosis: fall precautions, replace fluid and electrolytes, stop the cause, check electrolytes daily."}],"studio":"alkalosis"},{"id":"iv-plan","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"IV therapy: verify the prescription, solution and pump","notes":["15-overview","15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","15-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Flow dilutes — why the vein matters: SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min; Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots; Central tip dilutes it instantly","Medication safety (NPSG): Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation; High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers; Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA","Why: Small veins tolerate little → match infusate to vein; Every catheter is a doorway → asepsis, remove early; Pressure → Luer-Lok, 10-mL syringe, clamps","Why: IV = seconds to effect, full strength on the vein; Own checks: dilution, rate, pH/osmolarity, compatibility, vein"],"checks":["Explain flow dilutes — why the vein matters in IV therapy.","Explain medication safety (npsg) in IV therapy.","Explain why in IV therapy."],"recallAnswers":["SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min; Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots; Central tip dilutes it instantly","Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation; High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers; Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA","Small veins tolerate little → match infusate to vein; Every catheter is a doorway → asepsis, remove early; Pressure → Luer-Lok, 10-mL syringe, clamps"],"requires":[],"terms":["intravenous","isotonic","extravasation"],"questions":[{"id":"15|trad|1","sourceId":"15|c|1","q":"The nurse is about to hang a unit of packed red blood cells. Which action meets the National Patient Safety Goal?","o":["Check the client and unit with two identifiers and a second qualified professional","Confirm the client’s name with the room number","Ask the client whether the blood type on the label is correct","Check the unit alone and have the AP sign as a witness"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Two client identifiers and two qualified professionals before any blood product.","Incorrect. A room number is never an identifier.","Incorrect. The client can’t verify the unit’s compatibility.","Incorrect. The second checker must be a qualified professional."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A wrong-blood transfusion triggers an antibody attack on the donor cells (a hemolytic reaction) — preventable only by checking before the infusion starts.","rule":"Blood products: two client identifiers and two qualified professionals; the ISBT 128 label shows the facility, donor lot number, product code, and ABO and Rh type; bar coding adds safety."},{"id":"15|trad|4","sourceId":"15|c|4","q":"An adult client is admitted for IV antibiotics and may need a blood transfusion. Which catheter and site are most appropriate?","o":["A 20-gauge catheter in a forearm vein of the nondominant arm","A 24-gauge catheter in the back of the hand","A 14-gauge catheter in the antecubital fossa","An 18-gauge catheter on the palm side of the wrist"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A 20 g suits all therapies, including blood, in a suitable forearm vein.","Incorrect. Small and slow for blood, and hand veins are a poor choice.","Incorrect. Far larger than needed (more phlebitis) and at a joint of flexion.","Incorrect. The palm side of the wrist risks the median nerve."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A smaller catheter in a larger vein leaves room for blood to flow around it, diluting the infusate and protecting the lining; the gauge must still be large enough for blood.","rule":"Table 15.2: 22 g for most therapies (blood without damage), 20 g for all therapies, 18 g for surgery, 14–16 g for trauma; use the smallest gauge that works, in the forearm or upper arm of the nondominant arm."},{"id":"15|trad|9","sourceId":"15|c|9","q":"While preparing to hang a bag of IV fluid, the nurse notices tiny particles floating in it. What should the nurse do?","o":["Don’t hang it; return it and get a new bag","Hang it with an in-line filter","Shake it until the particles dissolve","Hang it and watch the client closely"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Check every container for cracks or pinholes and every fluid for cloudiness, particles or an odd color.","Incorrect. A filter doesn’t make a contaminated bag safe.","Incorrect. Particles suggest contamination or a precipitate.","Incorrect. Prevention beats detection."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Particles can be microbes or precipitates; infused, they lodge in the lung capillaries (a red cell is about the largest thing that passes) and add to phlebitis and infection.","rule":"Action Alert: inspect every container for cracks or pinholes and every fluid for cloudiness, particles or color change before hanging it."},{"id":"15|trad|11","sourceId":"15|c|11","q":"A client is receiving a high-alert drug through a smart pump with a drug library. Which statement about the nurse’s role is correct?","o":["The nurse still checks the calculation, the client and the site, and validates every alarm","The pump’s dose limits make a second check unnecessary","Alarms can be silenced if the pump has been accurate before","The pump replaces hourly site checks"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Smart pumps catch programming errors but can’t see a swollen arm or a wrong patient.","Incorrect. Libraries help, but the nurse remains the final check.","Incorrect. Alarm fatigue is a National Patient Safety Goal concern.","Incorrect. A pump never replaces assessment."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Technology moves errors rather than ending them: a dose limit can stop a 10-fold overdose, but not an infiltration, a disconnection or the wrong client.","rule":"Smart pumps with drug libraries and dose limits for high-alert drugs; keep manual calculation skills, validate every alarm, guard against alarm fatigue, and keep watching the client and site."},{"id":"15|trad|17","sourceId":"15|c|17","q":"An older adult with heart failure is on a fluid restriction and needs several IV antibiotic doses a day. What should the nurse ask the pharmacist about?","o":["Diluting the drugs in the smallest safe volume","Mixing each dose in 250 mL of saline","Giving all the doses together in one infusion","Running each dose by gravity"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The smallest diluent, by syringe pump or IV push, leaves room for drinking.","Incorrect. Large volumes and extra sodium strain the heart.","Incorrect. Drugs may be incompatible, and dosing times matter.","Incorrect. Gravity is less accurate; a pump is preferred."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Every diluent milliliter counts against the restriction, and routine saline diluent adds sodium that holds water — a concern with heart failure or hypertension.","rule":"Older adults: check that the order fits the client, use electronic pumps, smallest diluent volume (syringe pump or IV push); saline diluent adds sodium."}],"studio":"iv-plan"},{"id":"peripheral-iv","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Peripheral IVs: insertion, assessment and early complications","notes":["15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","15-complications-of-iv-therapy","15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein; Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining","Choosing the gauge (Table 15.2): Smallest gauge that works; 22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery; 14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation; Bigger → more phlebitis","Choosing the site (Box 15.1): Upper arm/forearm, nondominant, distal → proximal; Not: wrist, flexion, hard veins, legs; Not: mastectomy, lymph dissection, fistula, paralysis","Why#2: Midline = peripheral rules; TPN or vesicant in a midline = classic error"],"checks":["Explain why in Peripheral IVs.","Explain choosing the gauge (table 15.2) in Peripheral IVs.","Explain choosing the site (box 15.1) in Peripheral IVs."],"recallAnswers":["Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein; Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining","Smallest gauge that works; 22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery; 14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation; Bigger → more phlebitis","Upper arm/forearm, nondominant, distal → proximal; Not: wrist, flexion, hard veins, legs; Not: mastectomy, lymph dissection, fistula, paralysis"],"requires":[],"terms":["phlebitis","infiltration","extravasation"],"questions":[{"id":"15|trad|3","sourceId":"15|c|3","q":"The nurse has made two unsuccessful attempts to start a peripheral IV. What should the nurse do next?","o":["Ask a more experienced clinician or the IV team to try","Try a third time in the other arm","Try a smaller catheter instead","Tell the provider the client can’t have an IV"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Limit attempts to two per clinician, then escalate.","Incorrect. More attempts hurt and raise infection risk.","Incorrect. That is still a third attempt.","Incorrect. A more skilled clinician, ultrasound or another route may still work."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Each stick damages the vein and breaks the skin barrier; repeated attempts cause pain, hematoma and infection, and use up veins needed later.","rule":"Two attempts per clinician, then a more skilled clinician, the IV team or another route; ultrasound only by trained users."},{"id":"15|trad|12","sourceId":"15|c|12","q":"A client receiving refrigerated IV fluid reports cramping pain above the catheter site, and the rate has slowed. The site isn’t swollen. What should the nurse do?","o":["Slow the rate and apply a warm compress","Remove the catheter and restart in the other arm","Apply an ice pack","Increase the rate to push through the spasm"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Venous spasm: warmth relaxes the vein; don’t remove the catheter.","Incorrect. Spasm doesn’t require removal.","Incorrect. Cold makes a spasm worse.","Incorrect. Faster cold fluid aggravates it."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Cold or irritating fluid makes the smooth muscle of the vein wall contract → cramping pain and slower flow, without fluid leaking into the tissue.","rule":"Venous spasm: slow the rate, warm compress, don’t remove; prevent with room-temperature fluids."},{"id":"15|trad|16","sourceId":"15|c|16","q":"The nurse is choosing a site for a peripheral catheter in a 90-year-old client. Which choice is best?","o":["A vein in the forearm, higher than the hand","A vein on the back of the hand","A vein in the foot","The external jugular vein"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Start higher on the arm; hand veins in older adults are fragile and tortuous.","Incorrect. Hand veins tear and infiltrate easily in older adults.","Incorrect. Leg veins raise the risk of DVT and infiltration in adults.","Incorrect. Only in emergencies."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Older adults have thin skin with little supporting fat; the veins on the back of the hand are superficial, tortuous and easily blown.","rule":"Older adults: avoid hand veins, start higher on the arm; consider a midline or PICC when veins are few, fragile or hard."}],"studio":"peripheral-iv"},{"id":"central-lines","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Central lines: infection prevention and protecting the connection","notes":["15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","15-complications-of-iv-therapy"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk); Choose by duration and patient risks","PICCs: Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early; Short tip → thrombosis; Any infusate; > 14 days; Fewer infections; no pneumothorax","PICC care: Phlebitis, DVT, CRBSI; baseline arm circumference; Blood draw ≥ 4 Fr; transfusion via pump (< 4 hr); No heavy lifting; not with crutches; Flush 10 mL before/after, 20 mL after blood; 10-mL syringe","Why#2: Risk rises daily; Strongest step: daily “still needed?”"],"checks":["Explain why in Central lines.","Explain piccs in Central lines.","Explain picc care in Central lines."],"recallAnswers":["PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk); Choose by duration and patient risks","Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early; Short tip → thrombosis; Any infusate; > 14 days; Fewer infections; no pneumothorax","Phlebitis, DVT, CRBSI; baseline arm circumference; Blood draw ≥ 4 Fr; transfusion via pump (< 4 hr); No heavy lifting; not with crutches; Flush 10 mL before/after, 20 mL after blood; 10-mL syringe"],"requires":[],"terms":["PICC","asepsis","embolism"],"questions":[{"id":"15|trad|6","sourceId":"15|c|6","q":"The nurse is changing the transparent dressing on a client’s PICC. How should the nurse remove the old dressing?","o":["Peel it sideways or toward the insertion site","Pull it quickly away from the insertion site","Cut it off with scissors","Soak it with alcohol and lift it from the catheter end"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Peeling toward the site keeps the catheter from being pulled out.","Incorrect. Pulling away can drag the catheter out (dislodgment).","Incorrect. Scissors near a catheter can cut it (rupture, embolism).","Incorrect. Lifting from the catheter end pulls on the catheter."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The adhesive grips both skin and catheter; pulling away from the site pulls the catheter outward with it.","rule":"Central lines and midlines: gauze every 48 hours, transparent every 7 days, the first dressing within 24 hours; remove sideways or toward the site; note the external catheter length each time and report a change."},{"id":"15|trad|7","sourceId":"15|c|7","q":"The nurse meets resistance while flushing a client’s central line with saline. What should the nurse do?","o":["Stop flushing and assess the catheter and connections","Push harder with steady pressure","Switch to a 3-mL syringe to generate more force","Instill a dose of the next drug to clear it"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Resistance means stop — forcing it can rupture the catheter or push a clot into the circulation.","Incorrect. That is how catheters burst.","Incorrect. A smaller syringe creates even higher pressure.","Incorrect. A drug won’t clear a blockage and may precipitate."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A partial blockage (a clot or drug precipitate) plus hand pressure can burst the catheter wall or send the clot into the bloodstream.","rule":"Flushing (Critical Rescue): slow, gentle pressure; aspirate for a blood return; any resistance → stop; 10-mL syringes for central lines. Sudden block = precipitate; gradual = clot (alteplase as prescribed)."},{"id":"15|trad|8","sourceId":"15|c|8","q":"A client’s IV antibiotic finished 20 minutes ago. The provider wants blood cultures drawn from the central line. What should the nurse do?","o":["Wait until at least an hour after the antibiotic infusion to draw them","Draw them now, before the client’s temperature rises","Draw them from the lumen the antibiotic ran through","Flush with 1 mL of saline and then draw"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. No blood cultures within an hour after an antibiotic infusion — the drug can stop organisms from growing in the bottle.","Incorrect. Cultures drawn now may be falsely negative.","Incorrect. Leftover drug in that lumen skews the result.","Incorrect. That isn’t enough to clear the line, and the timing is still wrong."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antibiotic in the sample can suppress the organisms in the culture bottle, hiding the infection.","rule":"Blood from central lines only when the benefit outweighs the CRBSI risk; consider what has been infused (heparin, electrolytes skew results); no cultures within an hour after an antibiotic; scrub the hub; flush 10–20 mL after."},{"id":"15|trad|10","sourceId":"15|c|10","q":"The nurse finds a client’s central line tubing joined to the catheter with tape instead of a locking connection. Which risk is the nurse most concerned about?","o":["Air embolism and bleeding from a disconnection","Phlebitis","Speed shock","Infiltration"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A slip-tip held with tape can separate; an open central line can draw in air.","Incorrect. Not the main danger of a loose connection.","Incorrect. That comes from a drug given too fast.","Incorrect. Infiltration is a peripheral tip leaving the vein."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"When the chest pressure falls with each breath in, an open central catheter can pull air directly into the vena cava; a disconnection also lets blood escape.","rule":"Use Luer-Lok (threaded) connections everywhere — critical on central lines; tape is never an adequate connection; trace the line from the client to the bag before reconnecting."}],"studio":"central-lines"},{"id":"special-infusion-routes","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Special infusion routes: the device changes the precautions","notes":["15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock; IO = IV-speed access when no vein","Running a subcutaneous line: 25–27 g; pinch ~2 in; Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home); Irritation → remove; rotate weekly","Intraspinal safety: Filter + preservative-free; no alcohol (use povidone-iodine); Check alertness, breathing, itching; Stiff neck, > 101 °F, neuro change → report now; Epidural hematoma (anticoagulants)","Why#2: Artery: pressure → bleeding; Peritoneum: pressure, peritonitis; Spine: cord — infection, hematoma"],"checks":["Explain why in Special infusion routes.","Explain running a subcutaneous line in Special infusion routes.","Explain intraspinal safety in Special infusion routes."],"recallAnswers":["Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock; IO = IV-speed access when no vein","25–27 g; pinch ~2 in; Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home); Irritation → remove; rotate weekly","Filter + preservative-free; no alcohol (use povidone-iodine); Check alertness, breathing, itching; Stiff neck, > 101 °F, neuro change → report now; Epidural hematoma (anticoagulants)"],"requires":[],"terms":["hypodermoclysis","intraosseous","epidural"],"questions":[{"id":"15|trad|14","sourceId":"15|c|14","q":"The nurse is caring for a client with an epidural catheter for pain. Which antiseptic should the nurse use at the site?","o":["Povidone-iodine","Chlorhexidine with alcohol","70% alcohol","Hydrogen peroxide"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Alcohol can track along an intraspinal catheter and damage nerves, so povidone-iodine is used.","Incorrect. It contains alcohol.","Incorrect. Alcohol near an intraspinal catheter can injure nerves.","Incorrect. Not an infusion antiseptic."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antiseptic can follow the catheter into the epidural or intrathecal space, where alcohol is toxic to nerve tissue.","rule":"Intraspinal infusions: an in-line filter, preservative-free drugs only, no alcohol at the site (povidone-iodine), check alertness, breathing and itching; report a stiff neck, temperature over 101 °F or any neurologic change at once."},{"id":"15|trad|15","sourceId":"15|c|15","q":"Which action best prevents a life-threatening complication in a client with a radial arterial line?","o":["Keep every connection locked and visible","Disconnect it every hour to check for clots","Keep the arm covered with blankets for warmth","Lower the transducer below the heart"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A disconnected arterial line can bleed fast enough to be fatal; Luer-Lok every connection and keep it in view.","Incorrect. Every disconnection risks hemorrhage and infection.","Incorrect. Covering the line hides a disconnection.","Incorrect. The transducer is leveled at the atrium for accurate readings."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Arterial blood is under far higher pressure than venous blood, so an open connection bleeds rapidly; bleeding or a blocked artery is more serious than the same problem in a vein.","rule":"Arterial lines (Critical Rescue): Luer-Lok every connection; check the limb’s warmth, sensation, capillary refill and pulse; transducer at the level of the atrium."}],"studio":"special-infusion-routes"},{"id":"immune-defense","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Immune defense: innate, adaptive, active and passive","notes":["16-overview","16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","16-antibody-mediated-immunity","16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","16-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why inflammation looks the way it does: Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm; Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain; IL-1, TNF → fever","History: Infections, vaccines/boosters; Immunosuppressing drugs; Allergies, wounds, exposures","Priority problems: Risk for infection; sepsis; Pain, lost function; Incomplete vaccination","Why the second exposure is different: First time: slow, IgM, illness; Memory cells → fast, huge IgG response; Vaccines create memory; boosters refresh it"],"checks":["Explain why inflammation looks the way it does in Immune defense.","Explain history in Immune defense.","Explain priority problems in Immune defense."],"recallAnswers":["Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm; Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain; IL-1, TNF → fever","Infections, vaccines/boosters; Immunosuppressing drugs; Allergies, wounds, exposures","Risk for infection; sepsis; Pain, lost function; Incomplete vaccination"],"requires":[],"terms":["immunity","antibody","lymphocyte"],"questions":[{"id":"16|trad|7","sourceId":"16|c|7","q":"A client asks why a tetanus booster is needed when the client was vaccinated as a child. Which response is best?","o":["“Protection from a vaccine fades over the years; a booster brings your antibody level back up.”","“The first vaccine didn’t work.”","“Boosters are only for people who have had tetanus.”","“A booster gives you ready-made antibodies from someone else.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Artificial active immunity lasts years but needs boosters.","Incorrect. It worked; immunity simply wanes.","Incorrect. Boosters keep vaccinated people protected.","Incorrect. That describes passive immunity (immune globulin)."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antibody levels decline over the years; a booster re-stimulates memory cells to produce a fresh wave of antibody.","rule":"Artificial active immunity (vaccines) lasts years and needs boosters; older adults especially need tetanus and pertussis boosters."},{"id":"16|trad|8","sourceId":"16|c|8","q":"Why is protection from an injection of immune globulin short-lived?","o":["The donated antibodies are recognized as foreign and cleared, and no memory cells form","The injection destroys the client’s own B cells","Immune globulin works only after several weeks","The client becomes allergic to it"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Passive immunity is borrowed: immediate but temporary.","Incorrect. It doesn’t harm the client’s B cells.","Incorrect. It works at once.","Incorrect. Allergy isn’t the reason."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antibodies made by another person or animal are foreign proteins; they are gradually removed, and because the client’s own B cells weren’t sensitized, no memory remains.","rule":"Passive immunity bridges a dangerous gap (rabies, tetanus, snake venom, convalescent plasma) until active immunity can develop."},{"id":"16|trad|9","sourceId":"16|c|9","q":"Why does losing helper (CD4+) T cells weaken antibody responses as well as cell-mediated immunity?","o":["Helper T cells help B cells become sensitized and send cytokines that boost all immunity","Helper T cells make antibodies themselves","Helper T cells engulf bacteria directly","Helper T cells form the skin barrier"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. They are the organizers of inflammation, AMI and CMI.","Incorrect. Plasma cells make antibodies.","Incorrect. Neutrophils and macrophages phagocytize.","Incorrect. Skin is an innate barrier."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The macrophage presents the antigen, the helper T cell brings it to the B cell and releases cytokines; without helpers, B cells and cytotoxic T cells respond poorly.","rule":"Helper T cells (CD4+) are the organizers; their loss (as in HIV) cripples every part of immunity."}],"studio":"immune-defense"},{"id":"inflammation-cells","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Inflammation and white cells: explain the response","notes":["16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self","Five cardinal signs: Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function; Same for any cause; scale = intensity","Toll-like receptors: Recognize microbes; and stressed self cells","Why#2: Specialized cells → read the differential, not just the total"],"checks":["Explain why in Inflammation and white cells.","Explain five cardinal signs in Inflammation and white cells.","Explain toll-like receptors in Inflammation and white cells."],"recallAnswers":["Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self","Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function; Same for any cause; scale = intensity","Recognize microbes; and stressed self cells"],"requires":[],"terms":["neutrophil","eosinophil","phagocytosis"],"questions":[{"id":"16|trad|1","sourceId":"16|c|1","q":"Which laboratory result does the nurse recognize as consistent with an allergic response?","o":["Eosinophils 700/mm³","Neutrophils 4000/mm³","Lymphocytes 2500/mm³","Monocytes 400/mm³"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Above the normal 50–400/mm³ — eosinophils rise with allergy.","Incorrect. Within the normal 2500–7000/mm³.","Incorrect. Within the normal 1000–4000/mm³.","Incorrect. Within the normal 100–800/mm³."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Allergic and parasitic stimuli expand the eosinophil population; very high counts can cause or worsen allergic responses.","rule":"Table 16.3: neutrophils 2500–7000, lymphocytes 1000–4000, monocytes 100–800, eosinophils 50–400, basophils 25–100 per mm³."},{"id":"16|trad|2","sourceId":"16|c|2","q":"A client’s differential shows a left shift. What does this mean?","o":["The bone marrow is releasing immature neutrophils because infection is outpacing the supply","The client is having an allergic reaction","The client’s immunity is fully recovered","Lymphocytes are making more antibodies"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. More bands than usual — a struggling defense and sepsis risk.","Incorrect. Allergy raises eosinophils.","Incorrect. It signals the opposite.","Incorrect. A left shift concerns neutrophils."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bands are young neutrophils that can’t phagocytize; seeing many of them means the marrow is emptying its reserve.","rule":"Left shift (bandemia) = sepsis risk; report it with fever, tachycardia or falling BP."},{"id":"16|trad|3","sourceId":"16|c|3","q":"A client’s absolute neutrophil count (ANC) is very low. Which conclusion is correct?","o":["The client is highly susceptible to infection","The client is protected against infection","The client has an allergy","The client’s antibodies are not working"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The ANC measures mature neutrophils able to phagocytize — the lower it is, the more susceptible the client.","Incorrect. That would be a high ANC.","Incorrect. Not what the ANC measures.","Incorrect. The ANC concerns neutrophils, not antibodies."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Only mature neutrophils can phagocytize, so their number is the best measure of resistance to bacterial infection.","rule":"ANC: the higher, the better protected; the lower, the more susceptible."},{"id":"16|trad|4","sourceId":"16|c|4","q":"Which client has inflammation that is not caused by infection?","o":["A client with a sprained ankle","A client with appendicitis","A client with otitis media","A client with viral hepatitis"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Injury causes inflammation without germs.","Incorrect. An infection that inflames.","Incorrect. An infection that inflames.","Incorrect. A viral infection that inflames."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Inflammation is the same tissue program for any injury or invasion — sprains, MI, blisters, allergic rhinitis and contact dermatitis inflame without infection.","rule":"Inflammation can occur without infection; infection usually triggers inflammation. Inflammation alone gives no lasting immunity."},{"id":"16|trad|5","sourceId":"16|c|5","q":"A client’s surgical incision is red and slightly swollen on day 2. Which additional finding most suggests infection rather than normal inflammation?","o":["Purulent drainage with a temperature of 101.4 °F","Mild warmth around the incision","Slight tenderness when touched","Edges that are pink"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Pus and fever point to infection.","Incorrect. Warmth is part of normal inflammation.","Incorrect. Expected early after surgery.","Incorrect. Expected in healing."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Redness, warmth, swelling and pain are the vascular stage of any inflammation; pus (dead neutrophils and tissue) and fever signal an infection being fought.","rule":"Pus, fever, a rising WBC with a left shift, or a positive culture point to infection; report and document."},{"id":"16|trad|12","sourceId":"16|c|12","q":"At which point in inflammation do NSAIDs and other anti-inflammatory drugs act?","o":["The cyclooxygenase (COX) step of the arachidonic acid cascade","The release of antibodies by plasma cells","The formation of scar tissue","The killing of virus-infected cells"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. COX turns arachidonic acid into mediators such as prostaglandins that sustain inflammation.","Incorrect. That is antibody-mediated immunity.","Incorrect. That is repair.","Incorrect. That is cytotoxic T-cell work."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"In the cellular exudate stage, COX converts arachidonic acid into prostaglandins, leukotrienes and other mediators; blocking COX lowers them.","rule":"Stage II (cellular exudate): neutrophilia, pus and the AA–COX cascade that anti-inflammatory drugs block."},{"id":"16|trad|13","sourceId":"16|c|13","q":"After a myocardial infarction, scar replaces 20% of a client’s left ventricle. What does the nurse expect?","o":["The ventricle pumps at least 20% less effectively","The scar will contract like normal muscle within a year","The scar will eventually be replaced by new muscle","The pumping ability won’t change"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Scar never works like the original tissue.","Incorrect. Scar doesn’t contract.","Incorrect. Heart muscle can’t regrow; it scars.","Incorrect. Function falls in proportion to the scar."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Where cells can’t divide, repair forms new vessels and scar tissue; scar has no contractile fibers.","rule":"Stage III (repair): replacement where cells divide, scar where they can’t; scar means lost function."}],"studio":"inflammation-cells"},{"id":"immune-aging","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Aging immunity: subtle infection and weaker test responses","notes":["16-immunity-in-older-adults"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake; Look at function, not just temperature","Inflammation changes: Normal count, weak neutrophils; No ↑WBC, often no fever; Missed until sepsis","Antibody changes: New antigens: flu, pneumococcal, shingles vaccines; Old immunity fades: tetanus, pertussis boosters"],"checks":["Explain why in Aging immunity.","Explain inflammation changes in Aging immunity.","Explain antibody changes in Aging immunity."],"recallAnswers":["First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake; Look at function, not just temperature","Normal count, weak neutrophils; No ↑WBC, often no fever; Missed until sepsis","New antigens: flu, pneumococcal, shingles vaccines; Old immunity fades: tetanus, pertussis boosters"],"requires":[],"terms":["immunosenescence","immunosuppression"],"questions":[{"id":"16|trad|10","sourceId":"16|c|10","q":"Which finding is most likely to be the first sign of a serious infection in an 84-year-old client?","o":["New confusion","A temperature of 103 °F","A white count of 22,000/mm³","Shaking chills"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Older adults often have no fever and no leukocytosis; a change in thinking, walking or eating may come first.","Incorrect. Often absent in older adults.","Incorrect. Leukocytosis is often absent.","Incorrect. Less reliable in older adults."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"With age, neutrophils work less well and the body is slower to sense invaders, so the response that produces classic signs is muted; minor infections can be missed until the person is septic.","rule":"In older adults, look for confusion, falls, poor intake or a vague decline — not just fever or a high white count."},{"id":"16|trad|11","sourceId":"16|c|11","q":"An older adult who has been exposed to tuberculosis has a negative tuberculin skin test. What should the nurse consider?","o":["Weaker T-cell function with age can cause a false-negative result","A negative test always rules out TB","The client is cured of TB","The test was read too late"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The skin test depends on cell-mediated immunity, which weakens with age.","Incorrect. Not in older adults with weak CMI.","Incorrect. Nothing suggests treatment or cure.","Incorrect. Nothing suggests a reading error; the likely cause is weak T-cell function."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A tuberculin skin test reaction is a T-cell response; when T-cell function declines, the reaction can be absent even with infection.","rule":"Older adults: weaker T-cell function → false-negative TB skin tests and more bacterial and fungal infections of the skin, lungs and urinary tract."}],"studio":"immune-aging"},{"id":"allergy-airway","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema: airway changes the priority","notes":["17-overview","17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","17-angioedema","17-anaphylaxis","17-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why epinephrine comes first: Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough; Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways; Steroids: slow; prevent a late return","Early and late signs: Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives; Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness; ↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure","Treatment: O₂, epinephrine + corticosteroids; Stridor / can’t swallow → intubate; Tracheostomy below the swelling; icatibant, ecallantide if refractory","Why ACE inhibitors cause swelling: ACE breaks down bradykinin; Block ACE → bradykinin builds up; Late onset; histamine drugs may fail"],"checks":["Explain why epinephrine comes first in Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema.","Explain early and late signs in Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema.","Explain treatment in Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema."],"recallAnswers":["Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough; Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways; Steroids: slow; prevent a late return","Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives; Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness; ↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure","O₂, epinephrine + corticosteroids; Stridor / can’t swallow → intubate; Tracheostomy below the swelling; icatibant, ecallantide if refractory"],"requires":[],"terms":["anaphylaxis","angioedema","epinephrine"],"questions":[{"id":"17|trad|2","sourceId":"17|c|2","q":"Which finding in a client with angioedema means the client needs intubation?","o":["Stridor and inability to swallow","Swollen lips","Itching of the face","A heart rate of 100"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Stridor or inability to swallow means the larynx is swelling shut.","Incorrect. Needs close watching, but not yet intubation.","Incorrect. Not an airway sign.","Incorrect. Not an airway sign."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Swelling that spreads from the lips and tongue to the larynx narrows the airway; stridor is turbulent air through a narrowed larynx.","rule":"Angioedema: indications for intubation are stridor and inability to swallow; if intubation fails, an emergency tracheostomy below the swelling."},{"id":"17|trad|3","sourceId":"17|c|3","q":"A client’s angioedema has resolved after treatment. What does the nurse expect next?","o":["Keep the IV access and monitor the client for 2 to 6 hours","Discharge the client right away","Restart the ACE inhibitor at a lower dose","Remove the IV and let the client go home with a friend"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The causative drug may outlast the treatment, so swelling can return.","Incorrect. Rebound swelling is possible.","Incorrect. The client must avoid the causative drug.","Incorrect. IV access is maintained during monitoring."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The drug that caused the reaction often has a longer half-life than the drugs used to treat it, so symptoms can recur when treatment stops.","rule":"Angioedema: drug therapy may continue for hours after resolution; then IV access and monitoring for 2–6 hours; teach the cause, avoidance and to seek emergency care at the first sign."},{"id":"17|trad|4","sourceId":"17|c|4","q":"A client is experiencing anaphylaxis. The Rapid Response Team has been called. Which action should the nurse perform first?","o":["Inject epinephrine intramuscularly","Give diphenhydramine","Start a second IV line","Draw arterial blood gases"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. IM epinephrine is the first-line intervention; delay is the major factor in deaths.","Incorrect. Antihistamines are add-ons.","Incorrect. Fluids follow epinephrine.","Incorrect. Oxygen and epinephrine come before blood gases."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Epinephrine constricts dilated vessels, strengthens cardiac contraction and dilates the bronchioles within minutes.","rule":"Epinephrine IM first (no absolute contraindication during anaphylaxis); repeat every 5–15 minutes; IV infusion after three ineffective IM doses."},{"id":"17|trad|5","sourceId":"17|c|5","q":"How should the nurse give oxygen to a client with anaphylaxis who is not intubated?","o":["By high-flow nonrebreather mask at 90% to 100%","By nasal cannula at 2 L/min","Only after arterial blood gases are drawn","By simple face mask at 5 L/min"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Maximum oxygen to support gas exchange, before any blood gas.","Incorrect. Too little for hypoxemia from bronchoconstriction.","Incorrect. Don’t wait for ABGs.","Incorrect. Too little."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bronchoconstriction, mucosal swelling and mucus block gas exchange; a nonrebreather delivers the highest oxygen concentration without intubation.","rule":"Box 17.4: high-flow nonrebreather 90–100%; be ready to intubate; albuterol for bronchospasm; continuous breath-sound checks."},{"id":"17|trad|6","sourceId":"17|c|6","q":"A client with a penicillin allergy has a new prescription for cefazolin. What should the nurse do?","o":["Hold the drug and contact the provider about the allergy","Give it — cephalosporins are a different class","Give half the dose and watch for a reaction","Give it with diphenhydramine"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Penicillins and cephalosporins have similar structures, so a reaction is likely.","Incorrect. Cross-reactions are common.","Incorrect. Any dose can trigger anaphylaxis.","Incorrect. Premedication is a provider decision when a needed drug must be given anyway."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"IgE made against penicillin can recognize the similar ring structure in cephalosporins → mast cell degranulation.","rule":"Action Alert: ask about allergies before every drug; document the allergen and reaction; know cross-reactions; if a known allergen must be given, IV access, airway equipment at the bedside and premedication."},{"id":"17|trad|28","sourceId":"17|c|28","q":"A client is scheduled for intradermal testing for a suspected penicillin allergy. Which statement by the nurse is accurate?","o":["“The test is done under close supervision because it can trigger a reaction.”","“You can do this test at home and read the results yourself.”","“A blood test would be more specific than this skin test.”","“This test only works for food allergies.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Skin tests deliberately expose the client to an allergen, so they are done where a reaction can be treated.","Incorrect. Done in the outpatient setting under supervision.","Incorrect. Blood tests are less specific and can give false positives.","Incorrect. Intradermal tests are used for insect venom and penicillin; skin prick tests for environmental and food allergies."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Injecting a small amount of allergen just under the skin can trigger IgE-mediated release of histamine — usually local, but possibly systemic.","rule":"Allergy testing: skin prick (environmental, food), intradermal (insect venom, penicillin), patch tests; outpatient, under close supervision; blood tests confirm but are less specific."},{"id":"17|ngn|5","sourceId":"17|n|5","q":"The nurse is teaching a client who has a peanut allergy about an epinephrine autoinjector. Select the 4 statements that show the client understands.","o":["“I’ll carry two autoinjectors with me at all times.”","“I can inject it into the middle of my outer thigh, right through my jeans.”","“After the click, I’ll hold it in place for 3 seconds.”","“I’ll go to the hospital after using it, even if I feel better.”","“I’ll keep it in my car’s glove box so I always know where it is.”","“I’ll wait until I can’t breathe before I use it.”","“If the liquid is discolored, it’s still fine as long as it isn’t expired.”"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. A second dose may be needed.","Correct. Through clothing is fine; avoid seams and pockets.","Correct. Hold 3 seconds, then massage the site 10 seconds.","Correct. Symptoms can return; seek care right away.","Incorrect. Cars expose it to temperature extremes; keep it with you.","Incorrect. Use it at the first symptom — before calling 911.","Incorrect. A discolored drug needs a replacement device."],"ex":[],"selectCount":4,"phys":"Epinephrine is lifesaving only if it is at hand, potent (protected from light and heat) and used early.","rule":"Box 17.1: practice with a trainer, carry two, protect from light and temperature extremes, check the expiration date, clarity and safety release. Box 17.2: outer thigh at 90°, click, hold 3 s, massage 10 s, seek care."}],"studio":"allergy-airway"},{"id":"lupus","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Lupus: immune injury, organ monitoring and medication safety","notes":["17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Who and why: 3× as many women; Genes (HLA types) + triggers; Ask the family history","History: Past 5 years; pain without injury; Family history; unexplained fevers; Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide","Priority problems: Pain, fatigue, organ failure","Why: Genes load, triggers fire; Ask about relatives and recent triggers"],"checks":["Explain who and why in Lupus.","Explain history in Lupus.","Explain priority problems in Lupus."],"recallAnswers":["3× as many women; Genes (HLA types) + triggers; Ask the family history","Past 5 years; pain without injury; Family history; unexplained fevers; Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide","Pain, fatigue, organ failure"],"requires":[],"terms":["autoimmunity","hydroxychloroquine","prednisone"],"questions":[{"id":"17|trad|8","sourceId":"17|c|8","q":"A client with SLE who has taken prednisone daily for 2 years calls to say vomiting has kept the client from taking the dose for a day. What should the nurse advise?","o":["Contact the rheumatology provider right away to discuss other options","Skip the prednisone until the vomiting stops","Take a double dose tomorrow","Switch to over-the-counter ibuprofen instead"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Missing long-term corticosteroids can cause a life-threatening adrenal crisis.","Incorrect. Abrupt stopping is dangerous.","Incorrect. Doubling doesn’t solve the problem and isn’t prescribed.","Incorrect. NSAIDs don’t replace cortisol."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Long-term prednisone suppresses the adrenal glands; without the drug, the body lacks cortisol to maintain BP and glucose under stress.","rule":"Drug Alert: never stop long-term corticosteroids abruptly; if the client can’t tolerate the oral dose, contact the provider immediately."},{"id":"17|trad|9","sourceId":"17|c|9","q":"Which information should the nurse include when teaching a client with SLE who will start belimumab?","o":["It can be given intravenously or by subcutaneous injection","It has very few side effects","Don’t chew, crush or split the tablet","Take it at bedtime because it causes drowsiness"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Belimumab is given by infusion or subcutaneously; monitoring follows infusions.","Incorrect. Infusion and severe hypersensitivity reactions can occur.","Incorrect. It isn’t a tablet.","Incorrect. Not a known effect."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Belimumab is a monoclonal antibody that lowers autoantibody levels; as a protein, it is given by injection or infusion, not by mouth.","rule":"Belimumab (IV or subcutaneous) and anifrolumab (IV): given where a severe hypersensitivity reaction can be managed; monitor during and up to 2 hours after infusion."},{"id":"17|trad|10","sourceId":"17|c|10","q":"A client with SLE says fatigue makes it impossible to keep up with work and home. Which suggestion is best?","o":["Do the most important activities first and plan rest periods through the day","Push through the fatigue to build endurance","Stay in bed until the fatigue passes","Exercise vigorously once a week"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Box 17.7 — prioritize and plan rest.","Incorrect. Overexertion worsens fatigue.","Incorrect. Low-impact activity builds endurance.","Incorrect. Short, low-impact sessions with rest are better."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"SLE fatigue comes from chronic inflammation, poor sleep from pain, stress and infections; pacing spreads limited energy across the day.","rule":"Box 17.7: important activities first, planned rest, shop online, prepare meals ahead, say no, accept help, sleep and diet, no nicotine; low-impact exercise (2 × 15–30 min) with rest after."},{"id":"17|ngn|9","sourceId":"17|n|9","q":"A client with SLE takes hydroxychloroquine and prednisone. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","o":["Have an eye exam with visual fields every 6 months","Report any blurred vision","Never stop the prednisone suddenly","Call the provider at once if you can’t keep the prednisone down","Use sunscreen with SPF 30 or higher and wear a wide-brimmed hat","Stop the prednisone when your joints feel better","Tanning beds are safe because they are indoors"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Hydroxychloroquine can damage the retina.","Correct. A sign of retinal toxicity.","Correct. Abrupt withdrawal can cause adrenal crisis.","Correct. Missing doses risks adrenal crisis; other options are needed.","Correct. UV light worsens every part of SLE.","Incorrect. Doses are tapered exactly under the provider’s direction.","Incorrect. Tanning beds are UV light."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hydroxychloroquine accumulates in retinal cells and can cause irreversible central vision loss; long-term prednisone suppresses the adrenal glands, so a sudden stop leaves the body without cortisol.","rule":"Hydroxychloroquine: eye exam before starting and every 6 months; report blurred vision. Corticosteroids: never stop abruptly; taper exactly; call if unable to take them. Sun protection always."}],"studio":"lupus"},{"id":"hiv","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"HIV: suppress the virus, prevent transmission and protect immunity","notes":["17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","17-hiv-history-and-testing","17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS; The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI","Brain and nerves: Crypto: headache, stiff neck; Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report; ↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now","TasP — treatment as prevention: HIV-positive; keep the viral load undetectable; Greatly reduces sexual transmission; Beyond the book: U = U","Why#2: It kills the organizers (CD4+ cells); All immunity weakens → harmless organisms turn deadly"],"checks":["Explain why in HIV.","Explain brain and nerves in HIV.","Explain tasp — treatment as prevention in HIV."],"recallAnswers":["All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS; The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI","Crypto: headache, stiff neck; Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report; ↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now","HIV-positive; keep the viral load undetectable; Greatly reduces sexual transmission; Beyond the book: U = U"],"requires":[],"terms":["HIV","antiretroviral","opportunistic infection"],"questions":[{"id":"17|trad|12","sourceId":"17|c|12","q":"Which instruction should the nurse give a client starting preexposure prophylaxis (PrEP)?","o":["Continue safer sex practices while taking PrEP","Take PrEP within 24 hours after sex","Expect regular testing for hepatitis A","Take PrEP only with a partner known to have HIV"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. PrEP doesn’t replace safer sex; HIV tests every 3 months.","Incorrect. That describes post-exposure use; PrEP is taken before exposure.","Incorrect. The concern is hepatitis B with tenofovir combinations.","Incorrect. Partners’ status is often unknown."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"If infection occurs while on PrEP, the virus is exposed to too few drugs and resistance develops; condoms also prevent other STIs.","rule":"Drug Alert: PrEP and TasP don’t replace safer sex; HIV testing every 3 months; tenofovir combinations not for people with hepatitis B."},{"id":"17|trad|13","sourceId":"17|c|13","q":"A client on cART calls the telehealth nurse after forgetting the medication on vacation. The provider has sent a prescription to a local pharmacy. What should the nurse teach?","o":["“When you pick it up, take today’s next dose and continue as usual.”","“Take a double dose for the next 2 days.”","“Starting within 2 to 3 days will be fine.”","“You’ll need lab tests before you can restart.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Resume as soon as possible and continue the schedule.","Incorrect. Doubling isn’t recommended.","Incorrect. Delays promote resistance.","Incorrect. Restart without waiting for labs."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Every hour of low drug levels lets HIV replicate and mutate; restarting promptly restores suppression.","rule":"Drug Alert: avoid missing or delaying doses; resume and continue as soon as a missed dose is discovered; ongoing missed doses promote resistance."},{"id":"17|trad|14","sourceId":"17|c|14","q":"Which dietary advice should the nurse give a client with HIV and Candida esophagitis?","o":["“Eat soft, cool foods such as pudding and yogurt.”","“Eat more chicken and beef.”","“Cut back on all foods to avoid pain.”","“Limit fluids to 1 L a day.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Soft, cool, easy-to-swallow foods ease painful swallowing.","Incorrect. Tough meats are hard to swallow.","Incorrect. Nutrition must be maintained — wasting is a risk.","Incorrect. Fluids should be encouraged."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Candida inflames the esophageal lining, making swallowing painful and reducing intake — a path to wasting.","rule":"Thrush and Candida esophagitis: systemic fluconazole, soft toothbrush, high-protein high-calorie plan with soft, cool, easy-to-swallow foods."},{"id":"17|trad|16","sourceId":"17|c|16","q":"A nurse sustains a needlestick while giving an injection to a client with HIV. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Wash the area well, then contact employee health immediately","Finish the shift and report it tomorrow","Squeeze the site and apply bleach","Wait for an HIV test result before telling anyone"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Wash first; PEP must start within 72 hours, ideally within 2.","Incorrect. Delay reduces PEP’s effectiveness.","Incorrect. Wash with soap and water; bleach injures skin.","Incorrect. Treatment decisions can’t wait."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"After exposure, the virus takes hours to days to establish infection; antiretrovirals started early can stop it.","rule":"Occupational PEP: wash the area, contact employee health at once, start within 72 hours (the sooner the better), take for 28 days, test before and after."},{"id":"17|trad|17","sourceId":"17|c|17","q":"A client with HIV says, “My viral load is undetectable, so the virus is gone.” Which response by the nurse is best?","o":["“Undetectable means the virus is below what the test can measure; keep taking your medicine and practicing safer sex.”","“That’s right — you can stop your medicine now.”","“Undetectable means you never had HIV.”","“You don’t need further viral load tests.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The virus may still be present below the test’s limit.","Incorrect. cART controls but doesn’t kill the virus.","Incorrect. In someone with HIV, an undetectable result reflects treatment, not the absence of infection.","Incorrect. Monitoring continues every 3–4 months."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antiretrovirals suppress replication; the virus persists in the body, and stopping drugs lets it rebound.","rule":"Undetectable ≠ virus-free: continue cART and safer sex (your book); monitoring every 3–4 months."},{"id":"17|trad|18","sourceId":"17|c|18","q":"The nurse is taking a sexual history from a new client. Which question is most appropriate?","o":["“How many sexual partners have you had in the past 6 months?”","“Does your wife know you’re here today?”","“You always protect yourself, right?”","“You don’t have sex with men, do you?”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Neutral, nonjudgmental and specific (Box 17.10).","Incorrect. Assumes gender and relationship.","Incorrect. A leading question.","Incorrect. Assumes and judges."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Honest answers depend on the client feeling safe and unjudged; neutral language makes accurate risk assessment possible.","rule":"Sexual history: after rapport, private, confidential (with limits), gender-neutral language, no assumptions; the five Ps — partners, practices, protection, past history, prevention of pregnancy."},{"id":"17|trad|19","sourceId":"17|c|19","q":"A client with stage III HIV has a cough, night sweats and weight loss. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Place the client on Airborne Precautions in addition to Standard Precautions","Collect three sputum smears over 3 days before isolating","Encourage the client to walk in the hall","Place the client on Contact Precautions only"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Airborne plus Standard Precautions; a rapid molecular assay confirms or rules out TB.","Incorrect. Isolation comes first, and your book prefers a rapid molecular assay to smears.","Incorrect. Spreads TB on the unit.","Incorrect. TB spreads by airborne droplet nuclei."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"TB bacilli travel in tiny airborne particles that remain suspended; people with few CD4+ cells are at high risk of active TB.","rule":"Action Alert: Airborne plus Standard Precautions for a stage III client with TB symptoms; rapid molecular assay (not a sputum smear) for initial detection."},{"id":"17|trad|20","sourceId":"17|c|20","q":"A client with HIV may start a regimen containing abacavir. Which test must be done first?","o":["HLA-B∗5701","Trofile assay","Western blot","CD4:CD8 ratio"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It identifies people at high risk of a hypersensitivity reaction to abacavir.","Incorrect. Required before maraviroc (CCR5).","Incorrect. A confirmatory diagnostic test.","Incorrect. Part of monitoring, not drug screening."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Certain inherited HLA types make the immune system react strongly to abacavir — from mild symptoms to a fatal reaction.","rule":"Genetics: HLA-B∗5701 before abacavir; Trofile (CCR5) before maraviroc; resistance testing (genotype, tropism) guides regimens."},{"id":"17|trad|21","sourceId":"17|c|21","q":"Which skin finding should the nurse recognize as possible Kaposi sarcoma in a client with HIV?","o":["Raised purple, red or brown lesions, sometimes with a yellow halo","A bull’s-eye rash on the thigh","A scaly red rash across the cheeks and nose","Coin-shaped scaly lesions that scar"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Kaposi sarcoma is an AIDS-related vascular tumor that appears on the skin.","Incorrect. Erythema migrans of Lyme disease.","Incorrect. The malar rash of lupus.","Incorrect. Discoid lupus lesions."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Kaposi sarcoma is a low-grade vascular tumor that can involve skin, mucous membranes and internal organs when immunity is low.","rule":"Kaposi sarcoma: pink, red, purple or brown papular lesions, may have yellow halos; often shrinks with ART; document lesion number, size and location; keep open lesions clean and dressed."},{"id":"17|trad|26","sourceId":"17|c|26","q":"Which action is appropriate for a hospitalized client with stage III HIV and leukopenia?","o":["Keep a dedicated stethoscope, BP cuff and thermometer in the room","Use supplies from the shared supply room for convenience","Allow any visitor who wishes to come","Change gauze dressings once a week"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Dedicated equipment prevents cross-contamination.","Incorrect. Don’t use supplies from common areas.","Incorrect. Limit visitors to healthy adults.","Incorrect. Change gauze dressings daily."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Shared equipment carries organisms from patient to patient; with leukopenia, even ordinary organisms can cause serious infection.","rule":"Box 17.12: private room, limited staff, hand hygiene, healthy adult visitors only, daily cleaning, dedicated equipment and gloves, wrapped supplies, VS q4h, inspect mouth, skin and anal area every shift, IV sites q4h, daily gauze changes, cultures, strict asepsis."},{"id":"17|trad|29","sourceId":"17|c|29","q":"The sister of a client with HIV asks whether the family can keep sharing the bathroom and dishes. Which response by the nurse is best?","o":["“HIV isn’t spread by sharing a toilet or dishes, but don’t share razors or toothbrushes.”","“Use a separate bathroom and paper plates for your brother.”","“Bleach every dish after he uses it.”","“Avoid hugging him until his viral load is undetectable.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Casual household contact doesn’t transmit HIV; items that can carry blood aren’t shared.","Incorrect. Not needed; casual contact doesn’t spread HIV.","Incorrect. Hot, soapy water or a dishwasher is enough.","Incorrect. Hugging doesn’t transmit HIV."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"HIV transmission needs infected blood, semen, vaginal fluid or breast milk to reach the bloodstream or a mucous membrane; razors and toothbrushes can carry blood.","rule":"HIV spreads sexually, parenterally and perinatally — not by casual contact, sharing utensils, towels or toilets, or insects. Never share razors, toothbrushes or washcloths."},{"id":"17|ngn|20","sourceId":"17|n|20","q":"Review the telehealth note. What is the nurse’s best action?","o":["Tell the client to stop and report it to the provider at once — this may be a hypersensitivity reaction","Reassure the client that these are common early side effects that will pass","Tell the client to take an antihistamine and continue the drug","Tell the client to double the next dose to clear the virus faster"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fever, rash, muscle or joint aches and GI symptoms in the first 6 weeks of abacavir can mean a potentially fatal hypersensitivity reaction.","Incorrect. This combination must be reported immediately.","Incorrect. Continuing risks a fatal reaction.","Incorrect. Never double doses; this isn’t about the virus."],"ex":[{"h":"Telehealth note","lines":["Client with HIV started a regimen containing abacavir 3 weeks ago.","Reports fever of 101 °F, a new rash, aching muscles and joints, and nausea since yesterday.","HLA-B∗5701 test was reported negative before starting."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Some people’s immune systems react strongly to abacavir; genetic screening before the first dose lowers the risk but doesn’t remove it, so the warning signs must still be taught and acted on.","rule":"Abacavir: genetic screening before it is ever prescribed; teach to report fever, rash, myalgia, arthralgia and GI symptoms in the first 6 weeks immediately. Fostemsavir carries the same warning."},{"id":"17|ngn|23","sourceId":"17|n|23","q":"The nurse is teaching a client with stage III HIV how to prevent infection at home. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","o":["Take your temperature every day and report one over 100°F (37.8°C)","Don’t change the cat’s litter box","Don’t eat undercooked eggs, meat or fish","Avoid digging in the garden and caring for houseplants","Wear an N95 or KN95 mask in crowded places","A pet turtle is a good low-maintenance choice","Sharing a toothbrush with your partner is safe"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Report fever early — signs of infection may be muted.","Correct. Cat feces can carry Toxoplasma; use gloves if unavoidable.","Correct. Foodborne infections are dangerous with low immunity.","Correct. Soil and plants carry organisms such as fungi.","Correct. Avoid crowds and protect the airway.","Incorrect. Avoid turtles and reptiles (Salmonella).","Incorrect. Never share toothbrushes, razors or washcloths."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"With few working CD4+ cells, ordinary organisms in food, soil, pets and crowds cause opportunistic infections the body can’t contain.","rule":"Box 17.11: hand washing; avoid crowds (N95/KN95); no gardening or houseplants; daily temperature; report > 100°F (37.8°C), cough, pus, lesions, mouth ulcers, urinary changes; no shared personal items; no undercooked food; no litter boxes or reptiles."}],"studio":"hiv"},{"id":"lyme","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Lyme disease: exposure, recognition and prevention","notes":["17-lyme-disease"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Easiest to cure at the rash stage; Tick checks + report a rash or fever","Stage 1 — early localized: Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans); Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; Early treatment → better in ~20 days","Prevention and tick removal (Box 17.14): DEET, light colors, long sleeves, pants tucked in; Tweezers: a steady upward pull; No nail polish, jelly or heat; report a rash or fever"],"checks":["Explain why in Lyme disease.","Explain stage 1 — early localized in Lyme disease.","Explain prevention and tick removal (box 17.14) in Lyme disease."],"recallAnswers":["Easiest to cure at the rash stage; Tick checks + report a rash or fever","Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans); Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; Early treatment → better in ~20 days","DEET, light colors, long sleeves, pants tucked in; Tweezers: a steady upward pull; No nail polish, jelly or heat; report a rash or fever"],"requires":[],"terms":["erythema migrans","Lyme disease"],"questions":[{"id":"17|trad|22","sourceId":"17|c|22","q":"A client found a tick on the leg 10 days ago. Which finding suggests early Lyme disease?","o":["An expanding bull’s-eye rash with a low-grade fever","Arthritis of both knees","Transient heart block","Memory loss"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Erythema migrans marks stage 1.","Incorrect. Joint disease comes later.","Incorrect. Cardiac involvement comes later.","Incorrect. Cognitive deficits are late."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Borrelia burgdorferi spreads outward in the skin from the bite, producing the expanding ring; flulike symptoms reflect early infection.","rule":"Stage 1: bull’s-eye rash, low-grade fever, sometimes myalgia and headache; antibiotics (doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; azithromycin or clarithromycin if allergic to penicillin); most recover in about 20 days."},{"id":"17|ngn|26","sourceId":"17|n|26","q":"The nurse is teaching a group of hikers about Lyme disease. Which instructions should the nurse include? Select all that apply.","o":["Use DEET on your skin and clothes","Tuck your shirt into your pants and your pants into your socks","Remove a tick with tweezers, pulling upward with steady pressure","Clean the bite with alcohol or soap and water after removal","Report a rash or fever within several weeks of a bite","Coat the tick with nail polish so it lets go","Hold a hot match to the tick before removing it"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Insect repellent on skin and clothing.","Correct. It keeps ticks off the skin.","Correct. Steady upward pressure avoids breaking the tick.","Correct. Clean the area with an antiseptic.","Correct. Early treatment works best.","Incorrect. Don’t use nail polish, petroleum jelly or heat.","Incorrect. Heat isn’t used to make a tick detach."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The spirochete passes from the tick during feeding; keeping ticks off the skin and removing them intact and promptly lowers the chance of infection.","rule":"Box 17.14: avoid wooded, brushy areas; center of trails; DEET; light-colored, long clothing tucked in; bathe and check for ticks (arms, legs, scalp) and pets; tweezers, steady upward pull; dispose in alcohol, a sealed bag, tape or the toilet; report rash or fever."}],"studio":"lyme"},{"id":"transplant-immunity","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Transplantation: rejection and immunosuppression","notes":["17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Signs = failing organ + inflammation; Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent","Signs of rejection (Table 17.5): Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency; Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr; Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose","Teaching and safety: All → infection risk; Pregnancy: plan first; Hazardous-drug handling; stopping → rejection","Why#2: Balance: graft protection vs infection and toxicity; Grapefruit, missed doses tip it"],"checks":["Explain why in Transplantation.","Explain signs of rejection (table 17.5) in Transplantation.","Explain teaching and safety in Transplantation."],"recallAnswers":["Signs = failing organ + inflammation; Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent","Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency; Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr; Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose","All → infection risk; Pregnancy: plan first; Hazardous-drug handling; stopping → rejection"],"requires":[],"terms":["cyclosporine","rejection","immunosuppression"],"questions":[{"id":"17|trad|23","sourceId":"17|c|23","q":"A client taking cyclosporine after a kidney transplant drinks grapefruit juice every morning. What should the nurse teach?","o":["Avoid grapefruit juice — it can raise the drug level to toxic amounts","Grapefruit juice helps the drug work","Grapefruit juice is fine if taken 1 hour after the dose","Grapefruit juice prevents rejection"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It inhibits the drug’s metabolism and can raise the level 50–200%.","Incorrect. It raises the level unpredictably.","Incorrect. The interaction lasts much longer than an hour.","Incorrect. Not true."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Grapefruit blocks the enzymes that break down cyclosporine, everolimus and sirolimus, so blood levels rise → kidney and liver toxicity.","rule":"Table 17.6: cyclosporine — monitor for hypertension, liver and kidney labs, avoid grapefruit juice."},{"id":"17|trad|24","sourceId":"17|c|24","q":"Minutes after a transplanted kidney is connected to the recipient’s blood supply, it becomes dusky and stops working. What does the nurse expect?","o":["The organ will be removed because hyperacute rejection can’t be stopped","An increase in the immunosuppressant dose will save it","A biopsy in 1 week","The organ will recover on its own within days"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Preexisting antibodies clot the graft; the process is irreversible.","Incorrect. That can help acute rejection, not hyperacute.","Incorrect. There is no time — it is irreversible.","Incorrect. Necrosis has begun."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Antibodies already in the recipient bind the donor antigens in the graft’s vessels → complement → widespread clotting → ischemic necrosis.","rule":"Hyperacute rejection: minutes to hours; can’t be stopped; the organ is removed; now rare with careful HLA matching."}],"studio":"transplant-immunity"},{"id":"gi-tests","ch":"45","chapters":["45"],"title":"GI testing: preparation, bleeding and procedure complications","notes":["45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","45-overview","45-gi-changes-with-aging","45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","45-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 45; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules; FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep","Nutrition history: Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults; Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia; Unintended weight loss → GI cancer?; Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers","Priority problems: Aspiration · ↓ breathing (sedation); Bleeding/perforation; pancreatitis/sepsis (ERCP); Fluid + electrolyte loss (prep); Anxiety, embarrassment; teaching needs","What and why: Sees bleeding, ulcers, inflammation, tumors; biopsies (e.g., H. pylori); Informed consent every time"],"checks":["Explain why in GI testing.","Explain nutrition history in GI testing.","Explain priority problems in GI testing."],"recallAnswers":["Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules; FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep","Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults; Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia; Unintended weight loss → GI cancer?; Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers","Aspiration · ↓ breathing (sedation); Bleeding/perforation; pancreatitis/sepsis (ERCP); Fluid + electrolyte loss (prep); Anxiety, embarrassment; teaching needs"],"requires":[],"terms":["colonoscopy","ERCP","perforation"],"questions":[{"id":"45|trad|4","sourceId":"45|c|4","q":"A client is preparing for a guaiac fecal occult blood test (gFOBT). Which statement needs further teaching?","o":["“I’ll keep taking ibuprofen for my knees while I collect the samples.”","“I won’t eat red meat for 3 days before and during the test.”","“I’ll stop my high-dose vitamin C for a few days.”","“If it comes back positive, I’ll need a colonoscopy.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct — this needs teaching. NSAIDs must be stopped for 7 days before and during collection; they can cause small GI bleeds and a false-positive result.","Incorrect — this is right. Red meat contains hemoglobin that guaiac detects.","Incorrect — this is right. Vitamin C over 250 mg a day interferes with the test.","Incorrect — this is right. Any positive stool test is followed by a colonoscopy."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"NSAIDs block the prostaglandins that protect the GI lining → small erosions bleed → guaiac detects the blood → a false positive and an unneeded workup.","rule":"gFOBT: no NSAIDs 7 days, no red meat or vitamin C > 250 mg 3 days. FIT needs none of this."},{"id":"45|trad|6","sourceId":"45|c|6","q":"Which instruction should the nurse give a client scheduled for video capsule endoscopy?","o":["“Have nothing to eat or drink, not even water, for 12 hours before the test.”","“Clear liquids are fine until you swallow the capsule.”","“Save the capsule when it passes so we can download the pictures.”","“You’ll be sedated, so you’ll need a driver.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The small bowel must be empty for clear pictures: nothing, including water, for 12 hours before and for the first 2 hours of the test.","Incorrect. Even water is held for 12 hours before.","Incorrect. Images go to the recorder worn on a belt; the capsule is single use and flushed away.","Incorrect. No sedation is used; the client goes about normal (not vigorous) activity."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Food and fluid in the small bowel block the camera’s view → fasting empties it → the capsule then photographs the lining as peristalsis carries it along for about 8 hours.","rule":"Capsule: NPO (including water) 12 h before, clear liquids after 2 h, light lunch after 4 h; return the recorder; report a capsule not passed in 2 weeks."},{"id":"45|trad|10","sourceId":"45|c|10","q":"Several clients ask about using a mailed stool DNA (mt-sDNA) kit instead of a colonoscopy. For which clients should the nurse explain that a colonoscopy is preferred? Select all that apply.","o":["A 52-year-old whose brother had colon cancer at age 48","A 47-year-old being treated for ulcerative colitis","A 60-year-old whose FIT was positive last year","A 46-year-old with no symptoms or risk factors who dreads bowel prep","A 55-year-old with lactose intolerance","A 58-year-old whose colonoscopy 10 years ago was normal"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. A family history of colon cancer calls for a colonoscopy rather than a home test.","Correct. Inflammatory bowel disease (ulcerative colitis or Crohn’s) raises colon cancer risk, so colonoscopy is preferred.","Correct. After a previous positive screening test, a home test is not enough — the client needs a colonoscopy.","Incorrect. An average-risk adult from age 45 may choose a home test; it needs no bowel prep, which can encourage people who fear colonoscopy.","Incorrect. Lactose intolerance does not raise colon cancer risk.","Incorrect. A past normal colonoscopy is not a risk factor; an average-risk client who is due can choose any recommended option."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Home stool tests look for blood or tumor DNA shed into the stool → they can miss lesions and can’t remove polyps → in people more likely to have cancer, a miss matters more → colonoscopy sees the whole colon and treats what it finds.","rule":"Colonoscopy, not a home stool test, for a personal or family history of colon cancer, inflammatory bowel disease (Crohn’s or ulcerative colitis), or a previous positive screening test. Any positive home test → colonoscopy."},{"id":"45|trad|13","sourceId":"45|c|13","q":"A client with short-bowel syndrome has these results. Which one should the nurse report to the provider immediately?","o":["Total calcium 5.8 mg/dL","D-xylose 18 mg/dL at 60 minutes","Albumin 3.3 g/dL","Potassium 3.3 mEq/L"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Calcium below 6 mg/dL is a critical value (critical 13 mg/dL). Calcium is absorbed in the gut, so malabsorption can drive it this low.","Incorrect. A low D-xylose (normal 20–57 mg/dL at 60 minutes) is expected with short-bowel syndrome; it confirms malabsorption but is not an emergency.","Incorrect. Albumin is slightly low (normal 3.5–5.0 g/dL), as expected with undernutrition; it needs attention but is not critical.","Incorrect. Potassium is slightly low (normal 3.5–5.0 mEq/L) but above the critical value of 3 mEq/L."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A shortened small intestine has less surface to absorb → calcium, protein and sugars such as D-xylose are poorly absorbed → blood levels fall → a very low calcium makes nerves and muscles overexcitable, so it is reported at once.","rule":"Critical values in Table 45.2: calcium 13 mg/dL, potassium 6.1 mEq/L, total bilirubin > 12 mg/dL. Low D-xylose and low albumin are expected with malabsorption but are not critical."}],"studio":"gi-tests"},{"id":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma","ch":"46","chapters":["46"],"title":"Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma","notes":["46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","46-esophageal-trauma","46-overview","46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","46-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 46; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing; Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks; So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it","Heart and fluids: Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD); A-fib from vagus irritation","Oral care: q2h; ultrasoft \"chemobrush\" (low platelets); No foam swabs, no alcohol mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin; Water-based lubricant; saline/baking soda rinses","Why: Stomach pulled into the chest → suture line beside lungs, heart, mediastinum; Leak spills saliva + gastric juice into the chest; So nothing by mouth until barium swallow proves it sealed; fever + tachycardia = leak until proven otherwise"],"checks":["Explain why in Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma.","Explain heart and fluids in Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma.","Explain oral care in Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma."],"recallAnswers":["Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing; Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks; So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it","Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD); A-fib from vagus irritation","q2h; ultrasoft \"chemobrush\" (low platelets); No foam swabs, no alcohol mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin; Water-based lubricant; saline/baking soda rinses"],"requires":[],"terms":["esophagectomy","jejunostomy","perforation"],"questions":[{"id":"46|trad|1","sourceId":"46|c|1","q":"A client had a partial glossectomy and neck dissection. Which finding requires the nurse’s immediate action?","o":["High-pitched noise when breathing in (stridor)","Pain of 5 on a 0–10 scale","Thick saliva in the mouth","Anxiety about speaking"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Stridor means the airway is partly blocked by swelling — the priority.","Incorrect. Treat it, but airway comes first.","Incorrect. Suction and fluids help; stridor is the emergency.","Incorrect. Important to address, but not first."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Surgery on the tongue and neck → tissue swelling narrows the upper airway → air rushes through a narrowed passage → high-pitched stridor → complete obstruction can follow.","rule":"After oral cancer surgery: airway first — semi- or high-Fowler, suction at the bedside, report stridor at once."},{"id":"46|trad|8","sourceId":"46|c|8","q":"A client has jejunostomy (J-tube) feedings after an esophagectomy. Which action is correct?","o":["Give the feeding without aspirating for residual volume","Check residual volume every 4 hours by aspirating","Advance the feeding to full strength on day 1","Give the feedings only when the client is flat"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Aspirating a jejunostomy tube can tear the mucosa; the small bowel doesn’t hold a residual like the stomach.","Incorrect. Don’t aspirate for residual.","Incorrect. Feeds start on day 2 and increase slowly through day 5.","Incorrect. Keep the head of the bed at least 30°."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The jejunum moves feeding along continuously → there is no pool to measure → suction pulls the thin wall into the tube tip → mucosal tear.","rule":"Esophagectomy: J-tube feeds from day 2, increase slowly, no residual checks; barium swallow day 7 before oral intake."},{"id":"46|trad|9","sourceId":"46|c|9","q":"A client is brought to the ED after swallowing a strong alkaline drain cleaner. What is the nurse’s priority?","o":["Assess the airway and breathing","Give milk to neutralize the alkali","Insert an NG tube and lavage","Obtain a detailed history of the event"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Burns of the mouth and throat swell fast and can obstruct the airway.","Incorrect. Neutralizing creates heat and more injury; the client is kept NPO.","Incorrect. Blind tubes risk perforating a burned esophagus.","Incorrect. Important, but after the airway."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Alkalis dissolve proteins and fats (liquefaction) → they keep burning downward → swelling of the mouth and throat narrows the airway, and the esophagus can perforate within a minute.","rule":"Caustic ingestion: airway first, NPO, suction, esophageal rest, antibiotics, steroids, pain control; alkalis are worse than acids."},{"id":"46|trad|11","sourceId":"46|c|11","q":"A client has been vomiting forcefully for 2 days with gastroenteritis and now reports sudden severe chest pain and pain on swallowing. HR 118, RR 26, T 100.9°F (38.3°C). Which action should the nurse take?","o":["Keep the client NPO and report the findings to the provider immediately","Give a liquid antacid for the chest pain","Offer small sips of water to see how well the client swallows","Have the client lie flat and rest, then recheck vital signs in an hour"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Continuous severe vomiting can tear the esophagus; acid and organisms leaking into the mediastinum cause shock, respiratory failure or sepsis. Nothing by mouth, and the provider needs to know now (a chest CT looks for mediastinal air).","Incorrect. Anything swallowed can leak through a tear, and this is not simple reflux pain.","Incorrect. Testing the swallow sends water through a possible perforation.","Incorrect. Fever, tachycardia and tachypnea with new chest pain may be early sepsis; waiting an hour delays rescue."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Repeated violent vomiting raises pressure inside the esophagus → the wall tears → gastric acid, saliva and bacteria enter the mediastinum → inflammation and infection (mediastinitis) → fever, tachycardia, tachypnea, then shock and sepsis.","rule":"Suspected esophageal perforation (severe vomiting, seizures, straining, instruments or tubes, foreign bodies): airway and breathing first, keep NPO, report at once; chest CT for mediastinal air; then suction, esophageal rest, TPN and antibiotics."},{"id":"46|trad|15","sourceId":"46|c|15","q":"A client recovering from swallowing a strong alkali is receiving high-dose corticosteroids. The client asks why. Which response by the nurse is best?","o":["“They calm the inflammation and help keep scar tissue from narrowing your esophagus.”","“They neutralize any alkali left in your esophagus.”","“They are your main protection against infection.”","“They raise your appetite so you can eat again sooner.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. High-dose steroids reduce inflammation and help prevent strictures as the burn heals.","Incorrect. Nothing is given to neutralize the chemical; the damage happens within minutes.","Incorrect. Broad-spectrum antibiotics treat or prevent infection; steroids raise the infection risk.","Incorrect. The esophagus is rested (NPO, suction, TPN) for more than a week; appetite is not the goal."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Alkali liquefies the esophageal wall → intense inflammation → fibroblasts lay down collagen as it heals → the scar contracts into a stricture; steroids blunt the inflammation that drives the scarring.","rule":"Caustic injury care: airway first; NPO, suction and esophageal rest > 1 week, TPN, broad-spectrum antibiotics, high-dose steroids (inflammation, strictures), analgesics, viscous lidocaine for mouth burns."}],"studio":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma"},{"id":"adrenal-assessment","ch":"53","chapters":["53"],"title":"Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment","notes":["53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","53-overview","53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","53-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 53; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺; Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing","Family history and current symptoms: Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid; Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice; Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido","Safety and comfort: No palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland; Orthostatic precautions; Older adults: fluids q2h awake, layers, skin + perineal care","Why#2: Stress on the body = fast heart, wide pupils, sweating, slow gut, glucose ↑ (liver releases sugar); Why sick or stressed people with diabetes run high; why beta blockers calm thyroid tachycardia (ch 55)"],"checks":["Explain why in Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment.","Explain family history and current symptoms in Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment.","Explain safety and comfort in Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment."],"recallAnswers":["Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺; Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing","Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid; Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice; Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido","No palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland; Orthostatic precautions; Older adults: fluids q2h awake, layers, skin + perineal care"],"requires":[],"terms":["aldosterone","cortisol","catecholamine"],"questions":[{"id":"53|trad|2","sourceId":"53|c|2","q":"What does aldosterone do in the kidney?","o":["Reabsorbs sodium and water and excretes potassium","Excretes sodium and keeps potassium","Reabsorbs calcium","Excretes glucose"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Too much → fluid retention and low potassium; too little → volume loss and high potassium.","Incorrect. The opposite.","Incorrect. That is PTH.","Incorrect. Not an aldosterone effect."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Low volume → renin → angiotensin I → angiotensin II → aldosterone → sodium and water in, potassium out → volume restored; a rise in potassium of 0.1 mEq/L also triggers it.","rule":"Aldosterone = sodium in, water in, potassium out."},{"id":"53|trad|8","sourceId":"53|c|8","q":"Which effect results from epinephrine acting on beta-1 receptors?","o":["Faster heart rate and stronger contraction","Pupil dilation","Vasoconstriction of skin vessels","Sweating"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Beta-1 is the heart receptor.","Incorrect. Alpha.","Incorrect. Alpha.","Incorrect. Alpha (sympathetic cholinergic in practice)."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Epinephrine (85% of medullary output) acts mainly on beta receptors → beta-1 in the heart raises rate and contractility; norepinephrine (15%) acts mainly on alpha → vasoconstriction.","rule":"Alpha: constrict, pupils dilate, sphincters tighten. Beta-1: heart. Beta-2: bronchodilation, vessel dilation. This is why a beta-blocker calms thyroid tachycardia."},{"id":"53|trad|9","sourceId":"53|c|9","q":"Why does the time of day matter when cortisol is drawn?","o":["Cortisol peaks in the morning and is lowest about 12 hours later","Cortisol is constant all day","Cortisol is highest at midnight","Food has no effect on any hormone"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A “normal” value can be abnormal at the wrong time of day.","Incorrect. It has a daily rhythm.","Incorrect. It is lowest around midnight.","Incorrect. Many tests have food and timing rules."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"CRH and ACTH follow the sleep-wake cycle → cortisol rises before waking, peaks in the morning and falls through the day → stress raises it at any time.","rule":"Read a hormone level against its timing and its driver; draw at the ordered time."},{"id":"53|trad|12","sourceId":"53|c|12","q":"The nurse is performing a routine physical assessment on a 36-year-old client. Which finding needs further assessment for an endocrine problem?","o":["A visible fullness at the front of the lower neck while the client sits quietly","The thyroid isthmus briefly rises when the client swallows","A few gray hairs at the temples","A 6-lb (2.7-kg) weight loss over 4 months after the client began eating smaller portions"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The thyroid normally cannot be seen at rest; a visible gland suggests enlargement. Assess further (history, listen for a bruit) and do not press on it if hyperthyroidism is possible.","Incorrect. The isthmus may be seen moving upward on swallowing — a normal finding.","Incorrect. Graying hair is a normal age change.","Incorrect. A planned diet change explains this; the clue is a rapid weight change with no change in diet."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A normal thyroid lies flat under the neck muscles and is glimpsed only as it rises with a swallow → an enlarged gland bulges forward and becomes visible at rest → enlargement can come with too much or too little hormone, so it needs a fuller work-up.","rule":"A thyroid visible at rest needs further assessment: history, auscultation for a bruit, and no pressure if hyperthyroidism is possible. The isthmus rising on a swallow, graying hair and weight change explained by diet are expected."},{"id":"53|trad|13","sourceId":"53|c|13","q":"A healthy 30-year-old client eats about 10 g of sodium a day and asks whether that makes the blood sodium level high. The nurse explains that the endocrine system keeps serum sodium in which range?","o":["136–145 mEq/L (mmol/L)","125–135 mEq/L (mmol/L)","146–155 mEq/L (mmol/L)","It rises and falls in step with intake"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. In a healthy adult, hormonal control holds serum sodium at 136–145 mEq/L whether intake is 2 g or 12 g a day.","Incorrect. Below 136 mEq/L is low (hyponatremia).","Incorrect. Above 145 mEq/L is high (hypernatremia).","Incorrect. The kidneys, under hormonal control, change how much sodium and water they keep, so the serum level stays in range across a wide intake."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Sodium intake changes → hormones such as aldosterone (sodium kept) and ADH (water kept) adjust what the kidney reabsorbs or excretes → serum sodium stays at 136–145 mEq/L while volume and urine sodium change.","rule":"Normal serum sodium is 136–145 mEq/L (mmol/L). In a healthy adult, endocrine control holds it there whether intake is 2 g or 12 g a day, so a value outside the range points to a problem with control, not just diet."}],"studio":"adrenal-assessment"},{"id":"bowel-obstruction","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Bowel obstruction: fluid loss, decompression and ischemia","notes":["48-overview","48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","48-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why obstruction causes hypovolemia: Contents pile up above the block; ↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention; Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen; → hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury","Temperature and vital signs: Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F; > 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia; Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change","Priority problem: Life-threatening complications from blocked flow","Why: Colicky pain = bowel fighting the block; Constant + localized (or stops → tenderness) = ischemia or perforation; So analgesics may be held during workup — the pain pattern is the diagnosis"],"checks":["Explain why obstruction causes hypovolemia in Bowel obstruction.","Explain temperature and vital signs in Bowel obstruction.","Explain priority problem in Bowel obstruction."],"recallAnswers":["Contents pile up above the block; ↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention; Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen; → hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury","Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F; > 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia; Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change","Life-threatening complications from blocked flow"],"requires":[],"terms":["ileus","ischemia","nasogastric"],"questions":[{"id":"48|trad|4","sourceId":"48|c|4","q":"Which measure is most reliable for tracking fluid balance in a client with a bowel obstruction on NG suction?","o":["Daily weight","Skin turgor","Thirst","Number of emeses"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fluid trapped in the bowel and peritoneum can’t be seen or measured; weight captures the real change.","Incorrect. Unreliable, especially in older adults.","Incorrect. Some older adults don’t feel thirst.","Incorrect. Much of the loss is hidden in the bowel lumen."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A blocked bowel traps fluid in its lumen and leaks plasma into the peritoneum → circulating volume falls without visible loss → body weight still changes with total water.","rule":"Obstruction: daily weight is the best fluid measure; also I&O, VS, urine output, electrolytes."},{"id":"48|trad|10","sourceId":"48|c|10","q":"Why might the provider hold opioids briefly while a suspected bowel obstruction is being worked up?","o":["A change in the pain pattern can show strangulation or perforation","Opioids cause the obstruction","The client is not really in pain","Opioids interfere with CT scans"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Colicky pain turning constant — or stopping and becoming tenderness — is the diagnosis; opioids can mask it (and morphine slows motility).","Incorrect. They slow motility but don’t cause mechanical obstruction.","Incorrect. The pain is real; explain why and use non-drug comfort.","Incorrect. Not a CT issue."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Colic = the bowel fighting a block → constant or localized pain = the wall losing blood supply → sudden relief with tenderness = perforation → masking these hides the moment surgery is needed.","rule":"Report pain that becomes constant or localized, or stops suddenly; explain why analgesia may be limited; comfort with positioning (semi-Fowler)."},{"id":"48|trad|17","sourceId":"48|c|17","q":"An 84-year-old resident of a long-term care facility who takes an opioid for arthritis has had no bowel movement for 6 days. The lower abdomen is distended, cramping comes and goes, and there has been little vomiting. Which cause of this large-bowel obstruction should the nurse suspect first?","o":["Fecal impaction","Volvulus","Adhesions from surgery","Intussusception"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Older adults are the group most likely to have a bowel obstruction, usually from fecal impaction or a tumor; slower peristalsis, opioids and constipation set it up. A low impaction usually resolves with disimpaction and enemas.","Incorrect. Twisting of the bowel is a possible mechanical cause but much less likely than impaction in this client.","Incorrect. Adhesions follow abdominal surgery, and none is described.","Incorrect. Telescoping of the bowel has nothing in this history to suggest it."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Aging slows peristalsis → opioids slow it further → stool stays in the colon and dries → a hard mass forms (impaction) → stool and gas back up behind it → lower distention and cramping.","rule":"Older adults: obstruction is most often fecal impaction or cancer. Prevent impaction with fiber, water, daily walks, warm drinks or prune juice, bulk formers and softeners — not routine laxatives (atonic colon)."},{"id":"48|ngn|2","sourceId":"48|n|2","q":"Review the flow sheet for a client with a small-bowel obstruction from adhesions, managed with NG suction. What should the nurse do?","o":["Report the findings to the provider immediately","Give the PRN morphine and reassess in an hour","Irrigate the NG tube with 30 mL saline","Document — these changes are expected on day 2"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fever over 100°F with a rising pulse and pain that turned from colicky to constant and localized = strangulation, ischemia or peritonitis — a surgical emergency.","Incorrect. Pain relief matters, but the change in pain is the diagnosis and must reach the provider first; morphine also slows motility.","Incorrect. A plugged tube doesn’t explain fever, tachycardia and constant localized pain.","Incorrect. Uncomplicated obstruction rarely raises temperature above 100°F."],"ex":[{"h":"Flow sheet","table":[["","Day 1, 08:00","Day 2, 08:00","Day 2, 14:00"],["Temperature","99.0°F","99.8°F","101.4°F"],["Heart rate","92","104","122"],["Pain","Cramping, comes and goes, 6/10","Cramping 6/10","Constant, right side, 9/10"],["Abdomen","Distended, soft","Distended","Tender, guarding"]]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A loop of blocked bowel twists or swells until its blood supply fails → the wall becomes ischemic → it stops contracting (colic ends) and becomes constantly painful → bacteria and endotoxin leak → fever, tachycardia, peritonitis, septic shock.","rule":"Obstruction: temperature > 100°F with a rising pulse, or pain that becomes constant or localized = strangulation until proven otherwise → report now."},{"id":"48|ngn|3","sourceId":"48|n|3","q":"A client with a bowel obstruction has a Salem sump NG tube to low continuous suction. Which actions are correct? Select all that apply.","o":["Check placement, patency and output at least every 4 hours","Turn the suction off to listen for bowel sounds","Irrigate with 30 mL normal saline as ordered","Give frequent mouth and nares care","Listen for bowel sounds with the suction running","Clamp the blue vent (“pigtail”) to stop it leaking","Report increasing nausea and distention with less drainage"],"a":[0,1,2,3,6],"r":["Correct. The tube only helps if it is in the stomach and draining.","Correct. Suction gurgles — the nurse would “hear” peristalsis that isn’t there.","Correct. Saline irrigation clears a plugged tube when ordered.","Correct. NPO and a tube in the nose dry and irritate the mouth and nares.","Incorrect. Suction noise masks or mimics bowel sounds.","Incorrect. The vent lets air in so the stomach lining isn’t pulled into the tube; it should stay open.","Correct. Less output with more nausea and distention means the tube is out of position or plugged."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The NG tube removes the fluid and air piling up above the block → the bowel decompresses → less pressure on the wall, less vomiting, less fluid leaking out of the circulation.","rule":"NG for obstruction: low continuous suction, vent open, check every 4 h, listen with suction off, irrigate as ordered, report less output with more distention."}],"studio":"bowel-obstruction"},{"id":"colorectal-ostomy","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Colorectal cancer and colostomy: treatment and adapting care","notes":["48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia; Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood","Signs: Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency; Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced; Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red; Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids","Inherited syndromes: FAP (APC): thousands of polyps, ~100% cancer → colectomy by 20; Lynch/HNPCC: 80% risk by ~45; endometrial, ovarian, others; Teach genetic testing for families","Why: Stoma has no pain nerves → can't feel a tight wafer → measure + cut slightly larger; Color = its blood supply → dark/dry = bowel dying → report now"],"checks":["Explain why in Colorectal cancer and colostomy.","Explain signs in Colorectal cancer and colostomy.","Explain inherited syndromes in Colorectal cancer and colostomy."],"recallAnswers":["Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia; Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood","Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency; Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced; Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red; Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids","FAP (APC): thousands of polyps, ~100% cancer → colectomy by 20; Lynch/HNPCC: 80% risk by ~45; endometrial, ovarian, others; Teach genetic testing for families"],"requires":[],"terms":["colostomy","adenocarcinoma","stoma"],"questions":[{"id":"48|trad|6","sourceId":"48|c|6","q":"Which comfort measure is appropriate after an abdominoperineal resection?","o":["Sit on a foam pad or soft pillow, not an air ring","Sit on a rubber donut cushion","Sit upright for long periods to promote drainage","Avoid sitz baths until the wound is closed"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Foam or a soft pillow spreads pressure; a donut stretches the perineum and reduces blood flow to the wound.","Incorrect. Never use an air ring or donut.","Incorrect. Avoid long sitting; side-lying in bed.","Incorrect. Sitz baths 3–4 times a day ease pain and promote healing."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A donut lets the perineum sag through the center → wound edges stretch apart and blood flow falls → slower healing and more pain.","rule":"AP resection: sitz baths, absorbent pad, side-lying, foam cushion (never a donut), I&O with drain output, watch for infection; healing takes 6–8 months."},{"id":"48|trad|7","sourceId":"48|c|7","q":"What stool consistency should the nurse expect from a descending colostomy once it is working?","o":["Formed","Liquid","Pasty","Clear mucus only"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. By the descending colon most water has been absorbed.","Incorrect. Liquid comes from an ascending colostomy or ileostomy.","Incorrect. Pasty comes from a transverse colostomy.","Incorrect. Mucus alone comes from a distal mucous fistula."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The colon absorbs water along its length → ascending output is liquid, transverse pasty, descending formed.","rule":"Colostomy output: ascending liquid, transverse pasty, descending/sigmoid formed. Starts working in 2–3 days with lots of gas."},{"id":"48|trad|11","sourceId":"48|c|11","q":"A client with a colostomy reports a strong odor all day. What should the nurse check first?","o":["Whether the pouch is leaking or poorly closed","Whether the client is eating enough fiber","Whether the stoma is infected","Whether the client needs a larger pouch"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Odor usually means a leak or a poorly closed pouch.","Incorrect. Diet affects gas, but constant odor points to a leak.","Incorrect. Not the first thought.","Incorrect. Fit matters, but check for a leak first."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Modern pouches are odor-proof when sealed → odor escapes only through a broken seal or open end → a leak also exposes skin to stool.","rule":"Odor: check the seal and closure first; filters and deodorizers; avoid gas-producing foods; never aspirin in the pouch."},{"id":"48|trad|12","sourceId":"48|c|12","q":"Which finding should the nurse report as a possible sign of colorectal cancer?","o":["A change in stool shape with blood in the stool","One hard stool after a long trip","Mild gas after eating beans","Stool that floats occasionally"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Rectal bleeding, anemia and a change in stool consistency or shape are the most common signs.","Incorrect. A one-time change with a clear cause isn’t concerning.","Incorrect. Expected.","Incorrect. Not a cancer sign on its own."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A tumor bleeds from its fragile surface and narrows the lumen → blood in stool, narrow or changed stool, and over time anemia, fatigue and weight loss.","rule":"CRC signs: rectal bleeding, anemia, change in bowel habits or stool shape; fatigue, fullness and weight loss suggest advanced disease."},{"id":"48|trad|15","sourceId":"48|c|15","q":"Three days after an abdominoperineal (AP) resection, a client has a closed perineal wound with two Jackson-Pratt drains. Which finding should the nurse report first?","o":["Temperature 101.6°F with cloudy, foul-smelling drainage from the perineal wound","Serosanguineous drainage in the drains","A feeling of needing to pass stool through the rectum","Perineal pain that increases with sitting"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The perineal wound is at high risk for infection and abscess — fever with cloudy, foul drainage is the priority to report.","Incorrect. Serosanguineous drainage is expected and may continue for 1–2 months.","Incorrect. Phantom rectal sensations are common because the sympathetic nerves for rectal control were not cut.","Incorrect. Expected — the perineal wound often hurts more than the abdominal incision; side-lying, a foam cushion and sitz baths help."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Removing the rectum and anus leaves a perineal wound and cavity near a heavily colonized area → drainage that collects lets bacteria multiply → infection or abscess → fever and cloudy, foul drainage.","rule":"After AP resection the priority problem for the perineal wound is infection. Expected: serosanguineous drainage for 1–2 months, phantom rectal sensations, more pain than the abdominal incision. Report fever, foul or purulent drainage, redness, swelling or constant pain."},{"id":"48|trad|16","sourceId":"48|c|16","q":"A client with a right-sided colon tumor now has an ascending colostomy. Which statement shows that the client understands the teaching?","o":["“My stool will stay liquid, so I’ll keep my pouch on all the time.”","“I’ll irrigate my colostomy each morning so I can go without a pouch.”","“I’ll wait until the pouch is completely full before I empty it.”","“My stoma will get bigger over the next 6 weeks, so I’ll cut the opening larger.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Stool from the ascending colon stays liquid and drains often, so the pouch is worn at all times.","Incorrect. Irrigation can regulate a sigmoid colostomy, which passes formed stool — not an ascending one.","Incorrect. Empty it at one-third to one-half full; a heavy pouch pulls on the seal and leaks.","Incorrect. The stoma shrinks over 6–8 weeks; measure it weekly and cut the opening ⅛–1/16 inch larger than the stoma."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The colon absorbs water along its length → an ascending stoma diverts stool before most water is absorbed → output is liquid and frequent → a pouch must always be on; a sigmoid stoma passes formed stool that irrigation can regulate.","rule":"Colostomy site follows the tumor (Fig. 48.4): ascending (right), descending (left), sigmoid (rectal), transverse double-barrel in emergencies. Ascending = liquid, pouch always on; empty at ⅓–½ full; stoma shrinks 6–8 weeks; alcohol-free sealant before the wafer."},{"id":"48|ngn|6","sourceId":"48|n|6","q":"Which instructions should the nurse include when teaching a client to care for a new colostomy? Select all that apply.","o":["Empty the pouch when it is one-third to one-half full","Measure the stoma weekly and cut the opening ⅛–1/16 inch larger","Clean the skin with mild soap and water, not moisturizing soap","Report a stoma that turns dark red, purple or black","Put an aspirin in the pouch to control odor","Cut the wafer opening exactly the size of the stoma","A healthy stoma is pale pink and dry"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. A heavier pouch pulls on the seal and leaks.","Correct. The stoma shrinks for 6–8 weeks; a slightly larger opening protects it without exposing much skin.","Correct. Moisturizing soap leaves a film that stops the wafer from sticking.","Correct. Dark, purple, black or dry means the stoma is losing its blood supply.","Incorrect. Aspirin can ulcerate the stoma. Odor usually means a leak — use filters or deodorizers.","Incorrect. A tight opening cuts the stoma, which has no pain nerves to warn the client.","Incorrect. A healthy stoma is reddish pink to dark red-pink and moist."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A stoma is bowel mucosa turned out onto the skin → it has blood supply but no pain nerves → its color shows perfusion, and only a correct fit protects it from cuts and the skin from stool.","rule":"Ostomy: empty at ⅓–½ full; measure weekly, cut ⅛–1/16 inch larger; mild soap, alcohol-free sealant; report dark, dry, bleeding or separating stoma; never aspirin in the pouch."}],"studio":"colorectal-ostomy"},{"id":"ibs","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"IBS: symptom pattern and safe treatment","notes":["48-irritable-bowel-syndrome"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon); 10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2×; IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M","Assess: Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well; Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary; LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones","Self-management: 30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses water, regular meals, chew slowly; Exercise; stress reduction; Probiotics, peppermint oil","Why: Fiber (psyllium) holds water → softens hard stool and firms loose stool; Same advice for both: fiber + water"],"checks":["Explain what it is in IBS.","Explain assess in IBS.","Explain self-management in IBS."],"recallAnswers":["Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon); 10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2×; IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M","Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well; Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary; LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones","30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses water, regular meals, chew slowly; Exercise; stress reduction; Probiotics, peppermint oil"],"requires":[],"terms":["irritable bowel syndrome","linaclotide","alosetron"],"questions":[{"id":"48|trad|9","sourceId":"48|c|9","q":"A client with IBS asks why fiber is recommended for both diarrhea and constipation. Which response is accurate?","o":["Soluble fiber holds water — it softens hard stool and firms loose stool","Fiber speeds the bowel in everyone","Fiber only helps constipation; it was recommended by mistake","Fiber kills the bacteria that cause IBS"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Psyllium absorbs water into a gel, pulling stool toward a soft, bulky middle.","Incorrect. It normalizes rather than speeds.","Incorrect. It helps both types.","Incorrect. Fiber isn’t antibacterial."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Soluble fiber forms a gel that holds water → in constipation it adds water and bulk → in diarrhea it binds excess water → stool moves toward normal form.","rule":"IBS: 30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses of water, regular meals, exercise, stress care; drugs by type."},{"id":"48|trad|13","sourceId":"48|c|13","q":"A 29-year-old woman with constipation-predominant irritable bowel syndrome (IBS-C) is starting linaclotide. Which instruction should the nurse give?","o":["Take it once a day about 30 minutes before breakfast","Take it with your largest meal to prevent nausea","Take it only on days you have not had a bowel movement","Take it at bedtime with an antihistamine to help you sleep"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Linaclotide is taken once daily about 30 minutes before breakfast. It draws fluid into the intestine, speeds transit and eases pain and cramping.","Incorrect. Taking the drug with food and water is the teaching for lubiprostone, another IBS-C drug — not linaclotide.","Incorrect. It is a scheduled daily drug, not a rescue laxative.","Incorrect. There is no reason to pair it with an antihistamine; the warning about antihistamines and psychoactive drugs belongs to alosetron."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Linaclotide stimulates guanylate cyclase receptors on the intestinal lining → the cells secrete more fluid into the lumen → stool softens and moves faster → pain and cramping ease.","rule":"IBS-C: linaclotide once daily 30 min before breakfast; lubiprostone (women) with food and water; psyllium at meals with a full glass of water."},{"id":"48|ngn|9","sourceId":"48|n|9","q":"Review the phone note. What should the nurse tell the client?","o":["Stop the alosetron and be seen right away","Take the next dose with food","Double the fiber and keep taking the drug","Bloody diarrhea is expected for the first month"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Worsening pain and bloody diarrhea on alosetron can mean ischemic colitis — stop the drug and report at once.","Incorrect. Food doesn’t prevent ischemic colitis.","Incorrect. Fiber doesn’t treat ischemia.","Incorrect. Bleeding is never expected."],"ex":[{"h":"Phone note","lines":["32-year-old woman with IBS-D started alosetron 3 weeks ago.","“Since yesterday I’ve had cramping that keeps getting worse and some blood with diarrhea. Should I take another dose?”"]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Alosetron blocks 5-HT₃ receptors and slows the gut → in some women blood flow to the colon wall falls → ischemic colitis: pain and bloody diarrhea; severe constipation is also a warning.","rule":"Alosetron (women, IBS-D, last resort): stop and report severe constipation, worsening pain, bloody diarrhea or rectal bleeding."}],"studio":"ibs"},{"id":"trauma-hernia","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids: recognize danger","notes":["48-abdominal-trauma","48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Mechanisms: Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot); Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder","Prevention and signs: Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining; Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching","Diagnose and treat: FAST → free fluid · CT later; Unstable → laparotomy; Stable → ICU: VS, Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics (hollow viscus)","Why: Blood under the diaphragm irritates the phrenic nerve (C3–C5, same as shoulder); Brain reads it as left shoulder pain → think spleen"],"checks":["Explain mechanisms in Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids.","Explain prevention and signs in Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids.","Explain diagnose and treat in Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids."],"recallAnswers":["Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot); Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder","Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining; Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching","FAST → free fluid · CT later; Unstable → laparotomy; Stable → ICU: VS, Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics (hollow viscus)"],"requires":[],"terms":["strangulation","hemorrhoid","hemorrhage"],"questions":[{"id":"48|trad|1","sourceId":"48|c|1","q":"A client has an inguinal hernia that could be pushed back yesterday but cannot be reduced today. What should the nurse do?","o":["Arrange immediate surgical evaluation and don’t push on it","Apply firm pressure to push it back","Apply a truss over the bulge","Teach the client to lie flat until it goes back"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. An irreducible (incarcerated) hernia needs immediate surgical evaluation — it can strangulate.","Incorrect. Never force a hernia back; strangulated bowel could rupture.","Incorrect. A truss goes on only after a hernia is reduced.","Incorrect. Waiting delays care for possible strangulation."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bowel trapped in the hernial ring swells → the ring squeezes its blood supply → ischemia (strangulation) → necrosis, obstruction, perforation, sepsis.","rule":"Reducible: may be managed with a truss. Incarcerated: immediate surgical evaluation. Strangulated (pain, fever, tachycardia, distention, vomiting): emergency."},{"id":"48|trad|2","sourceId":"48|c|2","q":"A trauma client with no shoulder injury reports left shoulder pain. The nurse suspects which problem?","o":["Ruptured spleen with blood irritating the diaphragm","Rotator cuff tear","Myocardial infarction","Pneumonia"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Kehr sign: blood under the diaphragm irritates the phrenic nerve, and pain is referred to the left shoulder.","Incorrect. There is no shoulder injury.","Incorrect. Possible in other settings, but in abdominal trauma think spleen.","Incorrect. Not consistent with acute trauma."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Blood collects under the left diaphragm → irritates the phrenic nerve (C3–C5) → the brain maps the signal to the shoulder, which uses the same spinal levels → referred left shoulder pain.","rule":"Kehr sign = possible splenic rupture or diaphragm irritation. Cullen = periumbilical bruising. Turner = flank bruising (retroperitoneal bleeding)."},{"id":"48|trad|14","sourceId":"48|c|14","q":"A client with a liver laceration from blunt abdominal trauma is being managed without surgery in the ICU. Which finding should the nurse report to the surgeon immediately?","o":["Hemoglobin 13.2 → 10.1 g/dL over 4 hours, with heart rate 88 → 118","Abdominal pain steady at 4/10 since admission","Hypoactive bowel sounds","Urine output 50 mL/hr"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A falling hemoglobin with a rising heart rate means continued bleeding — deterioration under observation can mean the client needs a laparotomy.","Incorrect. Pain that holds steady is reassuring; worsening pain is what signals bleeding, perforation or abdominal compartment syndrome.","Incorrect. Bowel sounds are often decreased after abdominal trauma (bleeding, organ injury, ileus); on its own this isn’t the urgent finding.","Incorrect. Adequate output — above 30 mL/hr."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A torn liver keeps bleeding into the abdomen → red cell mass falls (Hgb, Hct) → the heart speeds up to hold cardiac output → without control, hypovolemic shock follows → surgery stops the bleeding.","rule":"Abdominal trauma under ICU observation: frequent VS, pain and Hgb/Hct; transfuse for active bleeding; a falling Hgb, rising pulse or worsening pain → report → possible laparotomy. Stable for 24–48 h → transfer."},{"id":"48|trad|18","sourceId":"48|c|18","q":"Four hours after a minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair, the nurse reviews discharge teaching. Which statement by the client needs follow-up?","o":["“I’ll drive myself to work tomorrow while I’m taking the oxycodone.”","“Some soreness is normal, but I’ll call if the pain gets much worse.”","“I’ll take the stool softener so I don’t strain.”","“I won’t lift anything heavier than 10 pounds until the surgeon says it’s OK.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct — needs follow-up. Clients taking oral opioids must not drive or operate heavy machinery, and return to work is usually in 1–2 weeks, when the surgeon allows.","Incorrect. Accurate: soreness and discomfort are expected; severe or increasing pain is reported.","Incorrect. Accurate: a stool softener prevents constipation and straining on the repair.","Incorrect. Accurate: no lifting over 10 lb (4.5 kg) until cleared."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Opioids depress the central nervous system → drowsiness and slower reactions → driving or using machinery is unsafe; straining and lifting raise intraabdominal pressure on the fresh repair.","rule":"After MIIHR: home in 3–5 h with an adult; no driving on opioids; soreness expected, severe pain reported; stool softener; ≤ 10 lb lifting; deep breathe, don’t cough; report fever, redness or drainage; follow-up call the next day."},{"id":"48|ngn|8","sourceId":"48|n|8","q":"Which instructions are appropriate after a minimally invasive inguinal hernia repair? Select all that apply.","o":["Take deep breaths, but avoid coughing","Use ice packs and a scrotal support","Men may stand to void","Report urine output below 30 mL an hour","Lift no more than 10 lb until cleared","Cough forcefully every hour to clear the lungs","Apply heat to the scrotum for swelling"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Deep breathing protects the lungs; coughing spikes pressure on the fresh repair.","Correct. Ice and support reduce scrotal swelling, especially after indirect inguinal repair.","Correct. Standing helps men void after groin surgery and anesthesia.","Correct. Urinary retention is common; report UO < 30 mL/hr (straight catheter if unable to void).","Correct. Heavy lifting raises intraabdominal pressure on the repair.","Incorrect. Coughing strains the repair; use deep breathing and incentive spirometry.","Incorrect. Heat increases swelling; use ice."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A hernia forms where raised abdominal pressure pushes through a weak spot → after repair, coughing, lifting and straining push on the same spot → swelling and anesthesia can block voiding.","rule":"After hernia repair: deep breathe, don’t cough, ice and scrotal support, stand to void, report UO < 30 mL/hr, ≤ 10 lb lifting, fiber and fluids."},{"id":"48|ngn|12","sourceId":"48|n|12","q":"A 26-year-old cyclist struck the handlebars with his upper abdomen. In the ED he is alert, reports left upper abdominal pain of 8/10 and has a bruise across the upper abdomen. HR 122, BP 96/62, RR 24, SpO₂ 91% on room air. Which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","o":["Complete the primary survey before a detailed abdominal assessment","Apply supplemental oxygen","Assist with a bedside FAST scan","Inspect the abdomen with the client lying flat, and repeat it often","Palpate deeply over the painful area to find the injury","Give sips of water for thirst","Ask how the injury happened once life threats are managed"],"a":[0,1,2,3,6],"r":["Correct. Airway, breathing, circulation and any resuscitation come before the focused abdominal exam.","Correct. SpO₂ 91% with a fast respiratory rate — impaired oxygenation is treated with oxygen, or ventilation if needed.","Correct. A FAST scan quickly shows free fluid (blood or GI contents) in the abdomen.","Correct. Repeated inspection catches new bruising (Cullen, Turner), distention and worsening findings.","Incorrect. Significant palpation and percussion are left to the surgeon or emergency care provider.","Incorrect. He may need emergency surgery — keep him NPO.","Correct. The mechanism of injury (the instrument and the force) hints at which organs may be damaged."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Blunt force tears a solid organ such as the spleen or liver → blood collects in the abdomen → circulating volume falls (tachycardia, low BP) and less oxygen reaches the tissues → the primary survey supports airway, breathing and circulation → a FAST scan finds the free fluid → unstable bleeding goes to laparotomy.","rule":"Blunt abdominal trauma: primary survey first; oxygen as needed; FAST for free fluid, CT after resuscitation; inspect lying flat and repeat; frequent VS and Hgb/Hct; NPO; significant palpation by the trained provider; unstable → laparotomy."}],"studio":"trauma-hernia"},{"id":"acute-abdomen","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Acute inflammation: appendicitis, peritonitis and diverticulitis","notes":["49-overview","49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","49-appendicitis","49-diverticular-disease","49-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why peritonitis empties the circulation: Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI; Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel; Bacteria in blood → septic shock; Distention + pain → breathing compromise","Cardinal signs: Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention; Localized → one area · generalized → widespread","Priority problems: 1. Acute pain; 2. Fluid volume shift (into interstitial/peritoneal space)","Why the appendix perforates: Lumen blocked by a fecalith → pressure ↑ → blood flow ↓; Slow → abscess · fast → peritonitis; Gangrene/sepsis 24–36 hr; perforation risk ↑↑ after 48 hr"],"checks":["Explain why peritonitis empties the circulation in Acute inflammation.","Explain cardinal signs in Acute inflammation.","Explain priority problems in Acute inflammation."],"recallAnswers":["Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI; Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel; Bacteria in blood → septic shock; Distention + pain → breathing compromise","Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention; Localized → one area · generalized → widespread","1. Acute pain; 2. Fluid volume shift (into interstitial/peritoneal space)"],"requires":[],"terms":["peritonitis","appendicitis","diverticulitis"],"questions":[{"id":"49|trad|8","sourceId":"49|c|8","q":"A client with suspected appendicitis has a WBC of 23,000/mm³ and a temperature of 101.8°F (38.8°C). What does the nurse suspect?","o":["The appendix may have perforated","Uncomplicated appendicitis","Dehydration only","A viral infection"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A WBC above 20,000, temperature over 101°F and a rising pulse suggest perforation and peritonitis.","Incorrect. Uncomplicated cases usually run 10,000–18,000 with a shift to the left.","Incorrect. Dehydration doesn’t raise WBC this much.","Incorrect. Viral infections don’t usually do this."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Rupture spills bacteria into the peritoneum → a much bigger infection → bone marrow releases more neutrophils → WBC climbs; fever rises.","rule":"Appendicitis WBC 10,000–18,000 with a shift to the left; > 20,000 with fever > 101°F → perforation?"},{"id":"49|trad|9","sourceId":"49|c|9","q":"Which position is best for a client just back from surgery for peritonitis?","o":["Semi-Fowler","Flat supine","Trendelenburg","Prone"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Gravity keeps infected fluid low in the pelvis instead of under the diaphragm, and the lungs expand more easily.","Incorrect. Lets fluid spread upward and limits breathing.","Incorrect. Moves fluid toward the diaphragm.","Incorrect. Not appropriate after abdominal surgery."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Upright, peritoneal fluid drains to the pelvis → less under the diaphragm → better lung expansion and less chance of a subdiaphragmatic abscess.","rule":"After peritonitis surgery: semi-Fowler; LOC, VS, respiratory status and I&O at least hourly."},{"id":"49|trad|12","sourceId":"49|c|12","q":"A client is treated at home for mild diverticulitis. Which finding should the client report?","o":["A temperature over 101°F, increasing pain or blood in the stool","Mild gas while starting fiber after recovery","A soft, formed stool","Feeling tired on the first day"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Prolonged fever, more pain or bleeding → admission (abscess, perforation, bleeding).","Incorrect. Expected when fiber is added back.","Incorrect. Normal.","Incorrect. Expected with illness."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"An infected pouch can wall off (abscess), perforate (peritonitis) or erode a vessel (bleeding) → fever, pain and blood show which.","rule":"Admit diverticulitis for temperature > 101°F, severe pain > 3 days, or lower GI bleeding, especially in older adults."},{"id":"49|trad|15","sourceId":"49|c|15","q":"A client with diverticulitis had emergency surgery yesterday for a pelvic abscess that ruptured and caused peritonitis. The client is NPO with an NG tube to low suction. Which assessment is the nurse’s priority now?","o":["Fluid and electrolyte status","Return of bowel sounds","Appearance of the incision","Readiness for discharge teaching"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fluid is shifting into the peritoneal cavity while NG suction and NPO status add losses, so hypovolemia, electrolyte imbalance and kidney injury are the immediate danger.","Incorrect. Bowel sounds are expected to stay quiet for a while after peritonitis surgery; they are not the first threat.","Incorrect. The wound is watched, but the fluid shift threatens perfusion first.","Incorrect. Teaching comes later, once the client is stable."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Peritoneal inflammation → leaky vessels → fluid shifts into the abdomen (third spacing) → NG suction and NPO add losses → circulating volume and electrolytes fall → hypotension and acute kidney injury.","rule":"After a rupture or peritonitis surgery, the priority assessment is fluid and electrolyte status: I&O, daily weight, VS, electrolytes, BUN and creatinine."},{"id":"49|ngn|2","sourceId":"49|n|2","q":"Review the admission orders for a client with suspected appendicitis. Which orders should the nurse question? Select all that apply.","o":["NPO","IV lactated Ringer’s","Semi-Fowler position","Heating pad to the right lower quadrant","Fleet enema","Morphine once the diagnosis is confirmed"],"a":[3,4],"r":["Incorrect — appropriate. Surgery is likely and food would worsen the inflammation.","Incorrect — appropriate. IV fluids keep fluid and electrolyte balance.","Incorrect — appropriate. Drainage stays in the lower abdomen.","Correct — question it. Heat brings more blood and swelling to an appendix that is about to rupture.","Correct — question it. Laxatives and enemas push contents and motility against a closed, swollen tube → perforation.","Incorrect — appropriate. Once the diagnosis is confirmed, pain is treated with opioids and antibiotics."],"ex":[{"h":"Admission orders","lines":["1. NPO","2. IV lactated Ringer’s at 125 mL/hr","3. Semi-Fowler position as tolerated","4. Heating pad to the right lower quadrant for comfort","5. Fleet enema now for constipation","6. Morphine IV PRN once the diagnosis is confirmed and surgery scheduled"]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A fecalith blocks the lumen → secretions build pressure → blood flow to the wall falls → heat adds blood and swelling, and enemas add motility → the wall gives way → peritonitis.","rule":"Appendicitis: NPO, IV fluids, semi-Fowler, antibiotics and opioids once confirmed — never heat, laxatives or enemas."},{"id":"49|ngn|10","sourceId":"49|n|10","q":"Review the nurse’s notes for a 19-year-old admitted with suspected appendicitis. Which findings suggest the appendix has perforated? Select all that apply.","o":["Pain that eased suddenly, then returned across the whole abdomen","A rigid abdomen with the client lying still, knees drawn up","Temperature 102.4°F with a heart rate of 118","WBC 23,000/mm³","Pain that began around the umbilicus and moved to the right lower quadrant","Nausea and vomiting that followed the pain","Loss of appetite"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Rupture vents the pressure in the blocked appendix, so pain can ease for a while; then the spilled contents spread over the peritoneum and the pain becomes diffuse.","Correct. A rigid, boardlike abdomen and lying still with the knees flexed are signs of peritonitis.","Correct. Perforation brings a temperature over 101°F (38.3°C) and a rising pulse; the falling BP adds concern for sepsis and fluid shift.","Correct. Appendicitis usually runs 10,000–18,000; a WBC above 20,000 suggests perforation.","Incorrect. This is the classic course of appendicitis itself, not a sign of rupture.","Incorrect. Pain first, then nausea and vomiting, is the usual appendicitis sequence.","Incorrect. Anorexia is common in uncomplicated appendicitis."],"ex":[{"h":"Nurse’s notes","lines":["Yesterday 2200 — Cramping pain around the umbilicus, then nausea and one episode of vomiting; no appetite.","Today 0600 — Pain has moved to the right lower quadrant, 7/10. T 100.2°F (37.9°C), HR 92, BP 124/76.","Today 1000 — “The pain suddenly got a lot better about an hour ago.”","Today 1130 — Pain 9/10 across the whole abdomen; abdomen rigid; lies still with knees drawn up. T 102.4°F (39.1°C), HR 118, BP 98/58. WBC 23,000/mm³."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A fecalith blocks the lumen → pressure rises and blood flow to the wall falls → the wall gives way → the pressure is vented (brief relief) → bacteria spill over the peritoneum → diffuse pain, rigidity, fever, tachycardia → fluid shift and sepsis lower the BP.","rule":"Appendicitis → perforation: pain that eases and then becomes diffuse, a rigid abdomen, temperature > 101°F with a rising pulse, WBC > 20,000 → report now and prepare for surgery."},{"id":"49|ngn|11","sourceId":"49|n|11","q":"A client returns to the surgical unit after an exploratory laparotomy for peritonitis from a perforated diverticulum. The client has two abdominal drains, an NG tube to low suction and IV fluids. Which actions should the nurse include in the first postoperative hours? Select all that apply.","o":["Check level of consciousness, vital signs, respiratory status and intake and output at least hourly","Keep the client in a semi-Fowler position","Use sterile technique for any prescribed irrigation through the drains","Compare the irrigation fluid returned with the amount instilled","Report a change in wound drainage to the surgeon right away","Stop the IV fluids once the client is awake and alert","Switch the IV antibiotic to oral tablets once the client can swallow","Keep the client flat to protect the incision"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. The Action Alert after abdominal surgery: LOC, VS, respiratory status and I&O at least hourly.","Correct. Gravity drains peritoneal contents into the lower abdomen, and the lungs expand more easily.","Correct. Strict asepsis lowers the chance of a new infection on top of peritonitis.","Correct. Irrigation fluid that stays in the abdomen causes distention and pain.","Correct. Wound drainage is documented, and changes are reported immediately.","Incorrect. The client is NPO with NG suction and is still shifting fluid into the abdomen, so IV replacement continues.","Incorrect. The client is NPO with an NG tube to suction; antibiotics stay IV.","Incorrect. Lying flat lets infected fluid move up under the diaphragm and limits breathing."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Infected peritoneum → leaky vessels keep shifting fluid out of the circulation → NG suction and NPO add losses → hypovolemia and septic shock stay possible → hourly checks catch them early; semi-Fowler keeps infected fluid low and eases breathing.","rule":"After peritonitis surgery: LOC, VS, respiratory status and I&O at least hourly; semi-Fowler; sterile drain care, irrigation in vs out; IV fluids and IV antibiotics continue; report drainage changes."}],"studio":"acute-abdomen"},{"id":"ibd","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"IBD: immune inflammation, complications and drug safety","notes":["49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","49-crohn-s-disease"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them; 5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy; Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated; Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects","Signs: Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus; Colicky lower pain relieved by BM; Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss; Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum; Older adults: dehydration, ↓ K⁺","Priority problems: 1. Diarrhea; 2. Pain (inflammation, ulcers, skin); 3. Lower GI bleeding → anemia","Why: Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?"],"checks":["Explain why in IBD.","Explain signs in IBD.","Explain priority problems in IBD."],"recallAnswers":["Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them; 5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy; Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated; Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects","Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus; Colicky lower pain relieved by BM; Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss; Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum; Older adults: dehydration, ↓ K⁺","1. Diarrhea; 2. Pain (inflammation, ulcers, skin); 3. Lower GI bleeding → anemia"],"requires":[],"terms":["ulcerative colitis","Crohn disease","adalimumab","infliximab"],"questions":[{"id":"49|trad|1","sourceId":"49|c|1","q":"Which instruction is most important for a client starting adalimumab for Crohn’s disease?","o":["Avoid crowds and sick people, and report any infection, even a cold","Take it with food to prevent nausea","Expect your stools to turn black","Stop it if you feel better"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Blocking TNF suppresses the immune response to infection.","Incorrect. It is injected, not taken with food.","Incorrect. Not an adalimumab effect.","Incorrect. It maintains remission; don’t stop on your own."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"TNF-alpha drives the gut inflammation and also helps fight infection → blocking it calms the bowel but weakens defense against bacteria, fungi and TB.","rule":"Biologics (infliximab, adalimumab): avoid crowds and sick people, report any infection; teach self-injection and site reactions."},{"id":"49|trad|2","sourceId":"49|c|2","q":"A client’s UC flare has improved on prednisone. The client says, “I feel great — I’ll stop the prednisone today.” What should the nurse say?","o":["“The dose has to be tapered — stopping suddenly can cause adrenal insufficiency.”","“That’s fine once the symptoms are gone.”","“Cut the dose in half and stop next week.”","“Switch to an over-the-counter steroid.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Long-term steroids suppress the adrenal glands; they need time to recover as the dose falls.","Incorrect. Abrupt stopping is dangerous.","Incorrect. Tapering is ordered by the provider.","Incorrect. Not appropriate."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Prednisone acts like cortisol → the pituitary stops sending ACTH → the adrenals shrink → if the drug stops suddenly there is no cortisol → adrenal insufficiency (hypotension, shock).","rule":"Steroids in IBD: short term, taper — never stop suddenly; watch glucose, bones, infection and ulcers."},{"id":"49|trad|10","sourceId":"49|c|10","q":"Why are antidiarrheal drugs used cautiously in severe ulcerative colitis?","o":["They can trigger toxic megacolon","They cause bleeding","They worsen the sulfa allergy","They raise blood glucose"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Slowing an inflamed colon can let it dilate massively → colonic ileus, gangrene and peritonitis.","Incorrect. Not their effect.","Incorrect. Not related.","Incorrect. Not related."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Inflammation weakens the colon’s muscle → an antidiarrheal stops the remaining motility → gas and stool distend the colon → it dilates massively and can perforate.","rule":"UC: antidiarrheals short term and with caution; report distention, fever, tachycardia (toxic megacolon)."},{"id":"49|trad|11","sourceId":"49|c|11","q":"A client had part of the terminal ileum removed for Crohn’s disease. Which therapy will the client need?","o":["Monthly vitamin B₁₂ injections","Daily oral iron only","Vitamin C supplements","A high-fiber diet"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The terminal ileum absorbs B₁₂; without it, oral B₁₂ isn’t absorbed.","Incorrect. Iron may be needed, but B₁₂ is the key loss.","Incorrect. Not the issue.","Incorrect. Low-residue is usual."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"B₁₂ bound to intrinsic factor is absorbed only in the terminal ileum → disease or resection there stops absorption → stores run out → megaloblastic anemia and nerve damage.","rule":"Crohn’s: watch B₁₂, folate, albumin, K⁺, Mg²⁺; monthly B₁₂ injections when the ileum is diseased or removed."},{"id":"49|trad|13","sourceId":"49|c|13","q":"A client has had ulcerative colitis of the whole colon for 12 years. What does the nurse reinforce?","o":["Frequent colonoscopies to look for cancer","Colonoscopy is no longer needed","Yearly stool tests are enough","Cancer risk falls with time"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. UC for more than 10 years raises colorectal cancer risk; cancer causes about a third of UC deaths.","Incorrect. Surveillance increases.","Incorrect. Colonoscopy with biopsy is needed.","Incorrect. Risk rises with time."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Years of inflammation and cell turnover → dysplasia in the lining → adenocarcinoma.","rule":"UC > 10 years of the whole colon → frequent surveillance colonoscopy; dysplasia on biopsy can lead to colectomy."},{"id":"49|trad|16","sourceId":"49|c|16","q":"A client with Crohn’s disease is scheduled for a first infusion of infliximab. Which findings in the client’s history should the nurse report to the provider before the infusion? Select all that apply.","o":["Heart failure","Breast cancer treated 4 years ago","Multiple sclerosis","A cold and sore throat that started 2 days ago","Lactose intolerance","Appendectomy at age 17"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. The textbook says these TNF-alpha blockers are not given to patients with a history of heart disease.","Correct. A history of cancer is on the same list.","Correct. So is multiple sclerosis. (The textbook places natalizumab in this same group, with the same cautions.)","Correct. Biologics suppress immunity; any infection, even a cold or sore throat, is reported right away.","Incorrect. Common in IBD and not a reason to hold the drug.","Incorrect. A past appendectomy doesn’t affect the drug."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"TNF-alpha drives the gut inflammation but is also part of the defense against infection → blocking it calms the bowel and lowers resistance to infection → the textbook adds that these drugs are not given with a history of cancer, heart disease or multiple sclerosis.","rule":"Anti-TNF biologics (infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab pegol): not with a history of cancer, heart disease or MS; report any infection; avoid crowds. The textbook lists natalizumab with them."},{"id":"49|ngn|8","sourceId":"49|n|8","q":"Review the findings for a 72-year-old client with a severe UC flare. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Call the Rapid Response Team and prepare two large-bore IVs","Give loperamide to slow the stools","Offer a high-fiber snack to firm the stools","Document the findings and recheck the Hgb tomorrow"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Falling Hgb and BP with rising HR and new confusion = lower GI bleeding with volume loss; blood products need large-bore access.","Incorrect. Antidiarrheals in severe UC can trigger toxic megacolon, and they don’t treat bleeding.","Incorrect. Fiber doesn’t stop bleeding and can worsen symptoms in a flare.","Incorrect. This is an active bleed now."],"ex":[{"h":"Findings","lines":["14 bloody stools in 24 hours. Newly confused this morning."]},{"h":"Trend","table":[["","Yesterday","Today"],["HR","96","118"],["BP","118/70","94/58"],["Hgb","10.2 g/dL","8.1 g/dL"]]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Ulcerated colon lining erodes into vessels → blood is lost in the stools → volume and hemoglobin fall → less oxygen reaches the brain → confusion may be the first sign in older adults.","rule":"UC bleeding: monitor stools, Hgb/Hct, VS; if bleeding begins → Rapid Response or provider now, two large-bore IVs, blood products."},{"id":"49|ngn|9","sourceId":"49|n|9","q":"A client with Crohn’s disease has an enterocutaneous fistula draining 400 mL a day. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","o":["Provide at least 3000 calories a day","Weigh daily and track intake and output","Pouch the fistula to protect the skin and measure output","Consult the wound, ostomy and continence nurse","Report fever, abdominal pain or a change in mental status","Offer a high-fiber diet","Limit fluids to reduce the drainage"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Fistula output drains calories and protein; healing needs ≥ 3000 kcal (TEN or TPN if oral intake falls short).","Correct. A drop in weight or urine output means dehydration.","Correct. Drainage over 100 mL in 24 hours needs a pouch; enzymes digest skin.","Correct. The CWOCN plans skin protection and pouching.","Correct. Abscess and sepsis are the dangers.","Incorrect. The diet is high-calorie, high-protein, low-fiber.","Incorrect. Losses must be replaced, not restricted."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Transmural inflammation tunnels to the skin → intestinal fluid rich in electrolytes and enzymes drains out → dehydration, hypokalemia and malnutrition → enzymes on the skin cause breakdown and infection.","rule":"Fistula: nutrition and fluid/electrolytes first (≥ 3000 kcal, I&O, daily weight), then skin (pouch > 100 mL/day, CWOCN), and watch for abscess and sepsis."}],"studio":"ibd"},{"id":"ileostomy","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Ileostomy and the J-pouch: perfusion, fluid and food","notes":["49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?","ERAS (Box 49.3): ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids; Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose; During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia; After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal","Skin is the priority: Drains often → always wear a pouch; Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast; Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)","Evaluate: ↓ diarrhea, ↓ pain, no bleeding, self-manages ostomy/pouch"],"checks":["Explain why in Ileostomy and the J-pouch.","Explain eras (box 49.3) in Ileostomy and the J-pouch.","Explain skin is the priority in Ileostomy and the J-pouch."],"recallAnswers":["Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?","ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids; Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose; During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia; After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal","Drains often → always wear a pouch; Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast; Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)"],"requires":[],"terms":["ileostomy","proctocolectomy","dehydration"],"questions":[{"id":"49|trad|17","sourceId":"49|c|17","q":"A client with a new ileostomy is planning what to eat at a family barbecue. Which foods should the nurse advise the client to avoid or eat only with caution? Select all that apply.","o":["Corn on the cob","Coconut cream pie","Celery sticks with dip","Baked fish","Mashed potatoes","Plain yogurt"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Corn is high in cellulose, its skins can block the stoma, and it causes gas.","Correct. Coconut is a high-fiber food that can cause a blockage.","Correct. Celery is fibrous and gas-forming.","Incorrect. Soft, low-fiber protein is well tolerated.","Incorrect. Soft and low in fiber.","Incorrect. Usually well tolerated."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Small-bowel contents leave through a narrow stoma → stringy, high-cellulose foods are not broken down → they can bunch up and block the opening (cramps, distention, no output) → gas-forming foods add bloating.","rule":"Ileostomy diet: chew thoroughly; be cautious with high-fiber, high-cellulose and gas-forming foods (popcorn, coconut, corn, peas, vegetable skins, cabbage, celery, nuts)."},{"id":"49|ngn|6","sourceId":"49|n|6","q":"Which instructions should the nurse include for a client with a new ileostomy? Select all that apply.","o":["Drink at least 500 mL extra fluid a day while output is high","Always wear the pouch with a skin barrier","Never take laxatives or enemas","If output stops with cramping, remove the pouch, lie knee-chest, massage the abdomen and drink hot tea","Call the provider if no stool has passed in 6–12 hours","Limit salt to reduce swelling","Expect formed stool within a week"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Output starts above 1 L a day; without a colon to reabsorb water, dehydration comes fast.","Correct. Small-bowel output is full of enzymes and bile salts that excoriate skin.","Correct. Loose stool is normal; no output may mean a food blockage, not constipation.","Correct. These steps can relieve a food blockage at the stoma.","Correct. Persistent no output is a possible obstruction.","Incorrect. The client needs adequate salt and water — the colon that saved them is gone.","Incorrect. It thickens to a paste, not formed stool."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The colon reabsorbs water and sodium → an ileostomy skips it → liquid, high-volume, enzyme-rich output → dehydration and sodium loss risk and rapid skin damage → over weeks the ileum adapts and absorbs more.","rule":"Ileostomy: +500 mL fluid, salt, barrier always, empty at ⅓–½, no laxatives, avoid enteric-coated pills, report stoma that is pale, gray, bluish or dark, or no output with cramps."}],"studio":"ileostomy"},{"id":"enteric-celiac","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Gastroenteritis and celiac disease: losses versus malabsorption","notes":["49-gastroenteritis","49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea; Viral > bacterial; small bowel; Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration","Signs (Box 49.5): Flares follow diet adherence; Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating; Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines","Diagnosis and treatment: Dx: blood test + endoscopy; Gluten-free diet = only treatment; heals in ~2 years; Gluten = wheat (bread, noodles, cookies); Hidden gluten: additives, drugs, cosmetics; dietitian","Why: Diarrhea partly flushes the organism out; Slowing the gut keeps it in longer; So replace losses (fluid, K⁺); antidiarrheal only if the provider says"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Gastroenteritis and celiac disease.","Explain signs (box 49.5) in Gastroenteritis and celiac disease.","Explain diagnosis and treatment in Gastroenteritis and celiac disease."],"recallAnswers":["Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea; Viral > bacterial; small bowel; Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration","Flares follow diet adherence; Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating; Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines","Dx: blood test + endoscopy; Gluten-free diet = only treatment; heals in ~2 years; Gluten = wheat (bread, noodles, cookies); Hidden gluten: additives, drugs, cosmetics; dietitian"],"requires":[],"terms":["gastroenteritis","celiac disease","malabsorption"],"questions":[{"id":"49|trad|4","sourceId":"49|c|4","q":"Several members of a family who share a well have diarrhea, and one has Giardia in the stool. Which teaching is most important?","o":["Have the water supply tested and have household members’ stools examined","Only the person with the positive test needs treatment","Boil only drinking water for adults","Giardia cannot spread between people"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Shared water is the likely source; household and sexual partners need stool exams.","Incorrect. Others are likely infected.","Incorrect. Everyone uses the same water.","Incorrect. It spreads fecal-oral."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Giardia cysts survive in untreated water → everyone drinking it swallows them → they inflame the small-intestine lining → diarrhea and malabsorption.","rule":"Parasites: stool analysis; metronidazole (Giardia); test the water and contacts; meticulous handwashing; no rectal-contact sex until treated."},{"id":"49|trad|5","sourceId":"49|c|5","q":"Which finding is often the first sign of dehydration in an older adult with gastroenteritis?","o":["Acute confusion","High fever","Excessive thirst","Weight gain"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. In older adults dehydration can be severe and fast, and confusion may be the only early sign.","Incorrect. Fever is less common in older adults.","Incorrect. Thirst is often blunted with aging.","Incorrect. Dehydration causes weight loss."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Fluid loss lowers circulating volume → less blood and oxygen to the brain → confusion; aging blunts thirst and fever.","rule":"Dehydration: weight loss, poor turgor, dry membranes, orthostatic BP (falls), oliguria — and in older adults, acute confusion."},{"id":"49|trad|6","sourceId":"49|c|6","q":"Which antidiarrheal should be avoided in an 80-year-old with gastroenteritis?","o":["Diphenoxylate with atropine","Oral rehydration solution","Loperamide if the provider decides it is needed","A barrier cream"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It is habit-forming and causes drowsiness → falls; it isn’t used in older adults.","Incorrect. ORT is the treatment.","Incorrect. Loperamide may be recommended if needed.","Incorrect. Protects perianal skin."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Diphenoxylate is an opioid that slows the gut; atropine adds anticholinergic effects → drowsiness, confusion and dizziness → falls.","rule":"Gastroenteritis: replace fluids; antidiarrheals only if prescribed; no diphenoxylate in older adults."},{"id":"49|trad|18","sourceId":"49|c|18","q":"A 32-year-old client with type 1 diabetes has iron deficiency anemia that has not improved with oral iron, osteoporosis on a bone scan, lactose intolerance and frequent migraines. Bowel movements are normal. Which condition should the nurse anticipate the provider will evaluate?","o":["Celiac disease","Diverticulosis","Ulcerative colitis","Norovirus gastroenteritis"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Celiac disease can show up with atypical symptoms in other body systems (Box 49.5), and other autoimmune diseases such as type 1 diabetes raise the risk. A screening blood test and endoscopy are used.","Incorrect. Usually silent and found in older adults on colonoscopy.","Incorrect. UC causes bloody diarrhea with tenesmus.","Incorrect. Norovirus brings a few days of vomiting and diarrhea."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Gluten triggers an autoimmune attack on the small-intestine lining → the lining atrophies → iron, calcium and other nutrients are poorly absorbed → anemia and osteoporosis can appear even without diarrhea.","rule":"Celiac disease: classic GI symptoms or atypical ones (iron deficiency anemia, osteoporosis, lactose intolerance, migraines, depression); higher risk with type 1 diabetes and RA; blood test + endoscopy; a gluten-free diet is the only treatment."},{"id":"49|trad|19","sourceId":"49|c|19","q":"A client newly diagnosed with celiac disease asks where gluten can hide besides bread and pasta. Which items should the nurse teach the client to check? Select all that apply.","o":["Prescription and over-the-counter medicines","Lipstick and other cosmetics","Food additives such as thickeners in canned soups and sauces","Vitamin and mineral supplements","Plain fresh fruit","Plain rice"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Drug fillers and coatings can contain gluten.","Correct. Cosmetics can contain gluten, and lip products are easily swallowed.","Correct. Additives and thickeners are common hidden sources.","Correct. Supplements are often needed in celiac disease, and they are products whose ingredients must be checked too.","Incorrect. Naturally gluten-free.","Incorrect. Naturally gluten-free."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Even small amounts of gluten restart the immune attack on the small-intestine lining → the lining stays damaged → malabsorption continues; it heals (in about 2 years) only when gluten is fully removed.","rule":"Celiac disease: a gluten-free diet is the only treatment; check foods, food additives, drugs and cosmetics for hidden gluten; vitamin and mineral supplements; a dietitian."},{"id":"49|ngn|4","sourceId":"49|n|4","q":"A client has norovirus gastroenteritis. Which instructions help prevent spread? Select all that apply.","o":["Wash hands with soap for at least 30 seconds, especially after a bowel movement","Don’t prepare or handle food for others","Keep your own glasses, dishes, utensils and toothpaste","Keep the bathroom clean","Tell your provider if symptoms last more than 3 days","Take loperamide right away to stop the diarrhea","Go back to your restaurant job once the vomiting stops"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. The road is feces to mouth; soap and friction break it.","Correct. Food handlers consult public health before returning to work.","Correct. Shared items carry the virus.","Correct. Contaminated surfaces spread norovirus.","Correct. Most cases resolve in about 3 days; longer needs evaluation.","Incorrect. Slowing the gut keeps the organism inside longer; antidiarrheals only if the provider decides.","Incorrect. Shedding continues after symptoms; food handlers need public health clearance."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Virus is shed in stool and vomit → hands, surfaces, food and shared items carry it → the next person swallows it → handwashing, cleaning and keeping ill people away from food break the chain.","rule":"Gastroenteritis: replace fluids (ORT), wash hands ≥ 30 s, separate utensils, no food handling, report symptoms > 3 days."}],"studio":"enteric-celiac"},{"id":"cirrhosis","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Cirrhosis and ascites: pressure, protein and fluid shifts","notes":["50-overview","50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","50-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why ascites forms: ↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out; ↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back; Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once; Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites","Late-stage signs (Box 50.3): Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin; Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas; Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K","Priority problems: 1 Fluid overload (ascites); 2 Hemorrhage (varices); 3 Confusion (ammonia, withdrawal); 4 Pruritus (bilirubin)","Why the patient bleeds three ways: Varices: thin detour veins stretch and rupture; ↓ Prothrombin + no vitamin K absorption → ↓ factors II, VII, IX, X → long PT/INR; Splenomegaly destroys platelets → thrombocytopenia (often the first sign)"],"checks":["Explain why ascites forms in Cirrhosis and ascites.","Explain late-stage signs (box 50.3) in Cirrhosis and ascites.","Explain priority problems in Cirrhosis and ascites."],"recallAnswers":["↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out; ↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back; Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once; Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites","Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin; Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas; Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K","1 Fluid overload (ascites); 2 Hemorrhage (varices); 3 Confusion (ammonia, withdrawal); 4 Pruritus (bilirubin)"],"requires":[],"terms":["cirrhosis","ascites","paracentesis","spironolactone"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|1","sourceId":"50|c|1","q":"The nurse is assessing a client with advanced cirrhosis. Which serum laboratory finding would the nurse anticipate?","o":["Elevated ammonia","Increased albumin","Decreased alkaline phosphatase","Decreased ALT that shows the liver is healing"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The failing liver can’t convert ammonia to urea.","Incorrect. Albumin falls — the liver makes less.","Incorrect. Alkaline phosphatase rises with biliary obstruction.","Incorrect. ALT may be normal late because burned-out cells have little left to leak — not because the liver is healing."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hepatocytes run the urea cycle → fewer working cells and blood shunted around the liver → ammonia rises in the blood.","rule":"Advanced cirrhosis: ammonia up, albumin down, PT/INR up, platelets down, bilirubin and alkaline phosphatase up; ALT and AST may be normal late."},{"id":"50|trad|7","sourceId":"50|c|7","q":"A client with ascites asks why both furosemide and spironolactone were prescribed. What is the best response?","o":["“The two work differently and together keep your sodium and potassium in better balance.”","“Two diuretics double the fluid removed so you can stop the low-sodium diet.”","“One protects your liver from the other.”","“Spironolactone prevents the itching caused by furosemide.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Furosemide wastes potassium; spironolactone spares it — the combination balances potassium while both remove sodium and water.","Incorrect. The sodium restriction continues.","Incorrect. Neither protects the liver.","Incorrect. Neither drug is for itching."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Loop diuretic → more sodium reaches the collecting duct → potassium is lost; spironolactone blocks aldosterone there → potassium is kept → the effects on potassium offset.","rule":"Furosemide + spironolactone for ascites: monitor weight, girth, I&O and electrolytes (hypokalemia, hyponatremia, dehydration)."},{"id":"50|trad|17","sourceId":"50|c|17","q":"Which task can the RN delegate to assistive personnel for a stable client with cirrhosis and ascites?","o":["Weigh the client every morning before breakfast on the same scale","Assess the client for asterixis","Teach the client about the low-sodium diet","Decide whether the client’s new confusion is significant"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Measuring weight is within AP scope; tell them to report the number right away.","Incorrect. Assessment is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Teaching is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Interpreting findings is the RN’s."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Daily weight is the most reliable fluid measure → accurate, consistent technique matters → AP can do it; the RN interprets the trend.","rule":"Delegate measurements (weight, I&O, VS on a stable client); keep assessment, teaching and judgment."}],"studio":"cirrhosis"},{"id":"varices","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Varices: portal pressure and life-threatening bleeding","notes":["50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg; Blood takes collateral detours","Where the blood goes: → Spleen (splenomegaly); → esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins; = ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","Nursing care during a bleed: NG to watch for new bleeding; PRBCs, FFP, albumin, platelets via large-bore IVs; VS every hour; PT, PTT, platelets, INR","Why: May pass out before blood shows (blood pools in stomach); Sudden ↑ HR, ↓ BP, fainting = variceal bleed until proven otherwise; Teach: no straining, heavy lifting, dry coarse food"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Varices.","Explain where the blood goes in Varices.","Explain nursing care during a bleed in Varices."],"recallAnswers":["Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg; Blood takes collateral detours","→ Spleen (splenomegaly); → esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins; = ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","NG to watch for new bleeding; PRBCs, FFP, albumin, platelets via large-bore IVs; VS every hour; PT, PTT, platelets, INR"],"requires":[],"terms":["varices","portal hypertension","propranolol"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|2","sourceId":"50|c|2","q":"A client has active upper GI bleeding from esophageal varices. Which interventions would the nurse anticipate to manage the bleeding? Select all that apply.","o":["Octreotide infusion","Endoscopic sclerotherapy","Endoscopic variceal ligation (banding)","Lactulose","Liver transplant today","Starting oral propranolol"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Octreotide constricts splanchnic vessels and lowers portal pressure.","Correct. Injecting a sclerosing agent seals the varix.","Correct. Bands cut off the varix’s blood supply.","Incorrect. Lactulose treats ammonia, not bleeding.","Incorrect. A transplant is for end-stage disease, not emergency bleeding control.","Incorrect. Propranolol prevents bleeding; it doesn’t stop an active bleed and can worsen hypotension."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Octreotide narrows gut vessels → less portal inflow → lower variceal pressure; banding or sclerotherapy closes the bleeding vein directly.","rule":"Active variceal bleed: octreotide + endoscopic banding or sclerotherapy (plus antibiotics, blood products). Prevention: nonselective beta blocker."},{"id":"50|trad|6","sourceId":"50|c|6","q":"A client with esophageal varices that have never bled is started on propranolol. Which teaching is most important?","o":["Check the pulse and report dizziness or a slow heart rate","Take it only when you vomit blood","Stop it if your blood pressure is normal","It will make the varices disappear"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The nonselective beta blocker lowers heart rate and BP — watch for bradycardia and hypotension.","Incorrect. It is daily prevention; vomiting blood is an emergency.","Incorrect. It is taken to lower portal pressure, not to treat hypertension.","Incorrect. It lowers the chance of bleeding; it doesn’t remove varices."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Beta-1 blockade slows the heart → less cardiac output; beta-2 blockade narrows gut arterioles → less portal flow → lower variceal pressure → less chance of rupture.","rule":"Propranolol prevents variceal bleeding: watch for bradycardia and hypotension; it isn’t treatment for an active bleed."}],"studio":"varices"},{"id":"encephalopathy","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration","notes":["50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Also PSE; Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early; Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure","Why it happens: Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA; Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient","Stages (Box 50.1): I: personality, mood, speech, sleep changes; II: confusion, disorientation, asterixis; III: stuporous but arousable, twitching, hyperreflexia, asterixis; IV: unarousable, no asterixis, Babinski +, rigidity, fetor, seizures","Why: Kidneys starved, not damaged: volume shifted to the abdomen; Kidney holds Na⁺ → urine scant, concentrated, low sodium; GI bleed (volume loss) often tips it"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration.","Explain why it happens in Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration.","Explain stages (box 50.1) in Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration."],"recallAnswers":["Also PSE; Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early; Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure","Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA; Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient","I: personality, mood, speech, sleep changes; II: confusion, disorientation, asterixis; III: stuporous but arousable, twitching, hyperreflexia, asterixis; IV: unarousable, no asterixis, Babinski +, rigidity, fetor, seizures"],"requires":[],"terms":["encephalopathy","lactulose","rifaximin"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|8","sourceId":"50|c|8","q":"Which finding in a client with end-stage liver disease suggests hepatorenal syndrome?","o":["Urine output of 380 mL in 24 hours with a rising creatinine","Dark, tea-colored urine","Increasing abdominal girth","Spider angiomas on the chest"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Oliguria (< 500 mL/24 hr) with rising BUN and creatinine is the hallmark.","Incorrect. Dark urine is bilirubin, not kidney failure.","Incorrect. That is ascites.","Incorrect. A chronic skin sign."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Low effective volume → the kidney constricts its vessels and holds sodium → little urine → BUN and creatinine rise.","rule":"Hepatorenal syndrome: UO < 500 mL/24 hr, rising BUN and creatinine, low urine sodium, high urine osmolarity; poor prognosis."},{"id":"50|trad|9","sourceId":"50|c|9","q":"A client with cirrhosis reports itching that is worse at night. Which comfort measure should the nurse suggest?","o":["Keep the room cool and moisturize the skin","Take a long, hot shower at bedtime","Use an alcohol-based lotion to dry the skin","Add an extra blanket to stay warm"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Pruritus worsens with warmth; cool surroundings, moisturizing and avoiding irritants help.","Incorrect. Heat worsens itching and dries the skin.","Incorrect. Alcohol dries and irritates.","Incorrect. Being too warm increases itching."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bilirubin and bile salts deposit in the skin → itching that warmth intensifies → cooling and moisture calm it.","rule":"Pruritus: stay cool, moisturize, avoid irritants; cool compresses or steroid cream; an SSRI such as sertraline if needed."},{"id":"50|trad|10","sourceId":"50|c|10","q":"The nurse teaches a client with cirrhosis about home care. Which statement by the client indicates a need for more teaching?","o":["“I’ll take acetaminophen whenever I get a headache.”","“I’ll avoid all alcohol.”","“I’ll call if I see black stools.”","“I’ll check with my provider before taking any vitamin.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Avoid all OTC drugs, especially acetaminophen and NSAIDs — the liver can’t clear them safely.","Incorrect. Correct — no alcohol.","Incorrect. Correct — report GI bleeding at once.","Incorrect. Correct — some vitamins and minerals are liver-toxic."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The liver breaks down most drugs → a failing liver lets them build up → acetaminophen in particular is directly toxic to liver cells.","rule":"Cirrhosis teaching: no alcohol, smoking or illicit drugs; no OTC drugs (especially acetaminophen, NSAIDs); no liver-toxic herbs; report bleeding, black stools, confusion."},{"id":"50|trad|15","sourceId":"50|c|15","q":"A client with alcoholic cirrhosis had the last drink 7 hours ago. Which finding should the nurse expect first if withdrawal is beginning?","o":["Tremors (\"the jitters\")","Seizures and coma","Asterixis","Jaundice"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Tremors can begin as early as 6–8 hours after the last drink.","Incorrect. Severe signs, if they come, follow later.","Incorrect. Asterixis is hepatic encephalopathy.","Incorrect. Jaundice is from bilirubin, not withdrawal."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Chronic alcohol enhances GABA and blocks glutamate → when it stops, the nervous system is overexcited → tremor first, then possible delirium tremens.","rule":"Alcohol withdrawal: tremors from 6–8 hr; DTs = confusion, hallucinations, tachycardia, high BP, diaphoresis — an emergency; thiamine and benzodiazepines as ordered."},{"id":"50|trad|16","sourceId":"50|c|16","q":"A client had a transjugular intrahepatic portal-systemic shunt (TIPS) placed for refractory ascites. Which complication should the nurse watch for most?","o":["Hepatic encephalopathy","Hypoglycemia","Esophageal stricture","Urinary retention"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Blood now bypasses the liver’s filtering, so ammonia and other toxins reach the brain.","Incorrect. Not the main risk.","Incorrect. Not related to TIPS.","Incorrect. Not related to TIPS."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Stent from portal vein to hepatic vein → portal blood skips the sinusoids → ammonia isn’t cleared → encephalopathy.","rule":"After TIPS: watch for encephalopathy (lactulose, diet, or narrowing the shunt) and pulmonary pressure/right heart strain; ultrasounds for a year for patency."}],"studio":"encephalopathy"},{"id":"hepatitis","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Hepatitis: route of spread, testing and treatment safety","notes":["50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Definition and causes: Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common; Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV; Report to local health department → CDC","Signs: Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches; Clay stools + dark urine; Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever; Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced","Health care workers (Box 50.6): Standard Precautions; needleless systems; HBV vaccine × 3 (also prevents D); IG after HAV exposure; report cases","Why: A, E = gut route, cleared, never chronic; B, C, D = blood route, hide for decades → cirrhosis → cancer; Teach A/E: handwashing, water · B/C/D: blood, needles, sex, screening"],"checks":["Explain definition and causes in Hepatitis.","Explain signs in Hepatitis.","Explain health care workers (box 50.6) in Hepatitis."],"recallAnswers":["Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common; Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV; Report to local health department → CDC","Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches; Clay stools + dark urine; Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever; Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced","Standard Precautions; needleless systems; HBV vaccine × 3 (also prevents D); IG after HAV exposure; report cases"],"requires":[],"terms":["hepatitis","antiviral","jaundice"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|11","sourceId":"50|c|11","q":"A client planning a trip to Mexico asks how to prevent hepatitis. Which advice is best?","o":["Get the hepatitis A vaccine before the trip and avoid tap water and raw shellfish","Get the hepatitis C vaccine","Avoid hugging people while traveling","Take an antiviral drug daily during the trip"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Hepatitis A (and E) are fecal-oral and common where sanitation is poor; vaccinate before travel.","Incorrect. There is no hepatitis C vaccine.","Incorrect. Hepatitis isn’t spread by hugging.","Incorrect. Antivirals aren’t used for travel prevention."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hepatitis A is shed in stool and contaminates water and food → vaccine-made antibodies stop it before it reaches the liver.","rule":"Hepatitis A prevention: handwashing, safe food and water (including tap water where it is common), vaccine before travel, immune globulin within 14 days of exposure."},{"id":"50|trad|13","sourceId":"50|c|13","q":"A client with acute hepatitis A has nausea and no appetite. Which diet should the nurse encourage?","o":["Small, frequent, high-carbohydrate, high-calorie meals, with moderate fat and protein added as nausea eases","Three large high-protein meals a day","Clear liquids only for a week","A high-fat diet to supply calories"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Small, frequent high-carbohydrate meals of favorite foods are tolerated best.","Incorrect. Large meals worsen nausea.","Incorrect. Too few calories for healing.","Incorrect. Fat is added moderately once nausea subsides."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The liver needs energy and protein to regenerate → small high-carbohydrate meals provide calories without overloading a nauseated gut.","rule":"Hepatitis: high carbohydrate, high calorie, small frequent meals, favorite foods, vitamins; alternate rest and activity; no alcohol."},{"id":"50|trad|14","sourceId":"50|c|14","q":"A client with hepatitis C genotype 1 is prescribed ledipasvir/sofosbuvir. The medication list includes amiodarone. What should the nurse do?","o":["Hold the antiviral and contact the provider about the combination","Give both and check the pulse once a day","Give the antiviral at bedtime to separate it from amiodarone","Tell the client to take the drugs with grapefruit juice"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The combination can cause severe symptomatic bradycardia — the book says not to give it with amiodarone.","Incorrect. The combination itself is the danger.","Incorrect. Spacing doesn’t prevent the interaction.","Incorrect. Grapefruit juice is not the issue here."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The two together depress the heart’s conduction → profound bradycardia, chest pain.","rule":"Never ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone (severe bradycardia). Ask about hepatitis B before any HCV DAA."}],"studio":"hepatitis"},{"id":"fatty-liver","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Fatty liver: metabolic risk and prevention of progression","notes":["50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Too much fat stored, not from alcohol; Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome; ~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH","Assessment: Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM; Often silent for years; Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later","Treatment: Book: no approved drugs → lifestyle: diet, exercise, behavior; Team: dietitian + mental health; Newer: resmetirom, semaglutide for MASH with fibrosis","Why: Liver = overflow tank for fat (insulin resistance); Treat like metabolic syndrome: weight loss + exercise; Fat + early inflammation can recede before scar"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Fatty liver.","Explain assessment in Fatty liver.","Explain treatment in Fatty liver."],"recallAnswers":["Too much fat stored, not from alcohol; Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome; ~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH","Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM; Often silent for years; Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later","Book: no approved drugs → lifestyle: diet, exercise, behavior; Team: dietitian + mental health; Newer: resmetirom, semaglutide for MASH with fibrosis"],"requires":[],"terms":["steatosis","steatohepatitis","fibrosis"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|18","sourceId":"50|c|18","q":"The nurse is screening clients at a community health fair. Which clients have risk factors for nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)? Select all that apply.","o":["A client with metabolic syndrome","A client with type 2 diabetes","A client with obesity and high triglycerides","A Latino client who carries the PNPLA3 gene variant","A client who recovered from hepatitis A last year","A client whose fatty liver comes from heavy daily drinking"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Metabolic syndrome is a key risk factor.","Correct. Type 2 diabetes is tied to NAFLD.","Correct. Obesity and high triglycerides are risk factors.","Correct. PNPLA3 carriers are at higher risk, and people of Latino descent carry it more often and have the highest risk.","Incorrect. Hepatitis A is self-limiting and is not a NAFLD risk factor.","Incorrect. Fat from heavy drinking is alcohol-related liver disease — NAFLD by definition is unrelated to alcohol."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Insulin resistance (obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome) sends extra fatty acids to the liver → fat builds up in hepatocytes; carriers of the PNPLA3 variant are at higher risk.","rule":"NAFLD risk: obesity, high cholesterol and triglycerides, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, age, and the PNPLA3 gene (Latino adults at highest risk)."},{"id":"50|trad|20","sourceId":"50|c|20","q":"The nurse is explaining nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD) and nonalcoholic steatohepatitis (NASH) to a client. Which statement is accurate?","o":["NASH adds inflammation, liver-cell injury and fibrosis to the fat, and it can progress to cirrhosis.","NASH is a fatty liver caused by heavy drinking.","NAFLD always progresses to NASH within a few years.","NASH is diagnosed from raised liver enzymes alone."],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. NASH is the more severe form: inflammation, cell injury and fibrosis on top of the fat; both can progress to cirrhosis, liver failure and liver cancer.","Incorrect. Both are unrelated to alcohol — that is what “nonalcoholic” means.","Incorrect. About 20% of people with NAFLD develop NASH.","Incorrect. ALT and AST rise, but elastography or biopsy confirms NAFLD or NASH and shows fibrosis."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Stored fat in hepatocytes → in about 20%, inflammation and cell injury → fibroblasts lay down scar (fibrosis) → cirrhosis if it continues.","rule":"NAFLD = fat without alcohol; NASH (~20%) = fat + inflammation + injury + fibrosis → cirrhosis, liver failure, cancer; confirm with elastography or biopsy."}],"studio":"fatty-liver"},{"id":"liver-transplant","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Liver transplantation: early complications and long-term protection","notes":["50-liver-transplantation"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Failing graft stops its jobs:; ↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion); Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status","Not candidates: Severe heart or lung disease; Metastatic cancer; Can't follow the drug/self-care plan","Donor livers: Mostly trauma donors; UNOS by acuity (MELD); Preserved up to 8 hr; DDLT: upper abdominal incision; lots of fluid + blood"],"checks":["Explain why in Liver transplantation.","Explain not candidates in Liver transplantation.","Explain donor livers in Liver transplantation."],"recallAnswers":["Failing graft stops its jobs:; ↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion); Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status","Severe heart or lung disease; Metastatic cancer; Can't follow the drug/self-care plan","Mostly trauma donors; UNOS by acuity (MELD); Preserved up to 8 hr; DDLT: upper abdominal incision; lots of fluid + blood"],"requires":[],"terms":["immunosuppression","rejection","transplantation"],"questions":[{"id":"50|trad|21","sourceId":"50|c|21","q":"A client is 2 days after a liver transplant. Which findings should the nurse report to the surgeon at once? Select all that apply.","o":["Oozing from the IV and catheter sites, with new petechiae","New confusion and trouble following commands","Abdominal distention with rigidity","Much less bile draining into the T-tube bag","Incisional pain of 3 out of 10, eased by the prescribed analgesic","Urine output of 50 mL per hour"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Bleeding signs may mean the new liver isn’t making clotting factors — report at once.","Correct. A neurologic change can mean the new liver isn’t working (encephalopathy).","Correct. Pain, distention and rigidity suggest peritonitis.","Correct. Less bile can mean rejection or a biliary or vascular complication such as hepatic artery thrombosis.","Incorrect. Controlled incisional pain is expected.","Incorrect. 50 mL an hour is adequate output; falling output with rising BUN and creatinine would point to acute kidney injury."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A failing graft stops making clotting factors (oozing, petechiae), stops clearing toxins (confusion) and stops making bile (less T-tube drainage); rigidity with distention means peritonitis.","rule":"After liver transplant: report at once fever, abdominal pain with distention and rigidity, neurologic change, bleeding signs, less bile or rising jaundice; keep the T-tube dependent and record its drainage."},{"id":"50|trad|22","sourceId":"50|c|22","q":"The nurse is teaching a client who is going home after a liver transplant. Which statement by the client shows a need for more teaching?","o":["“Once my new liver is working well, I can stop taking tacrolimus.”","“I’ll call my provider for a fever or any other sign of infection.”","“I’ll keep up my skin and breast self-exams and my Pap tests.”","“My blood pressure and kidney function will be checked because of my antirejection drug.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct — this needs more teaching. Antirejection drugs are taken for life; stopping them invites rejection.","Incorrect. This is right: immunosuppression makes infection likely at any time.","Incorrect. This is right: lifelong cancer screening is part of follow-up.","Incorrect. This is right: the drugs can raise blood pressure and harm the kidneys."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Tacrolimus blocks the T-cell signal that would attack the donor liver’s foreign proteins → the immune system never stops seeing the graft as foreign → the drug is needed for life.","rule":"After transplant: lifelong immunosuppression; watch for high BP, kidney toxicity, infection and GI upset; report any sign of infection; lifelong cancer, metabolic syndrome and diabetes screening."},{"id":"50|trad|23","sourceId":"50|c|23","q":"A client who had a liver transplant 6 weeks ago has a fever. After the first month, which type of infection is this client most at risk for?","o":["An opportunistic infection such as cytomegalovirus (CMV)","A wound infection at the incision","A urinary tract infection from the indwelling catheter","Hepatitis A from the donor liver"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. After the first month, opportunistic infections (CMV, mycobacteria, parasites) and reactivated TB or herpes simplex are the concern.","Incorrect. Wound infections are an early complication.","Incorrect. UTIs from catheters are early, while lines and tubes are in.","Incorrect. Not a recognized pattern; donors are screened."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Early infections come from the surgery itself (lines, tubes, immobility); later, steady immunosuppression lets organisms a healthy immune system controls — CMV, mycobacteria, parasites — cause disease or reactivate.","rule":"Infection after liver transplant: early = pneumonia, wound, UTI; after 1 month = opportunistic (CMV), with TB and HSV reactivation; prevent with prophylaxis, vaccines, early line removal and handwashing."}],"studio":"liver-transplant"},{"id":"gallbladder","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Gallbladder disease: obstruction, infection, surgery and drains","notes":["51-overview","51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","51-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder: Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones; Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall; Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene; Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)","Provider exam signs: Blumberg = rebound tenderness; Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation; Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts","Priority problems: 1 Pain · 2 Weight loss; Goal pain 2–3/10; Not controlled → lap chole","Why obstructed bile gives jaundice, itching, clay stools and dark urine: Bile salts in skin → itching; No bilirubin in colon → clay stools; Kidneys clear bilirubin → dark urine; No bile → steatorrhea, ↓ fat-soluble vitamins"],"checks":["Explain why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder in Gallbladder disease.","Explain provider exam signs in Gallbladder disease.","Explain priority problems in Gallbladder disease."],"recallAnswers":["Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones; Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall; Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene; Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)","Blumberg = rebound tenderness; Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation; Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts","1 Pain · 2 Weight loss; Goal pain 2–3/10; Not controlled → lap chole"],"requires":[],"terms":["cholecystitis","cholelithiasis","cholecystectomy","ursodiol"],"questions":[{"id":"51|trad|0","sourceId":"51|c|0","q":"A client has obstructive jaundice caused by gallstones. Which assessment findings would the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","o":["Icterus","Clay-colored stools","Dark urine","Peritonitis","Urinary retention"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Yellow sclerae from bilirubin backing up.","Correct. No bilirubin reaches the colon to make urobilinogen.","Correct. The kidneys clear the extra bilirubin.","Incorrect. Peritonitis follows perforation, not obstruction itself.","Incorrect. Not related."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Blocked duct → bilirubin into the blood (icterus, jaundice), none into the gut (clay stools), out by the kidneys (dark urine).","rule":"Obstructive jaundice: jaundice, icterus, pruritus, clay stools, dark urine, steatorrhea."},{"id":"51|trad|5","sourceId":"51|c|5","q":"A client is scheduled for a hepatobiliary (HIDA) scan. Which teaching should the nurse include?","o":["Stay NPO before the test; the camera is large and close to the body","Drink barium the night before","Expect to be awake through an incision","Avoid all fluids for 24 hours afterward"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. NPO before; a gamma camera tracks the tracer about 20 minutes after IV injection.","Incorrect. Barium is for GI x-rays.","Incorrect. It is a noninvasive nuclear scan.","Incorrect. No fluid restriction afterward."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The liver takes up the tracer and excretes it into bile → the camera watches it enter and leave the gallbladder → low ejection = disease or obstruction.","rule":"HIDA: NPO before, IV tracer, large camera close to the body; decreased bile flow = obstruction."},{"id":"51|trad|6","sourceId":"51|c|6","q":"Which question is most important for the nurse to ask a client before a magnetic resonance cholangiopancreatography (MRCP)?","o":["“Do you have a pacemaker or other implanted device?”","“Are you allergic to iodine or shellfish?”","“Have you had a barium study this week?”","“Do you take vitamin C?”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. MRI is contraindicated with a pacemaker or other incompatible devices.","Incorrect. Gadolinium has no iodine (though ask about hives or other allergies).","Incorrect. Not the safety priority.","Incorrect. Not relevant."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The MRI magnet can move, heat or disrupt metal and electronic devices → pacemakers and some implants are contraindicated.","rule":"MRCP: ask about pacemakers and implants (MRI) and allergies (hives); gadolinium has no iodine."},{"id":"51|trad|7","sourceId":"51|c|7","q":"A client with cholecystitis receives ketorolac. Which finding should the nurse report immediately?","o":["Increasing pain with tachycardia and a falling blood pressure","Pain decreased from 6 to 3","Mild drowsiness","A dry mouth"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Ketorolac is a potent NSAID that can cause GI bleeding.","Incorrect. The drug is working.","Incorrect. Not the danger sign.","Incorrect. Not the danger sign."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"NSAIDs block protective prostaglandins in the stomach lining → ulcers can bleed → tachycardia and hypotension.","rule":"Ketorolac: watch for increased pain, tachycardia and hypotension (GI bleeding)."},{"id":"51|trad|8","sourceId":"51|c|8","q":"A client is starting ursodiol to dissolve gallstones. Which instruction is correct?","o":["Take it with food and milk and report diarrhea, vomiting or severe abdominal pain","Take it on an empty stomach once a month","Stop it once the pain is gone","No follow-up imaging is needed"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Report diarrhea, vomiting or severe pain (especially radiating to the shoulders); take with food and milk.","Incorrect. It is long-term therapy with food.","Incorrect. Stones dissolve slowly; it is continued as prescribed.","Incorrect. Gallbladder ultrasound every 6 months in the first year."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Ursodiol lowers the cholesterol saturation of bile → cholesterol slowly leaves the stones → effect is checked by ultrasound.","rule":"Ursodiol/chenodiol: long term, with food and milk; ultrasound every 6 months the first year; report diarrhea, vomiting, severe pain."},{"id":"51|trad|9","sourceId":"51|c|9","q":"A client goes home with an external-only biliary drain. Which teaching is correct?","o":["Keep the bag lower than the insertion site; report if drainage decreases","Keep the bag above the waist so bile doesn’t leak","Clamp the tube at night","Expect drainage to stop within a day"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Gravity drainage; a reduction in bile drainage means the drain may not be working.","Incorrect. Above the site would stop drainage.","Incorrect. External-only drains aren’t clamped.","Incorrect. Decreased drainage is a warning sign."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bile flows downhill → the bag must sit below the site → a sudden drop in output means a block or dislodgment → bile backs up → jaundice.","rule":"Biliary drain: bag below the site; less drainage = not working; with an internal/external drain, jaundice or leakage → reconnect to a bag and get a cholangiogram."}],"studio":"gallbladder"},{"id":"acute-pancreatitis","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Acute pancreatitis: systemic risk and safe nutrition","notes":["51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["How it starts: Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released; Duct pressure → rupture → spill; 4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation","Vital signs and lungs: Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP; Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea","Priorities: 1 Pain · 2 Nutrition; ABCs + oxygen first; Pain goal 2–3/10; severe → ICU","Why: Fatty acids + calcium = soap (like soap scum); Calcium locked in dead fat → unusable; Watch twitching, numbness, irritability; expect IV calcium","Do not automatically withhold food solely to “rest the pancreas.” NICE recommends oral intake unless a clear reason prevents it; moderately severe/severe cases need enteral nutrition started within 72 hours. Individualize fluids, pain control and nutrition to the clinical course and prescribed plan."],"checks":["Explain how it starts in Acute pancreatitis.","Explain vital signs and lungs in Acute pancreatitis.","Explain priorities in Acute pancreatitis."],"recallAnswers":["Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released; Duct pressure → rupture → spill; 4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation","Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP; Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea","1 Pain · 2 Nutrition; ABCs + oxygen first; Pain goal 2–3/10; severe → ICU"],"requires":[],"terms":["pancreatitis","lipase","hypocalcemia"],"questions":[{"id":"51|trad|1","sourceId":"51|c|1","q":"Which serum laboratory findings would the nurse expect for a client with acute pancreatitis? Select all that apply.","o":["Elevated amylase","Elevated lipase","Increased leukocytes","Decreased glucose","Decreased elastase","Decreased trypsin"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Leaks from injured acinar cells.","Correct. Rises later and lasts longer.","Correct. Inflammation raises the WBC.","Incorrect. Glucose rises — injured islets release less insulin.","Incorrect. Elastase rises.","Incorrect. Trypsin rises."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Injured cells release amylase, lipase, trypsin and elastase into the blood → all rise; less insulin → glucose rises; inflammation → leukocytosis.","rule":"Acute pancreatitis: ↑ amylase, lipase, trypsin, elastase, glucose, WBC, CRP, bilirubin and ALT (biliary); ↓ calcium and magnesium."},{"id":"51|trad|10","sourceId":"51|c|10","q":"Which position should the nurse help a client with acute pancreatitis assume to ease the pain?","o":["Side-lying with the knees drawn up to the chest","Supine and flat","Prone","Trendelenburg"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The fetal position (or sitting and leaning forward) eases the pain.","Incorrect. Lying supine worsens it.","Incorrect. Presses on the inflamed area.","Incorrect. Not helpful and impairs breathing."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The pancreas lies behind the stomach → flexing the spine and knees takes stretch and pressure off the inflamed retroperitoneal tissue.","rule":"Pancreatitis comfort: fetal position or sitting leaning forward; PCA opioids; mouth and nares care."},{"id":"51|trad|11","sourceId":"51|c|11","q":"A client with acute pancreatitis has had paralytic ileus with an NG tube. Which finding best shows that peristalsis has returned?","o":["The client has passed flatus","Bowel sounds are heard in all four quadrants","The NG drainage turned green","The client is hungry"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Passing flatus or a stool is the most reliable sign.","Incorrect. Bowel sounds aren’t a reliable indicator.","Incorrect. Bile in the drainage doesn’t mean peristalsis is back.","Incorrect. Not reliable."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Sounds can come from small, uncoordinated movements → only flatus or stool shows coordinated peristalsis moving contents to the end of the bowel.","rule":"After ileus: flatus or a BM = peristalsis is back (most reliable); bowel sounds are not."},{"id":"51|trad|16","sourceId":"51|c|16","q":"Which task can the RN delegate to assistive personnel for a client with acute pancreatitis who is NPO with an NG tube?","o":["Provide frequent mouth and nares care","Assess bowel sounds and ask about flatus","Adjust the PCA settings","Evaluate the client’s calcium level"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Oral and nares hygiene keep membranes moist and prevent parotitis — within AP scope.","Incorrect. Assessment is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Medication management is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Interpreting labs is the RN’s."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"NPO and drying drugs dry the mouth → crusting and inflammation → parotitis → frequent mouth and nares care prevents it.","rule":"Delegate hygiene and routine measurements; keep assessment, medications and judgment."}],"clinicalUpdate":"Do not automatically withhold food solely to “rest the pancreas.” NICE recommends oral intake unless a clear reason prevents it; moderately severe/severe cases need enteral nutrition started within 72 hours. Individualize fluids, pain control and nutrition to the clinical course and prescribed plan.","studio":"acute-pancreatitis"},{"id":"chronic-pancreatitis","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Chronic pancreatitis: enzyme loss, pain and nutrition","notes":["51-chronic-pancreatitis"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Enzymes work only with food in the gut; → every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase); Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working","Other signs: Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass; Breathing problems; Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine; 3 Ps of diabetes","Loss of function: Steatorrhea: pale, bulky, frothy, foul; Weight loss, muscle wasting, starvation edema; Diabetes (lost islets)"],"checks":["Explain why in Chronic pancreatitis.","Explain other signs in Chronic pancreatitis.","Explain loss of function in Chronic pancreatitis."],"recallAnswers":["Enzymes work only with food in the gut; → every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase); Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working","Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass; Breathing problems; Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine; 3 Ps of diabetes","Steatorrhea: pale, bulky, frothy, foul; Weight loss, muscle wasting, starvation edema; Diabetes (lost islets)"],"requires":[],"terms":["pancrelipase","steatorrhea","malabsorption"],"questions":[{"id":"51|trad|12","sourceId":"51|c|12","q":"A client with chronic pancreatitis is receiving TPN. How often does the textbook direct the nurse to check fingerstick glucose?","o":["Every 2 to 4 hours","Once a day","Once a week","Only if the client feels shaky"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Clients on TPN are especially prone to hyperglycemia; regular insulin is added to the solution.","Incorrect. Too infrequent.","Incorrect. Too infrequent.","Incorrect. Hyperglycemia may cause no symptoms early."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys islet cells → less insulin → TPN’s hypertonic glucose → hyperglycemia.","rule":"Chronic pancreatitis on TPN: fingerstick glucose every 2–4 hours; regular insulin in the bag as prescribed."},{"id":"51|trad|13","sourceId":"51|c|13","q":"Which statement by a client taking pancrelipase shows a need for more teaching?","o":["“I’ll crush the capsules so they’re easier to swallow.”","“I take them with every meal and snack.”","“My stools should become fewer and less greasy.”","“I’ll tell my provider if my stools stay oily.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct — this needs correcting. Crushing destroys the delayed-release coating; acid then inactivates the lipase.","Incorrect. Correct statement.","Incorrect. Correct statement.","Incorrect. Correct statement."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Enteric coating protects enzymes through the stomach → crushing exposes them to acid → they are destroyed before reaching the duodenum.","rule":"Pancrelipase: don’t chew or crush; take with all meals and snacks; judge by stools."}],"studio":"chronic-pancreatitis"},{"id":"pancreatic-cancer","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure","notes":["51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it is deadly: Leading cancer killer; Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival","Signs: Jaundice first = advanced; Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia; New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance; Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue","Blood clots: VTE common: tumor is thromboplastic; + immobility, surgery","Why: Head tumor sits on the bile duct → small tumor still blocks it; Jaundice before pain; But usually already spread → late sign"],"checks":["Explain why it is deadly in Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure.","Explain signs in Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure.","Explain blood clots in Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure."],"recallAnswers":["Leading cancer killer; Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival","Jaundice first = advanced; Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia; New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance; Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue","VTE common: tumor is thromboplastic; + immobility, surgery"],"requires":[],"terms":["pancreaticoduodenectomy","anastomosis","jaundice"],"questions":[{"id":"51|trad|14","sourceId":"51|c|14","q":"A client with advanced pancreatic cancer has severe pain. Which approach is best?","o":["Give scheduled high-dose opioids before the pain escalates","Give opioids only when pain reaches 10/10","Limit opioids to prevent dependency","Use only acetaminophen"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Morphine is usually given on schedule before pain peaks; with this prognosis, dependency isn’t a consideration.","Incorrect. Waiting lets pain escalate and is harder to control.","Incorrect. Dependency is not the concern.","Incorrect. Not enough for severe cancer pain."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Tumor invades nerves and organs → constant pain → steady opioid levels keep it controlled better than chasing peaks.","rule":"Pancreatic cancer: palliative care; scheduled high-dose opioids before pain peaks; radiation or stents may also relieve pain."},{"id":"51|trad|15","sourceId":"51|c|15","q":"Which drain finding after a Whipple procedure should the nurse report immediately?","o":["Clear, colorless, bile-tinged fluid with increasing output","Serosanguineous drainage of 30 mL in 4 hours","Drainage that decreases each day","A drain secured without tension"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It suggests disruption or leakage of an anastomosis.","Incorrect. Expected.","Incorrect. Expected as healing proceeds.","Incorrect. Correct care."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A breaking anastomosis leaks pancreatic juice or bile → clear or bile-colored fluid replaces bloody serous drainage → output rises.","rule":"Whipple drains: serosanguineous is expected; clear, colorless, bile-tinged or frank blood with rising output → report now. Keep drains dependent without kinks."}],"studio":"pancreatic-cancer"},{"id":"nutrition-screen","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Nutrition: assess intake, weight change and barriers to eating","notes":["52-overview","52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","52-adpie"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why starvation eats muscle: Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein; Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain; = muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections","What a nutrition assessment includes: History, intake record, access; Labs, food-drug interactions; Exam, measurements, psychosocial","Priority problem: Weight loss: can't access, ingest, digest or absorb","Why albumin falls and edema appears: Albumin holds water in vessels; ↓ Protein → ↓ albumin → edema (can hide wasting); Prealbumin changes faster than albumin"],"checks":["Explain why starvation eats muscle in Nutrition.","Explain what a nutrition assessment includes in Nutrition.","Explain priority problem in Nutrition."],"recallAnswers":["Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein; Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain; = muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections","History, intake record, access; Labs, food-drug interactions; Exam, measurements, psychosocial","Weight loss: can't access, ingest, digest or absorb"],"requires":[],"terms":["malnutrition","cachexia","glossitis"],"questions":[{"id":"52|trad|4","sourceId":"52|c|4","q":"Which instruction should the nurse give assistive personnel about daily weights?","o":["Use the same scale at the same time each morning before breakfast, with the same clothing","Weigh after breakfast so the client isn’t hungry","Use whichever scale is closest","Let the client report their usual weight"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Consistency makes the trend meaningful; weight is the most reliable indicator of fluid change.","Incorrect. Before breakfast is preferred.","Incorrect. Scales differ.","Incorrect. Patients tend to underestimate weight — measure it."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A liter of fluid weighs a kilogram → small technique differences (scale, clothing, meal) can look like fluid change.","rule":"Weigh: same scale, same time (before breakfast), same clothing, no shoes; bed scale → record linens and lift lines."},{"id":"52|trad|5","sourceId":"52|c|5","q":"Which client’s weight change needs evaluation for undernutrition?","o":["Lost 4 kg in 1 month from 70 kg without trying","Lost 1 kg in 6 months from 80 kg","Gained 0.5 kg over a holiday week","Lost 2 kg on a planned diet over 3 months"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. 4 ÷ 70 ≈ 5.7% in a month — over the 5%-in-a-month threshold.","Incorrect. About 1% in 6 months.","Incorrect. A small gain.","Incorrect. Intentional and modest."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Unintended loss means intake isn’t meeting needs → the body is breaking down its own tissue.","rule":"Unintentional weight loss of 5% in 30 days or 10% in 6 months significantly affects nutrition — evaluate."},{"id":"52|trad|17","sourceId":"52|c|17","q":"An undernourished client is prescribed an iron supplement. Which teaching is correct?","o":["Take it immediately before or during meals, and expect constipation","Take it with an antacid to protect the stomach","Take it at bedtime on an empty stomach","Stop it if your stools turn dark"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The book’s timing; iron tends to cause constipation — teach fiber, fluids and exercise.","Incorrect. Antacids reduce iron absorption.","Incorrect. Not the book’s instruction.","Incorrect. Dark stools are expected with iron."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Unabsorbed iron stays in the bowel → firms and darkens the stool → constipation.","rule":"Iron: just before or with meals; expect constipation and dark stools; prevent constipation with fiber, fluids, exercise. Zinc can cause nausea and vomiting."},{"id":"52|trad|18","sourceId":"52|c|18","q":"A client who follows a vegan diet asks about nutrition. Which teaching is most important?","o":["Include a daily source of vitamin B12, such as a fortified cereal or soy drink","Avoid all fortified foods","Eat fish twice a week","Take extra vitamin C to prevent anemia"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Vegans can develop anemia from vitamin B12 deficiency.","Incorrect. Fortified foods supply the B12.","Incorrect. Vegans eat no fish (that is pescatarian).","Incorrect. B12 is the concern."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Vitamin B12 is needed to make DNA in new red cells → too little → megaloblastic anemia.","rule":"Vegan: daily B12 source (fortified cereal, soy drink, meat substitute). Ask about cultural and personal food preferences — never assume."},{"id":"52|trad|19","sourceId":"52|c|19","q":"A client says, “I’m allergic to milk.” What should the nurse do next?","o":["Ask exactly what reaction the client has after drinking milk","Document a milk allergy and order an EpiPen","Reassure the client that milk allergies are harmless","Tell the client to drink milk slowly"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Bloating, gas and diarrhea suggest lactose intolerance; swelling, wheezing or anaphylaxis suggests a true allergy.","Incorrect. Assess before documenting and planning.","Incorrect. A true allergy can cause anaphylaxis.","Incorrect. Doesn’t address the question."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Allergy = immune system (IgE) → swelling, anaphylaxis. Intolerance = too little lactase → undigested lactose draws water and feeds gut bacteria → bloating, diarrhea.","rule":"Food reactions: ask the specific reaction; allergies (nuts, peanuts, shellfish) can be life-threatening; intolerances are GI."},{"id":"52|trad|20","sourceId":"52|c|20","q":"Which task can the RN delegate to assistive personnel for an undernourished client?","o":["Record the percentage of each meal eaten and report choking","Complete the Mini Nutritional Assessment","Teach the client about supplements","Decide whether the client needs a dietitian"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Intake records, calorie counts and height and weight are within AP scope.","Incorrect. Assessment is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Teaching is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Clinical judgment is the RN’s."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Accurate intake data shows whether calories are actually reaching the client → the RN interprets them.","rule":"Delegate height, weight, I&O and calorie counts; the RN assesses, teaches and refers."}],"studio":"nutrition-screen"},{"id":"enteral-nutrition","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Tube feeding: placement, aspiration prevention and medication safety","notes":["52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: One danger: formula in the lungs; X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying; Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell","Ethics: Advance directive?; None → durable power of attorney decides; Neither → ethics committee (consensus first); DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA","Short-term tubes: Nasoenteric (Dobhoff) < 4 weeks: NG, ND, NJ; Small-bore < 12 Fr"],"checks":["Explain why in Tube feeding.","Explain ethics in Tube feeding.","Explain short-term tubes in Tube feeding."],"recallAnswers":["One danger: formula in the lungs; X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying; Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell","Advance directive?; None → durable power of attorney decides; Neither → ethics committee (consensus first); DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA","Nasoenteric (Dobhoff) < 4 weeks: NG, ND, NJ; Small-bore < 12 Fr"],"requires":[],"terms":["enteral","gastrostomy","aspiration"],"questions":[{"id":"52|trad|6","sourceId":"52|c|6","q":"A small-bore feeding tube has just been inserted. How should the nurse confirm placement before the first feeding?","o":["Obtain an x-ray","Listen over the stomach while injecting air","Test the pH of the aspirate","Ask the client to speak"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. X-ray is the most accurate method and should always be done on initial placement.","Incorrect. The book says not to rely on auscultation.","Incorrect. The book says not to rely on pH testing either.","Incorrect. Not reliable."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A tube can pass into the trachea without obvious signs → only imaging shows the tip → formula into the lung can be fatal.","rule":"Tube placement: x-ray first, mark the exit point, recheck the mark at each assessment; if in doubt, x-ray again; suspect dislodgment → remove."},{"id":"52|trad|7","sourceId":"52|c|7","q":"During routine care, the nurse cannot rotate a client’s gastrostomy tube. What should the nurse do?","o":["Notify the provider immediately and apply a dry sterile dressing","Rotate it more forcefully","Pull it out a little and retape it","Document it and check again tomorrow"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The retention disk may be embedded in the tissue.","Incorrect. Force can injure the stomach wall.","Incorrect. Moving it could dislodge it.","Incorrect. It needs prompt evaluation."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A disk pulled tight against the stomach wall erodes into it → the tube becomes fixed → it can’t be used safely.","rule":"G/J tubes: rotate 360° daily with about ¼ inch of play; if it can’t move → notify; dry sterile dressing changed at least daily."},{"id":"52|trad|8","sourceId":"52|c|8","q":"Which flushing practice is correct for a client receiving tube feedings and several medications?","o":["Flush with 30 mL of water using at least a 30-mL syringe before and after medications","Flush with 5 mL using a 10-mL syringe for more pressure","Mix all medications together, then flush once","Flush only when the feeding is stopped for the day"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Also every 4 hours and after any interruption; give drugs separately.","Incorrect. Small syringes create high pressure that can rupture the tube.","Incorrect. Flush between drugs — mixing them causes clogs.","Incorrect. Too infrequent."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Drug residue and formula proteins clump in a narrow tube → regular water flushes clear it; a small syringe generates enough pressure to burst it.","rule":"Flush 30 mL of water with a ≥ 30-mL syringe: every 4 hr, before and after drugs, after interruptions; liquid drugs when possible; don’t crush SR drugs."}],"studio":"enteral-nutrition"},{"id":"parenteral-nutrition","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Parenteral nutrition: line, glucose and infusion safety","notes":["52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Into a vein when the gut can't be used; Can give all nutrients","Compounded vs multichamber bags: Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days; Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized; ASPEN: premixed / multichamber","Line care: Tubing q24h; Dressing: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days","Why: TPN = concentrated glucose; insulin rises to match; Too fast → glucose + fluid surge; Stopped suddenly → hypoglycemia → bridge with D10W/D20W"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Parenteral nutrition.","Explain compounded vs multichamber bags in Parenteral nutrition.","Explain line care in Parenteral nutrition."],"recallAnswers":["Into a vein when the gut can't be used; Can give all nutrients","Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days; Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized; ASPEN: premixed / multichamber","Tubing q24h; Dressing: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days"],"requires":[],"terms":["parenteral","hyperglycemia","refeeding syndrome"],"questions":[{"id":"52|trad|10","sourceId":"52|c|10","q":"A client’s TPN bag is almost empty and the pharmacy says the next bag is delayed. What should the nurse anticipate?","o":["Hanging D10W or D20W as prescribed until the TPN arrives","Stopping all IV fluids until the bag arrives","Hanging normal saline at the TPN rate","Giving an oral glucose tablet"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Dextrose keeps glucose steady while the client’s insulin level is adjusted to TPN.","Incorrect. Stopping suddenly risks rebound hypoglycemia.","Incorrect. Provides no glucose.","Incorrect. Not a substitute for a continuous infusion."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The pancreas has matched insulin to steady high glucose → an abrupt stop leaves insulin unopposed → hypoglycemia.","rule":"TPN interruption: collaborate with the provider for D10W or D20W until TPN is available."},{"id":"52|trad|11","sourceId":"52|c|11","q":"A critically ill client receiving an IV fat emulsion develops fever and rising triglycerides with oozing at the IV site. What should the nurse do first?","o":["Stop the lipid infusion and report the changes","Slow the lipid infusion by half","Give acetaminophen and continue","Recheck the triglycerides tomorrow"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. These suggest fat overload syndrome — discontinue and report immediately.","Incorrect. The infusion must stop.","Incorrect. Treats the fever, not the cause.","Incorrect. Delay can lead to multisystem organ failure."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"More fat than the body can clear → triglycerides rise → fat particles disturb clotting and organ function → fever, bleeding, organ failure.","rule":"Lipids: watch for fever, ↑ triglycerides, clotting problems, multisystem failure (fat overload) → stop and report."}],"studio":"parenteral-nutrition"},{"id":"weight-bariatric","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery: individualized care","notes":["52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after"],"source":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation; Excess fat but often poor nutrients","History: Appetite, attitude to food; Drugs, activity, family history; Past attempts and results","Priority problem and setting: Weight gain stressing organs; Bariatric room: single, 18 m², 60-in door, 5-ft clearance; Low-air-loss bariatric bed; 1000-lb toilet, bars, lift; Right-sized gowns, BP cuffs, SCDs","Why: Small BP cuff → falsely high reading; Wrong-size SCDs, gowns → unsafe care; Right-sized equipment = accurate + safe"],"checks":["Explain what it is in Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery.","Explain history in Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery.","Explain priority problem and setting in Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery."],"recallAnswers":["Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation; Excess fat but often poor nutrients","Appetite, attitude to food; Drugs, activity, family history; Past attempts and results","Weight gain stressing organs; Bariatric room: single, 18 m², 60-in door, 5-ft clearance; Low-air-loss bariatric bed; 1000-lb toilet, bars, lift; Right-sized gowns, BP cuffs, SCDs"],"requires":[],"terms":["orlistat","semaglutide","bariatric"],"questions":[{"id":"52|trad|1","sourceId":"52|c|1","q":"Which information should the nurse include in discharge teaching after bariatric surgery? Select all that apply.","o":["Report back, shoulder or abdominal pain to the surgeon","Each reintroduced meal should contain about 5 tablespoons of food","Stay hydrated by drinking fruit juice","Solid food can be added in a week","Little urine output is normal for the first few weeks"],"a":[0,1],"r":["Correct. These can signal an anastomotic leak.","Correct. Pureed meals of about 5 tablespoons protect the small pouch.","Incorrect. Sugary drinks cause dumping syndrome.","Incorrect. Pureed foods come at about a week; solids around week 8.","Incorrect. Low urine output can mean dehydration or a leak."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"A small pouch and fresh anastomosis need small volumes; sugar draws water into the jejunum (dumping); referred pain and low output may mean a leak.","rule":"Bariatric discharge: diet stages, supplements, fluids (not juice), report fever, vomiting, back/shoulder/epigastric pain, wound changes, leg swelling, chest pain, dyspnea."},{"id":"52|trad|3","sourceId":"52|c|3","q":"A client says, “I keep trying to lose weight, but I can’t because I am genetically obese.” Which responses are appropriate? Select all that apply.","o":["“Genes can contribute to obesity.”","“Let’s talk about your family history with weight.”","“What are your feelings about increasing physical activity?”","“It would be good for us to discuss your usual nutritional intake.”","“Have you considered bariatric surgery?”"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. It acknowledges the client’s view honestly.","Correct. Gathers relevant history.","Correct. Explores what the client can control.","Correct. Explores intake — lifestyle still matters a great deal.","Incorrect. Jumps to surgery without assessment and dismisses the client’s concern."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Most obesity is polygenic — many genes amplified by an environment that favors weight gain → lifestyle changes still shift the balance.","rule":"Obesity communication: RESPECT, acknowledge genetics, focus on modifiable factors (activity, intake), explore motivation."},{"id":"52|trad|13","sourceId":"52|c|13","q":"Before a client starts phentermine-topiramate, which question is most important?","o":["“Are you pregnant or planning to become pregnant?”","“Do you have a history of hypothyroidism?”","“Do you eat a high-fiber diet?”","“Do you exercise in the morning?”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It can cause birth defects; it also shouldn’t be used while breastfeeding.","Incorrect. Hyperthyroidism (and glaucoma) are the contraindications.","Incorrect. Not a safety issue for this drug.","Incorrect. Not relevant."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Topiramate crosses the placenta and can harm fetal development → pregnancy must be ruled out and prevented.","rule":"Phentermine-topiramate: not in pregnancy or breastfeeding; not with glaucoma or hyperthyroidism."},{"id":"52|trad|14","sourceId":"52|c|14","q":"A client taking semaglutide for weight loss calls with severe, persistent abdominal pain radiating to the back. What should the nurse advise?","o":["Seek medical evaluation now — this could be pancreatitis","Take the next dose with food","Expect this as normal fullness","Double the water intake and wait a week"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. GLP-1 agonists carry a risk of pancreatitis (ALT and AST are monitored).","Incorrect. Doesn’t address a possible emergency.","Incorrect. Fullness is expected; severe pain to the back is not.","Incorrect. Delays evaluation."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"GLP-1 drugs are linked to pancreatitis → enzymes activate inside the pancreas → boring pain to the back.","rule":"Semaglutide/liraglutide: injection technique; report severe abdominal pain (pancreatitis); semaglutide rarely thyroid tumors; liraglutide + insulin → hypoglycemia (per the book)."},{"id":"52|trad|15","sourceId":"52|c|15","q":"A client with a BMI of 52 and a short, thick neck returns from bariatric surgery. What is the nurse’s priority?","o":["Airway management","Pain control","Skin care in the folds","Starting clear liquids"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Short, thick necks compromise the airway; aggressive respiratory support, even a ventilator, may be needed.","Incorrect. Important, but after the airway.","Incorrect. Important later.","Incorrect. Not before the airway is secure."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Excess soft tissue + anesthesia and opioids → airway collapses and breathing slows → hypoxemia; semi-Fowler and CPAP keep it open.","rule":"After bariatric surgery: airway first — semi-Fowler, SpO₂, oxygen, CPAP/BiPAP; then SCDs, anticoagulant, skin, mobility."},{"id":"52|trad|16","sourceId":"52|c|16","q":"A client has an NG tube after an open gastroplasty. The tube seems to have slipped out slightly. What should the nurse do?","o":["Leave it in place, check patency, and notify the surgeon","Push it back in to the original mark","Pull it out and insert a new one","Irrigate it forcefully"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Never reposition the NG after bariatric surgery — movement can disrupt the suture line.","Incorrect. Repositioning can tear the staple or suture line.","Incorrect. Reinsertion risks perforating the pouch.","Incorrect. Force can disrupt the anastomosis."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The tube lies against a new staple or suture line → any movement can tear it → leak and peritonitis.","rule":"After gastroplasty: monitor NG patency, never reposition it; it is removed around day 2 if the client passes flatus."}],"studio":"weight-bariatric"},{"id":"resp-prevention","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Protect the lungs: exposures, smoking and prevention","notes":["22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","22-adpie","22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Prevention means stopping the injury before cells change. Most respiratory questions here come down to smoke, vaccines and exposures.","Findings: Smoking / vaping history; Occupational dust, fumes, asbestos, radon; Secondhand smoke; Missed vaccines","Nursing actions: 5 A's: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange; Screen every visit; Offer counseling + meds together","Hemoptysis, weight loss or a changed chronic cough in a smoker → workup for lung cancer."],"checks":["Explain why in Protect the lungs.","Explain ¶ respiratory assessment always answers the same in Protect the lungs.","Explain procedure care in Protect the lungs."],"recallAnswers":["Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential; 5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages; \"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it","Same order every time: airway open? → air moving? → reaching alveoli that exchange?; Applies to any breathing problem, before the diagnosis is known","NPO until gag returns after throat anesthesia; After bronchoscopy: VS, breath sounds, SpO₂ every 15 min × 2 h; Thoracentesis: position + coach → CXR, watch for pneumothorax; After lung biopsy: check q4h × 24 h; ↓/absent breath sounds = report now"],"requires":[],"terms":["nicotine","hypoxia"],"questions":[{"id":"learn|prevention-risk","q":"An adult who smokes works with airborne dust. Which assessment best supports a lung-protection plan?","o":["Smoking and vaping exposure, workplace protection, symptoms and readiness to change","Only whether the patient has a cough today","A promise that a normal SpO₂ excludes lung damage"],"a":[0],"r":["Exposure and readiness guide a practical prevention plan.","A symptom snapshot misses cumulative exposure and prevention needs.","A normal saturation does not exclude lung disease or future risk."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|prevention-risk"},{"id":"learn|prevention-plan","q":"A patient wants to stop smoking but has relapsed before. Which response supports an actionable plan?","o":["“We can discuss triggers, counseling and prescribed cessation treatment, then arrange follow-up.”","“One relapse proves treatment will not help.”","“Switch to an unregulated inhaled product; it is risk-free.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Combine support, treatment options and follow-up around the patient’s goals.","Relapse is a reason to adjust support, not abandon treatment.","Inhaled alternatives are not risk-free; assess exposures and evidence-based treatment."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|prevention-plan"}],"studio":"resp-prevention"},{"id":"pack-years","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Pack-years: calculate the exposure, then assess the person","notes":["22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Packs per day × years smoked = pack-years. It measures the cumulative dose of carcinogens and oxidants.","Findings: 1 ppd × 20 yr = 20; 1.5 ppd × 20 yr = 30; 2 ppd × 15 yr = 30; 10 cigarettes/day × 40 yr = ½ × 40 = 20","Nursing actions: Convert cigarettes to packs (20 = 1 pack); Multiply by years smoked; Document in history","Higher pack-years = higher COPD and lung cancer risk; screen eligible patients."],"checks":["Explain why in Pack-years.","Explain quantify it in Pack-years.","Explain catch the social smoker in Pack-years."],"recallAnswers":["Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential; 5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages; \"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it","Pack-years = packs/day × years; Ask separately: secondhand (same space) + thirdhand (smoke on hair, clothes, surfaces)","Ask: \"Do you ever smoke in social situations?\"; They don't call themselves smokers — still damage + addiction"],"requires":[],"terms":["pack-years"],"questions":[{"id":"learn|pack-years-one","q":"A patient smoked 1.5 packs per day for 20 years. What is the smoking exposure?","o":["30 pack-years","21.5 pack-years","13.3 pack-years"],"a":[0],"r":["Packs per day × years: 1.5 × 20 = 30.","Adding the two values does not calculate cumulative exposure.","Dividing years by packs per day uses the wrong relationship."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|pack-years-one"},{"id":"learn|pack-years-two","q":"A patient smoked 10 cigarettes daily for 12 years, then 20 daily for 8 years. A pack contains 20 cigarettes. What is the total exposure?","o":["14 pack-years","20 pack-years","28 pack-years"],"a":[0],"r":["0.5 × 12 + 1 × 8 = 6 + 8 = 14 pack-years.","Twenty is the number of years; the daily amount varied.","Convert cigarettes to packs before multiplying each interval."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|pack-years-two"}],"studio":"pack-years"},{"id":"resp-aging","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Aging lungs: less reserve, closer observation","notes":["22-overview","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["A stiffer chest, less recoil and a weaker cough mean more trapped air, more aspiration and late warning signs.","Findings: ↑ AP diameter, kyphosis; Weaker cough; Dyspnea with exertion; Lower SpO₂ baseline","Nursing actions: Incentive spirometer, turn, ambulate; Upright for meals; Watch for silent aspiration","New confusion in an older adult can be the first sign of pneumonia or hypoxia."],"checks":["Explain why in Aging lungs.","Explain lower airway in Aging lungs.","Explain alveolar-capillary membrane in Aging lungs."],"recallAnswers":["Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","One cell thick each side; Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less); Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion"],"requires":[],"terms":["alveoli","aspiration"],"questions":[{"id":"learn|aging-reserve","q":"An older adult has weaker cough strength and less elastic recoil. What is the nursing implication?","o":["Assess secretion clearance and tolerance of illness, and support ordered mobility and breathing care","Assume every older adult is hypoxemic","Treat new confusion as an expected aging change"],"a":[0],"r":["Reduced reserve and clearance increase vulnerability during infection or immobility.","Age alone does not establish hypoxemia; use actual findings.","New confusion requires assessment for acute causes such as hypoxia or infection."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|aging-reserve"},{"id":"learn|aging-change","q":"An older adult being treated for pneumonia becomes newly restless and cannot follow instructions. What should the nurse do?","o":["Promptly assess airway, breathing, oxygenation and other acute causes, and escalate deterioration","Wait for cyanosis before reassessing","Attribute the behavior to normal aging"],"a":[0],"r":["An acute mental-status change may signal deterioration and needs prompt assessment.","Cyanosis can be a late or unreliable finding; do not wait for it.","New change from baseline is not explained by age alone."],"phys":"","ex":[],"sourceId":"learn|aging-change"}],"studio":"resp-aging"},{"id":"resp-assessment","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority","notes":["22-overview","22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","Lower airway: Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","Alveolar-capillary membrane: One cell thick each side; Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less); Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion","Why ETCO₂ warns earlier: CO₂ leaves faster than O₂ enters → ETCO₂ changes before hypoxia shows; ↑: hypoventilation, partial obstruction, rebreathing, fever, acidosis; ↓: PE, apnea, total obstruction, tube malposition, cardiac arrest; In a code: tells if CPR is effective + if circulation returned"],"checks":["Explain why in Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority.","Explain lower airway in Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority.","Explain alveolar-capillary membrane in Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority."],"recallAnswers":["Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","One cell thick each side; Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less); Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion"],"requires":[],"terms":["hypoxemia","hypercapnia","oximetry","capnography"],"questions":[{"id":"22|trad|2","sourceId":"22|c|2","q":"Which findings indicate the pulse oximetry reading may be unreliable? Select all that apply.","o":["Cold, poorly perfused fingers","Dark nail polish on the sensor finger","Suspected carbon monoxide poisoning","Client is sitting upright","Severe anemia"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Correct. Poor perfusion weakens the pulsatile signal.","Correct. Polish and artificial nails interfere with light transmission.","Correct. Carboxyhemoglobin is read as oxyhemoglobin, giving a falsely normal value.","Position does not affect accuracy.","Correct. Too little hemoglobin — saturation can be high while oxygen content is low."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Oximeters compare red and infrared light absorption by pulsatile hemoglobin.","rule":"Know when a monitor number cannot be trusted; assess the patient."},{"id":"22|trad|8","sourceId":"22|c|8","q":"Which trend shows a client with dyspnea is deteriorating?","o":["RR 28 → 22, speaking in longer sentences","SpO₂ 90% → 94% after repositioning","RR 30 → 12 with increasing drowsiness and a quiet chest","Accessory muscle use decreasing"],"a":[2],"r":["Improvement.","Improvement.","Correct. A falling rate with drowsiness and less audible air movement means fatigue and CO₂ retention — impending respiratory failure.","Improvement."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Tired respiratory muscles cannot sustain ventilation; CO₂ rises and causes CO₂ narcosis.","rule":"A “calmer” patient with worsening mentation is failing, not improving."},{"id":"22|trad|1","sourceId":"22|c|1","q":"ABG: pH 7.30, PaCO₂ 58 mm Hg, HCO₃⁻ 26 mEq/L. Which interpretation is correct?","o":["Metabolic acidosis","Respiratory acidosis, uncompensated","Respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory acidosis, fully compensated"],"a":[1],"r":["HCO₃⁻ is normal, so the kidney is not the cause.","Correct. Low pH with high CO₂ (opposite directions) and normal HCO₃⁻ = respiratory acidosis without compensation.","CO₂ is high, not low.","Full compensation would bring pH back to 7.35–7.45 with a raised HCO₃⁻."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hypoventilation retains CO₂, which forms carbonic acid and lowers pH.","rule":"ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal."},{"id":"22|ngn|0","sourceId":"22|n|0","q":"Which findings are early indicators of hypoxia in an adult client? Select all that apply.","o":["Restlessness","Tachycardia","Central cyanosis","New confusion","Tachypnea","Bradycardia with hypotension"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Correct. The brain is the most oxygen-sensitive organ, so behavior changes first.","Correct. The heart speeds up to deliver more oxygen per minute.","Incorrect. Cyanosis appears late, when a large amount of hemoglobin is desaturated; waiting for it delays rescue.","Correct. Cerebral hypoxia shows as confusion before color change.","Correct. Chemoreceptors drive faster breathing to raise oxygen and blow off CO₂.","Incorrect. This is a late, decompensated finding that often precedes arrest."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Compensation (sympathetic drive, faster breathing) comes before failure (bradycardia, cyanosis).","rule":"Recognize early cues; do not wait for late signs."},{"id":"22|ngn|2","sourceId":"22|n|2","q":"Review the exhibit. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Increase oxygen to 6 L/min","Stop the PCA, stimulate the client, support ventilation and give naloxone per protocol","Recheck the ABG in 1 hour","Document that SpO₂ is acceptable"],"a":[1],"r":["More oxygen raises SpO₂ but does nothing for hypoventilation; CO₂ keeps rising.","Correct. Opioid-induced respiratory depression with acute respiratory acidosis — remove the cause, stimulate, ventilate, reverse.","Delay in a worsening client is unsafe.","SpO₂ is falsely reassuring on supplemental oxygen."],"ex":[{"h":"Nurses’ notes — post-op day 1","lines":["0200: PCA morphine in use. Client sleeping, rousable to voice.","0400: Difficult to arouse; responds to sternal rub. Snoring respirations."]},{"h":"Vital signs","table":[["Time","RR","SpO₂","HR","O₂"],["0000","16","97%","84","2 L NC"],["0200","12","96%","78","2 L NC"],["0400","7","94%","70","2 L NC"]]},{"h":"ABG 0415","lines":["pH 7.28 · PaCO₂ 60 mm Hg · HCO₃⁻ 25 mEq/L · PaO₂ 78 mm Hg"]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Opioids blunt the medullary response to CO₂; retained CO₂ forms carbonic acid and deepens sedation.","rule":"Ventilation (RR, sedation, CO₂) outranks saturation in opioid safety."}],"studio":"resp-assessment"},{"id":"resp-procedures","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis: before, after, and complications","notes":["22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Two points cover every procedure:; Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs); Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)","Teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours: 24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger; One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis; → ED immediately","Thoracentesis limits and after care: ≤ ~1000 mL at once → avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema; CXR after; Document response, fluid volume + character, specimens, site, resp assessment before/during/after","Why: Every airway procedure: gag reflex + pleural space; Sedation/numbing removes the gag → NPO until it returns; Needle/scope near pleura → new sudden dyspnea after thoracentesis = pneumothorax until proven otherwise"],"checks":["Explain why in Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis.","Explain teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours in Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis.","Explain thoracentesis limits and after care in Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis."],"recallAnswers":["Two points cover every procedure:; Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs); Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)","24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger; One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis; → ED immediately","≤ ~1000 mL at once → avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema; CXR after; Document response, fluid volume + character, specimens, site, resp assessment before/during/after"],"requires":[],"terms":["bronchoscopy","thoracentesis","pneumothorax"],"questions":[{"id":"22|trad|7","sourceId":"22|c|7","q":"After thoracentesis, which finding is expected?","o":["Tracheal deviation away from the puncture side","Mild soreness at the puncture site","Absent breath sounds on the procedure side","Pink frothy sputum"],"a":[1],"r":["Signals tension pneumothorax — emergency.","Correct. Local discomfort is expected.","Suggests pneumothorax.","Suggests re-expansion pulmonary edema."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The lung should re-expand; breath sounds should improve, not disappear.","rule":"Know expected findings so the unexpected stands out."},{"id":"22|trad|0","sourceId":"22|c|0","q":"Which client should the nurse assess first?","o":["Client with COPD, SpO₂ 90% on 2 L/min, speaking in full sentences","Client 2 hours after bronchoscopy asking for a drink of water","Client after thoracentesis with new sudden dyspnea and HR 118","Client with chronic clubbing waiting for PFTs"],"a":[2],"r":["Within the usual COPD target and stable; not the priority.","Needs a gag check before fluids, but is not in distress.","Correct. Sudden dyspnea and tachycardia after thoracentesis suggest pneumothorax — an acute, unstable change.","Clubbing is chronic and expected for this client."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Air entering the pleural space removes negative pressure, collapsing the lung and reducing gas exchange.","rule":"Acute and unexpected beats chronic and expected (ABCs, then acute change)."},{"id":"22|ngn|1","sourceId":"22|n|1","q":"A client returns from bronchoscopy with biopsy under moderate sedation. Which actions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","o":["Keep NPO until gag and swallow reflexes return","Monitor for stridor and wheezing","Report blood-streaked sputum as a hemorrhage","Monitor SpO₂ and level of sedation","Offer ice chips immediately to soothe the throat","Report fever, chest pain or sudden dyspnea"],"a":[0,1,3,5],"r":["Correct. Topical anesthetic blocks the gag reflex; intake before it returns risks aspiration.","Correct. Laryngospasm or bronchospasm can follow airway instrumentation.","Incorrect. Small amounts of blood-streaked sputum are expected after biopsy; frank or increasing bleeding is reported.","Correct. Sedation can depress ventilation.","Incorrect. Ice chips are still oral intake and can be aspirated before the gag returns.","Correct. These suggest pneumothorax or infection."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Anesthetized pharynx + sedation = impaired airway protection and ventilation.","rule":"Protect the airway after procedures that remove protective reflexes."},{"id":"22|ngn|3","sourceId":"22|n|3","q":"Review the exhibit. Which findings require immediate report to the provider? Select all that apply.","o":["Mild soreness at the site","RR 30 with SpO₂ 88%","Absent breath sounds in the right upper field","Trachea midline","Pink frothy sputum"],"a":[1,2,4],"r":["Expected local discomfort.","Correct. New hypoxemia and tachypnea after the procedure.","Correct. Suggests pneumothorax from pleural puncture.","Reassuring — no mediastinal shift yet.","Correct. Suggests re-expansion pulmonary edema after large-volume removal."],"ex":[{"h":"Procedure note","lines":["Right thoracentesis, 1,400 mL straw-colored fluid removed. Client tolerated procedure."]},{"h":"Post-procedure flowsheet (30 min)","table":[["Finding","Value"],["Puncture site","Dry dressing, mild soreness"],["RR / SpO₂","30 / 88% on room air"],["Breath sounds right","Absent in upper field"],["Trachea","Midline"],["Cough","New, with pink frothy sputum"]]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Air in the pleural space collapses lung; rapid re-expansion of a compressed lung can leak fluid into alveoli.","rule":"Distinguish expected post-procedure findings from complications."}],"studio":"resp-procedures"},{"id":"upper-airway","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Upper airway: sleep apnea, bleeding, surgery and tracheostomy","notes":["23-overview","23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","23-adpie"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 23; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["In sleep the throat muscles relax and the tongue and soft palate fall back. The chest keeps pulling against a closed airway until CO₂ rises and the patient partly wakes — over and over, so deep sleep never comes.","Findings: Loud snoring with witnessed pauses, then gasping; Morning headache, dry mouth, daytime sleepiness; Hypertension that doesn't respond to treatment; Retracted jaw, small chin, short thick neck","Nursing actions: CPAP nightly — bring their own machine to the hospital; Side-lying; no sedatives or alcohol at bedtime; After airway surgery: check for narrowing q2 h × 24 h","After airway surgery: stridor, drooling, rising effort or falling SpO₂ means the airway is narrowing — call the Rapid Response Team."],"checks":["Explain why in Upper airway.","Explain warning signs to teach in Upper airway.","Explain nonsurgical management in Upper airway."],"recallAnswers":["Upper airway = one lumen, no backup → losing it is immediate; Lower airway = millions of paths → losses survivable; Every priority answer follows from this","Pain, lump (mouth, throat, neck), dysphagia, color change in mouth; Sore not healed in 2 weeks, unexplained bleeding, numbness, denture fit change; Burning with citrus/hot liquids, one-sided ear pain, hoarseness; Recurrent sore throat, dyspnea, weight loss","Mild: weight loss + position change; Oral appliance (jaw forward) — bulky, mucosa risk; CPAP = mainstay (continuous pressure, tight seal); Drugs don't work: sedatives worsen, stimulants don't fix cause"],"requires":[],"terms":["epistaxis","tracheostomy","apnea"],"questions":[{"id":"23|trad|3","sourceId":"23|c|3","q":"A client with OSA is prescribed an opioid after surgery. Which action is most important?","o":["Remove the CPAP to allow better sleep","Apply CPAP when sleeping and monitor SpO₂ and sedation continuously","Give the opioid only if the client asks","Place the client supine"],"a":[1],"r":["CPAP must continue.","Correct. Opioids worsen airway collapse and depress drive; CPAP and continuous monitoring reduce risk.","Pain still needs treatment; the issue is monitoring.","Supine worsens obstruction; side-lying or upright is better."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Opioids reduce CO₂ responsiveness and pharyngeal muscle tone.","rule":"High-risk drug + high-risk airway = continuous monitoring."},{"id":"23|trad|5","sourceId":"23|c|5","q":"After facial trauma, which finding requires immediate report?","o":["Periorbital bruising","Clear nasal drainage that tests positive for glucose","Nasal swelling","Pain 4/10 at the fracture"],"a":[1],"r":["Expected with trauma.","Correct. CSF leak indicates a basilar skull fracture and meningitis risk.","Expected.","Expected."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"CSF contains glucose; mucus does not.","rule":"Expected versus unexpected after trauma."},{"id":"23|trad|6","sourceId":"23|c|6","q":"A client’s new tracheostomy (day 2) is dislodged and the client is in distress. Which action first?","o":["Leave to get a new tube from central supply","Call for help, stay with the client, and maintain oxygenation per protocol","Force the old tube back in","Document the event"],"a":[1],"r":["Never leave the client.","Correct. Get help and support oxygenation; use the obturator/spare tube per protocol.","A fresh tract risks a false passage.","Care first."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"An immature stoma tract may collapse and a forced tube can enter soft tissue.","rule":"Stay with a client in airway danger; send someone else."},{"id":"23|trad|2","sourceId":"23|c|2","q":"Which actions are correct when suctioning a tracheostomy? Select all that apply.","o":["Hyperoxygenate before suctioning","Apply suction while inserting the catheter","Limit each pass to 10 seconds or less","Use sterile technique","Perform continuous suction for 30 seconds to clear thick secretions"],"a":[0,2,3],"r":["Correct. Prevents hypoxemia.","Suction only on withdrawal to reduce mucosal trauma and oxygen loss.","Correct.","Correct. The lower airway is sterile.","Causes hypoxemia and bradycardia."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Suction removes alveolar gas; vagal stimulation slows the heart.","rule":"Oxygen out equals oxygen in: limit time and replace it."},{"id":"23|trad|4","sourceId":"23|c|4","q":"A client with epistaxis has oxymetazoline ordered as a topical vasoconstrictor. Which teaching is correct?","o":["Use it several times daily for weeks","Use short-term only; prolonged use can cause rebound congestion","It is safe for clients with uncontrolled hypertension without monitoring","It prevents all future nosebleeds"],"a":[1],"r":["Prolonged use leads to rebound congestion.","Correct. Limit to the short course ordered (commonly no more than 3 days).","Systemic absorption can raise BP.","Not preventive."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction shrinks mucosa; vessels rebound when the drug wears off.","rule":"Topical decongestants: short-term only."},{"id":"23|trad|7","sourceId":"23|c|7","q":"Which task can the nurse assign to a licensed practical nurse (LPN) for a stable client with a mature tracheostomy?","o":["Routine tracheostomy care and suctioning","Initial teaching on self-suctioning","Assessing a new, dusky flap","Evaluating the first dose response to a new medication"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Routine care for a stable client with predictable outcomes.","Initial teaching is an RN responsibility.","Unstable finding requires RN assessment.","Evaluation of a new response is RN."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"—","rule":"Stable + predictable + routine can go to LPN."},{"id":"23|trad|0","sourceId":"23|c|0","q":"Which client with an upper airway problem should the nurse see first?","o":["Client with epistaxis leaning forward, bleeding slowing","Client 6 hours after neck surgery who is drooling and has new stridor","Client with OSA requesting help with the CPAP mask","Client with a nasal fracture rating pain 5/10"],"a":[1],"r":["Bleeding is controlled and the client is positioned correctly.","Correct. Stridor and drooling signal airway narrowing from edema or hematoma — emergency.","Important but not unstable.","Pain is a priority after airway."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Airflow falls sharply as the airway radius shrinks; the upper airway has no backup.","rule":"Airway first."},{"id":"23|ngn|0","sourceId":"23|n|0","q":"Which findings after head-and-neck surgery indicate airway compromise? Select all that apply.","o":["Stridor","Drooling and inability to swallow secretions","Incisional pain rated 4/10","Increasing neck swelling or tight dressing","Hoarse voice unchanged from pre-op","Rising respiratory effort and restlessness"],"a":[0,1,3,5],"r":["Correct. Upper-airway narrowing.","Correct. Swelling blocks the pharynx.","Incorrect. Expected post-op pain.","Correct. Hematoma or edema compressing the airway.","Incorrect. Unchanged baseline is not a new finding.","Correct. Early hypoxia and air hunger."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Airflow falls steeply as radius narrows.","rule":"Airway first — one lumen, no backup."},{"id":"23|ngn|1","sourceId":"23|n|1","q":"Which actions are appropriate after rhinoplasty with nasal packing? Select all that apply.","o":["Semi-Fowler position","Ice compresses to the nose and eyes","Teach not to blow the nose","Observe for frequent swallowing","Encourage straining exercises to prevent constipation","Use a straw to drink"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Reduces edema and bleeding.","Correct. Vasoconstriction reduces swelling and bruising.","Correct. Pressure dislodges clots.","Correct. Blood draining down the back of the throat.","Incorrect. Straining raises venous pressure and causes bleeding; use stool softeners.","Incorrect in many protocols — suction pressure can disturb the surgical site; follow surgeon instructions."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Posterior bleeding is swallowed and may not be seen externally.","rule":"Reduce pressure and edema; detect hidden bleeding."},{"id":"23|ngn|2","sourceId":"23|n|2","q":"Review the exhibit. What is the nurse’s priority concern?","o":["Epistaxis requiring anterior packing","Cerebrospinal fluid leak — question the NG order and avoid nasal instrumentation","Allergic rhinitis","Sinus infection"],"a":[1],"r":["Drainage is clear with a halo, not frank blood.","Correct. Halo sign and glucose-positive drainage indicate a basilar skull fracture; a nasal tube could enter the cranial vault.","Not consistent with trauma and halo sign.","Not an acute trauma finding."],"ex":[{"h":"ED nursing note","lines":["Client struck face in motor vehicle crash. Periorbital bruising bilaterally.","Clear fluid dripping from right nostril; drop on gauze shows yellow ring around blood.","Dipstick of fluid: glucose positive. GCS 15. Airway patent."]},{"h":"Orders","lines":["Insert NG tube for decompression.","CT head and face."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"CSF contains glucose; nasal mucus does not.","rule":"Question orders that are unsafe given new findings."},{"id":"23|ngn|3","sourceId":"23|n|3","q":"Review the exhibit. Which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","o":["Apply the client’s CPAP","Hold zolpidem and notify the provider","Give zolpidem so the client can sleep","Start continuous SpO₂ (and capnography if available)","Place supine flat"],"a":[0,1,3],"r":["Correct. Splints the collapsible airway.","Correct. Another CNS depressant in an over-sedated client with OSA is unsafe.","Incorrect. Stacking sedatives worsens respiratory depression.","Correct. High-risk client needs continuous monitoring.","Incorrect. Supine worsens obstruction; side-lying or head elevated is preferred."],"ex":[{"h":"History","lines":["Obstructive sleep apnea, uses CPAP nightly at home. BMI 41."]},{"h":"MAR — post-op night","table":[["Drug","Dose","Last given"],["Hydromorphone IV","0.5 mg q2h PRN","2200"],["Zolpidem PO","5 mg HS PRN","Requested now"]]},{"h":"Flowsheet 2330","lines":["Sedation: frequently drowsy, drifts off mid-sentence. RR 10. SpO₂ 89% RA. CPAP at bedside, not applied."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Opioids and hypnotics reduce drive and pharyngeal tone together.","rule":"Recognize stacked risk; hold and escalate."}],"studio":"upper-airway"},{"id":"asthma","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Asthma: opening the airway and controlling inflammation","notes":["24-overview","24-asthma-the-full-picture","24-adpie","24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Inflammation narrows the airway from inside; hyperresponsive smooth muscle squeezes from outside. Reversible. Air gets trapped on the way out.","Findings: Expiratory wheeze, cough; Chest tightness, dyspnea; Prolonged exhalation; Worse at night / with triggers","Nursing actions: Sit upright; O₂; SABA now; Reassess breath sounds + work of breathing","Silent chest, drowsiness or a normal-to-rising PaCO₂ mid-attack = airway closing, patient tiring. Emergency.","Use the prescribed asthma action plan. Current GINA care includes inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment; do not interpret a SABA rescue example as a recommendation for SABA-only long-term treatment. ICS-formoterol can be an anti-inflammatory reliever when that regimen is prescribed."],"checks":["Explain why co₂ retention is the late sign in Asthma.","Explain action plan and self-management in Asthma.","Explain why in Asthma."],"recallAnswers":["CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate; ABGs near-normal until advanced; Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻","Plan: daily controller, reliever use, control questions, how to step up, when to call, emergency steps; Symptom diary + peak flow during flares, after changes, when worse; Keep exercising → adjust or premedicate with SABA","Lower airway = millions of parallel paths → decline over months–years, not minutes; So: trend > single finding, the patient's own baseline is the reference; Work of breathing, not SpO₂, shows if the disease is winning"],"requires":[],"terms":["bronchospasm","albuterol","fluticasone"],"questions":[{"id":"24|trad|1","sourceId":"24|c|1","q":"A client uses albuterol and fluticasone inhalers. Which instruction is correct?","o":["Use fluticasone first, then albuterol","Use albuterol first, wait, then fluticasone and rinse mouth","Use fluticasone for acute attacks","Do not rinse after fluticasone"],"a":[1],"r":["Bronchodilator should open airways first.","Correct.","ICS is a controller.","Rinsing prevents thrush and dysphonia."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Bronchodilation improves steroid deposition.","rule":"Reliever opens the door; controller walks through."},{"id":"24|trad|8","sourceId":"24|c|8","q":"Which finding distinguishes asthma from COPD on PFTs?","o":["Reduced FEV₁","FEV₁ improves by 12% or more after bronchodilator","Reduced FEV₁/FVC ratio","Increased residual volume"],"a":[1],"r":["Both.","Correct. Reversibility defines asthma.","Both obstructive.","Both trap air."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Asthma narrowing is smooth muscle and inflammation; COPD is structural.","rule":"Reversibility = asthma."},{"id":"24|ngn|2","sourceId":"24|n|2","q":"Review the exhibit. What is the best interpretation?","o":["Improving — respiratory rate and wheeze are decreasing","Impending respiratory failure","Anxiety resolving","Metabolic acidosis from lactic acid"],"a":[1],"r":["A falling RR with fading wheeze and worsening speech is fatigue, not improvement.","Correct. PaCO₂ moving from low to high with falling pH means the client can no longer ventilate.","Speech and SpO₂ worsening contradict this.","PaCO₂ explains the acidosis; it is respiratory."],"ex":[{"h":"ED flowsheet","table":[["Time","RR","SpO₂","Speech","Wheeze"],["1000","32","93%","Short sentences","Loud, bilateral"],["1030","28","90%","2–3 words","Softer"],["1100","18","87%","Unable to speak","Barely audible"]]},{"h":"ABG trend","table":[["Time","pH","PaCO₂"],["1000","7.48","30"],["1100","7.30","52"]]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Early hyperventilation lowers CO₂; fatigue and air trapping then cause retention.","rule":"Trend over snapshot; a “normalizing” CO₂ in asthma is a warning sign."}],"clinicalUpdate":"Use the prescribed asthma action plan. Current GINA care includes inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment; do not interpret a SABA rescue example as a recommendation for SABA-only long-term treatment. ICS-formoterol can be an anti-inflammatory reliever when that regimen is prescribed.","studio":"asthma"},{"id":"copd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"COPD: air trapping, gas exchange and safe treatment","notes":["24-overview","24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Smoke tips proteases over alpha₁-antitrypsin, destroying elastin (emphysema) and inflaming airways (chronic bronchitis). Progressive, poorly reversible.","Findings: Barrel chest, tripod position; Pursed-lip breathing; Prolonged exhalation, diminished sounds; Clubbing, weight loss; Chronic productive cough (bronchitis)","Nursing actions: O₂ low flow to SpO₂ 88–92%; Upright / tripod; Pursed-lip + diaphragmatic breathing; Airway clearance, hydration","Drowsy or confused = rising CO₂. JVD + edema = right heart failure (cor pulmonale)."],"checks":["Explain why co₂ retention is the late sign in COPD.","Explain priority problems in COPD.","Explain why in COPD."],"recallAnswers":["CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate; ABGs near-normal until advanced; Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻","1. Gas exchange · 2. Weight loss · 3. Fatigue · 4. Infection risk","Lower airway = millions of parallel paths → decline over months–years, not minutes; So: trend > single finding, the patient's own baseline is the reference; Work of breathing, not SpO₂, shows if the disease is winning"],"requires":[],"terms":["emphysema","hypercapnia","tiotropium"],"questions":[{"id":"24|trad|2","sourceId":"24|c|2","q":"A client taking theophylline reports nausea and palpitations. Which lab should the nurse check?","o":["INR","Serum theophylline level","Potassium only","A1C"],"a":[1],"r":["Not relevant.","Correct. Narrow therapeutic range; toxicity causes GI upset, tachydysrhythmias and seizures.","Can be affected but not primary.","Not relevant."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Methylxanthines stimulate the CNS and heart.","rule":"Narrow therapeutic index drugs: check the level with toxicity signs."},{"id":"24|ngn|0","sourceId":"24|n|0","q":"Which teaching points are appropriate for a client with COPD? Select all that apply.","o":["Pursed-lip breathing during exertion","Small, frequent high-calorie meals","Increase oxygen to 6 L/min when short of breath without calling","Get annual influenza and recommended pneumococcal vaccines","Rest before meals","Stop smoking"],"a":[0,1,3,4,5],"r":["Correct. Keeps small airways open and prolongs exhalation.","Correct. Reduces diaphragm pressure and dyspnea while meeting high energy needs.","Incorrect. Oxygen is titrated to the ordered target; excess can worsen hypercapnia.","Correct. Infections trigger exacerbations.","Correct. Conserves energy for eating.","Correct. The single most effective intervention to slow decline."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Air trapping and high work of breathing drive dyspnea and weight loss.","rule":"Teaching targets the mechanism of disease progression."},{"id":"24|ngn|3","sourceId":"24|n|3","q":"Review the exhibit. Which findings require action by the nurse? Select all that apply.","o":["Theophylline level with tachycardia and nausea","White patches on the tongue","Albuterol used about 8 times per day","Tiotropium ordered once daily","Fluticasone/salmeterol ordered twice daily"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Level above the usual range with toxicity signs — hold and notify.","Correct. Oral candidiasis from ICS; needs treatment and rinse teaching.","Correct. Frequent rescue use indicates poor control — report.","Incorrect. Correct once-daily LAMA dosing.","Incorrect. Correct twice-daily ICS/LABA dosing."],"ex":[{"h":"MAR","table":[["Drug","Order"],["Tiotropium DPI","1 inhalation daily"],["Fluticasone/salmeterol DPI","1 inhalation BID"],["Albuterol MDI","2 puffs q4h PRN"],["Theophylline ER","300 mg BID"]]},{"h":"Labs and assessment","lines":["Theophylline level 24 mcg/mL. HR 118. Client reports nausea and insomnia.","White patches on the tongue. Uses albuterol about 8 times per day."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Theophylline has a narrow therapeutic index; ICS suppresses local immunity.","rule":"Identify abnormal data among normal orders."}],"studio":"copd"},{"id":"chest-tubes","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems","notes":["24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses; Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping; Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)","Warning signals to teach: Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough; Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis; Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis; Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing","Diagnosis and treatment: CXR finds → CT defines/guides biopsy → biopsy/pleural fluid cells = diagnosis; PET, MRI stage; Chemo first choice (esp. SCLC); Targeted needs the target: EGFR (afatinib, erlotinib), ALK (alectinib, crizotinib) → test cells first; Immunotherapy (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab): not curative, not first-line alone; Radiation: skin irritation, fatigue, nausea, taste change, esophagitis → bland high-cal food; no sun on the field during + ≥ 1 yr after; Photodynamic: drug collects in cancer cells 48–72 h → laser; Surgery = main treatment for stage I–II NSCLC: wedge/segment, lobectomy, sleeve, pneumonectomy; open or VATS","Why: Everything is downstream of water: Cl⁻ can't move → water doesn't follow → all secretions too thick; So therapy is mechanical (vest, drainage, mucolytics), and new drugs target the channel itself"],"checks":["Explain why in Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems.","Explain warning signals to teach in Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems.","Explain diagnosis and treatment in Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems."],"recallAnswers":["Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses; Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping; Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)","Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough; Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis; Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis; Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing","CXR finds → CT defines/guides biopsy → biopsy/pleural fluid cells = diagnosis; PET, MRI stage; Chemo first choice (esp. SCLC); Targeted needs the target: EGFR (afatinib, erlotinib), ALK (alectinib, crizotinib) → test cells first; Immunotherapy (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab): not curative, not first-line alone; Radiation: skin irritation, fatigue, nausea, taste change, esophagitis → bland high-cal food; no sun on the field during + ≥ 1 yr after; Photodynamic: drug collects in cancer cells 48–72 h → laser; Surgery = main treatment for stage I–II NSCLC: wedge/segment, lobectomy, sleeve, pneumonectomy; open or VATS"],"requires":[],"terms":["pneumothorax","hemothorax","atelectasis"],"questions":[{"id":"24|trad|3","sourceId":"24|c|3","q":"Which findings indicate a chest tube needs immediate provider notification? Select all that apply.","o":["Drainage over 70 mL/hr","Tracheal deviation","Tidaling with respiration","Sudden increased dyspnea","SpO₂ below 90%"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Correct. Possible hemorrhage.","Correct. Tension pneumothorax.","Expected with a patent system.","Correct.","Correct."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Air or blood accumulating under pressure displaces the mediastinum.","rule":"Know the chest tube escalation list."},{"id":"24|trad|7","sourceId":"24|c|7","q":"Which task can the nurse delegate to AP for a client with a chest tube?","o":["Milking or stripping the tubing","Keeping the drainage system below chest level and reporting bubbling changes","Assessing the insertion site","Changing the drainage system"],"a":[1],"r":["Not recommended and not AP scope.","Correct. Positioning and reporting observations is within AP scope.","Assessment is RN.","RN task."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Gravity drainage requires the system below the chest.","rule":"AP can maintain positioning and report; RN assesses."},{"id":"24|trad|5","sourceId":"24|c|5","q":"Which statement by a client with CF indicates correct understanding?","o":["“I take my pancreatic enzymes with every meal and snack.”","“I should follow a low-salt diet.”","“I can room with my friend who also has CF.”","“I only need enzymes when I have diarrhea.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct.","CF clients lose salt in sweat; often need extra.","Cross-infection risk (Burkholderia, Pseudomonas).","Needed with all food."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Blocked pancreatic ducts prevent digestive enzymes reaching the gut.","rule":"Enzymes with every meal/snack."},{"id":"24|ngn|1","sourceId":"24|n|1","q":"Which actions are correct for a client with a chest tube to water-seal drainage? Select all that apply.","o":["Keep the drainage system below chest level","Clamp the tube for transport","Check for tidaling in the water-seal chamber","Keep sterile water and an occlusive dressing at the bedside","Milk or strip the tubing routinely every hour","Mark drainage level at set intervals"],"a":[0,2,3,5],"r":["Correct. Gravity drainage; prevents backflow.","Incorrect. Clamping without an order can cause tension pneumothorax.","Correct. Fluctuation shows a patent system.","Correct. For disconnection or accidental removal.","Incorrect. Stripping creates high negative pressure; not routine.","Correct. Trends identify hemorrhage."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"The water seal lets air out but not back into the pleural space.","rule":"Maintain a closed, one-way system."}],"studio":"chest-tubes"},{"id":"pneumonia","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Pneumonia: infection, oxygenation and deterioration","notes":["25-overview","25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","25-adpie"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 25; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Microbes in the alveoli trigger inflammation that fills the air spaces with exudate. Blood passes unventilated alveoli → hypoxemia.","Findings: Fever, productive cough; Crackles, ↓ breath sounds; Dull percussion, ↑ tactile fremitus; Pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea; Older adult: confusion, maybe no fever","Nursing actions: Cultures first, then antibiotics fast; O₂, HOB up; Cough, deep breathe, incentive spirometer; Fluids 2–3 L unless restricted; ambulate","Low BP, falling SpO₂, new confusion, lactate >2 = sepsis."],"checks":["Explain why immunity is the whole prevention story in Pneumonia.","Explain ¶ on exam why the findings are in Pneumonia.","Explain priority problems in Pneumonia."],"recallAnswers":["Viral strains change a little every year → old antibodies partly work → several colds/yr, annual flu shot; Pandemic = organism with no human immunity (jumped from birds/animals): 1918 flu, 2009 H1N1, COVID-19","Fluid conducts sound better than air; Pneumonia: ↑ fremitus, dull percussion, bronchial sounds, crackles; Emphysema: ↓ fremitus, hyperresonance; Same tools, opposite findings — one lung too full, the other too empty","1. Gas exchange (↓ diffusion) · 2. Airway obstruction (secretions, fatigue, weakness) · 3. Sepsis"],"requires":[],"terms":["pneumonia","consolidation","sepsis"],"questions":[{"id":"25|trad|4","sourceId":"25|c|4","q":"A client with pneumonia has WBC 18,000/mm³ with a left shift. What does this indicate?","o":["Viral infection only","Active bacterial infection with immature neutrophils released","Anemia","Allergy"],"a":[1],"r":["Bacterial more likely.","Correct.","Not a WBC finding.","Eosinophils would rise."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Marrow releases bands to fight bacteria.","rule":"Interpret labs in context."},{"id":"25|trad|7","sourceId":"25|c|7","q":"Which task can be delegated to AP for a client with pneumonia?","o":["Assisting with ambulation and reminding to use the incentive spirometer","Assessing lung sounds","Teaching about antibiotics","Evaluating sputum changes"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct.","RN.","RN.","RN."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Mobility expands alveoli and mobilizes secretions.","rule":"Reinforcement and mobility can be delegated."},{"id":"25|trad|5","sourceId":"25|c|5","q":"Which statement about oseltamivir shows understanding?","o":["“It works best if started within 2 days of symptoms.”","“I no longer need a flu shot.”","“It cures the flu immediately.”","“It treats bacterial pneumonia.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct.","Vaccination still needed.","Shortens illness.","Antiviral only."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Blocks viral release early in replication.","rule":"Timing matters for antivirals."},{"id":"25|trad|6","sourceId":"25|c|6","q":"A client with a sore throat has drooling, muffled voice and trismus. Which action first?","o":["Give oral antibiotics","Assess airway and notify provider promptly","Offer warm salt-water gargles","Give acetaminophen"],"a":[1],"r":["Not first.","Correct. Peritonsillar abscess threatens the airway.","Comfort, not first.","Comfort."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Swelling narrows the oropharynx.","rule":"Airway first."},{"id":"25|trad|8","sourceId":"25|c|8","q":"Which finding points to the second phase of inhalation anthrax rather than influenza?","o":["Fever and myalgia","Mediastinal widening on chest x-ray with sudden distress","Sore throat","Headache"],"a":[1],"r":["Both.","Correct.","Nonspecific.","Nonspecific."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hemorrhagic mediastinitis from toxins.","rule":"Look for the finding that separates look-alikes."},{"id":"25|ngn|0","sourceId":"25|n|0","q":"Which interventions help prevent pneumonia in a hospitalized older adult? Select all that apply.","o":["Incentive spirometry hourly while awake","Head of bed elevated for meals and after","Early and frequent mobility","Oral care at least twice daily","Keep the client flat to rest the lungs","Hand hygiene before and after care"],"a":[0,1,2,3,5],"r":["Correct. Reinflates alveoli.","Correct. Reduces aspiration.","Correct. Improves ventilation and secretion clearance.","Correct. Reduces oral bacteria that are aspirated.","Incorrect. Flat positioning promotes atelectasis and aspiration.","Correct. Prevents transmission."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Stasis of secretions and collapsed alveoli harbor bacteria.","rule":"Prevention targets aspiration, atelectasis and transmission."},{"id":"25|ngn|2","sourceId":"25|n|2","q":"Review the exhibit. Which action should the nurse take first?","o":["Wait for the scheduled antibiotic at 1500","Activate rapid response/sepsis protocol and anticipate fluids, lactate and immediate antibiotics","Give acetaminophen because the fever is breaking","Encourage oral fluids"],"a":[1],"r":["Delaying antibiotics in sepsis increases mortality.","Correct. Hypotension, confusion, oliguria and rising oxygen needs meet sepsis criteria.","Temperature falling with worsening perfusion is not improvement.","Inadequate for hypotension and confusion."],"ex":[{"h":"Vital signs","table":[["Time","T","HR","BP","RR","SpO₂"],["0800","38.4","98","118/72","22","93% 2 L"],["1200","38.9","116","96/60","28","90% 2 L"],["1400","37.2","124","84/50","32","88% 4 L"]]},{"h":"Notes","lines":["1400: newly confused, urine 20 mL past hour. Blood cultures ×2 pending; first antibiotic dose due 1500."]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Inflammatory vasodilation and capillary leak reduce perfusion.","rule":"Recognize sepsis by perfusion, not fever."}],"studio":"pneumonia"},{"id":"tuberculosis","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Tuberculosis: airborne precautions and a complete drug plan","notes":["25-tuberculosis"],"source":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 25; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Adherence is a clinical intervention; 4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early; Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team","What happens in the lung: Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis; Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve; Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test; Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge","Latent, active, secondary: Latent: no symptoms for years–decades, not contagious; Few ever get active TB; Secondary = reactivation when immunity falls (older, chronic disease, HIV)"],"checks":["Explain why in Tuberculosis.","Explain what happens in the lung in Tuberculosis.","Explain latent, active, secondary in Tuberculosis."],"recallAnswers":["Adherence is a clinical intervention; 4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early; Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team","Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis; Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve; Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test; Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge","Latent: no symptoms for years–decades, not contagious; Few ever get active TB; Secondary = reactivation when immunity falls (older, chronic disease, HIV)"],"requires":[],"terms":["tuberculosis","isoniazid","rifampin"],"questions":[{"id":"25|trad|2","sourceId":"25|c|2","q":"A client on rifampin reports orange urine. Best response?","o":["“Stop the medication.”","“This is expected; it may also stain contact lenses.”","“This means kidney damage.”","“Drink less fluid.”"],"a":[1],"r":["Not necessary.","Correct.","Not a sign of damage.","Irrelevant."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Pigmented drug excreted in fluids.","rule":"Distinguish expected from adverse."},{"id":"25|trad|3","sourceId":"25|c|3","q":"Which actions are appropriate for a client admitted with active pulmonary TB? Select all that apply.","o":["Airborne infection isolation room","Staff wear fit-tested N95 respirators","Client wears a surgical mask during transport","Keep the door open for observation","Teach to cover mouth when coughing"],"a":[0,1,2,4],"r":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Door must stay closed for negative pressure.","Correct."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Droplet nuclei remain suspended.","rule":"Airborne precautions."},{"id":"25|ngn|1","sourceId":"25|n|1","q":"Which statements indicate understanding of first-line TB therapy? Select all that apply.","o":["“My urine and tears may turn orange.”","“I’ll take vitamin B6 with isoniazid.”","“I’ll stop the pills once my cough is gone.”","“I’ll avoid alcohol.”","“I’ll report vision changes.”","“My birth control pill is fully reliable with rifampin.”"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Correct. Expected rifampin effect.","Correct. Prevents INH neuropathy.","Incorrect. Stopping early causes relapse and resistance.","Correct. Adds to hepatotoxicity.","Correct. Ethambutol optic neuritis.","Incorrect. Rifampin induces enzymes that lower hormonal contraceptive levels."],"ex":[],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Hepatic metabolism and enzyme induction explain toxicity and interactions.","rule":"Adherence and drug-specific safety."},{"id":"25|ngn|3","sourceId":"25|n|3","q":"Review the exhibit. Which findings should be reported? Select all that apply.","o":["ALT 220 U/L","Uric acid 9.8 mg/dL","Creatinine 0.9 mg/dL","Red-green color confusion","Tingling feet","Orange urine"],"a":[0,1,3,4],"r":["Correct. Hepatotoxicity (INH, rifampin, pyrazinamide).","Correct. Pyrazinamide raises uric acid; gout risk.","Normal.","Correct. Ethambutol optic neuritis.","Correct. INH peripheral neuropathy.","Expected with rifampin."],"ex":[{"h":"Month 2 of RIPE therapy — labs","table":[["Test","Result","Reference"],["ALT","220 U/L","4–36"],["Uric acid","9.8 mg/dL","2.7–7.3"],["Creatinine","0.9 mg/dL","0.6–1.2"]]},{"h":"Client report","lines":["“Red and green look the same lately.” “My feet tingle.” “My urine is orange.”"]}],"selectCount":0,"phys":"Each drug has a signature toxicity.","rule":"Link each finding to the drug causing it."}],"studio":"tuberculosis"},{"id":"dka","guide":"56:2","ch":"56","title":"DKA: fluids, potassium, then insulin","notes":["56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie","56-overview","56-adpie"],"source":"Ch 56: DKA and HHS, Table 56.9; safety update: 2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus.","why":["Too little effective insulin permits fat breakdown and ketone production. Ketones cause metabolic acidosis; glucose in urine pulls water and electrolytes out. Vomiting adds losses.","A serum potassium that looks normal or high can hide depleted body stores. Insulin moves potassium into cells, so treatment can make the serum value fall rapidly.","Restore perfusion with prescribed isotonic fluids, assess potassium and renal status, and monitor. Current adult consensus: if K⁺ is below 3.5 mmol/L, replace it and delay insulin until above 3.5. When glucose falls below 250 mg/dL, dextrose can allow insulin to continue until ketoacidosis resolves. Glucose improvement alone does not prove resolution."],"checks":["Explain the separate causes of dehydration and ketoacidosis.","Explain why insulin is unsafe with K⁺ 3.2 and what must happen first.","State why ketones and acid–base recovery matter even when glucose improves."],"requires":["glands","insulin"],"studio":"dka","caseId":"dka","update":"crisis","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|30","q":"An adult with DKA has K⁺ 3.2 mmol/L. Isotonic IV fluids have started. Under the current adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus, which action is appropriate before insulin?","o":["Replace potassium as prescribed and delay insulin until K⁺ is above 3.5 mmol/L","Start insulin because the glucose is above 400 mg/dL","Give insulin first to correct the potassium deficit","Ignore potassium unless there is an ECG change"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Insulin can lower serum potassium further; the current adult consensus delays it for K⁺ < 3.5 until K⁺ > 3.5.","Incorrect. Severe hyperglycemia does not remove the potassium safety requirement.","Incorrect. Insulin shifts potassium into cells and can worsen the low serum value.","Incorrect. Dangerous hypokalemia can precede visible rhythm changes."],"ex":[],"phys":"Insulin shifts K⁺ into cells; depleted body stores plus a low serum value increase dysrhythmia and respiratory-muscle risk."},{"id":"56|trad|32","q":"During DKA treatment, a client’s glucose is 168 mg/dL and the anion gap has closed. Plasma ketones remain 1.6 mmol/L. Which conclusion follows the current adult consensus?","o":["Ketoacidosis has not met the ketone criterion for resolution","The normal glucose proves the DKA is resolved","Gap closure alone permits stopping insulin immediately","Urine ketones alone are the preferred resolution measure"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Plasma ketones must fall below 0.6 mmol/L, with the required pH or bicarbonate recovery.","Incorrect. Glucose can improve before ketoacidosis resolves.","Incorrect. The gap alone is not the recommended endpoint.","Incorrect. Urine ketones can remain misleading during recovery."],"ex":[],"phys":"Insulin must suppress ketogenesis beyond the initial glucose correction; prescribed dextrose may permit insulin to continue safely."}]},{"id":"gi-aging","guide":"45:0","ch":"45","title":"An aging gut: what changes, what needs assessment","notes":["45-gi-changes-with-aging"],"source":"Ch 45: GI changes associated with aging.","why":["The chapter connects aging with reduced GI motility and a weaker defecation signal. Intake, activity, medications and access to a toilet also influence constipation. Compare with the person’s baseline.","Reduced hepatic metabolism can allow some drugs to accumulate. Atrophic gastric changes and reduced acid can affect nutrient handling, including iron and vitamin B₁₂. These patterns guide assessment; they do not explain every new symptom.","Assess new bleeding, severe pain, persistent vomiting or a new bowel-pattern change. Review nutrition, hydration and medications rather than dismissing the problem as normal aging."],"checks":["Connect slower motility with constipation and nursing assessment.","Explain why a liver-metabolized drug may need closer monitoring.","Name a new GI finding that should not be dismissed as age."],"requires":[],"studio":"gi-aging","questions":[{"id":"45|trad|11","q":"A 79-year-old client starts a drug that is metabolized by the liver. Which age-related GI change makes the nurse monitor closely for drug toxicity?","o":["Fewer, smaller liver cells with lower enzyme activity","More hydrochloric acid, which speeds drug absorption","Faster peristalsis, which shortens drug transit time","More pancreatic lipase, which boosts absorption of fat-soluble drugs"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. With aging, liver cells shrink in number and size, fibrous tissue increases and enzyme activity falls — drugs are metabolized more slowly and can build up to toxic levels.","Incorrect. The gastric mucosa atrophies with age, so acid falls (hypochlorhydria); it does not rise.","Incorrect. Peristalsis slows with age, which leads to constipation, not faster transit.","Incorrect. Lipase production falls with age, causing poorer fat digestion and steatorrhea."],"ex":[],"phys":"The aging liver loses cells and mass and gains fibrous tissue → less enzyme activity → each dose is broken down more slowly → levels climb with repeated doses → toxicity."},{"id":"45|trad|12","q":"An 84-year-old client asks why the provider checks vitamin B₁₂ and iron levels at every annual visit. Which age-related GI change should the nurse explain?","o":["The stomach lining thins and makes less acid, so less B₁₂ and iron are absorbed","The pancreas makes less lipase, so fat passes into the stool","The bowel moves more slowly, so stool stays longer in the colon","The liver stores more iron and B₁₂ with age, which hides a deficiency"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Atrophy of the gastric mucosa lowers hydrochloric acid (hypochlorhydria), which reduces absorption of iron and vitamin B₁₂ and lets bacteria overgrow (atrophic gastritis).","Incorrect. Less lipase is a real aging change, but it causes poor fat digestion and steatorrhea, not low B₁₂ or iron.","Incorrect. Slower peristalsis is real, but it leads to constipation and impaction, not poor B₁₂ and iron absorption.","Incorrect. The liver does store iron and B₁₂, but it shrinks with age; it does not store more."],"ex":[],"phys":"The gastric mucosa atrophies with age → parietal cells make less hydrochloric acid → less iron and B₁₂ are absorbed → less acid also lets swallowed bacteria survive and overgrow → atrophic gastritis."}]},{"id":"quadrants","guide":"45:1","ch":"45","title":"Locate the organ before interpreting the symptom","notes":["45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","45-overview"],"source":"Ch 45: Table 45.1, abdominal quadrants and associated organs.","why":["RUQ: most of the liver, gallbladder, duodenum and pancreatic head. LUQ: stomach, spleen and pancreatic body/tail. RLQ: cecum and appendix. LLQ: descending and sigmoid colon.","The aorta and a distended bladder are midline landmarks. Some organs span regions. Location narrows an assessment; it does not establish a diagnosis. Use the full Table 45.1 notes to practise every listed structure."],"checks":["Locate liver/gallbladder, stomach/spleen, appendix and sigmoid colon.","Explain why pain location supports assessment but does not prove a diagnosis."],"requires":[],"studio":"quadrants","questions":[{"id":"quadrants-a","q":"A client points to the right upper abdomen. Which pair of organs is most closely associated with that quadrant?","o":["Liver and gallbladder","Spleen and stomach","Appendix and cecum","Descending and sigmoid colon"],"a":[0],"r":["Most of the liver and the gallbladder are in the RUQ.","These are mainly LUQ structures.","These are RLQ structures.","These are mainly left lower structures."]},{"id":"quadrants-b","q":"A client has left upper abdominal discomfort after an injury. Which organ’s location makes it relevant to the focused assessment?","o":["Appendix","Spleen","Gallbladder","Sigmoid colon"],"a":[1],"r":["The appendix is in the RLQ.","The spleen is in the LUQ; mechanism, vital signs and other findings still determine urgency.","The gallbladder is in the RUQ.","The sigmoid colon is mainly in the LLQ."]}]},{"id":"mouth","guide":"46:0","ch":"46","title":"Protect the mouth and keep eating safe","notes":["46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar"],"source":"Ch 46: Boxes 46.1–46.2 and stomatitis nursing care.","why":["Healthy-mouth prevention includes brushing, appropriate flossing, dental follow-up, oral self-examination and tobacco avoidance. Adapt care to bleeding risk, discomfort and the treatment plan.","With mucositis, fragile tissue needs gentle cleaning: a soft brush, prescribed nonirritating rinses, pain management and soft bland foods. Inspect the mouth, assess intake and swallowing, and report lesions or infection concerns.","A topical anesthetic can remove protective sensation as well as pain. Follow the product’s directions about eating and drinking; a numb mouth or throat increases aspiration and burn risk."],"checks":["Teach prevention, then adapt cleaning and food for mucositis.","Explain the eating and swallowing risks after a topical oral anesthetic."],"requires":[],"studio":"mouth","questions":[{"id":"46|ngn|0","q":"A client receiving chemotherapy has painful stomatitis. Which interventions belong in the plan of care? Select all that apply.","o":["Brush with a soft or ultrasoft toothbrush","Rinse often with warm saline or baking-soda solution","Offer soft, bland, cool foods","Remove dentures if the mouth is severely sore","Use an alcohol-based mouthwash to kill bacteria","Swab the mouth with lemon-glycerin swabs","Offer orange juice for vitamin C"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. A soft brush cleans plaque without tearing the inflamed mucosa.","Correct. Saline and baking soda clean and soothe without chemical irritation; baking soda also neutralizes acid.","Correct. Hard, spicy, salty, acidic and hot foods all hurt open ulcers; cool foods soothe.","Correct. Dentures rub on ulcers and trap organisms.","Incorrect. Alcohol dries and stings the mucosa, and a dry mouth lets organisms thrive.","Incorrect. Lemon-glycerin is acidic and drying — it feels fresh but slows healing.","Incorrect. Citrus stings open sores; give vitamin C as a supplement instead."],"ex":[],"phys":"Chemotherapy kills fast-dividing cells, including the mouth lining → the mucosa thins and ulcerates → ulcers hurt and let organisms in → gentle, nonirritating cleaning keeps it clean while it regrows."},{"id":"mouth-b","q":"A client’s mouth and throat are still numb after a prescribed oral anesthetic, and the client requests hot soup. What should the nurse do?","o":["Help the client drink it while pain is controlled","Follow the medication’s eating precautions and assess safe swallowing before oral intake","Give a straw to bypass all aspiration risk","Encourage extra doses to prolong the numbness"],"a":[1],"r":["Numb tissue cannot reliably detect heat and swallowing may be impaired.","Loss of protective sensation increases burn and aspiration risk; follow the specific product instructions.","A straw does not remove aspiration risk.","Extra doses increase adverse-effect risk and do not make intake safe."]}]},{"id":"gerd","guide":"46:1","ch":"46","title":"GERD: reduce reflux and recognize warning signs","notes":["46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce"],"source":"Ch 46: GERD, Box 46.6 and nonsurgical management.","why":["An ineffective lower esophageal sphincter lets stomach contents enter the esophagus. Heartburn and regurgitation are common; chronic cough or hoarseness can also occur.","Smaller meals, avoiding meals near bedtime, head-of-bed elevation and individualized trigger avoidance reduce reflux exposure. Acid suppression reduces acidity and injury. PPIs inhibit the acid pump; H₂ blockers reduce histamine-stimulated acid secretion.","Persistent injury can lead to esophagitis, stricture or Barrett changes. Dysphagia, bleeding and weight loss need follow-up. New chest pain requires assessment for a cardiac cause, even in someone with known reflux."],"checks":["Connect LES failure to symptoms and lifestyle measures.","Explain why new chest pain and dysphagia change the priority."],"requires":[],"studio":"gerd","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"46|ngn|4","q":"The nurse teaches a client with GERD. Which instructions are appropriate? Select all that apply.","o":["Eat 4–6 small meals instead of 3 large ones","Don’t eat within 3 hours of bedtime","Raise the head of the bed on blocks or use a wedge","Stay upright for at least an hour after eating","Lie down after meals to help digestion","Sleep on two or three extra pillows","Drink a carbonated beverage to bring up gas"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Large meals stretch the stomach and raise the pressure pushing up on the LES.","Correct. Night reflux does the most damage, and regurgitation lying down risks aspiration.","Correct. Blocks or a wedge tilt the whole esophagus above the stomach.","Correct. Gravity keeps the meal in the stomach while it empties.","Incorrect. Lying down removes gravity and invites reflux.","Incorrect. Extra pillows only bend the neck — the esophagus stays level with the stomach.","Incorrect. Carbonated drinks distend the stomach and are on the avoid list."],"ex":[],"phys":"A full, pressurized stomach pushes contents against a weak LES → gravity is the main thing holding them down → small meals, staying upright and a raised bed keep the gradient pointing the right way."},{"id":"46|trad|2","q":"A client comes to the ED with burning chest pain that spreads to the jaw. The client says, “It’s just my reflux again.” What should the nurse do first?","o":["Assess the pain as cardiac until proven otherwise","Give an antacid and reassess in 30 minutes","Teach the client to avoid fatty meals","Raise the head of the bed and leave the client to rest"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. GERD pain can mimic angina, so the heart is ruled out first — and the nurse stays with the frightened client.","Incorrect. Relief from an antacid doesn’t rule out an MI.","Incorrect. Teaching comes after the cause is known.","Incorrect. Don’t leave a client with possible cardiac pain."],"ex":[],"phys":"The esophagus and heart share sensory nerve pathways → pain from either is felt in the chest, neck and jaw → the client can’t tell which organ it comes from."}]},{"id":"hernia","guide":"46:2","ch":"46","title":"Hiatal hernia and the tighter junction after surgery","notes":["46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care"],"source":"Ch 46: hiatal hernia; Boxes 46.8–46.10.","why":["In a sliding hernia, the gastroesophageal junction moves above the diaphragm and reflux is common. In a paraesophageal hernia, stomach herniates beside the esophagus; obstruction or strangulation is a concern.","Fundoplication reinforces the junction. Swelling and the tighter wrap explain early dysphagia, difficulty belching and gas-bloat. Follow the ordered diet, use small meals, chew well and avoid carbonated drinks.","Report severe pain, persistent vomiting or worsening swallowing problems. Do not advance or reposition a postoperative NG tube without the surgeon’s direction; disturbing the repair can cause harm."],"checks":["Distinguish sliding from paraesophageal hernia.","Explain gas-bloat, food precautions and NG tube precautions after fundoplication."],"requires":["gerd"],"studio":"hernia","questions":[{"id":"46|trad|7","q":"Which teaching helps a client prevent gas bloat syndrome after fundoplication?","o":["Avoid carbonated drinks, chewing gum and straws","Drink a carbonated drink after meals to settle the stomach","Lie down after each meal","Eat three large meals a day"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. After the wrap the client often can’t belch — so swallowed air and carbonation stay trapped.","Incorrect. Carbonation adds gas that can’t be belched out.","Incorrect. Walking and moving help gas pass.","Incorrect. Smaller, more frequent meals fit the smaller stomach reservoir."],"ex":[],"phys":"The wrap tightens the lower esophagus → air can’t escape upward → swallowed air and gas distend the stomach → bloating and pain."},{"id":"46|trad|6","q":"Six hours after an open Nissen fundoplication, the nurse finds the client’s NG tube pulled partway out. What should the nurse do?","o":["Notify the surgeon; do not reinsert or advance the tube","Push the tube back to its original mark","Remove the tube completely and document","Irrigate the tube with 30 mL saline to check patency"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Blindly reinserting could perforate the fresh wrap; the surgeon decides.","Incorrect. Advancing it blindly risks piercing the fundoplication.","Incorrect. The tube keeps the stomach decompressed and sizes the wrap — the surgeon decides whether it stays out.","Incorrect. Don’t manipulate it without an order."],"ex":[],"phys":"The fundus is sutured around the lower esophagus → the NG tube passes through the fresh wrap → a blind push can pierce the sutured tissue → leak or perforation."}]},{"id":"pud","guide":"47:0","ch":"47","title":"Gastritis and ulcers: repair the defense and treat the cause","notes":["47-gastritis","47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","47-overview","47-adpie"],"source":"Ch 47: gastritis, PUD, Tables 47.1–47.2; ACG 2024 treatment update.","why":["Gastritis is inflammation of the stomach lining; an ulcer is deeper tissue injury. H. pylori and NSAIDs weaken mucosal defense by different routes. Assess medication use, alcohol, symptoms, bleeding and barriers to treatment.","Acid suppression supports healing but does not eradicate H. pylori. The current ACG update recommends 14-day optimized bismuth quadruple therapy as a principal empiric regimen when appropriate; clarithromycin triple therapy requires demonstrated susceptibility.","Teach completion and confirmation of eradication. The test of cure is at least 4 weeks after antibiotics, with PPIs/PCABs withheld for 2 weeks under the clinician’s plan. Pain timing can suggest a pattern but cannot reliably locate an ulcer by itself."],"checks":["Explain why acid suppression and H. pylori eradication do different jobs.","Identify NSAID-related risk and explain adherence and test of cure."],"requires":[],"studio":"pud","drugs":true,"update":"hpylori","questions":[{"id":"47|trad|7","q":"Which statement by a client recovering from acute gastritis shows understanding of prevention?","o":["“I’ll use acetaminophen instead of ibuprofen for my back pain, as my provider suggested.”","“Switching to decaf coffee means I can drink as much as I want.”","“A glass of wine with dinner helps digestion.”","“Calcium carbonate antacids are best because they are strongest.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Long-term NSAIDs are the biggest cause of acute gastritis.","Incorrect. Coffee itself — even decaf — stimulates acid.","Incorrect. Alcohol irritates the mucosa.","Incorrect. Calcium carbonate triggers gastrin → rebound acid."],"ex":[],"phys":"NSAIDs block COX → less prostaglandin → thinner mucus–bicarbonate barrier and blood flow → acid injures the lining."},{"id":"47|trad|20","q":"An adult with H. pylori has no susceptibility result and no contraindication to the recommended regimen. Which prescription should the nurse recognize as the ACG 2024 recommended empiric optimized bismuth quadruple regimen?","o":["PPI, bismuth, tetracycline and metronidazole for 14 days","Clarithromycin alone until pain stops","PPI and one antibiotic for 3 days","Clarithromycin triple therapy for every patient regardless of susceptibility"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. This is the recommended optimized BQT regimen when susceptibility is unknown.","Incorrect. Monotherapy and symptom-based stopping do not provide reliable eradication.","Incorrect. This is not the recommended eradication regimen.","Incorrect. Current guidance requires proven clarithromycin susceptibility for that triple regimen."],"ex":[],"phys":"Combination treatment supports eradication; resistance changes which regimen should be selected."}]},{"id":"ulcer-drugs","guide":"47:0","ch":"47","title":"Acid drugs: match the mechanism to the timing","notes":["47-ulcer-and-gastritis-drugs-table-47-1"],"source":"Ch 47: Table 47.1, ulcer and gastritis drugs.","why":["PPIs inhibit the final proton pump step in acid secretion; H₂ blockers reduce histamine-stimulated secretion. Antacids neutralize acid already present. These mechanisms are different, even when symptoms improve with each.","Sucralfate forms a protective barrier over injured tissue. It is generally given on an empty stomach and separated from interacting medications and antacids according to the prescription and product directions.","Misoprostol replaces a prostaglandin effect to protect the mucosa during NSAID use; pregnancy is a major safety concern. Use the linked drug table for adverse effects, interactions and the full prescribed regimen."],"checks":["Distinguish suppression, neutralization and a mucosal barrier.","Explain why timing and spacing matter and name misoprostol’s major pregnancy precaution."],"requires":["pud"],"studio":"ulcer-drugs","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"47|trad|1","q":"When should the nurse give a client’s daily omeprazole?","o":["30 minutes before the main meal","With the main meal","At bedtime on an empty stomach","Two hours after breakfast"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Food switches the proton pumps on; the drug must already be in the blood to block them.","Incorrect. Many pumps have already secreted acid by then.","Incorrect. Pumps are least active overnight.","Incorrect. The pumps did their work with breakfast."],"ex":[],"phys":"A PPI binds proton pumps that are actively secreting → food is the signal that activates them → dosing 30 minutes before the meal puts the drug in the blood as the pumps switch on."},{"id":"47|trad|2","q":"A client takes sucralfate and an aluminum-magnesium antacid. Which schedule is correct?","o":["Sucralfate 1 hour before meals; the antacid at least 30 minutes apart from it","Both together with meals","Sucralfate right after the antacid","Sucralfate with food to prevent nausea"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Sucralfate needs an acid environment and an empty stomach to coat the ulcer; antacids and food interfere.","Incorrect. Food and antacid block the coating.","Incorrect. The antacid raises pH and stops sucralfate from forming its paste.","Incorrect. Food interferes with it."],"ex":[],"phys":"In acid, sucralfate forms a sticky paste → it binds to the ulcer crater and shields it from acid and pepsin → antacids and food stop the paste from forming."}]},{"id":"gi-bleed","guide":"47:1","ch":"47","title":"Upper GI bleeding: perfusion before reassurance","notes":["47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies"],"source":"Ch 47: upper GI bleeding, ulcer emergencies and nursing priorities.","why":["Hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis and melena suggest GI bleeding. Blood loss can reduce venous return and cardiac output; tachycardia, hypotension, confusion and reduced urine output suggest impaired perfusion.","Assess airway and hemodynamic stability, activate help for instability, keep NPO and prepare prescribed IV access, fluids, labs, blood products and endoscopic treatment. An early hemoglobin alone can be misleading during acute loss.","Sudden severe pain with a rigid abdomen suggests perforation and demands urgent evaluation. Improvement means recovering perfusion and stable trends, not simply a change from red to coffee-ground drainage."],"checks":["Link blood loss to pulse, pressure, mental status and urine output.","Name evidence of recovery and a finding suggesting perforation."],"requires":["pud"],"studio":"gi-bleed","caseId":"ugib","questions":[{"id":"47|trad|15","q":"A client admitted with a gastric ulcer has these findings this morning. Which should the nurse report to the provider immediately?","o":["Hematocrit 30%, down from 38% on admission","Serum creatinine 1.0 mg/dL","Epigastric pain 3/10 after breakfast","Fasting blood glucose 96 mg/dL"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A falling hematocrit points to bleeding — hemorrhage is the most serious ulcer complication. Check vital signs and orthostatics and report at once.","Incorrect. Within the normal range.","Incorrect. Expected with a gastric ulcer and within the goal of 3 or less.","Incorrect. Normal."],"ex":[],"phys":"An ulcer that erodes into a vessel bleeds into the gut → red cells are lost → as fluid shifts in to restore volume, hemoglobin and hematocrit fall → a falling BP and rising HR follow if the loss continues."},{"id":"47|trad|16","q":"A client was treated for upper GI bleeding from a duodenal ulcer with IV fluids, 2 units of packed RBCs and endoscopic therapy. Which finding the next day best shows that treatment has been effective?","o":["BP 118/74 and HR 84 with no change on standing; hemoglobin stable on two checks","NG drainage has changed from bright red to coffee-ground","Urine output 20 mL/hr for the past 3 hours","BUN has risen from 28 to 36 mg/dL"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Normal vital signs with no orthostatic change and a stable hemoglobin mean volume is restored and the bleeding has stopped — the expected outcome, and a discharge criterion.","Incorrect. Coffee-ground drainage is still blood, digested by acid; it does not prove the bleeding has stopped.","Incorrect. Under 30 mL/hr suggests the kidneys are still underperfused.","Incorrect. A rising BUN suggests continued bleeding (digested blood) or dehydration."],"ex":[],"phys":"With the vessel sealed and volume replaced, venous return and cardiac output recover → BP normalizes, the compensatory tachycardia settles and standing no longer drops the BP → hemoglobin stops falling."}]},{"id":"gastric-cancer","guide":"47:1","ch":"47","title":"Gastric cancer and life after gastrectomy","notes":["47-gastric-cancer-and-life-after-gastrectom"],"source":"Ch 47: gastric cancer, gastrectomy, dumping and discharge teaching.","why":["Persistent indigestion, early satiety, weight loss or anemia warrant assessment; early symptoms can be vague. H. pylori and dietary exposures are among the chapter’s risk factors. Support nutrition and access to evaluation.","After gastrectomy, assess for bleeding, anastomotic complications, intake problems and altered nutrient absorption. Loss of intrinsic-factor production after total gastrectomy requires a lifelong vitamin B₁₂ replacement plan.","Rapid emptying can cause early dumping: fluid shifts bring cramping, diarrhea and vasomotor symptoms soon after eating. Later, an insulin response may cause hypoglycemia. Small frequent meals, limiting simple sugars and separating fluids from meals are part of the individualized plan."],"checks":["Explain why vague persistent symptoms need follow-up.","Connect total gastrectomy to B₁₂ and distinguish early from late dumping."],"requires":["gi-bleed"],"studio":"gastric-cancer","questions":[{"id":"47|trad|6","q":"A client is going home after a total gastrectomy. Which teaching is essential?","o":["You will need vitamin B₁₂ injections for life","Take vitamin B₁₂ tablets with each meal","You will need iron only if you feel tired","Your diet will return to normal in a week"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Without the stomach there is no intrinsic factor, so B₁₂ can’t be absorbed from food or pills — only injections work.","Incorrect. Oral B₁₂ needs intrinsic factor.","Incorrect. Iron, folate, calcium and vitamin D deficiencies are also expected and monitored.","Incorrect. Small meals and dumping precautions continue."],"ex":[],"phys":"Parietal cells make intrinsic factor → it binds B₁₂ so the ileum can absorb it → removing the stomach removes intrinsic factor → B₁₂ stores run out → pernicious (megaloblastic) anemia and a beefy tongue."},{"id":"47|ngn|6","q":"Which instructions help prevent dumping syndrome after a gastrectomy? Select all that apply.","o":["Eat small amounts at a time","Don’t drink liquids with meals","Choose high-protein, high-fat, low-sugar foods","Lie down flat for a short time after eating","Drink a full glass of water with each meal","Have a sweet dessert with each meal for calories","Take a brisk walk right after eating"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. Smaller loads empty more slowly into the small intestine.","Correct. Liquids speed the meal through; drink between meals instead.","Correct. Protein and fat empty slowly; sugar causes the late insulin overshoot.","Correct. Lying down slows emptying.","Incorrect. Fluid with meals washes the food into the jejunum faster.","Incorrect. Concentrated sweets trigger both kinds of dumping.","Incorrect. Being upright and active speeds emptying; lie down briefly instead."],"ex":[],"phys":"Without the stomach as a reservoir, anything that adds volume (fluids), concentration (sugar) or gravity (standing, walking) speeds contents into the jejunum → fluid shifts and sugar spikes. Small, dry, protein-rich meals and lying down slow them."}]},{"id":"glands","guide":"53:0","ch":"53","title":"The pancreas, thyroid and parathyroids set the controls","notes":["53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre"],"source":"Ch 53: pancreas, thyroid and parathyroid anatomy and physiology (assigned portion).","why":["Pancreatic beta cells release insulin: glucose uptake and fuel storage increase, and hepatic glucose output and ketone production decrease. Alpha-cell glucagon promotes hepatic glucose release when fuel is needed.","The pituitary’s TSH stimulates thyroid T₃/T₄. Thyroid hormones affect metabolic rate. Negative feedback means rising thyroid hormone normally suppresses TSH; interpret the pair rather than one result alone.","Parathyroid hormone helps raise serum calcium through bone, kidney and vitamin D-related intestinal effects. Low calcium increases neuromuscular excitability. Calcitonin has an opposing calcium-lowering effect."],"checks":["Contrast insulin and glucagon on liver glucose output.","Explain thyroid negative feedback and the direction of PTH’s calcium effect."],"requires":[],"studio":"glands","questions":[{"id":"glands-a","q":"A fasting client asks how glucose can remain available without eating. Which explanation is accurate?","o":["Glucagon promotes release of glucose from the liver","Insulin normally makes liver glucose output rise during a fast","TSH directly replaces dietary glucose","PTH breaks glucose into ketones"],"a":[0],"r":["Glucagon supports hepatic glucose production and release.","Insulin generally suppresses hepatic glucose output.","TSH signals the thyroid; it does not supply glucose.","PTH primarily regulates calcium and phosphate."]},{"id":"glands-b","q":"A client has high free T₄ from an overactive thyroid gland. With intact pituitary feedback, which TSH pattern is expected?","o":["High, because T₄ stimulates TSH release","Low, because T₄ suppresses pituitary TSH release","Always normal regardless of T₄","TSH and T₄ measure the same hormone"],"a":[1],"r":["Rising T₄ normally suppresses TSH through negative feedback.","An overactive gland can raise T₄ while feedback lowers TSH.","TSH responds to thyroid-hormone feedback.","TSH is a pituitary signal; T₄ is a thyroid hormone."]}]},{"id":"endocrine-aging","guide":"53:1","ch":"53","title":"Endocrine aging: fluids, bone and glucose","notes":["53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g"],"source":"Ch 53: endocrine changes associated with aging (assigned portion; not the COVID section).","why":["The chapter connects aging with changes in fluid regulation and thirst, estrogen-related bone loss and declining glucose tolerance. These are assessment considerations, not reasons to dismiss new illness.","Offer and monitor fluids within the person’s prescribed plan, assess nutrition and falls risk, and watch for hyperglycemia clues such as recurrent infection or slow healing. Heart or kidney disease can require fluid restrictions.","New confusion, weakness or functional decline needs assessment. Compare with baseline, review medications and investigate reversible causes."],"checks":["Connect aging to fluid, bone and glucose risks.","Adapt prevention to the patient’s restrictions and report a new change."],"requires":[],"studio":"endocrine-aging","questions":[{"id":"53|trad|0","q":"Why does the nurse teach assistive personnel to offer an 84-year-old client fluids at least every 2 hours while awake?","o":["Less ADH means the kidneys can’t concentrate urine well, so dehydration comes fast","Older adults retain too much water","It prevents hypoglycemia","Aging increases thirst"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. With little ADH reserve, urine stays dilute even when intake is low.","Incorrect. The problem is losing water, not keeping it.","Incorrect. Not the reason.","Incorrect. Thirst is blunted with aging."],"ex":[],"phys":"ADH makes the kidney reabsorb water → aging lowers ADH reserve → urine stays dilute when intake falls → water is lost → dehydration, orthostatic hypotension, confusion."},{"id":"53|trad|5","q":"Which finding in an older adult should prompt the nurse to suggest diabetes testing?","o":["A foot wound that has not healed in 2 weeks","Hair thinning on the scalp","A heart rate of 64","Dry skin in winter"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Also persistent yeast infections, persistent hunger and thirst, or a lasting drop in energy.","Incorrect. Common with aging.","Incorrect. Normal.","Incorrect. Common."],"ex":[],"phys":"High glucose impairs white cell function and blood flow → wounds heal slowly and yeast thrives → these are often the first clues in older adults."}]},{"id":"urine","guide":"53:2","ch":"53","title":"A 24-hour urine result is only as good as the collection","notes":["53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job"],"source":"Ch 53: 24-hour urine specimen collection, assigned diagnostic-test portion.","why":["At the exact start time, have the patient void and discard that urine. Collect every subsequent void throughout the interval, including the final void at the exact end time.","Use the correct container, preservative and storage conditions for the ordered test; follow lab instructions. Explain the plan to the patient and everyone helping with toileting.","A missed or discarded specimen can invalidate the result. Notify the appropriate team and follow the lab’s restart instructions; do not silently estimate the missing volume."],"checks":["State what happens to the start void and the end void.","Explain how storage or a missed void can invalidate the test."],"requires":[],"studio":"urine","questions":[{"id":"53|trad|15","q":"The nurse delegates the 24-hour urine collection to the assistive personnel (AP). Which instruction is correct?","o":["“Save every void in the container on ice until 0800 tomorrow, including the 0800 void, and tell me if any urine is thrown away.”","“Save the urine only when the client remembers to use the hat.”","“Throw away tomorrow’s 0800 void — that one is extra.”","“If one void is missed, just add extra water so the total looks right.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Clear, specific, with what to report — a missed void means the test must restart.","Incorrect. Every void must be saved.","Incorrect. The final void is kept; it is the first one that is discarded.","Incorrect. That falsifies the result."],"ex":[],"phys":"A correct total depends on every void in exactly 24 hours → the AP can collect if told exactly what to save and when → reporting a lost void lets the RN restart the test."},{"id":"urine-b","q":"During a 24-hour urine collection, a client accidentally voids into the toilet. What should the nurse do?","o":["Estimate the amount and continue without reporting it","Notify the team/lab and follow instructions about restarting the collection","Collect for an extra hour without asking","Send the incomplete sample labeled as complete"],"a":[1],"r":["Estimating volume does not replace the missing analyte.","The result may be unreliable; the lab determines the appropriate restart plan.","An extra hour does not reliably replace the missed interval.","Labeling an incomplete specimen as complete can lead to a false interpretation."]}]},{"id":"hypothyroid","guide":"55:0","ch":"55","title":"Hypothyroidism: slower metabolism and careful replacement","notes":["55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","55-overview","55-adpie"],"source":"Ch 55: hypothyroidism, levothyroxine and nursing safety.","why":["Low thyroid hormone slows metabolic activity: fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin and bradycardia can result. Primary gland failure usually produces low free T₄ with high TSH; pituitary causes have a different pattern.","Levothyroxine replaces hormone. Teach consistent administration on an empty stomach as prescribed and separation from calcium and iron according to directions. Follow symptoms and thyroid labs over time.","Older adults and people with coronary disease need careful titration: increased metabolic demand can stress the heart. Severe hypothyroidism also increases sensitivity to sedating medications."],"checks":["Connect low hormone to assessment findings and primary-hypothyroid labs.","Explain administration, interaction and cardiac precautions with levothyroxine."],"requires":["glands"],"studio":"hypothyroid","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"55|trad|5","q":"A 76-year-old with coronary artery disease starts levothyroxine. What should the nurse teach?","o":["Report chest pain or shortness of breath right away","Expect a high dose at first to correct the problem quickly","Take it with your calcium supplement","Stop it if you feel your heart beating faster"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Rising metabolism raises the heart’s oxygen demand → angina, dysrhythmias, MI if the dose rises too fast.","Incorrect. Start low, go slow.","Incorrect. Separate from calcium by 4 hours.","Incorrect. Report it; never change the dose alone."],"ex":[],"phys":"Thyroid hormone increases oxygen use in every tissue, including the myocardium → a narrowed coronary artery can’t increase supply → ischemia."},{"id":"55|trad|14","q":"A client who started levothyroxine 3 months ago says she is still exhausted and wears a cardigan even on warm days. She takes the pill with breakfast, along with her calcium and iron supplements. Which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","o":["Take vital signs, including temperature","Review the medication record and ask about missed doses","Teach her to take levothyroxine alone on an empty stomach, at least 4 hours apart from the calcium and iron","Report the findings so the provider can check the TSH","Tell her to double the dose until she feels warm","Assess her swallowing reflex"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. A slow pulse and low temperature would confirm persisting hypothyroid signs.","Correct. Missed doses or wrong timing are common reasons replacement falls short.","Correct. Food, calcium and iron reduce absorption, so she is likely absorbing too little.","Correct. The final dose is set by the TSH and the client’s response.","Incorrect. Never change the dose without the provider; too much too fast stresses the heart.","Incorrect. Nothing suggests a swallowing problem."],"ex":[],"phys":"Levothyroxine is absorbed in the small bowel → food, calcium and iron bind or delay it → less hormone reaches the blood → metabolism stays slow → she stays tired and cold, and the TSH stays high."}]},{"id":"myxedema","guide":"55:0","ch":"55","title":"Myxedema crisis: slow breathing is an emergency","notes":["55-myxedema-coma"],"source":"Ch 55: myxedema coma and emergency nursing care.","why":["Severe decompensated hypothyroidism can cause hypoventilation, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension and altered mental status. The term myxedema coma does not require the patient to be fully comatose.","Activate emergency help, support airway and ventilation, and prepare prescribed thyroid-hormone and other supportive therapy. Monitor cardiac rhythm, glucose, sodium, temperature and perfusion.","Avoid actions that further depress breathing. Use ordered, cautious warming and treat the precipitating illness; this is not a routine follow-up for fatigue."],"checks":["Recognize the combination of low temperature, depressed breathing and mental-status change.","State the immediate respiratory priority and why sedatives are risky."],"requires":["hypothyroid"],"studio":"myxedema","questions":[{"id":"myxedema-a","q":"A client with untreated hypothyroidism is minimally responsive, T 34.7°C, RR 7 and BP 80/48. Which action has priority?","o":["Arrange an outpatient TSH check","Activate emergency help and support airway and ventilation","Give a sedative for restlessness","Encourage an unassisted walk to warm up"],"a":[1],"r":["Severely depressed breathing and circulation need immediate care.","Hypoventilation and instability are immediate threats in suspected myxedema crisis.","Sedation can worsen respiratory depression.","The client is unstable and minimally responsive."]},{"id":"myxedema-b","q":"A client recovering from myxedema crisis becomes more difficult to arouse after an opioid. What should the nurse assess immediately?","o":["Respiratory rate, ventilation and oxygenation","Only the timing of the next thyroid lab","Whether constipation has resolved","Whether the client wants another opioid"],"a":[0],"r":["Severe hypothyroidism increases vulnerability to respiratory depression; assess and escalate promptly.","A scheduled lab does not address a new airway or breathing threat.","Constipation is lower priority than ventilation.","Further sedation can worsen the immediate problem."]}]},{"id":"hyperthyroid","guide":"55:1","ch":"55","title":"Hyperthyroidism: excess drive and targeted treatments","notes":["55-hyperthyroidism-and-graves-disease","55-treating-hyperthyroidism-drugs-radioacti"],"source":"Ch 55: Graves disease, antithyroid drugs, radioactive iodine and comfort/eye care.","why":["Excess thyroid hormone increases metabolic activity: heat intolerance, weight loss, tachycardia, tremor and anxiety can result. Primary hyperthyroidism typically has suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormone; Graves is an autoimmune cause.","Antithyroid drugs reduce new hormone synthesis; beta blockers control adrenergic symptoms. Teach urgent reporting of fever or sore throat with methimazole because of agranulocytosis risk. Radioactive iodine has pregnancy and radiation precautions.","Support rest, nutrition and coping. With Graves eye disease, protect exposed corneas and follow the eye-care plan. Emotional symptoms deserve assessment and support."],"checks":["Connect excess hormone to symptoms and feedback labs.","Distinguish hormone synthesis treatment from symptom control and name an urgent antithyroid-drug warning."],"requires":["glands"],"studio":"hyperthyroid","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"55|trad|1","q":"Which instruction is important for a client starting methimazole?","o":["Avoid crowds and sick people, and report pregnancy","Expect relief within 24 hours","Take it with an iodine supplement from the store","Stop it if you feel cold"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It lowers the immune response, and it can cause birth defects (the drug may be switched in the first trimester).","Incorrect. Relief is delayed because stored hormone keeps being released.","Incorrect. Iodine is given only as ordered.","Incorrect. Report cold intolerance, weight gain or a slow pulse — the dose may be too high — but don’t stop on your own."],"ex":[],"phys":"Methimazole stops iodide from binding in the gland → no new hormone → but stored hormone keeps releasing for weeks → relief is delayed; it can also suppress white cells."},{"id":"55|trad|3","q":"A client with Graves disease asks why propranolol was added. Which response is accurate?","o":["It relieves the racing heart, sweating and anxiety while other treatment takes effect","It stops the thyroid from making hormone","It cures Graves disease","It replaces thyroid hormone"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Beta blockers treat effects now; they don’t lower hormone production.","Incorrect. That is the thionamide.","Incorrect. It is supportive only.","Incorrect. That is levothyroxine."],"ex":[],"phys":"Thyroid hormone increases sympathetic sensitivity → beta receptors drive tachycardia, tremor and sweating → blocking them relieves symptoms; propranolol also slows T4 → T3 conversion."}]},{"id":"thyroidectomy","guide":"55:1","ch":"55","title":"After thyroidectomy: airway, bleeding and calcium","notes":["55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm"],"source":"Ch 55: postoperative thyroidectomy assessment and complications.","why":["After neck surgery, swelling, hematoma or laryngeal dysfunction can threaten the airway. Assess breathing, neck swelling/tightness, bleeding including behind the neck, and changes in the voice.","Stridor or rapidly worsening swelling requires immediate help and airway support. Maintain prescribed positioning and the unit’s emergency equipment plan. Stable mild hoarseness still needs assessment and trending.","Parathyroid disruption can lower calcium. Perioral/finger tingling, tetany or spasms need prompt assessment, calcium review and prescribed treatment; laryngospasm is an airway emergency."],"checks":["Distinguish a stable voice change from stridor or expanding neck swelling.","Connect tingling and tetany to postoperative low calcium."],"requires":["hyperthyroid"],"studio":"thyroidectomy","caseId":"thyroidectomy","questions":[{"id":"55|ngn|4","q":"Which actions belong in the care of a client just back from a thyroidectomy? Select all that apply.","o":["Vital signs every 15 minutes until stable","Semi-Fowler with the head and neck supported; avoid neck extension","Check the dressing and behind the neck for blood","Keep tracheostomy equipment, oxygen and suction in the room","Check the voice every 2 hours","Keep the client flat with the neck hyperextended","Remove the drain if serosanguineous fluid collects"],"a":[0,1,2,3,4],"r":["Correct. Monitoring for complications is the most important nursing action.","Correct. Supports the incision and breathing.","Correct. Blood pools behind the neck; bleeding can compress the trachea.","Correct. Swelling, bleeding, tetany or nerve damage can close the airway.","Correct. Hoarseness shows laryngeal nerve injury (usually temporary).","Incorrect. Extension strains the incision; semi-Fowler with support.","Incorrect. A moderate amount of serosanguineous drainage is normal."],"ex":[],"phys":"The gland lies on the trachea (bleeding and swelling compress it), with the parathyroids behind it (calcium) and the laryngeal nerves beside it (voice, cord closure) → each complication follows from anatomy."},{"id":"55|ngn|3","q":"Review the nurse’s note 8 hours after a total thyroidectomy. Which actions should the nurse take? Select all that apply.","o":["Report the findings and check the serum calcium","Make sure IV calcium gluconate is available","Watch for stridor or laryngospasm","Document the tingling as an expected effect of anesthesia","Have the client extend the neck to ease the twitching"],"a":[0,1,2],"r":["Correct. Tingling and a positive Chvostek sign mean low calcium — the parathyroids were bruised or removed.","Correct. Acute hypocalcemia is treated with IV calcium gluconate.","Correct. Low calcium can make the larynx spasm — an airway emergency.","Incorrect. These are calcium signs, not anesthesia effects.","Incorrect. Avoid neck extension after thyroidectomy; it doesn’t treat low calcium."],"ex":[{"h":"Nurse’s note","lines":["Client reports tingling around the mouth and in the fingertips. Facial twitch when the cheek in front of the ear is tapped.","Voice slightly hoarse. Dressing dry; nothing behind the neck. SpO₂ 97%, no stridor."]}],"phys":"The parathyroids are removed, bruised or lose their blood supply → PTH falls → serum calcium falls → nerve membranes become too excitable → tingling, twitching, tetany, laryngospasm."}]},{"id":"thyroid-storm","guide":"55:1","ch":"55","title":"Thyroid storm: fever plus cardiovascular and brain changes","notes":["55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm"],"source":"Ch 55: thyroid storm, precipitating factors and emergency management.","why":["Severe thyrotoxicosis with systemic decompensation can produce marked fever, tachycardia, agitation/delirium and cardiovascular instability. Infection, surgery or interrupted treatment can precipitate it.","Activate emergency care, assess airway, breathing and circulation, monitor continuously, and prepare the ordered antithyroid, cardiovascular and supportive therapies. Cooling, fluids and treatment of the trigger are important.","When ordered for storm, iodine follows a thionamide so hormone synthesis is blocked before iodine is supplied. Monitor response across temperature, heart rate, mentation and perfusion rather than using a single number."],"checks":["Distinguish storm from uncomplicated heat intolerance.","Name emergency priorities and explain why ordered antithyroid-treatment sequence matters."],"requires":["hyperthyroid"],"studio":"thyroid-storm","questions":[{"id":"storm-a","q":"A client with uncontrolled hyperthyroidism develops T 40°C, HR 156, agitation and falling BP during an infection. What is the priority?","o":["Document expected anxiety and reassess next shift","Activate emergency care for possible thyroid storm","Delay assessment until a routine TSH returns","Encourage vigorous exercise to reduce restlessness"],"a":[1],"r":["Fever, severe tachycardia and instability indicate decompensation.","This constellation requires emergency assessment and treatment.","Treatment of instability cannot wait for a routine result.","Exercise increases physiologic demand in an unstable client."]},{"id":"storm-b","q":"Which trend best supports improvement during treatment for thyroid storm?","o":["Heart rate and temperature falling toward target, clearer mentation and stable perfusion","Temperature falls but new hypotension and confusion develop","TSH is still suppressed, so all clinical improvement is irrelevant","The client is harder to awaken after a sedative"],"a":[0],"r":["Improvement spans metabolic drive, neurologic status and circulation.","New hypotension and confusion suggest worsening perfusion or another complication.","Clinical trends guide immediate response; TSH can remain suppressed.","Reduced arousal may reflect deterioration or a medication effect."]}]},{"id":"thyroiditis-cancer","guide":"55:1","ch":"55","title":"Thyroiditis, cancer and ongoing replacement","notes":["55-thyroiditis-and-thyroid-cancer"],"source":"Ch 55: thyroiditis, thyroid cancer and postoperative transition teaching.","why":["An inflamed thyroid can release stored hormone, causing a temporary hyperthyroid phase, then a hypothyroid phase. Painful subacute thyroiditis may follow a viral illness; follow symptoms and labs over time.","A thyroid nodule or a persistent local change needs evaluation. Thyroid cancer treatment and prognosis depend on type and stage; do not assume every thyroid cancer behaves the same way.","After total thyroidectomy, lifelong hormone replacement and follow-up are necessary. Teach wound and airway concerns, medication use, any prescribed radioactive-iodine precautions, and when to report symptoms."],"checks":["Explain how thyroiditis can change hormone patterns over time.","Describe follow-up and replacement needs after total thyroidectomy."],"requires":["hypothyroid","thyroidectomy"],"studio":"thyroiditis-cancer","questions":[{"id":"55|trad|15","q":"Two weeks after a cold, a client has a painful, hard, enlarged thyroid, palpitations and heat intolerance. Which statement by the nurse about this subacute thyroiditis is accurate?","o":["“The overactive phase usually passes; the gland may then be underactive for a while before it returns to normal.”","“You will need thyroid hormone replacement for the rest of your life.”","“This is a bacterial infection, so the gland will need to be drained surgically.”","“A painful thyroid like this is the usual first sign of thyroid cancer.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Viral (subacute, granulomatous) thyroiditis typically runs hyperthyroid → hypothyroid → normal, though function can stay normal.","Incorrect. That fits Hashimoto thyroiditis, which is painless and leads to lasting hypothyroidism.","Incorrect. Infectious (bacterial) thyroiditis causes sudden one-sided pain and fever and usually resolves with IV antibiotics; this one follows a viral illness.","Incorrect. Thyroid cancer usually starts as a single painless nodule."],"ex":[],"phys":"A virus inflames the gland → follicles break open and stored hormone leaks out (hyperthyroid) → the store runs out and damaged cells make little (hypothyroid) → the gland heals (euthyroid)."},{"id":"55|trad|16","q":"A client is going home after a total thyroidectomy with neck lymph node dissection for papillary thyroid cancer. Which statements show correct understanding? Select all that apply.","o":["“I will take thyroid hormone every day for the rest of my life.”","“I may get radioactive iodine to destroy any thyroid tissue that is left.”","“I will report tingling around my mouth or in my fingers right away.”","“Papillary cancer grows slowly, so this surgery gives me a good chance of cure.”","“Once my energy is back, I can stop the thyroid pills.”","“Radioactive iodine is the main treatment for every type of thyroid cancer.”"],"a":[0,1,2,3],"r":["Correct. With the whole gland gone, the client is hypothyroid and needs lifelong replacement.","Correct. RAI may follow surgery to destroy remaining tissue.","Correct. The parathyroids may have been injured; tingling means low calcium.","Correct. Papillary cancer is the most common, slow-growing type and is often curable when confined to the thyroid.","Incorrect. Replacement is lifelong; stopping it can lead to myxedema coma.","Incorrect. RAI is not used for anaplastic cancer, which is treated mainly with external beam radiation and/or chemotherapy."],"ex":[],"phys":"Removing the gland removes all T3 and T4 → lifelong replacement; the parathyroids sit behind it → calcium can fall; papillary cells still trap iodine → RAI destroys what is left."}]},{"id":"calcium","guide":"55:2","ch":"55","title":"Parathyroids: low calcium excites, high calcium weakens","notes":["55-hypoparathyroidism-and-hyperparathyroidi"],"source":"Ch 55: hypoparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism and parathyroidectomy.","why":["Too little PTH can lower calcium: tingling, cramps, tetany, seizures and laryngospasm reflect increased excitability. Assess symptoms and calcium trends; severe symptoms require urgent care and prescribed calcium treatment.","Excess PTH can raise serum calcium while weakening bones. Stones, constipation, weakness and cognitive changes may occur. Protect against falls and fractures and follow the fluid and treatment plan.","After parathyroidectomy, calcium can fall as bone takes it up and hormone effects change. Monitor closely. Long-term treatment may include calcium and active vitamin D for low calcium, or other therapies for high calcium; these are not interchangeable."],"checks":["Contrast low-calcium neuromuscular findings with high-calcium bone/renal risks.","Explain postoperative monitoring and why calcium and vitamin D may be prescribed."],"requires":["glands"],"studio":"calcium","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"55|trad|9","q":"Why is a client at risk for hypocalcemic crisis after removal of a parathyroid adenoma?","o":["The remaining glands were suppressed and take days to weeks to recover","Surgery causes vitamin D toxicity","The kidneys stop excreting calcium","Calcium is lost through the drain"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Years of high calcium shut the normal glands down; they can’t restart at once.","Incorrect. Not the mechanism.","Incorrect. The opposite problem.","Incorrect. Not the mechanism."],"ex":[],"phys":"The adenoma kept calcium high → feedback switched the other glands off → they atrophied → after removal, PTH is suddenly low → calcium falls → tetany."},{"id":"55|trad|10","q":"Which statement shows a client understands long-term treatment of hypoparathyroidism?","o":["“I’ll take my calcium and calcitriol every day for life and wear a medical alert bracelet.”","“I can stop the calcium once the tingling goes away.”","“I should keep my calcium as high as possible.”","“I need to avoid all dairy products.”"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Therapy is lifelong.","Incorrect. Stopping brings symptoms back.","Incorrect. Calcium is kept low-normal — enough to prevent symptoms, low enough to keep urine calcium down and prevent stones.","Incorrect. Calcium-rich foods in moderation are encouraged."],"ex":[],"phys":"Without PTH, calcium is not reabsorbed or released from bone → replacement keeps it up → but without PTH the kidney spills more calcium → high doses cause stones."}]},{"id":"diabetes-types","guide":"56:0","ch":"56","title":"Diabetes types: different routes to ineffective insulin","notes":["56-types-risk-and-screening"],"source":"Ch 56: types, risk factors, prevention and screening.","why":["Type 1 usually involves autoimmune beta-cell destruction and severe insulin deficiency. Type 2 combines insulin resistance with progressive beta-cell dysfunction. Both require individualized care; body size or age alone does not determine type.","Glucose above the kidney’s reabsorption capacity spills into urine and draws water: polyuria causes dehydration and thirst. Insufficient effective insulin also alters fuel storage and use.","Risk assessment, appropriate screening, nutrition, activity and weight-management support can reduce or delay type 2 risk. Other forms, including monogenic diabetes, need type-specific assessment and treatment."],"checks":["Contrast type 1 insulin deficiency with type 2 insulin resistance and declining secretion.","Explain polyuria and thirst and why diabetes treatment must match the type."],"requires":["glands"],"studio":"diabetes-types","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|21","q":"Which feature is typical of type 1 diabetes?","o":["Abrupt onset with weight loss and ketosis, needing insulin","Gradual onset with obesity and no symptoms","Usually managed with diet and pills","Caused by insulin resistance"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Autoimmune beta-cell destruction → absolute insulin lack → ketosis-prone; everyone needs insulin.","Incorrect. Typical of type 2.","Incorrect. Type 2.","Incorrect. Type 2."],"ex":[],"phys":"Autoimmune destruction of beta cells → no insulin → glucose can’t enter cells → fat is burned (weight loss, ketones) → abrupt symptoms."},{"id":"56|trad|34","q":"A young adult is diagnosed with MODY after genetic evaluation. Which statement by the nurse is accurate?","o":["Treatment depends on the genetic subtype; insulin is not required for every form","MODY is always autoimmune type 1 diabetes","All MODY requires the same lifelong insulin regimen","The diagnosis means glucose monitoring and follow-up are unnecessary"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Monogenic subtypes differ in their treatment requirements.","Incorrect. MODY is a monogenic disorder, not automatically autoimmune type 1.","Incorrect. Some subtypes need insulin, others use different management.","Incorrect. Follow-up is individualized; the label does not remove care needs."],"ex":[],"phys":"Different gene defects alter insulin secretion in different ways, so a type 1 treatment assumption can be wrong."}]},{"id":"monitoring","guide":"56:0","ch":"56","title":"Glucose, A1C and CGM answer different questions","notes":["56-diagnosing-and-monitoring-glucose-a1c-cg"],"source":"Ch 56: diagnosis and monitoring; current device-specific CGM clarification in source review.","why":["Diagnostic thresholds include A1C ≥6.5%, fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL, or 2-hour OGTT ≥200 mg/dL. Random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms or crisis can establish diabetes; otherwise confirm abnormal testing as appropriate.","A1C reflects longer-term glycemia, while a meter gives a point-in-time glucose. CGM measures interstitial glucose and shows trends; lag or sensor issues can cause disagreement with symptoms or a meter.","Calibration and confirmatory meter requirements depend on the specific device. Keep BGM available and follow its instructions when readings do not match expectations. For symptomatic suspected hypoglycemia, do not delay necessary treatment."],"checks":["Distinguish diagnostic testing, a spot glucose and longer-term A1C.","Explain CGM limitations and device-specific meter confirmation."],"requires":["diabetes-types"],"studio":"monitoring","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|0","q":"Which result supports a diagnosis of diabetes when repeated?","o":["Fasting glucose of 132 mg/dL","A1C of 6.0%","Fasting glucose of 108 mg/dL","2-hour OGTT of 165 mg/dL"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Fasting glucose ≥ 126 (8 hours without calories) is diagnostic, confirmed by a second abnormal result.","Incorrect. 5.7–6.4% is prediabetes.","Incorrect. 100–125 is prediabetes (impaired fasting glucose).","Incorrect. 140–199 is prediabetes."],"ex":[],"phys":"Without enough insulin action, the liver keeps releasing glucose overnight → fasting glucose stays high → ≥ 126 marks diabetes."},{"id":"56|trad|33","q":"A client switches from an older CGM to a new real-time system whose instructions state user calibration is not required. Which teaching is appropriate?","o":["Follow the new device instructions and keep a BGM meter available for suspected discordance or device interruption","Calibrate every system twice a day because all CGMs require it","Discard the BGM meter because CGM can never be inaccurate","Use A1C instead of checking a suspected immediate low"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Calibration depends on the actual system; BGM remains available for situations requiring confirmation.","Incorrect. That blanket instruction reflects older devices.","Incorrect. Sensor discordance, warm-up and interrupted readings can require BGM.","Incorrect. A1C describes longer-term exposure, not an immediate glucose level."],"ex":[],"phys":"CGM measures interstitial glucose; changing technology does not eliminate the need to assess a mismatch with the clinical situation."}]},{"id":"insulin","guide":"56:1","ch":"56","title":"Insulin: basal coverage, meals and safe delivery","notes":["56-insulin-types-timing-injection-and-stora"],"source":"Ch 56: insulin types, timing, injection, storage and pump care.","why":["Basal insulin covers ongoing needs between meals and overnight. Prandial insulin covers meals; correction doses address elevations under the plan. Match rapid/short-acting insulin timing to food and orders, and know the product’s onset and peak.","Check the correct product, concentration, dose and route. Rotate sites within an area and avoid damaged tissue. Storage, mixing rules and in-use discard times depend on the insulin product; use the exact label and pharmacy instructions.","A pump interruption can rapidly remove insulin delivery. Persistent unexpected hyperglycemia needs assessment of the delivery system, ketones when indicated, and the prescribed backup plan. A person with type 1 diabetes needs basal insulin even when not eating, with dose adjustment by the team."],"checks":["Distinguish basal, meal and correction roles.","Explain why missed food, wrong products and pump interruption can cause harm."],"requires":["glands"],"studio":"insulin","drugs":true,"questions":[{"id":"insulin-a","q":"A client with type 1 diabetes is NPO and asks to stop all insulin. Which explanation is appropriate?","o":["Basal insulin is still needed; the team adjusts doses and monitoring for fasting","All insulin is only for food, so none is needed","Double the usual meal dose without checking orders","Switch to an unprescribed oral drug for the day"],"a":[0],"r":["Basal coverage restrains hepatic glucose output and ketone production even during fasting.","Omitting all insulin can precipitate DKA in type 1 diabetes.","Meal dosing while fasting requires the prescribed plan, not doubling.","Substituting a medication does not replace the basal insulin requirement."]},{"id":"insulin-b","q":"A client’s pump reports an occlusion and glucose is rising. Which response is appropriate?","o":["Wait until the next routine appointment","Assess delivery, check glucose/ketones as directed, and use the prescribed backup/contact plan promptly","Assume a pump can never cause interrupted insulin delivery","Stop monitoring until the pump resets itself"],"a":[1],"r":["An interruption can lead to insulin deficiency and DKA.","Address possible delivery failure promptly and follow the individualized backup plan.","Occlusion can interrupt delivery.","Monitoring is essential during suspected delivery failure."]}]},{"id":"diabetes-drugs","guide":"56:1","ch":"56","title":"Non-insulin drugs: predict the risk from the mechanism","notes":["56-antidiabetic-drugs-non-insulin"],"source":"Ch 56: antidiabetic drugs (non-insulin).","why":["Metformin reduces hepatic glucose output; sulfonylureas increase insulin secretion and can cause hypoglycemia. Risk changes with renal function, intake and other medications.","GLP-1 receptor agonists affect glucose-dependent insulin secretion and gastric emptying; SGLT2 inhibitors increase urinary glucose loss. SGLT2-associated ketoacidosis can occur without a marked glucose rise. Follow drug-specific sick-day and perioperative instructions.","Thiazolidinediones can cause fluid retention; watch for edema, rapid weight gain or heart-failure symptoms. Use the full drug cards for contraindications, administration, other classes and adverse effects rather than treating all diabetes pills alike."],"checks":["Match secretion, liver glucose output and urinary glucose loss to drug classes.","Predict hypoglycemia, fluid retention and SGLT2-related DKA concerns."],"requires":["diabetes-types"],"studio":"diabetes-drugs","drugs":true,"update":"crisis","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|3","q":"Which teaching is most important for a client starting glipizide?","o":["Know the signs of low blood sugar, and take it with or just before a meal","It never causes low blood sugar","Take it at bedtime without food","It works even if your pancreas makes no insulin"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. It forces insulin out of beta cells whether glucose is high or not.","Incorrect. Hypoglycemia is its main risk.","Incorrect. Without food it causes lows.","Incorrect. It needs working beta cells."],"ex":[],"phys":"Sulfonylureas close potassium channels on beta cells → preformed insulin is released regardless of glucose → without a meal, glucose falls."},{"id":"56|trad|31","q":"A client with type 2 diabetes taking an SGLT2 inhibitor has vomiting, deep rapid breathing, glucose 184 mg/dL, elevated blood ketones and metabolic acidosis. What is the priority interpretation?","o":["Possible euglycemic DKA requiring urgent evaluation","DKA is excluded because glucose is below 300 mg/dL","An expected SGLT2 effect needing no action","HHS is established by this glucose value"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Ketones and acidosis with these symptoms require urgent evaluation even without marked hyperglycemia.","Incorrect. The textbook’s typical > 300 value must not be used to rule out DKA.","Incorrect. Vomiting and acidosis are concerning, not routine glucosuria.","Incorrect. This value does not establish the marked hyperglycemia/hyperosmolality of HHS."],"ex":[],"phys":"SGLT2-related urinary glucose loss can limit the measured glucose rise while insulin deficiency permits ketogenesis."}]},{"id":"hypoglycemia","guide":"56:2","ch":"56","title":"Hypoglycemia: swallowing decides the route","notes":["56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule"],"source":"Ch 56: hypoglycemia; ADA 2026 Standards of Care, sections 6 and 16.","why":["Glucose below 70 mg/dL calls for action. Sweating, tremor and palpitations can precede confusion, seizures or loss of consciousness. Symptoms vary; beta blockers and impaired awareness can hide warnings.","For a conscious adult who can safely swallow and is permitted oral intake, the common 15–15 approach is 15 g fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck after 15 minutes, and repeat if still low under the treatment plan.","If swallowing is unsafe or the person is unresponsive, do not give oral food or liquid. Get emergency help and give prescribed glucagon or IV dextrose as appropriate. Recheck and investigate the cause; long-acting insulin or sulfonylureas can cause recurrence."],"checks":["Explain the 15–15 approach when swallowing is safe and oral intake is allowed.","Change the treatment route for unsafe swallowing and plan rechecks."],"requires":["insulin"],"studio":"hypoglycemia","caseId":"hypo","questions":[{"id":"hypo-a","q":"An alert adult has glucose 62 mg/dL and can swallow safely. Which initial action fits the usual 15–15 plan?","o":["Give 15 g fast-acting carbohydrate and recheck glucose in 15 minutes","Give insulin for the shaking","Offer only a high-fat meal and recheck tomorrow","Wait until unconsciousness to treat"],"a":[0],"r":["Fast carbohydrate treats the low; prompt reassessment confirms the response.","Insulin lowers glucose further.","Fat delays absorption and tomorrow is too late to reassess.","Treat promptly before progression."]},{"id":"56|trad|10","q":"A family member finds a client with diabetes unresponsive and sweating; a meter reads 38. What should the family member do first?","o":["Give glucagon, then turn the client on the side","Pour juice into the mouth","Give the next insulin dose","Wait 15 minutes and recheck"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Glucagon (IM, subcutaneous or nasal) is first-line when the client can’t swallow; it can cause vomiting, so turn to the side. Then call 911 right away — current product labels say to get emergency help immediately after giving glucagon; a repeat dose follows that product’s label (the textbook says 10 minutes; BAQSIMI’s label says 15 minutes, from a new device, while waiting for help).","Incorrect. Fluid in the mouth of an unconscious person can be aspirated.","Incorrect. Insulin would lower glucose further.","Incorrect. Severe lows need immediate treatment."],"ex":[],"phys":"Glucagon makes the liver release stored glucose → glucose rises within minutes → nausea and vomiting can follow → side-lying protects the airway."}]},{"id":"hhs","guide":"56:2","ch":"56","title":"HHS: extreme water loss and neurologic change","notes":["56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie"],"source":"Ch 56: Table 56.9; 2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus.","why":["In HHS, enough insulin remains to limit major ketone production, but not enough to prevent severe hyperglycemia. Prolonged osmotic diuresis produces profound dehydration and hyperosmolality.","An older adult with type 2 diabetes, infection, reduced intake and new confusion may fit the pattern. Assess glucose, osmolality, electrolytes, ketones, acid–base status, perfusion and mentation. DKA and HHS can overlap.","Urgent treatment includes prescribed fluids, electrolyte management and insulin, with close monitoring and controlled correction. Neurologic status and fluid balance matter throughout; absent significant ketones does not make this safe."],"checks":["Explain why HHS can have profound dehydration without major ketoacidosis.","Name neurologic and perfusion concerns and why monitored correction matters."],"requires":["diabetes-types"],"studio":"hhs","update":"crisis","questions":[{"id":"hhs-a","q":"An older adult has glucose 820 mg/dL, serum osmolality 340 mOsm/kg, severe dehydration and confusion without significant ketosis or acidosis. Which interpretation is most appropriate?","o":["No emergency because ketones are absent","HHS requiring urgent monitored treatment","Simple fasting hypoglycemia","A normal glucose change with aging"],"a":[1],"r":["Hyperosmolality and severe dehydration are dangerous without ketones.","The pattern is consistent with HHS; urgent fluids, electrolytes and other prescribed care are needed.","The glucose is extremely high, not low.","This is not a normal aging finding."]},{"id":"hhs-b","q":"During HHS treatment, which new finding needs immediate assessment?","o":["Improving mentation and perfusion","New seizure or worsening level of consciousness","A documented fluid-balance record","A scheduled repeat electrolyte panel"],"a":[1],"r":["These trends suggest improvement, though monitoring continues.","A new neurologic change may reflect a serious complication and requires urgent assessment.","Accurate fluid balance supports monitoring.","Repeat electrolytes are part of monitored treatment."]}]},{"id":"diabetes-complications","guide":"56:3","ch":"56","title":"Eyes, kidneys, nerves and feet: damage can be silent","notes":["56-chronic-complications-vessels-eyes-nerve","56-foot-care-kidneys-and-preventing-injury"],"source":"Ch 56: chronic complications and foot/renal care.","why":["Long-term vascular and nerve injury can affect the eyes, kidneys, heart, brain and feet. Follow the screening plan: a lack of symptoms does not rule out early damage.","Loss of protective sensation means a patient may not notice trauma. Combine daily inspection, appropriate footwear, safe nail/skin care and timely referral for a new wound, redness or ischemic change. Avoid burns from heating pads or hot water.","Declining renal function can reduce insulin clearance and increase hypoglycemia risk. Review glucose trends, kidney monitoring and medication plans. Foot risk assessment includes both sensation and circulation."],"checks":["Connect neuropathy and poor perfusion to unnoticed wounds and poor healing.","Explain screening and why declining kidney function can cause more lows."],"requires":["diabetes-types"],"studio":"diabetes-complications","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|19","q":"During a foot exam the client cannot feel the 10-g monofilament at several sites on each foot. What does this mean?","o":["Loss of protective sensation — high risk for foot ulcers","Normal for age","Poor circulation only","The test should be repeated with a heavier filament"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. The client won’t feel injuries; foot exams every 3–6 months and specialist referral.","Incorrect. Not a normal finding.","Incorrect. It tests sensation; check pulses for perfusion.","Incorrect. The 10-g filament is the standard."],"ex":[],"phys":"Neuropathy damages sensory fibers → pressure and injuries go unfelt → wounds enlarge before anyone notices."},{"id":"56|trad|17","q":"A client’s kidney function is declining, and the client is having more frequent low glucose readings on the same insulin dose. What explains this?","o":["The kidneys clear insulin more slowly, so each dose lasts longer","The diabetes is being cured","The client is eating more","Insulin stops working in kidney disease"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. “Improving” glucose with worsening kidneys is a warning, not a success.","Incorrect. Diabetes isn’t cured.","Incorrect. That would raise glucose.","Incorrect. Its effect lasts longer."],"ex":[],"phys":"The kidneys break down and clear insulin → as they fail, insulin stays in the blood longer → each dose has a bigger, longer effect → hypoglycemia; dialysis needs lower doses."}]},{"id":"self-management","guide":"56:3","ch":"56","title":"Make the diabetes plan workable at home","notes":["56-eating-and-exercise-with-diabetes","56-teaching-self-management"],"source":"Ch 56: nutrition, activity, sick days, self-management and health equity.","why":["Use an individualized eating and activity plan. Coordinate carbohydrate intake, medications and exercise to prevent highs and lows. Carry a rapid glucose source and know when ketones or illness make exercise unsafe.","Sick-day planning includes continued essential basal insulin for type 1 diabetes, closer glucose/ketone checks, hydration and clear contact thresholds. Other drugs may need temporary holds; use the medication-specific plan.","Use teach-back and demonstration, then check whether the patient can obtain insulin, supplies, food and follow-up. Cost, health literacy, work, distress and support affect whether the plan can actually be followed."],"checks":["Explain the links between food, activity, medication and glucose.","Describe sick-day preparation and assess practical barriers using teach-back."],"requires":["insulin","hypoglycemia"],"studio":"self-management","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|14","q":"A client with type 1 diabetes has a glucose of 290 mg/dL and moderate urine ketones before a planned jog. What should the nurse advise?","o":["Don’t exercise now","Jog to bring the glucose down","Eat a snack, then jog","Jog but drink a sports drink"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Ketones mean the cells lack insulin; exercise adds stress hormones and pushes glucose and ketones higher toward DKA.","Incorrect. Exercise raises glucose and ketones without insulin.","Incorrect. More carbohydrate makes it worse.","Incorrect. Same problem."],"ex":[],"phys":"Without insulin, exercise releases glucagon and epinephrine → the liver pours out glucose that cells can’t use → glucose and ketones rise."},{"id":"56|trad|35","q":"A client can correctly explain insulin use but says, “I cannot afford the insulin and strips until next month.” Which response best supports safe discharge?","o":["Explore the immediate supply gap and coordinate an affordable plan with the diabetes team and pharmacy","Repeat the same handout until the client memorizes it","Document nonadherence and discharge without further discussion","Tell the client to skip basal insulin whenever supplies run low"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Demonstrated knowledge does not solve an access barrier; the team must address safe access and follow-up.","Incorrect. The stated barrier is affordability, not lack of information.","Incorrect. Labeling the patient does not resolve the safety problem.","Incorrect. Omitting essential basal insulin can precipitate DKA."],"ex":[],"phys":"A self-management plan only works if the patient can obtain and use its essential supplies."}]},{"id":"hospital-diabetes","guide":"56:3","ch":"56","title":"In hospital: fasting, illness and a changing insulin need","notes":["56-diabetes-in-the-hospital-and-around-surg"],"source":"Ch 56: hospital and perioperative diabetes care.","why":["Illness, surgery, infection and some medications raise stress hormones and glucose. Reduced intake, kidney dysfunction or a treatment mismatch can cause hypoglycemia. Reassess when the pattern changes.","NPO does not mean that every diabetes medication is automatically stopped or continued. The team adjusts basal, meal and correction therapy, glucose checks and drug-specific holds. Type 1 diabetes still requires basal coverage.","The RN assesses and interprets findings and teaches the patient. Assistive personnel may perform routine tasks within policy for a stable patient, but abnormal results require prompt reporting and RN assessment."],"checks":["Explain why illness can increase glucose even without more food.","Distinguish a delegated routine task from RN assessment and treatment decisions."],"requires":["insulin"],"studio":"hospital-diabetes","questions":[{"id":"56|trad|13","q":"A hospitalized client’s glucose has been well controlled, but today it is 280–320 with no change in diet or insulin. What should the nurse suspect first?","o":["An infection","The meter is broken","The client is eating too little","Normal day-to-day variation"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. Counter-regulatory hormones often raise glucose before a fever appears — look for UTI, pneumonia, cellulitis or wound infection.","Incorrect. Possible, but infection is the classic cause.","Incorrect. Eating less lowers glucose.","Incorrect. A jump this large needs a cause."],"ex":[],"phys":"Infection triggers cortisol, epinephrine and glucagon → the liver releases glucose and insulin works less well → glucose rises, often before fever."},{"id":"56|trad|27","q":"Which task can the RN delegate to the assistive personnel (AP) for a stable client with type 2 diabetes?","o":["Check a fingerstick glucose before lunch and report the result","Teach the client to give an insulin injection","Assess the feet for loss of sensation","Give the prescribed subcutaneous insulin"],"a":[0],"r":["Correct. A point-of-care glucose check on a stable client is a routine, trained task; the RN acts on the result.","Incorrect. Teaching is the RN’s.","Incorrect. Assessment is the RN’s.","Incorrect. APs don’t give medications; an LPN or RN does."],"ex":[],"phys":"Delegation follows the need for judgment → a fingerstick follows set steps and is reported → teaching, assessing and medication administration need a licensed nurse."}]}],"studios":{"version":1,"mapPage":"Final Map.html","learnPage":"Final Learn.html","labs":[{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Fluid balance: compartments, volume and safe replacement","cards":[{"title":"Why sodium and potassium matter so much","text":"Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium · K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in · 4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change"},{"title":"Heart and vessels","text":"HR ↑, pulses weak, thready · BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand) · Flat neck/hand veins even supine"},{"title":"Safety first: critical rescue","text":"Assess q2h for pulmonary edema · Bounding pulse, ↑JVD, crackles, ↑edema, ↓urine → call"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Daily weight is the most reliable measure of fluid status: 1 kg ≈ 1 L.","task":"Which assessment gives the nurse the most reliable picture of a hospitalized client’s fluid gain or loss from one day to the next?","cue":"Tissue mass changes slowly, so a quick weight change is water — the scale counts fluid in all compartments, including fluid hidden in tissues.","rule":"Daily weight is the most reliable measure of fluid status: 1 kg ≈ 1 L.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"fluid-balance","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Fluid balance: compartments, volume and safe replacement","notes":["13-overview","13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","13-rehydrating-safely","13-fluid-overload","13-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why sodium and potassium matter so much: Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium; K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in; 4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change","Heart and vessels: HR ↑, pulses weak, thready; BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand); Flat neck/hand veins even supine","Safety first: critical rescue: Assess q2h for pulmonary edema; Bounding pulse, ↑JVD, crackles, ↑edema, ↓urine → call","Why: Protected volumes: plasma (BP) and cells; Interstitial space = buffer; Judge hydration by perfusion signs + weight, not edema alone"],"terms":["hypovolemia","hypervolemia","isotonic","osmosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"13-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"13-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Body = >50% water carrying electrolytes in narrow ranges","Moved by filtration, diffusion, osmosis; steered by aldosterone, ADH, NPs and RAAS","Exemplar dehydration: weight, orthostatics, pulse, urine, mental status → replace fluid, prevent falls","Also fluid overload and Na, K, Ca, Mg highs and lows — the heart is the danger"]},{"note":"13-overview","title":"Filtration — pushed by pressure","lines":["High → low hydrostatic pressure","BP pushes fluid out of capillaries","Backed-up veins (right HF) → edema"]},{"note":"13-overview","title":"Diffusion — particles spread out","lines":["Particles: high → low concentration","Na⁺ pumps keep Na⁺ ~10× higher outside","Glucose + insulin = facilitated diffusion"]},{"note":"13-overview","title":"Osmosis — water follows particles","lines":["Water → more concentrated side","Isotonic 270–300; hypertonic >300 pulls water out of cells","Hypotonic <270 → cells swell","Osmoreceptors shrink → thirst"]},{"note":"13-overview","title":"Why sodium and potassium matter so much","lines":["Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium","K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in","4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change"]},{"note":"13-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Protected volumes: plasma (BP) and cells","Interstitial space = buffer","Judge hydration by perfusion signs + weight, not edema alone"]}]},{"id":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Body water, compartments and IV solutions","blocks":[{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"How much","lines":["55–60% young adults; 50–55% older adults","Women and obese adults: less water (fat holds little)"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"The compartments","lines":["ICF ⅔ (~25 L)","ECF ⅓ (~15 L): plasma 3.5–6 L, interstitial 10–12 L (\"third space\"), transcellular"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Solvent, solute, electrolytes","lines":["Solvent = water; solute = particles; electrolytes = charged","Cations +, anions −","Osmolarity ≈ osmolality"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Why","lines":["Plasma = small slice of body water","Lose it → BP falls fast; kidneys and hormones react first"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Isotonic (270–300 mOsm/L)","lines":["0.9% NaCl, LR, D5W (in the bag)","Stays in the ECF → expands volume"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Hypotonic (under 270)","lines":["0.45% NaCl","Water → into cells","Careful with brain swelling, low BP"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Hypertonic (over 300)","lines":["3%/5% NaCl, D10W, D5NS, D5½NS, D5LR","Pulls water out of cells into vessels","3% saline: small volumes, controller"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"The dextrose catch","lines":["Dextrose is burned off after infusion","D5W → acts like free water","D5½NS → acts hypotonic"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Crystalloids vs colloids","lines":["Crystalloids: spread everywhere — first choice","Colloids: stay in plasma — second-line"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Why#2","lines":["Tonicity = where the water goes","Isotonic → vessels; hypotonic → cells; hypertonic → out of cells"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Who is at risk","lines":["Mild: intake/output changes, easy to fix","Severe: older adults, kidney/endocrine disease, drugs","Every ill adult has some risk"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Sodium 136–145 mEq/L","lines":["↑ dehydration, kidney disease, ↑ cortisol","↓ overload, liver disease, adrenal insufficiency","Highest: processed/preserved; lowest: fresh foods"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Potassium 3.5–5.0 mEq/L","lines":["↑ kidney disease, acidosis, crush injury","↓ diuretics, alkalosis, insulin, aldosterone","80% excreted by the kidneys"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Calcium 9.0–10.5 mg/dL","lines":["↑ hyperparathyroidism, hyperthyroidism","↓ low vitamin D, hypoparathyroidism, kidney disease, high phosphorus"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Chloride 98–106 mEq/L","lines":["↑ metabolic acidosis, respiratory alkalosis","↓ vomiting, diarrhea, diuretics, overload"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Magnesium 1.3–2.1 mEq/L and osmolarity 270–300","lines":["Mg²⁺ ↑ kidney disease; ↓ malnutrition, alcohol, ketoacidosis","Osmolarity ↑ dehydration, ↑Na⁺, hyperglycemia; ↓ overload, ↓Na⁺"]},{"note":"13-body-water-compartments-and-iv-solutions","title":"Why#3","lines":["Kidneys keep; diuretics/GI remove","Acid–base, insulin shift K⁺","Water dilutes or concentrates"]}]},{"id":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Hormones, RAAS and daily intake and output","blocks":[{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Aldosterone","lines":["Adrenal cortex; released for low sodium","Keeps Na⁺ + water, dumps K⁺"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"ADH (vasopressin)","lines":["Posterior pituitary; triggered by concentrated blood","Keeps water only","Dilute blood → ADH off → more urine"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Natriuretic peptides","lines":["ANP (atria), BNP (ventricles) — stretch","Opposite of aldosterone: lose Na⁺ + water"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"How sodium is kept in range","lines":["Low Na⁺ → ↓ADH, ↓NP, ↑RAAS → keep Na⁺","High Na⁺ → ↓aldosterone, ↑ADH, ↑NP → lose Na⁺"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Why","lines":["Aldosterone: enough sodium?","ADH: too concentrated?","NPs: heart overstretched?"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"What starts it","lines":["Triggers: low BP, low volume, low O₂, low Na⁺","→ kidney releases renin"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"The cascade","lines":["Renin → angiotensinogen → angiotensin I","ACE → angiotensin II"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"What angiotensin II does","lines":["Vasoconstriction everywhere","Kidney arterioles → ↓ GFR, ↓ urine","Aldosterone → keep Na⁺ + water"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"The drugs that use it","lines":["Diuretics: lose Na⁺","ACE inhibitors (-pril): less angiotensin II","ARBs (-sartan): block it","Low-sodium diet"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Why#2","lines":["Overlapping defenses: squeeze vessels, stop urine, hold Na⁺ + water","So urine falls before BP"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Intake (Table 13.2)","lines":["~2300 mL/day; food up to 85% water","Thirst: ↑ osmolarity or ↓ volume","Irrigation intake = in − out","Output measured: urine, emesis, stool, drains"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Output and the kidneys","lines":["Kidney = main, adjustable route","Obligatory output 400–600 mL/day"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Insensible loss","lines":["500–1000 mL/day, uncontrolled","↑ with fever, burns, ventilation, fast RR, GI suction"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Older adults (age-related changes)","lines":["Turgor unreliable; kidneys can’t concentrate","Less muscle = less water; weak thirst","Adrenal atrophy → ↓Na⁺, ↑K⁺; ask about drugs"]},{"note":"13-hormones-raas-and-daily-intake-and-outpu","title":"Why#3","lines":["Older adults: less water, less thirst, more kidney loss","First sign often new confusion"]}]},{"id":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Dehydration — the concept exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Definition","lines":["Intake/retention < need → plasma volume ↓","A condition, not a disease","Vascular dehydration = shift out of plasma"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Isotonic dehydration","lines":["Most common: water + electrolytes lost together","ECF only → hypovolemia","Compensation: vasoconstriction"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Causes (Box 13.1)","lines":["Bleeding, vomiting, diarrhea, fistulas, ileostomy","Sweating, burns, fever, hyperventilation","Diuretics, GI suction, NPO","Can’t drink: swallowing, thirst, mobility, unconscious; AVP deficiency"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Higher risk","lines":["Older adults: less water, weak thirst, mobility, diuretics/laxatives","Women: less body water"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Prevention","lines":["Drink more: exercise, dry climate, altitude","No caffeine or alcohol to rehydrate"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["A condition with a cause","Treat both: refill + fix the cause"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"History","lines":["Exact I&O; weigh, don’t ask","Drugs (diuretics, laxatives); kidney/endocrine disease","Rings/shoes looser"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Weight is the best measure","lines":["1 L = 1 kg = 2.2 lb; 1 lb ≈ 500 mL",">½ lb/day loss = fluid"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Heart and vessels","lines":["HR ↑, pulses weak, thready","BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand)","Flat neck/hand veins even supine"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Lungs, skin, mouth","lines":["RR ↑","Tenting; older adults: sternum","Mouth dry, sticky; tongue furrowed"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Brain and temperature","lines":["Confusion — often first in older adults","Low-grade fever; each 1 °C ↑ = ≥500 mL lost"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Kidneys and urine","lines":["Urine dark, concentrated, SG >1.030","<500 mL/day = concern"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["Hemoconcentration: Hgb, Hct, osmolarity, BUN, electrolytes ↑","Not after hemorrhage","ICU: ultrasound/echo"]},{"note":"13-dehydration-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Why#2","lines":["Findings = defending BP (fast HR, tight vessels, little urine)","Standing tips it → orthostatics + fall prevention"]}]},{"id":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Rehydrating safely","blocks":[{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Oral first","lines":["Mild–moderate + can swallow → oral","Respect limits (thickened, low-Na⁺, sugar-free)","Split by shift; small amounts hourly"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Working with assistive personnel","lines":["AP: 60–120 mL every hour, exact amounts","Never withhold fluids to prevent incontinence"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Oral rehydration solutions","lines":["ORS: absorbed despite vomiting/diarrhea","Home, long-term care, poor veins"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"IV replacement","lines":["Severe or can’t drink → IV crystalloids","Amount: weight loss; rate: heart, lungs, kidneys","Watch urine output + pulse"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Monitoring during replacement","lines":["Every 2 hr: pulse, pulse pressure, urine; weight every 8 hr","Overload: bounding pulse, dyspnea, JVD upright, edema","IV site hourly"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Drugs for the cause","lines":["Antiemetics, antidiarrheals, antibiotics, antipyretics"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Why","lines":["Too slow → underperfusion; too fast → pulmonary edema","Even schedule + reassess q2h"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Falls","lines":["Falls: orthostasis, dysrhythmia, weakness, confusion","Rise slowly; sit if dizzy; ask for help"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Getting ready to walk","lines":["Before walking: orthostatic BP first"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Signs it is working","lines":["Intake ≥1500 mL/day (or 500 mL > urine)","BP normal; moist mouth, normal turgor","Neck veins full supine; urine steady","No falls; knows the signs"]},{"note":"13-rehydrating-safely","title":"Why#2","lines":["Neck veins full supine = plasma volume back"]}]},{"id":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Fluid overload","blocks":[{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Definition and causes","lines":["Intake/retention > need → hypervolemia","Too much IV fluid, kidney failure, HF","Steroids, SIADH, polydipsia, water intoxication"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"How the body copes","lines":["Copes: edema, ↓ADH/aldosterone, ↑NPs, ↑ urine","Severe or weak heart/kidneys → HF, pulmonary edema","Dilution → seizures, coma"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Key features (Box 13.3)","lines":["Bounding pulse, ↑BP, JVD, weight gain","Crackles, SOB, fast shallow breathing","Pitting edema, pale cool skin","Headache, ↓LOC; big liver"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Labs","lines":["Electrolytes often normal","Hemodilution: ↓Hgb, Hct, protein"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Why","lines":["Backs up into neck veins, lungs, dependent tissue","Crackles = fluid in alveoli → pulmonary edema next"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Safety first: critical rescue","lines":["Assess q2h for pulmonary edema","Bounding pulse, ↑JVD, crackles, ↑edema, ↓urine → call"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Skin","lines":["Pressure overlay; check bony points daily","Turn q2h"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Drugs","lines":["Furosemide (loop) if kidneys work","SIADH: conivaptan/tolvaptan (water only)","Watch weight, urine, Na⁺/K⁺, ECG"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Diet","lines":["Limit fluid + sodium","Mild: no added salt; teach labels, daily record"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Weights and I&O","lines":["Weight gain = best sign; >½ lb/day → each lb ≈ 500 mL","Same time, scale, clothes","Bed scale: same linens; exact I&O"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Going home","lines":["Daily weight log","Call for >3 lb/week or >2 lb/24 hr"]},{"note":"13-fluid-overload","title":"Why#2","lines":["Scale sees fluid before edema","Rule: >2 lb/day or >3 lb/week"]}]},{"id":"13-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"13-adpie","title":"¶ For every fluid or electrolyte problem the","lines":["Watch volume: weight, pulse, BP, neck veins, lungs, urine","Watch excitable tissue: mind, reflexes, gut, ECG","Watch safety: falls, breathing, IV site"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"History","lines":["I&O, daily weight","Drugs: diuretics, laxatives, steroids, ACEIs/ARBs, K⁺, salt substitutes","Kidney/heart/endocrine disease; neck surgery; diet"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["Pulse, orthostatic BP, neck veins, lungs, edema","Turgor (sternum), mouth, LOC","Grip, DTRs, Trousseau/Chvostek, bowel sounds"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Labs and ECG","lines":["Na 136–145 · K 3.5–5.0 · Ca 9–10.5 · Mg 1.3–2.1 · osm 270–300","Hgb/Hct, BUN, SG","ECG: peaked T, U wave, long/short QT"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Read electrolytes with volume signs","Confusion + bounding pulse ≠ confusion + thready pulse"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Dehydration: F&E imbalance + injury (falls)","Overload: pulmonary edema, skin breakdown","Electrolytes: dysrhythmias, breathing failure, seizures"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Intake ≥1500 mL/day; weight, BP stable","Electrolytes + ECG normal","No falls; no pulmonary edema; knows the signs"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Fluid","lines":["Dehydration: scheduled oral, IV crystalloids, reassess q2h","Overload: diuretics, fluid/Na⁺ limits, skin care, daily weight"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Electrolytes","lines":["K⁺: diluted, 5–10 mEq/hr, never push; high → monitor","Na⁺: saline, 3% on controller","Ca²⁺: replace + calm room / stop + saline","Mg²⁺: IV MgSO₄ + hourly DTRs"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Safety and teaching","lines":["Falls: gait belt, rise slowly","Teach daily weights (>2 lb/day, >3 lb/week)","Read labels; no salt substitutes with high K⁺"]},{"note":"13-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Weight back; pulse full; no orthostatic drop","Neck veins full supine; lungs clear","Urine steady; alert; electrolytes + ECG normal","No falls; knows when to call"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"fluid-balance:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which assessment gives the nurse the most reliable picture of a hospitalized client’s fluid gain or loss from one day to the next?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Daily weight is the most reliable measure of fluid status: 1 kg ≈ 1 L.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Daily weight on the same scale at the same time","correct":true,"why":"Correct. 1 kg of weight ≈ 1 L of fluid — weight is the best single indicator."},{"id":"o1","text":"Skin turgor on the back of the hand","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Hand skin loses elasticity with age; turgor is unreliable there."},{"id":"o2","text":"How thirsty the client says they are","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Thirst is weak in older adults and varies widely."},{"id":"o3","text":"The number of times the client voids each shift","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The number of voids says nothing about the volume."}]},{"id":"fluid-balance:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why sodium and potassium matter so much?","answer":"Na⁺ holds ECF water — water follows sodium; K⁺ 3.5–5.0 out, ~150 in; 4 → 3 mEq/L = 25% change","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"fluid-balance:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about heart and vessels?","answer":"HR ↑, pulses weak, thready; BP and pulse pressure ↓; orthostatic (lie → sit → stand); Flat neck/hand veins even supine","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Potassium: cardiac risk before a reassuring appearance","cards":[{"title":"Why it matters","text":"Low K⁺ → cells less responsive · Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic · Kills via heart + breathing muscles"},{"title":"Heart and ECG","text":"Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic · ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave"},{"title":"Safety","text":"Gait belt, fall precautions · Severe: breathing hourly (SpO₂, cough, ABGs)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Hyperkalemia risks: kidney failure, potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, salt substitutes, fast IV potassium, transfusions, tissue damage, acidosis.","task":"Which client is at greatest risk for hyperkalemia?","cue":"The kidneys excrete most of the potassium we eat → in kidney failure it accumulates → a potassium-chloride salt substitute adds more.","rule":"Hyperkalemia risks: kidney failure, potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, salt substitutes, fast IV potassium, transfusions, tissue damage, acidosis.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"potassium","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Potassium: cardiac risk before a reassuring appearance","notes":["13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it matters: Low K⁺ → cells less responsive; Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic; Kills via heart + breathing muscles","Heart and ECG: Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic; ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave","Safety: Gait belt, fall precautions; Severe: breathing hourly (SpO₂, cough, ABGs)","Why: Insulin pulls K⁺ into cells fast; Insulin drips → check K⁺ often"],"terms":["hypokalemia","hyperkalemia","potassium chloride"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Potassium: hypokalemia and hyperkalemia","blocks":[{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Why it matters","lines":["Low K⁺ → cells less responsive","Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic","Kills via heart + breathing muscles"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Causes (Box 13.6)","lines":["Loss: diuretics, steroids, vomiting, diarrhea, NG suction","Shift: alkalosis, insulin (pushes K⁺ into cells), TPN","Low Mg²⁺ travels with low K⁺"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Who to watch","lines":["Older adults, diuretics, steroids","IV insulin (DKA) → K⁺ shifts in fast"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Breathing","lines":["Weak, shallow breathing","Check breathing q2h (q1h if severe)"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Muscles, nerves, gut","lines":["Weakness, hyporeflexia → flaccid paralysis","Anxiety → confusion, coma","GI slows: constipation, ileus"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Heart and ECG","lines":["Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic","ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Replacing potassium safely","lines":["Pharmacy-diluted only; 5–10 mEq/hr on a pump","Never IV push, IM or subcut","IV site hourly; infiltration → stop, remove, notify, photo"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Oral potassium and diet","lines":["Oral K⁺ with/after meals","Avoid loop/thiazide; spironolactone spares K⁺","Foods prevent; supplements correct"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Safety","lines":["Gait belt, fall precautions","Severe: breathing hourly (SpO₂, cough, ABGs)"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Why","lines":["Insulin pulls K⁺ into cells fast","Insulin drips → check K⁺ often"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Why it matters#2","lines":["Heart is most sensitive → heart block, VF","Sudden rise: trouble at 6–7; slow: at ≥8"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Causes (Box 13.7)#2","lines":["Kidney failure, K⁺-sparing diuretics, ACEIs/ARBs","Salt substitutes, KCl, fast IV K⁺, transfusions","Shift out: tissue damage, acidosis, uncontrolled DM"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Heart (the main danger)","lines":["Bradycardia, hypotension","ECG: peaked T, long PR, flat P, wide QRS","→ heart block, asystole, VF"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Muscles, nerves, gut#2","lines":["Early: twitching, tingling, numbness","Late: ascending weakness → flaccid paralysis","GI ↑: diarrhea"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Labs","lines":["Dehydration: everything ↑","Kidney failure: ↑ creatinine, BUN, ↓ pH"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Treatment","lines":["Stop K⁺ infusion, keep line open; low-K⁺ diet","Patiromer (gut binder); diuretics or dialysis","IV insulin + glucose → watch lows","Cardiac monitor"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Critical rescue","lines":["HR <60 or peaked T → Rapid Response","K⁺ 6.5: cardiac monitor first"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Teaching","lines":["Low-K⁺ foods; read labels","No salt substitutes"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Beyond the book","lines":["ECG changes: IV calcium gluconate protects the heart (doesn’t lower K⁺)","Then insulin + glucose, albuterol, binders"]},{"note":"13-potassium-hypokalemia-and-hyperkalemia","title":"Why#2","lines":["Speed of change matters — cells adapt","So: IV K⁺ slow + diluted; ECG decides urgency"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"potassium:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which client is at greatest risk for hyperkalemia?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Hyperkalemia risks: kidney failure, potassium-sparing diuretics, ACE inhibitors and ARBs, salt substitutes, fast IV potassium, transfusions, tissue damage, acidosis.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A client with kidney failure who uses a salt substitute","correct":true,"why":"Correct. The kidneys can’t excrete potassium, and salt substitutes are potassium chloride."},{"id":"o1","text":"A client taking furosemide for heart failure","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Loop diuretics waste potassium."},{"id":"o2","text":"A client with 3 days of vomiting","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Vomiting loses potassium."},{"id":"o3","text":"A client receiving an IV insulin infusion for DKA","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Insulin drives potassium into cells — the risk is a falling level."}]},{"id":"potassium:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why it matters?","answer":"Low K⁺ → cells less responsive; Slow loss: few symptoms; rapid fall: dramatic; Kills via heart + breathing muscles","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"potassium:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about heart and ecg?","answer":"Weak thready, irregular pulse; orthostatic; ECG: ST ↓, flat/inverted T, prominent U wave","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Sodium, calcium and magnesium: brain, muscles and airway","cards":[{"title":"Why it causes problems","text":"↓ Excitability (slower depolarization) · Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema"},{"title":"Brain and muscles","text":"Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma · Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs"},{"title":"Treatment","text":"Deficit: IV saline; severe: 3% saline, small, controller · Excess: conivaptan, tolvaptan; SIADH: lithium, demeclocycline · Mild: ↑ salt, ↓ fluid; check hourly"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"IV magnesium sulfate: check deep tendon reflexes at least hourly; absent reflexes → stop and notify; calcium reverses severe effects.","task":"A client is receiving IV magnesium sulfate for hypomagnesemia. Which finding requires the nurse to stop the infusion and notify the provider?","cue":"Magnesium blocks signals at the nerve–muscle junction → reflexes fade first → then muscles weaken, breathing slows and the heart slows.","rule":"IV magnesium sulfate: check deep tendon reflexes at least hourly; absent reflexes → stop and notify; calcium reverses severe effects.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"sodium-calcium-magnesium","ch":"13","chapters":["13"],"title":"Sodium, calcium and magnesium: brain, muscles and airway","notes":["13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 13; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it causes problems: ↓ Excitability (slower depolarization); Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema","Brain and muscles: Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma; Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs","Treatment: Deficit: IV saline; severe: 3% saline, small, controller; Excess: conivaptan, tolvaptan; SIADH: lithium, demeclocycline; Mild: ↑ salt, ↓ fluid; check hourly","Why: Symptoms = brain swelling inside the skull; Correct slowly, on a controller"],"terms":["hyponatremia","hypernatremia","hypocalcemia","hypermagnesemia"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Sodium: hyponatremia and hypernatremia","blocks":[{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Why it causes problems","lines":["↓ Excitability (slower depolarization)","Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Causes (Box 13.4)","lines":["Loss: GI losses, sweating, diuretics, burns, ↓aldosterone, hyperglycemia","Dilution: excess water, SIADH, HF, cirrhosis, kidney failure, hypotonic irrigation"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Brain","lines":["Confusion (older adults) → seizures, coma"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Muscles and gut","lines":["Weakness, ↓ DTRs → check breathing","GI ↑: nausea, diarrhea, cramps, hyperactive sounds"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Heart: depends on volume","lines":["Low volume: weak thready, ↓BP, orthostatic","Overload: bounding, normal/↑BP"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Treatment","lines":["Deficit: IV saline; severe: 3% saline, small, controller","Excess: conivaptan, tolvaptan; SIADH: lithium, demeclocycline","Mild: ↑ salt, ↓ fluid; check hourly"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Why","lines":["Symptoms = brain swelling inside the skull","Correct slowly, on a controller"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Why it causes problems#2","lines":["Early: irritable tissue","Water leaves cells → shrinkage → later no response"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Causes (Box 13.5)#2","lines":["Actual: aldosterone↑, steroids, Cushing, Na⁺ IV fluids, kidney failure","Relative: dehydration, fever, sweating, watery diarrhea"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Brain and muscles","lines":["Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma","Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Heart","lines":["↓ Contractility (Na⁺ slows Ca²⁺ entry)","Low volume: fast, thready, hypotensive","Overload: bounding, JVD upright, ↑ diastolic"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Treatment#2","lines":["Fluid loss: 0.9% NaCl or D5½NS (hypotonic once in)","Kidney retention: furosemide, bumetanide","Enough water; limit sodium in kidney disease"]},{"note":"13-sodium-hyponatremia-and-hypernatremia","title":"Why#2","lines":["Usually too little water, not too much salt","Classic: older adult + fever/diarrhea → treat with water"]}]},{"id":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Calcium: hypocalcemia and hypercalcemia","blocks":[{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Forms and jobs","lines":["Bound (albumin) vs ionized (active)","Bone, muscle and heart contraction, nerves, clotting"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Raising it: PTH and vitamin D","lines":["PTH: bone release, vitamin D activation, kidneys keep Ca²⁺","Gut absorption needs active vitamin D"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Lowering it: calcitonin","lines":["High Ca²⁺ → calcitonin: stops bone release, ↑ kidney excretion"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Calcium and excitability","lines":["Ca²⁺ stabilizes membranes (slows Na⁺ entry)","Low = overexcited; high = sluggish"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Why","lines":["Ca²⁺ = brake on nerve firing","Low: tingling, tetany; high: weakness, constipation"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Causes (Box 13.8)","lines":["Loss: low intake/vit D, malabsorption, ESKD, diarrhea","Relative: alkalosis, pancreatitis, ↑ phosphorus","Parathyroid removal (thyroid surgery, neck radiation)"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Acute vs chronic","lines":["Acute: rapid, dangerous; chronic: milder (body adjusts)","Ask: dairy, supplements, bone surgery, height loss"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Postmenopausal women","lines":["Less weight bearing + less estrogen","Keep walking"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Nerves and muscles","lines":["Tingling: hands, feet, lips","→ cramps, spasms (at rest, at night) → tetany"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Trousseau and Chvostek","lines":["Trousseau: cuff above systolic 1–4 min → hand spasm","Chvostek: tap in front of the ear → facial twitch"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Heart, gut, bones","lines":["ECG: prolonged QT; severe hypotension","GI ↑: diarrhea, cramps","Chronic: osteoporosis, fractures, height loss"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Treatment and safety","lines":["Calcium + vitamin D; Ca²⁺-rich diet","Quiet, dim, calm room","Lift sheet — fragile bones"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Why#2","lines":["Cuff → brief hypoxia of hand nerves","Low Ca²⁺ → they fire on their own → spasm"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Causes (Box 13.9)#2","lines":["Excess calcium/vit D, CKD, thiazides","Hyperparathyroidism, cancer, immobility, dehydration"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Speed matters","lines":["Fast mild rise > slow severe rise"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Heart (most serious)","lines":["Mild: ↑ HR, BP; severe: slows","ECG: short QT"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Clots","lines":["Clots where flow is slow","Measure calves; check foot perfusion"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Muscles, brain, gut","lines":["Weakness, ↓ reflexes, no tingling","Confusion, lethargy","GI ↓: constipation, distention"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Treatment","lines":["Stop Ca²⁺ (LR, antacids, vit D)","IV 0.9% NaCl; thiazide → furosemide","Calcitonin, bisphosphonates, phosphorus; dialysis","Cardiac monitor"]},{"note":"13-calcium-hypocalcemia-and-hypercalcemia","title":"Why#3","lines":["Thiazides keep Ca²⁺; loops lose it","High Ca²⁺ → switch to furosemide"]}]},{"id":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Magnesium: hypomagnesemia and hypermagnesemia","blocks":[{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"What magnesium does","lines":["Mostly in bone and inside cells","Muscle, energy, heart","Low Mg²⁺ → low K⁺ and Ca²⁺"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Causes (Box 13.10)","lines":["Poor intake, loop/thiazide diuretics","Alcohol, malnutrition, pancreatitis, GI disease","Drugs: PPIs, aminoglycosides, cisplatin, amphotericin B"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Heart","lines":["Dysrhythmias: AF, VF, long QT","More damage after MI"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Nerves and muscles","lines":["Hyperactive DTRs, tingling, cramps","Chvostek/Trousseau +","→ tetany, seizures"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Gut","lines":["GI ↓: constipation, ileus"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Treatment","lines":["Stop Mg²⁺-wasting drugs","Severe: IV magnesium sulfate","Check DTRs hourly; fix calcium too"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Why","lines":["Low Mg²⁺ → K⁺ won’t stay up","Stubborn low K⁺ → check Mg²⁺"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Causes","lines":["Mg²⁺ antacids/laxatives, IV Mg²⁺, kidney failure","Symptoms usually >4 mEq/L"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Heart#2","lines":["Bradycardia, hypotension","ECG: long PR, wide QRS, long QT","→ cardiac arrest"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Brain, muscles, breathing","lines":["Lethargy → coma","↓ or absent DTRs","Respiratory muscle failure"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Treatment#2","lines":["Stop all Mg²⁺","Mg²⁺-free IV fluids + furosemide","Severe heart effects: calcium"]},{"note":"13-magnesium-hypomagnesemia-and-hypermagnes","title":"Why#2","lines":["DTRs = bedside Mg²⁺ meter","Check hourly on IV magnesium"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"sodium-calcium-magnesium:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client is receiving IV magnesium sulfate for hypomagnesemia. Which finding requires the nurse to stop the infusion and notify the provider?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"IV magnesium sulfate: check deep tendon reflexes at least hourly; absent reflexes → stop and notify; calcium reverses severe effects.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Absent patellar reflexes","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Fading or absent reflexes mean magnesium is too high — breathing and heart effects come next."},{"id":"o1","text":"Warmth and flushing at the start of the infusion","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Flushing from vasodilation can occur, but it isn’t the sign of toxicity the reflexes are."},{"id":"o2","text":"Magnesium 1.9 mEq/L","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Within the normal range (1.3–2.1)."},{"id":"o3","text":"Urine output 50 mL/hr","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Adequate output helps excrete magnesium."}]},{"id":"sodium-calcium-magnesium:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why it causes problems?","answer":"↓ Excitability (slower depolarization); Water into cells → swelling → cerebral edema","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"sodium-calcium-magnesium:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about brain and muscles?","answer":"Low volume: agitated, confused; overload: lethargic → coma; Muscles: twitching → weakness, ↓ DTRs","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Read an ABG as a relationship","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days · Metabolic problem → lungs compensate · Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)"},{"title":"History","text":"COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses · Opioids/sedation · Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"Respiratory acidosis: reduced gas exchange · Acidosis: dysrhythmias (K⁺), falls · Alkalosis: injury (falls, tetany, seizures), weak breathing"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"pH 7.45 = alkalosis; the type comes from PaCO₂ (respiratory) and HCO₃⁻ (metabolic).","task":"A client’s arterial pH is 7.31. What can the nurse conclude from this value alone?","cue":"The pH is the result of the kidneys’ bicarbonate over the lungs’ CO₂ — it shows the balance is off, not which side moved.","rule":"pH 7.45 = alkalosis; the type comes from PaCO₂ (respiratory) and HCO₃⁻ (metabolic).","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"abg","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Read an ABG as a relationship","notes":["14-overview","14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","14-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days; Metabolic problem → lungs compensate; Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)","History: COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses; Opioids/sedation; Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin","Priority problems: Respiratory acidosis: reduced gas exchange; Acidosis: dysrhythmias (K⁺), falls; Alkalosis: injury (falls, tetany, seizures), weak breathing","Why: pH = result; PaCO₂ = lungs; HCO₃⁻ = kidneys; The value that matches the pH is the cause"],"terms":["acidosis","alkalosis","bicarbonate"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"14-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"14-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Arterial pH 7.35–7.45 — H⁺ is the most tightly controlled substance","Defenses: buffers (seconds) → lungs/CO₂ (minutes) → kidneys/HCO₃⁻ (hours–days)","Exemplar acidosis: metabolic or respiratory → depressed tissue + high K⁺ → check the heart first","Alkalosis overexcites (low Ca²⁺, low K⁺); the ABG names the problem"]},{"note":"14-overview","title":"pH is a hydrogen-ion scale run backward","lines":["Lower pH = more H⁺","1 unit = tenfold change","Normal 7.35–7.45; 7.8 usually fatal","Off balance: hormones, enzymes, drugs fail; excitable tissue changes"]},{"note":"14-overview","title":"Acids, bases and buffers","lines":["Acids release H⁺; bases bind it (bicarbonate)","Buffers = sponges: bicarbonate (ECF), phosphate (cells), proteins, hemoglobin","Ratio 1 carbonic acid : 20 bicarbonate"]},{"note":"14-overview","title":"CO₂ is acid you can breathe out","lines":["CO₂ ⇌ carbonic acid ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻","CO₂ ↑ → pH ↓; CO₂ ↓ → pH ↑","Brain chemoreceptors adjust breathing in minutes"]},{"note":"14-overview","title":"The kidneys: slow but strongest","lines":["Keep/make HCO₃⁻; excrete H⁺ (phosphate, ammonium)","24–48 hr, but strongest","No disease overproduces HCO₃⁻ → acidosis is more common"]},{"note":"14-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days","Metabolic problem → lungs compensate","Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)"]}]},{"id":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"Reading an arterial blood gas","blocks":[{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.35–7.45","lines":["7.45 alkalosis","Says how much, not why"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"PaCO₂ 35–45 mm Hg — the lungs","lines":["Lung value; CO₂ = acid",">45 → acid side · <35 → alkaline side"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"HCO₃⁻ 21–28 mEq/L — the kidneys","lines":["Kidney/metabolic value; HCO₃⁻ = base","28 → alkalosis or compensation"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"PaO₂ 80–100 mm Hg and lactate","lines":["PaO₂ = oxygenation; low first in lung failure","Lactate ↑ = cells without oxygen (also exercise, insulin infusion, dehydration)"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"Why","lines":["pH = result; PaCO₂ = lungs; HCO₃⁻ = kidneys","The value that matches the pH is the cause"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"1 · Look at the pH","lines":["7.45","In range + abnormal values → which side of 7.40?"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"2 · Look at the PaCO₂","lines":["PaCO₂ opposite to pH → respiratory","ROME: Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"3 · Look at the HCO₃⁻","lines":["HCO₃⁻ same way as pH → metabolic"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"4 · Check for compensation","lines":["Other value moving to pull pH back = compensation","pH still off = partial; in range = full","Both pull toward acid = combined acidosis"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"5 · Look at the oxygen and the patient","lines":["PaO₂/SpO₂ for oxygen","Match the gas to the patient and the cause"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"Why#2","lines":["Fixed order: pH → CO₂ → HCO₃⁻ → compensation → O₂","Avoids mistaking compensation for the cause"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.30 · PaCO₂ 58 · HCO₃⁻ 25","lines":["Respiratory acidosis, uncompensated","e.g., opioid respiratory depression"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.36 · PaCO₂ 62 · HCO₃⁻ 34#2","lines":["Respiratory acidosis, fully compensated","Typical of chronic COPD"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.28 · PaCO₂ 28 · HCO₃⁻ 13#3","lines":["Metabolic acidosis, partly compensated","e.g., DKA with Kussmaul breathing"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.50 · PaCO₂ 29 · HCO₃⁻ 23#4","lines":["Respiratory alkalosis, uncompensated","e.g., anxiety hyperventilation"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.52 · PaCO₂ 44 · HCO₃⁻ 35#5","lines":["Metabolic alkalosis, uncompensated","e.g., vomiting / NG suction"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"pH 7.10 · PaCO₂ 60 · HCO₃⁻ 15#6","lines":["Combined acidosis","Both values pull toward acid — e.g., DKA + COPD"]},{"note":"14-reading-an-arterial-blood-gas","title":"Why#3","lines":["Which way? Who caused it? Is the other helping?"]}]},{"id":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Buffers, lungs, kidneys and compensation","blocks":[{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"1 · Buffers — seconds","lines":["Bicarbonate, phosphate, proteins, hemoglobin","Immediate, but only for small swings"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"2 · Lungs — minutes","lines":["High acid → faster, deeper breathing","Low acid → slower, shallower","Fast but limited"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"3 · Kidneys — hours to days","lines":["Keep HCO₃⁻, excrete H⁺ — or the reverse","Strongest, slowest (24–48 hr)"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Why","lines":["Fastest = weakest; strongest = slowest","Acute → uncompensated; chronic → kidneys have compensated"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Lungs cover for metabolic problems","lines":["Metabolic acidosis → lungs blow off CO₂","Kussmaul = respiratory compensation"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Kidneys cover for lung problems","lines":["Chronic CO₂ retention → kidneys keep HCO₃⁻","Gas: high PaCO₂ + high HCO₃⁻"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Full, partial — or not at all","lines":["Full: pH in range, values abnormal","Partial: pH still off","Never overshoots"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Combined (mixed) acidosis","lines":["Both at once — e.g., DKA + COPD","No organ left to compensate → more severe"]},{"note":"14-buffers-lungs-kidneys-and-compensation","title":"Why#2","lines":["Compensation buys time, doesn’t cure","Compare with the baseline / admission gas"]}]},{"id":"14-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"14-adpie","title":"¶ For any acid–base problem the nurse asks","lines":["Which way (pH)? Which organ (CO₂ vs HCO₃⁻)?","Acidosis: heart + K⁺ first; alkalosis: nerves, Ca²⁺, K⁺","Treat the cause; prevent falls"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"History","lines":["COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses","Opioids/sedation","Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["Heart first: rhythm, T waves, QRS, BP","Breathing: Kussmaul? effort, sentences, SpO₂","Mind vs baseline; grip, reflexes, Chvostek/Trousseau"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Labs","lines":["ABG: 7.35–7.45 · CO₂ 35–45 · HCO₃⁻ 21–28 · O₂ 80–100","K⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻, glucose/ketones, kidney labs","ECG"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Weakness or confusion can be either problem","Gas + electrolytes + bedside together"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Respiratory acidosis: reduced gas exchange","Acidosis: dysrhythmias (K⁺), falls","Alkalosis: injury (falls, tetany, seizures), weak breathing"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["pH → normal (or baseline); gases improving","K⁺, Ca²⁺ in range; stable ECG","No falls; knows the warning signs"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Acidosis","lines":["Metabolic: fluids, insulin, antidiarrheals, HCO₃⁻ if pH <7.2","Respiratory: airway, bronchodilators, lowest O₂","Ventilator if SpO₂ <90% or fatigue"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Alkalosis","lines":["Stop the cause","Replace fluids + K⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻; antiemetics","Anxiety: slow breathing after checking SpO₂"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Safety and teaching","lines":["Falls; neuro q2h; trend ABGs + K⁺","Teach: drugs as prescribed; no antacid overuse","Report persistent GI losses, weakness, confusion, dyspnea"]},{"note":"14-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["pH >7.2 → normal or baseline","PaO₂ >90 (+10); PaCO₂ <45 (−15)","K⁺, Ca²⁺ normal; rhythm, alertness, strength back","No tetany, seizures or falls"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"abg:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client’s arterial pH is 7.31. What can the nurse conclude from this value alone?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"pH 7.45 = alkalosis; the type comes from PaCO₂ (respiratory) and HCO₃⁻ (metabolic).","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"The client has acidosis, but its cause isn’t yet known","correct":true,"why":"Correct. The pH confirms acidosis; the PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ show whether it is respiratory or metabolic."},{"id":"o1","text":"The client has respiratory acidosis","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. That needs the PaCO₂."},{"id":"o2","text":"The client has metabolic acidosis","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. That needs the HCO₃⁻."},{"id":"o3","text":"The client’s acidosis is fully compensated","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. A fully compensated gas has a pH in range."}]},{"id":"abg:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Buffers seconds → lungs minutes → kidneys hours–days; Metabolic problem → lungs compensate; Lung problem → kidneys compensate (high HCO₃⁻ in chronic COPD)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"abg:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about history?","answer":"COPD, kidney failure, diabetes, GI losses; Opioids/sedation; Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids, digoxin","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Acidosis: ventilation, metabolism and the underlying cause","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Acid made or kept? · Bicarbonate not made or lost?"},{"title":"Signs it is working","text":"pH >7.2 → toward 7.35 · PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission · PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission"},{"title":"Treat the cause","text":"Fluids · Insulin for DKA; antidiarrheals for diarrhea"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Metabolic acidosis: acid made (DKA, starvation, lactic acid, alcohol, aspirin), acid kept (kidney failure), base not made (kidney, liver, pancreas failure, dehydration) or base lost (diarrhea).","task":"Which client is most at risk for metabolic acidosis?","cue":"Intestinal fluid is rich in bicarbonate → prolonged diarrhea removes base → the remaining acid lowers the pH.","rule":"Metabolic acidosis: acid made (DKA, starvation, lactic acid, alcohol, aspirin), acid kept (kidney failure), base not made (kidney, liver, pancreas failure, dehydration) or base lost (diarrhea).","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"acidosis","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Acidosis: ventilation, metabolism and the underlying cause","notes":["14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","14-recognizing-acidosis","14-caring-for-acidosis"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Acid made or kept?; Bicarbonate not made or lost?","Signs it is working: pH >7.2 → toward 7.35; PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission; PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission","Treat the cause: Fluids; Insulin for DKA; antidiarrheals for diarrhea","Why#2: All roads: CO₂ in > CO₂ out; Fix = better ventilation, not a pH drug"],"terms":["respiratory acidosis","metabolic acidosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Acidosis — the concept exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Too much acid made","lines":["DKA, starvation","Exercise, seizures, fever; lactic acid (hypoxia, shock)","Alcohol, methanol, aspirin"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Too little acid excreted","lines":["Kidney failure"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Too little bicarbonate made","lines":["Kidney, pancreas, liver failure; dehydration"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Too much bicarbonate lost","lines":["Diarrhea"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Actual vs relative","lines":["Actual: more acid","Relative: too little base"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Acid made or kept?","Bicarbonate not made or lost?"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Depressed breathing","lines":["Opioids, anesthetics, sedatives","Head injury, raised ICP"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"The chest can’t expand","lines":["Muscle weakness, rib fractures, deformity","Casts, scars, obesity"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Air can’t move or gas can’t cross","lines":["Airway obstruction","Pneumonia, emphysema/COPD, ARDS, PE, pulmonary edema"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"A ventilator set too low","lines":["Tidal volume or rate too low"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Who is at risk","lines":["Impaired breathing = highest acute risk","Older adults; diuretics, aspirin","COPD = most common chronic cause"]},{"note":"14-acidosis-the-concept-exemplar","title":"Why#2","lines":["All roads: CO₂ in > CO₂ out","Fix = better ventilation, not a pH drug"]}]},{"id":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Recognizing acidosis","blocks":[{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Heart — assess first","lines":["Early ↑ HR; then bradycardia → heart block","Tall T, wide QRS, long PR","Low BP, thready pulses — check first"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Brain","lines":["Lethargy → confusion → coma","Often the first sign: count back by threes","Compare with baseline (family)"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Muscles","lines":["Hyporeflexia, bilateral weakness → flaccid paralysis","Hand squeeze; feet push against a board"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Breathing and skin","lines":["Metabolic: Kussmaul + warm, flushed, dry skin","Respiratory: shallow, ineffective + pale/cyanotic"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Why","lines":["Similar signs — H⁺ disturbs K⁺","Hard breathing + flushed = metabolic","Poor breathing + dusky = respiratory"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Ask about","lines":["Lung, kidney, diabetes, diarrhea, pancreatitis, fever","Drugs: diuretics, aspirin, antacids","Last 24 hr of fluids and urine"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Assessment points for older adults","lines":["Mental status vs baseline","Can they finish a sentence?","Nail beds, mouth; turgor on sternum/forehead; pulse"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Why#2","lines":["Older adults: less lung and kidney reserve","First clue often a change in thinking"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Metabolic acidosis","lines":["pH ↓, HCO₃⁻ ↓ (15–20 or less)","PaO₂ normal; PaCO₂ normal or ↓"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Respiratory acidosis","lines":["pH ↓, PaCO₂ >50, PaO₂ <90","PaO₂ falls first","HCO₃⁻ normal (acute) → high (chronic)"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Potassium","lines":["Acidosis → K⁺ ↑ (H⁺ in, K⁺ out)","Chronic respiratory: normal or low","Monitor K⁺ in every acidosis"]},{"note":"14-recognizing-acidosis","title":"Why#3","lines":["pH confirms; ABG names the type","Types need different treatment"]}]},{"id":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Caring for acidosis","blocks":[{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Treat the cause","lines":["Fluids","Insulin for DKA; antidiarrheals for diarrhea"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Bicarbonate — only sometimes","lines":["HCO₃⁻ low + pH <7.2","Kidneys can’t compensate; rhabdomyolysis not responding to fluids"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Monitor continuously","lines":["Heart + skeletal muscle first","Trend ABGs and K⁺"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Why","lines":["Fix the cause; bicarbonate only if pH <7.2 and HCO₃⁻ low"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Priority problem","lines":["Reduced gas exchange","Goal: fewer acidosis signs"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Drugs and oxygen","lines":["Bronchodilators, anti-inflammatories, mucolytics","Lowest O₂ that prevents hypoxemia","Positioning + breathing techniques"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Ventilation support","lines":["SpO₂ can’t stay ≥90% or muscle fatigue → ventilator"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Hourly breathing checks","lines":["Breath sounds, retractions","Neck accessory muscles","Audible grunt/wheeze; cyanosis = late"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Why#2","lines":["Fix = move air","Work of breathing changes before the gas"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Prevent falls","lines":["Low BP + confusion → falls","Fall precautions for any acid–base problem"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Neuro checks","lines":["Neuro at least every 2 hr"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Signs it is working","lines":["pH >7.2 → toward 7.35","PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission","PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission"]},{"note":"14-caring-for-acidosis","title":"Why#3","lines":["Judge against admission values, not only the normal range"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"acidosis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which client is most at risk for metabolic acidosis?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Metabolic acidosis: acid made (DKA, starvation, lactic acid, alcohol, aspirin), acid kept (kidney failure), base not made (kidney, liver, pancreas failure, dehydration) or base lost (diarrhea).","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A client with 4 days of severe diarrhea","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Diarrhea carries bicarbonate out of the gut — a base-loss acidosis."},{"id":"o1","text":"A client on continuous NG suction","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Suction removes acid → metabolic alkalosis."},{"id":"o2","text":"A client who takes a bicarbonate antacid daily","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Extra base → metabolic alkalosis."},{"id":"o3","text":"A client hyperventilating from anxiety","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Blowing off CO₂ → respiratory alkalosis."}]},{"id":"acidosis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Acid made or kept?; Bicarbonate not made or lost?","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"acidosis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs it is working?","answer":"pH >7.2 → toward 7.35; PaO₂ >90 or +10 from admission; PaCO₂ <45 or −15 from admission","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Alkalosis: CO₂ loss versus acid loss","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Usually taken in or lost out · Often the nurse can stop the cause"},{"title":"Heart and breathing","text":"↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP · Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity · Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)"},{"title":"Respiratory — CO₂ blown off","text":"Anxiety/fear hyperventilation; ventilator settings · Fever, brain lesions, salicylates, altitude · Gas: pH ↑ + PaCO₂ ↓"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety: check SpO₂ (rule out hypoxia), stay, reassure, coach slow breathing.","task":"A client is breathing fast after hearing bad news and says the fingers tingle. SpO₂ 99%; ABG pH 7.50, PaCO₂ 29 mm Hg. Which action is best?","cue":"Anxiety speeds breathing → CO₂ is exhaled faster than it is made → pH rises → ionized calcium falls → tingling.","rule":"Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety: check SpO₂ (rule out hypoxia), stay, reassure, coach slow breathing.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"alkalosis","ch":"14","chapters":["14"],"title":"Alkalosis: CO₂ loss versus acid loss","notes":["14-alkalosis"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 14; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Usually taken in or lost out; Often the nurse can stop the cause","Heart and breathing: ↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP; Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity; Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)","Respiratory — CO₂ blown off: Anxiety/fear hyperventilation; ventilator settings; Fever, brain lesions, salicylates, altitude; Gas: pH ↑ + PaCO₂ ↓","Why#2: Looks like low Ca²⁺ + low K⁺ together; Nerves overfire, muscles weaken"],"terms":["respiratory alkalosis","metabolic alkalosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"14-alkalosis","title":"Alkalosis","blocks":[{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Metabolic — base gained","lines":["Antacids, IV bicarbonate","Massive transfusion (citrate), TPN","Gas: pH ↑ + HCO₃⁻ ↑"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Metabolic — acid lost","lines":["Vomiting, NG suction","Excess cortisol/aldosterone","Loop/thiazide diuretics"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Respiratory — CO₂ blown off","lines":["Anxiety/fear hyperventilation; ventilator settings","Fever, brain lesions, salicylates, altitude","Gas: pH ↑ + PaCO₂ ↓"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Why","lines":["Usually taken in or lost out","Often the nurse can stop the cause"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Overexcited nerves","lines":["Anxiety, irritability, confusion","Tingling mouth and toes","Chvostek, Trousseau, seizures"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Muscles: twitchy but weak","lines":["Hyperreflexia, cramps, tetany (= worsening fast)","Low K⁺ → weak grip, can’t stand","Breathing effort weakens"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Heart and breathing","lines":["↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP","Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity","Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Why#2","lines":["Looks like low Ca²⁺ + low K⁺ together","Nerves overfire, muscles weaken"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Stop the cause","lines":["Stop/modify: NG suction, IV fluids, diuretics","Antiemetics; replace fluid + electrolytes"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Replace and monitor","lines":["Prevent loss of H⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Cl⁻","Electrolytes daily"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Prevent falls","lines":["Falls = the priority (low BP + weakness)"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety","lines":["Calm reassurance; coach slow breathing","O₂ as needed; watch HCO₃⁻"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Beyond the book","lines":["Paper bag can cause hypoxemia","Rule out PE, asthma, MI first — check SpO₂"]},{"note":"14-alkalosis","title":"Why#3","lines":["Danger: falls, tetany, seizures, electrolyte loss","Stop the loss + keep safe"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"alkalosis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Usually taken in or lost out; Often the nurse can stop the cause","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"alkalosis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about heart and breathing?","answer":"↑ HR, thready pulse, low BP; Low K⁺ → digoxin toxicity; Fast breathing (respiratory) vs weak (metabolic)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"IV therapy: verify the prescription, solution and pump","cards":[{"title":"Flow dilutes — why the vein matters","text":"SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min · Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots · Central tip dilutes it instantly"},{"title":"Medication safety (NPSG)","text":"Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation · High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers · Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA"},{"title":"Why","text":"Small veins tolerate little → match infusate to vein · Every catheter is a doorway → asepsis, remove early · Pressure → Luer-Lok, 10-mL syringe, clamps"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"A complete fluid order: type of fluid, rate in mL/hr (or a volume over a set time), and additives. Make sure the order is right for this client and complete; clarify anything unclear before giving it.","task":"A prescription reads “D5W to keep the vein open.” What should the nurse do?","cue":"Without a rate, the volume the client receives is up to whoever sets the pump — an error waiting to happen, especially for a client at risk of overload.","rule":"A complete fluid order: type of fluid, rate in mL/hr (or a volume over a set time), and additives. Make sure the order is right for this client and complete; clarify anything unclear before giving it.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"iv-plan","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"IV therapy: verify the prescription, solution and pump","notes":["15-overview","15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","15-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Flow dilutes — why the vein matters: SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min; Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots; Central tip dilutes it instantly","Medication safety (NPSG): Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation; High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers; Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA","Why: Small veins tolerate little → match infusate to vein; Every catheter is a doorway → asepsis, remove early; Pressure → Luer-Lok, 10-mL syringe, clamps","Why: IV = seconds to effect, full strength on the vein; Own checks: dilution, rate, pH/osmolarity, compatibility, vein"],"terms":["intravenous","isotonic","extravasation"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"15-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"15-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Fluids and drugs by vein (or bone, artery, abdomen, spine) — the most common invasive therapy","Chain: complete order → match fluid to vein → choose device → place aseptically → keep safe","pH, osmolarity decide peripheral vs central","Dangers: infiltration/extravasation, phlebitis, thrombosis, CRBSI, overload, speed shock, embolism"]},{"note":"15-overview","title":"Flow dilutes — why the vein matters","lines":["SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min","Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots","Central tip dilutes it instantly"]},{"note":"15-overview","title":"How catheters become infected","lines":["Outside: skin organisms track along the catheter (neck, groin worst)","Inside: contaminated hubs","Tunnel + cuff block; risk rises each day"]},{"note":"15-overview","title":"Pressure, syringes and rupture","lines":["Smaller barrel → higher pressure","Can rupture a catheter or dislodge a clot","Flush central lines with 10-mL syringes"]},{"note":"15-overview","title":"Air embolism on removal","lines":["Breathing in → chest pressure falls → air drawn in","Flat/Trendelenburg + Valsalva, clamp","Occlusive dressing ≥ 24 hr"]},{"note":"15-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Small veins tolerate little → match infusate to vein","Every catheter is a doorway → asepsis, remove early","Pressure → Luer-Lok, 10-mL syringe, clamps"]}]},{"id":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"IV fluids, blood and the prescription","blocks":[{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Tonicity","lines":["Isotonic: 0.9% NaCl, D5W, LR → overload risk","Hypertonic: D5½NS, D10W, PN; hypotonic: 0.45% NaCl","Hyper/hypo → phlebitis, infiltration"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"pH and osmolarity limits","lines":["Usual pH 3.5–6.2","pH 9, > 600 mOsm/L → central","TPN > 1400 — never peripheral"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Irritants and vesicants (Drug Alert)","lines":["Irritants: amiodarone, vancomycin, ciprofloxacin → phlebitis","Vesicants: dopamine, chemo → necrosis","Check pH/osmolarity before choosing access"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Blood and blood products","lines":["PRBCs, platelets, FFP, albumin, factors","Two identifiers + two professionals","ISBT label: facility, lot, product, ABO/Rh"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Why","lines":["IV = seconds to effect, full strength on the vein","Own checks: dilution, rate, pH/osmolarity, compatibility, vein"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"A complete fluid order","lines":["Fluid + rate (mL/hr) + additives","“TKO/KVO” alone is incomplete"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"A complete drug order","lines":["Name, dose, route, frequency, time, duration","Purpose (some agencies)"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Action Alert","lines":["Appropriate + complete → clarify first"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Medication safety (NPSG)","lines":["Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation","High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers","Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA"]},{"note":"15-iv-fluids-blood-and-the-prescription","title":"Why#2","lines":["Errors start before the needle","Check the order; layered technology catches the rest"]}]},{"id":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Infusion systems: containers, tubing, connectors and pumps","blocks":[{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Containers","lines":["Glass: inert, vented; plastic: collapses, light","DEHP-free bags: stiffer, can burst","PVC binds insulin, nitroglycerin","Check for cracks, cloudiness, particles"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Administration sets and change times","lines":["Continuous primary/secondary: ≤ 7 days (or q96h)","Intermittent: 24 hr; PN 24 hr; lipids 12 hr","Blood tubing: ≤ 4 hr"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Connections","lines":["Luer-Lok everywhere (air embolism)","Never tape connections","Trace the line before reconnecting"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Filters","lines":["Near the hub; 1.2 µm (lipids) / 0.2 µm","Particles lodge in lung capillaries","Blood filters are different (170–260 µm)"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Why","lines":["Every junction = a door for air, particles, microbes","Inspect · lock · filter · change on time"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Needleless connectors","lines":["Scrub the hub 5–15 s every access","Change with the set, weekly, with blood residue or leaks","Know your valve type for flushing"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Electronic pumps","lines":["Alarms + free-flow protection","Syringe pumps: small volumes, PCA, fluid limits","Pump ≠ assessment"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Smart pumps","lines":["Drug libraries + limits","Keep manual math; validate alarms","Alarm fatigue = NPSG"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Mechanical infusers","lines":["No power; preset rate","Home antibiotics, post-op pain","~50–100 mL"]},{"note":"15-infusion-systems-containers-tubing-conne","title":"Why#2","lines":["Pumps move errors, don’t end them","Nurse = final check (site, patient, line)"]}]},{"id":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"IV therapy in older adults","blocks":[{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Fragile skin","lines":["May not feel a bad stick","Skin tears from tape","CHG preferred; povidone-iodine 2 min"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Hair and adhesives","lines":["Clip, don’t shave","Skin protectant; adhesive remover"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Choosing the vein","lines":["Skip hand veins; go higher","Flat tourniquet over cloth; or BP cuff < diastolic","Warmth 10–20 min; arm down","10–15° angle; release tourniquet early"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Device choice and confusion","lines":["Poor veins → midline/PICC","Trendelenburg hard with COPD, kyphosis","Confused: site protector, still visible"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Why","lines":["Skin: tears, feels less","Veins: roll, fill slowly → higher, gentler"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Accurate volumes","lines":["Easy overload or dehydration","Pumps for accuracy"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Fluid restriction and sodium","lines":["Smallest diluent; syringe pump/IV push","Saline diluent adds sodium"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Saving veins","lines":["Vein-depleted patients","SC and IO routes for isotonic fluids"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Why#2","lines":["Little margin → precise volume and rate","Protect remaining veins"]}]},{"id":"15-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"15-adpie","title":"¶ For any infusion the nurse checks the","lines":["Order complete? Match fluid → vein, device → duration","Access: site, dressing, connections, patency, length","Patient: fluid, infection; act on CRBSI, overload, speed shock, embolism"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Before","lines":["Order; pH, osmolarity, vesicant","Duration; allergies","Arms: mastectomy, fistula, paralysis; heart/kidneys"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"During","lines":["Site + whole limb; dressing; connections","Rate, volume, labels; blood return; external length","I&O, overload, temperature"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Fixed path: patient → site → tubing → pump → bag"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Infection (CRBSI)","Injury: infiltration, extravasation, embolism","Fluid imbalance; tissue integrity"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Prescribed rate; patent, secure device","No CRBSI; complications prevented","Patient knows signs to report; line out when not needed"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Placing","lines":["Device by duration; site by Box 15.1","HANDS; 2 attempts; CRBSI bundle","Confirm tip before use"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Maintaining","lines":["Checks q4h; scrub the hub; Luer-Lok","Flush (10-mL, stop at resistance)","Dressings/tubing on time; daily review"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Responding and teaching","lines":["Complication actions by Tables 15.3–15.5","Teach device, hygiene, limits, signs","Document insertion + assessments"]},{"note":"15-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Right rate; site clean, painless","Blood return; external length unchanged","Afebrile; I&O balanced; skin intact","Patient knows signs; device out at the end"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"iv-plan:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A prescription reads “D5W to keep the vein open.” What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"A complete fluid order: type of fluid, rate in mL/hr (or a volume over a set time), and additives. Make sure the order is right for this client and complete; clarify anything unclear before giving it.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Contact the provider to clarify the rate before starting it","correct":true,"why":"Correct. A fluid order needs the fluid, the rate and any additives; “keep the vein open” alone is incomplete."},{"id":"o1","text":"Run it at 10 mL/hr, the usual keep-open rate","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The nurse can’t supply a missing rate."},{"id":"o2","text":"Run it at 125 mL/hr","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. That guesses at a rate that may overload the client."},{"id":"o3","text":"Start it and ask about the rate at the end of the shift","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Clarify before giving it."}]},{"id":"iv-plan:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about flow dilutes — why the vein matters?","answer":"SVC ~2 L/min vs axillary ~200 mL/min; Concentrated infusate injures the lining → phlebitis, clots; Central tip dilutes it instantly","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"iv-plan:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about medication safety (npsg)?","answer":"Labels, anticoagulants, reconciliation; High-alert, look-alike drugs; single-dose containers; Smart pumps + CPOE + BCMA","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Peripheral IVs: insertion, assessment and early complications","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein · Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining"},{"title":"Choosing the gauge (Table 15.2)","text":"Smallest gauge that works · 22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery · 14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation · Bigger → more phlebitis"},{"title":"Choosing the site (Box 15.1)","text":"Upper arm/forearm, nondominant, distal → proximal · Not: wrist, flexion, hard veins, legs · Not: mastectomy, lymph dissection, fistula, paralysis"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Nerve injury (Critical Rescue): avoid the palm side of the wrist and the cephalic vein near the wrist; tingling, “pins and needles” or numbness → stop, remove, new site.","task":"While the nurse is inserting a peripheral catheter, the client says, “I feel pins and needles shooting down my fingers.” What should the nurse do first?","cue":"Nerves run beside veins — the median nerve on the palm side of the wrist; touching one sends tingling or electric pain along its path, and continued probing can injure it permanently.","rule":"Nerve injury (Critical Rescue): avoid the palm side of the wrist and the cephalic vein near the wrist; tingling, “pins and needles” or numbness → stop, remove, new site.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"peripheral-iv","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Peripheral IVs: insertion, assessment and early complications","notes":["15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","15-complications-of-iv-therapy","15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein; Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining","Choosing the gauge (Table 15.2): Smallest gauge that works; 22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery; 14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation; Bigger → more phlebitis","Choosing the site (Box 15.1): Upper arm/forearm, nondominant, distal → proximal; Not: wrist, flexion, hard veins, legs; Not: mastectomy, lymph dissection, fistula, paralysis","Why#2: Midline = peripheral rules; TPN or vesicant in a midline = classic error"],"terms":["phlebitis","infiltration","extravasation"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Peripheral catheters: short catheters and midlines","blocks":[{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"What they are","lines":["Cannula over a stylet","Safety mechanism required","¾–1¼ inch; 26 g (small) → 14 g (large)"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Choosing the gauge (Table 15.2)","lines":["Smallest gauge that works","22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery","14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation","Bigger → more phlebitis"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Choosing the site (Box 15.1)","lines":["Upper arm/forearm, nondominant, distal → proximal","Not: wrist, flexion, hard veins, legs","Not: mastectomy, lymph dissection, fistula, paralysis"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Two attempts, then escalate","lines":["Two tries per clinician → escalate","Ultrasound: trained users; deep leaks found late","Vein lights: ~10 mm deep"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Nerve injury (Critical Rescue)","lines":["Avoid palmar wrist, cephalic vein at the wrist","Tingling/numbness → stop, remove, new site"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Dwell, checks and locks","lines":["Remove when clinically indicated (no routine 72–96 hr change)","Check q4h (continuous); q1–2h critical/confused; more with vesicants","Saline lock: flush before/after; no routine blood draws"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Why","lines":["Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein","Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"HANDS (Box 15.2)","lines":["Hygiene · Antisepsis (CHG–alcohol, 30 s, dry)","No touch · Document (dates)","Scrub the hub every time"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Midline catheters","lines":["3–8 in, upper arm (basilic), tip ≤ axillary vein","6–14 days of therapy","Sterile insertion and dressings"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"What a midline can’t take","lines":["pH 5–9, < 600 mOsm/L","No vesicants, no PN, no routine blood draws","No incompatible drugs in two lumens"]},{"note":"15-peripheral-catheters-short-catheters-and","title":"Why#2","lines":["Midline = peripheral rules","TPN or vesicant in a midline = classic error"]}]},{"id":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Daily care: assessing, dressing, flushing and removing","blocks":[{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Teaching before insertion","lines":["Device, hand hygiene, therapy, alternatives, limits","Signs to report; written + interpreter"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"A systematic check","lines":["Know type, length, site, tip","Site → tubing → rate → label","Whole limb/chest (PICC); tunnel; pocket"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Action Alerts at the bedside","lines":["No BP cuff on the catheter arm","Blood draw: opposite arm","Keep the site dry for baths"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Securing and dressings","lines":["Securement devices; clean kit tape only","Peripheral: change only if loose/soiled","Central: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days; first within 24 hr","Peel toward the site; check external length"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Why","lines":["Problems show at the site first","Systematic checks catch them small"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Flushing (Critical Rescue)","lines":["Keeps lumens open, drugs apart","Resistance → stop (rupture, clot)","3 mL peripheral; 5–10 mL others; bacteriostatic ≤ 30 mL/day","Prefilled; flush right away; push-pause"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Blood from central lines","lines":["Only if benefit > CRBSI risk","Heparin/electrolytes skew results; cultures > 1 hr after antibiotics","Flush 10–20 mL after"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Removing peripheral lines and PICCs","lines":["Peripheral: pressure, intact tip","PICC: short segments, never force","Stuck: heat, warm drinks, wait"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Removing central lines","lines":["Flat/Trendelenburg + Valsalva, clamped","Occlusive dressing ≥ 24 hr","Shorter than inserted → emergency"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Documentation","lines":["Date/time, inserter, vein","Device, gauge, length, attempts","Dressing, securement, teaching"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Why#2","lines":["Breaks happen at flushing and removal","Stop at resistance; measure after removal"]}]},{"id":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Complications of IV therapy","blocks":[{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Infiltration","lines":["Edema, cool tight skin, slowing rate","Stop, remove, elevate; new site other arm","Prevent: small catheter, avoid flexion"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Extravasation","lines":["Vesicant → blisters, necrosis (may be delayed)","Stop, disconnect, aspirate; catheter in for antidote","Cold — except vinca alkaloids, epipodophyllotoxins","Photograph"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Phlebitis","lines":["Mechanical / chemical / bacterial","Red, painful, cordlike vein; even after removal","Remove peripheral; warm compresses; PICC: treat in place first week","Prevent: pH 5–9, < 500 mOsm/L peripherally"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Thrombosis and thrombophlebitis","lines":["Swollen limb, slowed flow; engorged chest veins (CVC)","Remove peripheral; cold, elevate","Thrombophlebitis: cold → warm"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Bleeding, infection, spasm and nerves","lines":["Hematoma: ice 24 hr → warm","Infection: clean, remove, culture tip","Spasm: warm, slow — don’t remove","Nerve: stop"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Why","lines":["Cold = narrow: clots, bleeding, most vesicants","Warm = open: spasm, phlebitis, vinca alkaloids"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Circulatory overload","lines":["Dyspnea, cough, JVD, crackles, ↑BP","Slow, upright, notify; O₂, diuretics","Prevent: I&O"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Speed shock","lines":["Too fast → toxic level","Dizzy, chest tight, flushed, irregular pulse","Stop, isotonic KVO, notify"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Catheter embolism","lines":["Broken piece → arrest risk","Tourniquet high on limb, notify emergently","Never reinsert the stylet; no scissors"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Migration and dislodgment","lines":["Migration: same length — gurgling sound, back pain","Dislodgment: length changed","Stop, flush, notify; never re-advance"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Rupture and occlusion","lines":["Rupture: forced flush, scissors, pinch-off","Sudden block = precipitate; gradual = clot (alteplase)","Prevent: flush between and after"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Catheter-related bloodstream infection","lines":["Early: fever, chills, malaise","Late: ↑HR, ↓BP, ↓urine","Change the system, cultures, antibiotics"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Why#2","lines":["External length = ruler for the hidden tip","Changed length → tip moved → stop and notify"]}]},{"id":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"IV therapy in older adults","blocks":[{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Fragile skin","lines":["May not feel a bad stick","Skin tears from tape","CHG preferred; povidone-iodine 2 min"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Hair and adhesives","lines":["Clip, don’t shave","Skin protectant; adhesive remover"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Choosing the vein","lines":["Skip hand veins; go higher","Flat tourniquet over cloth; or BP cuff < diastolic","Warmth 10–20 min; arm down","10–15° angle; release tourniquet early"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Device choice and confusion","lines":["Poor veins → midline/PICC","Trendelenburg hard with COPD, kyphosis","Confused: site protector, still visible"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Why","lines":["Skin: tears, feels less","Veins: roll, fill slowly → higher, gentler"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Accurate volumes","lines":["Easy overload or dehydration","Pumps for accuracy"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Fluid restriction and sodium","lines":["Smallest diluent; syringe pump/IV push","Saline diluent adds sodium"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Saving veins","lines":["Vein-depleted patients","SC and IO routes for isotonic fluids"]},{"note":"15-iv-therapy-in-older-adults","title":"Why#2","lines":["Little margin → precise volume and rate","Protect remaining veins"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"peripheral-iv:case","kind":"choice","scene":"While the nurse is inserting a peripheral catheter, the client says, “I feel pins and needles shooting down my fingers.” What should the nurse do first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Nerve injury (Critical Rescue): avoid the palm side of the wrist and the cephalic vein near the wrist; tingling, “pins and needles” or numbness → stop, remove, new site.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Stop and remove the catheter","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Tingling or numbness means a nerve is being touched; stop at once and choose a new site."},{"id":"o1","text":"Advance the catheter quickly to finish","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Advancing could injure the nerve further."},{"id":"o2","text":"Reassure the client that this is expected","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. It isn’t expected."},{"id":"o3","text":"Withdraw slightly and redirect the catheter","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Probing near a nerve can cause permanent injury."}]},{"id":"peripheral-iv:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Smallest catheter, biggest suitable vein; Blood flows around it → dilutes, protects the lining","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"peripheral-iv:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about choosing the gauge (table 15.2)?","answer":"Smallest gauge that works; 22 g: most therapy, blood OK; 18–20 g: surgery; 14–16 g: trauma/resuscitation; Bigger → more phlebitis","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Central lines: infection prevention and protecting the connection","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk) · Choose by duration and patient risks"},{"title":"PICCs","text":"Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early · Short tip → thrombosis · Any infusate; > 14 days · Fewer infections; no pneumothorax"},{"title":"PICC care","text":"Phlebitis, DVT, CRBSI; baseline arm circumference · Blood draw ≥ 4 Fr; transfusion via pump (< 4 hr) · No heavy lifting; not with crutches · Flush 10 mL before/after, 20 mL after blood; 10-mL syringe"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Tell AP: no blood pressure cuff and no blood draws on an arm with a catheter; use the opposite arm; keep the site dry for bathing.","task":"The nurse sees an assistive personnel (AP) about to take the blood pressure on the arm with a client’s PICC. What should the nurse say?","cue":"Inflating a cuff obstructs the vein above the catheter, raising the pressure inside it → fluid leaks (infiltration) or the catheter and vein are irritated or damaged.","rule":"Tell AP: no blood pressure cuff and no blood draws on an arm with a catheter; use the opposite arm; keep the site dry for bathing.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"central-lines","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Central lines: infection prevention and protecting the connection","notes":["15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","15-complications-of-iv-therapy"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk); Choose by duration and patient risks","PICCs: Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early; Short tip → thrombosis; Any infusate; > 14 days; Fewer infections; no pneumothorax","PICC care: Phlebitis, DVT, CRBSI; baseline arm circumference; Blood draw ≥ 4 Fr; transfusion via pump (< 4 hr); No heavy lifting; not with crutches; Flush 10 mL before/after, 20 mL after blood; 10-mL syringe","Why#2: Risk rises daily; Strongest step: daily “still needed?”"],"terms":["PICC","asepsis","embolism"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Central lines: PICCs, central catheters, tunneled catheters and ports","blocks":[{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Where the tip belongs","lines":["Tip at the cavoatrial junction","Confirm by x-ray/ECG before use","Only power-rated lines for CT contrast"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"PICCs","lines":["Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early","Short tip → thrombosis","Any infusate; > 14 days","Fewer infections; no pneumothorax"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"PICC care","lines":["Phlebitis, DVT, CRBSI; baseline arm circumference","Blood draw ≥ 4 Fr; transfusion via pump (< 4 hr)","No heavy lifting; not with crutches","Flush 10 mL before/after, 20 mL after blood; 10-mL syringe"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Nontunneled central catheters","lines":["Subclavian/IJ; femoral = highest infection","Short-term: ICU, trauma, surgery","Insertion in Trendelenburg (not with ↑ICP, lung disease)"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Why","lines":["PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk)","Choose by duration and patient risks"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Tunneled catheters","lines":["Tunnel + cuff = barrier + anchor","Weeks–months; paraplegia, needle-averse"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Implanted ports","lines":["Septum + reservoir under the skin — best body image","> 1 year; noncoring needle, mask, asepsis","Chest ~2000 sticks; arm ~750"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Check blood return first (Drug Alert)","lines":["No blood return → hold the drug","Fibrin sheath → vesicant leaks backward","Needle can rebound on removal"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Hemodialysis catheters","lines":["Heparin dwell — aspirate first","Dialysis only (emergency excepted)"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"CRBSI prevention bundle (Box 15.3)","lines":["Checklist + stop; hand hygiene; time-out","Maximal barriers; masks for all; little traffic","Chlorhexidine; avoid femoral","Daily review — remove early"]},{"note":"15-central-lines-piccs-central-catheters-tu","title":"Why#2","lines":["Risk rises daily","Strongest step: daily “still needed?”"]}]},{"id":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Daily care: assessing, dressing, flushing and removing","blocks":[{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Teaching before insertion","lines":["Device, hand hygiene, therapy, alternatives, limits","Signs to report; written + interpreter"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"A systematic check","lines":["Know type, length, site, tip","Site → tubing → rate → label","Whole limb/chest (PICC); tunnel; pocket"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Action Alerts at the bedside","lines":["No BP cuff on the catheter arm","Blood draw: opposite arm","Keep the site dry for baths"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Securing and dressings","lines":["Securement devices; clean kit tape only","Peripheral: change only if loose/soiled","Central: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days; first within 24 hr","Peel toward the site; check external length"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Why","lines":["Problems show at the site first","Systematic checks catch them small"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Flushing (Critical Rescue)","lines":["Keeps lumens open, drugs apart","Resistance → stop (rupture, clot)","3 mL peripheral; 5–10 mL others; bacteriostatic ≤ 30 mL/day","Prefilled; flush right away; push-pause"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Blood from central lines","lines":["Only if benefit > CRBSI risk","Heparin/electrolytes skew results; cultures > 1 hr after antibiotics","Flush 10–20 mL after"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Removing peripheral lines and PICCs","lines":["Peripheral: pressure, intact tip","PICC: short segments, never force","Stuck: heat, warm drinks, wait"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Removing central lines","lines":["Flat/Trendelenburg + Valsalva, clamped","Occlusive dressing ≥ 24 hr","Shorter than inserted → emergency"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Documentation","lines":["Date/time, inserter, vein","Device, gauge, length, attempts","Dressing, securement, teaching"]},{"note":"15-daily-care-assessing-dressing-flushing-a","title":"Why#2","lines":["Breaks happen at flushing and removal","Stop at resistance; measure after removal"]}]},{"id":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Complications of IV therapy","blocks":[{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Infiltration","lines":["Edema, cool tight skin, slowing rate","Stop, remove, elevate; new site other arm","Prevent: small catheter, avoid flexion"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Extravasation","lines":["Vesicant → blisters, necrosis (may be delayed)","Stop, disconnect, aspirate; catheter in for antidote","Cold — except vinca alkaloids, epipodophyllotoxins","Photograph"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Phlebitis","lines":["Mechanical / chemical / bacterial","Red, painful, cordlike vein; even after removal","Remove peripheral; warm compresses; PICC: treat in place first week","Prevent: pH 5–9, < 500 mOsm/L peripherally"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Thrombosis and thrombophlebitis","lines":["Swollen limb, slowed flow; engorged chest veins (CVC)","Remove peripheral; cold, elevate","Thrombophlebitis: cold → warm"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Bleeding, infection, spasm and nerves","lines":["Hematoma: ice 24 hr → warm","Infection: clean, remove, culture tip","Spasm: warm, slow — don’t remove","Nerve: stop"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Why","lines":["Cold = narrow: clots, bleeding, most vesicants","Warm = open: spasm, phlebitis, vinca alkaloids"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Circulatory overload","lines":["Dyspnea, cough, JVD, crackles, ↑BP","Slow, upright, notify; O₂, diuretics","Prevent: I&O"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Speed shock","lines":["Too fast → toxic level","Dizzy, chest tight, flushed, irregular pulse","Stop, isotonic KVO, notify"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Catheter embolism","lines":["Broken piece → arrest risk","Tourniquet high on limb, notify emergently","Never reinsert the stylet; no scissors"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Migration and dislodgment","lines":["Migration: same length — gurgling sound, back pain","Dislodgment: length changed","Stop, flush, notify; never re-advance"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Rupture and occlusion","lines":["Rupture: forced flush, scissors, pinch-off","Sudden block = precipitate; gradual = clot (alteplase)","Prevent: flush between and after"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Catheter-related bloodstream infection","lines":["Early: fever, chills, malaise","Late: ↑HR, ↓BP, ↓urine","Change the system, cultures, antibiotics"]},{"note":"15-complications-of-iv-therapy","title":"Why#2","lines":["External length = ruler for the hidden tip","Changed length → tip moved → stop and notify"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"central-lines:case","kind":"choice","scene":"The nurse sees an assistive personnel (AP) about to take the blood pressure on the arm with a client’s PICC. What should the nurse say?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Tell AP: no blood pressure cuff and no blood draws on an arm with a catheter; use the opposite arm; keep the site dry for bathing.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Please use the other arm — pressure can damage the catheter.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. No BP cuff on an arm with any catheter."},{"id":"o1","text":"“That’s fine as long as the cuff isn’t over the insertion site.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The cuff squeezes the whole vein, not just the site."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Take it quickly so the catheter isn’t compressed for long.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Even brief pressure can cause leaking or damage."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Use a thigh cuff on that arm instead.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Any cuff on that arm is the problem."}]},{"id":"central-lines:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"PICC: central power via the arm (no pneumothorax; DVT risk); Choose by duration and patient risks","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"central-lines:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about piccs?","answer":"Arm vein (basilic), 18–29 in; place early; Short tip → thrombosis; Any infusate; > 14 days; Fewer infections; no pneumothorax","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Special infusion routes: the device changes the precautions","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock · IO = IV-speed access when no vein"},{"title":"Running a subcutaneous line","text":"25–27 g; pinch ~2 in · Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home) · Irritation → remove; rotate weekly"},{"title":"Intraspinal safety","text":"Filter + preservative-free; no alcohol (use povidone-iodine) · Check alertness, breathing, itching · Stiff neck, > 101 °F, neuro change → report now · Epidural hematoma (anticoagulants)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Hypodermoclysis: slow isotonic fluid for steady rehydration; not for emergencies or large volumes; sites with intact skin and more fat (thighs, hips, upper abdomen, below the clavicle), not the arms; check q4h in hospital, rotate at least weekly.","task":"For which client is hypodermoclysis (a subcutaneous infusion) most appropriate?","cue":"Fluid in the subcutaneous fat is absorbed into the capillaries gradually; hyaluronidase can speed it, but the route can’t restore volume quickly.","rule":"Hypodermoclysis: slow isotonic fluid for steady rehydration; not for emergencies or large volumes; sites with intact skin and more fat (thighs, hips, upper abdomen, below the clavicle), not the arms; check q4h in hospital, rotate at least weekly.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"special-infusion-routes","ch":"15","chapters":["15"],"title":"Special infusion routes: the device changes the precautions","notes":["15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 15; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock; IO = IV-speed access when no vein","Running a subcutaneous line: 25–27 g; pinch ~2 in; Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home); Irritation → remove; rotate weekly","Intraspinal safety: Filter + preservative-free; no alcohol (use povidone-iodine); Check alertness, breathing, itching; Stiff neck, > 101 °F, neuro change → report now; Epidural hematoma (anticoagulants)","Why#2: Artery: pressure → bleeding; Peritoneum: pressure, peritonitis; Spine: cord — infection, hematoma"],"terms":["hypodermoclysis","intraosseous","epidural"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Other routes: subcutaneous, intraosseous, arterial, peritoneal and spinal","blocks":[{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Subcutaneous infusion","lines":["Isotonic, slow — not for emergencies","Sites: thighs, hips, upper abdomen, below clavicle; not arms","Hyaluronidase aids absorption"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Running a subcutaneous line","lines":["25–27 g; pinch ~2 in","Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home)","Irritation → remove; rotate weekly"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Intraosseous access","lines":["Marrow absorbs like IV; crises","Proximal tibia most common; 15–16 g","≤ 24 hours; absolute no: fractured bone"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"IO complications","lines":["Infiltration most common","Osteomyelitis; marrow blockage","Compartment syndrome: cool, swollen, mottled → report"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Why","lines":["Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock","IO = IV-speed access when no vein"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Arterial lines","lines":["Sampling + pressure monitoring; transducer at atrium level","Luer-Lok — disconnection = hemorrhage","Distal pulse, warmth, refill; carotid → neuro"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Intraperitoneal therapy","lines":["Instill → dwell 1–4 hr → drain","Semi-Fowler’s; dyspnea/nausea → slow","Severe pain → migration; watch peritonitis"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Epidural and intrathecal","lines":["Epidural (outside dura) vs intrathecal (CSF — far smaller doses)","Pain, CNS chemo, spasticity","Know which space"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Intraspinal safety","lines":["Filter + preservative-free; no alcohol (use povidone-iodine)","Check alertness, breathing, itching","Stiff neck, > 101 °F, neuro change → report now","Epidural hematoma (anticoagulants)"]},{"note":"15-other-routes-subcutaneous-intraosseous-a","title":"Why#2","lines":["Artery: pressure → bleeding","Peritoneum: pressure, peritonitis","Spine: cord — infection, hematoma"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"special-infusion-routes:case","kind":"choice","scene":"For which client is hypodermoclysis (a subcutaneous infusion) most appropriate?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Hypodermoclysis: slow isotonic fluid for steady rehydration; not for emergencies or large volumes; sites with intact skin and more fat (thighs, hips, upper abdomen, below the clavicle), not the arms; check q4h in hospital, rotate at least weekly.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A client in long-term care with mild dehydration and poor veins","correct":true,"why":"Correct. A slow isotonic infusion into the fat gives steady rehydration and spares the veins."},{"id":"o1","text":"A client in hypovolemic shock after trauma","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not for emergencies — absorption is too slow."},{"id":"o2","text":"A client who needs a vesicant chemotherapy drug","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Vesicants need central access."},{"id":"o3","text":"A client who needs 3 L of fluid in the next hour","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Hypodermoclysis delivers small volumes slowly."}]},{"id":"special-infusion-routes:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Marrow doesn’t collapse in shock; IO = IV-speed access when no vein","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"special-infusion-routes:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about running a subcutaneous line?","answer":"25–27 g; pinch ~2 in; Check q4h (hospital), twice daily (home); Irritation → remove; rotate weekly","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Immune defense: innate, adaptive, active and passive","cards":[{"title":"Why inflammation looks the way it does","text":"Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm · Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain · IL-1, TNF → fever"},{"title":"History","text":"Infections, vaccines/boosters · Immunosuppressing drugs · Allergies, wounds, exposures"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"Risk for infection; sepsis · Pain, lost function · Incomplete vaccination"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"IgM positive = “now”; IgG alone = “before.”","task":"A client’s blood test shows IgM antibodies against a virus. What does the nurse understand this to mean?","cue":"A newly sensitized B cell makes IgM first; memory cells later make IgG on re-exposure.","rule":"IgM positive = “now”; IgG alone = “before.”","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"immune-defense","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Immune defense: innate, adaptive, active and passive","notes":["16-overview","16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","16-antibody-mediated-immunity","16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","16-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why inflammation looks the way it does: Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm; Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain; IL-1, TNF → fever","History: Infections, vaccines/boosters; Immunosuppressing drugs; Allergies, wounds, exposures","Priority problems: Risk for infection; sepsis; Pain, lost function; Incomplete vaccination","Why the second exposure is different: First time: slow, IgM, illness; Memory cells → fast, huge IgG response; Vaccines create memory; boosters refresh it"],"terms":["immunity","antibody","lymphocyte"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"16-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"16-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Innate: barriers, inflammation, complement, NK — fast, nonspecific","Adaptive: B cells → antibodies; T cells organize/kill; memory","Depends on self vs non-self (HLAs)","Read the WBC differential; older adults may have no fever, no ↑WBC"]},{"note":"16-overview","title":"Why inflammation looks the way it does","lines":["Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm","Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain","IL-1, TNF → fever"]},{"note":"16-overview","title":"The neutrophil supply line","lines":["12–14 days to make; 12–18 hr to live","> 100 billion/day; G-CSF speeds it","Left shift = defense losing ground"]},{"note":"16-overview","title":"Why the second exposure is different","lines":["First time: slow, IgM, illness","Memory cells → fast, huge IgG response","Vaccines create memory; boosters refresh it"]},{"note":"16-overview","title":"Why tissue type matters","lines":["HLAs: match only an identical twin","Graft = non-self → T and NK cells attack","Match + immunosuppress → infection risk"]},{"note":"16-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Balance of attack vs restraint","Too little → infection, cancer; too much → allergy, autoimmunity","Drugs push the balance"]}]},{"id":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"How immunity is gained: innate and adaptive, active and passive","blocks":[{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Innate (general) immunity","lines":["Barriers: skin, cornea, mucosa","Inflammation, complement, NK cells","Fast, same every time, no memory"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"The microbiome","lines":["Normal flora support immunity","Harmless where they belong"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Adaptive (acquired) immunity","lines":["AMI (B cells, antibodies) + CMI (T cells)","Specific, with memory","Needs innate immunity to start"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Immunocompetence and infection","lines":["Immunocompetent = all parts working","Weakened by disease, injury, treatment","Excess → allergy, autoimmunity (Ch 17)"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Why","lines":["Innate buys time; adaptive wins and remembers"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Natural active","lines":["Illness → own antibodies","Strongest, longest"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Artificial active (vaccine-induced)","lines":["Vaccine → own antibodies","Years; needs boosters"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Natural passive","lines":["Placenta, breast milk","Immediate, temporary"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Artificial passive","lines":["Immune globulin, antitoxin, convalescent plasma","Immediate, short-lived, no memory"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Putting them together: a tetanus-prone wound","lines":["Now: antitoxin (passive)","Later: tetanus toxoid (active)"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Convalescent plasma","lines":["Best for severe/high-risk COVID-19","Within 72 hours of symptoms"]},{"note":"16-how-immunity-is-gained-innate-and-adapti","title":"Why#2","lines":["Active: slow, lasting (memory)","Passive: instant, borrowed, cleared","Passive bridges until active develops"]}]},{"id":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Antibody-mediated immunity","blocks":[{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Where B cells mature","lines":["Marrow → spleen, nodes, tonsils, gut"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Exposure and recognition","lines":["Enough antigen to overwhelm innate defense","Macrophage presents; helper T brings it to B cell"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Sensitization","lines":["Sensitized to one antigen, for life","→ plasma cells (300 antibodies/sec)","→ memory cells"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Antibodies travel","lines":["Free in blood (humoral)","Transferable → passive immunity"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Memory (sustained immunity)","lines":["Memory cells → fast, large response","Usually no repeat illness"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Why","lines":["Macrophage shows, helper T organizes, B cell makes","Lose helper T → antibodies fail too"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Antibody shape","lines":["Y: arms (Fab) bind antigen","Stem (Fc) binds white cells"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Five actions","lines":["Agglutination, lysis","Complement fixation, precipitation","Inactivation (covers the active site)"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"IgM and IgG","lines":["IgM: first, 10 binding sites","IgG: re-exposure, most abundant, long-term"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"IgA, IgE, IgD","lines":["IgA: mucosal (airway, gut, GU)","IgE: allergy, parasites","IgD: B-cell receptor"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Immunoglobulins = gamma globulins","lines":["Antibodies = immunoglobulins = gamma globulins"]},{"note":"16-antibody-mediated-immunity","title":"Why#2","lines":["Antibodies mark and gather","Complement + phagocytes finish the job"]}]},{"id":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Cell-mediated immunity and cytokines","blocks":[{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Where and how","lines":["Mature in the thymus, nodes","Clones for millions of antigens","Named by CD markers"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Helper T cells (CD4+)","lines":["CD4+ = organizers","Cytokines boost all immunity"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Regulatory T cells (Tregs)","lines":["Restrain immunity (IL-10)","Prevent self-attack","Fail → autoimmunity"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Cytotoxic T cells","lines":["Kill virus-infected self cells","Attack grafts","CTLA-4 protects healthy self"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Natural killer cells (CD16+)","lines":["No sensitization needed","Best vs cancer, virus-infected cells"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"What CMI protects against","lines":["Cancer and intracellular infections","Regulates AMI and inflammation"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Why","lines":["Antibodies can’t reach inside cells","Weak CMI → viruses, fungi, cancers"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"What they are","lines":["Hormone-like messengers","Need a receptor on the responder","ILs, IFNs, CSFs, TNF, TGF"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Proinflammatory","lines":["IL-1 → fever","IL-2 → T, B, NK","IL-5 → eosinophils","IL-6, IL-17, IL-23"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Inhibitory","lines":["IL-10 → suppresses, tolerance","TNF: fever, RA damage, sepsis, rejection"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Growth factors","lines":["G-CSF → neutrophils","Erythropoietin → RBCs","Thrombopoietin → platelets"]},{"note":"16-cell-mediated-immunity-and-cytokines","title":"Why#2","lines":["Cytokines = messages","Block them (TNF blockers) or give them (G-CSF)"]}]},{"id":"16-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"16-adpie","title":"¶ For immunity the nurse asks: are the","lines":["Defenses intact? history, drugs, age, vaccines","Inflammation/infection? signs, temp, differential, ANC","Who won’t show it? older adults, immunosuppressed"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Infections, vaccines/boosters","Immunosuppressing drugs","Allergies, wounds, exposures"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Physical and labs","lines":["Cardinal signs, pus, temperature","Differential, ANC, bands, eosinophils","Older adults: mental status, function"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Differential + change from baseline > thermometer"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Risk for infection; sepsis","Pain, lost function","Incomplete vaccination"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["No infection or early detection","Differential normalizing; wounds heal","Vaccines current; knows signs"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Prevent","lines":["Hand hygiene, asepsis, skin care","Vaccines + boosters","Tetanus wound: antitoxin + vaccine"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Detect and respond","lines":["Pus: temperature, photograph, notify","Report left shift, low ANC, sepsis","Older adult: confusion = possible infection"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Teach and handle drugs safely","lines":["Boosters; signs of infection","Inflammation ≠ always infection","Safe handling of immunosuppressants"]},{"note":"16-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Vitals at baseline; inflammation subsiding","Fewer bands, ANC up","Wound healing; vaccines current"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"immune-defense:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client’s blood test shows IgM antibodies against a virus. What does the nurse understand this to mean?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"IgM positive = “now”; IgG alone = “before.”","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A new or recent infection","correct":true,"why":"Correct. IgM is the first antibody made after a new sensitization."},{"id":"o1","text":"Immunity from a vaccine given years ago","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Old immunity shows as IgG."},{"id":"o2","text":"An allergic reaction to the virus","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Allergy involves IgE."},{"id":"o3","text":"Protection passed from the client’s mother","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Maternal antibodies are IgG and fade in infancy."}]},{"id":"immune-defense:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why inflammation looks the way it does?","answer":"Histamine: dilate arterioles, pool blood → red, warm; Kinins: leaky capillaries → swelling, pain; IL-1, TNF → fever","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"immune-defense:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about history?","answer":"Infections, vaccines/boosters; Immunosuppressing drugs; Allergies, wounds, exposures","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Inflammation and white cells: explain the response","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self"},{"title":"Five cardinal signs","text":"Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function · Same for any cause; scale = intensity"},{"title":"Toll-like receptors","text":"Recognize microbes · and stressed self cells"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Eosinophilia (normal 1–4%, 50–400/mm³): allergic rhinitis, asthma, atopic dermatitis, chronic hives, parasites, transplant rejection.","task":"A client has seasonal allergic rhinitis (hay fever). Which change in the WBC differential does the nurse expect?","cue":"Allergic reactions draw eosinophils, which prolong later inflammation and can trigger asthma in allergic asthma.","rule":"Eosinophilia (normal 1–4%, 50–400/mm³): allergic rhinitis, asthma, atopic dermatitis, chronic hives, parasites, transplant rejection.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"inflammation-cells","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Inflammation and white cells: explain the response","notes":["16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self","Five cardinal signs: Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function; Same for any cause; scale = intensity","Toll-like receptors: Recognize microbes; and stressed self cells","Why#2: Specialized cells → read the differential, not just the total"],"terms":["neutrophil","eosinophil","phagocytosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Self vs non-self, immune cells and the WBC count","blocks":[{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"HLAs — the “product code”","lines":["On most cells; = tissue type","Match only an identical twin","Another person’s HLAs = antigens"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Self-tolerance","lines":["Match = self; mismatch = attack","Non-self: infected, cancer, foreign cells, microbes","Tregs keep tolerance"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Toll-like receptors","lines":["Recognize microbes","and stressed self cells"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Why","lines":["Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Stem cells and growth factors","lines":["Marrow stem cells → any blood cell","Growth factors decide (erythropoietin → RBCs)"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Granulocytes and agranulocytes","lines":["Granulocytes: neutrophils, basophils, eosinophils (+ mast cells)","Agranulocytes: monocytes, lymphocytes"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Who does what","lines":["Inflammation: neutrophils, macrophages, eos, basos","AMI: B, plasma, memory cells","CMI: helper, cytotoxic, regulatory T, NK"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"What leukocytes do","lines":["Recognize, destroy, make antibodies","Complement, tolerance, cytokines"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Why#2","lines":["Specialized cells → read the differential, not just the total"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Normal values","lines":["Total 5000–10,000","Segs 55–70%, bands ~5%, lymphs 20–40%","Monos 2–8%, eos 1–4%, basos 0.5–1%"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Absolute neutrophil count","lines":["ANC = mature neutrophils","Low ANC → infection risk"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Left shift (bandemia)","lines":["More bands than usual","Marrow can’t keep up → sepsis risk","Bands can’t phagocytize"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Eosinophils up","lines":["↑ with allergy, asthma, parasites, rejection","Hay fever → ↑ eosinophils"]},{"note":"16-self-vs-non-self-immune-cells-and-the-wb","title":"Why#3","lines":["Normal total can hide abnormal parts","Check each line against its range"]}]},{"id":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Inflammation: cells, phagocytosis and the three stages","blocks":[{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Neutrophils","lines":["55–70%; segs (mature) vs bands","Live 12–18 hr; > 100 billion/day","One phagocytosis each"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Macrophages","lines":["Monocytes → macrophages in tissue","Many phagocytoses; present antigens","Secrete cytokines"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Basophils and mast cells","lines":["Carry IgE; release histamine, kinins, heparin","Pooling + capillary leak","Allergic inflammation"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Eosinophils","lines":["Parasites; prolong inflammation","↑ in allergy, asthma"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Complement","lines":["20 proteins, cascade","Membrane attack complex → easier phagocytosis"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Why","lines":["Mast cells/basophils open vessels","Neutrophils kill; macrophages clean + call AMI/CMI"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Seven steps of phagocytosis","lines":["Exposure → attraction → adherence (opsonins)","Recognition → ingestion","Phagosome → degradation"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Stage I — vascular","lines":["Histamine, kinins → red, warm, swollen, painful","24–72 hr; macrophages first","CSF → neutrophils in hours"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Stage II — cellular exudate","lines":["Neutrophilia, pus","AA → COX → mediators (NSAIDs block)","Prolonged → bands, sepsis risk"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Stage III — repair","lines":["Replace (cells divide) or scar","Scar = lost function (MI example)"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Five cardinal signs","lines":["Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function","Same for any cause; scale = intensity"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Inflammation without infection","lines":["Sprain, MI, blister, allergy = no infection","No lasting immunity from inflammation"]},{"note":"16-inflammation-cells-phagocytosis-and-the","title":"Why#2","lines":["Signs say that and where","History, cultures, differential say what"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"inflammation-cells:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client has seasonal allergic rhinitis (hay fever). Which change in the WBC differential does the nurse expect?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Eosinophilia (normal 1–4%, 50–400/mm³): allergic rhinitis, asthma, atopic dermatitis, chronic hives, parasites, transplant rejection.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"An increase in eosinophils","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Eosinophils rise with allergic reactions."},{"id":"o1","text":"An increase in bands","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Bands rise when infection outpaces the marrow."},{"id":"o2","text":"An increase in basophils to 10%","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Basophils release histamine, but the differential change expected is in eosinophils."},{"id":"o3","text":"A decrease in lymphocytes","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not a feature of allergy."}]},{"id":"inflammation-cells:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Same code read two ways: spare self, attack non-self","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"inflammation-cells:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about five cardinal signs?","answer":"Warmth, redness, swelling, pain, ↓function; Same for any cause; scale = intensity","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Aging immunity: subtle infection and weaker test responses","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake · Look at function, not just temperature"},{"title":"Inflammation changes","text":"Normal count, weak neutrophils · No ↑WBC, often no fever · Missed until sepsis"},{"title":"Antibody changes","text":"New antigens: flu, pneumococcal, shingles vaccines · Old immunity fades: tetanus, pertussis boosters"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"In older adults, look for confusion, falls, poor intake or a vague decline — not just fever or a high white count.","task":"Which finding is most likely to be the first sign of a serious infection in an 84-year-old client?","cue":"With age, neutrophils work less well and the body is slower to sense invaders, so the response that produces classic signs is muted; minor infections can be missed until the person is septic.","rule":"In older adults, look for confusion, falls, poor intake or a vague decline — not just fever or a high white count.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"immune-aging","ch":"16","chapters":["16"],"title":"Aging immunity: subtle infection and weaker test responses","notes":["16-immunity-in-older-adults"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 16; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake; Look at function, not just temperature","Inflammation changes: Normal count, weak neutrophils; No ↑WBC, often no fever; Missed until sepsis","Antibody changes: New antigens: flu, pneumococcal, shingles vaccines; Old immunity fades: tetanus, pertussis boosters"],"terms":["immunosenescence","immunosuppression"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Immunity in older adults","blocks":[{"note":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Peak and decline","lines":["Best in the 20s–30s","Both innate and adaptive decline"]},{"note":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Inflammation changes","lines":["Normal count, weak neutrophils","No ↑WBC, often no fever","Missed until sepsis"]},{"note":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Antibody changes","lines":["New antigens: flu, pneumococcal, shingles vaccines","Old immunity fades: tetanus, pertussis boosters"]},{"note":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Cell-mediated changes","lines":["Weaker T-cell function","False-negative TB skin tests","More skin, lung, urinary infections; autoantibodies"]},{"note":"16-immunity-in-older-adults","title":"Why","lines":["First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake","Look at function, not just temperature"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"immune-aging:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"First sign may be confusion, falls, poor intake; Look at function, not just temperature","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"immune-aging:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about inflammation changes?","answer":"Normal count, weak neutrophils; No ↑WBC, often no fever; Missed until sepsis","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema: airway changes the priority","cards":[{"title":"Why epinephrine comes first","text":"Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough · Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways · Steroids: slow; prevent a late return"},{"title":"Early and late signs","text":"Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives · Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness · ↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure"},{"title":"Treatment","text":"O₂, epinephrine + corticosteroids · Stridor / can’t swallow → intubate · Tracheostomy below the swelling; icatibant, ecallantide if refractory"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Angioedema: ask about drugs (especially for BP) and defer the rest of the history until treatment is under way.","task":"A client arrives with sudden swelling of the lips and tongue. After ensuring the airway, which question is most important for the nurse to ask?","cue":"ACE inhibitors stop the breakdown of bradykinin, which accumulates and causes deep swelling; patients may not connect it to a long-term drug.","rule":"Angioedema: ask about drugs (especially for BP) and defer the rest of the history until treatment is under way.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"allergy-airway","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Allergy, anaphylaxis and angioedema: airway changes the priority","notes":["17-overview","17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","17-angioedema","17-anaphylaxis","17-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why epinephrine comes first: Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough; Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways; Steroids: slow; prevent a late return","Early and late signs: Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives; Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness; ↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure","Treatment: O₂, epinephrine + corticosteroids; Stridor / can’t swallow → intubate; Tracheostomy below the swelling; icatibant, ecallantide if refractory","Why ACE inhibitors cause swelling: ACE breaks down bradykinin; Block ACE → bradykinin builds up; Late onset; histamine drugs may fail"],"terms":["anaphylaxis","angioedema","epinephrine"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"17-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"17-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Too much: hypersensitivity types I–IV; anaphylaxis → IM epinephrine first","Misdirected: autoimmunity (SLE), transplant rejection","Too little: HIV destroys CD4+ cells → opportunistic infections","Also Lyme disease: tick-borne, bull’s-eye rash"]},{"note":"17-overview","title":"Why epinephrine comes first","lines":["Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough","Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways","Steroids: slow; prevent a late return"]},{"note":"17-overview","title":"Why ACE inhibitors cause swelling","lines":["ACE breaks down bradykinin","Block ACE → bradykinin builds up","Late onset; histamine drugs may fail"]},{"note":"17-overview","title":"Why lupus targets kidneys and vessels","lines":["Complexes circulate and lodge","Glomeruli → proteinuria","Vessel walls → vasculitis, ↓ perfusion"]},{"note":"17-overview","title":"Why the CD4+ count predicts infections","lines":["CD4+ cells direct all immunity","HIV disables them","< 200 → opportunistic infections"]},{"note":"17-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Too much → airway","Inward → organs","Out → infection"]}]},{"id":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Hypersensitivity: the four types","blocks":[{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Allergy and allergens","lines":["Overreaction in susceptible people","Usually after a prior exposure","Inhaled, ingested, injected, touched"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"The tendency is inherited, not the allergy","lines":["Inherit the tendency","Not the specific allergen"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"How type I works","lines":["1st exposure → IgE on mast cells (sensitized)","Re-exposure → histamine (primary phase)","Leukotrienes, bradykinin, prostaglandins (secondary phase)"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Local or systemic","lines":["Same mechanism, local or systemic","Anaphylaxis = emergency"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Why","lines":["First exposure sets the trap (IgE)","Re-exposure springs it","Past tolerance doesn’t guarantee safety"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Type I — immediate (IgE)","lines":["IgE, histamine; < 1 hour","Hives, wheeze, angioedema, anaphylaxis","Venom, latex, peanuts, shellfish, drugs"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Type II — cytotoxic (IgG)","lines":["IgG destroys tagged self cells; minutes–hours","Wrong-blood transfusion, hemolytic anemia, ITP","Stop the blood/drug; watch the kidneys"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Type III — immune complex","lines":["Complexes lodge in vessels; 4–10 days","RA, SLE, serum sickness","Most autoimmune disease"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Type IV — delayed (T cells)","lines":["T cells; 12–72 hours","Contact dermatitis (poison ivy, metals)","Steroids help; antihistamines don’t"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Why#2","lines":["< 1 hr = I; cells destroyed = II","Days, kidneys/joints = III; 12–72 hr at the contact site = IV"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Skin tests","lines":["Prick: environmental, food","Intradermal: venom, penicillin","Close supervision"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Blood tests","lines":["Confirm; less specific","False positives possible"]},{"note":"17-hypersensitivity-the-four-types","title":"Why#3","lines":["Testing = controlled exposure → be ready to treat"]}]},{"id":"17-angioedema","title":"Angioedema","blocks":[{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Deep swelling","lines":["Deep, all skin layers; bradykinin","Lips, face, tongue, larynx, neck","Intestinal: cramping pain, vomiting"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"ACE inhibitors (and NSAIDs)","lines":["ACE inhibitors most common; NSAIDs, aspirin","ACE breaks down bradykinin → it accumulates","Highest risk in the first 24 hr, but even years later"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Health equity","lines":["Black patients: 3–5× the risk","Watch after the first dose; teach 911"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Why","lines":["Bradykinin, not histamine → slow and drug-resistant","Ask about BP drugs, even long-term ones"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Airway first","lines":["Lip swelling or a throat itch can progress fast","Ask about BP drugs; defer the full history"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"What you see","lines":["Deep, firm swelling; hard to speak or drink","Can’t swallow, lump in the throat, stridor = larynx","Diagnosed by the signs; no lab delay"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Treatment","lines":["O₂, epinephrine + corticosteroids","Stridor / can’t swallow → intubate","Tracheostomy below the swelling; icatibant, ecallantide if refractory"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Don’t stop too soon","lines":["Treat for hours after it resolves","Keep the IV; watch 2–6 hours","Teach: avoid the cause; return at the first sign"]},{"note":"17-angioedema","title":"Why#2","lines":["Lips → larynx: act early","Watch for a rebound (the drug outlasts the treatment)"]}]},{"id":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Anaphylaxis","blocks":[{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"What it is","lines":["Seconds–minutes: vasodilation, ↓ cardiac output, bronchoconstriction","Deaths: delayed epinephrine","Hospital: drugs, contrast; community: food, stings"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Prevention","lines":["Avoid allergens; medical alert bracelet","Kit: epinephrine, H1, H2, albuterol","Carry two autoinjectors"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Caring for the autoinjector (Box 17.1)","lines":["Trainer; always carry two","Replace if expired, discolored or the release is loose","Use at the first symptom, then 911"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Using it (Box 17.2)","lines":["Outer thigh, 90°, through clothes","Click → hold 3 s, massage 10 s","Seek care even if better"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Beyond the book","lines":["Nasal spray approved 2024","Exam: the autoinjector"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Why","lines":["Epinephrine must be at hand and early","Carry two; use at the first sign"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Flag the allergy (Action Alert)","lines":["Allergens prominent; ask before every drug","Penicillin ↔ cephalosporins","Must give it? IV, airway kit, premedicate"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"The two criteria","lines":["Skin/mucosa + breathing, BP or GI","Or after an allergen: ↓ BP, bronchospasm, larynx; no skin signs needed","↓ BP = SBP 30% drop"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Early and late signs","lines":["Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives","Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness","↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Why#2","lines":["Skin signs not required","Allergen + ↓ BP or airway = treat"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Call and assess","lines":["Rapid Response Team","Airway, breathing, SpO₂","Stop the suspect infusion"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Epinephrine IM first","lines":["IM epinephrine first; no absolute contraindication","Repeat every 5–15 min","After 3 doses → IV infusion"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Position, oxygen, fluids","lines":["Flat, legs up","Nonrebreather 90–100% before blood gases","Rapid normal saline; change the tubing if an IV drug caused it"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Add-on drugs","lines":["Albuterol; H1 + H2 for itching and hives","Steroids are slow; they prevent a late recurrence","Vasopressors, glucagon; stay"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Monitoring","lines":["SpO₂, breath sounds, stridor","Suction; ABGs","Severe → ICU"]},{"note":"17-anaphylaxis","title":"Why#3","lines":["Antihistamines block one mediator","Epinephrine reverses vessels, heart, airways","Epinephrine first; the rest are add-ons"]}]},{"id":"17-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"17-adpie","title":"¶ For allergy and immunity, the nurse asks","lines":["Airway safe? allergen, swelling, stridor, ↓ BP","Self-attack? lupus flare, organs, rejection","Immunity failing? CD4+, infections, transmission"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Allergies + reactions; ACE inhibitors","Lupus: 5-year history, sun, fevers","HIV: risks, 5 Ps, adherence"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Physical and labs","lines":["Stridor, SpO₂, BP; skin","CBC, ANA, creatinine, urine protein","CD4+, viral load; rejection signs"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Airway, BP · urine, creatinine · CD4+, infections"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Airway, shock","SLE: pain, fatigue, organ failure","HIV: transmission, infection; transplant: rejection"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Airway and BP maintained","SLE: acceptable pain, longer remissions","HIV: no transmission, no infection; graft working"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Rescue","lines":["Anaphylaxis: RRT, IM epinephrine, legs up, O₂, saline","Angioedema: intubate for stridor; watch 2–6 hr"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Protect and treat","lines":["Lupus: steroid taper, eye exams, sun","HIV: adherence, TMP-SMX, precautions, nutrition","Transplant: lifelong drugs, report flulike symptoms"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Teach and prevent","lines":["Two autoinjectors; thigh, then 911","Safer sex, PrEP/PEP/TasP, testing","Ticks; Standard Precautions"]},{"note":"17-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Breathing, BP normal; allergen plan","Lupus: remissions, eye exams","HIV: viral load ↓, CD4+ ↑; graft working"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"allergy-airway:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client arrives with sudden swelling of the lips and tongue. After ensuring the airway, which question is most important for the nurse to ask?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Angioedema: ask about drugs (especially for BP) and defer the rest of the history until treatment is under way.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“What medicines do you take for blood pressure?”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. ACE inhibitors are the most common cause of angioedema — even after years of use."},{"id":"o1","text":"“Has anyone in your family had swelling like this?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Relevant later, but the drug history comes first."},{"id":"o2","text":"“When did you last eat?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not the priority."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Do you have any pets?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not the likely cause."}]},{"id":"allergy-airway:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why epinephrine comes first?","answer":"Many mediators → an antihistamine isn’t enough; Epinephrine: vessels, heart, airways; Steroids: slow; prevent a late return","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"allergy-airway:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about early and late signs?","answer":"Early: doom, cramping, itching, hives; Then wheeze, dyspnea, stridor, hoarseness; ↓ BP, rapid weak pulse → failure","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Lupus: immune injury, organ monitoring and medication safety","cards":[{"title":"Who and why","text":"3× as many women · Genes (HLA types) + triggers · Ask the family history"},{"title":"History","text":"Past 5 years; pain without injury · Family history; unexplained fevers · Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"Pain, fatigue, organ failure"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine: eye exam with visual fields before starting and every 6 months; report blurred vision.","task":"A client with SLE has taken hydroxychloroquine for a year. Which occasional symptom should the nurse investigate first?","cue":"Antimalarials accumulate in retinal cells and can cause retinopathy that permanently destroys central vision.","rule":"Hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine: eye exam with visual fields before starting and every 6 months; report blurred vision.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"lupus","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Lupus: immune injury, organ monitoring and medication safety","notes":["17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Who and why: 3× as many women; Genes (HLA types) + triggers; Ask the family history","History: Past 5 years; pain without injury; Family history; unexplained fevers; Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide","Priority problems: Pain, fatigue, organ failure","Why: Genes load, triggers fire; Ask about relatives and recent triggers"],"terms":["autoimmunity","hydroxychloroquine","prednisone"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Autoimmunity and lupus (SLE): recognizing it","blocks":[{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"What goes wrong","lines":["Lost self-tolerance","Chronic, progressive, incurable; remissions","~4% of people"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Who and why","lines":["3× as many women","Genes (HLA types) + triggers","Ask the family history"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"One target or many","lines":["Selective: myasthenia gravis","Widespread: SLE, RA"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Common examples (Box 17.5)","lines":["MS, MG, Guillain-Barré","Graves, Hashimoto, type 1 DM","RA, SLE, psoriasis, Crohn’s, UC, celiac"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Why","lines":["Genes load, triggers fire","Ask about relatives and recent triggers"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Immune complexes everywhere","lines":["Autoantibodies vs nuclear proteins","Type III complexes; vasculitis","Deaths: kidney and heart; joint pain in nearly all"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Flares and remission","lines":["Remission vs flare","Damage happens during high activity"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Triggers and who gets it","lines":["Triggers: viruses, drugs, estrogen, UV/sun","Women of childbearing age; women of color","Slow, vague: ~54 months to diagnosis"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Skin-only lupus","lines":["Acute, subacute, chronic cutaneous (discoid)"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Why#2","lines":["Complexes in the blood → anywhere","Kidneys filter → they catch complexes"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"History","lines":["Past 5 years; pain without injury","Family history; unexplained fevers","Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Skin","lines":["Butterfly rash after sun","Discoid lesions scar; photosensitivity","Alopecia, mouth ulcers"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Fever, fatigue, joints and muscles","lines":["Low-grade fever, chronic fatigue","Bilateral arthritis, no deformity","Osteonecrosis after ≥ 5 yr of steroids"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Kidneys and heart","lines":["Foamy urine = protein (nephritis)","Pericarditis, thickened valves","Raynaud, anemia, leukopenia"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Anxiety, depression, withdrawal","Myth: lupus is fatal; with treatment, a normal life span"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Why#3","lines":["No single sign: clusters","Rash + joints + fever + urine protein"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"No single test","lines":["Several tests + the findings","ANA positive = autoantibodies; not used alone"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Blood counts","lines":["Anemia, leukopenia","Low platelets → immune thrombocytopenia","↑ monocytes, ↑ eosinophils"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Kidneys","lines":["↑ Creatinine = kidney damage","Urine RBCs ≤ 2; UPCR < 0.15"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Other tests","lines":["ESR, CRP, C3/C4, anti-dsDNA","Chest pain → ECG; dyspnea → PE workup","CT, MRI by symptoms"]},{"note":"17-autoimmunity-and-lupus-sle-recognizing-i","title":"Why#4","lines":["ANA shows that autoantibodies exist","Lupus = ANA + typical damage"]}]},{"id":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Caring for lupus: pain, fatigue, drugs and organs","blocks":[{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Pain, fatigue, organ failure"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Pain","lines":["Acetaminophen (not anti-inflammatory) or NSAIDs","Flare: ↑ steroids, then taper exactly","Moist heat, rest, low-impact activity"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Fatigue","lines":["Prioritize, delegate, plan rest","Vaccines OK except live; no nicotine","Exercise 2 × 15–30 min, rest after"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Why","lines":["One inflammation drives pain, fatigue, organ damage","Control activity + pace + avoid sun"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Corticosteroids","lines":["Prednisone = cornerstone","Moon face, ulcers, infection, fragile skin","Never stop abruptly; can’t take it → call"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Antimalarials","lines":["Hydroxychloroquine → remission","Risk: retinopathy (central vision)","Eye exam before + every 6 months; report blurred vision"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"NSAIDs and aspirin","lines":["NSAIDs with food; kidney labs","Low-dose aspirin against clots"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Immunosuppressives","lines":["Cyclophosphamide, azathioprine, mycophenolate, methotrexate","Infection risk","Report a fever > 100°F (37.8°C)"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Monoclonal antibodies","lines":["Belimumab: IV or subcut","Anifrolumab: IV; interferon","Watch for anaphylaxis up to 2 hr after"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Why#2","lines":["Each drug has one danger to teach","Steroids: don’t stop; hydroxychloroquine: eyes; immunosuppressants: infection"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Avoid UV (Action Alert)","lines":["UV worsens everything","Long sleeves, wide hat, SPF ≥ 30","Early morning or evening; shade"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Bones and organs","lines":["Bone monitoring","Regular visits: kidneys, heart","No nicotine; avoid stress"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Home and self-management","lines":["Flares: one-floor living","Know the drugs, infection signs, when to call","Lupus Foundation"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Adherence; acceptable pain","Energy; longer remissions"]},{"note":"17-caring-for-lupus-pain-fatigue-drugs-and","title":"Why#3","lines":["Sun → systemic flare","Sun protection = disease control"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"lupus:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client with SLE has taken hydroxychloroquine for a year. Which occasional symptom should the nurse investigate first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Hydroxychloroquine or chloroquine: eye exam with visual fields before starting and every 6 months; report blurred vision.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Blurred vision","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Retinal toxicity can lead to irreversible loss of central vision."},{"id":"o1","text":"Headache","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Less specific to this drug’s major risk."},{"id":"o2","text":"Loss of appetite","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Less serious."},{"id":"o3","text":"Diarrhea","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Less serious."}]},{"id":"lupus:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about who and why?","answer":"3× as many women; Genes (HLA types) + triggers; Ask the family history","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"lupus:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about history?","answer":"Past 5 years; pain without injury; Family history; unexplained fevers; Drug-induced: hydralazine, isoniazid, procainamide","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"HIV: suppress the virus, prevent transmission and protect immunity","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS · The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI"},{"title":"Brain and nerves","text":"Crypto: headache, stiff neck · Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report · ↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now"},{"title":"TasP — treatment as prevention","text":"HIV-positive; keep the viral load undetectable · Greatly reduces sexual transmission · Beyond the book: U = U"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Stage I: fever, sore throat, rash, night sweats, chills, headache, muscle aches; very high viral load — highly contagious.","task":"A client says, “I’ve just been diagnosed with stage I HIV infection.” Which assessment finding does the nurse expect?","cue":"In the first weeks, the virus multiplies rapidly and the immune system responds with a generalized viral illness that soon passes.","rule":"Stage I: fever, sore throat, rash, night sweats, chills, headache, muscle aches; very high viral load — highly contagious.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"hiv","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"HIV: suppress the virus, prevent transmission and protect immunity","notes":["17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","17-hiv-history-and-testing","17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS; The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI","Brain and nerves: Crypto: headache, stiff neck; Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report; ↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now","TasP — treatment as prevention: HIV-positive; keep the viral load undetectable; Greatly reduces sexual transmission; Beyond the book: U = U","Why#2: It kills the organizers (CD4+ cells); All immunity weakens → harmless organisms turn deadly"],"terms":["HIV","antiretroviral","opportunistic infection"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"HIV: the virus, the stages and how it spreads","blocks":[{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Stage I — acute infection","lines":["Within 4 weeks: fever, sore throat, rash, aches","It passes → easily missed","High viral load → very contagious"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Stage II — chronic infection","lines":["Diagnosed after seroconversion","Treated = chronic disease","Viral load guides care"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Stage III — AIDS","lines":["CD4+ < 200 or an AIDS-defining condition","Leukopenia, lymphocytopenia","The diagnosis stays stage III"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Opportunistic infections (Box 17.8)","lines":["Normal flora overgrow (Candida)","PCP, TB, MAC, crypto, CMV, toxo","Cancers: Kaposi, lymphoma, cervical"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Older adults","lines":["Any age: take the history","Lower immunity → infection more likely"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Why","lines":["All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS","The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"A retrovirus","lines":["Genes in RNA; reverse transcriptase","Docks on CD4+ T cells","The cell becomes a virus factory"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"The seven-step life cycle","lines":["Binding → fusion → reverse transcription","Integration (integrase) → replication","Assembly → budding (protease)"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Types and genetics","lines":["HIV-1 common, virulent; HIV-2 in West Africa","Nonprogressors can still transmit"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"How common","lines":["1.2 million in the US; 13% unaware","Out of proportion: Black, Hispanic, MSM, transgender adults"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Why#2","lines":["It kills the organizers (CD4+ cells)","All immunity weakens → harmless organisms turn deadly"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Fluids and routes","lines":["Highest: blood, semen, vaginal fluid, breast milk","Sexual, parenteral, perinatal"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"What doesn’t spread it","lines":["Not: casual contact, utensils, toilets","Not: insects","Splash on intact skin: low risk; still get checked"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Sexual risk","lines":["Complete protection: abstinence or mutual monogamy","Highest: receptive anal intercourse","Women infected more easily"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Blood and donation","lines":["Blood is screened; risk 1 in 1.5–2 million","Window period = a tiny risk","Book: 3-month deferrals; 2023: the same questions for all"]},{"note":"17-hiv-the-virus-the-stages-and-how-it-spre","title":"Why#3","lines":["Needs high-virus fluid + a way in","Casual contact has neither"]}]},{"id":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Preventing HIV: safer sex, syringes, PrEP, PEP and TasP","blocks":[{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Teach everyone","lines":["Teach everyone","New condom for every act; water-based lubricant","Dental dam; gloves, changed between sites"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Syringe services programs","lines":["Free sterile needles, no prescription","↓ HIV and hepatitis C by ~50%","Also PrEP, naloxone"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Cleaning a syringe (Box 17.9)","lines":["Water → bleach → fresh water","Each: fill, shake 30 s, empty","Nine steps, in order"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Why","lines":["Block the routes","Practical, no judgment, for everyone"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"PrEP — before exposure","lines":["HIV-negative, before exposure","Cuts the sexual risk by ~99%","Tenofovir combinations: not with hepatitis B (black box)"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"PrEP teaching (Drug Alert)","lines":["Still use safer sex","Infected on PrEP → resistance","HIV test every 3 months"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"PEP — after exposure","lines":["Emergencies only; nPEP vs occupational PEP","Within 72 hr (ideally 2 hr); 28 days","Needlestick: wash, then call employee health"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"TasP — treatment as prevention","lines":["HIV-positive; keep the viral load undetectable","Greatly reduces sexual transmission","Beyond the book: U = U"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Why#2","lines":["PrEP: before (HIV-negative)","PEP: within 72 hr after (HIV-negative)","TasP: treat the HIV-positive partner"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Standard Precautions","lines":["Standard Precautions for all","Use the sharps safety feature, especially at disposal"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Protecting patients (Action Alert)","lines":["Gloves for mucosa, broken skin","Weeping sores → gloves or no direct care"]},{"note":"17-preventing-hiv-safer-sex-syringes-prep-p","title":"Why#3","lines":["Status often unknown → precautions for all"]}]},{"id":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"HIV: history and testing","blocks":[{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Who and how often","lines":["Test everyone 13–64 at least once","Monitor every 2–6 months"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"History","lines":["Private; a timeline of infections","Risks: unprotected sex, needles, transfusion","Also STIs, TB, hepatitis"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Sexual history","lines":["After rapport; confidential, with limits","Don’t assume; gender-neutral words","5 Ps: partners, practices, protection, past, pregnancy"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Physical and psychosocial","lines":["HIV-negative: flulike signs","Always check for opportunistic infections","Depression, suicide risk; proxy, advance directives"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Critical rescue","lines":["LOC, pupils, limb strength → report now","TB signs → Airborne + a rapid molecular assay"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Why","lines":["An honest history needs safety, no judgment","Neutral words are clinical tools"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"The window period","lines":["3-week window → false negative","Exposed in the past month → retest"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Screening tests","lines":["Rapid test (< 1 hr) recommended","Ag/Ab combination: 5–7 days earlier","Then the HIV-1/HIV-2 test → NAT"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Other ways to test","lines":["Home kits: have support present","Gum swab, urine if no blood draw"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Why#2","lines":["Antibodies take time → early negatives","Antigen tests close the gap"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Lymphocytes","lines":["WBC 5000–10,000; lymphocytes 20–40%","Stage III: WBC < 4000, lymphocytes < 1500"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"CD4+ count and ratio","lines":["Normal 600–1500","< 200 → opportunistic infections likely","CD4:CD8 normally > 1; falls in HIV"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Viral load","lines":["HIV RNA = the best progress guide","Baseline ×2 (2–4 wk apart); then every 3–4 months","After a drug change: every 4–6 weeks; undetectable ≠ cured"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Other tests and genetics","lines":["Resistance testing (genotype)","HLA-B∗5701 before abacavir","Trofile (CCR5) before maraviroc"]},{"note":"17-hiv-history-and-testing","title":"Why#3","lines":["CD4+ = immunity left","Viral load = virus activity, drug effect"]}]},{"id":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"HIV drugs (cART), resistance and IRIS","blocks":[{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Principles","lines":["Controls, doesn’t kill","Combination of classes","Lifelong, costly → social work"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"NRTIs","lines":["All: lactic acidosis, fatty liver","Abacavir: fever or rash in the first 6 wk = hypersensitivity","Tenofovir: kidneys, bones; zidovudine: marrow"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"NNRTIs","lines":["Common: anemia","Efavirenz: teratogenic; nevirapine: liver (first 12 wk)","Etravirine: no St. John’s wort"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Protease inhibitors","lines":["PIs: glucose, lipids, fat redistribution","PR prolongation","Atazanavir: jaundice; darunavir = sulfa"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Entry, integrase and boosters","lines":["Enfuvirtide: subcut twice a day, rotate","Maraviroc: Trofile first; rash or jaundice → ER","Dolutegravir: liver, glucose; ritonavir: neuropathy"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Why","lines":["Classes block different steps","Combination → hard to escape or resist"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Take it exactly as prescribed (Drug Alert)","lines":["Missed doses → resistance","Missed one? Resume as soon as possible; no doubling","Long half-lives forgive a little"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Resistance is permanent","lines":["Resistant virus is stored forever","Transmissible to others","Lenacapavir for multidrug resistance"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Complementary therapies","lines":["Ask about herbs, supplements","Check with the pharmacist"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Why#2","lines":["Low levels → a chance to mutate","A surviving mutant never leaves"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"What happens","lines":["Recovering T cells overreact","Fever, night sweats, worse infection","1–12 weeks after starting"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Common culprits and care","lines":["TB, crypto, MAC, PCP, CMV","Short-term steroids + treat the infection","Keep cART going"]},{"note":"17-hiv-drugs-cart-resistance-and-iris","title":"Why#3","lines":["IRIS = immunity returning","Manage it; don’t stop cART"]}]},{"id":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Stage III (AIDS): infections, complications and care","blocks":[{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Transmission (all stages)","Infection and complications (stages II, III)"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Catch it early","lines":["Act on a low-grade fever (signs are muted)","Daily CBC, ANC; vital signs every 4 hr","Mouth every shift; lungs every 8 hr; perirectal skin"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"At home (Box 17.11)","lines":["Hand washing; avoid crowds; no gardening","Report > 100°F (37.8°C), cough, pus, mouth ulcers","No undercooked food; no litter box, no reptiles"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"In the hospital (Box 17.12)","lines":["Private room, healthy visitors, dedicated equipment","IV sites every 4 hr; gauze dressings daily","Culture suspicious areas; strict asepsis"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Why","lines":["Few WBCs → muted signs","Scheduled checks; small clues = big"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Gas exchange","lines":["TMP-SMX for PCP; watch K⁺, kidneys","Assess every 8 hr; head of bed up; O₂","Pace activities; cluster care"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Pain","lines":["Neuropathy; enlarged organs; tumors"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Nutrition","lines":["Wasting: loss of ≥ 10% of body weight","Thrush → fluconazole; soft, cool foods","High-protein, high-calorie; somatropin at bedtime"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Diarrhea and skin","lines":["Diarrhea: perineal care; report a pimple","Kaposi: purple-red lesions; shrinks with ART","Herpes, zoster: acyclovir, valacyclovir"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Why#2","lines":["Small problems → infection, wasting","Protect barriers and nutrition"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Lungs","lines":["PCP: dry cough, rapid breathing → O₂","TB: night sweats → Airborne, rapid assay","MAC: fever may last 2–4 weeks"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Brain and nerves","lines":["Crypto: headache, stiff neck","Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report","↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Mouth, gut and hormones","lines":["Candida: soft food; watch for aspiration","Crypto, Salmonella: fluids, electrolytes","Adrenal insufficiency; lipodystrophy"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Heart, kidneys, skin and cancers","lines":["Nephropathy: watch the GFR","Kaposi: track the lesions","HPV: Pap screening, vaccine"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Why#3","lines":["Opportunistic infections show up in the organ attacked","Assess every system"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Planning for home","lines":["Mostly home; the case manager for housing, money","Respite, hospice as needed","Personal health records and apps"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Infection control at home (Box 17.13)","lines":["¼ cup bleach : 1 gallon of water","A normal wash kills the virus; cold soak for stains","Sharps bottle: seal at 75%, “DO NOT RECYCLE”"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Adherent; safer sex","No transmission; no acute or opportunistic infections"]},{"note":"17-stage-iii-aids-infections-complications","title":"Why#4","lines":["Protects caregivers and the patient"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"hiv:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client says, “I’ve just been diagnosed with stage I HIV infection.” Which assessment finding does the nurse expect?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Stage I: fever, sore throat, rash, night sweats, chills, headache, muscle aches; very high viral load — highly contagious.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Sore throat with muscle aches","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Stage I is an acute flulike illness — fever, sore throat, rash, aches — within about 4 weeks."},{"id":"o1","text":"A CD4+ count of 150 cells/mm³","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. That defines stage III."},{"id":"o2","text":"Candida overgrowth in the mouth","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. An opportunistic infection of profound immune loss."},{"id":"o3","text":"Kaposi sarcoma lesions","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. An AIDS-defining cancer."}]},{"id":"hiv:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"All AIDS is HIV; not all HIV is AIDS; The line: CD4+ < 200 or an OI","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"hiv:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about brain and nerves?","answer":"Crypto: headache, stiff neck; Toxo (cat litter, raw meat): seizures → report; ↓ LOC, unequal pupils → notify now","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Lyme disease: exposure, recognition and prevention","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Easiest to cure at the rash stage · Tick checks + report a rash or fever"},{"title":"Stage 1 — early localized","text":"Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans) · Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime · Early treatment → better in ~20 days"},{"title":"Prevention and tick removal (Box 17.14)","text":"DEET, light colors, long sleeves, pants tucked in · Tweezers: a steady upward pull · No nail polish, jelly or heat; report a rash or fever"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Stage 1: bull’s-eye rash, low-grade fever, sometimes myalgia and headache; antibiotics (doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; azithromycin or clarithromycin if allergic to penicillin); most recover in about 20 days.","task":"A client found a tick on the leg 10 days ago. Which finding suggests early Lyme disease?","cue":"Borrelia burgdorferi spreads outward in the skin from the bite, producing the expanding ring; flulike symptoms reflect early infection.","rule":"Stage 1: bull’s-eye rash, low-grade fever, sometimes myalgia and headache; antibiotics (doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; azithromycin or clarithromycin if allergic to penicillin); most recover in about 20 days.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"lyme","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Lyme disease: exposure, recognition and prevention","notes":["17-lyme-disease"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Easiest to cure at the rash stage; Tick checks + report a rash or fever","Stage 1 — early localized: Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans); Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; Early treatment → better in ~20 days","Prevention and tick removal (Box 17.14): DEET, light colors, long sleeves, pants tucked in; Tweezers: a steady upward pull; No nail polish, jelly or heat; report a rash or fever"],"terms":["erythema migrans","Lyme disease"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Lyme disease","blocks":[{"note":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Cause","lines":["Borrelia burgdorferi; deer tick","Most common vector-borne disease; reportable","Not autoimmune: chronic inflammation"]},{"note":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Stage 1 — early localized","lines":["Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans)","Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime","Early treatment → better in ~20 days"]},{"note":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Stages 2 and 3","lines":["3–12 weeks: joint pain, dysrhythmias","Months–years: knee arthritis, heart block, cognition","Heart involvement → admit"]},{"note":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Prevention and tick removal (Box 17.14)","lines":["DEET, light colors, long sleeves, pants tucked in","Tweezers: a steady upward pull","No nail polish, jelly or heat; report a rash or fever"]},{"note":"17-lyme-disease","title":"Why","lines":["Easiest to cure at the rash stage","Tick checks + report a rash or fever"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"lyme:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Easiest to cure at the rash stage; Tick checks + report a rash or fever","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"lyme:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about stage 1 — early localized?","answer":"Bull’s-eye rash (erythema migrans); Doxycycline, amoxicillin, cefuroxime; Early treatment → better in ~20 days","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Transplantation: rejection and immunosuppression","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Signs = failing organ + inflammation · Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent"},{"title":"Signs of rejection (Table 17.5)","text":"Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency · Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr · Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose"},{"title":"Teaching and safety","text":"All → infection risk · Pregnancy: plan first · Hazardous-drug handling; stopping → rejection"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Table 17.5 lung signs: fever > 100°F and chills, dry cough, low energy, falling home spirometry, chest pain, poor appetite, dyspnea. Teach recipients with flulike symptoms to seek care emergently.","task":"A client who received a lung transplant 9 months ago calls to report a dry cough that started 3 days ago. What should the nurse say?","cue":"Rejection inflames the lung graft; immunosuppressants blunt inflammation, so even mild symptoms may signal significant rejection or infection.","rule":"Table 17.5 lung signs: fever > 100°F and chills, dry cough, low energy, falling home spirometry, chest pain, poor appetite, dyspnea. Teach recipients with flulike symptoms to seek care emergently.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"transplant-immunity","ch":"17","chapters":["17"],"title":"Transplantation: rejection and immunosuppression","notes":["17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 17; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Signs = failing organ + inflammation; Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent","Signs of rejection (Table 17.5): Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency; Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr; Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose","Teaching and safety: All → infection risk; Pregnancy: plan first; Hazardous-drug handling; stopping → rejection","Why#2: Balance: graft protection vs infection and toxicity; Grapefruit, missed doses tip it"],"terms":["cyclosporine","rejection","immunosuppression"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Transplant rejection and anti-rejection drugs","blocks":[{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Non-self tissue","lines":["HLA mismatch → non-self","NK + cytotoxic T cells; CMI most","Lifelong immunosuppression"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Hyperacute","lines":["Minutes–hours; preexisting antibodies","Clots → necrosis; can’t be stopped","Organ removed; now rare"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Acute","lines":["1 wk–3 months (or later)","Most have ≥ 1 episode","Kidney: ↑ creatinine, proteinuria → biopsy; treatable"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Chronic","lines":["Months–years; fibrosis","Lungs: airways; liver: bile ducts","Heart: allograft vasculopathy"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Signs of rejection (Table 17.5)","lines":["Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency","Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr","Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Why","lines":["Signs = failing organ + inflammation","Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Calcineurin inhibitors","lines":["Cyclosporine: HTN, kidneys, no grapefruit","Tacrolimus: tremor, gum overgrowth, kidneys"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Steroids and cytotoxic drugs","lines":["Prednisone: falls, fractures","Azathioprine: ↓ counts; cyclophosphamide: hydrate (cystitis)","Leflunomide: teratogenic for 2 yr; mycophenolate: no Mg/Al antacids"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"mTOR inhibitors and antibodies","lines":["Sirolimus, everolimus: lipids, no grapefruit","Basiliximab: anaphylaxis; crash cart","ATG: premedicate, ≥ 4–6 hr, slow for chills"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Teaching and safety","lines":["All → infection risk","Pregnancy: plan first","Hazardous-drug handling; stopping → rejection"]},{"note":"17-transplant-rejection-and-anti-rejection","title":"Why#2","lines":["Balance: graft protection vs infection and toxicity","Grapefruit, missed doses tip it"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"transplant-immunity:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client who received a lung transplant 9 months ago calls to report a dry cough that started 3 days ago. What should the nurse say?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Table 17.5 lung signs: fever > 100°F and chills, dry cough, low energy, falling home spirometry, chest pain, poor appetite, dyspnea. Teach recipients with flulike symptoms to seek care emergently.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Please go to the nearest emergency department to be evaluated.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. A dry cough is a sign of lung rejection; flulike symptoms after a transplant need emergency evaluation."},{"id":"o1","text":"“A dry cough is normal after a lung transplant.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. It may be rejection."},{"id":"o2","text":"“An over-the-counter cough suppressant should help.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Masks a warning sign."},{"id":"o3","text":"“If it lasts less than a week, don’t worry.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Delays treatment."}]},{"id":"transplant-immunity:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Signs = failing organ + inflammation; Drugs blunt the signs → flulike = urgent","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"transplant-immunity:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs of rejection (table 17.5)?","answer":"Fever > 100°F, chills; flulike → emergency; Kidney: ↓ urine, a gain of 2–4 lb in 24 hr; Lung: dry cough, ↓ spirometry; pancreas: ↑ glucose","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"GI testing: preparation, bleeding and procedure complications","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules · FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep"},{"title":"Nutrition history","text":"Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults · Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia · Unintended weight loss → GI cancer? · Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"Aspiration · ↓ breathing (sedation) · Bleeding/perforation; pancreatitis/sepsis (ERCP) · Fluid + electrolyte loss (prep) · Anxiety, embarrassment; teaching needs"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"After ERCP: severe pain, fever, or nausea and vomiting that won’t settle in the next 2 days → call. Gas pain is expected.","task":"A client is going home after an ERCP with removal of a gallstone. Which statement shows the client understands the teaching?","cue":"Instruments and dye in the pancreatic and bile ducts irritate them → the pancreas can activate its own enzymes (pancreatitis) → bacteria can enter the ducts (sepsis) → symptoms build over hours to days.","rule":"After ERCP: severe pain, fever, or nausea and vomiting that won’t settle in the next 2 days → call. Gas pain is expected.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"gi-tests","ch":"45","chapters":["45"],"title":"GI testing: preparation, bleeding and procedure complications","notes":["45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","45-overview","45-gi-changes-with-aging","45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","45-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 45; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules; FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep","Nutrition history: Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults; Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia; Unintended weight loss → GI cancer?; Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers","Priority problems: Aspiration · ↓ breathing (sedation); Bleeding/perforation; pancreatitis/sepsis (ERCP); Fluid + electrolyte loss (prep); Anxiety, embarrassment; teaching needs","What and why: Sees bleeding, ulcers, inflammation, tumors; biopsies (e.g., H. pylori); Informed consent every time"],"terms":["colonoscopy","ERCP","perforation"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"GI labs and stool tests","blocks":[{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"CBC and clotting","lines":["GI bleeding = most common cause of adult anemia","PT prolonged in liver disease (no clotting proteins)"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Liver enzymes","lines":["ALT 4–36, AST 0–35 → ↑ liver disease, highest with necrosis (hepatitis, cirrhosis)","ALT ↑: hepatitis significant, cirrhosis moderate, pancreatitis mild; slightly ↑ in older adults, men, African American people","AST ↑ also liver metastasis, drug injury; ↓ kidney disease, dialysis; slightly ↑ older adults, ↓ females","Alk phos 30–120 (slightly ↑ older adults) → ↑ biliary obstruction, cirrhosis, liver tumor; ↓ low phosphate, undernutrition","Albumin 3.5–5.0 → ↓ liver disease, undernutrition; ↑ dehydration"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Pancreas","lines":["Amylase + lipase ↑ = acute pancreatitis (also chronic)","Amylase up within 24 h, ~5 days; urine amylase 5–7 days","May be normal with extensive necrosis","Amylase also ↑ cholecystitis, perforated ulcer/bowel; slightly ↑ older adults","Lipase also ↑ biliary disease, bowel obstruction, peptic ulcer"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Bilirubin and ammonia","lines":["Bilirubin 0.3–1.0 (critical > 12): direct ↑ = obstruction, gallstones · indirect ↑ = hemolysis/liver","GGT ↑ also = impaired excretion · urine urobilinogen shows trouble before jaundice","Ammonia 10–80 mcg/dL ↑ → cirrhosis, hepatic encephalopathy/coma, portal hypertension, GI bleed or obstruction + liver disease"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Electrolytes and others","lines":["K⁺ 3.5–5.0 (critical 6.1) ↓ with diarrhea, vomiting, NG suction, ascites; ↑ hemolysis, kidney failure","Vomiting, diarrhea → Na⁺ + K⁺ lost → replace","Ca 9.0–10.5 (critical 13; slightly ↓ older adults) ↓ malabsorption, low albumin, hypoparathyroidism, pancreatitis; ↑ hyperparathyroidism, bone metastasis","Cholesterol < 200: ↑ biliary obstruction, pancreatitis; ↓ liver damage","D-xylose 20–57 (60 min), 30–58 (120 min) → ↓ = malabsorption, short bowel"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Tumor markers","lines":["CEA < 5, CA 19-9 0–37 → monitor treatment + recurrence, not diagnosis","Also ↑ in benign inflammation, cirrhosis, Crohn's, ulcer, gallstones","CA 19-9: pancreas, stomach, colon, gallbladder cancer · CEA: GI, pancreas, hepatobiliary, breast, lung"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Colorectal screening at home","lines":["FIT yearly · gFOBT yearly · stool DNA every 3 years","DNA kit: prescription, mailed home + back; less specific than colonoscopy; suits the fearful or cost-worried","At home, no prep, usually covered (Medicare)","False negatives/positives occur → positive → colonoscopy; prompt follow-up"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"gFOBT preparation","lines":["gFOBT: no NSAIDs 7 days, no red meat + vitamin C > 250 mg for 3 days","FIT: no restrictions → better adherence"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Who should get a colonoscopy instead","lines":["Personal/family colon cancer, IBD, past positive test → colonoscopy","Higher incidence: Black, AI/AN → advocate screening"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Other stool tests","lines":["O&P · fecal fat (steatorrhea) · C. diff (older adults, antibiotics)"]},{"note":"45-gi-labs-and-stool-tests","title":"Why","lines":["Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules","FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep"]}]},{"id":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Imaging and endoscopy — EGD, ERCP, capsule","blocks":[{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Plain film and acute abdomen series","lines":["Plain film: masses, gas patterns (obstruction)","Acute abdomen series: chest + supine + upright films → free air = perforation"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"CT","lines":["Contrast: ask past reactions/allergies, NPO ≥ 4 h, IV","Expect warm flush, metallic taste","10 min (no contrast) / 30 min (contrast)"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"MRI, ultrasound and others","lines":["MRI/MRCP: check implants; longer, no radiation","Ultrasound: painless, no radiation, maybe fasting, lie still, no aftercare","EUS: tumors of wall, nodes, pancreas; care as for endoscopy","Liver-spleen scan: small safe dose; ask pregnancy/breastfeeding; lie still","Tracer in urine 24 h → handwashing"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"What and why","lines":["Sees bleeding, ulcers, inflammation, tumors; biopsies (e.g., H. pylori)","Informed consent every time"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"What it can do","lines":["Esophagus, stomach, duodenum (esophagoscopy, gastroscopy = one organ)","Stops bleeding, dilates strictures, biopsies (celiac)"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Before","lines":["Stop anticoagulants, aspirin, NSAIDs days–1 week before","Diabetes → instructions · NPO 6–8 h (no food, water or smoking) · dentures out"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"During","lines":["Moderate sedation (midazolam, fentanyl, propofol) → ↓ breathing","Throat spray → no gag reflex · bite block","RR < 10 or exhaled CO₂ < 20% → sternal rub"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"After — prevent aspiration","lines":["VS q15–30 min, rails up","NPO until gag reflex returns (30–60 min) = prevent aspiration","Perforation: pain, bleeding, fever","Driver, no driving 12–18 h; sore throat → lozenges"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"What it does","lines":["Bile + pancreatic ducts → find obstruction","Mostly treatment: papillotomy to remove stones, stents, biopsies"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Before and during","lines":["Like EGD: NPO 6–8 h, dentures out, IV","Reconcile anticoagulants, NSAIDs, antiplatelets, diabetes drugs","Ask contrast allergy, ICD (may be turned off)","Left side → prone; 30 min–2 h"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"After","lines":["VS q15 min; gag reflex before eating; stop IV once fluids stay down","Hours–2 days later: pancreatitis, cholecystitis, infection, bleeding, perforation, biloma → severe pain; fever = sepsis","Gas pain from air is expected","Driver, no driving 12–18 h"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"What and how","lines":["Camera capsule → small bowel bleeding","NPO 12 h before + first 2 h; clear liquids at 2 h, light lunch at 4 h","Belt recorder; no vigorous activity; ~8 h"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Afterward","lines":["Single use, flushed away","Report obstruction, fever, chest pain, dyspnea, or not passed in 2 weeks"]},{"note":"45-imaging-and-endoscopy-egd-ercp-capsule","title":"Why","lines":["Midazolam, fentanyl, propofol all depress breathing; spray removes gag reflex","Danger during = hypoventilation → watch RR + capnography","Danger after = aspiration → nothing by mouth until gag returns"]}]},{"id":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Colonoscopy, sigmoidoscopy and colorectal screening","blocks":[{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"From age 45","lines":["From 45: colonoscopy q10 yr · sigmoidoscopy q5 yr · virtual colonoscopy q5 yr · stool tests","High risk / past polyps → more often","Rising in young adults with obesity"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"What it does","lines":["Whole colon; biopsies, polypectomy, finds + treats bleeding"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Preparation","lines":["Stop aspirin, anticoagulants, antiplatelets; diabetes plan","Prep night before (chill it); clear liquids day before","No red, orange or purple liquids/gelatin","Electrolyte drinks; diarrhea ~1 h after starting","Poor prep → missed polyps"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"During","lines":["Left side, knees up; CO₂ insufflation = less pain","30–60 min; atropine ready (vasovagal bradycardia)","RR < 10 / CO₂ < 20% → sternal rub"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"After (Box 45.2)","lines":["NPO until awake; fluids after flatus","VS q15–30 min; left side; rails up","Expected: fullness, cramping, gas; small blood after polypectomy","Perforation: severe pain, guarding (fever later)","Shock: ↓ BP, ↑ HR, dizzy, pale/ash-gray, confusion (older adults)","Report heavy bleeding or severe pain now","Driver; no driving, legal decisions or focused work that day"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Virtual colonoscopy (CT colonography)","lines":["3-D CT, same prep","Polyp/bleeding found → real colonoscopy anyway"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Flexible sigmoidoscopy","lines":["Rectum + sigmoid; every 5 yr from 45","Clear liquids, laxative night before, Fleet enema morning","No sedation, 5–15 min, left side knee-chest","Gas pain; report heavy bleeding"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Health literacy","lines":["Teach-back for prep and follow-up"]},{"note":"45-colonoscopy-sigmoidoscopy-and-colorectal","title":"Why","lines":["Prep floods the colon → water + electrolytes lost → electrolyte drinks","Red/purple liquids look like blood on camera","No food → diabetes drugs need a plan"]}]},{"id":"45-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"45-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["One 25-ft tube + salivary glands, liver, gallbladder, pancreas","Jobs: secretion, digestion, absorption, motility, elimination","Disease shows as nutrition, elimination or pain problems","Toolkit for ch 46–49: organs, exam order, labs, endoscopy + bowel prep safety"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"The wall","lines":["Mucosa (glands) → submucosa → muscularis (moves contents) → serosa","Same layers the whole length"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Digestion and absorption","lines":["Food → chyme (liquid) before absorption","Stomach HCl, liver bile, organ enzymes","Absorption → into the blood"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Mouth and esophagus","lines":["32 teeth; saliva: amylase (carbs) + lipase (fats)","Upper sphincter keeps air out","LES keeps acid down → fails = GERD"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Stomach","lines":["LUQ; LES above, pyloric sphincter below","Parietal cells: HCl + intrinsic factor (B₁₂ → else pernicious anemia)","Chief cells: pepsinogen","Duodenum releases secretin + CCK → ↓ acid, ↓ motility"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Pancreas","lines":["Behind the stomach (head, body, tail)","Exocrine: amylase, lipase, proteases","Endocrine: glucagon, insulin"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Liver and gallbladder","lines":["Liver (RUQ): ~1200 mL/min blood flow","Bile; stores iron, copper, A, D, E, B₁₂","Detoxifies drugs + alcohol (↓ with age → toxicity)","Ammonia → urea; makes albumin, prothrombin, fibrinogen","Gallbladder stores + concentrates bile, releases it for fat"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Small intestine","lines":["9–16 ft: duodenum (ampulla of Vater) → jejunum → ileum → ileocecal valve","Villi → main absorption organ","3–6 h transit"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Large intestine","lines":["5–6 ft: cecum (+ appendix) → colon → rectum → anal canal","Absorbs water + electrolytes → feces","Distention → peristalsis to rectum"]},{"note":"45-overview","title":"Why","lines":["RUQ: liver, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas head","LUQ: stomach, spleen, pancreas body/tail","RLQ: cecum, appendix · LLQ: sigmoid","Midline: aorta → never press a pulsating mass"]}]},{"id":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"GI changes with aging","blocks":[{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Stomach","lines":["Mucosa atrophies → ↓ HCl","↓ iron + B₁₂ absorption; bacterial overgrowth → atrophic gastritis"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Bowel","lines":["↓ peristalsis, dull nerve signals → postponed BMs → constipation, impaction"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Pancreas","lines":["↓ lipase → poor fat digestion → steatorrhea"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Liver","lines":["Smaller liver, ↓ enzymes → drugs build up → toxicity"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Gut microbiota","lines":["Altered microbiota → anorexia, undernutrition"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Lab values in older adults (Table 45.2)","lines":["Older adults: ALT, AST, alk phos, amylase slightly ↑","Calcium slightly ↓","ALT also ↑ in men, African American people · AST ↓ in females"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Who gets what","lines":["Older: stomach cancer · younger: IBD","Colon cancer rising under 50","Travel or camping by lakes/streams → diarrhea clue"]},{"note":"45-gi-changes-with-aging","title":"Why","lines":["Aging = less acid, motility, lipase, liver enzyme","Expect: slow drug clearance, constipation, fatty stools, ↓ B₁₂ + iron","Never: more lipase, more stools, better fat absorption","Slightly off-range lab can be normal for age; a critical value never is"]}]},{"id":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"GI history and the abdominal exam","blocks":[{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Questions to ask (Box 45.1)","lines":["Diet, drugs, supplements, appetite, weight change","Chewing/swallowing, dentures, heartburn","Past GI disease/surgery, family history","Tobacco, alcohol, travel, laxatives","Bowel pattern, bleeding, stool change, past scopes"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Nutrition history","lines":["Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults","Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia","Unintended weight loss → GI cancer?","Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Money, culture and genes","lines":["Food deserts, budgets → poor diet, OTC substitutes, delayed care","Spices, fasting practices","FAP (autosomal dominant) → colon cancer"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"The current problem","lines":["Timeline (diary helps)","Stool color + consistency, blood or tarry stools, distention","Weight change + timing","Skin: itching, jaundice, bruising"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Pain — PQRST","lines":["P: trigger + what helps · Q: quality","R: region, radiation · S: 0–10, effect on activity","T: onset, duration, frequency, meals, night waking"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Embarrassing → ask privately, matter-of-factly","Stress → IBS flares","Cancer → grief → refer"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Set-up and order","lines":["Empty bladder, supine, knees bent, arms at sides","Inspect → auscultate → palpate","Nurse: inspect, auscultate, light palpation · provider: deep palpation, percussion","No palpation with suspected appendicitis or aneurysm"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Inspect","lines":["RUQ → LUQ → LLQ → RLQ; painful area last","Asymmetry, scars, distention, bulging flanks, taut shiny skin","Contour at eye level; umbilicus; breathing movement","Aortic pulsation occasionally visible · bulging pulsating mass = alarm"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Organs by quadrant (Table 45.1)","lines":["RUQ: most of liver, gallbladder, duodenum, pancreas head, hepatic flexure, part ascending + transverse colon","LUQ: left liver lobe, stomach, spleen, pancreas body + tail, splenic flexure, part transverse + descending colon","RLQ: cecum, appendix, right ureter, right ovary + tube, right spermatic cord","LLQ: part descending colon, sigmoid, left ureter, left ovary + tube, left spermatic cord","Midline: aorta, uterus (if enlarged), bladder (if distended)","Asymmetry → think of the organs beneath"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Two action alerts","lines":["Visible peristalsis → report (obstruction)","Pulsating mass → don't touch, notify now (aneurysm)"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Auscultate","lines":["Diaphragm, start RLQ","Normal 5–30/min","Absent/hypo: after surgery, peritonitis, ileus","Hyper (borborygmus): diarrhea, gastroenteritis, above an obstruction","Aortic bruit → aneurysm → no palpation, notify"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Is peristalsis back after surgery?","lines":["Best sign: flatus in 8 h or stool in 12–24 h"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Light palpation","lines":["4 fingers, ½–1 inch deep, rotating","Guarding = voluntary · rigidity = peritoneal inflammation"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Percussion (provider)","lines":["Tympany = air · dull = solid organ or fluid","Hepatomegaly, splenomegaly (dull at left anterior axillary line)"]},{"note":"45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","title":"Why","lines":["Listen first → sounds are the patient's, not stirred up by your hands","Pain last → abdomen stays relaxed","Don't press a pulsating mass or inflamed appendix → avoid rupture"]}]},{"id":"45-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"45-adpie","title":"¶ For an assessment chapter the nursing process","lines":["Job 1: history + exam that point to the organ","Job 2: get the patient safely through the test (sedation, prep, dye, aftercare)"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Diet, supplements, weight change, swallowing, heartburn","Stool pattern + color, blood/tarry stool, laxatives","Alcohol, tobacco, travel, family history","Pain by PQRST; jaundice, itching, bruising"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Exam","lines":["Bladder empty, knees bent; inspect → listen → light palpation","Pain last; sounds from RLQ (5–30/min); bruits","Rigidity = peritoneal inflammation","Pulsating mass → hands off"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Before a test","lines":["Contrast/drug allergies; anticoagulants, NSAIDs; diabetes drugs","Implants (ICD, MRI), pregnancy, dentures, NPO, a driver","Health literacy"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Aspiration · ↓ breathing (sedation)","Bleeding/perforation; pancreatitis/sepsis (ERCP)","Fluid + electrolyte loss (prep)","Anxiety, embarrassment; teaching needs"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Prep done right","No aspiration; RR ≥ 10, SpO₂ OK","No perforation or bleeding; driver home","Teach-back of warning signs; screening from 45"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Before","lines":["Consent; NPO (6–8 h scope, 4 h CT contrast, 12 h capsule)","Stop anticoagulants/NSAIDs; diabetes plan; dentures out; IV","Teach prep: clear liquids, no red/purple, electrolytes"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"During","lines":["RR, SpO₂, capnography; < 10 / < 20% → sternal rub","Atropine for bradycardia; bite block (EGD); left side (colonoscopy)"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"After","lines":["VS q15–30 min, rails up","EGD/ERCP: NPO until gag returns · colonoscopy: left side, fluids after flatus","Watch pain, guarding, fever, bleeding, ↓ BP, confusion","Driver, no driving 12–18 h; ERCP problems up to 2 days"]},{"note":"45-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Gag reflex back before eating/drinking","VS baseline; no perforation, bleeding, pancreatitis, sepsis","States warning signs (severe pain, fever, vomiting, black/bloody stool, trouble swallowing)","Has a driver, knows next screening"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"gi-tests:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client is going home after an ERCP with removal of a gallstone. Which statement shows the client understands the teaching?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"After ERCP: severe pain, fever, or nausea and vomiting that won’t settle in the next 2 days → call. Gas pain is expected.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“If I get severe belly pain or a fever in the next two days, I’ll call right away.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Pancreatitis, cholecystitis, bleeding and perforation can appear hours to 2 days later; all bring severe pain, and fever means infection."},{"id":"o1","text":"“Some gassy, crampy pain is a sign something went wrong.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Colicky pain and flatulence from the instilled air are expected."},{"id":"o2","text":"“I can drive myself home once I feel awake.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. After sedation the client needs a driver and must not drive for 12–18 hours."},{"id":"o3","text":"“I can drink as soon as I get home, even if my throat is still numb.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Nothing by mouth until the gag reflex returns."}]},{"id":"gi-tests:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Guaiac detects hemoglobin's chemistry → red meat + some foods/drugs fool it → diet rules; FIT uses antibodies to human hemoglobin → no prep","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"gi-tests:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about nutrition history?","answer":"Lactose intolerance ~50% of adults; Anorexia, taste loss (COVID-19), dysphagia, dyspepsia; Unintended weight loss → GI cancer?; Alcohol + caffeine → gastritis, ulcers","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing · Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks · So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it"},{"title":"Heart and fluids","text":"Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD) · A-fib from vagus irritation"},{"title":"Oral care","text":"q2h; ultrasoft \"chemobrush\" (low platelets) · No foam swabs, no alcohol mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin · Water-based lubricant; saline/baking soda rinses"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Oral cancer: painless red, raised or eroded lesion, a lump in the cheek, or any lesion not healed in 2 weeks → report.","task":"During oral care, which finding should the nurse report as a possible sign of oral cancer?","cue":"Normal mouth sores heal as the lining turns over every few days → a lesion that persists is tissue that isn’t following normal cell turnover — possibly squamous cell carcinoma.","rule":"Oral cancer: painless red, raised or eroded lesion, a lump in the cheek, or any lesion not healed in 2 weeks → report.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma","ch":"46","chapters":["46"],"title":"Oral and esophageal cancer, surgery and trauma","notes":["46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","46-esophageal-trauma","46-overview","46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","46-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 46; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing; Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks; So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it","Heart and fluids: Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD); A-fib from vagus irritation","Oral care: q2h; ultrasoft \"chemobrush\" (low platelets); No foam swabs, no alcohol mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin; Water-based lubricant; saline/baking soda rinses","Why: Stomach pulled into the chest → suture line beside lungs, heart, mediastinum; Leak spills saliva + gastric juice into the chest; So nothing by mouth until barium swallow proves it sealed; fever + tachycardia = leak until proven otherwise"],"terms":["esophagectomy","jejunostomy","perforation"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Oral tumors, oral cancer and salivary glands","blocks":[{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Erythroplakia","lines":["Red, velvety, precancerous"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Leukoplakia","lines":["White, can't scrape off","Lips/tongue can turn cancerous; tobacco, bad dentures, cheek chewing","Hairy leukoplakia → HIV (early) or EBV"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Prevention and risk","lines":["Dentist 2×/year (inspect + palpate); no tobacco/alcohol; lip sun protection; avoid HPV","White men: ↑ HPV oropharyngeal cancer → vaccinate + screen men","PAH exposure (worse with HPV), gum disease with jaw bone loss","Genetics: ask about any cancer history; TP53 (p53 controls cell division)"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Types","lines":["Squamous (most): red, raised, eroded; not healed in 2 weeks","Basal cell on lips (sun), pearly border","Kaposi: purple nodule, AIDS"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Key features (Box 46.3)","lines":["Bleeding, chewing/swallowing trouble, weight loss","Thick or no saliva · painless red raised lesion · cheek lump"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Speech, eating and self-image","lines":["Tumor/treatment → trouble chewing, swallowing, speaking; pain limits self-care","Tongue/jaw removal, tracheostomy → body image + speech change","Assess self-image, support system, cultural/spiritual needs","Team: speech therapist, RDN, social worker, spiritual leader"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Diagnose","lines":["Gloves, blade, penlight; provider feels neck nodes","Biopsy; CT/MRI for staging"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Airway first","lines":["Airway = priority: dyspnea, wheeze, stridor; deep breathing to cough","Semi/high-Fowler, fluids if gag intact, Yankauer suction","Steroids (edema), antibiotics, cool mist"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Aspiration precautions","lines":["Check LOC + gag reflex before fluids","Upright, suction ready, small bites","Visitors ask before feeding; thickened liquids, SLP"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Oral care","lines":["q2h; ultrasoft \"chemobrush\" (low platelets)","No foam swabs, no alcohol mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin","Water-based lubricant; saline/baking soda rinses"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Treatment","lines":["Surgery, radiation, chemo — often combined; Mohs for lip BCC","Glossectomy, G-tube, laryngectomy, jaw resection, neck dissection, tracheostomy","Oral care before surgery; small excision → liquids 1 day"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"At home (Box 46.4)","lines":["Daily mouth check; ultrasoft brush","Thickened liquids; soft foods; saliva substitute"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Sialadenitis","lines":["Inflamed parotid/submandibular gland","Causes: infection (CMV), HIV, Sjögren, radiation, phenothiazines, tetracyclines","Abscess → can spread to neck/mediastinum · prevent with oral hygiene"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Assess and treat","lines":["Swelling, pain, pus; check CN VII","Hydrate, moist heat, massage, NSAIDs, antibiotics, sialagogues"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Xerostomia","lines":["Radiation → dry mouth within 24 h","Frequent water sips + mouth care before meals","Saliva substitutes afterward"]},{"note":"46-oral-tumors-oral-cancer-and-salivary-gla","title":"Why","lines":["Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing","Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks","So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it"]}]},{"id":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Esophageal cancer and esophagectomy","blocks":[{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Alcohol, smoking, low fruit/veg, pickled/fermented foods","Malnutrition, obesity, untreated GERD","Men higher"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Key features (Box 46.11)","lines":["Silent early; progressive dysphagia = most common","Weight loss > 20 lb","Food sticking, odynophagia, halitosis, hiccups, cough, hoarseness, pain"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Diagnose and support","lines":["EGD + biopsy; EUS staging; PET for spread","Fear of choking, lost joy in eating → support, referrals"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Nutrition and swallowing","lines":["Daily weight (same scale, before breakfast)","Upright after meals, HOB ≥ 30°","Semisoft + thickened liquids, supplements; tube feeding if needed","SLP + OT; watch every meal for aspiration"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Cancer treatments","lines":["Chemoradiation; PDT + porfimer (palliation)","Dilation (perforation risk); stents = palliation (antibiotics vs sepsis/endocarditis; migration)","Trastuzumab (HER2)"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Before","lines":["Stop smoking 2–4 weeks before","Respiratory rehab + nutrition support; track weight, I&O","Dental care; oral care 4×/day; antibiotics day of surgery"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Lungs first","lines":["Respiratory = highest priority; ventilator 16–24 h","TCDB q1–2h, splint, analgesia","Semi/high-Fowler; chest tube patent"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Heart and fluids","lines":["Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD)","A-fib from vagus irritation"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Anastomotic leak","lines":["Infection 4–5 days; leak 2–10 days → NPO","Leak → mediastinitis → sepsis","Fever, fluid collection, tachycardia, tachypnea, confusion → surgeon + RRT now"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"NG tube after esophageal surgery (Box 46.12)","lines":["Placement q4–8h; bloody → yellow-green by end of day 1","Secure it; never irrigate or reposition without order","Oral/nasal care q2–4h; HOB ≥ 30°; first sips upright"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Feeding after surgery","lines":["J-tube feeds day 2; no residual checks","Barium swallow day 7 → NG out if no leak","Liquids for 2 weeks"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"At home","lines":["Walk + spirometer; no bed rest","Small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein, soft; upright","Report ≥ 5 lb loss in a month; home tube feeding/PN if needed","Handwashing; report returning dysphagia","Quality of life; hospice when needed"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-cancer-and-esophagectomy","title":"Why","lines":["Stomach pulled into the chest → suture line beside lungs, heart, mediastinum","Leak spills saliva + gastric juice into the chest","So nothing by mouth until barium swallow proves it sealed; fever + tachycardia = leak until proven otherwise"]}]},{"id":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Esophageal trauma","blocks":[{"note":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Causes","lines":["Blunt trauma, chemicals, severe vomiting, straining, seizures, foreign bodies, tubes/scopes","Tear → acid into the mediastinum → shock, respiratory failure, sepsis"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Acid vs alkali","lines":["Acid → superficial · alkali → deep, can perforate in 1 minute","Later: aspiration, bleeding, strictures"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Assess","lines":["Airway + breathing first; chest pain, dysphagia, bleeding","CT for mediastinal air"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Treat","lines":["NPO, suction, esophageal rest > 1 week, TPN","Broad-spectrum antibiotics; high-dose steroids (prevent strictures)","Analgesia; viscous lidocaine; surgery/G-tube if needed"]},{"note":"46-esophageal-trauma","title":"Why","lines":["Acid → coagulates surface proteins → crust limits depth","Alkali → liquefies proteins + fats → keeps burning deeper (drain cleaner)","So swallowed alkali is more dangerous — perforates in minutes"]}]},{"id":"46-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"46-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Mouth + esophagus problems threaten tissue integrity, nutrition and the airway","Exemplars: stomatitis (tissue) and GERD (nutrition)","Also: oral cancer, salivary glands, hiatal hernia, esophageal cancer, trauma","First question always: can they breathe and swallow safely?"]},{"note":"46-overview","title":"The LES and the pressure gradient","lines":["LES pressure > stomach pressure → nothing refluxes","Reflux = LES relaxes or abdominal pressure rises (obesity, pregnancy, belts, bending, big meals)","NG tube holds it open · lying flat removes gravity"]},{"note":"46-overview","title":"What lowers LES pressure (Box 46.5)","lines":["Caffeine, chocolate, peppermint, citrus, tomatoes, alcohol, smoking","Drugs: nitrates, CCBs, anticholinergics; high estrogen/progesterone","NG tube"]},{"note":"46-overview","title":"How reflux injures","lines":["Acid → reflux esophagitis","Heals with Barrett's (acid-resistant but premalignant) or scar → stricture","Reaches throat/lungs → asthma, laryngitis, night cough, dental decay, aspiration"]},{"note":"46-overview","title":"Why esophageal cancer spreads early","lines":["No serosa + rich lymphatics → fast growth, early spread","Squamous: upper 2/3 · adenocarcinoma (most common): distal, near Barrett's"]},{"note":"46-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Swallowing protects the airway (tongue, palate, epiglottis, gag reflex)","Pain, numbness, swelling, narrowing → protection fails","So aspiration precautions in almost every plan"]}]},{"id":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Stomatitis — the tissue integrity exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Definition","lines":["Mouth inflammation; canker sores → pain, bleeding, infection","Recurrent aphthous stomatitis ~20%, more women"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Primary vs secondary","lines":["Primary: aphthous, herpes simplex, trauma","Secondary: infection in immunocompromised, chemo, radiation, steroids"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Candidiasis","lines":["Antibiotics kill normal flora → Candida overgrows","White plaques that wipe off → red, sore base","High risk: older adults, diabetes, malnutrition, stress, dry mouth → teach mouth care (prevention)"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Other causes","lines":["B vitamins, folate, zinc, iron deficiency","Tobacco, alcohol; trigger foods (coffee, nuts, citrus, gluten)"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Who is at risk for mouth disease","lines":["Tobacco, alcohol, sugar, low fluoride","Poor dental access; diabetes, atherosclerosis, Alzheimer's"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Prevention (Box 46.1)","lines":["Water, balanced diet, stress control","Weekly mouth self-exam; well-fitting dentures","Brush + floss twice daily; no alcohol mouthwash; dentist regularly"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Assess","lines":["History: hygiene, drugs, infections, stress","Lesions: location, size, color, drainage","Pharynx + dysphagia → may extend down esophagus","Severe swelling can block the airway","Coping, body image · dehydration (↑ Na, BUN) dries mucosa → restore fluids"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Tissue integrity · 2. Pain"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Dysphagia action alert (PASS)","lines":["Dysphagia → obstruction, aspiration, malnutrition","PASS: Probable? Account for history · Screen · SLP referral","Start aspiration precautions"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Oral care (Box 46.2)","lines":["Dentures out if very sore; care 2× daily + after meals (do it if they can't), q2h if uncontrolled","Soft brush, SLS-free paste; no mouthwash, no lemon-glycerin","Rinse: warm saline / baking soda","Soft, bland, nonacidic food; topical analgesics → document effect","AP can do it → nurse inspects"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Drugs","lines":["Infection: clotrimazole troches, nystatin swish + spit, chlorhexidine","Pain: viscous lidocaine, diphenhydramine, dyclonine, antacid mixes","Teach swallow vs spit vs oral; finish antibiotics","Pain drugs (up to opioids): side effects, no driving"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Viscous lidocaine alert","lines":["Numb mouth → can't feel hot liquid burns","Numb throat → aspiration risk"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Comfort and recovery","lines":["Cool liquids; no spicy, salty, acidic, hard foods","High protein to heal","Outcome: healthy mucosa, minimal pain"]},{"note":"46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Mouthwash (alcohol) + lemon-glycerin (acid) dry + sting → organisms thrive","Saline / baking soda clean without irritation, neutralize acid"]}]},{"id":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"GERD — the nutrition exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Definition","lines":["GER = backflow · GERD = chronic disease from persistent reflux","Most common upper GI disorder; 18–28% of adults; mostly middle-aged/older but rising in younger adults","May be silent at first"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Obesity (biggest), H. pylori, NG tube","Pregnancy, tight belts, bending, ascites","Sleep apnea; genetics + lifestyle"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Complications","lines":["Esophagitis → Barrett's (premalignant), stricture","Asthma, laryngitis, dental decay, bleeding, aspiration"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Health equity","lines":["Disparities for Hispanic/Latino patients → professional medical translators/interpreters"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Ask","lines":["Heartburn, atypical chest pain","New asthma, morning hoarseness, night cough/wheeze","Dysphagia, odynophagia"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Key features (Box 46.6)","lines":["Main: dyspepsia + regurgitation","Water brash (no sour taste), pyrosis, globus, dental caries, belching","After meals 20 min–2 h; worse lying/bending; better upright, fluids, antacids","Can mimic cardiac pain · listen for crackles"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"The frightened patient","lines":["ED visit fearing MI → stay with them"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Older adults","lines":["Older adults: dysphagia, vomiting, anorexia, anemia, cough instead of heartburn"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["Usually symptoms + history","EGD for atypical symptoms","pH monitoring = most accurate (24–48 h + diary)","Manometry before surgery (not diagnostic alone)"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Nutrition · 2. Pain"]},{"note":"46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Symptoms follow gravity + pressure: bending, straining, big meals, lying flat → reflux","Upright, small meals, antacids → relief","Best treatments: positions, portions, timing"]}]},{"id":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Treating GERD — diet, lifestyle, drugs, procedures","blocks":[{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Food choices","lines":["Chronic → teaching is key; work with RDN","Avoid peppermint, chocolate, fried/fatty food, caffeine, carbonated drinks","Limit spicy + acidic (OJ, tomatoes) until healed"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Meals and sleep","lines":["4–6 small meals; eat slowly","No eating within 3 h of bedtime","HOB on blocks or a wedge pillow"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Habits","lines":["No alcohol, no tobacco; lose weight","Upright ≥ 1 h after eating","No lifting, straining, bending; loose clothes","CPAP helps night reflux","Habits bring prompt relief; teach signs of stricture, Barrett's; RDN/support group"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Drugs that cause reflux","lines":["Review: contraceptives, anticholinergics, sedatives, NSAIDs, nitrates, CCBs"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"The three classes","lines":["Antacids, H₂ blockers, PPIs","Lower acid · speed emptying · protect mucosa","IV PPI → stress ulcer prevention","Symptoms recur when PPIs stop; long-term use can mask reflux"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Long-term PPI cautions","lines":["Long-term PPIs: poor absorption, infections, kidney/liver/heart/dementia/GI tumor links → monitor","Older adults: ↓ calcium absorption (+ protein digestion) → hip fracture"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Stretta procedure","lines":["Radiofrequency via scope → ↓ vagus activity; may not suit obesity/severe symptoms","Clear liquids 24 h, then soft; no NSAIDs/aspirin 10 days","No NG tube for 1 month","Report pain, bleeding, dysphagia, vomiting, dyspnea"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Laparoscopic Nissen fundoplication (LNF)","lines":["Standard surgery: fundus wrapped around lower esophagus","For: drugs fail, high-volume reflux, intolerance/nonadherence, large hiatal hernia","Keep antireflux habits — recurrence common"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"LINX","lines":["Magnetic ring around LES","Tell every provider; older devices: no MRI"]},{"note":"46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce","title":"Why","lines":["PPIs block the proton pump (last step of acid making) → fastest healing","Acid also frees calcium, iron, B₁₂ and kills microbes","Years of PPIs → fractures, deficiencies, infections"]}]},{"id":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Hiatal hernia and fundoplication care","blocks":[{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Types","lines":["Stomach pushes through the hiatus into the chest","Sliding (I, most common) → reflux","Paraesophageal (II–IV) → volvulus, obstruction, strangulation, iron deficiency anemia"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Key features (Box 46.8)","lines":["Most asymptomatic; worse after meals, lying flat","Sliding: heartburn, regurgitation, chest pain, dysphagia, belching","Paraesophageal: fullness, breathlessness, suffocation after meals, angina-like pain, worse lying down"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Diagnose","lines":["Barium swallow = most specific; EGD; manometry"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Treatment","lines":["Sliding → treat like GERD","Paraesophageal → medical care if possible; surgery if complications","Pre-op: lose weight, quit smoking"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"The operation","lines":["Fundus wrapped + sutured, esophagus anchored below diaphragm","LNF fewer complications · thoracic → chest tube + NG"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"After LNF (Box 46.9)","lines":["Watch for bleeding, infection (uncommon)","Soft diet ~1 week; antireflux drugs ≥ 1 month","No driving 1 week; walk, no lifting","Shower at 2 days; strips stay till day 10","Report fever > 101°F (> 100°F if > 65), vomiting, bloating, pain"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"After open repair — lungs first","lines":["Priority: prevent respiratory complications","HOB ≥ 30°, walk early, splint to cough, spirometer"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"The NG tube","lines":["Large-bore NG: drainage dark brown → yellow-green in 8 h","Placement q4–8h; anchor well — reinsertion can perforate the wrap","Keep patent (no retching/vomiting); oral care; I&O"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Complications (Box 46.10)","lines":["Temporary dysphagia (persistent → too tight)","Gas bloat: can't belch","Atelectasis/pneumonia","Blocked NG: nausea, vomiting, distention, no drainage"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Eating again and gas","lines":["Clear → near-normal over 4–6 weeks; small frequent meals","Supervise first feedings","Gas bloat: no carbonation, gum, straws, gassy/fatty foods; walk; simethicone"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Going home","lines":["Activity limits 3–6 wk (open) / ~1 wk (lap)","Small frequent meals → near-normal by 4–6 wk; no carbonation, gassy/fatty food, gum, straws","Stool softeners (no straining); check incision daily","Avoid sick people — coughing can dehisce the repair"]},{"note":"46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care","title":"Why","lines":["Fundoplication = sutured cuff → everything protects it","NG sizes the wrap + empties stomach (no retching); never reinsert blindly","Prevent coughing, straining, vomiting = pressure spikes on sutures"]}]},{"id":"46-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"46-adpie","title":"¶ Every patient in this chapter is assessed","lines":["Order: airway + swallowing → tissue + pain → nutrition","Sore mouth, numb throat, narrowed esophagus, fresh sutures → a meal can become aspiration"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Mouth","lines":["Hygiene, dentures, tobacco/alcohol, drugs (antibiotics, chemo, steroids)","Inspect: ulcers, plaques, red/white patches, lesions > 2 weeks","Eating, swallowing, speaking; body image"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Swallowing and airway","lines":["Coughing/choking, sticking, dysphagia, odynophagia","Stridor, wheeze, crackles; gag reflex, LOC","PASS → SLP"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Esophagus","lines":["Heartburn, regurgitation — meals, lying, bending","Atypical chest pain, night cough, hoarseness, asthma","LES-lowering habits; weight loss + progressive dysphagia (cancer)"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Stomatitis: tissue integrity + pain","GERD: nutrition + pain","Cancer: airway/aspiration, malnutrition, anxiety","After surgery: respiratory, leak, fluids, A-fib"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Healthy mucosa, minimal pain","No aspiration","Avoids reflux foods; small meals, none 3 h before bed; HOB raised; no alcohol/tobacco; takes drugs; weight stable","After surgery: clear lungs, no leak, diet advancing"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Oral care and comfort","lines":["Oral care 2× + after meals (q2h if bad); ultrasoft brush","No mouthwash, lemon-glycerin, foam swabs; saline/baking soda","Antifungals as ordered; lidocaine → burn + aspiration caution","AP does care → nurse inspects"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Airway and swallowing","lines":["Semi/high-Fowler, Yankauer ready","Gag reflex before fluids; small bites, thickened liquids","Visitors ask first; SLP"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"GERD teaching","lines":["4–6 small meals; avoid the LES-lowering list","Nothing 3 h before bed; HOB blocks/wedge; upright after meals","Antacids / H₂ blockers / PPIs; LINX → MRI alert"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"After surgery","lines":["Fundoplication: HOB ≥ 30°, walk, splint; anchor NG; supervise feeding","Esophagectomy: TCDB q1–2h; BP, fluids, A-fib","Don't irrigate/move NG; J-tube, no residuals","Leak signs → surgeon + RRT"]},{"note":"46-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Mouth healing, pain controlled","Safe swallowing, clear lungs","Reflux ↓ (reflux diet, small early meals, HOB raised, drugs); weight stable","After surgery: clear lungs, expected NG drainage, no leak","States swish/spit drugs, GERD rules, what to report"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma:case","kind":"choice","scene":"During oral care, which finding should the nurse report as a possible sign of oral cancer?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Oral cancer: painless red, raised or eroded lesion, a lump in the cheek, or any lesion not healed in 2 weeks → report.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A red, raised, eroded lesion on the tongue that has not healed in 3 weeks","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Any lesion not healed in 2 weeks — especially red, raised or eroded — needs further assessment."},{"id":"o1","text":"White plaques on the palate that wipe off easily","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Plaques that wipe off are Candida."},{"id":"o2","text":"A canker sore that appeared 3 days ago","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Aphthous ulcers usually heal on their own in days."},{"id":"o3","text":"Gums that bleed slightly with brushing","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Common with gingivitis; report if heavy, but not a cancer sign."}]},{"id":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Saliva washes teeth, buffers acid, fights bacteria, helps swallowing; Lose it → infections climb the ducts, decay, food sticks; So treatment = bring saliva back or replace it","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"upper-gi-cancer-trauma:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about heart and fluids?","answer":"Hypotension; fluid overload (crackles, JVD); A-fib from vagus irritation","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺ · Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing"},{"title":"Family history and current symptoms","text":"Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid · Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice · Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido"},{"title":"Safety and comfort","text":"No palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland · Orthostatic precautions · Older adults: fluids q2h awake, layers, skin + perineal care"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Cortisol excess: round face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity, striae, bruising, high glucose, infections, poor healing.","task":"Which finding suggests too much adrenocortical hormone?","cue":"Excess cortisol breaks down protein and collagen → thin skin, striae, bruising → fat moves to the trunk, face and upper back → round face and buffalo hump.","rule":"Cortisol excess: round face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity, striae, bruising, high glucose, infections, poor healing.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"adrenal-assessment","ch":"53","chapters":["53"],"title":"Adrenal hormones and a focused endocrine assessment","notes":["53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","53-overview","53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","53-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 53; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺; Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing","Family history and current symptoms: Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid; Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice; Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido","Safety and comfort: No palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland; Orthostatic precautions; Older adults: fluids q2h awake, layers, skin + perineal care","Why#2: Stress on the body = fast heart, wide pupils, sweating, slow gut, glucose ↑ (liver releases sugar); Why sick or stressed people with diabetes run high; why beta blockers calm thyroid tachycardia (ch 55)"],"terms":["aldosterone","cortisol","catecholamine"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"The adrenal glands — aldosterone, cortisol, catecholamines","blocks":[{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Aldosterone — the sodium and potassium hormone","lines":["Mineralocorticoid: kidney keeps Na⁺ + water, excretes K⁺","Driven by RAAS, serum K⁺, and ACTH"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"The RAAS, step by step","lines":["↓ fluid volume (blood loss, Na⁺ loss, posture) or hypoxemia → renin","Renin → angiotensin I → enzyme → angiotensin II (active) → aldosterone","Kidney reabsorbs Na⁺ + water → volume + osmolarity normal"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Potassium switches it on fast","lines":["K⁺ up by just 0.1 mEq/L → aldosterone → kidney excretes K⁺"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Cortisol — essential for life","lines":["Main glucocorticoid, essential for life","Stress response, metabolism, mood, immunity, Na⁺ balance","Needed for catecholamines to work + normal heart excitability"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"What glucocorticoids do (Box 53.1)","lines":["Prevent hypoglycemia: ↑ gluconeogenesis, ↓ tissue glucose use","Keep cardiac muscle responsive","↑ lipolysis, ↑ protein breakdown, degrade collagen + connective tissue","↑ mature neutrophils released","Antiinflammatory: fewer inflammatory cells reach injuries","Maintain behavior + cognition"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Cortisol's rhythm","lines":["Controlled by ACTH/CRH ← free cortisol, sleep-wake cycle, stress","Peaks in the morning, lowest ~12 h later; stress raises it"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Sex hormones","lines":["Small androgen + estrogen output","In women, adrenals = main androgen source"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Why","lines":["Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺","Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"A sympathetic ganglion that secretes","lines":["Sympathetic stimulation → epinephrine ~85% + norepinephrine ~15%","Not essential for life; drive fight-or-flight"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Receptors decide the effect (Table 53.2)","lines":["Subtypes: α1, α2 · β1, β2, β3; the target tissue's receptor decides the response","NE → mainly alpha · epinephrine → mainly beta","Heart β1: ↑ rate + force · bronchioles β2: dilate","Vessels: α constrict, β2 dilate · pupils dilate (α)","GI: sphincters tighten (α), motility ↓ (β) · skin: sweating (α)","Liver (α): gluconeogenesis + glycogenolysis → glucose ↑ · fat (β): lipolysis","Pancreas: α ↓, β ↑ insulin + glucagon"]},{"note":"53-the-adrenal-glands-aldosterone-cortisol","title":"Why#2","lines":["Stress on the body = fast heart, wide pupils, sweating, slow gut, glucose ↑ (liver releases sugar)","Why sick or stressed people with diabetes run high; why beta blockers calm thyroid tachycardia (ch 55)"]}]},{"id":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Endocrine assessment — history and physical exam","blocks":[{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Age and sex","lines":["Age: diabetes, ovarian loss, ↓ thyroid more common later","Sex: thyroid problems more common in women"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Past history, menses and menopause","lines":["Past endocrine problems, symptoms, hospitalizations","Women: age at menarche, menopause yet?, still cycling, last period, change in flow, duration, frequency","Everyone: libido change, concerns about sexuality/reproduction"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Drugs and hormone misuse","lines":["Ask prescribed + OTC drugs (some alter endocrine function): cortisone, levothyroxine, oral contraceptives, antihypertensives","Misused androgens, thyroid hormone or steroids → serious multigland dysfunction → teach all patients"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Health equity","lines":["T2DM more common in some minority groups + lower income","Screen everyone, advocate early, teach all at risk"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Nutrition clues","lines":["Thirst → AVP deficiency/resistance · salt craving → adrenal hypofunction","Lasting hunger + thirst → diabetes · weight change without diet change → thyroid/hypothalamus","Iodine — body can't make it; ask which + how much (Box 53.3)","Dairy (cheese, milk, yogurt), eggs, iodized salt, saltwater fish (salmon, sardines, tuna), seaweed (kelp, nori), shellfish (scallops, shrimp), iodine multivitamins"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Family history and current symptoms","lines":["Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid","Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice","Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Inspection — general and head","lines":["Height, weight, fat distribution, muscle mass, hair","Face: prominent forehead/jaw, round puffy face, flat expression, exophthalmos"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Inspection — neck","lines":["Thyroid normally not visible (may rise on swallowing)","Visible thyroid → assess further · JVD → fluid overload"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Inspection — skin","lines":["Adrenal excess: fungal infections, slow healing, bruising, petechiae","Diabetes: skin infections, foot ulcers, slow healing","Dark skin over joints/scars (↑ ACTH, MSH) · vitiligo = primary adrenal hypofunction","Document location, color, size, distribution of changes vs last visit/baseline"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Inspection — body","lines":["Nails thick/brittle → thyroid · edema → fluid balance · chest size + symmetry","Truncal obesity + buffalo hump + purple striae → adrenocortical excess","Breasts (men too): size, symmetry, pigment, discharge · gynecomastia (↓ testosterone)","Hirsutism (woman's face, chest, midline abdomen) · scalp hair loss or texture change beyond aging","Genitals: pubic hair change in hypogonadism; ask about change in genital size"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Auscultation","lines":["Heart rate + rhythm baseline (dysrhythmias, dehydration)","Orthostatic BP + pulse","Enlarged thyroid → listen for a bruit (↑ blood flow)"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Which glands can be felt","lines":["Palpable: thyroid + testes","Done by the primary health care provider or the nurse (depends on setting) · testes: ch 61 + 64"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Palpating the thyroid","lines":["From behind, head slightly forward, fingers beside the trachea","Feel it rise when they swallow","Right lobe: head right, left fingers move trachea, right hand palpates (reverse for left)","Size, shape, symmetry, nodules, tenderness"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Action alert","lines":["Never palpate/press a (possibly) hyperthyroid gland → hormone burst → thyroid storm"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Mood, personality, behavior, self-image change → ask; ask family (with permission)","Appearance, infertility, impotence → invite feelings","Lifelong drugs → readiness to learn, ability, resources, referrals"]},{"note":"53-endocrine-assessment-history-and-physica","title":"Why","lines":["Exam = search for hormone fingerprints","Cortisol: round face, buffalo hump, striae, bruises · thyroid: eyes, nails, HR, visible neck","Aldosterone/ADH: edema, JVD, orthostatic drops","Hyperthyroid gland is full of stored hormone → never press it"]}]},{"id":"53-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"53-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Glands are ductless → hormones go into the blood → only target tissues with the receptor respond","Every problem = too much, too little, or poor receptor response","Toolkit for ch 55 (thyroid, parathyroid) and ch 56 (diabetes): who makes what, feedback, body signs, testing done right"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"What the glands keep steady","lines":["Regulate temperature, metabolism, growth, nutrition, elimination, fluid + electrolytes, reproduction","Core temp held near 98.6°F (37°C) whatever the weather","Serum Na⁺ 136–145 mEq/L (mmol/L) whether intake is 2 g or 12 g/day"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Lock and key","lines":["Hormones reach every tissue but act only on target tissues with a matching receptor","Each receptor fits one hormone (lock and key) → cell changes its activity"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Simple negative feedback","lines":["Glucose ↑ → insulin → cells take up glucose → glucose ↓ → insulin stops","\"Negative\" = the action opposes the change that triggered it"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Complex negative feedback (an axis)","lines":["Low cortisol → CRH (hypothalamus) → ACTH (anterior pituitary) → cortisol (adrenal cortex) → shuts off CRH + ACTH","Thyroid, same pattern: TRH → TSH → T3/T4; high T3/T4 switches TRH + TSH off"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Hypothalamus and pituitary","lines":["Hypothalamus → portal circulation → anterior pituitary (tiny amounts reach the rest of the body)","Posterior pituitary only stores hypothalamic ADH and oxytocin"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Three ways it goes wrong","lines":["Excess, deficiency, or poor receptor response","Normal ranges are narrow → one gland off can affect many systems"]},{"note":"53-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Every hormone sits in a feedback loop → one level means little alone","Wide normal ranges → stimulation or suppression shows if the gland responds","So timing + collection matter as much as which test"]}]},{"id":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Pituitary, thyroid, parathyroid and pancreas","blocks":[{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Hypothalamus","lines":["Makes CRH, GnRH, GHRH, TRH + somatostatin, PIH, MIH","Each stimulates or inhibits the anterior pituitary"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Anterior pituitary (adenohypophysis)","lines":["Tropic hormones target other glands; prolactin acts directly on tissue","ACTH (corticotropin) → adrenal cortex (+ its growth) · TSH (thyrotropin) → thyroid","FSH + LH → ovary/testis (estrogen, ovulation, progesterone · sperm, testosterone)","GH → growth (lipolysis, protein building, insulin antagonism)","Prolactin → breast development + milk (no known role in men) · MSH → pigmentation","Drugs, diet, lifestyle, disease alter pituitary release"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis)","lines":["ADH → kidney keeps water · oxytocin → uterus + breast","Made in hypothalamus, stored here · sits in the sella turcica"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Why","lines":["GH, cortisol, epinephrine, thyroid hormone all push glucose up","Insulin is the only hormone that lowers it → many causes of high glucose, few of low"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"What they are and make","lines":["Gonads = reproductive endocrine glands","Ovaries → estrogen + progesterone · testes → testosterone","Far more sex hormone than the adrenal cortex (but women's androgens come mainly from the adrenals)"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Switched on at puberty","lines":["Dormant until puberty","Then GnRH → FSH + LH (gonadotropins) → glands + external genitalia mature","Testes → testosterone · ovaries → estrogen → secondary sex characteristics","FSH: follicles, sperm · LH: ovulation, progesterone, testosterone (Table 53.3) · details ch 61"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Why#2","lines":["Same 3-step chain as thyroid + adrenal: GnRH → FSH/LH → estrogen, progesterone, testosterone","Problem can start in the gonad or higher up (pituitary, hypothalamus)","Clues: history (menarche, menses, menopause, libido) + exam (hair, breasts, genital size)"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Where and what","lines":["Anterior neck below the cricoid; 2 lobes + isthmus over the trachea","Follicular cells → T4 + T3 · parafollicular → calcitonin"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"T3 and T4 set the metabolic rate","lines":["↑ metabolism → ↑ O₂ use + ↑ heat","Mostly protein-bound; free hormone acts","In cells T4 → T3 (most active); ↓ by stress, starvation, dyes, drugs; ↑ by cold"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Control — the thyroid axis","lines":["TRH → TSH → T3 + T4","High T3/T4 → ↓ TRH + TSH · low → ↑ (negative feedback)","Cold + stress → ↑ TRH → ↑ TSH"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Making the hormone","lines":["Needs dietary protein + iodine","Thyroid traps iodide + tyrosine → T4/T3 → stored on thyroglobulin","In cells: bind DNA receptors → set basal metabolic rate"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"What thyroid hormones do (Box 53.2)","lines":["Set metabolic rate + body heat · insulin antagonists","Growth, skeleton, nervous system development · muscle tone, Ca²⁺ mobilization, RBCs","Heart rate, force, output · breathing + O₂ use · GI secretion","Protein, carb, fat metabolism; lipid turnover, cholesterol synthesis"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Calcitonin","lines":["Lowers Ca²⁺ + phosphate (less bone release) — opposite of PTH","High Ca²⁺ → ↑ calcitonin; low Ca²⁺ → ↓"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Why#3","lines":["Thyroid hormone = thermostat + accelerator","Too much → everything fast (heat intolerance, ↑ HR, diarrhea, weight loss)","Too little → everything slow (cold intolerance, ↓ HR, constipation, weight gain)"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Parathyroid hormone (PTH) raises calcium","lines":["4 glands behind the thyroid; PTH raises Ca²⁺","Bone (main Ca²⁺ store): releases Ca²⁺ + phosphorus · kidney: keeps Ca²⁺, dumps phosphorus, activates vitamin D · gut: ↑ absorption","Low Ca²⁺ → ↑ PTH; high Ca²⁺ → ↓ PTH"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Pancreas — two jobs","lines":["Exocrine: enzymes → duodenum","Endocrine islets: alpha → glucagon, beta → insulin, delta → somatostatin"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Glucagon, insulin, somatostatin","lines":["Glucagon ↑ glucose (released when glucose ↓) · insulin ↓ glucose (moves it into cells, stores fuel)","Somatostatin inhibits insulin, glucagon, gastrin, secretin"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Pineal and thymus","lines":["Pineal → melatonin in the dark → sleep-wake cycle","Thymus mainly childhood; also lymphatic"]},{"note":"53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre","title":"Why#4","lines":["PTH pulls Ca²⁺ into the blood (bone, kidney, gut) · calcitonin pushes it back into bone","Repeated fractures → think calcium first","Parathyroids injured in thyroid surgery → Ca²⁺ falls within hours (ch 55)"]}]},{"id":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Aging and COVID-19 — what changes in the glands","blocks":[{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"The general rule","lines":["Aging → less gland function + less hormone","Exception: prostate enlarges · ovaries decline faster (abrupt after hysterectomy)","Illness, diet, sleep, drugs blur normal vs abnormal → regular screening"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Less ADH","lines":["↓ ADH → dilute urine, can't concentrate → dehydration risk","Assess often · AP offer fluids at least every 2 h awake (unless restricted)"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Less estrogen","lines":["↓ estrogen → bone loss, thin dry skin, dry perineal tissue → ↑ cystitis","Teach weight-bearing exercise, protein, 2 L fluid/day (unless contraindicated), perineal care ≥ 2×/day, void after sex, lubricants","Protect skin integrity"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Less glucose tolerance","lines":["↓ glucose tolerance → weight above ideal, ↑ fasting/random glucose, slow healing, yeast infections, polydipsia + polyuria","Ask family history (obesity, T2DM) · teach exercise, healthy weight","Report: thirst, frequent urination, hunger, slow healing, yeast infections, blurred vision, fatigue","Suggest diabetes testing: persistent candidiasis, wound not healed in 2 weeks (leg/foot), lasting hunger + thirst, ↓ energy"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Slower general metabolism","lines":["↓ metabolism → cold intolerance, ↓ appetite, ↓ HR + BP → dress in layers","Disorder clues: lethargy, new constipation, slow thinking/speech, temp < 97°F (36°C), HR < 60"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Why","lines":["Normal aging looks like mild hypothyroidism + mild diabetes → compare to their baseline","Red flags: temp stays < 97°F, wound not healed in 2 weeks, unending thirst","Low ADH reserve turns dangerous fastest → drinks less → dehydrated quickly"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Pancreas","lines":["New hyperglycemia in people without diabetes (virus infects pancreatic cells)","Ketoacidosis in older non-White patients with T2DM"]},{"note":"53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g","title":"Thyroid and ovaries","lines":["Subacute thyroiditis → usually normal again within months","~1/3 reported menstrual changes (irregular, heavy, postmenopausal bleeding)"]}]},{"id":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Diagnostic tests and the nurse's job","blocks":[{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Which fluid","lines":["Blood, urine, saliva","Saliva works for steroids (cortisol, testosterone, progesterone, estradiol), not protein hormones (pituitary, thyroid)","Check the lab for tube, timing, handling"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Assays","lines":["Immunoassays + chromatography/mass spectrometry","Very sensitive; mass spec measures many hormones at once"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Provocative and suppression tests","lines":["Used when one level can't decide","Stimulation for suspected underactivity → no rise = hypofunction","Suppression for high levels → no fall = hyperfunction"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Blood draws done right (Box 53.5)","lines":["Check tube, timing, test drugs · catecholamines on ice, to lab now","IV line: flush/clear first; never from a double/triple lumen","Test drug on time (alarm at night)"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"24-hour urine (Box 53.5)","lines":["Better than one sample for pulsed hormones (adrenal)","Check lab rules · preservative in the container from the start","Start: void and discard, note the time","Collect all urine for exactly 24 h; end: void even without urge and add it","Keep the preservative (caustic — don't splash); cold if needed, not with food","2-, 4-, 8-, 12-h collections: same start + end rules","No new drugs, OTC included, without asking (interference)"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Glucose, A1C and genetic tests","lines":["Glucose + OGTT → diabetes","A1C = average glucose over 100–120 days","Genetic tests when levels too low or family history unknown"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Why","lines":["Most errors happen at the bedside: first void kept, test drug late, wrong lumen, catecholamines warm","Each changes the number → teaching is the intervention"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Imaging","lines":["Skull x-ray: sella turcica erosion = growth","MRI with contrast = best for pituitary","CT: adrenals, ovaries, pancreas · ultrasound: thyroid, parathyroids, ovaries, testes"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Thyroid scan","lines":["Swallow radioactive iodine pill → collects in thyroid → image 4–6 h later","Ask about pregnancy + breastfeeding first; follow nuclear medicine’s instructions"]},{"note":"53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job","title":"Fine needle aspiration","lines":["Ultrasound-guided FNA of a thyroid nodule → decides on surgery"]}]},{"id":"53-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"53-adpie","title":"¶ An assessment chapter has one aim for","lines":["Aim: which gland, and which direction (excess, deficiency, poor response)","Tests collected so results can be trusted; keep the patient safe during the work-up"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Age, sex, onset, past treatment","Energy, sleep, heat/cold, weight in 6 months, thirst + urine + nocturia, bowels","Menarche, menses, menopause, libido + sexual concerns","Prescribed + OTC drugs + misused hormones · iodine · family history (incl. growth problems)"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["Build + fat distribution; face, exophthalmos","Visible thyroid, JVD; skin (vitiligo, bruising, foot ulcers); nails; edema","Buffalo hump, striae, gynecomastia, hirsutism","HR/rhythm, orthostatics, thyroid bruit · palpate thyroid unless hyperthyroid suspected"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Psychosocial and learning","lines":["Coping, support, mood changes (ask family)","Feelings about appearance, fertility, sexuality","Readiness to learn, ability, resources for lifelong care"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Onset is often slow and insidious → patient may not notice","History asks about months → catches slow change (family often sees it first)"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Fluid and electrolyte imbalance","lines":["Potential dehydration (older adults) or overload","Na⁺, K⁺, Ca²⁺ imbalance (aldosterone, ADH, PTH, calcitonin)"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Glucose dysregulation","lines":["Potential hyperglycemia: aging, stress hormones, illness (COVID-19), steroids","Potential hypoglycemia if insulin unopposed"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Body image and knowledge","lines":["Disturbed body image (face, eyes, hair, weight, striae)","Need for teaching about exact test collection"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Fluid balance: stable weight, moist membranes, no orthostatic drop","Specimens right the first time · abnormal results reported promptly","Patient voices feelings about changes · explains hormone misuse dangers"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Testing","lines":["Confirm tube, timing, handling · ice catecholamines","Cleared single-lumen draws only · test drugs on time","Teach 24-hour urine: discard first, keep all, final void, preservative, cold","Report abnormal results + test complications to the provider"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Safety and comfort","lines":["No palpation of a possibly hyperthyroid gland","Orthostatic precautions","Older adults: fluids q2h awake, layers, skin + perineal care"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Teaching and support","lines":["Teach diabetes signs (thirst, urination, hunger, slow healing, yeast, blurred vision); suggest testing (non-healing wounds, yeast infections)","Teach hormone/steroid misuse dangers · encourage screening","Invite concerns · refer for cost · explain each test clearly"]},{"note":"53-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Weight, I&O, orthostatics stable","Specimen valid · abnormal values reported","Patient repeats collection steps, names misuse dangers, talks about changes"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"adrenal-assessment:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which finding suggests too much adrenocortical hormone?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Cortisol excess: round face, buffalo hump, truncal obesity, striae, bruising, high glucose, infections, poor healing.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Truncal obesity with a “buffalo hump” and reddish-purple striae","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Cortisol excess redistributes fat and breaks down connective tissue (striae, bruising)."},{"id":"o1","text":"Exophthalmos","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. A thyroid sign."},{"id":"o2","text":"Vitiligo","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Linked to primary adrenal hypofunction."},{"id":"o3","text":"Brittle nails and cold intolerance","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Thyroid signs."}]},{"id":"adrenal-assessment:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Aldosterone = Na⁺ in, water in, K⁺ out → excess: fluid retention + ↓ K⁺; lack: volume loss + ↑ K⁺; Cortisol = sugar up, inflammation down → excess: ↑ glucose, infections, poor healing","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"adrenal-assessment:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about family history and current symptoms?","answer":"Family: obesity, growth, diabetes, infertility, thyroid; Box 53.4: onset + speed, past treatment (worked?), effect on ADLs, energy, hot/cold, sleep, hair, face/body shape, eyes, voice; Diet + weight over 6 months, urine amount + nocturia, bowels, menses, libido","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Bowel obstruction: fluid loss, decompression and ischemia","cards":[{"title":"Why obstruction causes hypovolemia","text":"Contents pile up above the block · ↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention · Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen · → hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury"},{"title":"Temperature and vital signs","text":"Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F · > 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia · Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change"},{"title":"Priority problem","text":"Life-threatening complications from blocked flow"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Alvimopan: short-term, oral, for postoperative ileus.","task":"Why is alvimopan given after bowel surgery?","cue":"Opioids bind mu receptors in the gut wall → peristalsis slows (ileus) → alvimopan blocks those receptors but doesn’t cross into the brain → motility returns while analgesia continues.","rule":"Alvimopan: short-term, oral, for postoperative ileus.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"bowel-obstruction","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Bowel obstruction: fluid loss, decompression and ischemia","notes":["48-overview","48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","48-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why obstruction causes hypovolemia: Contents pile up above the block; ↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention; Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen; → hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury","Temperature and vital signs: Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F; > 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia; Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change","Priority problem: Life-threatening complications from blocked flow","Why: Colicky pain = bowel fighting the block; Constant + localized (or stops → tenderness) = ischemia or perforation; So analgesics may be held during workup — the pain pattern is the diagnosis"],"terms":["ileus","ischemia","nasogastric"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"48-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"48-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Bowel blocked, growing, injured or herniated (not primarily inflamed)","Exemplar: intestinal obstruction; also colorectal cancer + colostomy, IBS, trauma, hernias, hemorrhoids","Watch fluids + electrolytes and changes in pain","Teach fiber, fluids, no straining"]},{"note":"48-overview","title":"Why obstruction causes hypovolemia","lines":["Contents pile up above the block","↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention","Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen","→ hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury"]},{"note":"48-overview","title":"Acid-base by location","lines":["High block → lose acid → alkalosis","Mid → both lost","Low block → lose alkaline fluid → acidosis"]},{"note":"48-overview","title":"Strangulation and sepsis","lines":["Stagnant bacteria OK until blood flow fails","Strangulated/closed-loop (blocked at 2 points) → ischemia → endotoxins → septic shock","Blood loss + ↑ pressure → compartment syndrome"]},{"note":"48-overview","title":"From polyp to cancer","lines":["Adenocarcinoma from adenomatous polyps","Polyps: most benign, some turn malignant → removed at scope","> half in the rectosigmoid","Spread: wall, lymph, blood (liver most common), seeding"]},{"note":"48-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Colicky pain = bowel fighting the block","Constant + localized (or stops → tenderness) = ischemia or perforation","So analgesics may be held during workup — the pain pattern is the diagnosis"]}]},{"id":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Intestinal obstruction — the elimination exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Mechanical obstruction","lines":["Physical block: outside (adhesions), wall (strictures), lumen (tumor)","Also hernia, fecal impaction, intussusception (telescoping), volvulus (twist)","Most common site: ileum"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Nonmechanical (paralytic ileus)","lines":["Peristalsis stops (no physical block)","Post-op ileus (handling bowel); hypokalemia predisposes","Peritonitis; mesenteric ischemia → gangrene, sepsis"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Who and prevention","lines":["Older adults, after bowel surgery, tumors","Prevent: fluids, exercise, fiber, screening"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"History","lines":["Vomit color; pain pattern — pain that stops → tenderness = perforation?","Flatus + last BM; hiccups","Suspected → NPO + call provider"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Temperature and vital signs","lines":["Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F","> 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia","Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Small-bowel obstruction","lines":["Cramping comes and goes; constant if strangulated","Vomiting profuse if high; foul orange-brown if low ileal","Obstipation (complete) · diarrhea (partial)","Severe fluid + electrolyte imbalance; ± metabolic alkalosis"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Large-bowel obstruction","lines":["Mild colicky lower pain, lower distention","Little or no vomiting","Ribbon stools if partial; blood with cancer/diverticulitis","No major fluid/electrolyte imbalance; ± metabolic acidosis"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"The abdomen","lines":["Distention; visible peristalsis","Mechanical: borborygmi above the block → then silent","Tense fluid-filled loop → closed-loop strangulation","Ileus: constant diffuse pain, sounds ↓ then absent"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["WBC normal unless strangulated/gangrene","↑ Hgb, Hct, BUN (dehydration) · ↓ Na⁺, Cl⁻, K⁺","CT/MRI first; no scope if perforation/complete block suspected"]},{"note":"48-intestinal-obstruction-the-elimination-e","title":"Priority problem","lines":["Life-threatening complications from blocked flow"]}]},{"id":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Caring for obstruction — the NG tube, fluids and surgery","blocks":[{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"When","lines":["Partial, no strangulation; ileus (responds well); terminal illness","Impaction → disimpact + enemas · intussusception → barium enema"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"The NG tube","lines":["NPO + Salem sump to low continuous suction; vent protects mucosa","q4h: placement, patency, output, nasal skin","Bowel sounds with suction OFF; abdomen (sounds, distention, flatus) ≥ 2×/day","↓ output + nausea/distention → blocked or moved tube","Irrigate 30 mL NS q4h as ordered; mouth + nares care"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Fluids and electrolytes","lines":["NS/LR 2–4 L + K⁺; watch overload in older/cardiac/kidney patients","Daily weight = best fluid measure; lung sounds, I&O","VS, UO, turgor q2–4h; PN if prolonged NPO"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Comfort","lines":["Mouth care (AP can give it); ice chips only per protocol, no surgery planned","Colicky → constant pain = report","Analgesics may be held in workup; morphine slows the gut","Semi-Fowler, reposition often","Food/fluids worsen pain; pain milder with ileus"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Drugs","lines":["Alvimopan for post-op ileus (gut-only opioid blocker)","Antibiotics if strangulation suspected","Metoclopramide for partial/ileus"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"When and what","lines":["Complete → surgery; strangulation → always surgery","Laparotomy: lyse adhesions, resection ± colostomy, embolectomy, colectomy","Laparoscopic usual: faster, often no NG"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"After open surgery","lines":["NG until peristalsis returns","Clamp trial with clear liquids; vomit → resume suction","No nausea while clamped → remove"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Prevent fecal impaction (older adults)","lines":["Fiber (cost-aware), water, daily walks","No routine laxatives (→ atonic colon)","Warm drinks, prune juice, bulk formers, softeners"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Teaching","lines":["Report pain, distention, nausea, vomiting","Opioids → docusate + senna","Outcome: relieved, no complications, usual BMs"]},{"note":"48-caring-for-obstruction-the-ng-tube-fluid","title":"Why","lines":["Suction gurgles → you \"hear\" peristalsis that isn't there → turn it off to listen","Clamp trial proves the bowel moves contents on its own"]}]},{"id":"48-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"48-adpie","title":"¶ Every patient in this chapter is watched","lines":["Watch: fluid loss, pain that changes, failing bowel blood supply","Teach: fiber, fluids, no straining, screening"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Past surgery; vomit color; last BM + flatus","Stool shape, blood; weight loss","Family CRC/FAP/Lynch; screening","Fiber, fluids, laxatives; injury mechanism; IBS triggers"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["VS + temp > 100°F?","Distention, bowel sounds (suction off)","Pain character, rigidity","Cullen, Turner, Kehr; hernia lying/standing","Stoma color/size/skin; daily weight"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Labs and tests","lines":["Hgb/Hct, BUN, Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, WBC","FIT/FOBT, CEA, CT/MRI, FAST, colonoscopy"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Obstruction: hypovolemia, electrolytes, strangulation, sepsis","CRC surgery: infection, stoma problems, body image, sexuality","Trauma: hemorrhage/shock · hernia: strangulation · IBS: pain, anxiety"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Obstruction resolved, no complications","Stable weight, UO ≥ 30 mL/hr","Stoma pink, moist; patient manages pouch","Perineal wound heals; IBS controlled; screening on time"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Obstruction","lines":["NPO + NG suction (q4h checks; listen with suction off)","IV NS/LR + K⁺; daily weight","Semi-Fowler; alvimopan (post-op ileus)","Report constant pain, fever, tachycardia, rigidity"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Colorectal surgery and ostomy","lines":["CWOCN marks site","Stoma checks; empty ⅓–½ full; measure weekly","Sealant; no aspirin in pouch","Perineal: sitz baths, side-lying, no donut","No lifting/straining; UOAA"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"IBS, trauma, hernia, hemorrhoids","lines":["IBS: fiber + water, stress care, alosetron cautions","Trauma: FAST, Hgb/Hct, transfusion","Hernia: no forced reduction; don't cough, stand to void","Hemorrhoids: sitz baths, softeners; be near for first BM"]},{"note":"48-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Bowel sounds + flatus back; diet tolerated","Weight, lytes, UO stable; no fever or rigidity","Healthy stoma; demonstrates pouch care","Perineal wound improving; IBS flares ↓; knows screening schedule"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"bowel-obstruction:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Why is alvimopan given after bowel surgery?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Alvimopan: short-term, oral, for postoperative ileus.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"It blocks opioid receptors in the gut to speed return of motility without reducing pain relief","correct":true,"why":"Correct. It is a peripherally acting mu-opioid antagonist — it works only in the gut."},{"id":"o1","text":"It is an opioid that treats incision pain","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. It is an opioid antagonist."},{"id":"o2","text":"It prevents nausea by acting in the brain","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. It doesn’t act centrally."},{"id":"o3","text":"It softens stool","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not a stool softener."}]},{"id":"bowel-obstruction:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why obstruction causes hypovolemia?","answer":"Contents pile up above the block; ↑ peristalsis → ↑ secretions → ↑ distention; Leaky swollen wall → plasma into peritoneum + fluid trapped in lumen; → hypovolemia, electrolyte loss; severe → acute kidney injury","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"bowel-obstruction:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about temperature and vital signs?","answer":"Uncomplicated: temp ≤ 100°F; > 100°F + rising pulse → strangulation / peritonitis / ischemia; Report: fever, tachycardia, hypotension, ↑ pain, rigidity, skin color change","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Colorectal cancer and colostomy: treatment and adapting care","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia · Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood"},{"title":"Signs","text":"Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency · Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced · Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red · Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids"},{"title":"Inherited syndromes","text":"FAP (APC): thousands of polyps, ~100% cancer → colectomy by 20 · Lynch/HNPCC: 80% risk by ~45; endometrial, ovarian, others · Teach genetic testing for families"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"FAP: colectomy by ~20. Lynch (HNPCC): 80% lifetime risk, plus endometrial and other cancers. Teach families about genetic testing.","task":"A 19-year-old has familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP). What does the nurse expect?","cue":"The APC tumor-suppressor gene is mutated → colonic cells divide unchecked → thousands of adenomatous polyps over 10–15 years → one becomes malignant.","rule":"FAP: colectomy by ~20. Lynch (HNPCC): 80% lifetime risk, plus endometrial and other cancers. Teach families about genetic testing.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"colorectal-ostomy","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Colorectal cancer and colostomy: treatment and adapting care","notes":["48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia; Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood","Signs: Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency; Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced; Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red; Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids","Inherited syndromes: FAP (APC): thousands of polyps, ~100% cancer → colectomy by 20; Lynch/HNPCC: 80% risk by ~45; endometrial, ovarian, others; Teach genetic testing for families","Why: Stoma has no pain nerves → can't feel a tight wafer → measure + cut slightly larger; Color = its blood supply → dark/dry = bowel dying → report now"],"terms":["colostomy","adenocarcinoma","stoma"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Colorectal cancer — risk, screening and diagnosis","blocks":[{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"How common","lines":["4th most common, 2nd cancer killer; curable early"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Age > 50; family history (1st-degree → 3–4×); rising under 50","FAP, Crohn's, UC; HPV, S. bovis","Smoking, obesity, inactivity, alcohol","Red-meat fat, low-fiber refined carbs"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Inherited syndromes","lines":["FAP (APC): thousands of polyps, ~100% cancer → colectomy by 20","Lynch/HNPCC: 80% risk by ~45; endometrial, ovarian, others","Teach genetic testing for families"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Healthy People 2030 (Box 48.3)","lines":["Healthy People 2030: ↓ CRC death rate, ↑ adults screened"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Screening schedule","lines":["Discuss at 40; screen from 45","FOBT/FIT yearly · stool DNA q3y · colonoscopy q10y · sigmoidoscopy/CT colonography q5y","History → earlier + more often","75–85 optional, > 85 not recommended (surgery withheld if found)","Equal access = justice","Lowest screening: uninsured, < high school"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Health equity","lines":["Lowest survival: Black adults (esp. < 50)","30–40% higher mortality in poor regions → teach, find resources"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Lifestyle","lines":["↓ fat, refined carbs; ↑ fiber, cruciferous veg","Baked/broiled; healthy weight","No smoking, less alcohol, exercise"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Signs","lines":["Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency","Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced","Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red","Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"By location","lines":["Transverse/descending: obstruction, gas pains","Rectosigmoid: hematochezia, narrow stools, straining","Right: big before symptoms; anemia, RLQ mass, dark blood"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Stool blood tests","lines":["Prep depends on the test type (avoids false positives)","May need: no aspirin, vitamin C, iron, red meat 48 h; stop NSAIDs/steroids","Ch 45 guaiac: NSAIDs 7 d, red meat/vit C 3 d; FIT: no prep","FOBT: 2–3 samples, 3 days in a row","Negative ≠ no cancer"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Other tests","lines":["CEA < 5: monitor + recurrence (not diagnosis)","Virtual: no biopsy","Colonoscopy = definitive"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-risk-screening-and-dia","title":"Why","lines":["Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia","Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood"]}]},{"id":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Colorectal cancer — treatment, surgery and colostomy care","blocks":[{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"The plan","lines":["Remove the tumor; TNM staging","Surgery first; adjuvant therapy around it"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Radiation","lines":["Rectal cancer: radiation usual · colon: mostly palliative","Watch diarrhea, fatigue"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Chemotherapy and targeted drugs","lines":["Stage II–III: 5-FU/leucovorin, capecitabine, irinotecan, oxaliplatin","Side effects: diarrhea, mucositis, leukopenia, neuropathy","Bevacizumab (VEGF → ↓ tumor blood supply) · cetuximab, panitumumab (EGFR)","Liver mets: intrahepatic arterial 5-FU"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Surgery","lines":["Clear margins + nodes (node count = prognosis)","Resection, colostomy, colectomy/ileostomy","AP resection (rectal): sigmoid + rectum + anus removed → permanent colostomy","Laparoscopic common"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Preparing the patient","lines":["Colostomy possible — tell them","CWOCN marks the site: visible to patient, avoid folds, scars, bones, belt line, in rectus muscle","Assess sensory limits, support, cost of supplies"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Low rectal surgery","lines":["AP resection → risk of sexual dysfunction + urinary incontinence","Discuss before consent; teach PCA, TCDB"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Bowel prep and antibiotics","lines":["Bowel prep controversial; dehydration in older adults","Antibiotic dose before incision","NG after open surgery only","Open: PCA 24–36 h; liquids → solids after NG out","Lap: solids soon, walk early, home 1–2 days"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Types","lines":["Loop (transverse, temporary, rod — don't dislodge)","End (descending/sigmoid, permanent)","Double-barrel: proximal = stool, distal = mucous fistula","Site: ascending = right tumor · descending = left · sigmoid = rectal · transverse = emergency (quick)"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Stoma assessment","lines":["Healthy: red-pink, moist, ~2 cm","Early edema + slight bleeding OK (6–8 wk)","Report now: purple/black/dry (necrosis), heavy bleeding, separation from skin"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Output","lines":["Works in 2–3 days","Empty at 1/3–1/2 full","Ascending liquid · transverse pasty · descending formed","Ascending: liquid → pouch on at all times"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Peristomal skin and fit","lines":["Watch dermatitis (stool), stripping (adhesive), candida","Retracted/flush stoma → convex + belt","Shrinks 6–8 weeks → measure weekly","Opening ⅛–1/16 inch larger than stoma"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Changing the pouch","lines":["Shave outward; mild soap — no moisturizing soap","Alcohol-free sealant, let dry","Powder/paste for raw skin; antifungal for candida"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Gas and odor","lines":["Odor = leak usually; filters, deodorizers, mint","No food forbidden; vegetables → gas, odor","No aspirin in the pouch (ulcerates stoma)","Limit gassy foods, carbonation; 4–6 wk to learn","Sigmoid → irrigation possible"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Perineal wound","lines":["2 JP drains; serosanguineous 1–2 months; heals 6–8 months","Phantom rectal sensations possible","Sitz baths 10–20 min, 3–4×/day; absorbent pad, jockey briefs","Side-lying; foam/pillow — no donut","I&O incl. drainage; watch infection"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Going home","lines":["No lifting/straining","Open: no driving 4–6 wk · lap: back in 1–2 wk","Softeners; report pain, cramping, vomiting","Supplies not hot or cold"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Teaching the ostomy","lines":["Practice handling + applying the pouch","Normal stoma; report purple/black stoma, heavy bleeding, separation, retraction, prolapse, skin breakdown","Measuring, skin care, limit gassy foods + carbonation, work/travel/sex","Home: stoma color/size/prolapse/stenosis, skin, fit, coping"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Feelings and resources","lines":["Fears: rejection, body image, sexuality","Invite feelings; plan for leaks, odor, intimacy","UOAA visitor, ACS, home health, hospice"]},{"note":"48-colorectal-cancer-treatment-surgery-and","title":"Why","lines":["Stoma has no pain nerves → can't feel a tight wafer → measure + cut slightly larger","Color = its blood supply → dark/dry = bowel dying → report now"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"colorectal-ostomy:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A 19-year-old has familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP). What does the nurse expect?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"FAP: colectomy by ~20. Lynch (HNPCC): 80% lifetime risk, plus endometrial and other cancers. Teach families about genetic testing.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"A colectomy is usually needed by about age 20","correct":true,"why":"Correct. FAP produces thousands of polyps with nearly 100% cancer risk, so most need colectomy (with ileostomy or ileoanal pull-through) by 20."},{"id":"o1","text":"Colonoscopy starting at age 45","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Age 45 is for average-risk adults."},{"id":"o2","text":"Yearly FIT testing is enough","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Stool tests are for average risk."},{"id":"o3","text":"No increased cancer risk","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Risk is nearly 100%."}]},{"id":"colorectal-ostomy:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Right colon: liquid stool flows around the mass → first clue is anemia; Left/rectum: formed stool → narrow stools, obstruction, bright red blood","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"colorectal-ostomy:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs?","answer":"Rectal bleeding, anemia, change in stool shape/consistency; Fatigue, fullness, weight loss = advanced; Blood: occult, mahogany, or bright red; Other lower GI bleeders: UC, polyps, diverticula, hemorrhoids","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"IBS: symptom pattern and safe treatment","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon) · 10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2× · IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M"},{"title":"Assess","text":"Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well · Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary · LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones"},{"title":"Self-management","text":"30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses water, regular meals, chew slowly · Exercise; stress reduction · Probiotics, peppermint oil"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Hydrogen breath test: NPO (water OK) 12 h → baseline → lactulose → samples every 20 min for 1–2 h.","task":"Which instruction prepares a client for a hydrogen breath test?","cue":"Bacteria fermenting carbohydrate make hydrogen → it is absorbed and exhaled → overgrowth or malabsorption raises breath hydrogen after the lactulose drink; recent food would confuse the baseline.","rule":"Hydrogen breath test: NPO (water OK) 12 h → baseline → lactulose → samples every 20 min for 1–2 h.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"ibs","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"IBS: symptom pattern and safe treatment","notes":["48-irritable-bowel-syndrome"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon); 10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2×; IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M","Assess: Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well; Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary; LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones","Self-management: 30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses water, regular meals, chew slowly; Exercise; stress reduction; Probiotics, peppermint oil","Why: Fiber (psyllium) holds water → softens hard stool and firms loose stool; Same advice for both: fiber + water"],"terms":["irritable bowel syndrome","linaclotide","alosetron"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Irritable bowel syndrome","blocks":[{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"What it is","lines":["Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon)","10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2×","IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Causes","lines":["Triggers: caffeine, carbonation, dairy; bacterial overgrowth","Immune/genetic, hormones, stress","Anxiety, depression (fear of no bathroom → stays home)"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Assess","lines":["Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well","Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary","LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Tests","lines":["Routine labs normal","Hydrogen breath test: NPO 12 h (water OK), lactulose, samples q20 min × 1–2 h"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Self-management","lines":["30–40 g fiber, 8–10 glasses water, regular meals, chew slowly","Exercise; stress reduction","Probiotics, peppermint oil"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Drugs for IBS-C","lines":["Psyllium with water at meals","Lubiprostone (women): with food + water","Linaclotide: 30 min before breakfast"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Drugs for IBS-D and pain","lines":["Loperamide, psyllium","Alosetron: women, last resort → ischemic colitis — stop + report constipation, bloody diarrhea","Rifaximin (gut-only antibiotic)","Amitriptyline 30–45 min before meals for pain"]},{"note":"48-irritable-bowel-syndrome","title":"Why","lines":["Fiber (psyllium) holds water → softens hard stool and firms loose stool","Same advice for both: fiber + water"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"ibs:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which instruction prepares a client for a hydrogen breath test?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Hydrogen breath test: NPO (water OK) 12 h → baseline → lactulose → samples every 20 min for 1–2 h.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Nothing to eat for at least 12 hours; water is allowed","correct":true,"why":"Correct. After fasting, the client blows a baseline sample, drinks lactulose, then gives samples every 20 minutes for 1–2 hours."},{"id":"o1","text":"Drink only clear liquids for 3 days","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not the prep."},{"id":"o2","text":"Stop all fiber for a week","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not required."},{"id":"o3","text":"Take a laxative the night before","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not part of the prep."}]},{"id":"ibs:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Functional (no damage): diarrhea, constipation or both + pain, bloating (spastic/mucous/nervous colon); 10–15%; starts in young adults; women 2×; IBS-D, IBS-C, IBS-M","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"ibs:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about assess?","answer":"Bowel pattern, mucus; no weight loss; looks well; Triggers: caffeine, sorbitol, fructose, dairy; food diary; LLQ pain; hard small stools → softer ones","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids: recognize danger","cards":[{"title":"Mechanisms","text":"Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot) · Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder"},{"title":"Prevention and signs","text":"Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining · Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching"},{"title":"Diagnose and treat","text":"FAST → free fluid · CT later · Unstable → laparotomy · Stable → ICU: VS, Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics (hollow viscus)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"After hemorrhoidectomy: softeners, sitz baths 3–4×/day, analgesics, mild laxative if no BM by day 3 — and someone nearby for the first BM.","task":"What should the nurse plan for a client’s first bowel movement after a hemorrhoidectomy?","cue":"Pain and straining stimulate the vagus nerve → heart rate and BP drop → less blood to the brain → light-headedness and syncope on the toilet.","rule":"After hemorrhoidectomy: softeners, sitz baths 3–4×/day, analgesics, mild laxative if no BM by day 3 — and someone nearby for the first BM.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"trauma-hernia","ch":"48","chapters":["48"],"title":"Abdominal trauma, hernias and hemorrhoids: recognize danger","notes":["48-abdominal-trauma","48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 48; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Mechanisms: Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot); Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder","Prevention and signs: Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining; Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching","Diagnose and treat: FAST → free fluid · CT later; Unstable → laparotomy; Stable → ICU: VS, Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics (hollow viscus)","Why: Blood under the diaphragm irritates the phrenic nerve (C3–C5, same as shoulder); Brain reads it as left shoulder pain → think spleen"],"terms":["strangulation","hemorrhoid","hemorrhage"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Abdominal trauma","blocks":[{"note":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Mechanisms","lines":["Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot)","Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder"]},{"note":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Complications","lines":["Hemorrhage → hypovolemic shock (liver/spleen)","Perforation → peritonitis → septic shock","Compartment syndrome: edema → ↑ pressure → organ ischemia → decompress"]},{"note":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Assess","lines":["Primary survey first; mechanism","Cullen (umbilicus) · Turner (flank) · Kehr (left shoulder → spleen)","Distention, ↓ bowel sounds; worsening pain → act"]},{"note":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Diagnose and treat","lines":["FAST → free fluid · CT later","Unstable → laparotomy","Stable → ICU: VS, Hgb/Hct, transfusion, antibiotics (hollow viscus)"]},{"note":"48-abdominal-trauma","title":"Why","lines":["Blood under the diaphragm irritates the phrenic nerve (C3–C5, same as shoulder)","Brain reads it as left shoulder pain → think spleen"]}]},{"id":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Hernias and hemorrhoids","blocks":[{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"What and why","lines":["Weak wall + ↑ pressure (obesity, pregnancy, lifting)"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Types","lines":["Indirect inguinal = most common, men, into scrotum","Direct (older) · femoral · umbilical (obesity) · incisional"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Reducible, incarcerated, strangulated","lines":["Reducible → goes back","Incarcerated → surgery evaluation now","Strangulated → no blood supply: pain, vomiting, fever, tachycardia"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Assess","lines":["Inspect lying + standing; provider: strain/cough","Absent bowel sounds → emergency","Never force reduction"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Treatment","lines":["Truss after reduction, on waking; skin care","MIIHR preferred; hernioplasty = mesh","NPO = most important pre-op teaching; ride + adult at home"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"After repair (Box 48.8)","lines":["Home in 3–5 h; VS for bleeding","Deep breathe, don't cough; walk early; ice","Scrotal support + elevation","Stand to void; UO < 30 mL/hr → report; 1500–2500 mL/day","Soreness OK, severe pain not; no driving on opioids; stool softener","Report fever, chills, redness, heat, drainage, separation, ↑ pain","≤ 10 lb lifting; no straining for weeks; work in 1–2 wk"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"What and why#2","lines":["↑ abdominal pressure → veins distend → prolapse","Internal (hidden) vs external (visible)","Worse: pregnancy, straining, obesity, sitting, low fluids"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Prevention and signs","lines":["Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining","Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Conservative care","lines":["Cold packs, tepid sitz baths 3–4×/day","Lidocaine; dibucaine short term only; hydrocortisone","Moist tissues, dab","Fiber + fluids, docusate, no stimulant laxatives, no long sitting","Better in 3–5 days"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Hemorrhoidectomy","lines":["Complications: pain, bleeding, infection, abscess","Sitz baths; softeners before + after; laxative if no BM by day 3","First BM very painful → someone nearby (vasovagal syncope)"]},{"note":"48-hernias-and-hemorrhoids","title":"Why","lines":["Both come from intraabdominal pressure","Same teaching: no constipation, no straining, careful lifting, healthy weight","After surgery: no coughing, lifting, straining on the repair"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"trauma-hernia:case","kind":"choice","scene":"What should the nurse plan for a client’s first bowel movement after a hemorrhoidectomy?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"After hemorrhoidectomy: softeners, sitz baths 3–4×/day, analgesics, mild laxative if no BM by day 3 — and someone nearby for the first BM.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Stay with or near the client because of possible fainting","correct":true,"why":"Correct. The first bowel movement can be very painful; a vasovagal response can cause light-headedness, sweating and syncope."},{"id":"o1","text":"Give a strong stimulant laxative the evening of surgery","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Stool softeners are used; habit-forming stimulant laxatives are avoided."},{"id":"o2","text":"Have the client sit on a rubber donut afterward","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Sitz baths and moist heat are used for comfort."},{"id":"o3","text":"Withhold pain medication so the client can bear down","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Give analgesics and softeners; straining is avoided."}]},{"id":"trauma-hernia:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about mechanisms?","answer":"Blunt (impact, deceleration) · penetrating (stab, gunshot); Liver, spleen, bowel, bladder","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"trauma-hernia:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about prevention and signs?","answer":"Prevent constipation: fiber, water (unless heart/kidney limits), no straining; Bright red blood on tissue, prolapse, pain (thrombosis), itching","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Acute inflammation: appendicitis, peritonitis and diverticulitis","cards":[{"title":"Why peritonitis empties the circulation","text":"Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI · Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel · Bacteria in blood → septic shock · Distention + pain → breathing compromise"},{"title":"Cardinal signs","text":"Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention · Localized → one area · generalized → widespread"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"1. Acute pain · 2. Fluid volume shift (into interstitial/peritoneal space)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Pain then vomiting → think appendicitis. Vomiting then cramps and diarrhea → think gastroenteritis.","task":"A client reports abdominal pain that began 12 hours ago, followed by nausea and vomiting. Which condition does this sequence suggest?","cue":"A blocked appendix distends and hurts first → the visceral pain reflexively triggers nausea and vomiting. In gastroenteritis the infected lining causes vomiting first.","rule":"Pain then vomiting → think appendicitis. Vomiting then cramps and diarrhea → think gastroenteritis.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"acute-abdomen","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Acute inflammation: appendicitis, peritonitis and diverticulitis","notes":["49-overview","49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","49-appendicitis","49-diverticular-disease","49-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why peritonitis empties the circulation: Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI; Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel; Bacteria in blood → septic shock; Distention + pain → breathing compromise","Cardinal signs: Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention; Localized → one area · generalized → widespread","Priority problems: 1. Acute pain; 2. Fluid volume shift (into interstitial/peritoneal space)","Why the appendix perforates: Lumen blocked by a fecalith → pressure ↑ → blood flow ↓; Slow → abscess · fast → peritonitis; Gangrene/sepsis 24–36 hr; perforation risk ↑↑ after 48 hr"],"terms":["peritonitis","appendicitis","diverticulitis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"49-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"49-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Bowel inflamed or infected","Acute: peritonitis (infection exemplar), appendicitis, gastroenteritis","Chronic: ulcerative colitis (inflammation exemplar), Crohn's, diverticular disease; also celiac, parasites","Watch fluid, nutrition, electrolytes, skin; pain pattern signals peritonitis"]},{"note":"49-overview","title":"Why peritonitis empties the circulation","lines":["Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI","Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel","Bacteria in blood → septic shock","Distention + pain → breathing compromise"]},{"note":"49-overview","title":"Why the appendix perforates","lines":["Lumen blocked by a fecalith → pressure ↑ → blood flow ↓","Slow → abscess · fast → peritonitis","Gangrene/sepsis 24–36 hr; perforation risk ↑↑ after 48 hr"]},{"note":"49-overview","title":"Lining vs all layers","lines":["UC = lining: red, swollen, bleeds, ulcers → bloody stools + mucus","Crohn's = all layers: strictures, cobblestone, fistulas, malabsorption","TNF-alpha + interleukins drive both → anti-TNF drugs"]},{"note":"49-overview","title":"Why diverticula form and get infected","lines":["Low fiber → hard stool → high pressure → pouches at vessel weak points (sigmoid)","Trapped food/bacteria → ↓ blood supply → diverticulitis","→ perforation, abscess, peritonitis, bleeding"]},{"note":"49-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Inflammation = leaky vessels on purpose (WBCs wall off bacteria)","Walling off fails → same leak → third spacing → shock with no visible loss","So treat peritonitis as a fluid problem too: hourly I&O, daily weight, IV fluids + antibiotics"]}]},{"id":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Peritonitis — the infection exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"The peritoneum","lines":["Normal: ~50 mL sterile fluid (no friction)","Peritonitis = acute inflammation + infection of the peritoneum","Post-op mortality up to 50%"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"How contamination gets in","lines":["Perforation (appendix, diverticulum, ulcer)","Penetrating wound, gangrenous gallbladder/bowel, obstruction, genital tract","Surgery leaks, tumors, CAPD"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Organisms and chemicals","lines":["E. coli, Strep, Staph, pneumococci, gonococci","Chemical: bile, pancreatic enzymes, gastric acid","Most common: young adults (appendicitis), older adults"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Walled off or spread","lines":["First: wall off (vessel dilation → WBCs → phagocytosis)","Fails → diffuse infection → third spacing, ileus, sepsis"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"History","lines":["Pain: character, location, localized vs generalized","Low-grade fever or spikes"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Key features","lines":["Rigid, boardlike abdomen (classic)","Pain (may refer to shoulder/chest), distention, N/V","↓ bowel sounds, no flatus, rebound tenderness","High fever, tachycardia, poor turgor, ↓ UO, hiccups"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Cardinal signs","lines":["Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention","Localized → one area · generalized → widespread"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"How the patient looks","lines":["Lies still, knees flexed, guarded","Pain with cough/movement","Progressive distention; bowel sounds disappear"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Feelings","lines":["Fear, anxiety → calm presence, let them talk, nonjudgmental listening"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Labs","lines":["WBC ~20,000 (normal 5000–10,000), ↑ neutrophils","Blood cultures → organism + antibiotic","BUN, creatinine, Hgb, Hct; SpO₂, ABGs"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Imaging","lines":["X-ray free air/fluid = perforation","Ultrasound or CT"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Acute pain","2. Fluid volume shift (into interstitial/peritoneal space)"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Watch for septic shock","lines":["Hospitalized; critical care if complications extensive","VS often:↓ BP, ↓ pulse pressure, ↑ HR, fever, skin change, ↑ RR","Mental status (confusion, ↓ LOC)","Goal pain 2–3/10"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Infection control","lines":["Handwashing + strict asepsis (wounds, drains, catheter)","Report drainage changes now","Broad-spectrum antibiotics; O₂ per SpO₂"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Fluid balance","lines":["IV fluids + broad-spectrum antibiotics immediately","Daily weight (same scale, AM), careful I&O","NG if open laparotomy expected","Irrigation: compare in vs out","After rupture/surgery: priority assessment = fluid + electrolytes"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Surgery","lines":["Laparotomy or laparoscopy: control contamination, drain","Appendectomy; colon resection ± colostomy","Peritoneum irrigated with antibiotics; drains","Open wound → 2nd/3rd intention; sterile irrigation"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Right after surgery (Action Alert)","lines":["Hourly: LOC, VS, respiratory status, I&O","Semi-Fowler: drainage to lower abdomen + lung expansion","Plus routine post-op: O₂ until awake, incentive spirometry, SCDs","NPO + NG → IV fluids + IV antibiotics continue"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["After rupture/surgery the fluid shift is the first danger → priority = fluid + electrolytes","Semi-Fowler: gravity keeps infected fluid low, away from the diaphragm and lungs"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"How long","lines":["Drained abscess → home in days","Others: ventilator, dialysis, transitional care","Longer recovery (multisystem)"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Report right away","lines":["Foul drainage; incision swelling, redness, warmth, bleeding","Temp > 101°F (38.3°C)","Abdominal pain; dehiscence or ileus"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Wound and help at home","lines":["Open wound: dressings + irrigation (catheter-tip syringe)","Home care nurse; aide or skilled facility for ADLs"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Drugs and activity","lines":["Opioid + acetaminophen → docusate + laxative","No extra acetaminophen (liver)","≤ 5–10 lb for 6 weeks (open); laparoscopic → 1–2 weeks"]},{"note":"49-peritonitis-the-infection-exemplar","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain relieved/controlled; fluid + electrolyte balance"]}]},{"id":"49-appendicitis","title":"Appendicitis","blocks":[{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"What it is","lines":["Inflamed appendix (off the cecum, below the ileocecal valve)","Peaks 15–19 years"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Why it happens","lines":["Fecalith blocks lumen → pressure ↑, blood flow ↓","Slow → abscess · fast → peritonitis","Gangrene/sepsis 24–36 hr; perforation risk ↑ after 48 hr","Perforated: temp > 101°F + ↑ pulse"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"The order of symptoms","lines":["Pain → then N/V = appendicitis","N/V → then pain = gastroenteritis","Starts periumbilical/epigastric; anorexia"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Where the pain moves","lines":["Shifts to RLQ — McBurney point (midway iliac crest ↔ umbilicus; Fig. 49.1)","Worse with cough/movement, better bending right hip/knees → perforation","Pain eases, then returns diffuse + fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP → perforated (NGN 49.1)","Rigidity, guarding, rebound tenderness"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["WBC 10,000–18,000 + shift to the left","> 20,000 → perforation?","Ultrasound (enlarged); CT if recurrent (fecaloma)"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Before surgery","lines":["Hospitalize; NPO","IV fluids; semi-Fowler (drainage stays low)","Confirmed + scheduled → opioids + antibiotics"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Never","lines":["No laxatives or enemas (perforation)","No heat (↑ blood flow → ↑ inflammation → rupture)"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Surgery","lines":["Appendectomy ASAP","Laparoscopic: home same day, activity in 1–2 weeks","Unclear + high risk → exploratory laparotomy"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"If peritonitis or abscess is found","lines":["Drains ± NG tube; IV antibiotics + opioids","Out of bed that evening (prevent atelectasis)","Stay 3–5 days; activity 4–6 weeks"]},{"note":"49-appendicitis","title":"Why","lines":["Blocked appendix = closed tube, wall already starved of blood","Laxative/enema → more contents + motility; heat → more blood + swelling","Both raise pressure → rupture → peritonitis"]}]},{"id":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Diverticular disease","blocks":[{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Terms","lines":["Diverticula = mucosal pouches through the wall (sigmoid)","-osis = pouches · -itis = inflamed/infected"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Why they form","lines":["Muscle thick + rigid; high pressure inside","Pouches at weak points (where vessels cross)","Causes: aging, low fiber (constipation)"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Why they get infected","lines":["Trapped food/bacteria → ↓ blood supply → infection","→ perforation, abscess, peritonitis; bleeding"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Who","lines":["⅔ of adults > 80","More men; often unaware"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Diverticulosis","lines":["Usually no symptoms; found on colonoscopy","Maybe intermittent LLQ pain, constipation"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Diverticulitis","lines":["LLQ pain: intermittent → steady","N/V, fever to 101°F, chills, tachycardia","Guarding, rebound (peritoneal irritation)","Peritonitis → sepsis, hypotension, shock"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Tests","lines":["Diverticulitis: ↑ WBC; ↓ Hgb/Hct if bleeding","X-ray free air; CT (abscess)","Colonoscopy 4–8 weeks after (rule out tumor)"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Who is admitted","lines":["Mild → home","Admit: temp > 101°F, severe pain > 3 days, lower GI bleeding, older, complications"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Drugs","lines":["Metronidazole + TMP/SMZ or ciprofloxacin","Mild analgesic; severe → IV fluids + IV drugs"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Rest the bowel","lines":["Rest; no lifting, straining, coughing, bending (perforation)","Low fiber/clear liquids → severe: NPO + NG","No laxatives or enemas","Fiber back gradually after it resolves"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Older adults","lines":["Drug effects: confusion, orthostatic hypotension","No typical pain/fever → mental status change","Frequent abdominal checks; stools for blood"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Surgery","lines":["Emergency for peritonitis, obstruction, pelvic abscess","Colon resection ± colostomy"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Why","lines":["Diverticulosis: danger = pressure → high fiber keeps it low","Diverticulitis: wall inflamed, may perforate → no fiber, laxatives, enemas, straining","Rest first, fiber later"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Fiber","lines":["No fiber during symptoms → then gradually high fiber","25–35 g/day: wheat bran, whole grains, fruit, veg","Add slowly; psyllium; plenty of fluids"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Foods and habits","lines":["Avoid alcohol","Maybe avoid nuts, corn, popcorn, cucumbers, tomatoes, figs","Only bulk-forming laxatives; no enemas"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"Report","lines":["Report fever, pain, bloody/mahogany/tarry stools"]},{"note":"49-diverticular-disease","title":"After surgery","lines":["Incision + colostomy care","Body image, sexuality","Home care nurse; ostomate visit, UOAA"]}]},{"id":"49-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"49-adpie","title":"¶ Inflammation and infection of the bowel threaten","lines":["Threats: fluid, nutrition, skin; can become peritonitis, bleeding, sepsis","Track stools, pain, VS, weight; protect skin; stop spread","Teach chronic care: drugs, diet, ostomy","Infection precautions: handwashing after BMs, own utensils/towels (infectious diarrhea), avoid crowds on steroids/biologics"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Order: pain → N/V (appendix) vs N/V → pain (GE)","Travel, food, shared water","Antibiotics (C. diff), NSAIDs, smoking, family history","Stools: number, blood, mucus, fat; weight loss"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["VS: fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP, orthostatic","Boardlike abdomen, rebound, bowel sounds","Hydration: UO, daily weight; mental status","Perianal fistulas; stoma color"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Labs and tests","lines":["WBC, cultures, CRP/ESR, Hgb/Hct","K⁺, Mg²⁺, albumin, B12","X-ray free air, CT, MRE, colonoscopy","Stool analysis; celiac blood test"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Anxiety, depression; \"tied to the toilet\"","Body image, sexuality, coping, support","Costs, access to care"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Peritonitis: pain, fluid shift","UC: diarrhea, pain, bleeding/anemia","Crohn's/fistula: malnutrition, dehydration, ↓ K⁺, skin, sepsis","GE: dehydration, spread · appendix/diverticulitis: perforation"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Pain ≤ 2–3/10","Fluid + electrolyte balance","↓ diarrhea, no bleeding","Self-manages ostomy/pouch; fistula heals; no spread"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Acute abdomen","lines":["Peritonitis: VS for sepsis, antibiotics + IV fluids, NG, asepsis","Post-op: hourly LOC, VS, resp, I&O; semi-Fowler","Appendicitis: NPO; no laxatives, enemas, heat","Diverticulitis: rest, low fiber/NPO, no laxatives"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Chronic IBD","lines":["Stool diary, weights","Sulfasalazine (sulfa, folate); taper steroids; biologics → avoid crowds","Watch bleeding; antidiarrheals short term","Fistula: ≥ 3000 kcal, drainage off skin","Ileostomy: pink stoma, + 500 mL/day, no laxatives"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Infection and celiac","lines":["GE: ORT, no antimotility, sitz baths, wash hands ≥ 30 sec","Parasites: metronidazole, stool rechecks, test household","Celiac: gluten-free, hidden gluten, dietitian"]},{"note":"49-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain controlled; VS, weight, UO, lytes stable","Fever + WBC ↓; ↓ diarrhea, no bleeding","Pink stoma; demonstrates pouch care","Follows diet; knows drug precautions (folic acid with sulfasalazine, report infection on steroids/biologics, no sudden steroid stop)","Keeps colonoscopy + follow-up"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"acute-abdomen:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client reports abdominal pain that began 12 hours ago, followed by nausea and vomiting. Which condition does this sequence suggest?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Pain then vomiting → think appendicitis. Vomiting then cramps and diarrhea → think gastroenteritis.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Appendicitis","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Pain first, then nausea and vomiting → appendicitis."},{"id":"o1","text":"Gastroenteritis","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Gastroenteritis brings nausea and vomiting first, then cramping and diarrhea."},{"id":"o2","text":"Irritable bowel syndrome","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. IBS is chronic and recurrent."},{"id":"o3","text":"Celiac disease","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Celiac is chronic."}]},{"id":"acute-abdomen:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why peritonitis empties the circulation?","answer":"Leaky vessels → third spacing → hypovolemic shock → AKI; Peristalsis stops; 7–8 L/day pools in the bowel; Bacteria in blood → septic shock; Distention + pain → breathing compromise","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"acute-abdomen:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about cardinal signs?","answer":"Cardinal: pain, tenderness, distention; Localized → one area · generalized → widespread","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"IBD: immune inflammation, complications and drug safety","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them · 5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy · Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated · Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects"},{"title":"Signs","text":"Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus · Colicky lower pain relieved by BM · Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss · Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"1. Diarrhea · 2. Pain (inflammation, ulcers, skin) · 3. Lower GI bleeding → anemia"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Natalizumab: infection-free before each dose; report cognitive, motor or sensory changes immediately.","task":"A client receiving natalizumab for Crohn’s disease reports new trouble finding words and weakness in one arm. What should the nurse do?","cue":"Natalizumab blocks white cells from leaving the blood into tissues — including the brain → immune surveillance there falls → a latent virus can reactivate → PML.","rule":"Natalizumab: infection-free before each dose; report cognitive, motor or sensory changes immediately.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"ibd","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"IBD: immune inflammation, complications and drug safety","notes":["49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","49-crohn-s-disease"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them; 5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy; Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated; Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects","Signs: Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus; Colicky lower pain relieved by BM; Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss; Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum; Older adults: dehydration, ↓ K⁺","Priority problems: 1. Diarrhea; 2. Pain (inflammation, ulcers, skin); 3. Lower GI bleeding → anemia","Why: Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?"],"terms":["ulcerative colitis","Crohn disease","adalimumab","infliximab"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Ulcerative colitis — the inflammation exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Where and how","lines":["Rectum → continuous toward cecum (can be whole colon)","Remissions + flares; infection can trigger","Lining: hyperemic, swollen, red → bleeds, ulcers, abscesses","Thickening → narrow colon, partial obstruction"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Signs","lines":["Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus","Colicky lower pain relieved by BM","Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss","Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum","Older adults: dehydration, ↓ K⁺"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Causes and risk","lines":["Unknown: genetic + immune + lifestyle","Interleukins, TNF-alpha damage lining → cancer risk over time","Risk: smoking; high fat/sugar, low fruit/veg","~3 million with IBD (half UC); diagnosed 20–35"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Severity (Table 49.3)","lines":["Mild 4 · severe > 6 bloody · fulminant > 10 bloody","Severe: fever, ↑ HR, anemia, ↑ CRP/ESR","Fulminant: transfusion, colon distention on x-ray","CRP rises in 6 hr (more sensitive than ESR)"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"In the bowel","lines":["Hemorrhage/perforation; abscesses","Toxic megacolon → gangrene, peritonitis","Malabsorption (more in Crohn's); obstruction","Fistulas (Crohn's; bladder → pyuria, fecaluria)"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Colorectal cancer","lines":["> 10 years of UC → high CRC risk","~⅓ of UC deaths","→ frequent colonoscopies"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Outside the bowel","lines":["Arthritis, liver/biliary (gallstones)","Mouth + skin lesions, iritis","Osteoporosis (esp. Crohn's)"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"History","lines":["Family history; therapy; surgery","Milk, fried, spicy foods; stool color/number/consistency","Antibiotics in 2–3 months → rule out C. diff","NSAIDs → flare; travel; joints, mouth, eyes, skin"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Physical","lines":["Mild: VS normal · severe: low-grade fever 99–100°F","Nonspecific; infections mimic UC","Fever + tachycardia → dehydration, peritonitis, perforation"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Fear of incontinence → \"tied to the toilet\"","Anxiety, depression; painful meals","Explore: life events/stress ↔ flares, support, cancer worries, internet/telehealth"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Labs","lines":["↓ Hgb/Hct (blood loss)","↑ WBC, CRP, ESR","↓ Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻; ↓ albumin (protein in stool)"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Imaging and scopes","lines":["MRE = main IBD test","Teach: fast 4–6 hr, lots of contrast (cramps) → void first","Prone; subcut glucagon slows bowel","Colonoscopy (prep hard), CT, barium enema (UC vs Crohn's)"]},{"note":"49-ulcerative-colitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Diarrhea","2. Pain (inflammation, ulcers, skin)","3. Lower GI bleeding → anemia"]}]},{"id":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Treating ulcerative colitis — drugs, diet and bleeding watch","blocks":[{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Goal","lines":["Problem: bloody diarrhea, incontinence (tenesmus)","Goal: ↓ diarrhea, formed stools, control → lining heals"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Diet, smoking and rest","lines":["Low animal fat + sugar, more fruit/veg","Food insecurity → food banks","Stop smoking (find free programs)","Physical + emotional rest"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Track it","lines":["Stool diary: color, volume, frequency, consistency","Perianal skin; stool cultures if ongoing","Weight 1–2×/week home · daily, same scale, before breakfast in hospital"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"5-aminosalicylates (5-ASA)","lines":["5-ASA: mild–moderate UC + maintain remission","Inhibit prostaglandins; work in 2–4 weeks"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Sulfasalazine","lines":["Bacteria split it → 5-ASA (works) + sulfapyridine (side effects)","Folic acid supplement","Check sulfa allergy; thiazide may contraindicate","High dose: hemolytic anemia, hepatitis, infertility, agranulocytosis"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Mesalamine","lines":["No sulfapyridine → better tolerated","Delayed- or extended-release; enema/suppository","Little systemic absorption → fewer side effects"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Glucocorticoids","lines":["Prednisone for flares, then taper","Long-term: ↑ glucose, osteoporosis, ulcer, infection, adrenal insufficiency","Rectal: enemas/suppositories; budesonide = fewer systemic effects"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Immunomodulators and biologics","lines":["Immunomodulators + steroids → lower steroid dose","Infliximab (anti-TNF), adalimumab: refractory UC","Immunosuppression → report infection (even a cold), avoid crowds","Vedolizumab: blocks WBCs entering gut"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Why","lines":["Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them","5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy","Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated","Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Bowel rest","lines":["Severe → NPO (bowel rest) ± TPN","Less severe → elemental formulas"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Food triggers","lines":["Caffeine, alcohol ↑ diarrhea","Raw veg/high fiber; limit lactose","Stimulants: carbonated drinks, pepper, nuts, corn, dried fruit, smoking"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Rest and complementary therapy","lines":["Rest in flares; bathroom/commode close","Complementary: flaxseed, selenium, vitamin C, yoga, acupuncture (unproven)"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Pain","lines":["Goal ≤ 2–3/10; multimodal","↑ pain → peritonitis?","Antidiarrheals short term only (toxic megacolon)","Perineal: medicated wipes, barrier"]},{"note":"49-treating-ulcerative-colitis-drugs-diet-a","title":"Lower GI bleeding (Critical Rescue)","lines":["Stools: bright red or black/tarry (melena)","Hgb, Hct, lytes, VS; mental status (older adults)","Bleeding → Rapid Response Team now","Two large-bore IVs for transfusion"]}]},{"id":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Surgery for ulcerative colitis — the J-pouch and ileostomy","blocks":[{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"When","lines":["Drugs fail, or toxic megacolon, hemorrhage, perforation, dysplasia, cancer","UC surgery works better than Crohn's","Health equity: worse outcomes in African Americans (access, insurance)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"ERAS (Box 49.3)","lines":["ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids","Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose","During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia","After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Before surgery","lines":["CWOCN picks the stoma site","Ostomate visit may help","Ileostomy = ileum through abdominal wall → pouch + wafer"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Approaches","lines":["MIS common (no ICU)","Obese, prior surgery, adhesions → open (brief ICU)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"RPC-IPAA — the gold standard","lines":["Gold standard for UC; 2 stages","Stage 1: remove colon + most rectum; keep anus + sphincter; J-pouch from last 18 in of ileum; temporary ileostomy","Stage 2 (1–2 months): close ileostomy","Good continence; night leakage, frequent stools/urgency expected"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy","lines":["Remove colon, rectum, anus; close anus","Permanent ileostomy"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Stoma check (Critical Rescue)","lines":["RLQ, below belt line; no prolapse/retraction","Pink to cherry red = good","Pale, gray, bluish, dark → surgeon NOW"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Output","lines":["Within 24 hr, > 1 L/day, dark green liquid","+ 500 mL fluid/day","Adaptation: thicker, yellow-green/brown, little odor","Foul odor → blockage or infection"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Skin is the priority","lines":["Drains often → always wear a pouch","Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast","Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"After surgery","lines":["MIS/ERAS → usually no NG","Open → NPO + NG 1–2 days","Stay 1–4 days; CWOCN teaching"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"After the J-pouch","lines":["Pouchitis → metronidazole 7–10 days","Burning with BMs (acid)","Avoid gas/odor foods (cabbage, beans) and nuts, corn"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Why","lines":["Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption)","→ liquid, high output → add fluid + salt","Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always","Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Skin and pouch care","lines":["Barrier, sealants, fillers; check skin","Empty at ⅓–½ full","Change when inactive: before meals, bedtime, on waking, 2–4 hr after eating","Whole system every 3–7 days; extended wear for high output"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Food","lines":["Chew thoroughly","Watch: popcorn, coconut, nuts, corn, cabbage, celery, peas, tough meat, skins","B12 if terminal ileum removed","Salt + water; careful with sweating, hard exercise, heat, vomiting, diarrhea"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Drugs","lines":["No enteric-coated or capsule drugs","Tell prescriber + pharmacist","No laxatives/enemas; no stool 6–12 hr → call"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"If it stops draining","lines":["Drastic ↑ or ↓ output → report","Stops + cramps: remove pouch, knee-chest, massage, moist towels, hot tea","Not working or severe pain → call now"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Living with it","lines":["Sexual + social concerns; position change may help","Depression common → mental health referral","UOAA, Crohn's & Colitis Foundation, support groups"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Teaching about UC","lines":["Flares + remissions; relapses preventable","Immunosuppressed → report infection, avoid crowds","Report: fever > 101°F, ↑ HR, palpitations, ↑ diarrhea, severe pain, N/V"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Evaluate","lines":["↓ diarrhea, ↓ pain, no bleeding, self-manages ostomy/pouch"]}]},{"id":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Crohn's disease","blocks":[{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Where","lines":["Small intestine most often — terminal ileum; mouth to anus","Skip lesions (patchy)","Flares + remissions; more severe complications, more admissions"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"What it does","lines":["Thick wall, strictures, cobblestone ulcers → fistulas","Severe diarrhea + malabsorption → anemia, malnutrition","Cancer rare (after 15–20 years)"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Fistulas and obstruction","lines":["Fistulas common (rare in UC): bowel, skin, bladder, vagina, perirectal abscess","Obstruction: edema first → scar/fibrosis later","Most need surgery eventually"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Who and why","lines":["~1 million in US; diagnosed 15–35","~20% family history; NOD2 gene","TNF-alpha, interleukins (drug targets)","Risk: smoking, urban, Ashkenazi Jewish, high fat/sugar diet"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"History and abdomen","lines":["Weight loss, stools, blood, fever, pain","Perianal fissures, fistulas, ulcers","Bowel sounds ↓/absent, or high-pitched rushing over narrowed loops"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Symptoms","lines":["Diarrhea, pain, low-grade fever","Ileum: 5–6 soft stools/day; steatorrhea","Pain constant, RLQ","Fatigue (activity + anxiety) → energy conservation","Weight loss"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Watch for (Action Alert)","lines":["Alert for peritonitis, small bowel obstruction, nutrition/fluid imbalance"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["Anemia; ↓ folate + B12; ↓ albumin","↑ CRP, ESR","Pyuria → bowel-bladder fistula?","↓ K⁺ + Mg²⁺; x-ray, MRE"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Assess coping, support","Depression + anxiety common"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Mild to moderate","lines":["Mild: 5-ASA (mixed results)","Moderate–severe: azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine → serious infections"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Biologics","lines":["Infliximab, adalimumab, certolizumab → anti-TNF","Anti-TNF not with cancer, heart disease, MS","Natalizumab → keeps WBCs out of tissue (not anti-TNF)","Book lists natalizumab with the anti-TNF group + same cautions → an exam item may group them","Equity: non-White patients get them less often"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Biologic teaching (Drug Alert)","lines":["Infliximab in a health care setting","Subcut: self-injection; report site redness/swelling","Avoid crowds; report any infection now (cold, sore throat)"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Natalizumab and PML","lines":["Natalizumab IV q4 weeks","PML = deadly brain infection","Infection-free before dosing; report cognitive, motor, sensory changes","Vedolizumab: weeks 0, 2, 6, then q8 weeks"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Steroids and antibiotics","lines":["Steroids mask infection → short term","Never stop abruptly (adrenal insufficiency)","Cipro + metronidazole for fistulas, abscesses"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Why","lines":["UC = lining → bleeds; continuous → surgery removes it all","Crohn's = all layers, patchy → strictures, fistulas; surgery can't cure","Terminal ileum absorbs B12 → low B12 → monthly shots"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Nutrition","lines":["Poor nutrition → poor healing, muscle loss, ↓ immunity","Severe → bowel rest + TPN","Ensure/Sustacal; avoid caffeine, alcohol"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Fistula types","lines":["Enterovesical (bladder), enteroenteric, enterocutaneous (skin), enterovaginal","Abscess → I&D","Fistula → nutrition, electrolytes, skin care, infection prevention"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Fistula priorities (Action Alerts)","lines":["Priority: nutrition + fluid/electrolytes (dehydration, ↓ K⁺)","UO + daily weight; drop → give fluids","≥ 3000 kcal/day; high-calorie, high-protein, low-fiber; TEN/TPN","Skin: drainage never on skin (enzymes caustic)"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Fistula skin and drainage","lines":["CWOCN; < 100 mL/24 hr → barrier/dressing","Heavy → pouch (measures output); wafer 1/8 inch around","Antifungal powder (Candida)","Suction catheter in wound bed, not the tract (short term only); wound VAC (heals by 2nd intention; not if bleeding risk)","Sepsis signs: fever, pain, mental status, ↑ WBC"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Surgery","lines":["For perforation, hemorrhage, obstruction/stricture, abscess, cancer, fistula","Less successful than UC","Resection (laparoscopic or open); stricturoplasty widens strictures"]},{"note":"49-crohn-s-disease","title":"Going home","lines":["More readmissions (1 in 4 within 90 days)","Monthly B12 injections","Low-residue, high-calorie; avoid milk, gluten, caffeine","Rest, stress management, CRC screening","Support complementary therapies (ginger, acupuncture; unproven)","Independent fistula care before discharge"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"ibd:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client receiving natalizumab for Crohn’s disease reports new trouble finding words and weakness in one arm. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Natalizumab: infection-free before each dose; report cognitive, motor or sensory changes immediately.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Report it immediately and hold the next dose","correct":true,"why":"Correct. New cognitive, motor or sensory changes can signal progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a deadly brain infection."},{"id":"o1","text":"Reassure the client that fatigue is common","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. These are neurologic changes, not fatigue."},{"id":"o2","text":"Give the next dose as scheduled","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Never give it until PML is ruled out."},{"id":"o3","text":"Increase protein in the diet","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not related."}]},{"id":"ibd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Sulfasalazine = two molecules; gut bacteria split them; 5-ASA calms the lining · sulfapyridine → side effects + sulfa allergy; Mesalamine = 5-ASA alone → better tolerated; Acts locally (enema, budesonide) → fewer systemic effects","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"ibd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs?","answer":"Blood + mucus in stool; tenesmus; Colicky lower pain relieved by BM; Anemia, dehydration, fever, weight loss; Joints (polyarthritis, ankylosing spondylitis), erythema nodosum; Older adults: dehydration, ↓ K⁺","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Ileostomy and the J-pouch: perfusion, fluid and food","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption) · → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt · Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always · Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?"},{"title":"ERAS (Box 49.3)","text":"ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids · Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose · During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia · After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal"},{"title":"Skin is the priority","text":"Drains often → always wear a pouch · Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast · Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Ileostomy: pinkish to cherry red is healthy; pale, ash gray, bluish or dark → surgeon now.","task":"On day 1 after a proctocolectomy with ileostomy, the stoma looks ash gray. What should the nurse do?","cue":"The stoma is bowel with its own blood supply → if the mesentery is kinked or tight, perfusion falls → the mucosa turns pale, gray, blue or dark → necrosis.","rule":"Ileostomy: pinkish to cherry red is healthy; pale, ash gray, bluish or dark → surgeon now.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"ileostomy","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Ileostomy and the J-pouch: perfusion, fluid and food","notes":["49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?","ERAS (Box 49.3): ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids; Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose; During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia; After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal","Skin is the priority: Drains often → always wear a pouch; Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast; Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)","Evaluate: ↓ diarrhea, ↓ pain, no bleeding, self-manages ostomy/pouch"],"terms":["ileostomy","proctocolectomy","dehydration"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Surgery for ulcerative colitis — the J-pouch and ileostomy","blocks":[{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"When","lines":["Drugs fail, or toxic megacolon, hemorrhage, perforation, dysplasia, cancer","UC surgery works better than Crohn's","Health equity: worse outcomes in African Americans (access, insurance)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"ERAS (Box 49.3)","lines":["ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids","Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose","During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia","After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Before surgery","lines":["CWOCN picks the stoma site","Ostomate visit may help","Ileostomy = ileum through abdominal wall → pouch + wafer"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Approaches","lines":["MIS common (no ICU)","Obese, prior surgery, adhesions → open (brief ICU)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"RPC-IPAA — the gold standard","lines":["Gold standard for UC; 2 stages","Stage 1: remove colon + most rectum; keep anus + sphincter; J-pouch from last 18 in of ileum; temporary ileostomy","Stage 2 (1–2 months): close ileostomy","Good continence; night leakage, frequent stools/urgency expected"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Total proctocolectomy with permanent ileostomy","lines":["Remove colon, rectum, anus; close anus","Permanent ileostomy"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Stoma check (Critical Rescue)","lines":["RLQ, below belt line; no prolapse/retraction","Pink to cherry red = good","Pale, gray, bluish, dark → surgeon NOW"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Output","lines":["Within 24 hr, > 1 L/day, dark green liquid","+ 500 mL fluid/day","Adaptation: thicker, yellow-green/brown, little odor","Foul odor → blockage or infection"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Skin is the priority","lines":["Drains often → always wear a pouch","Enzymes + bile salts → skin breaks down fast","Skin barrier (gelatin/pectin)"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"After surgery","lines":["MIS/ERAS → usually no NG","Open → NPO + NG 1–2 days","Stay 1–4 days; CWOCN teaching"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"After the J-pouch","lines":["Pouchitis → metronidazole 7–10 days","Burning with BMs (acid)","Avoid gas/odor foods (cabbage, beans) and nuts, corn"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Why","lines":["Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption)","→ liquid, high output → add fluid + salt","Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always","Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Skin and pouch care","lines":["Barrier, sealants, fillers; check skin","Empty at ⅓–½ full","Change when inactive: before meals, bedtime, on waking, 2–4 hr after eating","Whole system every 3–7 days; extended wear for high output"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Food","lines":["Chew thoroughly","Watch: popcorn, coconut, nuts, corn, cabbage, celery, peas, tough meat, skins","B12 if terminal ileum removed","Salt + water; careful with sweating, hard exercise, heat, vomiting, diarrhea"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Drugs","lines":["No enteric-coated or capsule drugs","Tell prescriber + pharmacist","No laxatives/enemas; no stool 6–12 hr → call"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"If it stops draining","lines":["Drastic ↑ or ↓ output → report","Stops + cramps: remove pouch, knee-chest, massage, moist towels, hot tea","Not working or severe pain → call now"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Living with it","lines":["Sexual + social concerns; position change may help","Depression common → mental health referral","UOAA, Crohn's & Colitis Foundation, support groups"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Teaching about UC","lines":["Flares + remissions; relapses preventable","Immunosuppressed → report infection, avoid crowds","Report: fever > 101°F, ↑ HR, palpitations, ↑ diarrhea, severe pain, N/V"]},{"note":"49-surgery-for-ulcerative-colitis-the-j-pou","title":"Evaluate","lines":["↓ diarrhea, ↓ pain, no bleeding, self-manages ostomy/pouch"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"ileostomy:case","kind":"choice","scene":"On day 1 after a proctocolectomy with ileostomy, the stoma looks ash gray. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Ileostomy: pinkish to cherry red is healthy; pale, ash gray, bluish or dark → surgeon now.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Notify the surgeon immediately","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Pale, gray, bluish or dark = poor blood supply → report at once."},{"id":"o1","text":"Document it as expected","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Expected is pinkish to cherry red."},{"id":"o2","text":"Apply a warm compress","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Doesn’t treat ischemia."},{"id":"o3","text":"Change the pouch and recheck tomorrow","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Waiting risks necrosis."}]},{"id":"ileostomy:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Ileostomy skips the colon (water + salt reabsorption); → liquid, high output → add fluid + salt; Enzymes + bile salts → barrier always; Loose = normal → no laxatives; no output = blockage?","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"ileostomy:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about eras (box 49.3)?","answer":"ERAS: nutrition, prevent ileus, nonopioid pain, goal-directed fluids; Before: less fasting, carb loading, frailty survey, glucose; During: MIS, multimodal anesthesia; After: walk early, early feeding, early catheter removal","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Gastroenteritis and celiac disease: losses versus malabsorption","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea · Viral > bacterial; small bowel · Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration"},{"title":"Signs (Box 49.5)","text":"Flares follow diet adherence · Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating · Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines"},{"title":"Diagnosis and treatment","text":"Dx: blood test + endoscopy · Gluten-free diet = only treatment; heals in ~2 years · Gluten = wheat (bread, noodles, cookies) · Hidden gluten: additives, drugs, cosmetics; dietitian"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Celiac: strict lifelong gluten-free diet — the only treatment; check foods, additives, drugs and cosmetics; supplements; dietitian.","task":"Which foods should a client with celiac disease avoid?","cue":"Gluten triggers an autoimmune attack on the small-intestine lining → villi atrophy → malabsorption and diarrhea → removing gluten lets the lining heal (about 2 years).","rule":"Celiac: strict lifelong gluten-free diet — the only treatment; check foods, additives, drugs and cosmetics; supplements; dietitian.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"enteric-celiac","ch":"49","chapters":["49"],"title":"Gastroenteritis and celiac disease: losses versus malabsorption","notes":["49-gastroenteritis","49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 49; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea; Viral > bacterial; small bowel; Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration","Signs (Box 49.5): Flares follow diet adherence; Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating; Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines","Diagnosis and treatment: Dx: blood test + endoscopy; Gluten-free diet = only treatment; heals in ~2 years; Gluten = wheat (bread, noodles, cookies); Hidden gluten: additives, drugs, cosmetics; dietitian","Why: Diarrhea partly flushes the organism out; Slowing the gut keeps it in longer; So replace losses (fluid, K⁺); antidiarrheal only if the provider says"],"terms":["gastroenteritis","celiac disease","malabsorption"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Gastroenteritis","blocks":[{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"What it is","lines":["Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea","Viral > bacterial; small bowel","Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Norovirus","lines":["Leading foodborne cause; Nov–April","Fecal-oral, food, water, surfaces; vomit → airborne","Incubation 1–2 days","Outbreaks: cruise ships, nursing homes, prisons, dorms"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Epidemic viral","lines":["Parvovirus-type; fecal-oral","Incubation 10–51 hr; contagious while acutely ill"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Bacterial","lines":["Campylobacter: animals/infants; contagious 2–7 wk","E. coli: food, water, fomites","Shigellosis: contagious to 4 wk after; carriers for months"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"History","lines":["Travel (tropical Asia, Africa, Mexico, Central/South America)","Restaurants in 24–36 hr; spinach, lettuce","Raw/undercooked: oysters, sushi, rare meat"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"How it shows","lines":["N/V first → then cramps + diarrhea","Hypokalemia → weakness, dysrhythmias","Distention, hyperactive bowel sounds"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Dehydration (Action Alert)","lines":["Weight loss, poor turgor, dry mucosa","Orthostatic BP (falls), hypotension, oliguria","Fever uncommon in older adults","Older adults: acute confusion may be the only first sign"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Fluids","lines":["Oral fluids for all types","ORT: sports drinks, Pedialyte","IV fluids if severe/older"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Drugs","lines":["No antimotility drugs (organism stays in) — loperamide if needed","Diphenoxylate/atropine: habit-forming; not for older adults (falls)","Bacterial + fever: ciprofloxacin or azithromycin"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Perianal skin","lines":["No toilet paper; warm water, pat dry","Barrier cream between stools","Sitz baths 10 min, 2–3×/day","Night: pads, not briefs (air)","AP: perineum clean + dry, frequent changes"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Stop the spread (Box 49.2)","lines":["Wash hands ≥ 30 sec with soap (after BMs)","Own glasses, dishes, utensils, toothpaste","Report symptoms > 3 days","Don't handle food for others; food handlers → public health dept"]},{"note":"49-gastroenteritis","title":"Why","lines":["Diarrhea partly flushes the organism out","Slowing the gut keeps it in longer","So replace losses (fluid, K⁺); antidiarrheal only if the provider says"]}]},{"id":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Celiac disease and parasitic infections","blocks":[{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"What it is","lines":["Autoimmune inflammation of the small-intestine lining","→ wall atrophy, malabsorption, diarrhea","↑ risk with RA, type 1 diabetes","Complications: lymphoma, GI cancers, deficiencies"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Signs (Box 49.5)","lines":["Flares follow diet adherence","Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating","Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Diagnosis and treatment","lines":["Dx: blood test + endoscopy","Gluten-free diet = only treatment; heals in ~2 years","Gluten = wheat (bread, noodles, cookies)","Hidden gluten: additives, drugs, cosmetics; dietitian"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Why","lines":["Gluten = the trigger of the immune attack","Remove it → lining heals (~2 years)","Hidden gluten (drugs, cosmetics) keeps the damage going","Flares track diet adherence"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"How they spread","lines":["Oral-fecal: food, water, oral-anal sex, feces","Dx: stool analysis"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Giardiasis (Giardia lamblia)","lines":["Small intestine → diarrhea, malabsorption, weight loss","Acute self-limiting; chronic for years","Metronidazole (drug of choice); stools 2 weeks after"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Amebiasis (Entamoeba histolytica)","lines":["Large intestine ulcers; poor sanitation; can be asymptomatic","Mild: foul stools, no blood","Severe dysentery: mucus + blood, fever to 104°F, tenesmus","Ulcers → pain, bleeding, obstruction; rectal → blood on formed stool","Amebic liver abscess (can rupture)"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Treating amebiasis","lines":["Metronidazole → then paromomycin (luminal)","Severe: IV fluids ± diphenoxylate/atropine","Admit: extraintestinal or severe dehydration (older adults); else outpatient","Cure: 3 stools, 2–3 days apart, starting 2–4 weeks after"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Cryptosporidium","lines":["Immunosuppressed; swimming pools","Diarrhea; self-limiting if immune system normal","Paromomycin → dizziness"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"History","lines":["Travel (symptoms 1–2 wk after return); immigrants","Group illness → shared water (Giardia, Crypto)","Pork → trichinosis"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Stop the spread (Action Alert)","lines":["Handwashing = best prevention","Contagious until treated","Avoid stool, clean toilets, antimicrobial soap after BMs, daily bath","Avoid dog and beaver stool","No rectal-contact sex until done","Household + partners → stool exams; test water"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Chagas disease","lines":["T. cruzi via kissing bug (Latin America)","Chronic: dysrhythmias, heart failure, dilated colon/esophagus","~300,000 in US; transfusion, transplant"]},{"note":"49-celiac-disease-and-parasitic-infections","title":"Why#2","lines":["All travel feces → mouth","So teach: stool, shared water, household/partners","Break the road → handwashing = best prevention"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"enteric-celiac:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which foods should a client with celiac disease avoid?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Celiac: strict lifelong gluten-free diet — the only treatment; check foods, additives, drugs and cosmetics; supplements; dietitian.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Wheat, barley and rye products","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Gluten is in wheat (and barley and rye): bread, noodles, cookies. Hidden gluten in additives, drugs and cosmetics counts too."},{"id":"o1","text":"Rice and potatoes","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Naturally gluten-free."},{"id":"o2","text":"Fresh fruits and vegetables","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Gluten-free."},{"id":"o3","text":"Plain meat and fish","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Gluten-free unless breaded or sauced."}]},{"id":"enteric-celiac:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Inflamed stomach/intestinal mucosa → vomiting, diarrhea; Viral > bacterial; small bowel; Self-limiting ~3 days; older/immunosuppressed → dehydration","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"enteric-celiac:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs (box 49.5)?","answer":"Flares follow diet adherence; Classic: weight loss, diarrhea/constipation, steatorrhea, pain, bloating; Atypical: osteoporosis, iron deficiency anemia, lactose intolerance, depression, migraines","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Cirrhosis and ascites: pressure, protein and fluid shifts","cards":[{"title":"Why ascites forms","text":"↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out · ↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back · Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once · Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites"},{"title":"Late-stage signs (Box 50.3)","text":"Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin · Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas · Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"1 Fluid overload (ascites) · 2 Hemorrhage (varices) · 3 Confusion (ammonia, withdrawal) · 4 Pruritus (bilirubin)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Before paracentesis: explain, VS and weight, void, HOB up.","task":"A client with massive ascites is being prepared for a paracentesis. Which action is most important for the nurse to take at this time?","cue":"A full bladder rises above the pubic bone into the lower abdomen → it lies in the path of the paracentesis needle → emptying it prevents perforation.","rule":"Before paracentesis: explain, VS and weight, void, HOB up.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"cirrhosis","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Cirrhosis and ascites: pressure, protein and fluid shifts","notes":["50-overview","50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","50-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why ascites forms: ↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out; ↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back; Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once; Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites","Late-stage signs (Box 50.3): Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin; Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas; Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K","Priority problems: 1 Fluid overload (ascites); 2 Hemorrhage (varices); 3 Confusion (ammonia, withdrawal); 4 Pruritus (bilirubin)","Why the patient bleeds three ways: Varices: thin detour veins stretch and rupture; ↓ Prothrombin + no vitamin K absorption → ↓ factors II, VII, IX, X → long PT/INR; Splenomegaly destroys platelets → thrombocytopenia (often the first sign)"],"terms":["cirrhosis","ascites","paracentesis","spironolactone"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"50-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Liver does 400+ jobs: albumin, clotting factors, clears ammonia, bilirubin, drugs, stores vitamins, makes bile","Exemplars: cirrhosis (cellular regulation) and viral hepatitis A–E (infection); also NAFLD and liver transplant","Cirrhosis → portal hypertension → ascites, bleeding varices, jaundice, encephalopathy","Nurse: daily weight, watch bleeding + confusion, lactulose to goal, no alcohol / acetaminophen / liver-toxic drugs, teach how hepatitis spreads"]},{"note":"50-overview","title":"Why ascites forms","lines":["↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out","↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back","Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once","Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites"]},{"note":"50-overview","title":"Why the patient bleeds three ways","lines":["Varices: thin detour veins stretch and rupture","↓ Prothrombin + no vitamin K absorption → ↓ factors II, VII, IX, X → long PT/INR","Splenomegaly destroys platelets → thrombocytopenia (often the first sign)"]},{"note":"50-overview","title":"Why ammonia builds up","lines":["Gut bacteria + protein (or blood) → ammonia","Normally liver → urea; in cirrhosis blood bypasses the liver","Ammonia + GABA reach the brain → encephalopathy","Level ≠ symptoms → watch the patient"]},{"note":"50-overview","title":"Why bilirubin shows up in skin, urine and stool","lines":["Liver conjugates bilirubin → bile → gut → brown stool","Blocked or failing → jaundice, icterus, pruritus","Spills into urine → dark urine; less in gut → clay stools"]},{"note":"50-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Two failures: blood can't get through (varices, spleen, ascites) and liver can't do its chemistry (albumin, clotting, ammonia, bilirubin)","Pressure problems → volume + bleeding care","Chemistry problems → lactulose, vitamin K, skin care, drug caution"]}]},{"id":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Cirrhosis — the cellular regulation exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"The disease","lines":["Irreversible scarring after chronic inflammation + cell death","Fibrous bands + nodules block bile ducts, blood and lymph flow","Early large + firm → later small + hard","May be silent until a complication"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Three types (by cause)","lines":["Postnecrotic: viral (esp. hep C), drugs, toxins","Alcoholic (Laennec's)","Biliary (cholestatic): bile duct blockage, PBC"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Compensated vs decompensated","lines":["Compensated: scarred, still working, few symptoms","Decompensated: obvious liver failure"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"The main complications","lines":["Portal hypertension → ascites + varices","Biliary obstruction, encephalopathy","Hepatorenal syndrome, SBP, breathing trouble"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Most common causes (US)","lines":["US: alcohol, viral hepatitis, bile duct disease, NAFLD/NASH","Hep C = #1 cause of cirrhosis + liver cancer in US; alcohol speeds it","Worldwide: hep B and D"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Alcohol","lines":["Directly toxic → fat + inflammation (alcoholic hepatitis) → collagen scar","Stop drinking → fat + inflammation reverse","Continue → necrosis → scar; cocaine similar; binge → hepatitis, fatty liver"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Women need less alcohol to get cirrhosis","lines":["Women: 2–3 drinks/day × ≥ 10 years","Men: about 6 drinks/day × 10 years","Less can still harm memory (cortex)"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Veterans and hepatitis C","lines":["Vietnam / post-Vietnam veterans: blood exposure, shared razors, tattoos, jet-gun vaccines","Ask about hep C testing; arrange it if not done"]},{"note":"50-cirrhosis-the-cellular-regulation-exempl","title":"Prevention and equity","lines":["HP 2030: ↓ cirrhosis deaths, ↑ people who cleared hep C","Black patients: more admissions, longer stays, more readmissions","Help with transportation, access, chronic-disease self-care"]}]},{"id":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Portal hypertension, ascites and varices","blocks":[{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it is","lines":["Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg","Blood takes collateral detours"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Where the blood goes","lines":["→ Spleen (splenomegaly)","→ esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins","= ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Splenomegaly and platelets","lines":["Big spleen destroys platelets","Low platelets = often the first sign"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Portal hypertensive gastropathy","lines":["Slow gastric oozing → occult blood, anemia"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it is#2","lines":["↑ Hydrostatic + ↓ albumin (↓ colloid osmotic pressure) → third spacing","Hypovolemia + edema together","Massive ascites → RAAS → Na⁺/water kept → more ascites"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it looks like","lines":["Bulging flanks, protruding umbilicus, caput medusae","Orthopnea, dyspnea; poor posture and balance","Hernias; small ascites hard to see (esp. obesity)"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)","lines":["Gut bacteria → ascitic fluid (advanced disease)","Fever, chills, abdominal pain, tenderness — or only ↑ confusion / jaundice","Paracentesis: > 250 PMN → treat","IV cephalosporin or fluoroquinolone; then daily low-dose antibiotic"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Breathing trouble from ascites","lines":["Massive ascites limits chest + diaphragm → dyspnea","Watch SpO₂"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Varices","lines":["Thin, swollen, twisted esophageal/gastric veins","Bigger = more likely to bleed; mostly distal esophagus"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Bleeding varices — an emergency","lines":["Life-threatening: massive bleed → shock","Hematemesis or melena; may pass out before blood shows","Triggers: lifting, straining, vigorous exercise, chest trauma, dry hard food","#1 cause of upper GI bleeding"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Biliary obstruction and vitamin K","lines":["↓ Bile → no vitamin K (and A, D, E)","↓ Factors II, VII, IX, X → bleeding, bruising","Biliary cirrhosis: gallbladder disease, PBC"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Jaundice in cirrhosis","lines":["Hepatocellular (can't excrete) or obstructive (channels scarred)","Both → itching"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Why","lines":["May pass out before blood shows (blood pools in stomach)","Sudden ↑ HR, ↓ BP, fainting = variceal bleed until proven otherwise","Teach: no straining, heavy lifting, dry coarse food"]}]},{"id":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Assessing cirrhosis — history, signs and tests","blocks":[{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Exposures","lines":["Alcohol, drugs (Rx + illicit), herbs, chemicals — even long ago","Needlesticks; sexual history (MSM → hep A, B, C)"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Sensitive questions","lines":["Family alcoholism / liver disease; amount of alcohol","Drugs (oral, IV, intranasal); tattoos; military, prison; HCW, firefighter, police","Sobriety length","Explain why, privacy, nonjudgmental"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Past illness","lines":["Prior jaundice/hepatitis, biliary disease","Transfusions, autoimmune, obesity, lipids, HF, liver injury"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Mood and family","lines":["Lability, euphoria, depression, sleep changes","Family strain; substance use may continue","Refer social work, substance-use, mental health"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Alcohol withdrawal","lines":["Tremors from 6–8 hr after last drink","DTs: confusion, anxiety, hallucinations, delusions","↑ HR, ↑ BP, diaphoresis — emergency"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Early (vague)","lines":["Fatigue, weight change, anorexia, vomiting, RUQ tenderness","Often found by accident: abnormal LFTs or low platelets"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Late-stage signs (Box 50.3)","lines":["Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin","Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas","Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Liver and spleen","lines":["Early hepatomegaly → later small, hard, nodular","Splenomegaly in nonalcoholic causes"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Measuring abdominal girth","lines":["Flat, widest point (umbilicus), end of exhalation","Mark the skin for the same spot","Weight gain = most reliable fluid sign"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Other findings","lines":["Blood in vomit/stool; fetor hepaticus","Women: amenorrhea; men: testicular atrophy, gynecomastia, impotence","Neuro checks (family knows baseline), asterixis"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Liver enzymes","lines":["↑ AST, ALT, LDH (may be normal late)","ALT = most liver-specific","AST/ALT > 1 → alcoholic"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Obstruction markers and bilirubin","lines":["↑ Alk phos + GGT = biliary obstruction","↑ Indirect bilirubin in cirrhosis; ↑ urine urobilinogen","↓ Fecal urobilinogen → clay stools"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"What the failing liver can't make","lines":["↓ Albumin → ascites","↑ PT/INR (↓ prothrombin)","↓ Platelets; ↑ globulin"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Blood counts, ammonia, kidneys, sodium","lines":["Anemia, ↓ WBC","↑ Ammonia; ↑ creatinine (hepatorenal)","Dilutional ↓ Na⁺","Hypovolemia: ↑ BUN, ↓ protein, ↑ Hct"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Imaging","lines":["Ultrasound first; Doppler → portal vein clot","MRI: benign vs malignant mass","Arteriography / portal venogram"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Elastography, biopsy, endoscopy","lines":["Elastography: normal 5.0 kPa, > 13 kPa = cirrhosis","Biopsy risks bleeding → transjugular route","EGD → varices; ERCP → bile ducts"]},{"note":"50-assessing-cirrhosis-history-signs-and-te","title":"Why","lines":["ALT/AST = cells breaking open (loud acute, quiet late)","Albumin, PT/INR = what the liver can still make","Normal enzymes + low albumin, long INR = sicker, not better"]}]},{"id":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Managing ascites — diet, diuretics, paracentesis and TIPS","blocks":[{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1 Fluid overload (ascites)","2 Hemorrhage (varices)","3 Confusion (ammonia, withdrawal)","4 Pruritus (bilirubin)"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Sodium restriction","lines":["1–2 g Na⁺/day","Read labels, no table salt","Flavor: lemon, vinegar, parsley, oregano, pepper"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Nutrition","lines":["Usually malnourished → IV thiamine, folate, multivitamin","Liver can't store vitamins; biliary → no fat-soluble absorption","Malnutrition → hospitalizations → refer RDN"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Diuretics","lines":["Monitor daily weight, I&O, girth, edema, electrolytes","Loop → dehydration, ↓ K⁺, ↓ Na⁺","Furosemide + spironolactone balance K⁺"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"SBP","lines":["Any ascites → risk","Fever, poor appetite, pain, mental change; check rigidity","IV cephalosporin / fluoroquinolone"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"When and how","lines":["When ascites hurts breathing","Ultrasound-guided trocar/drain","Tunneled drain for home drainage","Sample → cell count + culture"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Before","lines":["Explain; VS + weight","Void first (bladder)","HOB up"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"During and after","lines":["Measure + describe fluid; label + send specimen","Dressing, check leakage; bed rest","VS + weight after (should drop)","Home: ≤ 2000 mL at a time (shock)"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Breathing support (Action Alert)","lines":["SpO₂; O₂ if needed","HOB ≥ 30° + feet up","Lungs q4–8h","AP: weigh every morning before breakfast, same scale"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"What it is","lines":["Nonsurgical shunt for refractory ascites or bleeding","Jugular → liver → portal vein; stent holds tract open","Portal blood bypasses the liver; detour veins may be embolized"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Before and after","lines":["Jugular US before; sedation/GA","US after + for 1 year (patency)","Home 1–2 days"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Watch for","lines":["Encephalopathy (blood skips the filter) → lactulose, narrow shunt","↑ Pulmonary pressure → check right HF first"]},{"note":"50-managing-ascites-diet-diuretics-paracent","title":"Why","lines":["TIPS = planned detour around the liver","↓ Pressure → less ascites + bleeding","Skips the ammonia filter → encephalopathy; closing it may bring bleeding back"]}]},{"id":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Confusion, pruritus and going home with cirrhosis","blocks":[{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Goal","lines":["Lower ammonia → clearer thinking","Assess neuro often; safety first"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Diet","lines":["Cirrhosis: high carb, moderate fat, high protein","With encephalopathy: moderate protein + fat, simple carbs","No strict protein restriction","Brief, simple teaching; small frequent meals"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Drugs used sparingly","lines":["Limit opioids, sedatives, barbiturates"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Lactulose (or lactitol)","lines":["Oral or NG; any stage","Pulls water into colon + traps ammonia","Goal 2–3 soft stools/day + less confusion","Watch ↓ K⁺, dehydration, bloating; skin care"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Rifaximin or neomycin","lines":["Rifaximin / neomycin kill ammonia-making bacteria","Neomycin = nephrotoxic → short-term, not with kidney disease","Older adults: weak, dehydrated"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Watch closely","lines":["LOC, orientation, asterixis, fetor","Thiamine + benzodiazepines for withdrawal risk"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Pruritus","lines":["Worse warm, evening, night","Cool, moisturize, no irritants; cool compresses, steroid cream","Sertraline if needed"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Setting up home","lines":["Bedroom near bathroom; commode; briefs","Semi- to high-Fowler or recliner","Tunneled drain: ≤ 2000 mL at once"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Teaching","lines":["No alcohol, smoking, illicit drugs","No OTC drugs (esp. acetaminophen, NSAIDs), liver-toxic herbs","Avoid extra fat-soluble vitamins, iron, niacin","Family: spot encephalopathy; constipation, bleeding, infection trigger it","Report bleeding / melena now"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Support","lines":["AA, Al-Anon, SMART Recovery","Sponsor/friend support; finances","Not a transplant candidate → hospice, grief"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Evaluate","lines":["↓ Ascites, balanced fluids","No bleed / encephalopathy (or fast treatment)","Abstinent, well nourished"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Why","lines":["Lactulose dosed by effect: 2–3 soft stools/day","No stools → ammonia builds","Too many → ↓ K⁺ + dehydration — also triggers"]}]},{"id":"50-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"50-adpie","title":"¶ Every patient with liver disease is watched","lines":["Watch: fluid shift (ascites), bleeding, ammonia → confusion","Protect: no alcohol, acetaminophen, liver-toxic drugs; rest, nutrition","Teach: how each hepatitis spreads, vaccines, screening"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Alcohol, drugs, herbs, toxins","Needlesticks, tattoos, transfusion < 1992, sex, prison, military","Travel, food/water; vaccines, HCV screen","Ask privately, without judgment"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["Daily weight, girth, edema","Jaundice, bruising, spider angiomas","Mental status, asterixis, fetor","Blood in vomit/stool; SpO₂; withdrawal signs"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Labs and tests","lines":["ALT, AST, alk phos, bilirubin","Albumin, PT/INR, platelets, ammonia","BUN, creatinine, Na⁺, K⁺","Hepatitis serology; US, elastography, EGD"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Cirrhosis: fluid overload, hemorrhage, confusion, pruritus","Hepatitis: weight loss, fatigue","Transplant: rejection, infection, bile leak, AKI"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["↓ Ascites, adequate volume","No bleed; oriented, 2–3 soft stools","Hepatitis: weight + energy, no spread","Transplant: no rejection/infection; abstinence"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Fluid","lines":["Low Na⁺; spironolactone + furosemide","Weight, I&O, girth, K⁺/Na⁺","Paracentesis: void, weigh before/after","HOB ≥ 30°, SpO₂, O₂"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Bleeding and confusion","lines":["Prevent: propranolol, no straining","Bleed: octreotide + banding, antibiotics, large-bore IVs, blood, VS q1h","Confusion: lactulose 2–3 stools, rifaximin, limit sedatives","Thiamine + benzos for withdrawal"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Hepatitis, NAFLD and transplant","lines":["Hepatitis: small high-carb meals, rest, ask about HBV before DAAs","Prevent spread: handwashing, no shared razors","NAFLD: weight loss, exercise","Transplant: immunosuppressants, temp, report fever, jaundice, oozing"]},{"note":"50-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Weight + girth falling; electrolytes stable","No bleeding; oriented, no asterixis","States what to avoid + what to report","Hepatitis: energy, protects others; transplant: meds + report signs"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"cirrhosis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client with massive ascites is being prepared for a paracentesis. Which action is most important for the nurse to take at this time?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Before paracentesis: explain, VS and weight, void, HOB up.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Have the client empty the bladder","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Voiding first moves the bladder out of the needle’s path and prevents puncture."},{"id":"o1","text":"Place the client flat in bed","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The head of the bed is elevated."},{"id":"o2","text":"Restrict fluids for 24 hours","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not part of the preparation."},{"id":"o3","text":"Apply supplemental oxygen at 6 L/min","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Oxygen is given if SpO₂ or breathing require it — it is not the key pre-procedure step."}]},{"id":"cirrhosis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why ascites forms?","answer":"↑ Hydrostatic pressure (portal hypertension) pushes plasma out; ↓ Albumin → ↓ colloid osmotic pressure → nothing pulls it back; Third spacing: hypovolemic and edematous at once; Renal vasoconstriction → RAAS → Na⁺ and water kept → more ascites","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"cirrhosis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about late-stage signs (box 50.3)?","answer":"Jaundice, icterus, pruritus, dry skin; Petechiae, ecchymoses, palmar erythema, spider angiomas; Ascites, dependent edema, ↓ vitamins A, D, E, K","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Varices: portal pressure and life-threatening bleeding","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg · Blood takes collateral detours"},{"title":"Where the blood goes","text":"→ Spleen (splenomegaly) · → esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins · = ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids"},{"title":"Nursing care during a bleed","text":"NG to watch for new bleeding · PRBCs, FFP, albumin, platelets via large-bore IVs · VS every hour; PT, PTT, platelets, INR"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Active variceal bleed: octreotide + endoscopic banding or sclerotherapy (plus antibiotics, blood products). Prevention: nonselective beta blocker.","task":"A client has active upper GI bleeding from esophageal varices. Which interventions would the nurse anticipate to manage the bleeding? Select all that apply.","cue":"Octreotide narrows gut vessels → less portal inflow → lower variceal pressure; banding or sclerotherapy closes the bleeding vein directly.","rule":"Active variceal bleed: octreotide + endoscopic banding or sclerotherapy (plus antibiotics, blood products). Prevention: nonselective beta blocker.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"varices","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Varices: portal pressure and life-threatening bleeding","notes":["50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg; Blood takes collateral detours","Where the blood goes: → Spleen (splenomegaly); → esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins; = ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","Nursing care during a bleed: NG to watch for new bleeding; PRBCs, FFP, albumin, platelets via large-bore IVs; VS every hour; PT, PTT, platelets, INR","Why: May pass out before blood shows (blood pools in stomach); Sudden ↑ HR, ↓ BP, fainting = variceal bleed until proven otherwise; Teach: no straining, heavy lifting, dry coarse food"],"terms":["varices","portal hypertension","propranolol"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Portal hypertension, ascites and varices","blocks":[{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it is","lines":["Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg","Blood takes collateral detours"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Where the blood goes","lines":["→ Spleen (splenomegaly)","→ esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins","= ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Splenomegaly and platelets","lines":["Big spleen destroys platelets","Low platelets = often the first sign"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Portal hypertensive gastropathy","lines":["Slow gastric oozing → occult blood, anemia"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it is#2","lines":["↑ Hydrostatic + ↓ albumin (↓ colloid osmotic pressure) → third spacing","Hypovolemia + edema together","Massive ascites → RAAS → Na⁺/water kept → more ascites"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"What it looks like","lines":["Bulging flanks, protruding umbilicus, caput medusae","Orthopnea, dyspnea; poor posture and balance","Hernias; small ascites hard to see (esp. obesity)"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis (SBP)","lines":["Gut bacteria → ascitic fluid (advanced disease)","Fever, chills, abdominal pain, tenderness — or only ↑ confusion / jaundice","Paracentesis: > 250 PMN → treat","IV cephalosporin or fluoroquinolone; then daily low-dose antibiotic"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Breathing trouble from ascites","lines":["Massive ascites limits chest + diaphragm → dyspnea","Watch SpO₂"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Varices","lines":["Thin, swollen, twisted esophageal/gastric veins","Bigger = more likely to bleed; mostly distal esophagus"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Bleeding varices — an emergency","lines":["Life-threatening: massive bleed → shock","Hematemesis or melena; may pass out before blood shows","Triggers: lifting, straining, vigorous exercise, chest trauma, dry hard food","#1 cause of upper GI bleeding"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Biliary obstruction and vitamin K","lines":["↓ Bile → no vitamin K (and A, D, E)","↓ Factors II, VII, IX, X → bleeding, bruising","Biliary cirrhosis: gallbladder disease, PBC"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Jaundice in cirrhosis","lines":["Hepatocellular (can't excrete) or obstructive (channels scarred)","Both → itching"]},{"note":"50-portal-hypertension-ascites-and-varices","title":"Why","lines":["May pass out before blood shows (blood pools in stomach)","Sudden ↑ HR, ↓ BP, fainting = variceal bleed until proven otherwise","Teach: no straining, heavy lifting, dry coarse food"]}]},{"id":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Bleeding varices — prevention and emergency care","blocks":[{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Screen everyone","lines":["Endoscopy screening for every cirrhotic patient"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Propranolol","lines":["Propranolol = prevention","↓ HR + ↓ portal pressure","Watch bradycardia, hypotension"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Antibiotics on admission","lines":["Antibiotics on admission for any cirrhotic GI bleed","Infection triggers bleeds (up to 20% infected)"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Vasoactive drugs + endoscopy","lines":["Octreotide + endoscopy, fast","Octreotide ↓ GI blood flow + portal pressure","Vasopressin rarely (potent, side effects)"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Banding and sclerotherapy","lines":["EVL: rubber bands, painless","EST: injected sclerosant → risk of ulceration","Both effective, ↑ survival"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Rescue therapy","lines":["Recurs → 2nd endoscopy, balloon tamponade, TIPS","Tamponade risks aspiration, asphyxia, perforation","Intubate first"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Nursing care during a bleed","lines":["NG to watch for new bleeding","PRBCs, FFP, albumin, platelets via large-bore IVs","VS every hour; PT, PTT, platelets, INR"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"NCLEX pattern","lines":["Active bleed: octreotide + sclerotherapy/banding","Beta blocker = prevention","Blood in gut → ↑ ammonia → watch for encephalopathy"]},{"note":"50-bleeding-varices-prevention-and-emergenc","title":"Why","lines":["Three problems: pressure, can't clot, blood = ammonia load","Octreotide + banding · FFP + platelets · watch mental status / lactulose"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"varices:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Portal pressure 3 → ≥ 10 mm Hg; Blood takes collateral detours","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"varices:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about where the blood goes?","answer":"→ Spleen (splenomegaly); → esophagus, stomach, abdomen, rectum veins; = ascites, varices, caput medusae, hemorrhoids","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Also PSE · Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early · Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure"},{"title":"Why it happens","text":"Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA · Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient"},{"title":"Stages (Box 50.1)","text":"I: personality, mood, speech, sleep changes · II: confusion, disorientation, asterixis · III: stuporous but arousable, twitching, hyperreflexia, asterixis · IV: unarousable, no asterixis, Babinski +, rigidity, fetor, seizures"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Hepatic encephalopathy: safety first (fall precautions, close observation), then lactulose to goal, avoid sedatives, monitor neuro status and asterixis.","task":"A client with late-stage cirrhosis has stage II hepatic encephalopathy. Which is the priority for the client’s care at this time?","cue":"Ammonia and GABA depress the brain → confusion and poor judgment → the client may get up unaided, fall or pull out lines.","rule":"Hepatic encephalopathy: safety first (fall precautions, close observation), then lactulose to goal, avoid sedatives, monitor neuro status and asterixis.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"encephalopathy","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Hepatic encephalopathy and renal deterioration","notes":["50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Also PSE; Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early; Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure","Why it happens: Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA; Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient","Stages (Box 50.1): I: personality, mood, speech, sleep changes; II: confusion, disorientation, asterixis; III: stuporous but arousable, twitching, hyperreflexia, asterixis; IV: unarousable, no asterixis, Babinski +, rigidity, fetor, seizures","Why: Kidneys starved, not damaged: volume shifted to the abdomen; Kidney holds Na⁺ → urine scant, concentrated, low sodium; GI bleed (volume loss) often tips it"],"terms":["encephalopathy","lactulose","rifaximin"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Hepatic encephalopathy, hepatorenal syndrome and other complications","blocks":[{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"What it is","lines":["Also PSE","Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early","Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Why it happens","lines":["Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA","Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Stages (Box 50.1)","lines":["I: personality, mood, speech, sleep changes","II: confusion, disorientation, asterixis","III: stuporous but arousable, twitching, hyperreflexia, asterixis","IV: unarousable, no asterixis, Babinski +, rigidity, fetor, seizures"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"What sets it off","lines":["Protein load, infection, hypovolemia, hypokalemia, constipation","GI bleed = protein load","Drugs: sedatives, opioids, hypnotics, diuretics, illicit"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Asterixis and fetor hepaticus","lines":["Asterixis = hand-flapping tremor","Fetor hepaticus = fruity/musty breath","Both = worsening encephalopathy"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Hepatorenal syndrome","lines":["Poor prognosis; often cause of death","UO < 500 mL/day, ↑ BUN + creatinine, ↓ urine Na⁺, ↑ urine osmolarity","Often after GI bleed or encephalopathy"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Infection","lines":["Infection triggers variceal bleeds","Up to 20% admitted with UGI bleed are infected"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Liver cancer","lines":["HBV/HCV → cirrhosis → primary liver cancer","TACE, cryotherapy, ablation, chemo, SIRT","Also a metastasis site"]},{"note":"50-hepatic-encephalopathy-hepatorenal-syndr","title":"Why","lines":["Kidneys starved, not damaged: volume shifted to the abdomen","Kidney holds Na⁺ → urine scant, concentrated, low sodium","GI bleed (volume loss) often tips it"]}]},{"id":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Confusion, pruritus and going home with cirrhosis","blocks":[{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Goal","lines":["Lower ammonia → clearer thinking","Assess neuro often; safety first"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Diet","lines":["Cirrhosis: high carb, moderate fat, high protein","With encephalopathy: moderate protein + fat, simple carbs","No strict protein restriction","Brief, simple teaching; small frequent meals"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Drugs used sparingly","lines":["Limit opioids, sedatives, barbiturates"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Lactulose (or lactitol)","lines":["Oral or NG; any stage","Pulls water into colon + traps ammonia","Goal 2–3 soft stools/day + less confusion","Watch ↓ K⁺, dehydration, bloating; skin care"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Rifaximin or neomycin","lines":["Rifaximin / neomycin kill ammonia-making bacteria","Neomycin = nephrotoxic → short-term, not with kidney disease","Older adults: weak, dehydrated"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Watch closely","lines":["LOC, orientation, asterixis, fetor","Thiamine + benzodiazepines for withdrawal risk"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Pruritus","lines":["Worse warm, evening, night","Cool, moisturize, no irritants; cool compresses, steroid cream","Sertraline if needed"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Setting up home","lines":["Bedroom near bathroom; commode; briefs","Semi- to high-Fowler or recliner","Tunneled drain: ≤ 2000 mL at once"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Teaching","lines":["No alcohol, smoking, illicit drugs","No OTC drugs (esp. acetaminophen, NSAIDs), liver-toxic herbs","Avoid extra fat-soluble vitamins, iron, niacin","Family: spot encephalopathy; constipation, bleeding, infection trigger it","Report bleeding / melena now"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Support","lines":["AA, Al-Anon, SMART Recovery","Sponsor/friend support; finances","Not a transplant candidate → hospice, grief"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Evaluate","lines":["↓ Ascites, balanced fluids","No bleed / encephalopathy (or fast treatment)","Abstinent, well nourished"]},{"note":"50-confusion-pruritus-and-going-home-with-c","title":"Why","lines":["Lactulose dosed by effect: 2–3 soft stools/day","No stools → ammonia builds","Too many → ↓ K⁺ + dehydration — also triggers"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"encephalopathy:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client with late-stage cirrhosis has stage II hepatic encephalopathy. Which is the priority for the client’s care at this time?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Hepatic encephalopathy: safety first (fall precautions, close observation), then lactulose to goal, avoid sedatives, monitor neuro status and asterixis.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Keep the client safe and free from injury","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Confusion, disorientation and asterixis put the client at high risk for falls and injury — safety comes first."},{"id":"o1","text":"Reorient the client to reality frequently","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Helpful, but it doesn’t prevent the injury a confused client is at risk for."},{"id":"o2","text":"Monitor the serum bilirubin level","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Bilirubin doesn’t guide encephalopathy care."},{"id":"o3","text":"Give antibiotic therapy","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Rifaximin may be used, but safety is the immediate priority."}]},{"id":"encephalopathy:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Also PSE; Early: sleep, mood, mental status, speech — reversible if caught early; Slow in chronic, fast in acute failure","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"encephalopathy:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why it happens?","answer":"Blood bypasses the liver → ↑ ammonia, GABA; Ammonia ≠ symptoms — assess the patient","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Hepatitis: route of spread, testing and treatment safety","cards":[{"title":"Definition and causes","text":"Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common · Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV · Report to local health department → CDC"},{"title":"Signs","text":"Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches · Clay stools + dark urine · Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever · Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced"},{"title":"Health care workers (Box 50.6)","text":"Standard Precautions; needleless systems · HBV vaccine × 3 (also prevents D) · IG after HAV exposure; report cases"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Before HCV DAAs: ask about hepatitis B (reactivation); monitor ALT. Never ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone (severe bradycardia).","task":"A client is about to begin glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for hepatitis C. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask before the first dose?","cue":"Clearing HCV quickly removes its suppression of a coexisting HBV infection → HBV replicates → hepatitis flare → possible liver failure.","rule":"Before HCV DAAs: ask about hepatitis B (reactivation); monitor ALT. Never ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone (severe bradycardia).","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"hepatitis","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Hepatitis: route of spread, testing and treatment safety","notes":["50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Definition and causes: Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common; Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV; Report to local health department → CDC","Signs: Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches; Clay stools + dark urine; Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever; Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced","Health care workers (Box 50.6): Standard Precautions; needleless systems; HBV vaccine × 3 (also prevents D); IG after HAV exposure; report cases","Why: A, E = gut route, cleared, never chronic; B, C, D = blood route, hide for decades → cirrhosis → cancer; Teach A/E: handwashing, water · B/C/D: blood, needles, sex, screening"],"terms":["hepatitis","antiviral","jaundice"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Viral hepatitis A to E — the infection exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Definition and causes","lines":["Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common","Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV","Report to local health department → CDC"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"What happens in the liver","lines":["Liver swells → RUQ pain","Distorted lobules → ↑ portal pressure","Swollen bile channels → jaundice"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Fulminant and chronic","lines":["No regeneration → fulminant (often fatal)","Chronic = > 6 months: B, C (± D)","→ cirrhosis, liver cancer; co-infection with HIV common"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Hepatitis A","lines":["RNA; survives on hands; killed by bleach, high heat","Fecal-oral: food, water, shellfish, food handlers, oral-anal sex","Incubation 15–50 days","Mild, flulike; worse > 40 or with liver disease"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Hepatitis B","lines":["DNA; HBcAg, HBsAg, HBeAg","Sex, needles, razors/toothbrushes, needlesticks, transfusion < 1992, dialysis, birth","Symptoms 25–180 days","Most adults clear + immune; carriers → cirrhosis, cancer"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Hepatitis C","lines":["RNA, ≥ 6 genotypes, no vaccine","IV drug use (highest), blood < 1992, born 1945–65, needlesticks, dialysis, prison","Not casual contact; don't share razors, toothbrushes, earrings","Incubation 2 wk–6 mo; most → chronic"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Hepatitis D (delta)","lines":["Needs HBV to replicate → only with B","Usually chronic; IV drugs, sex","Hep B vaccine prevents D"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Hepatitis E","lines":["Waterborne, fecal-oral; floods","US cases = travelers","Incubation 15–64 days; self-limiting, not chronic"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"How common","lines":["A and B declining (vaccines)","US: ~2.7 M hep C, ~2.2 M hep B","World: 58 M chronic hep C"]},{"note":"50-viral-hepatitis-a-to-e-the-infection-exe","title":"Why","lines":["A, E = gut route, cleared, never chronic","B, C, D = blood route, hide for decades → cirrhosis → cancer","Teach A/E: handwashing, water · B/C/D: blood, needles, sex, screening"]}]},{"id":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis — prevention, signs and blood tests","blocks":[{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis A","lines":["Handwashing (shellfish); safe food/water","Immune globulin within 14 days of exposure","Vaccine: travel (Mexico, Caribbean), dorms, prisons, day care, LTC","Combined HAV + HBV vaccine"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Who needs the hepatitis B vaccine","lines":["Multiple partners, STI, MSM","Chronic liver disease, HIV, immunosuppressants","Blood exposure at work, prison, household/sex contacts","CDC now: all adults 19–59"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Health care workers (Box 50.6)","lines":["Standard Precautions; needleless systems","HBV vaccine × 3 (also prevents D)","IG after HAV exposure; report cases"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis C","lines":["No vaccine","Book: one-time screen for baby boomers","CDC now: all adults ≥ 18 once"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Healthy People 2030 (Box 50.5)","lines":["↓ A, acute B, acute C","↑ people who know they have chronic B; ↓ B deaths","↑ people who cleared C"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"History","lines":["Known exposure?","High ALT/AST → full risk history"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Signs","lines":["Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches","Clay stools + dark urine","Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever","Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Examining","lines":["Light RUQ palpation; pain with jarring","Jaundice of skin, sclerae, mucosa","Scratch marks; look at urine + stool"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Chronic hepatitis C beyond the liver","lines":["Depression, arthritis, myalgia","Kidney, cognitive, vasculitis, heart disease"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Depression, guilt, stigma, isolation","Family fear → teach real transmission","Lost wages, costs"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Liver tests","lines":["ALT/AST into the thousands","↑ Bilirubin; alk phos normal or ↑"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis A#2","lines":["IgM = now (3–6 wk)","IgG = past + immune"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis B","lines":["HBsAg + IgM anti-HBc = diagnosis; HBV DNA PCR","HBsAg present = infectious; ≥ 6 months = chronic/carrier","Anti-HBs = immune (recovered or vaccinated)"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis C#2","lines":["Anti-HCV (~4 wk) = exposed","HCV RNA = active + viral load","OraQuick rapid test"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Hepatitis D and E","lines":["D: rising HDV-Ab","E: HEV IgM (travelers)"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Liver biopsy","lines":["Stage + grade; cause","Percutaneous; abnormal clotting → CT-guided / transjugular"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-prevention-signs-and-blood-tes","title":"Why","lines":["HBsAg = piece of the virus → infectious","Anti-HBs = immune answer → recovered or vaccinated","Antigen = virus here; antibody = protected"]}]},{"id":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Hepatitis — care, antiviral drugs and preventing spread","blocks":[{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1 Weight loss · 2 Fatigue","Mostly home; older / dehydrated → admit","Goal: rest the liver, regenerate, prevent complications"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Nutrition","lines":["High carb + high calorie","Add moderate fat + protein once nausea eases","Small, frequent meals; favorite foods; snacks"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Rest and activity","lines":["Rest ↓ demand, ↑ liver blood flow","No strict bed rest: alternate rest + activity","Add ADLs gradually"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Drugs used sparingly","lines":["Few drugs (antiemetic)","Chronic B, C → antivirals"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Hepatitis B","lines":["Interferon (less used); tenofovir, entecavir, lamivudine, adefovir","All → monitor kidney function","Tenofovir: bone loss → falls","Lamivudine: don't stop → flare","Entecavir: no alcohol"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Hepatitis C","lines":["Now DAAs by genotype","Protease inhibitors: CBC + chemistry; rash, itch, nausea, headache"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"NS5A combinations","lines":["Glecaprevir/pibrentasvir, elbasvir/grazoprevir, ledipasvir/sofosbuvir, sofosbuvir/velpatasvir","Ask about hepatitis B first → reactivation, liver failure","Monitor ALT","Ledipasvir/sofosbuvir + amiodarone → severe bradycardia"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Infection risk","lines":["Avoid crowds, sick people"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Preventing spread","lines":["A/E: handwashing, sanitary disposal of stool","B/C/D: don't share razors, toothbrushes, needles"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Action Alert","lines":["Teach transmission prevention","No alcohol; ask before any drug, vitamin, herb","Gradual activity; small high-carb meals; rest"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Nutrition OK; more energy; infection controlled"]},{"note":"50-hepatitis-care-antiviral-drugs-and-preve","title":"Why","lines":["DAAs clear HCV fast → hidden HBV can reactivate","First ask: \"Ever had hepatitis B?\"","Follow ALT"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"hepatitis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client is about to begin glecaprevir/pibrentasvir for hepatitis C. Which question is most important for the nurse to ask before the first dose?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Before HCV DAAs: ask about hepatitis B (reactivation); monitor ALT. Never ledipasvir/sofosbuvir with amiodarone (severe bradycardia).","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Have you ever had any type of hepatitis, especially hepatitis B?”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. These drugs can reactivate hepatitis B and cause liver failure."},{"id":"o1","text":"“Do you have a history of heart palpitations?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The heart concern (bradycardia) is with ledipasvir/sofosbuvir plus amiodarone."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Has anyone told you that your bones are weak?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Bone loss is a tenofovir (hepatitis B drug) concern."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Are you planning a trip to Mexico or the Caribbean?”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Travel matters for hepatitis A prevention, not this drug."}]},{"id":"hepatitis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about definition and causes?","answer":"Inflammation + infection of liver cells; viral most common; Also toxic/drug, EBV, HSV, VZV, CMV; Report to local health department → CDC","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"hepatitis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs?","answer":"Pain, icterus, jaundice, joint/muscle aches; Clay stools + dark urine; Fatigue, malaise, anorexia, N/V, fever; Pruritus, dry skin; often seen only once jaundiced","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Fatty liver: metabolic risk and prevention of progression","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Too much fat stored, not from alcohol · Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome · ~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH"},{"title":"Assessment","text":"Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM · Often silent for years · Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later"},{"title":"Treatment","text":"Book: no approved drugs → lifestyle: diet, exercise, behavior · Team: dietitian + mental health · Newer: resmetirom, semaglutide for MASH with fibrosis"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"NAFLD risk: obesity, high cholesterol and triglycerides, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, age, and the PNPLA3 gene (Latino adults at highest risk).","task":"The nurse is screening clients at a community health fair. Which clients have risk factors for nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD)? Select all that apply.","cue":"Insulin resistance (obesity, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome) sends extra fatty acids to the liver → fat builds up in hepatocytes; carriers of the PNPLA3 variant are at higher risk.","rule":"NAFLD risk: obesity, high cholesterol and triglycerides, type 2 diabetes, metabolic syndrome, age, and the PNPLA3 gene (Latino adults at highest risk).","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"fatty-liver","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Fatty liver: metabolic risk and prevention of progression","notes":["50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Too much fat stored, not from alcohol; Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome; ~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH","Assessment: Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM; Often silent for years; Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later","Treatment: Book: no approved drugs → lifestyle: diet, exercise, behavior; Team: dietitian + mental health; Newer: resmetirom, semaglutide for MASH with fibrosis","Why: Liver = overflow tank for fat (insulin resistance); Treat like metabolic syndrome: weight loss + exercise; Fat + early inflammation can recede before scar"],"terms":["steatosis","steatohepatitis","fibrosis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Nonalcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD and NASH)","blocks":[{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"What it is","lines":["Too much fat stored, not from alcohol","Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome","~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Genetics","lines":["PNPLA3 gene → ↑ risk","Latino adults at highest risk"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"NASH","lines":["NASH = inflammation + injury + fibrosis","~20% of NAFLD","→ cirrhosis, failure, cancer"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Assessment","lines":["Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM","Often silent for years","Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["↑ ALT, AST — not enough alone","Elastography + biopsy confirm"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Treatment","lines":["Book: no approved drugs → lifestyle: diet, exercise, behavior","Team: dietitian + mental health","Newer: resmetirom, semaglutide for MASH with fibrosis"]},{"note":"50-nonalcoholic-fatty-liver-disease-nafld-a","title":"Why","lines":["Liver = overflow tank for fat (insulin resistance)","Treat like metabolic syndrome: weight loss + exercise","Fat + early inflammation can recede before scar"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"fatty-liver:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client is newly diagnosed with nonalcoholic fatty liver disease. Which statement by the client shows understanding of the treatment plan?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"NAFLD care: lifestyle change for weight and fat loss (dietitian, exercise, behavior change, mental health support); NASH can progress to cirrhosis, liver failure and liver cancer.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Losing weight with a healthier diet and more exercise can reduce the fat in my liver.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Weight and fat loss through nutrition, exercise and behavior change is the core of treatment; the fat and early inflammation can recede."},{"id":"o1","text":"“Since I don’t drink, this can’t turn into cirrhosis.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. NAFLD — especially NASH — can progress to cirrhosis, liver failure and liver cancer."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Once my liver enzymes are normal, I can go back to my old eating habits.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Enzymes alone don’t show the liver is healed; the lifestyle changes have to last."},{"id":"o3","text":"“I need to stop eating protein so my liver can rest.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Protein restriction is not part of NAFLD care."}]},{"id":"fatty-liver:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Too much fat stored, not from alcohol; Tied to obesity, ↑ lipids, type 2 DM, metabolic syndrome; ~100 million Americans; also called MASLD / MASH","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"fatty-liver:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about assessment?","answer":"Risks: age, obesity, metabolic syndrome, DM; Often silent for years; Fatigue, malaise, RUQ pain, pruritus; jaundice later","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Liver transplantation: early complications and long-term protection","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Failing graft stops its jobs: · ↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion) · Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status"},{"title":"Not candidates","text":"Severe heart or lung disease · Metastatic cancer · Can't follow the drug/self-care plan"},{"title":"Donor livers","text":"Mostly trauma donors; UNOS by acuity (MELD) · Preserved up to 8 hr · DDLT: upper abdominal incision; lots of fluid + blood"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"After liver transplant: early — bleeding (oozing, petechiae, ecchymosis), neuro status; days 7–14 — rejection (fever, RUQ pain, less bile, rising jaundice and enzymes); any time — infection.","task":"A client had a liver transplant yesterday. Which assessment is the priority at this time?","cue":"Many vascular joins + a new liver that hasn’t yet restored clotting factors → bleeding risk is highest right after surgery → rejection develops later as T cells respond.","rule":"After liver transplant: early — bleeding (oozing, petechiae, ecchymosis), neuro status; days 7–14 — rejection (fever, RUQ pain, less bile, rising jaundice and enzymes); any time — infection.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"liver-transplant","ch":"50","chapters":["50"],"title":"Liver transplantation: early complications and long-term protection","notes":["50-liver-transplantation"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 50; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Failing graft stops its jobs:; ↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion); Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status","Not candidates: Severe heart or lung disease; Metastatic cancer; Can't follow the drug/self-care plan","Donor livers: Mostly trauma donors; UNOS by acuity (MELD); Preserved up to 8 hr; DDLT: upper abdominal incision; lots of fluid + blood"],"terms":["immunosuppression","rejection","transplantation"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Liver transplantation","blocks":[{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Who","lines":["End-stage or acute failure, other treatment failed","Cirrhosis = #1 reason; hep B/C, bile duct, autoimmune, alcohol, fatty liver"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Not candidates","lines":["Severe heart or lung disease","Metastatic cancer","Can't follow the drug/self-care plan"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Donor livers","lines":["Mostly trauma donors; UNOS by acuity (MELD)","Preserved up to 8 hr","DDLT: upper abdominal incision; lots of fluid + blood"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Living donors","lines":["LDLT: relative/spouse gives one lobe","Both livers regrow"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Ethics","lines":["Donor informed consent; HCV+ livers now used","Men > women; fewer Black, Hispanic recipients","Nonmaleficence, beneficence → advocate"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Most common complications","lines":["Rejection, vascular/biliary obstruction, infection","ICU; report at once"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Rejection","lines":["Days 7–14: fever, ↑ HR, RUQ/flank pain, ↓ bile, ↑ jaundice","↑ Bilirubin, ALT, AST, alk phos, ↑ PT/INR","Prevent: prednisone, tacrolimus, cyclosporine","Fails → retransplant"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Infection","lines":["Any time; immunosuppression, lines, immobility","Early: pneumonia, wound, UTI; after 1 month: CMV, TB/HSV reactivate","Antibiotics, vaccines, cultures, pull lines early, handwashing"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Biliary and vascular complications","lines":["↓ Bile, ↑ RUQ pain, guarding, jaundice, clay stools","T-tube dependent + secured; record drainage","Report → may need surgery"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Acute kidney injury","lines":["Causes: hypotension, antibiotics, cyclosporine, hypothermia","Watch UO, BUN, creatinine","Prevent hypotension; drug levels"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Action Alerts","lines":["Temp often; pain, distention, rigidity = peritonitis","Neuro change = liver not working","Oozing, petechiae, bruising → report now"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Long-term teaching","lines":["Immunosuppressants: ↑ BP, nephrotoxic, infection, GI","Screen for cancer, metabolic syndrome, DM","Report infection signs"]},{"note":"50-liver-transplantation","title":"Why","lines":["Failing graft stops its jobs:","↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion)","Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"liver-transplant:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client had a liver transplant yesterday. Which assessment is the priority at this time?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"After liver transplant: early — bleeding (oozing, petechiae, ecchymosis), neuro status; days 7–14 — rejection (fever, RUQ pain, less bile, rising jaundice and enzymes); any time — infection.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Observe for signs of internal hemorrhage","correct":true,"why":"Correct. On the first day after a long surgery with large blood loss and a liver just starting to make clotting factors, bleeding is the immediate threat; report oozing, petechiae, falling BP or rising pulse."},{"id":"o1","text":"Assess for signs of organ rejection","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Acute rejection usually appears 7–14 days after surgery."},{"id":"o2","text":"Monitor for wound infection","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Infection can occur at any time, but hemorrhage is the more immediate risk on day 1."},{"id":"o3","text":"Check whether the client understands the immunosuppressant schedule","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Teaching matters but isn’t the priority assessment now."}]},{"id":"liver-transplant:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Failing graft stops its jobs:; ↓ Bile (less drainage, jaundice) · ↓ clotting (oozing, ↑ INR) · ↓ toxin clearance (confusion); Watch the bile bag, IV sites, mental status","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"liver-transplant:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about not candidates?","answer":"Severe heart or lung disease; Metastatic cancer; Can't follow the drug/self-care plan","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Gallbladder disease: obstruction, infection, surgery and drains","cards":[{"title":"Why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder","text":"Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones · Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall · Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene · Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)"},{"title":"Provider exam signs","text":"Blumberg = rebound tenderness · Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation · Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts"},{"title":"Priority problems","text":"1 Pain · 2 Weight loss · Goal pain 2–3/10 · Not controlled → lap chole"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Obstructive jaundice: jaundice, icterus, pruritus, clay stools, dark urine, steatorrhea.","task":"A client has obstructive jaundice caused by gallstones. Which assessment findings would the nurse anticipate? Select all that apply.","cue":"Blocked duct → bilirubin into the blood (icterus, jaundice), none into the gut (clay stools), out by the kidneys (dark urine).","rule":"Obstructive jaundice: jaundice, icterus, pruritus, clay stools, dark urine, steatorrhea.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"gallbladder","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Gallbladder disease: obstruction, infection, surgery and drains","notes":["51-overview","51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","51-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder: Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones; Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall; Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene; Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)","Provider exam signs: Blumberg = rebound tenderness; Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation; Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts","Priority problems: 1 Pain · 2 Weight loss; Goal pain 2–3/10; Not controlled → lap chole","Why obstructed bile gives jaundice, itching, clay stools and dark urine: Bile salts in skin → itching; No bilirubin in colon → clay stools; Kidneys clear bilirubin → dark urine; No bile → steatorrhea, ↓ fat-soluble vitamins"],"terms":["cholecystitis","cholelithiasis","cholecystectomy","ursodiol"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"51-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"51-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Gallbladder stores bile (fat digestion); pancreas makes enzymes + insulin/glucagon","Cholecystitis: a gallstone blocks the duct → trapped bile inflames the gallbladder","Acute pancreatitis: enzymes activate inside the pancreas → autodigestion → shock, ARDS, DIC","Also chronic pancreatitis and pancreatic cancer / Whipple","Nurse: pain control, NPO, fluids + calcium, watch breathing and shock; teach low fat, no alcohol"]},{"note":"51-overview","title":"Why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder","lines":["Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones","Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall","Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene","Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)"]},{"note":"51-overview","title":"Why obstructed bile gives jaundice, itching, clay stools and dark urine","lines":["Bile salts in skin → itching","No bilirubin in colon → clay stools","Kidneys clear bilirubin → dark urine","No bile → steatorrhea, ↓ fat-soluble vitamins"]},{"note":"51-overview","title":"Autodigestion: the four processes","lines":["Trypsin activates the rest","Lipase → fat necrosis → calcium bound → hypocalcemia","Elastase → vessels dissolve → hemorrhage","Kallikrein → bradykinin → vasodilation, leak → shock"]},{"note":"51-overview","title":"Why chronic pancreatitis causes fatty stools, edema and diabetes","lines":["Lose acinar cells → no lipase → steatorrhea (pale, bulky, frothy, foul)","↓ Protein absorption → ↓ albumin → starvation edema","Lose islet cells → diabetes"]},{"note":"51-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Enzymes kept safe only by being inactive","Switched on inside: fat necrosis steals calcium, vessels bleed, capillaries leak","→ shock, ↓ Ca²⁺, lung failure","So: fluids, calcium, oxygen, NPO"]}]},{"id":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Cholecystitis — the inflammation exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"What it is","lines":["Gallbladder inflammation; acute > chronic","Affluent countries; women > men"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Calculous (with stones)","lines":["~95% have stones","Stone in cystic duct (most often) or common bile duct","Trapped bile → ischemia → gangrene → perforation"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"The stones","lines":["Cholesterol (most common US), pigment, mixed","Stasis favors stones","Bacteria → suppurative cholangitis (pus in ducts)"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Acalculous (no stones)","lines":["Stasis, no stones","Critically ill: sepsis, trauma, burns, long-term TPN, MODS, major surgery, hypovolemia"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Chronic cholecystitis","lines":["Repeated blockage → fibrotic, shrunken gallbladder","Acalculous chronic pain mistaken for gastritis (very low-fat diet)","Complications: pancreatitis, cholangitis, jaundice"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Jaundice","lines":["More common in chronic","Extrahepatic (stone, edema) or intrahepatic","Itching, clay stools, tea-colored urine"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Family, sedentary, high-cholesterol diet","Type 2 DM, gastric bypass, Crohn's, rapid weight loss, sickle cell"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Women","lines":["Obesity; pregnancy, HRT, birth control pills","\"Female, forty, fat, fertile\" — but thin, young and old too"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Prevention","lines":["Less fat/cholesterol, regular exercise","Lose weight slowly (rapid loss → stones)","Address food insecurity, access; telehealth"]},{"note":"51-cholecystitis-the-inflammation-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Gallbladder sitting still → bile concentrates → stones","Rapid weight loss, fasting, pregnancy, hormones all slow emptying","So lose weight slowly; TPN patients get acalculous disease"]}]},{"id":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Recognizing cholecystitis — signs and tests","blocks":[{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"History","lines":["Diet: high fat, low fiber, refined carbs","Symptoms after fatty food: gas, dyspepsia, belching, N/V","Sedentary? Family history? HRT / birth control?"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"The pain","lines":["Indigestion → steady RUQ pain","Radiates to right shoulder / scapula","Episodic attacks after fatty or large meals"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Older adults and diabetes","lines":["May have no pain, no fever","Only local tenderness; delirium may come first"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Provider exam signs","lines":["Blumberg = rebound tenderness","Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation","Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Late and systemic signs","lines":["Late: jaundice, clay stools, dark urine, steatorrhea","Fever 99–102°F, tachycardia, dehydration","Avoids eating"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Key features (Box 51.1)","lines":["Pain → right shoulder, after fatty/big meals","N/V, dyspepsia, belching, gas, fullness","Blumberg, Murphy, fever","Jaundice, clay stools, dark urine"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Labs","lines":["Rule out ulcer, hepatitis, pancreatitis","↑ WBC","↑ Alk phos, AST, LDH, bilirubin (obstruction)","↑ Amylase/lipase = pancreas involved"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Ultrasound first","lines":["RUQ ultrasound = best first test","Shows wall edema + fluid","X-ray: only calcified stones"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"HIDA scan","lines":["IV tracer → camera measures ejection","Low flow = obstruction","NPO before; big camera close"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"ERCP and MRCP","lines":["ERCP: unclear cause or jaundice; removes stones","MRCP: safer, gadolinium MRI","Ask allergies; no MRI with pacemaker; gadolinium has no iodine"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-cholecystitis-signs-and-test","title":"Why","lines":["Murphy: breathing in pushes the gallbladder onto the fingers → breath stops","Fat → cholecystokinin → gallbladder squeezes against a blocked duct → colic"]}]},{"id":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Treating cholecystitis — pain, drugs and drains","blocks":[{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1 Pain · 2 Weight loss","Goal pain 2–3/10","Not controlled → lap chole"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Diet and fluids","lines":["Avoid fatty foods","NPO during N/V; IV fluids"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Pain drugs","lines":["Morphine / hydromorphone; all opioids → some Oddi spasm","Ketorolac → watch GI bleeding","Antiemetics; IV antibiotics as needed"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Critical Rescue: biliary colic","lines":["Stone → spasm → severe colic","Tachycardia, pallor/ash-gray, diaphoresis, prostration","Assess for shock; call RRT; stay; HOB flat"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Dissolving stones","lines":["Ursodiol, chenodiol dissolve stones (long term)","Ultrasound q6 months first year","Report diarrhea, vomiting, severe pain to shoulders; take with food + milk"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Percutaneous biliary drain","lines":["Cholecystostomy when surgery isn't possible","Internal → duodenum; internal/external → part to bag (cap; jaundice/leak → reconnect)","External bag below the site","↓ Drainage = drain not working"]},{"note":"51-treating-cholecystitis-pain-drugs-and-dr","title":"Why","lines":["Severe pain alone can drop BP (vagal) — or it is perforation / sepsis","HOB flat helps blood return to the heart","Stay, watch the trend, get help"]}]},{"id":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Cholecystectomy — laparoscopic and open","blocks":[{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"The gold standard","lines":["Gold standard; usually same-day","Few complications, less pain, faster recovery","Teach deep breathing, leg exercises"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"The procedure","lines":["Umbilical puncture ± small incisions","3–4 L CO₂ insufflation","Cystic artery + duct closed; out through umbilicus"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Action Alert","lines":["Pulse oximetry often","Deep breathing + IS every hour"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"After surgery","lines":["Eat once awake; ondansetron for N/V","HOB up; void; walk early (absorbs CO₂)","Opioid + anti-inflammatory; strips/glue off in 7–10 days"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"CO₂ shoulder pain","lines":["CO₂ → shoulder/chest pain","Walk; apply heat"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Going home","lines":["Home same day; shower next day","Ice + oral opioids; rest 24 hr, normal in ~1 week","Greasy food → pain, diarrhea; reintroduce fats one at a time"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"NOTES","lines":["Through vagina (most often for gallbladder)","No visible scar, less pain"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"When","lines":["Severe obstruction, converted lap, suspected cancer","RUQ incision; JP drain","Drainage serosanguineous, bile-tinged first 24 hr"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Care","lines":["Splint to cough and breathe; early antiemetics","Watch incision; walk early","Clear → solid as peristalsis returns; home 1–2 days"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Nutrition","lines":["High fiber, low fat, no gas-formers","Small frequent meals; weigh regularly","Long term: no special diet — avoid fried food, butter, fast food"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Postcholecystectomy syndrome (PCS, Box 51.2)","lines":["Pain + vomiting/diarrhea weeks–months later","Biliary: retained stone, leak, stricture, Oddi dysfunction","Nonbiliary: CAD, neuritis, diverticula","ERCP finds + fixes"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain controlled; nutrition adequate"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"NCLEX pattern (lap chole teaching)","lines":["Expect: antiemetic, strips/glue, pulse oximetry","Not: 2-day stay, drain, NG tube"]},{"note":"51-cholecystectomy-laparoscopic-and-open","title":"Why","lines":["No gallbladder → bile trickles constantly, no concentrated squirt","Large greasy meal outruns it → cramps, diarrhea","So add fats one at a time; long term usually no special diet"]}]},{"id":"51-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"51-adpie","title":"¶ Every patient in this chapter is watched","lines":["Watch: pain, shock (fluid shift, bleeding), breathing failure","Do: NPO / NG, fluids, calcium, nutrition","Teach: low fat, no alcohol, enzymes with meals"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Pain pattern: RUQ after fat vs boring epigastric to back","Alcohol (CAGE, private), gallstones, ERCP","Drugs: thiazides, steroids, GLP-1, DPP-4","Rapid weight loss, hormones, smoking"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["VS, SpO₂, lungs","Abdomen: rigidity, flatus, Cullen/Turner","Jaundice, clay stools, steatorrhea","Twitching (↓ Ca²⁺), LOC"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Labs and tests","lines":["Amylase, lipase, ALT, bilirubin","Glucose, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺, CRP, albumin","RUQ US, HIDA, CT, ERCP/MRCP","Cancer: CA 19-9, EUS"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Gallbladder: pain, weight loss","Acute pancreatitis: pain, nutrition + shock, ARDS, ↓ Ca²⁺","Chronic: pain, malabsorption, diabetes","Cancer/Whipple: leak, bleeding, glucose, grief"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Pain 2–3/10; weight stable","Stable VS, UO; clear lungs","Ca²⁺, glucose normal; flatus back","Fewer fatty stools; no leak; abstinent"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Gallbladder","lines":["Avoid fat; opioids; antiemetics","Colic + shock signs → RRT, stay, HOB flat","Lap chole: SpO₂, IS q1h, walk, heat for CO₂ pain","Fats back one at a time"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Acute pancreatitis","lines":["ABCs, O₂, NPO, IV fluids, Ca²⁺/Mg²⁺","PCA, fetal position, mouth care","Lungs q4h; watch ↓ Ca²⁺; flatus = bowel back","Jejunal feeding; then low-fat bland meals, no alcohol"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Chronic pancreatitis, cancer and Whipple","lines":["Pancrelipase with meals, not crushed; count stools","4000–6000 kcal; insulin; skin barrier","Cancer: scheduled opioids; feeds, sterile TPN","Whipple: drains, report leak signs, semi-Fowler, shock watch, glucose"]},{"note":"51-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain at goal; eating; weight stable","VS, UO, SpO₂, Ca²⁺, glucose in range","States diet, enzymes and what to report: pain, jaundice, clay stools, dark urine"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"gallbladder:case","kind":"choice","scene":"An 84-year-old client with cholecystitis may present differently from a younger adult. Which finding might be the first sign?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Older adults and clients with diabetes: cholecystitis may show only localized tenderness or delirium, without pain or fever.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"New confusion (delirium)","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Older adults and people with diabetes may have no pain or fever; acute confusion may be the first symptom."},{"id":"o1","text":"High fever with chills","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Fever may be absent."},{"id":"o2","text":"Severe colicky pain after every meal","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Pain may be absent; localized tenderness may be the only sign."},{"id":"o3","text":"Projectile vomiting","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not typical."}]},{"id":"gallbladder:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why trapped bile inflames the gallbladder?","answer":"Thick, stagnant bile + cholesterol imbalance or slow emptying → stones; Stone blocks cystic duct → bile chemically irritates wall; Edema + distention + ↓ blood flow → ischemia, infection, gangrene; Perforation → abscess (small) or peritonitis (large)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"gallbladder:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about provider exam signs?","answer":"Blumberg = rebound tenderness; Murphy = inspiratory arrest on RUQ palpation; Sausage-shaped mass; posterior rib percussion hurts","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Acute pancreatitis: systemic risk and safe nutrition","cards":[{"title":"How it starts","text":"Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released · Duct pressure → rupture → spill · 4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation"},{"title":"Vital signs and lungs","text":"Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP · Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea"},{"title":"Priorities","text":"1 Pain · 2 Nutrition · ABCs + oxygen first · Pain goal 2–3/10; severe → ICU"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Acute pancreatitis: ↑ amylase, lipase, trypsin, elastase, glucose, WBC, CRP, bilirubin and ALT (biliary); ↓ calcium and magnesium.","task":"Which serum laboratory findings would the nurse expect for a client with acute pancreatitis? Select all that apply.","cue":"Injured cells release amylase, lipase, trypsin and elastase into the blood → all rise; less insulin → glucose rises; inflammation → leukocytosis.","rule":"Acute pancreatitis: ↑ amylase, lipase, trypsin, elastase, glucose, WBC, CRP, bilirubin and ALT (biliary); ↓ calcium and magnesium.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"acute-pancreatitis","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Acute pancreatitis: systemic risk and safe nutrition","notes":["51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["How it starts: Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released; Duct pressure → rupture → spill; 4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation","Vital signs and lungs: Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP; Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea","Priorities: 1 Pain · 2 Nutrition; ABCs + oxygen first; Pain goal 2–3/10; severe → ICU","Why: Fatty acids + calcium = soap (like soap scum); Calcium locked in dead fat → unusable; Watch twitching, numbness, irritability; expect IV calcium","Do not automatically withhold food solely to “rest the pancreas.” NICE recommends oral intake unless a clear reason prevents it; moderately severe/severe cases need enteral nutrition started within 72 hours. Individualize fluids, pain control and nutrition to the clinical course and prescribed plan."],"terms":["pancreatitis","lipase","hypocalcemia"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Acute pancreatitis — the inflammation exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"The disease","lines":["Enzymes activate too early, inside the pancreas → autodigestion","Mild edema → necrotizing hemorrhagic (NHP)","Exocrine (inactive enzymes) + endocrine (insulin)"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"How it starts","lines":["Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released","Duct pressure → rupture → spill","4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Fat necrosis and calcium","lines":["Lipase → fat necrosis (hallmark)","Fatty acids bind calcium → hypocalcemia"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Bleeding and shock","lines":["Elastase dissolves vessels → hemorrhage into retroperitoneum","Kallikrein → bradykinin → vasodilation, leak","Hypovolemic shock = common cause of death"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Abscess and pseudocyst","lines":["Infection → abscess → drain promptly (sepsis)","Walled-off fluid → pseudocyst; can rupture"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"The list","lines":["Sepsis, hemorrhage, AKI, ileus, shock","Pleural effusion, ARDS, atelectasis, pneumonia","MODS, DIC, diabetes"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Lungs","lines":["Left pleural effusion common","ARDS; lungs cause > half of first-week deaths"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Circulation, kidneys and clotting","lines":["Vasodilation + fluid loss → shock → AKI","Enzymes in blood → DIC","NHP (≤ 10%) → MODS"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Gut, bile and glucose","lines":["Enzymes irritate peritoneum → ileus","Swollen head → jaundice","Hyperglycemia → possible diabetes"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Most common causes","lines":["Gallstones ≤ 40%, alcohol ~30%","Trauma, ERCP, tumors","↑ Triglycerides, ↑ calcium, hyperparathyroidism","Drugs: thiazides, steroids, DPP-4, GLP-1, sulfonamides","Smoking, CF, childbirth, viruses"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"ERCP risk","lines":["ERCP: ~10% pancreatitis, 3% bleeding","Tell patients before consent"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Who and outlook","lines":["Holidays (men, alcohol); women: gallstones, childbirth","Worse in older, hospitalized","Prognosis: biliary good, alcohol poor"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Prevention","lines":["Avoid excess alcohol (any type)","Prevent obesity; lose weight slowly","Address food insecurity, access"]},{"note":"51-acute-pancreatitis-the-inflammation-exem","title":"Why","lines":["Fatty acids + calcium = soap (like soap scum)","Calcium locked in dead fat → unusable","Watch twitching, numbness, irritability; expect IV calcium"]}]},{"id":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Recognizing acute pancreatitis — pain, signs and labs","blocks":[{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"History","lines":["Interview after pain control","Pain after alcohol or fatty meal? Alcohol amount + years","Recent ERCP / surgery, biliary disease, viruses","All drugs + supplements"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"The pain","lines":["Sudden, severe epigastric/LUQ pain","Radiates to back, left flank, left shoulder","Boring, continuous; worse supine","Relief: fetal position, sit up + lean forward"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Abdominal exam","lines":["Jaundice; Cullen (umbilicus), Turner (flanks) — gray-blue","↓/absent bowel sounds = ileus","Rigidity, guarding = peritonitis; dullness = ascites"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Vital signs and lungs","lines":["Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP","Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Critical Rescue","lines":["↓ BP + ↑ HR = hemorrhage, fluid shift, sepsis → shock","LOC change = withdrawal, hypoxia, sepsis","Call RRT (esp. LOC)"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Alcohol history","lines":["After pain control, private, trusting","CAGE screen","When? holidays, weekends; recent stress/loss"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Pancreatic enzymes","lines":["↑ Amylase, lipase, trypsin, elastase","Amylase: up in 12–24 hr, lasts 2–3 days","Lipase: later, lasts 5–7 days","Persistent ↑ = obstruction or leak"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Biliary pancreatitis","lines":["↑ Bilirubin","ALT ≥ 3× = biliary cause"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Inflammation and glucose","lines":["↑ WBC, ESR, CRP, glucose = severity","↓ Insulin → hyperglycemia"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Calcium, magnesium and the rest","lines":["↓ Ca²⁺, ↓ Mg²⁺ (Ca²⁺ low 7–10 days)","↑ BUN, glucose, triglycerides","Hemoconcentration, ↓ platelets, ↓ albumin"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Imaging","lines":["US: finds gallstones; gas hides pancreas","Contrast CT = most reliable","CXR: left effusion, raised left diaphragm","Pancreatic stones → ERCP"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"NCLEX pattern (labs)","lines":["Expect ↑ amylase, ↑ lipase, ↑ WBC","Glucose ↑; trypsin, elastase ↑"]},{"note":"51-recognizing-acute-pancreatitis-pain-sign","title":"Why","lines":["Bloody enzyme fluid tracks to umbilicus (Cullen) and flanks (Turner)","= hemorrhagic, severe pancreatitis → expect shock"]}]},{"id":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Caring for acute pancreatitis — pain, NPO and nutrition","blocks":[{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Priorities","lines":["1 Pain · 2 Nutrition","ABCs + oxygen first","Pain goal 2–3/10; severe → ICU"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"NPO and fluids","lines":["Do not routinely withhold food without a clinical reason (for example vomiting). Follow the individualized nutrition and fluid plan.","Assess perfusion, respiratory status, urine output and overload during prescribed IV fluid replacement."]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"NG tube","lines":["NG only if continuous vomiting / obstruction","Keeps acid out of duodenum → less pancreatic stimulation"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Action Alert: ileus","lines":["Ileus common → long NG use","Flatus or BM = most reliable; bowel sounds aren't"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Pain drugs","lines":["PCA morphine / hydromorphone; fentanyl, epidural","Mild: 2–3 days; severe: up to 2 weeks","Famotidine / pantoprazole","Antimicrobials are for a clinical indication such as infection, not routine prophylaxis in acute pancreatitis."]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Comfort","lines":["Fetal position / sit with knees up","Mouth + nares care (parotitis risk)","Explain, reassure; music, reading, visitors"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Action Alert: lungs and calcium","lines":["Resp status q4h; O₂","Overload: weight gain, crackles, dyspnea","Hypocalcemia: twitching, numbness, irritability"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Procedures and surgery","lines":["Gallstones → ERCP + sphincterotomy → lap chole if fails","Surgery only for pseudocyst / abscess (lap or CT drain)"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Feeding","lines":["Offer oral intake when clinically appropriate; do not require prolonged fasting to rest the pancreas.","For moderately severe/severe disease, start enteral nutrition within 72 hours under the treatment plan."]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Eating again","lines":["Offer oral intake when clinically appropriate; do not require prolonged fasting to rest the pancreas.","For moderately severe/severe disease, start enteral nutrition within 72 hours under the treatment plan."]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Home","lines":["One floor, limit stairs","Case manager; home care if complicated","Teach once pain subsides"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Teaching","lines":["No alcohol → or chronic pancreatitis","Report pain, jaundice, clay stools, dark urine","AA; Al-Anon, Alateen"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain controlled; nutrition adequate"]},{"note":"51-caring-for-acute-pancreatitis-pain-npo-a","title":"Why","lines":["Goal: don't wake the pancreas","Food + duodenal acid → enzyme secretion","So NPO, ↓ acid, NG if vomiting, feed into the jejunum"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Do not automatically withhold food solely to “rest the pancreas.” NICE recommends oral intake unless a clear reason prevents it; moderately severe/severe cases need enteral nutrition started within 72 hours. Individualize fluids, pain control and nutrition to the clinical course and prescribed plan.","sources":[{"title":"NICE NG104: acute pancreatitis and nutrition","url":"https://www.nice.org.uk/guidance/ng104/chapter/Recommendations"}],"steps":[{"id":"acute-pancreatitis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"The nurse is teaching ways to help prevent acute pancreatitis. Which statement should the nurse include?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Prevent pancreatitis: avoid excess alcohol; prevent obesity with less high-fat, high-cholesterol food and exercise; lose weight slowly.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“Avoid drinking excessive alcohol of any type.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Alcohol causes about 30% of acute pancreatitis."},{"id":"o1","text":"“If you become obese, follow a weight-loss program that works rapidly.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Rapid weight loss causes gallstones — the other main cause; lose weight slowly."},{"id":"o2","text":"“Avoid ever having an ERCP.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. ERCP carries risk, but is sometimes needed; clients are informed before consent."},{"id":"o3","text":"“Avoid multiple pregnancies because you could get gallstones.”","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Not a reasonable prevention teaching."}]},{"id":"acute-pancreatitis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about how it starts?","answer":"Obstruction → trypsin, lipase, elastase released; Duct pressure → rupture → spill; 4 processes: lipolysis, proteolysis, vessel necrosis, inflammation","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"acute-pancreatitis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about vital signs and lungs?","answer":"Fever, ↑ HR, ↓ BP; Lungs: crackles / diminished; dyspnea, orthopnea","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Chronic pancreatitis: enzyme loss, pain and nutrition","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Enzymes work only with food in the gut · → every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase) · Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working"},{"title":"Other signs","text":"Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass · Breathing problems · Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine · 3 Ps of diabetes"},{"title":"Loss of function","text":"Steatorrhea: pale, bulky, frothy, foul · Weight loss, muscle wasting, starvation edema · Diabetes (lost islets)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Chronic pancreatitis on TPN: fingerstick glucose every 2–4 hours; regular insulin in the bag as prescribed.","task":"A client with chronic pancreatitis is receiving TPN. How often does the textbook direct the nurse to check fingerstick glucose?","cue":"Chronic pancreatitis destroys islet cells → less insulin → TPN’s hypertonic glucose → hyperglycemia.","rule":"Chronic pancreatitis on TPN: fingerstick glucose every 2–4 hours; regular insulin in the bag as prescribed.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"chronic-pancreatitis","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Chronic pancreatitis: enzyme loss, pain and nutrition","notes":["51-chronic-pancreatitis"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Enzymes work only with food in the gut; → every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase); Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working","Other signs: Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass; Breathing problems; Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine; 3 Ps of diabetes","Loss of function: Steatorrhea: pale, bulky, frothy, foul; Weight loss, muscle wasting, starvation edema; Diabetes (lost islets)"],"terms":["pancrelipase","steatorrhea","malabsorption"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Chronic pancreatitis","blocks":[{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"The disease","lines":["Progressive, flare-ups + remissions","Fibrosis → insufficiency; no cure"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Types","lines":["Calcifying (alcohol, most common): protein plugs → calcification","Obstructive: sphincter of Oddi, gallstones","Autoimmune: linked to other cancers","Hereditary: SPINK1, CFTR, BRCA2 → 50–60× cancer risk"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Loss of function","lines":["Steatorrhea: pale, bulky, frothy, foul","Weight loss, muscle wasting, starvation edema","Diabetes (lost islets)"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Lungs","lines":["Pleural effusion, infiltrates","Ascites limits breathing; ARDS"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Pain","lines":["Continuous burning / gnawing + flare-ups","Relentless; flares ↑ with fibrosis","Tenderness less than acute"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Other signs","lines":["Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass","Breathing problems","Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine","3 Ps of diabetes"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["ERCP, CT, MRI, EUS","Amylase/lipase normal or mildly ↑","↑ Bilirubin, alk phos; intermittent hyperglycemia"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Pain#2","lines":["Opioids (risk of dependency) + nonopioids"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Pancreatic enzyme replacement (PERT)","lines":["PERT = standard of care","Don't chew or crush; with all meals + snacks","Fewer, less fatty stools = working"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Acid control","lines":["Acid destroys lipase → H₂ blocker / PPI / bicarbonate"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Action Alert: glucose","lines":["Insulin / oral agents","TPN → regular insulin in the bag","Fingerstick glucose q2–4h"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Nutrition","lines":["4000–6000 kcal/day","High carb + protein, low fat","No alcohol; TPN/TEN if needed"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Surgery","lines":["For intractable pain, abscess, pseudocyst","Lap drainage, cholecystectomy, sphincterotomy","Distal pancreatectomy, necrosectomy","Auto islet transplant into the liver"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Home setup","lines":["Toilet close / commode","Finances; home nurse checks pain, enzymes, coping"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Skin","lines":["Clean after each stool + moisture barrier","Report skin breakdown"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Preventing flare-ups","lines":["No caffeine, alcohol, nicotine","Bland, low-fat, high-protein; small high-calorie meals","Enzymes with meals; rest, one floor"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Diabetes and support","lines":["CDE teaching if diabetic","AA, Al-Anon, counseling"]},{"note":"51-chronic-pancreatitis","title":"Why","lines":["Enzymes work only with food in the gut","→ every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase)","Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"chronic-pancreatitis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Enzymes work only with food in the gut; → every meal + snack; don't crush (acid destroys lipase); Judge by stools: fewer, less greasy = working","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"chronic-pancreatitis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about other signs?","answer":"Ascites (fluid wave), LUQ mass; Breathing problems; Steatorrhea, clay stools, weight loss, jaundice, dark urine; 3 Ps of diabetes","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure","cards":[{"title":"Why it is deadly","text":"Leading cancer killer · Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival"},{"title":"Signs","text":"Jaundice first = advanced · Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia · New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance · Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue"},{"title":"Blood clots","text":"VTE common: tumor is thromboplastic · + immobility, surgery"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Priority setting: airway and breathing first, then circulation, then the rest — even when several complications are listed.","task":"A client had a laparoscopic Whipple procedure. Which postoperative complication would be the priority for the nurse to manage?","cue":"Major abdominal surgery limits breathing → atelectasis, pneumonia, PE and ARDS can impair gas exchange → hypoxemia kills faster than high glucose or infection.","rule":"Priority setting: airway and breathing first, then circulation, then the rest — even when several complications are listed.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"pancreatic-cancer","ch":"51","chapters":["51"],"title":"Pancreatic cancer and the Whipple procedure","notes":["51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 51; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why it is deadly: Leading cancer killer; Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival","Signs: Jaundice first = advanced; Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia; New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance; Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue","Blood clots: VTE common: tumor is thromboplastic; + immobility, surgery","Why: Head tumor sits on the bile duct → small tumor still blocks it; Jaundice before pain; But usually already spread → late sign"],"terms":["pancreaticoduodenectomy","anastomosis","jaundice"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Pancreatic cancer — risk, signs and treatment","blocks":[{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Why it is deadly","lines":["Leading cancer killer","Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"The tumor","lines":["Adenocarcinoma, fast-spreading","Head (most common) → compresses bile duct → jaundice","Body/tail → large, palpable; → liver"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Blood clots","lines":["VTE common: tumor is thromboplastic","+ immobility, surgery"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Smoking, obesity, chronic pancreatitis, cirrhosis, > 65, genetics","More in men, Black patients (later diagnosis, less insurance)"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Signs","lines":["Jaundice first = advanced","Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia","New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance","Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Tests","lines":["No diagnostic blood test","CEA ↑; CA 19-9 to monitor","EUS = gold standard","PTC + PTBD drains blocked bile"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Goal","lines":["Mostly palliative: ↓ spread, ↓ pain"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Chemotherapy","lines":["FOLFIRINOX × 6 months (folinic acid, 5-FU, irinotecan, oxaliplatin)","Next: gemcitabine + capecitabine or nab-paclitaxel; gemcitabine alone","Watch fatigue, rash, anorexia, diarrhea"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Pain control","lines":["High-dose morphine before pain peaks","Dependency not a concern"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Radiation and stents","lines":["Radiation: pain relief; no survival gain when advanced","Biliary stents via ERCP or PTC"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Surgery","lines":["Resection = only cure (small tumors)","Whipple / PPPD = head; distal = body/tail","Radical for locally advanced","Laparoscopic growing; open still common"]},{"note":"51-pancreatic-cancer-risk-signs-and-treatme","title":"Why","lines":["Head tumor sits on the bile duct → small tumor still blocks it","Jaundice before pain","But usually already spread → late sign"]}]},{"id":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"The Whipple procedure — surgery and recovery","blocks":[{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Getting ready","lines":["Jejunostomy feeds: low → increase, by pump; watch diarrhea","± TPN via PICC — sterile line care","Follow prealbumin, albumin; NPO 6–8 hr"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"What is removed","lines":["Out: pancreatic head, duodenum, part of jejunum, stomach (part/all), gallbladder ± spleen","3 joins to the jejunum: pancreatic duct, bile duct, stomach"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Complications (Table 51.2)","lines":["Hemorrhage, MI, HF, clots","Atelectasis, pneumonia, PE, ARDS","Unstable diabetes","Ileus, pancreatitis, fistulas, infection, dehiscence"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"After laparoscopic Whipple","lines":["Watch: diabetes, hemorrhage, wound infection, obstruction, abscess","Hemorrhage → pulse, BP, skin color, LOC"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Tubes and drains","lines":["NG low continuous / high intermittent — rests pancreas","Drains dependent, no kinks","Serosanguineous OK; clear, bile-tinged or bloody + ↑ output = leak (days 7–10)","Semi-Fowler"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Critical Rescue: fistula","lines":["Fistula = most common + most serious","Pancreatic fluid corrodes skin; inside → chemical peritonitis (boardlike)","Call surgeon / RRT now"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Action Alert: fluids and shock","lines":["Blood loss + drains + low albumin → third spacing → shock","Watch ↓ BP, ↑ HR, ↓ UO, edema, intake ≫ output","SCDs"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Fluids, electrolytes and glucose","lines":["Isotonic + colloids; Hgb/Hct","Replace Na⁺, K⁺, Cl⁻, Ca²⁺","Glucose high or low; total pancreatectomy → insulin"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"NCLEX pattern (laparoscopic Whipple)","lines":["Bleeding, infection, obstruction, diabetes, abscess"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Equity","lines":["Black patients: more complications, ↑ mortality","Low-volume hospitals, insurance/access gaps"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Where next","lines":["Case manager","Home, SNF or hospice by stage + resources"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Support","lines":["Emotional support; realistic about care needs","Caregiver health at risk → counseling","Chaplain / spiritual support"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Preparing for death","lines":["Diagnosed months before death; palliative","Help with will, visits, funeral wishes","Hospice, support groups"]},{"note":"51-the-whipple-procedure-surgery-and-recove","title":"Why","lines":["3 joins carry digestive fluids","Leak → enzymes eat skin, chemical peritonitis","Drain: serosanguineous = OK; clear / bile-tinged = leak"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"pancreatic-cancer:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client had a laparoscopic Whipple procedure. Which postoperative complication would be the priority for the nurse to manage?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Priority setting: airway and breathing first, then circulation, then the rest — even when several complications are listed.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Respiratory distress","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Airway and breathing come first; atelectasis, pneumonia, PE, ARDS and pulmonary edema are all listed complications."},{"id":"o1","text":"Hyperglycemia","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Important but not before breathing."},{"id":"o2","text":"Wound infection","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Develops later and threatens life less immediately."},{"id":"o3","text":"Deep vein thrombosis","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Prevented with SCDs; respiratory distress is the more immediate threat."}]},{"id":"pancreatic-cancer:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why it is deadly?","answer":"Leading cancer killer; Hidden → found late; very low 5-year survival","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"pancreatic-cancer:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about signs?","answer":"Jaundice first = advanced; Dull pain to the back; weight loss, anorexia; New-onset diabetes, glucose intolerance; Calf pain (clot), GI bleeding, ascites, fatigue","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Nutrition: assess intake, weight change and barriers to eating","cards":[{"title":"Why starvation eats muscle","text":"Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein · Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain · = muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections"},{"title":"What a nutrition assessment includes","text":"History, intake record, access · Labs, food-drug interactions · Exam, measurements, psychosocial"},{"title":"Priority problem","text":"Weight loss: can't access, ingest, digest or absorb"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Full MNA: 24–30 normal, 17–23.5 at risk, < 17 malnourished → refer (RDN, provider).","task":"The nurse completes a full Mini Nutritional Assessment for an older client. The malnutrition indicator score is 15. Which action should the nurse take?","cue":"The MNA adds intake, weight loss, mobility, stress, BMI and body measurements → a low total means the client is already malnourished.","rule":"Full MNA: 24–30 normal, 17–23.5 at risk, < 17 malnourished → refer (RDN, provider).","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"nutrition-screen","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Nutrition: assess intake, weight change and barriers to eating","notes":["52-overview","52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","52-adpie"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why starvation eats muscle: Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein; Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain; = muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections","What a nutrition assessment includes: History, intake record, access; Labs, food-drug interactions; Exam, measurements, psychosocial","Priority problem: Weight loss: can't access, ingest, digest or absorb","Why albumin falls and edema appears: Albumin holds water in vessels; ↓ Protein → ↓ albumin → edema (can hide wasting); Prealbumin changes faster than albumin"],"terms":["malnutrition","cachexia","glossitis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"52-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"52-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Malnutrition = too little, too much or unbalanced intake — both ends","Exemplars: undernutrition (PEU, starvation) and obesity (BMI ≥ 30)","Screen within 24 hr; weigh accurately; oral first","Tube feeding + TPN safety; watch refeeding syndrome","Obesity: diet, activity, behavior, drugs; after bariatric surgery watch airway + leaks"]},{"note":"52-overview","title":"Why starvation eats muscle","lines":["Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein","Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain","= muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections"]},{"note":"52-overview","title":"Why albumin falls and edema appears","lines":["Albumin holds water in vessels","↓ Protein → ↓ albumin → edema (can hide wasting)","Prealbumin changes faster than albumin"]},{"note":"52-overview","title":"Refeeding syndrome","lines":["Starved cells low on phosphate, K⁺, Mg²⁺","Food → insulin → glucose + minerals into cells","Blood ↓ PO₄, ↓ K⁺, ↓ Mg²⁺ → heart failure, dysrhythmias, seizures, respiratory failure","Na⁺/water kept → edema"]},{"note":"52-overview","title":"Why fat around the waist matters most","lines":["Fat releases adipokines (appetite, inflammation)","Central fat = most harmful","Waist > 35 in (F) / > 40 in (M) predicts CAD better than BMI","WHR ≥ 0.95 (M) / ≥ 0.8 (F) = android"]},{"note":"52-overview","title":"Why","lines":["1 L fluid = 1 kg: overnight change = fluid","So same scale, same time (before breakfast), same clothes","5% in a month / 10% in 6 months = real warning"]}]},{"id":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Healthy eating, diets and food sensitivities","blocks":[{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Energy balance","lines":["Intake > use = gain; use > intake = loss","Calories come first — protein burned when calories are short","Many factors: culture, money, activity, drugs"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"How many calories","lines":["EER 1600–3200 kcal/day","↓ to lose weight; ↑ to gain or heal"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Guidelines","lines":["DRIs: 40+ nutrients","Dietary Guidelines (every 5 yr): limit added sugar, saturated fat, sodium, alcohol","MyPlate: fruit, veg, grains, protein, dairy"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"The ideal diet","lines":["Complex carbs, lean protein, mono/polyunsaturated fat","Under- and over-nutrition = one continuum"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Older adults","lines":["Water + fiber prevent constipation"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Vegetarian types (Box 52.2)","lines":["Lacto = dairy · ovo = eggs · lacto-ovo = both","Pescatarian = fish","Vegan = plants only"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Vegans and vitamin B12","lines":["Vegan → B12 anemia","Daily fortified B12 source"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Culture","lines":["Ask, never assume","Unfamiliar hospital food → undernutrition"]},{"note":"52-healthy-eating-diets-and-food-sensitivit","title":"Allergy vs intolerance","lines":["Allergy = immune: nuts, peanuts, shellfish → anaphylaxis","Intolerance = GI: lactose → bloating, diarrhea, gas","Ask the exact reaction"]}]},{"id":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Nutrition screening and body measurements","blocks":[{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"What a nutrition assessment includes","lines":["History, intake record, access","Labs, food-drug interactions","Exam, measurements, psychosocial"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Within 24 hours","lines":["Screen within 24 hr (Joint Commission)","Measured height + weight, weight history, habits","Chewing/swallowing, recent appetite change"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"The MNA","lines":["MNA for older adults","Screen: 12–14 normal, 8–11 at risk, 0–7 malnourished","Full (30): 24–30 normal, 17–23.5 at risk, < 17 malnourished","Malnourished → nutrition consult"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Screening questions (Box 52.3)","lines":["Loss (wounds, diarrhea), higher need (fever, burns)","NPO ≥ 3 days; unexplained weight loss","Chewing, swallowing, teeth; dialysis; pressure injuries; cachexia"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Height and weight","lines":["Delegate to AP; report at once","Measure (patients misjudge)","Same scale, same clothes, no shoes","Knee-height caliper; bed scale — note linens, lift lines"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Action Alert","lines":["Same time, before breakfast","Weight = most reliable fluid indicator"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Weight loss that matters","lines":["5% / 1 month or 10% / 6 months → evaluate"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"BMI (Table 52.1)","lines":["kg ÷ m²","< 18.5 under · 18.5–24.9 normal · 25–29.9 over · ≥ 30 obese","e.g., 123.4 kg, 1.73 m → 41"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"BMI limits","lines":["Can't separate fat vs muscle","Poor in older adults","Ignores sex, race (Asian adults more fat at same BMI)"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Older adults' weight","lines":["Weight ↑ until ~60","Some eat less, some don't — don't assume"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Body surface area","lines":["BSA → chemo dosing, burns","√(cm × kg ÷ 3600)","Avg 1.9 m² men, 1.6 m² women"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Skinfolds and circumferences","lines":["Triceps + subscapular skinfolds","Mid-arm, calf circumference: midpoint, gentle, cm"]},{"note":"52-nutrition-screening-and-body-measurement","title":"Why","lines":["Bed scale weighs everything on it","Extra blankets/pump = false \"gain\"","Record linens, lift lines → honest trend"]}]},{"id":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Undernutrition — the nutrition exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"The problem","lines":["Multinutrient shortfall","Hospital causes: no time to feed, NPO for tests/surgery"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Complications (Box 52.4)","lines":["↓ Cardiac output, ↓ vital capacity","Infections, poor wound healing","Cachexia, ↓ muscle, weakness, cold intolerance"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Protein-energy undernutrition (PEU)","lines":["Marasmus = calories ↓, wasted, proteins OK","Kwashiorkor = protein ↓, weight normal-ish, proteins low","Starvation = no nutrients (acute, severe)"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Acute and chronic PEU","lines":["Acute: catabolic (infection, stress, injury) + not fed","Chronic: cancer, ESKD, liver disease, neuro disease"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Eating disorders","lines":["Anorexia: starvation from fear of fatness (+ BDD)","Bulimia: binge + purge; death from starvation, infection, suicide","Binge eating: binges, no purging","→ psychiatric consult"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"How common","lines":["462 million underweight worldwide","50–71% of older adults malnourished on admission; ⅓ get worse in hospital","Protocol: readmissions 42% → 13.3%"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Hair and eyes","lines":["Hair easy to pull / dull = protein","Corkscrew hair = vitamin C; alopecia = zinc","Dry eyes, Bitot spots = vitamin A"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Mouth and gut","lines":["Bleeding gums = vitamin C","Glossitis = B vitamins, zinc","Cheilosis = pyridoxine, iron"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Skin, muscle and bone","lines":["Bruising = vitamin C; dermatitis = niacin","Fat loss = calories; edema = protein","Bone pain = vitamin D"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Blood, nerves and heart","lines":["Anemia = B12, iron, folate","Bleeding = vitamin K","Thiamine: confabulation, neuropathy, heart failure","Dysrhythmias = magnesium"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Older adults (Box 52.5)","lines":["Teeth/dentures, dry mouth, drugs, appetite","Failure to thrive = 3 of 5","Depression, loneliness, income, transportation","Refer mental health / social work"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"History","lines":["Usual intake, preferences, appetite, weight change","24-hour recall","Can they afford food? Food deserts?"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Action Alert: swallowing","lines":["Dysphagia → undernutrition, dehydration, aspiration pneumonia"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Physical and psychosocial","lines":["Inspect hair, eyes, mouth, skin","3-day calorie count (AP report intake + choking)","Money, living/cooking arrangements, mood"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Labs","lines":["↓ Albumin, prealbumin, transferrin","↓ Hgb, Hct, cholesterol","Read with the whole patient"]},{"note":"52-undernutrition-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Priority problem","lines":["Weight loss: can't access, ingest, digest or absorb"]}]},{"id":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Feeding the undernourished patient — meals, supplements and drugs","blocks":[{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Why oral","lines":["Oral = preferred: immunity, safer, cheaper, enjoyable"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Meals","lines":["High-calorie (shakes, cheese, supplements)","Six small meals","Pureed / dental soft if chewing is hard"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Promoting intake (Box 52.6)","lines":["Remove bedpans, basins, odors","Pain med / antiemetic ≥ 1 hr before","Toilet + mouth care first; glasses, hearing aids; chair","Open cartons, cut food, record % eaten; protect mealtime"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Supplements","lines":["Ensure, Sustacal, Carnation (lactose-free)","Glucerna for diabetes","Duocal (carb + fat), Beneprotein (protein)"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Drugs","lines":["Multivitamin, zinc, iron; follow Hgb/Hct","Iron → constipation; zinc → N/V"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Settings and resources","lines":["Case manager: can they afford supplements?","Home TEN/TPN → home nutrition team incl. pharmacist"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Teaching","lines":["High-calorie, high-protein","Iron just before / with meals; it constipates","Fiber, fluids, exercise"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Weight + protein maintained, hydrated"]},{"note":"52-feeding-the-undernourished-patient-meals","title":"Why","lines":["Hospital undernutrition = barriers around the food","Out of reach, unopened, bedpan nearby, untreated nausea, interruptions","Record % eaten to catch it"]}]},{"id":"52-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"52-adpie","title":"¶ Every patient in this chapter is watched","lines":["Watch: weight trend, aspiration / fluid overload, refeeding shifts","Do: screen in 24 hr, weigh right, oral first, TEN/TPN safety rules","Support realistic, respectful weight change"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Screen ≤ 24 hr; 5% / 10% loss","24-hr recall, culture, swallowing","Money, access, food deserts; mood","Obesity: motivation, past attempts (RESPECT)"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["Height + weight → BMI; waist, WHR","MNA; hair, eyes, mouth, skin for deficiencies","Skinfolds (intertrigo)","On feeding: mark, residuals, lungs, edema"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Labs","lines":["↓ Albumin, prealbumin, transferrin","Glucose, BUN, electrolytes","PO₄, K⁺, Mg²⁺ when refeeding; triglycerides on lipids"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["Undernutrition: weight loss + aspiration, refeeding, infection","Obesity: weight gain","Bariatric: airway, leak, VTE, dumping, skin"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Weight + protein maintained; no aspiration; electrolytes stable","Obesity: 5–10% loss, nutrient-dense, no overeating","Bariatric: airway, no leak, no infection"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Undernutrition and TEN","lines":["Oral first; small high-calorie meals; Box 52.6","TEN: x-ray, mark, remove if dislodged","HOB ≥ 30°, residuals q6h, flush 30 mL q4h + with drugs","4 hr formula; rotate G-tube"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Parenteral and refeeding","lines":["TPN: pump, 2nd nurse, never catch up, D10W if late","Daily weight, glucose, electrolytes","Stop lipids for fat overload","Refeeding: ↓ PO₄, K⁺, Mg²⁺ → call"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Obesity and bariatric surgery","lines":["RESPECT; −500 kcal/day, walk 20 min","Drugs: orlistat multivitamin; GLP-1 pancreatitis; pregnancy check","Post-op: airway, SCDs, skinfolds, never move NG","1-oz liquids → pureed; leak + dumping watch; lifelong supplements"]},{"note":"52-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Weight trend toward goal (same method)","% eaten; albumin, prealbumin up","No aspiration, clogs, diarrhea, refeeding","Bariatric: no leak; knows diet stages, supplements, what to report"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"nutrition-screen:case","kind":"choice","scene":"The nurse completes a full Mini Nutritional Assessment for an older client. The malnutrition indicator score is 15. Which action should the nurse take?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Full MNA: 24–30 normal, 17–23.5 at risk, < 17 malnourished → refer (RDN, provider).","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Contact the health care provider for a nutrition consultation","correct":true,"why":"Correct. On the full MNA (out of 30), a score under 17 means malnourished — the client needs a nutrition consult."},{"id":"o1","text":"Discuss weight-loss strategies with the client","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The client is undernourished, not overweight."},{"id":"o2","text":"Document that the client is adequately nourished","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. 24–30 is normal; 15 is malnourished."},{"id":"o3","text":"Ask the dietitian for a lower-calorie diet","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The client needs more nutrition, not less."}]},{"id":"nutrition-screen:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why starvation eats muscle?","answer":"Short on calories → glycogen → fat → body protein; Protein → liver makes glucose for the brain; = muscle wasting, ↓ vital capacity, ↓ cardiac output, poor healing, infections","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"nutrition-screen:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what a nutrition assessment includes?","answer":"History, intake record, access; Labs, food-drug interactions; Exam, measurements, psychosocial","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Tube feeding: placement, aspiration prevention and medication safety","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"One danger: formula in the lungs · X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying · Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell"},{"title":"Ethics","text":"Advance directive? · None → durable power of attorney decides · Neither → ethics committee (consensus first) · DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA"},{"title":"Short-term tubes","text":"Nasoenteric (Dobhoff) < 4 weeks: NG, ND, NJ · Small-bore < 12 Fr"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Feeding decisions: advance directive first; if none, the DPOA; if neither, the ethics committee seeks consensus before acting.","task":"A comatose client with terminal cancer has a durable power of attorney for health care but no advance directive. Total enteral nutrition is prescribed. Which action is appropriate?","cue":"Artificial nutrition is a treatment the client may accept or refuse → the DPOA voices the client’s wishes when the client can’t.","rule":"Feeding decisions: advance directive first; if none, the DPOA; if neither, the ethics committee seeks consensus before acting.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"enteral-nutrition","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Tube feeding: placement, aspiration prevention and medication safety","notes":["52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: One danger: formula in the lungs; X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying; Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell","Ethics: Advance directive?; None → durable power of attorney decides; Neither → ethics committee (consensus first); DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA","Short-term tubes: Nasoenteric (Dobhoff) < 4 weeks: NG, ND, NJ; Small-bore < 12 Fr"],"terms":["enteral","gastrostomy","aspiration"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Tube feeding (total enteral nutrition)","blocks":[{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Who gets TEN","lines":["1 Can't eat enough · 2 Can't swallow (permanent) · 3 Temporarily can't eat","Provider prescribes; RDN picks product + amount"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Ethics","lines":["Advance directive?","None → durable power of attorney decides","Neither → ethics committee (consensus first)","DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Short-term tubes","lines":["Nasoenteric (Dobhoff) < 4 weeks: NG, ND, NJ","Small-bore < 12 Fr"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Long-term tubes","lines":["PEG, PEG/J","Jejunostomy / DPEJ bypasses stomach (gastric disease, obstruction, poor emptying)"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Feeding methods","lines":["Bolus: set amount q4h","Continuous: nonstop by pump/gravity","Cyclic: off ≥ 6 hr/day (morning)","Follow orders for free water"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Placement","lines":["X-ray first placement (always)","In doubt → x-ray again","Mark exit point","Not auscultation / pH / CO₂","Dislodged? → remove now"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Securing and the site","lines":["Skin protectant + secure","G/J tube: rotate 360° daily, ¼ inch play","Ports → fewer openings"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Action Alert: tube won't move","lines":["Can't rotate → notify now (buried disk)","Dry sterile dressing daily"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Residuals and the pump","lines":["Residual q6h (and before bolus/drugs with large-bore NG)","Residual → provider: slow or stop","Check pump + rate"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Preventing contamination","lines":["Bag + tubing q24–48h","Only 4 hr of formula in the bag","Open cans: refrigerate, toss after 24 hr","Clean gloves (sterile if immunocompromised); no food dye"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Preventing aspiration","lines":["HOB ≥ 30° during + 1 hr after bolus","Semi-Fowler always for continuous/cyclic"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Monitoring","lines":["BUN, electrolytes, Hct, prealbumin, glucose","Diarrhea; weight, I&O"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Prevent clogs","lines":["Clogs = most common problem","Flush between drugs; liquids when possible","Don't mix drugs with formula; don't crush SR"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Flushing","lines":["30 mL water, ≥ 30-mL syringe","q4h, before + after drugs, after interruptions"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Unclogging","lines":["Warm water, piston syringe, clamp 20 min","No cola / cranberry (worsens clog)","Then pancreatic enzyme → kit → replace"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Aspiration","lines":["Misplaced tube → aspiration","Fever, dyspnea, chest pain, ↓ lower-lobe sounds","Lungs q4–8h; CXR → antibiotics"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Distention, nausea and vomiting","lines":["Overfeeding → check residuals","Slow/stop, restart slower"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Fluid and electrolytes","lines":["Overload: edema, weight gain, crackles, ↑ BP, bounding pulse","Most common: ↑ K⁺ and ↓ Na⁺","Kidney + high K⁺ → low-K⁺ formula"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Diarrhea","lines":["Hyperosmolar + fast → diarrhea (iso-osmolar)","Foul → C. diff","Liquid drugs (acetaminophen, furosemide, phenytoin) → dilute/change"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Critical Rescue: refeeding syndrome","lines":["Starved + fed fast → shift","↓ Phosphate, ↓ K⁺ (and ↓ Mg²⁺)","Heart failure, edema, rhabdo, seizures, hemolysis, respiratory failure","Call the provider now"]},{"note":"52-tube-feeding-total-enteral-nutrition","title":"Why","lines":["One danger: formula in the lungs","X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying","Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"enteral-nutrition:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A comatose client with terminal cancer has a durable power of attorney for health care but no advance directive. Total enteral nutrition is prescribed. Which action is appropriate?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Feeding decisions: advance directive first; if none, the DPOA; if neither, the ethics committee seeks consensus before acting.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Contact the durable power of attorney","correct":true,"why":"Correct. The DPOA makes health decisions when the client can’t."},{"id":"o1","text":"Begin the tube feeding immediately","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The client’s designated decision-maker should be involved first."},{"id":"o2","text":"Consult the ethics committee first","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The committee is for situations with no directive and no DPOA."},{"id":"o3","text":"Ask the provider whether to start nutrition","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. The provider already prescribed it; the decision now rests with the DPOA."}]},{"id":"enteral-nutrition:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"One danger: formula in the lungs; X-ray = where · mark = moved? · HOB up = gravity · residuals = stomach emptying; Out of place → remove; auscultation can't tell","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"enteral-nutrition:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about ethics?","answer":"Advance directive?; None → durable power of attorney decides; Neither → ethics committee (consensus first); DPOA, no AD → contact the DPOA","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Parenteral nutrition: line, glucose and infusion safety","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Into a vein when the gut can't be used · Can give all nutrients"},{"title":"Compounded vs multichamber bags","text":"Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days · Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized · ASPEN: premixed / multichamber"},{"title":"Line care","text":"Tubing q24h · Dressing: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"TPN: never catch up; if the bag is unavailable, D10W or D20W per the provider; monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.","task":"A client’s TPN is 2 hours behind schedule. What should the nurse do?","cue":"Faster TPN → more glucose than insulin can handle → hyperglycemia and osmotic diuresis, plus fluid overload.","rule":"TPN: never catch up; if the bag is unavailable, D10W or D20W per the provider; monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"parenteral-nutrition","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Parenteral nutrition: line, glucose and infusion safety","notes":["52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Into a vein when the gut can't be used; Can give all nutrients","Compounded vs multichamber bags: Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days; Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized; ASPEN: premixed / multichamber","Line care: Tubing q24h; Dressing: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days","Why: TPN = concentrated glucose; insulin rises to match; Too fast → glucose + fluid surge; Stopped suddenly → hypoglycemia → bridge with D10W/D20W"],"terms":["parenteral","hyperglycemia","refeeding syndrome"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Parenteral nutrition — PPN and TPN","blocks":[{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"What it is","lines":["Into a vein when the gut can't be used","Can give all nutrients"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Compounded vs multichamber bags","lines":["Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days","Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized","ASPEN: premixed / multichamber"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"PPN","lines":["Large arm vein, short term","Fat-based; large volumes","Watch site irritation"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Critical Rescue: fat overload","lines":["Lipids → fever, ↑ triglycerides, clotting problems, MODS","Stop the lipid, report now"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"TPN","lines":["Central line (neck/chest, tunneled, port, PICC)","Hypertonic, high glucose → pump"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Checks","lines":["Bag vs prescription; second nurse check","Insulin as ordered; pump rate"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"If the bag isn't there or is behind","lines":["No bag → D10W / D20W","Behind → never catch up"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Monitoring","lines":["Daily weight; electrolytes + glucose daily","I&O; site for infection"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Line care","lines":["Tubing q24h","Dressing: gauze 48 hr, transparent 7 days"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Complications","lines":["Solution or catheter (infection)","Overload with heart/kidney disease","K⁺, Na⁺ swings (insulin); hypercalcemia"]},{"note":"52-parenteral-nutrition-ppn-and-tpn","title":"Why","lines":["TPN = concentrated glucose; insulin rises to match","Too fast → glucose + fluid surge","Stopped suddenly → hypoglycemia → bridge with D10W/D20W"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"parenteral-nutrition:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client’s TPN is 2 hours behind schedule. What should the nurse do?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"TPN: never catch up; if the bag is unavailable, D10W or D20W per the provider; monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Continue at the prescribed rate and follow agency policy","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Never try to catch up by increasing the rate."},{"id":"o1","text":"Increase the rate to catch up by the end of the shift","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Speeding it floods the client with glucose and fluid."},{"id":"o2","text":"Double the rate for 1 hour","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Same danger."},{"id":"o3","text":"Stop the TPN and restart it tomorrow","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Stopping suddenly risks hypoglycemia."}]},{"id":"parenteral-nutrition:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Into a vein when the gut can't be used; Can give all nutrients","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"parenteral-nutrition:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about compounded vs multichamber bags?","answer":"Compounded: personalized, error risk, 7–9 days; Multichamber: 12+ months, less customized; ASPEN: premixed / multichamber","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery: individualized care","cards":[{"title":"What it is","text":"Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation · Excess fat but often poor nutrients"},{"title":"History","text":"Appetite, attitude to food · Drugs, activity, family history · Past attempts and results"},{"title":"Priority problem and setting","text":"Weight gain stressing organs · Bariatric room: single, 18 m², 60-in door, 5-ft clearance · Low-air-loss bariatric bed; 1000-lb toilet, bars, lift · Right-sized gowns, BP cuffs, SCDs"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Bariatric discharge: diet stages, supplements, fluids (not juice), report fever, vomiting, back/shoulder/epigastric pain, wound changes, leg swelling, chest pain, dyspnea.","task":"Which information should the nurse include in discharge teaching after bariatric surgery? Select all that apply.","cue":"A small pouch and fresh anastomosis need small volumes; sugar draws water into the jejunum (dumping); referred pain and low output may mean a leak.","rule":"Bariatric discharge: diet stages, supplements, fluids (not juice), report fever, vomiting, back/shoulder/epigastric pain, wound changes, leg swelling, chest pain, dyspnea.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"weight-bariatric","ch":"52","chapters":["52"],"title":"Obesity treatment and bariatric recovery: individualized care","notes":["52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after"],"book":"Final · Ignatavicius chapter 52; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["What it is: Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation; Excess fat but often poor nutrients","History: Appetite, attitude to food; Drugs, activity, family history; Past attempts and results","Priority problem and setting: Weight gain stressing organs; Bariatric room: single, 18 m², 60-in door, 5-ft clearance; Low-air-loss bariatric bed; 1000-lb toilet, bars, lift; Right-sized gowns, BP cuffs, SCDs","Why: Small BP cuff → falsely high reading; Wrong-size SCDs, gowns → unsafe care; Right-sized equipment = accurate + safe"],"terms":["orlistat","semaglutide","bariatric"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Obesity — the nutrition exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"What it is","lines":["Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation","Excess fat but often poor nutrients"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Definitions","lines":["Overweight 25–29.9 · obese ≥ 30","Class I 30–< 35 · II 35–< 40 · III ≥ 40"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Where the fat is","lines":["Waist > 35 in (F) / > 40 in (M) = central obesity","→ CAD, stroke, DM, cancer, sleep apnea","WHR ≥ 0.95 (M) / ≥ 0.8 (F) = android"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Complications (Box 52.10)","lines":["CAD, HTN, lipids, PAD","Insulin resistance, type 2 DM","Gallstones, incontinence, slow healing","OA, stroke, depression, sleep apnea"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Diet and activity","lines":["Saturated + trans fat, cholesterol","Inactivity (time, habit, mobility)"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Drugs that cause weight gain","lines":["Steroids, estrogen, NSAIDs","Antidepressants, antipsychotics, antiepileptics","Some antihypertensives, antidiabetics"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Genetics","lines":["Polygenic = most common","Lifestyle still matters → focus on what they control","Respond: \"genes can contribute\" + explore history, activity, intake"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"How common","lines":["Doubled since 1980; ⅓ of world","Leading preventable death"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Prevention","lines":["HP 2030: ↓ adults with obesity","↑ veg, fruit, whole grains, Ca, K, vit D · ↓ sugar, saturated fat, sodium","5% loss helps; walk 20 min/day"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Approach","lines":["RESPECT: rapport, safety, privacy, realistic goals, compassion, tact"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"History","lines":["Appetite, attitude to food","Drugs, activity, family history","Past attempts and results"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Physical","lines":["Accurate height + weight","Lift skinfolds: intertrigo (Candida), panniculitis"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Their view of their weight","Meaning of food, motivation, barriers, support","Depression > 2 weeks → mental health"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Priority problem and setting","lines":["Weight gain stressing organs","Bariatric room: single, 18 m², 60-in door, 5-ft clearance","Low-air-loss bariatric bed; 1000-lb toilet, bars, lift","Right-sized gowns, BP cuffs, SCDs"]},{"note":"52-obesity-the-nutrition-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Small BP cuff → falsely high reading","Wrong-size SCDs, gowns → unsafe care","Right-sized equipment = accurate + safe"]}]},{"id":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Treating obesity — diet, exercise, drugs and behavior","blocks":[{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Diet programs","lines":["Fasting / VLCD 200–800 kcal: cardiac check, supervision; ketosis; regain","Balanced 1200–1800 kcal = practical","Low-carb: popular, mixed results"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"The diet plan","lines":["Evidence-based, balanced, low-risk, practical","Fits the patient's life"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Calorie math","lines":["−500 kcal/day ≈ 1 lb/week; −1000 ≈ 2 lb/week","5–10% loss → better glucose, cholesterol, BP"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Exercise","lines":["Diet + exercise > diet alone; loses fat, keeps muscle","Walk 20 min/day, build up"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Orlistat","lines":["Lipase blocker","Daily multivitamin","Fat < 30% or loose, oily stools, cramps","Rare liver injury"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Semaglutide and liraglutide","lines":["Injected; ↓ appetite, ↑ fullness","Watch pancreatitis (ALT/AST)","Semaglutide: rare thyroid tumor","Liraglutide + insulin → hypoglycemia"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Phentermine-topiramate","lines":["No glaucoma, hyperthyroidism","Birth defects → check pregnancy; not breastfeeding"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Bupropion-naltrexone","lines":["No uncontrolled HTN, seizures, eating disorders, withdrawal","Not with other bupropion","Watch suicidal ideation"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Setmelanotide","lines":["MC4R (genetic obesity); injected","Priapism, mood changes","Skin exams (darker skin, moles)"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Other options","lines":["Cryolipolysis ≠ obesity treatment","Hydrogel fills ~25% of stomach → ~5% loss","Complementary: mixed evidence"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Behavioral management","lines":["Food diary + mood/events","Stimulus control, reinforcement, cognitive restructuring","Overeaters Anonymous"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Self-care","lines":["↓ Fat; don't rely on appetite drugs","Food + mood journal"]},{"note":"52-treating-obesity-diet-exercise-drugs-and","title":"Why","lines":["1 lb fat ≈ 3500 kcal → −500/day ≈ 1 lb/week","Target 5–10% loss, not \"ideal\" weight","Already improves BP, cholesterol, glucose"]}]},{"id":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Bariatric surgery — care before and after","blocks":[{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Candidates","lines":["Failed other treatment + BMI ≥ 40, or ≥ 35 with health risks","Newer guidelines: ≥ 35 alone; ≥ 30 with metabolic disease","Liposuction ≠ obesity treatment"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"The operations","lines":["Band, sleeve (¾ removed) = restrictive","Duodenal switch: ~80% removed, malabsorptive","Roux-en-Y = most common; fast loss, more complications","Vagal-blocking device"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Commitment","lines":["Lifelong lifestyle change","Often resolves DM, HTN, sleep apnea"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Risks and testing","lines":["↑ Lung + clot complications","Psychological evaluation (depression, substance use)","Not if not oriented or lacks strength/mobility","Nurse reinforces teaching"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Airway first","lines":["Airway = priority","Semi-Fowler; SpO₂; CPAP/BiPAP"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Safety and skin","lines":["Extra staff; side rails off the skin","Skinfolds: clean, dry, padding","Turn q2h (trapeze); PT"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Clots, wound, bladder","lines":["SCDs + anticoagulant","Binder (open surgery)","Catheter out ≤ 24 hr; daily girth"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Pain","lines":["PCA ≤ 24 hr, then liquid oral opioids"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Action Alert: NG tube","lines":["Never reposition the NG (suture line)","Out on day 2 if flatus"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Diet progression","lines":["Clear (1-oz cups) → full liquids (discharge) → pureed ~1 week (5 Tbsp) → soft → solid ~week 8","Slow, small, stop before full","High protein; no fatty or sugary foods"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Dumping syndrome","lines":["Food too fast into intestine → ≤ 30 min after eating","Tachycardia, nausea, diarrhea, cramps, sweating, dizziness","Diet → antidiarrheals / acarbose"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Critical Rescue: anastomotic leak","lines":["#1 serious complication / cause of death","↑ Back, shoulder, abdominal pain, restlessness","Unexplained tachycardia, oliguria → surgeon now"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Teaching topics","lines":["Diet progression, supplements, fluids","Wound: cover to shower; no lifting","Report: fever, back/shoulder/epigastric pain, wound signs, leg swelling, chest pain, dyspnea"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Supplements for life","lines":["Vitamin D, B-complex, iron, calcium","Adherence = success"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Follow-up and resources","lines":["Follow-up for years","Panniculectomy at 18–24 months","OA, ASMBS"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Nutrient-dense, no overeating; no infection","Teach: report back/shoulder/abdominal pain; 5 Tbsp meals"]},{"note":"52-bariatric-surgery-care-before-and-after","title":"Why","lines":["Pouch holds 1–2 oz, empties straight into jejunum","Big/sugary meal → water pulled in → dumping","So 1-oz cups, 5 Tbsp meals, stop before full (protect the anastomosis)"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"weight-bariatric:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client starting orlistat for weight loss asks how to avoid side effects. What should the nurse teach?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Orlistat: fat < 30% of intake, daily multivitamin, monitor liver enzymes (rare liver injury).","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Keep fat under 30% of daily intake and take a daily multivitamin","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Unabsorbed fat causes loose stools, cramps and nausea; fat-soluble vitamins are lost."},{"id":"o1","text":"Take it with a high-fat meal to make it work","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. More fat means more GI side effects."},{"id":"o2","text":"Avoid all vitamins while taking it","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. A multivitamin is needed."},{"id":"o3","text":"Expect constipation, so take a laxative daily","correct":false,"why":"Incorrect. Loose stools are the problem, not constipation."}]},{"id":"weight-bariatric:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what it is?","answer":"Adipokines dysregulated → appetite, inflammation; Excess fat but often poor nutrients","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"weight-bariatric:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about history?","answer":"Appetite, attitude to food; Drugs, activity, family history; Past attempts and results","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Protect the lungs: exposures, smoking and prevention","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"Prevention means stopping the injury before cells change. Most respiratory questions here come down to smoke, vaccines and exposures."},{"title":"Observe","text":"Smoking / vaping history · Occupational dust, fumes, asbestos, radon · Secondhand smoke · Missed vaccines"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"5 A's: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange · Screen every visit · Offer counseling + meds together"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Answer the task using the patient’s actual findings","task":"An adult who smokes works with airborne dust. Which assessment best supports a lung-protection plan?","cue":"Smoking / vaping history · Occupational dust, fumes, asbestos, radon · Secondhand smoke · Missed vaccines","rule":"Identify the mechanism, match the cues and choose the response that addresses this situation.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"resp-prevention","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Protect the lungs: exposures, smoking and prevention","notes":["22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","22-adpie","22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Prevention means stopping the injury before cells change. Most respiratory questions here come down to smoke, vaccines and exposures.","Findings: Smoking / vaping history; Occupational dust, fumes, asbestos, radon; Secondhand smoke; Missed vaccines","Nursing actions: 5 A's: Ask, Advise, Assess, Assist, Arrange; Screen every visit; Offer counseling + meds together","Hemoptysis, weight loss or a changed chronic cough in a smoker → workup for lung cancer."],"terms":["nicotine","hypoxia"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Inhalation injury and smoking cessation","blocks":[{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"The single biggest modifiable factor","lines":["Inhaled irritants, above all cigarette smoke = #1 cause of chronic respiratory problems","Damaging parts: tar, nicotine, carbon monoxide"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Quantify it","lines":["Pack-years = packs/day × years","Ask separately: secondhand (same space) + thirdhand (smoke on hair, clothes, surfaces)"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Catch the social smoker","lines":["Ask: \"Do you ever smoke in social situations?\"","They don't call themselves smokers — still damage + addiction"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Gauge dependence","lines":["Smoke on waking? Wake at night to smoke? Hard not to where prohibited? Smoke when ill?","More yes = more dependent"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"The 5 As","lines":["Ask · Advise · Assess willingness · Assist · Arrange follow-up","Under 3 minutes, a nursing duty at every encounter"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Nicotine replacement","lines":["OTC: patch, gum, lozenge · Rx: nasal spray, inhaler","Never smoke on NRT → combined nicotine → stroke/MI risk"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Drugs","lines":["Bupropion → less craving, withdrawal, depression · black box: suicidal thoughts → patient + family report any change in thinking/behavior now","Varenicline → blocks nicotine receptors → smoking less rewarding"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Vaping and hookah","lines":["Hookah ≥ cigarettes in exposure (not safer)","EVALI (vaping lung injury) even with < 2 years of vaping; higher risk with THC products"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Occupational and environmental screening — I PREPARE","lines":["I PREPARE: Investigate exposures · Present work · Residence · Environment · Past work · Activities/hobbies · Resources/referrals · Educate","High-risk: coal miners, stone masons, welders, woodworkers, potters, bakers, printers, grain + foundry workers"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Why","lines":["Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential","5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages","\"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it"]}]},{"id":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Questions to ask this patient","blocks":[{"note":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Why","lines":["Dyspnea has no lab value → measure what they can no longer do (blocks, stairs)","\"Show me how you use it\" = the inhaler assessment; most \"adherent\" patients use it wrong"]}]},{"id":"22-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["The order = triage: observation catches trouble before equipment does","Stridor and a silent wheeze are heard, not measured; SpO₂ can be normal while the airway closes"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Why#2","lines":["Airway clearance outranks gas exchange when secretions/obstruction are the cause (clearing it is the gas-exchange fix)","Restlessness = hypoxia, not anxiety → don't sedate it"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Why#3","lines":["Goal = lowest effective FiO₂, not \"SpO₂ 100%\"","Too much → absorptive atelectasis, lung injury, blunted drive in retainers"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Why#4","lines":["Independent care is mostly mechanical: position, humidity, hydration, incentive spirometry","Because the problems are mechanical: alveoli collapse, secretions plug, chest can't expand"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Why#5","lines":["Biggest evaluation error: less noise ≠ better","Silent chest = no air moving · calm after restless = hypoxia → obtundation"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"¶ Respiratory assessment always answers the same","lines":["Same order every time: airway open? → air moving? → reaching alveoli that exchange?","Applies to any breathing problem, before the diagnosis is known"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Pack-years; exposures (I PREPARE); allergies; family/childhood lung disease; travel; immunizations","Drugs that damage lungs (bleomycin, amiodarone) or cause cough (ACE inhibitors, ARBs)","Symptom story: cough timing, sputum amount + color, hemoptysis, chest pain","Grade dyspnea by what they can no longer do (blocks, stairs, pillows)"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Inspect","lines":["Position + work of breathing first: tripod, pursed lips, accessory muscles, retractions, seesaw — before the oximeter","Rate, depth, rhythm","Barrel chest (AP:lateral → 1:1); clubbing (nail angle 160° → 180°)","Central cyanosis: lips, oral mucosa, conjunctivae","Chronic signs: weight loss, thin limbs, big neck/chest muscles"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Palpate and percuss","lines":["Fremitus ↑ over consolidation, ↓ over fluid or air; crepitus","Trachea pushed away (tension pneumo, effusion) or pulled toward (atelectasis, resection)","Expansion: symmetric?","Percussion: hyperresonant = trapped air, dull = fluid or solid"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Auscultate and measure","lines":["Apex → base, side to side, mouth open, on skin","Name each sound by its mechanism","SpO₂ as a trend, ETCO₂, ABGs by ROME, imaging, PFTs"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Independent","lines":["Upright / high-Fowler; cough, deep breathing, incentive spirometer on schedule; reposition","Fluids thin secretions; pace activity, cluster care","Humidify O₂ above 4 L/min; protect ears, neck, nose","O₂ safety: signage, no fire hazards","Cessation (5 As) every visit; stay with a client in distress"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Dependent","lines":["O₂ titrated to the ordered target with the right device","Nonrebreather: check valve + flaps hourly; never block the Venturi port","Bronchodilators, steroids, mucolytics, antibiotics","CPAP reopens alveoli; BiPAP raises tidal volume; prepare for intubation"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Procedure care","lines":["NPO until gag returns after throat anesthesia","After bronchoscopy: VS, breath sounds, SpO₂ every 15 min × 2 h","Thoracentesis: position + coach → CXR, watch for pneumothorax","After lung biopsy: check q4h × 24 h; ↓/absent breath sounds = report now"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"Collaborative","lines":["RT (devices, technique); pulmonary rehab; dietitian (weight loss)","Cessation + home O₂ referrals — backup plan for power outages"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"¶ The cues that named the problem","lines":["The cues that named the problem measure the fix","Work of breathing settles; sounds diminish; SpO₂ on target with less O₂; ABGs normalize","Speaks full sentences; walks further","Teach-back: inhaler technique, O₂ safety, when to return"]},{"note":"22-adpie","title":"¶ Unmet looks like: stridor, a wheeze","lines":["Unmet: stridor, a wheeze gone silent, ↑ ETCO₂ with normal SpO₂","New tracheal deviation + absent sounds","Restless → then quiet","→ escalate, don’t re-plan"]}]},{"id":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"The airway, lungs and aging in detail","blocks":[{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Nose and sinuses","lines":["Nose: smell (CN I); septum, rich blood supply","Turbinates → filter, warm, humidify","Sinuses: voice resonance, lighter skull, cushion trauma"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"The three parts of the pharynx","lines":["Nasopharynx: adenoids, eustachian tubes","Oropharynx: air + food; palatine tonsils","Laryngopharynx: where airway and esophagus split"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"The larynx and its cartilages","lines":["Thyroid cartilage = Adam’s apple; cricoid below","Cricothyroid membrane → emergency cricothyroidotomy","Glottis between true cords; epiglottis covers it"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Why","lines":["Upper airway: warm, clean, protect","Bypass it → add humidity and suction"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Trachea and bronchi","lines":["Trachea: 6–10 C-rings, open at the back (shared wall with the esophagus)","Splits at the carina","Right bronchus wider, shorter, more vertical → tubes and aspiration go right","Cilia + mucus sweep particles out"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Bronchioles, alveoli and surfactant","lines":["Bronchioles: no cartilage → held open by elastic recoil","~290 million alveoli ≈ a tennis court","Exchange only in alveoli","Type II pneumocytes → surfactant; none → atelectasis"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"The two lungs and the pleura","lines":["Right: 3 lobes, 55–60% of function","Left: 2 lobes","Apex above the clavicle; base on the diaphragm","Parietal (chest wall) + visceral (lung) pleura, fluid between"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Two blood supplies","lines":["Bronchial: feeds lung tissue, no exchange","Pulmonary: RV → alveolar capillaries → pulmonary veins → LA","Hard breathing recruits back and abdominal muscles"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Why#2","lines":["No cartilage → airways collapse without recoil","Right bronchus → check both sides after intubation; aspiration → right lower lobe"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"What changes","lines":["↓ diffusion, ↓ recoil → airways close early","RV ↑, VC ↓","↓ cough, cilia, IgA; macrophages altered","Weaker diaphragm; slack vocal cords","↑ pulmonary vascular resistance","AP ↑, kyphoscoliosis, stiff chest wall, osteoporosis","Blunted response to hypoxia and hypercarbia"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"What the nurse does","lines":["Turn, cough, deep breathe, incentive spirometer","Upright → less V/Q mismatch","Cheyne-Stokes can be normal in older adults","Oral hygiene; talk face to face","Check LOC and cognition: confusion = hypoxia?","Rest periods; calcium; stay fit"]},{"note":"22-the-airway-lungs-and-aging-in-detail","title":"Why#3","lines":["Less reserve, weaker alarms","First sign is often confusion","Prevention beats rescue"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"resp-prevention:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential; 5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages; \"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"resp-prevention:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about ¶ respiratory assessment always answers the same?","answer":"Same order every time: airway open? → air moving? → reaching alveoli that exchange?; Applies to any breathing problem, before the diagnosis is known","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Pack-years: calculate the exposure, then assess the person","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"Packs per day × years smoked = pack-years. It measures the cumulative dose of carcinogens and oxidants."},{"title":"Observe","text":"1 ppd × 20 yr = 20 · 1.5 ppd × 20 yr = 30 · 2 ppd × 15 yr = 30 · 10 cigarettes/day × 40 yr = ½ × 40 = 20"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Convert cigarettes to packs (20 = 1 pack) · Multiply by years smoked · Document in history"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Answer the task using the patient’s actual findings","task":"A patient smoked 1.5 packs per day for 20 years. What is the smoking exposure?","cue":"1 ppd × 20 yr = 20 · 1.5 ppd × 20 yr = 30 · 2 ppd × 15 yr = 30 · 10 cigarettes/day × 40 yr = ½ × 40 = 20","rule":"Identify the mechanism, match the cues and choose the response that addresses this situation.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"pack-years","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Pack-years: calculate the exposure, then assess the person","notes":["22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Packs per day × years smoked = pack-years. It measures the cumulative dose of carcinogens and oxidants.","Findings: 1 ppd × 20 yr = 20; 1.5 ppd × 20 yr = 30; 2 ppd × 15 yr = 30; 10 cigarettes/day × 40 yr = ½ × 40 = 20","Nursing actions: Convert cigarettes to packs (20 = 1 pack); Multiply by years smoked; Document in history","Higher pack-years = higher COPD and lung cancer risk; screen eligible patients."],"terms":["pack-years"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Inhalation injury and smoking cessation","blocks":[{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"The single biggest modifiable factor","lines":["Inhaled irritants, above all cigarette smoke = #1 cause of chronic respiratory problems","Damaging parts: tar, nicotine, carbon monoxide"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Quantify it","lines":["Pack-years = packs/day × years","Ask separately: secondhand (same space) + thirdhand (smoke on hair, clothes, surfaces)"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Catch the social smoker","lines":["Ask: \"Do you ever smoke in social situations?\"","They don't call themselves smokers — still damage + addiction"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Gauge dependence","lines":["Smoke on waking? Wake at night to smoke? Hard not to where prohibited? Smoke when ill?","More yes = more dependent"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"The 5 As","lines":["Ask · Advise · Assess willingness · Assist · Arrange follow-up","Under 3 minutes, a nursing duty at every encounter"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Nicotine replacement","lines":["OTC: patch, gum, lozenge · Rx: nasal spray, inhaler","Never smoke on NRT → combined nicotine → stroke/MI risk"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Drugs","lines":["Bupropion → less craving, withdrawal, depression · black box: suicidal thoughts → patient + family report any change in thinking/behavior now","Varenicline → blocks nicotine receptors → smoking less rewarding"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Vaping and hookah","lines":["Hookah ≥ cigarettes in exposure (not safer)","EVALI (vaping lung injury) even with < 2 years of vaping; higher risk with THC products"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Occupational and environmental screening — I PREPARE","lines":["I PREPARE: Investigate exposures · Present work · Residence · Environment · Past work · Activities/hobbies · Resources/referrals · Educate","High-risk: coal miners, stone masons, welders, woodworkers, potters, bakers, printers, grain + foundry workers"]},{"note":"22-inhalation-injury-and-smoking-cessation","title":"Why","lines":["Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential","5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages","\"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it"]}]},{"id":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Questions to ask this patient","blocks":[{"note":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Why","lines":["Dyspnea has no lab value → measure what they can no longer do (blocks, stairs)","\"Show me how you use it\" = the inhaler assessment; most \"adherent\" patients use it wrong"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"pack-years:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Exposure history is a physical assessment finding → changes the differential; 5 As don't need willingness to quit — brief advice at every contact moves people between stages; \"Not interested\" is not a reason to skip it","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"pack-years:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about quantify it?","answer":"Pack-years = packs/day × years; Ask separately: secondhand (same space) + thirdhand (smoke on hair, clothes, surfaces)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Aging lungs: less reserve, closer observation","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"A stiffer chest, less recoil and a weaker cough mean more trapped air, more aspiration and late warning signs."},{"title":"Observe","text":"↑ AP diameter, kyphosis · Weaker cough · Dyspnea with exertion · Lower SpO₂ baseline"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Incentive spirometer, turn, ambulate · Upright for meals · Watch for silent aspiration"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Answer the task using the patient’s actual findings","task":"An older adult has weaker cough strength and less elastic recoil. What is the nursing implication?","cue":"↑ AP diameter, kyphosis · Weaker cough · Dyspnea with exertion · Lower SpO₂ baseline","rule":"Identify the mechanism, match the cues and choose the response that addresses this situation.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"resp-aging","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Aging lungs: less reserve, closer observation","notes":["22-overview","22-questions-to-ask-this-patient"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["A stiffer chest, less recoil and a weaker cough mean more trapped air, more aspiration and late warning signs.","Findings: ↑ AP diameter, kyphosis; Weaker cough; Dyspnea with exertion; Lower SpO₂ baseline","Nursing actions: Incentive spirometer, turn, ambulate; Upright for meals; Watch for silent aspiration","New confusion in an older adult can be the first sign of pneumonia or hypoxia."],"terms":["alveoli","aspiration"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"22-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"22-overview","title":"Upper airway","lines":["Nose + sinuses warm, humidify, filter → pharynx → larynx (epiglottis, vocal cords)","Obstruction here is mechanical and fast","Epiglottis closes the airway when you swallow → protects the lungs"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Lower airway","lines":["Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli","Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right","Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma","Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Alveolar-capillary membrane","lines":["One cell thick each side","Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less)","Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Ventilation versus perfusion (V/Q)","lines":["Air, no blood = dead space (PE)","Blood, no air = shunt (pneumonia, atelectasis, pulmonary edema)","Both → hypoxemia; only dead space improves much with O₂ alone"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Control of breathing","lines":["Central chemoreceptors → CO₂ + pH (the normal drive)","Peripheral chemoreceptors → low O₂","→ why high-flow O₂ is used with caution in chronic CO₂ retainers"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving","Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there","Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away","Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["One question, four ways (inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate): is air reaching alveoli that can exchange it?","Learn what each finding means mechanically → no memorized sound lists"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"¶ On exam Aging changes — alveoli","lines":["Aging: alveoli lose elasticity, chest wall stiffens, muscles weaken","Cough and cilia weaken, PaO₂ falls with age","Same pneumonia → fewer symptoms, less reserve"]}]},{"id":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Questions to ask this patient","blocks":[{"note":"22-questions-to-ask-this-patient","title":"Why","lines":["Dyspnea has no lab value → measure what they can no longer do (blocks, stairs)","\"Show me how you use it\" = the inhaler assessment; most \"adherent\" patients use it wrong"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"resp-aging:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"resp-aging:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about lower airway?","answer":"Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving · Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there · Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away · Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention"},{"title":"Lower airway","text":"Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli · Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right · Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma · Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing"},{"title":"Alveolar-capillary membrane","text":"One cell thick each side · Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less) · Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Know when a monitor number cannot be trusted; assess the patient.","task":"Which findings indicate the pulse oximetry reading may be unreliable? Select all that apply.","cue":"Oximeters compare red and infrared light absorption by pulsatile hemoglobin.","rule":"Know when a monitor number cannot be trusted; assess the patient.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"resp-assessment","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Oxygenation, ventilation and the clues that change priority","notes":["22-overview","22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","Lower airway: Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","Alveolar-capillary membrane: One cell thick each side; Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less); Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion","Why ETCO₂ warns earlier: CO₂ leaves faster than O₂ enters → ETCO₂ changes before hypoxia shows; ↑: hypoventilation, partial obstruction, rebreathing, fever, acidosis; ↓: PE, apnea, total obstruction, tube malposition, cardiac arrest; In a code: tells if CPR is effective + if circulation returned"],"terms":["hypoxemia","hypercapnia","oximetry","capnography"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"22-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"22-overview","title":"Upper airway","lines":["Nose + sinuses warm, humidify, filter → pharynx → larynx (epiglottis, vocal cords)","Obstruction here is mechanical and fast","Epiglottis closes the airway when you swallow → protects the lungs"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Lower airway","lines":["Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli","Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right","Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma","Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Alveolar-capillary membrane","lines":["One cell thick each side","Gas diffuses down a pressure gradient (more → less)","Thicker membrane (fluid, fibrosis, pus) or less surface (collapse, resection, emphysema) → less diffusion"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Ventilation versus perfusion (V/Q)","lines":["Air, no blood = dead space (PE)","Blood, no air = shunt (pneumonia, atelectasis, pulmonary edema)","Both → hypoxemia; only dead space improves much with O₂ alone"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Control of breathing","lines":["Central chemoreceptors → CO₂ + pH (the normal drive)","Peripheral chemoreceptors → low O₂","→ why high-flow O₂ is used with caution in chronic CO₂ retainers"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving","Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there","Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away","Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["One question, four ways (inspect, palpate, percuss, auscultate): is air reaching alveoli that can exchange it?","Learn what each finding means mechanically → no memorized sound lists"]},{"note":"22-overview","title":"¶ On exam Aging changes — alveoli","lines":["Aging: alveoli lose elasticity, chest wall stiffens, muscles weaken","Cough and cilia weaken, PaO₂ falls with age","Same pneumonia → fewer symptoms, less reserve"]}]},{"id":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Reading the numbers — oximetry, capnography, PFTs","blocks":[{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Oximetry thresholds","lines":["Normal 95–100%","< 90% (no chronic lung disease) → assess + intervene now","< 85% tissues struggle · < 75% life-threatening","Chronic CO₂ retainer: > 90% may be the target"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"When the reading lies","lines":["Poor perfusion, hypothermia, motion, dark nail polish","CO poisoning → falsely normal","Poor peripheral perfusion → use the forehead"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Health equity","lines":["Less accurate in darker skin → hypoxia missed more","Use the trend + ABGs + clinical signs, not one number"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Capnometry versus capnography","lines":["Both = exhaled CO₂; capnometry = color/number, capnography = waveform","Normal ETCO₂ 35–45 mm Hg"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Why ETCO₂ warns earlier","lines":["CO₂ leaves faster than O₂ enters → ETCO₂ changes before hypoxia shows","↑: hypoventilation, partial obstruction, rebreathing, fever, acidosis","↓: PE, apnea, total obstruction, tube malposition, cardiac arrest","In a code: tells if CPR is effective + if circulation returned"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"PFTs — what and why","lines":["Mainly to find the cause of dyspnea","With exercise: lung vs heart vs muscle weakness","Compared with expected for age, sex, race, height, weight, smoking"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"PFT preparation","lines":["No smoking 6–8 h; bronchodilators may be held 4–6 h","Effort-dependent → good coaching = good data"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"The values","lines":["FVC = all air forced out after a max breath · FEV₁ = volume in the first second","FEV₁/FVC ↓ = obstruction; normal/high = restrictive","RV, FRC, TLC ↑ with air trapping (emphysema), ↓ in restrictive","DLCO = surface area → ↓ in emphysema, fibrosis, pulmonary HTN"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"After the test","lines":["Watch for bronchospasm / more dyspnea from repeated maneuvers","Document drugs given during testing"]},{"note":"22-reading-the-numbers-oximetry-capnography","title":"Why","lines":["SpO₂ = saturation only, not ventilation","On high-flow O₂, SpO₂ can be perfect while CO₂ climbs → narcosis → capnography fills that gap","Opioid-sedated patient → monitor RR + CO₂, not SpO₂ alone"]}]},{"id":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Oxygen as a drug — devices, doses, hazards","blocks":[{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"The principle","lines":["O₂ is a drug: dose, therapeutic + toxic effects, assess response","Goal = lowest FiO₂ that gives an acceptable O₂ level","Room air 21%; most need 2–4 L/min cannula or up to 40% Venturi","Fever, sepsis, anemia ↑ the need"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Low-flow versus high-flow","lines":["Low-flow: room air dilutes → FiO₂ varies with rate/pattern","High-flow: supplies the whole breath → precise FiO₂"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Nasal cannula","lines":["1–6 L/min, ~24% at 1 L, +4% per liter → 44%","Not above 6 L/min → dead space already full; dries + cracks mucosa"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Simple mask","lines":["Simple mask 40–60% at 5–8 L/min; min 5 L/min to flush CO₂","Partial rebreather 60–75% at 6–11 L/min; bag stays slightly inflated at end-inspiration, no kinks"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Nonrebreather","lines":["Highest low-flow: 80–95%+ at 10–15 L/min","Check valves/flaps hourly → stuck valve ↓ FiO₂, can suffocate"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Venturi mask","lines":["Most accurate without intubation: 24–50%; never block the entrainment port","High-flow nasal cannula: precise FiO₂ at 30–60 L/min, heated + humidified, can talk + clear secretions","Face tent = facial trauma/burns · aerosol mask = high humidity"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"NPPV","lines":["Positive pressure without intubation: dyspnea, hypercarbia, COPD flare, pulmonary edema, acute asthma","CPAP = one pressure, reopens collapsed alveoli","BiPAP = higher in, lower out → ↑ tidal volume"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Combustion","lines":["O₂ doesn't burn but feeds fire","No flame/smoking, signs posted, grounded 3-prong equipment, no frayed cords, no alcohol/oil products"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Oxygen toxicity","lines":["Depends on concentration, duration, lung disease: > 50% for > 24–48 h may injure lung","Early signs: dyspnea, dry cough, substernal chest pain, GI upset, crackles","Notify when PaO₂ > 90 → dose comes down; prevention is the only treatment"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Absorptive atelectasis","lines":["Nitrogen (79%) splints alveoli open","High O₂ washes N₂ out → O₂ absorbed → alveolus collapses","Watch new crackles, ↓ breath sounds; assess q1–2 h when high O₂ starts"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Drying and infection","lines":["Humidify above 4 L/min; confirm it bubbles","Drain condensation by disconnecting — never back into the humidifier","Ears/neck/face q4–8 h, mouth care q8 h, nonpetroleum lubricant"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Home oxygen","lines":["Medicare: PaO₂ < 55 or SpO₂ < 88% on room air at rest","Green tank (check gauge, secure in stand) · liquid (lasts longer, costs more) · concentrator needs power → backup plan"]},{"note":"22-oxygen-as-a-drug-devices-doses-hazards","title":"Why","lines":["\"More O₂ is safer\" is wrong 3 ways:","Too much too long → lung inflammation · washes out N₂ → atelectasis · retainers → blunted drive","So the order has a dose, and titrating down is a nursing action"]}]},{"id":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Breath sounds — sound to mechanism","blocks":[{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Fine crackles","lines":["Alveoli popping open against fluid/collapse","Late inspiratory, bases","HF, pneumonia, atelectasis, fibrosis","Don't clear with cough"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Coarse crackles","lines":["Air bubbling through secretions in larger airways","Wetter, louder, may partly clear"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Wheeze","lines":["Air forced through narrowed airways","Expiratory in asthma, COPD","Silent chest in a wheezer = worse → no air moving"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Rhonchi","lines":["Low-pitched snoring, secretions in large airways","Often clears with cough"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Stridor","lines":["High-pitched inspiratory crowing = upper airway narrowing","Emergency → airway, call for help, prepare to intubate"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Pleural friction rub","lines":["Creaking leather, in + out, painful","Unchanged by cough · inflamed pleura"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Diminished or absent","lines":["Air not reaching the area","Effusion, pneumothorax, consolidation, very poor effort"]},{"note":"22-breath-sounds-sound-to-mechanism","title":"Why","lines":["The sound names the obstacle: fluid pops, narrowing whistles, secretions rattle, inflamed surfaces grate, silence = nothing moving","Exam: \"act first\" = airway closing → stridor or a wheeze gone silent"]}]},{"id":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Diagnostic tests, and your job around each","blocks":[{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Pulse oximetry","lines":["Noninvasive saturation","Unreliable: poor perfusion, hypothermia, motion, dark polish · falsely normal in CO poisoning"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"ABG","lines":["The truth on ventilation + acid-base","Pressure 5 min after (20 min on anticoagulants), sample on ice, note the O₂ the patient was on"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Chest x-ray","lines":["Structure, infiltrates, effusion, pneumothorax, tube/line placement"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Pulmonary function tests","lines":["Volumes + flow rates","No bronchodilator or smoking 4–6 h before; effort-dependent → coach"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Bronchoscopy","lines":["Consent, NPO 4–8 h, sedation","After: NPO until gag returns, watch bleeding; blood-streaked sputum expected","Report frank bleeding, stridor, dyspnea"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Thoracocentesis","lines":["Consent; sit up leaning over a table; hold still, no coughing during insertion","After: CXR, watch for pneumothorax (sudden dyspnea, tracheal deviation, absent sounds)","Remove ≤ ~1000 mL at a time"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Sputum culture","lines":["Early morning, deep cough (not saliva), rinse mouth with water first","Before antibiotics"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Why","lines":["Every airway procedure: gag reflex + pleural space","Sedation/numbing removes the gag → NPO until it returns","Needle/scope near pleura → new sudden dyspnea after thoracentesis = pneumothorax until proven otherwise"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"resp-assessment:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client receiving IV morphine after surgery has SpO₂ 95% on 2 L/min, RR 8, and is difficult to arouse. Which action first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Sedation level and respiratory rate come before saturation for opioid safety.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Document that oxygenation is adequate","correct":false,"why":"SpO₂ is misleading on oxygen; RR 8 with sedation is respiratory depression."},{"id":"o1","text":"Stimulate the client, stop the opioid, and prepare naloxone per protocol","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Opioid-induced respiratory depression: stimulate, hold opioid, support ventilation, give naloxone per order/protocol."},{"id":"o2","text":"Increase oxygen to 6 L/min","correct":false,"why":"More oxygen does not fix hypoventilation or CO₂ retention."},{"id":"o3","text":"Recheck in 30 minutes","correct":false,"why":"Delay is unsafe."}]},{"id":"resp-assessment:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Every finding = one of 4 problems: airway narrowed · alveoli collapsed · membrane thickened · blood not arriving; Wheeze = narrowed · crackles = fluid/reopening alveoli · diminished = air not getting there; Absent + tracheal deviation = air or blood pushing the lung away; Name the mechanism → you get the priority + the intervention","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"resp-assessment:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about lower airway?","answer":"Trachea → R + L mainstem bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli; Right bronchus is more vertical → aspiration and a too-deep tube go right; Bronchiole smooth muscle constricts in asthma; Alveoli lined with surfactant → keeps them from collapsing","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis: before, after, and complications","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Two points cover every procedure: · Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs) · Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)"},{"title":"Teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours","text":"24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger · One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis · → ED immediately"},{"title":"Thoracentesis limits and after care","text":"≤ ~1000 mL at once → avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema · CXR after · Document response, fluid volume + character, specimens, site, resp assessment before/during/after"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Protect the airway: no oral intake until protective reflexes return.","task":"A client returns from bronchoscopy. Which action is the priority before offering food or fluid?","cue":"Topical anesthetic blocks the sensory limb of the gag reflex.","rule":"Protect the airway: no oral intake until protective reflexes return.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"resp-procedures","ch":"22","chapters":["22"],"title":"Bronchoscopy and thoracentesis: before, after, and complications","notes":["22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 22; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Two points cover every procedure:; Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs); Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)","Teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours: 24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger; One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis; → ED immediately","Thoracentesis limits and after care: ≤ ~1000 mL at once → avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema; CXR after; Document response, fluid volume + character, specimens, site, resp assessment before/during/after","Why: Every airway procedure: gag reflex + pleural space; Sedation/numbing removes the gag → NPO until it returns; Needle/scope near pleura → new sudden dyspnea after thoracentesis = pneumothorax until proven otherwise"],"terms":["bronchoscopy","thoracentesis","pneumothorax"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Endoscopy, thoracentesis, and biopsy — the nursing care","blocks":[{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"The three endoscopic studies","lines":["Bronchoscopy → airways to secondary bronchi + samples","Laryngoscopy → vocal cords, remove foreign bodies, biopsy","Mediastinoscopy → through chest wall above sternum, between lungs"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Before","lines":["Consent, 2 identifiers, NPO 4–8 h","Often CBC, platelets, PT, electrolytes, CXR"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"After bronchoscopy","lines":["NPO until gag reflex returns","VS, breath sounds, SpO₂ q15 min × 2 h","Blood-streaked sputum = expected · frank bleeding, stridor, new dyspnea = report"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Thoracentesis positioning","lines":["Sitting up, leaning over a table, arms supported","Hold still, no coughing or deep breaths during insertion","Nurse steadies patient; watch for shock, pain, nausea, pallor/ash-gray, diaphoresis, cyanosis, tachypnea, dyspnea"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Thoracentesis limits and after care","lines":["≤ ~1000 mL at once → avoid re-expansion pulmonary edema","CXR after","Document response, fluid volume + character, specimens, site, resp assessment before/during/after"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours","lines":["24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger","One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis","→ ED immediately"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Lung biopsy","lines":["Transbronchial/needle (bronchoscopy), percutaneous needle, or open (thoracotomy + chest tube)","CXR or CT after → rule out pneumothorax","VS + breath sounds ≥ q4 h × 24 h; ↓/absent breath sounds = report now; watch hemoptysis"]},{"note":"22-endoscopy-thoracentesis-and-biopsy-the-n","title":"Why","lines":["Two points cover every procedure:","Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs)","Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)"]}]},{"id":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Diagnostic tests, and your job around each","blocks":[{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Pulse oximetry","lines":["Noninvasive saturation","Unreliable: poor perfusion, hypothermia, motion, dark polish · falsely normal in CO poisoning"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"ABG","lines":["The truth on ventilation + acid-base","Pressure 5 min after (20 min on anticoagulants), sample on ice, note the O₂ the patient was on"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Chest x-ray","lines":["Structure, infiltrates, effusion, pneumothorax, tube/line placement"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Pulmonary function tests","lines":["Volumes + flow rates","No bronchodilator or smoking 4–6 h before; effort-dependent → coach"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Bronchoscopy","lines":["Consent, NPO 4–8 h, sedation","After: NPO until gag returns, watch bleeding; blood-streaked sputum expected","Report frank bleeding, stridor, dyspnea"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Thoracocentesis","lines":["Consent; sit up leaning over a table; hold still, no coughing during insertion","After: CXR, watch for pneumothorax (sudden dyspnea, tracheal deviation, absent sounds)","Remove ≤ ~1000 mL at a time"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Sputum culture","lines":["Early morning, deep cough (not saliva), rinse mouth with water first","Before antibiotics"]},{"note":"22-diagnostic-tests-and-your-job-around-eac","title":"Why","lines":["Every airway procedure: gag reflex + pleural space","Sedation/numbing removes the gag → NPO until it returns","Needle/scope near pleura → new sudden dyspnea after thoracentesis = pneumothorax until proven otherwise"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"resp-procedures:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client returns from bronchoscopy. Which action is the priority before offering food or fluid?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Protect the airway: no oral intake until protective reflexes return.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Check the gag and swallow reflex","correct":true,"why":"Correct. The throat was anesthetized; eating before the gag returns risks aspiration."},{"id":"o1","text":"Encourage deep coughing to clear sputum","correct":false,"why":"Vigorous coughing can increase bleeding; not the priority."},{"id":"o2","text":"Obtain a chest x-ray","correct":false,"why":"Ordered only if pneumothorax is suspected or biopsy performed."},{"id":"o3","text":"Give throat lozenges for soreness","correct":false,"why":"Lozenges before gag returns is itself an aspiration risk."}]},{"id":"resp-procedures:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Two points cover every procedure:; Protect the airway until the gag reflex returns (numb throat → food into lungs); Watch for pneumothorax (anything in the chest → air where it shouldn't be)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"resp-procedures:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about teach the pneumothorax signs for the next 24 hours?","answer":"24 h: side pain worst at end of breath in/out, tachycardia, rapid shallow breathing, air hunger; One side bulges + doesn't move, trachea shifts to the unaffected side, new cough, cyanosis; → ED immediately","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Upper airway: sleep apnea, bleeding, surgery and tracheostomy","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"In sleep the throat muscles relax and the tongue and soft palate fall back. The chest keeps pulling against a closed airway until CO₂ rises and the patient partly wakes — over and over, so deep sleep never comes."},{"title":"Observe","text":"Loud snoring with witnessed pauses, then gasping · Morning headache, dry mouth, daytime sleepiness · Hypertension that doesn't respond to treatment · Retracted jaw, small chin, short thick neck"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"CPAP nightly — bring their own machine to the hospital · Side-lying; no sedatives or alcohol at bedtime · After airway surgery: check for narrowing q2 h × 24 h"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Positioning prevents aspiration.","task":"Which statement by a client with a nosebleed shows correct understanding?","cue":"Direct pressure lets platelets form a plug at the Kiesselbach plexus.","rule":"Positioning prevents aspiration.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"upper-airway","ch":"23","chapters":["23"],"title":"Upper airway: sleep apnea, bleeding, surgery and tracheostomy","notes":["23-overview","23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","23-adpie"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 23; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["In sleep the throat muscles relax and the tongue and soft palate fall back. The chest keeps pulling against a closed airway until CO₂ rises and the patient partly wakes — over and over, so deep sleep never comes.","Findings: Loud snoring with witnessed pauses, then gasping; Morning headache, dry mouth, daytime sleepiness; Hypertension that doesn't respond to treatment; Retracted jaw, small chin, short thick neck","Nursing actions: CPAP nightly — bring their own machine to the hospital; Side-lying; no sedatives or alcohol at bedtime; After airway surgery: check for narrowing q2 h × 24 h","After airway surgery: stridor, drooling, rising effort or falling SpO₂ means the airway is narrowing — call the Rapid Response Team."],"terms":["epistaxis","tracheostomy","apnea"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"23-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"23-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Upper airway = one lumen, no backup → losing it is immediate","Lower airway = millions of paths → losses survivable","Every priority answer follows from this"]},{"note":"23-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Everything is above the trachea → every problem is plumbing with a short fuse","Swelling, blood or a foreign body can close the airway in minutes","Ask: is it open, and how fast is it changing?"]},{"note":"23-overview","title":"¶ The pharynx collapses during sleep when","lines":["Sleep → muscle tone falls → pharynx collapses","Narrowing makes it worse: obesity, big tonsils, small jaw, alcohol, sedatives","→ repeated pauses = apnea (a- without, -pnea breathing)"]},{"note":"23-overview","title":"¶ The larynx holds the vocal cords","lines":["Larynx = vocal cords + epiglottis","Removed → permanent neck stoma, no nose-to-lung connection","No humidifying, filtering, smell, or voice through the mouth"]},{"note":"23-overview","title":"¶ Trauma and swelling anywhere in this","lines":["Flow depends on the radius squared → halve the diameter, quarter the airflow","That’s why upper-airway problems get worse suddenly, not gradually"]},{"note":"23-overview","title":"The nose","lines":["Nose is vascular on purpose (warms + humidifies)","Kiesselbach plexus (anterior septum) → most bleeds anterior + reachable","Posterior bleeds: bigger vessels, into the throat, far more dangerous"]}]},{"id":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Obstructive sleep apnea — the full picture","blocks":[{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"The mechanism","lines":["Sleep → neck muscles relax → tongue + soft palate fall back","Chest effort continues against a closed airway","CO₂ ↑, pH ↓ → chemoreceptors → partial waking (as often as every 5 min) → no deep sleep"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Definitions","lines":["OSA = airway collapse in sleep · OSAS = symptomatic, apneas ≥ 10 s + intermittent hypoxia"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Underdiagnosis","lines":["~90% undiagnosed (snoring dismissed)","Risk ↑ age, male, obesity, big tonsils/uvula/adenoids; sharp rise > 60"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Health equity","lines":["Black adults (esp. men): more + more severe","Less access/screening, sleepiness blamed on other things, mistrust → screen deliberately"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"What to ask","lines":["Daytime sleepiness (reading, computer, driving)","Woken by own snoring; nightmares; witnessed pauses → gasping/snorting","OTC sleep aids + evening alcohol (worsen it)"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"GERD connection","lines":["Strong effort during apneas → nighttime reflux","Ask: waking with heartburn, stomach contents in mouth, choking cough"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"What to examine","lines":["Height/weight; retracted jaw, small chin, short neck","Pharynx shape, uvula, thick tongue, big tonsils/soft palate","HTN that doesn't respond to therapy = classic clue"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Psychosocial","lines":["Irritability, depression, social withdrawal, poor memory/concentration","Long-term untreated → mild cognitive impairment, dementia"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["Screen: STOP-Bang, Epworth, Pittsburgh, MSLT","Then home study → definitive polysomnography (ECG, EEG, oximetry, EOM, EMG, effort, CO₂)"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Nonsurgical management","lines":["Mild: weight loss + position change","Oral appliance (jaw forward) — bulky, mucosa risk","CPAP = mainstay (continuous pressure, tight seal)","Drugs don't work: sedatives worsen, stimulants don't fix cause"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"CPAP adherence and care","lines":["Quiet + humidified; Medicare covers if used ≥ 6 h/day","Clean mask + tubing daily, distilled water, never share (fungal risk)"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Surgical options","lines":["For anatomy/severe resistant disease","Hypoglossal nerve stimulator (contracts airway muscles in sleep)","Tonsil/adenoid/uvula/septum surgery; UPPP (old: taste/voice changes; modUPPP: 2+ days, 3–6 wk recovery)","Tracheostomy = last resort"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"After surgery","lines":["q2 h × 24 h for narrowing: ↑ effort, stridor, drooling, can't swallow, ↓ SpO₂, ↑ ETCO₂ → RRT","Scheduled analgesia, not PRN; no aspirin/NSAIDs × 24 h","Dark blood in secretions OK; bright red/increasing = report","Watch excessive swallowing or belching (bleeding)"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea-the-full-picture","title":"Why","lines":["OSA = muscle tone fails → opioids + sedatives remove that tone → reproduce the disease","→ continuous monitoring, cautious dosing, never sedatives for their insomnia"]}]},{"id":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Obstruction, epistaxis, and trauma","blocks":[{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Causes of upper airway obstruction","lines":["Tongue edema or occlusion (lost gag/tone, unconscious)","Laryngeal edema (smoke, toxins, anaphylaxis), abscess, cancer","Thick secretions, stroke, trauma, burns, foreign body"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"The preventable one","lines":["Crusted oral/nasal secretions can block the airway → death","From poor oral hygiene → prevented by nursing","High risk: altered mental status, dehydration, can't communicate, weak cough, aspiration risk → assess oral care daily, teach UAP why"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Recognizing it","lines":["Partial: vague diaphoresis, tachycardia, anxiety, ↑ BP","Then hypoxia/hypercarbia, restlessness, retractions, seesaw chest, impending doom","Oximetry + ETCO₂; watch stridor, cyanosis, ↓ LOC"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Acting on it","lines":["Slight head/neck extension, nasal/oral airway, suction","Foreign body: abdominal thrusts if conscious","Unconscious → CPR compressions (not thrusts), even with a foreign body","Then laryngoscopy, intubate before swelling, cricothyroidotomy, tracheotomy (< 2 min in emergency)"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Epistaxis — why it bleeds","lines":["Dense capillaries; trauma, HTN, blood disorders (leukemia), inflammation, tumor, dry air, picking/blowing, cocaine, NG suction","Older adults → posterior bleeds"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Emergency care","lines":["Sit up, lean forward (blood out of the larynx)","Calm them (anxiety ↑ BP)","Direct lateral pressure 15–20 min + ice/cool compress; loose packing","No nose blowing × 24 h after it stops"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"When it does not stop","lines":["Cautery (silver nitrate/electro) + anterior packing","Posterior = more severe, airway risk: posterior packing/catheter/gel, out in 1–3 days","Watch breathing; on opioids → gag + cough hourly; oximetry","Refractory → ligation/embolization (facial pain, necrosis, facial paralysis, blindness)"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Nasal fracture","lines":["Deviation, crooked bridge, breathing change, crepitus, bruising, pain","Closed reduction within 24 h (then edema delays it days)"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"The CSF test","lines":["Clear drainage → CSF (skull fracture)","Positive for glucose; dried on filter paper → yellow halo","Rare, serious injury"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Rhinoplasty and nasoseptoplasty","lines":["Packing both nostrils, drip pad, splint","Frequent swallowing = posterior bleeding → penlight the throat","Semi-Fowler, move slowly, cool compresses, ≥ 2500 mL/day once gag returns","No forceful cough, straining, sniffing, blowing, closed-mouth sneezing; no aspirin/NSAIDs","Final look 6–12 months"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Facial trauma and the Le Fort classification","lines":["Mandible fractures most common","Le Fort I nasoethmoid · II + maxilla · III + orbit/zygoma = midface detached from skull","Rich blood supply → heavy bleeding/bruising"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Assessment priorities in facial trauma","lines":["Airway first: stridor, dyspnea, restlessness, hypoxia, cyanosis, ↓ LOC","Then bleeding, fractures, edema, asymmetry, CSF from ears/nose, vision + eye movement","Battle sign (bruising behind ears) → skull fracture","Get C-spine imaging"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Fixed occlusion and IMF","lines":["Jaws wired 6–10 weeks; irrigating oral care, liquid diet","Wire cutters always at hand → vomiting → cut wires (prevent aspiration) → surgeon to rewire"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Laryngeal trauma","lines":["Crush/blow, fracture, prolonged intubation","Dyspnea, aphonia, hoarseness, subcutaneous emphysema, hemoptysis","VS + gas exchange q15–30 min; distress → stay, prepare intubation/tracheotomy"]},{"note":"23-obstruction-epistaxis-and-trauma","title":"Why","lines":["Unconscious → stop thrusts, start compressions (even with a known object)","Nosebleed → lean forward, not back (back hides loss + sends blood to airway)"]}]},{"id":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Head and neck cancer — treatment and surgical care","blocks":[{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"The disease","lines":["~4% of US cancers; squamous cell, slow, curable early","Irritation → leukoplakia (white) or erythroplakia (red velvety)","Spreads to nodes, muscle, bone → later lung, liver"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Tobacco + alcohol (the big two)","Voice abuse, laryngitis, chemicals/dust, poor oral hygiene, GERD, oral HPV","Men, > 50; calculate pack-years + drinks/day × years"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Warning signs to teach","lines":["Pain, lump (mouth, throat, neck), dysphagia, color change in mouth","Sore not healed in 2 weeks, unexplained bleeding, numbness, denture fit change","Burning with citrus/hot liquids, one-sided ear pain, hoarseness","Recurrent sore throat, dyspnea, weight loss"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Nose and sinus cancer","lines":["Rare; wood/textile/leather/flour dust, nickel, chromium, mustard gas, radium (+ smoking)","Mimics sinusitis → symptoms persist after sinusitis treatment"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Radiation","lines":["≥ 40% cure for small local tumors","Hoarseness, sore throat, dysphagia, skin reaction, taste change, dry mouth","Protect skin (sun, heat, cold, shaving), soft cotton, mild soap","Xerostomia: sip cool fluids, sugar-free candy/gum, sprays, humidify, dental consult (caries)"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Chemotherapy and targeted therapy","lines":["Cisplatin + 5-FU, often with radiation","Cetuximab (EGFR) → severe skin reactions","Nivolumab, pembrolizumab for some HPV cancers"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Surgery and voice","lines":["Cord stripping/cordectomy (voice OK) → hemilaryngectomy (hoarse)","Total laryngectomy = no natural voice + permanent stoma","Neck dissection (nodes, SCM, jugular, CN XI) → shoulder drop expected"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Before surgery","lines":["Teach airway care, suctioning, pain, ICU, feeding, discharge","With SLP: set up + practice communication before surgery"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"After surgery","lines":["8+ h surgery → ICU; VS + bleeding hourly × 24 h","Laryngectomy tube (shorter, wider) keeps stoma from shrinking","Clean suture line q1–2 h first days, then q4 h","Healthy stoma = bright pink + shiny"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Flaps and wound breakdown","lines":["Flaps hourly × 72 h: refill, color, drainage, Doppler; not dependent","Breakdown common (esp. after radiation) → carotid can be exposed"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Carotid artery rupture","lines":["Check hourly for leak","Suspected leak → RRT, don't touch it","Rupture → constant direct pressure, secure airway, stay with patient, straight to surgery","High stroke + death risk"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"The team","lines":["RT (airway/trach teaching), dietitian (avg ≥ 20 lb loss), SLP (communication + swallowing)"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-treatment-and-surgi","title":"Why","lines":["Don't touch a carotid leak — palpation can cause rupture","Communication plan before surgery: the writing board is airway safety equipment"]}]},{"id":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Obstructive sleep apnea","blocks":[{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"What it is","lines":["Apneas ≥ 10 s in sleep from upper airway collapse","Each → O₂ drop + sympathetic surge"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Obesity, thick neck, big tonsils/uvula, small jaw, nasal obstruction","Smoking, bedtime alcohol/sedatives, male, older"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Cues","lines":["Loud snoring + witnessed pauses, gasping, morning headache, dry mouth","Unrefreshing sleep, daytime sleepiness, irritability","Over time: HTN, AF, right HF"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["Overnight polysomnography → apneas/hypopneas per hour (AHI)"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Management","lines":["Weight loss, side-lying, no alcohol/sedatives at bedtime, treat nasal obstruction","CPAP = mainstay (pressure splint)","Oral appliances; UPPP for some"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Nursing priority","lines":["CPAP adherence: mask fit, skin, dry nose, claustrophobia; bring own machine to hospital","Extreme caution with opioids + sedatives"]},{"note":"23-obstructive-sleep-apnea","title":"Why","lines":["OSA = cardiovascular disease in a sleep costume (hypoxemia + surges → HTN, dysrhythmias, right HF)","Post-op: pain drugs remove the tone holding the airway open"]}]},{"id":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Epistaxis, trauma, and obstruction","blocks":[{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"If it will not stop","lines":["Packing/balloon, cautery, sometimes ligation","Posterior packing → watch airway, hypoxia, infection, pain; humidify + oral care"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Teach after a bleed","lines":["No nose blowing, straining, heavy lifting, aspirin/NSAIDs","Humidify; saline spray or water-soluble lubricant"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Facial and nasal trauma","lines":["Airway → bleeding → fracture","Clear drainage + glucose / halo = CSF (skull base fracture) → don't pack, don't suction nose, notify"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Rhinoplasty after care","lines":["HOB up, ice; edema + bruising expected","Frequent swallowing = bleeding down the throat"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Upper airway obstruction","lines":["Tongue (unresponsive), aspiration, edema (anaphylaxis, burns, infection), tumor, trauma","Cues: stridor, choking, gasping, wheeze, restlessness, cyanosis","Call for help, open airway, thrusts if responsive, suction, prepare intubation/cricothyroidotomy"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Why","lines":["Frequent swallowing = earliest sign of nose/throat bleeding (blood runs back)","Lean forward keeps blood visible (not in stomach → vomiting, aspiration)"]},{"note":"23-epistaxis-trauma-and-obstruction","title":"Epistaxis first aid:","lines":["Sit up, lean forward","Pinch the soft part 10–15 min continuously; ice to nose/face; keep calm","Leaning back → blood down the throat, hides the loss"]}]},{"id":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Head and neck cancer, and the laryngectomy patient","blocks":[{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Risk factors","lines":["Tobacco + alcohol (esp. together), HPV, sun (lip), occupational, poor oral hygiene, irritation"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Warning signs to teach","lines":["Hoarseness > 2 weeks, neck/mouth lump, non-healing sore","Dysphagia/painful swallowing, unexplained ear pain, one-sided nasal obstruction/bleeding"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Airway is the postoperative priority","lines":["Airway + bleeding → pain, nutrition, communication","HOB up, suction ready, spare trach tube + obturator at bedside","Never remove a fresh airway"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Communication plan","lines":["Before surgery: writing board, pictures, tablet, distress signal","Later: esophageal speech, electrolarynx, TE puncture prosthesis"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Nutrition","lines":["Swallow evaluation before eating; upright; thickened liquids or tube feeds","Weight + albumin"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Stoma care teaching","lines":["Cover stoma loosely, humidify, no swimming, careful showers, avoid aerosols/dust","ID: \"neck breather\" → rescue breaths into the stoma"]},{"note":"23-head-and-neck-cancer-and-the-laryngectom","title":"Why","lines":["Laryngectomy separates airway from mouth/nose → no humidifying (humidify), no filter (cover), never swim, rescue breathing to the stoma"]}]},{"id":"23-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Restlessness + anxiety = airway findings (earliest cue)","Reassurance or sedation removes tone + the report keeping them alive"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Why#2","lines":["Communication ranks above nutrition/body image: can't speak → can't report the airway problem"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Why#3","lines":["Communication outcome deadline = before surgery"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Why#4","lines":["Unconscious → stop thrusts, start compressions","Don't palpate a carotid leak"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Why#5","lines":["Evaluate on a clock: bleeding hourly, airway q2 h after surgery, flaps hourly × 72 h"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Airway, always first","lines":["Voice + can they speak?","Stridor, drooling, can’t swallow secretions; rising effort; retractions; seesaw","Restlessness, sense of doom; SpO₂ + ETCO₂","Early partial obstruction is vague: sweating, tachycardia, anxiety, ↑ BP → treat the cluster as airway"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Sleep and breathing history","lines":["Snoring + witnessed pauses + gasping","Daytime sleepiness, morning headache, dry mouth, poor concentration","Screen: STOP-Bang, Epworth","Look at jaw, neck, uvula, tonsils, tongue; resistant hypertension"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Bleeding and trauma","lines":["Epistaxis: site, volume, anterior vs posterior","Frequent swallowing = earliest sign of posterior/post-op bleeding","Facial trauma: deviation, crepitus, vision + eye movement, Battle sign","Clear drainage: glucose + / halo ring = CSF","Assume C-spine injury"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Cancer and stoma","lines":["Warning signs > 2 weeks: hoarseness, neck/oral lump, nonhealing sore, dysphagia, one-sided ear pain","Pack-years + drinks","Stoma/flap: mucosa color, secretions, flap capillary refill + Doppler, suture line"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"¶ 1. Potential for airway obstruction related","lines":["#1 diagnosis: potential airway obstruction (edema, secretions, blood, foreign body, lost tone)","Cues: stridor, retractions, restlessness, falling SpO₂","Outranks everything in this chapter"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Independent","lines":["New airway bedside kit: suction, spare tube (same + one smaller), obturator, bag-valve mask","Never remove a fresh trach tube","Oral care — crusted secretions can cause fatal obstruction","Stoma: continuous humidification","Suction: sterile, preoxygenate, 10–15 s, suction on withdrawal only","Epistaxis: ice + pressure, leaning forward; wire cutters at bedside for jaw wiring"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Dependent","lines":["O₂ + humidification","Scheduled (not PRN) analgesia; no aspirin/NSAIDs first 24 h","Antiemetics; packing, cautery or embolization for refractory bleeds","Tube feeds or thickened liquids after a swallow study; antibiotics, wound care"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Emergency actions","lines":["Obstruction: call for help, open/position the airway, oral/nasal airway, suction","Responsive adult: abdominal thrusts; unresponsive: chest compressions (even with a known foreign body)","Prepare for intubation before swelling closes the option; cricothyroidotomy","Carotid leak: RRT, don’t touch; rupture → constant direct pressure, secure airway, don’t leave"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Collaborative","lines":["SLP: communication plan + swallow studies; RT","Dietitian — head/neck surgery clients lose ≥ 20 lb","Sleep medicine + CPAP supply; smoking/alcohol cessation; social work"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"¶ Airway patency maintained throughout, with no","lines":["Airway open throughout, no emergency airway event","Effort, SpO₂, ETCO₂ stable; bleeding stopped","Communicates by the agreed method; uses the call light","Safe swallow, weight stable","Stoma bright pink + shiny, thin secretions; family demonstrates care + suctioning"]},{"note":"23-adpie","title":"Unmet looks like:","lines":["New/worse stridor, drooling, ↑ effort, ↓ SpO₂ or ↑ ETCO₂","Shrinking oropharynx after surgery; frequent swallowing or bright red bleeding","Dusky flap; any suspected carotid leak"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"upper-airway:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which statement by a client with a nosebleed shows correct understanding?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Positioning prevents aspiration.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"“I will tilt my head back.”","correct":false,"why":"Causes swallowing and aspiration of blood."},{"id":"o1","text":"“I will sit and lean forward and pinch the soft part of my nose for 10 to 15 minutes.”","correct":true,"why":"Correct."},{"id":"o2","text":"“I will take aspirin for the pain.”","correct":false,"why":"Aspirin impairs platelet function."},{"id":"o3","text":"“I will blow my nose to clear the clots.”","correct":false,"why":"Dislodges clots."}]},{"id":"upper-airway:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Upper airway = one lumen, no backup → losing it is immediate; Lower airway = millions of paths → losses survivable; Every priority answer follows from this","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"upper-airway:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about warning signs to teach?","answer":"Pain, lump (mouth, throat, neck), dysphagia, color change in mouth; Sore not healed in 2 weeks, unexplained bleeding, numbness, denture fit change; Burning with citrus/hot liquids, one-sided ear pain, hoarseness; Recurrent sore throat, dyspnea, weight loss","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Asthma: opening the airway and controlling inflammation","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"Inflammation narrows the airway from inside; hyperresponsive smooth muscle squeezes from outside. Reversible. Air gets trapped on the way out."},{"title":"Observe","text":"Expiratory wheeze, cough · Chest tightness, dyspnea · Prolonged exhalation · Worse at night / with triggers"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Sit upright · O₂ · SABA now · Reassess breath sounds + work of breathing"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes. See the highlighted clinical update below."},"strategy":{"title":"Silent chest is an emergency.","task":"A client in an asthma attack had loud wheezing. Now wheezing is absent, SpO₂ is 86%, and the client is drowsy. Which action first?","cue":"No airflow = no wheeze; retained CO₂ causes drowsiness.","rule":"Silent chest is an emergency.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"asthma","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Asthma: opening the airway and controlling inflammation","notes":["24-overview","24-asthma-the-full-picture","24-adpie","24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Inflammation narrows the airway from inside; hyperresponsive smooth muscle squeezes from outside. Reversible. Air gets trapped on the way out.","Findings: Expiratory wheeze, cough; Chest tightness, dyspnea; Prolonged exhalation; Worse at night / with triggers","Nursing actions: Sit upright; O₂; SABA now; Reassess breath sounds + work of breathing","Silent chest, drowsiness or a normal-to-rising PaCO₂ mid-attack = airway closing, patient tiring. Emergency.","Use the prescribed asthma action plan. Current GINA care includes inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment; do not interpret a SABA rescue example as a recommendation for SABA-only long-term treatment. ICS-formoterol can be an anti-inflammatory reliever when that regimen is prescribed."],"terms":["bronchospasm","albuterol","fluticasone"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"24-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"24-overview","title":"The tree","lines":["Trachea → 2 mainstem → 5 secondary bronchi → bronchioles → alveolar ducts → alveoli (exchange)","Problem anywhere along the tube → ↓ gas exchange + ↓ systemic perfusion"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"The flattened diaphragm","lines":["Hyperinflated lung → diaphragm pushed flat → weak pull","Recruits neck, chest wall, abdominal accessory muscles → more O₂ burned for less air","Inhales before exhale finishes → uncoordinated pattern + air hunger"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"The pressure chain to the heart","lines":["Trapped air + collapsed airways + stiff walls → pulmonary vessels narrowed","Thin RV must push high pressure → hypertrophies → fails = cor pulmonale (right HF from lung disease)","Normal pulmonary pressure 15–18 mm Hg; hypertension > 25"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Why CO₂ retention is the late sign","lines":["CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate","ABGs near-normal until advanced","Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Lower airway = millions of parallel paths → decline over months–years, not minutes","So: trend > single finding, the patient's own baseline is the reference","Work of breathing, not SpO₂, shows if the disease is winning"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ On exam Intro. Below the larynx","lines":["Below the larynx the airway becomes a tree (bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli)","Nothing closes in minutes → this chapter is chronic, progressive loss","Six ways it fails: narrowed tube (asthma, chronic bronchitis) · destroyed sac (emphysema) · thick membrane (fibrosis) · mucus plugs (CF) · squeezed vessel (PAH) · tumor (lung cancer)","Name which one → findings, drug class and priority follow"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ ICS → local immune suppression →","lines":["ICS → local immune suppression → oral thrush (rinse + spit)","LABA alone in asthma → masks symptoms while inflammation worsens → always with ICS","Epoprostenol half-life minutes → interruption = rebound PAH"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ On exam Proteases versus elastin —","lines":["Proteases normally destroy inhaled particles","Smoke drives excess proteases → they digest elastin → emphysema","Alpha₁-antitrypsin is the brake; inherited AAT deficiency removes it","Emphysema in a non-smoker or before 40 → think genetics (AAT)"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Elastic recoil","lines":["Recoil empties the lung (exhalation is passive)","Elastin destroyed → inflates but won't deflate → trapped air, bullae, ↓ surface area"]}]},{"id":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Asthma — the full picture","blocks":[{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Definition","lines":["Chronic, reversible, intermittent airway obstruction","Inflammation narrows from inside + hyperresponsiveness squeezes from outside → most adults have both → 2 drug classes","> 3500 US deaths/yr; > 20 million adults; ~half had it as children"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Two inflammatory types","lines":["Eosinophilic (~50%) → IL-5 · Neutrophilic → IL-17","Same symptoms; different long-term epithelial effect → monoclonals target the mediator"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Triggers and history","lines":["Intermittent dyspnea, chest tightness, cough, wheeze, ↑ mucus","Ask: continuous, seasonal, activity/work-related, worse at night?","Symptoms can last 4–8 weeks after a cold · atopic → other allergies","Wheeze in a non-smoker = diagnostically important"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Control level (GINA)","lines":["4 questions: daytime symptoms > 2×/wk · night waking · reliever > 2×/wk · activity limited","0 = controlled · 1–2 = partly · 3–4 = uncontrolled"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"During an attack","lines":["Wheeze louder on exhalation at first, prolonged exhalation","Retractions (sternum, suprasternal notch, intercostal); only a few words per breath","Long-term severe → barrel chest: AP:lateral 1:1.5 → 1:1, flat diaphragm, wide rib spaces"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Labs","lines":["PaO₂ ↓","PaCO₂ ↓ early (blowing off) → ↑ late with ETCO₂ = retention starting","Allergic: ↑ eosinophils + IgE; sputum eosinophils + Curschmann spirals (mucus plugs)"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"PFTs are the diagnosis","lines":["FVC, FEV₁, peak expiratory flow","Diagnosis = ↑ ≥ 12% after bronchodilator (reversibility)","Methacholine challenge provokes bronchospasm in susceptible adults"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Reliever versus control","lines":["SABA (albuterol, levalbuterol, terbutaline) → stops an attack, premedicate before triggers; always carry, check doses left","LABA (salmeterol, formoterol) → prevents, can't stop an attack, takes days · black box: never alone","LAMA/anticholinergic (ipratropium short, tiotropium daily) → bronchodilate + dry secretions","Theophylline = last resort, narrow therapeutic range → monitor levels"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Antiinflammatories are control drugs only","lines":["ICS = most effective controller (fluticasone, budesonide, mometasone)","Systemic steroids: short-term moderate, daily only severe","Cromolyn stabilizes mast cells · nedocromil blocks mediator release","Leukotriene: montelukast, zafirlukast block receptor · zileuton blocks synthesis","Monoclonals: omalizumab (anti-IgE), dupilumab (IL-4R), benralizumab/mepolizumab/reslizumab (IL-5)","None of these for an acute attack"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Inhaler technique is examinable","lines":["MDI: shake 5 s, inhale slowly + deeply, hold 5–10 s; spacer if timing is hard","DPI: exhale away, inhale fast + deep 2–3 s, hold 4–10 s, never wash with water","SMI: prime 3× first use + after 3 days unused, don't cover side vents, hold 10 s"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Action plan and self-management","lines":["Plan: daily controller, reliever use, control questions, how to step up, when to call, emergency steps","Symptom diary + peak flow during flares, after changes, when worse","Keep exercising → adjust or premedicate with SABA"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Status asthmaticus","lines":["Severe, won't respond to usual therapy: extreme work of breathing, wheeze, accessory muscles, JVD","Now: IV fluids, bronchodilators, steroids, epinephrine, O₂ → prepare to intubate","Wheeze suddenly gone + low SpO₂ = complete obstruction → tracheotomy","Untreated → pneumothorax, cardiac/respiratory arrest"]},{"note":"24-asthma-the-full-picture","title":"Why","lines":["Bronchodilators open the tube; antiinflammatories calm the lining — neither does the other's job","→ two classes · steroid inhaler useless in an attack · LABA alone kills (masks inflammation)","Silent chest = no air moving = worse"]}]},{"id":"24-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Words per breath beats SpO₂ (free, early)","SpO₂ can sit at the usual 89% while work of breathing doubles → effort fails first"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"4. Potential for pneumonia or other respiratory infection","lines":["Excess mucus + poor clearance = breeding ground","Infection triggers most exacerbations"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Why#2","lines":["Weight loss ranks above fatigue","Malnutrition → ↓ muscle, elasticity, alveolar surface → worse gas exchange → feeding = a respiratory intervention"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Why#3","lines":["Outcome = \"at the patient's usual baseline\"","Chronic SpO₂ 89% + PaCO₂ 55 = at goal; pushing to \"normal\" is an error"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Why#4","lines":["Independent actions = mechanical fixes","Pursed lips replace lost elastin · tripod lends arms to accessory muscles · controlled cough keeps airways open · hydration thins mucus"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Why#5","lines":["Evaluate by trends + demonstrations","Still on a clock: sharp CO₂ rise and muscle fatigue + hypoxemia that won't lift = respiratory failure"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"¶ These diseases are chronic and progressive,","lines":["Chronic + progressive → reference value is this client’s own baseline","Assess work of breathing, not one number","Plan = teach the client to co-manage a disease that outlasts the admission"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Work of breathing","lines":["Rate, rhythm, depth, accessory muscles — before any intervention","Words between breaths; retractions; abdomen sucked in; tripod","RR 40–50 in an exacerbation = act now","Hyperresonance, ↓ fremitus, ↓ sounds, wheeze","Silent chest = obstruction or pneumothorax, not improvement","Grade dyspnea (Visual Analog Dyspnea Scale) and reuse it to judge therapy"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"History that changes the plan","lines":["Pack-years, vaping, occupational/environmental exposure","Family history: AAT deficiency, CF, PAH","Asthma pattern: seasonal, activity, work, night waking, after colds; GINA control questions","Compare activity + dyspnea with a month and a year ago; orthopnea, ADLs, sleep, eating; weight trend"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Numbers and trends","lines":["ABGs first, then SpO₂ trend vs usual; PaCO₂ falls early, rises late","PFTs: ≥ 12% FEV₁ rise after bronchodilator = asthma; GOLD stage by FEV₁ % predicted; ↓ FEV₁/FVC","Polycythemia; low phosphate, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ weaken breathing muscles","AAT level, sweat chloride ≥ 60 (CF), right heart cath (PAH), honeycombing on CT (IPF)"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Right heart, nutrition, psychosocial","lines":["Cor pulmonale: JVD, dependent edema, big tender liver, warm cyanotic extremities","Chronic hypoxia: clubbing, slow refill","Nutrition: weight, early satiety, dyspnea with meals, prealbumin","Isolation, embarrassment about sputum; inhaler cost → many use them only in flares"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"¶ Above all of them, when present:","lines":["Acute airway obstruction outranks everything: status asthmaticus, silent chest, sudden CO₂ rise, chest-tube emergency","Chronic priorities wait when the airway is closing now"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"¶ Before discharge the patient holds a","lines":["Before discharge: written action plan (green / yellow / red)","Demonstrates inhaler technique","States when to call and when to call 911; follow-up already booked"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Independent","lines":["Airway maintenance is the top focus","Upright, arms supported; chair 1 h, 2–3 × a day","Teach pursed-lip + diaphragmatic breathing when NOT dyspneic","Controlled cough: on rising, before meals, at bedtime; fluids ≥ 2 L/day","Small, frequent, high-calorie, high-protein meals; bronchodilator 30 min before","Pace activity, no talking while walking; assess COPD client ≥ q2h; suction only when needed"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Dependent","lines":["O₂ 2–4 L cannula or ≤ 40% Venturi → SpO₂ 88–92%, check ABGs","SABA = relief; ICS + LABA or LAMA = control, stepped up/down","Mucolytics; systemic steroids + antibiotics in exacerbations","PAH therapy via specialist center; nintedanib/pirfenidone (IPF); CFTR modulators (CF)","Lung cancer: chemo, targeted therapy, immunotherapy, radiation; morphine for palliation"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Emergency actions","lines":["Status asthmaticus: IV fluids, bronchodilators, steroids, epinephrine, O₂ → prepare to intubate","Wheeze suddenly gone + low SpO₂ = complete obstruction","Sudden CO₂ rise or PaO₂ fall → NIV or ventilation","Chest tube out → occlusive dressing; disconnected → end in sterile water, then call","Call now: tracheal deviation, new dyspnea, SpO₂ 70 mL/hr, visible eyelets, drainage stopping in first 24 h"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Collaborative","lines":["Dietitian (weight, prealbumin); RT (technique, clearance, BiPAP)","Pulmonary rehab: walk up to 20 min, 2–3 × weekly; OT for adaptive tools","Case manager: home O₂, nebulizer, equipment; social work for drug cost","Cessation (I-PREPARE); hospice in end-stage IPF/cancer","CF/transplant teams — separate CF clients (Burkholderia)"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"¶ Gas exchange is back at the","lines":["Gas exchange back to this client’s chronic baseline; secretions cleared","Breathing costs less effort; endurance up","Weight within 10% of ideal, prealbumin stable","No serious infection, vaccines current","Inhaler technique demonstrated, not described; peak flows self-monitored; action plan in hand"]},{"note":"24-adpie","title":"Unmet looks like:","lines":["↑ effort/rate, SpO₂ below their usual, sharp PaCO₂ rise","Muscle fatigue + hypoxemia that won't lift = impending respiratory failure","New crackles or silent chest; CF FEV₁ ↓ 10%","Weight still falling, edema/JVD, readmission (plan, appointment, or drug cost failed)"]}]},{"id":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Drugs and inhalers for asthma and COPD","blocks":[{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Controllers and relievers","lines":["Controller: daily, even without symptoms","Reliever: stops an attack","Some need drugs only during episodes","Combination drugs: 2+ classes"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Stepping up and down","lines":["Start at step 1, step up to control","Controlled 3 months → step down one step at a time","Goal: control on the lowest dose"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"The five steps","lines":["SABA as needed at every step","1: the SABA (or an ICS-formoterol reliever)","2: + daily LTRA, SLIT","3: ICS-formoterol maintenance + reliever ± LTRA or theophylline","4: medium-dose ICS-formoterol ± LAMA","5: refer → LAMA, biologics, azithromycin, oral steroids"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Formoterol, read carefully","lines":["Formoterol = LABA; the reliever is ICS + formoterol","Table’s “COPD only” → formoterol alone","Exam: SABA = rescue; a LABA alone never","Beyond the book: formoterol works fast; GINA: no SABA-only treatment"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Why","lines":["Climb to control, hold 3 months, come down","Lowest dose that works = safest"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"SABAs in detail","lines":["Albuterol, levalbuterol: fast beta₂ relief","Carry it always","Overuse → tachycardia: watch the HR","Use ≥ 5 min before other inhaled drugs"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"LABAs in detail","lines":["Salmeterol (asthma)","Indacaterol, formoterol, arformoterol: COPD","Slow onset → never a reliever"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Cholinergic antagonists in detail","lines":["Block acetylcholine → airways open, fewer secretions","Aclidinium, umeclidinium: prevention","Ipratropium: relief + prevention · tiotropium once daily","Shake the MDI; fluids for dry mouth","Overdose: blurred vision, eye pain, headache, nausea, palpitations, tremor, insomnia"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Inhaled corticosteroids in detail","lines":["Fluticasone, beclomethasone, budesonide: prevent, don’t open","Daily; full effect after 48–72 h of regular use","Mouth care, check daily → Candida","Some once daily; ICS-LABA combinations once daily"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Oral prednisone teaching","lines":["Explain the expected side effects","Avoid people with colds","Avoid injury: bruising, petechiae","With food (ulcers)","Never stop suddenly (adrenal suppression)"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Cromolyns and montelukast in detail","lines":["Nedocromil: daily mast-cell stabilizer; keep the inhaler clean","Montelukast: boxed warning → mood changes, suicidal thoughts","Daily; doesn’t replace steroids or the reliever"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Biologic injections in detail","lines":["IL-5 blockers → eosinophilic asthma only","Reslizumab: slow IV only; the others subcutaneous","Omalizumab (anti-IgE) → allergic asthma only","Not relievers","Watch ≥ 2 h for anaphylaxis"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"COPD’s long-acting drugs","lines":["Long-acting: arformoterol, indacaterol, olodaterol, tiotropium, aclidinium","Combinations: fluticasone/vilanterol; triple + umeclidinium","More systemic drugs than in asthma","Guaifenesin + dextromethorphan raises the cough threshold","Show me the inhaler at every visit"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Why#2","lines":["Fast → carry it, use it first","Slow → daily, never in an attack","Steroids → lower defenses","Biologics → 2-hour anaphylaxis watch"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"The three kinds of inhaler","lines":["MDI: liquid spray, propellant, spacer preferred","DPI: powder, pulled in by the patient’s breath","SMI: spring-made mist; more reaches the small airways","DPI and SMI: no spacer"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Why technique fails","lines":["Poor technique → drug in mouth, throat, nose","Teach in person or by video (use, care, cleaning)","Dose counter → refill on time"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Box 24.4 step by step","lines":["MDI: shake 5 s each puff, mouth not nose, slow and deep, hold 5–10 s","DPI: exhale away from it, between the front teeth, fast 2–3 s, hold 4–10 s, no water","SMI: prime 3× toward the floor; once after > 3 days unused","SMI: aim at the throat, vents uncovered, hold 10 s","Follow the manufacturer"]},{"note":"24-drugs-and-inhalers-for-asthma-and-copd","title":"Why#3","lines":["MDI: the propellant pushes → breathe in slowly","DPI: no propellant → breathe in fast","Never breathe out into a DPI"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"Use the prescribed asthma action plan. Current GINA care includes inhaled corticosteroid-containing treatment; do not interpret a SABA rescue example as a recommendation for SABA-only long-term treatment. ICS-formoterol can be an anti-inflammatory reliever when that regimen is prescribed.","sources":[{"title":"GINA 2026: asthma management and prevention","url":"https://ginasthma.org/2026-gina-summary-guide/"}],"steps":[{"id":"asthma:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client in an asthma attack had loud wheezing. Now wheezing is absent, SpO₂ is 86%, and the client is drowsy. Which action first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Silent chest is an emergency.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Document improvement","correct":false,"why":"Not improvement — silent chest."},{"id":"o1","text":"Call the rapid response team and give the prescribed SABA and oxygen","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Silent chest with drowsiness means near-absent air movement and rising CO₂."},{"id":"o2","text":"Encourage pursed-lip breathing","correct":false,"why":"Useful for COPD, not this crisis."},{"id":"o3","text":"Give the inhaled corticosteroid","correct":false,"why":"ICS does not relieve acute bronchospasm."}]},{"id":"asthma:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why co₂ retention is the late sign?","answer":"CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate; ABGs near-normal until advanced; Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"asthma:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about action plan and self-management?","answer":"Plan: daily controller, reliever use, control questions, how to step up, when to call, emergency steps; Symptom diary + peak flow during flares, after changes, when worse; Keep exercising → adjust or premedicate with SABA","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"COPD: air trapping, gas exchange and safe treatment","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"Smoke tips proteases over alpha₁-antitrypsin, destroying elastin (emphysema) and inflaming airways (chronic bronchitis). Progressive, poorly reversible."},{"title":"Observe","text":"Barrel chest, tripod position · Pursed-lip breathing · Prolonged exhalation, diminished sounds · Clubbing, weight loss"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"O₂ low flow to SpO₂ 88–92% · Upright / tripod · Pursed-lip + diaphragmatic breathing · Airway clearance, hydration"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Change from baseline beats abnormal-but-baseline.","task":"Which COPD client needs attention first?","cue":"CO₂ narcosis depresses the brain.","rule":"Change from baseline beats abnormal-but-baseline.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"copd","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"COPD: air trapping, gas exchange and safe treatment","notes":["24-overview","24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Smoke tips proteases over alpha₁-antitrypsin, destroying elastin (emphysema) and inflaming airways (chronic bronchitis). Progressive, poorly reversible.","Findings: Barrel chest, tripod position; Pursed-lip breathing; Prolonged exhalation, diminished sounds; Clubbing, weight loss; Chronic productive cough (bronchitis)","Nursing actions: O₂ low flow to SpO₂ 88–92%; Upright / tripod; Pursed-lip + diaphragmatic breathing; Airway clearance, hydration","Drowsy or confused = rising CO₂. JVD + edema = right heart failure (cor pulmonale)."],"terms":["emphysema","hypercapnia","tiotropium"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"24-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"24-overview","title":"The tree","lines":["Trachea → 2 mainstem → 5 secondary bronchi → bronchioles → alveolar ducts → alveoli (exchange)","Problem anywhere along the tube → ↓ gas exchange + ↓ systemic perfusion"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"The flattened diaphragm","lines":["Hyperinflated lung → diaphragm pushed flat → weak pull","Recruits neck, chest wall, abdominal accessory muscles → more O₂ burned for less air","Inhales before exhale finishes → uncoordinated pattern + air hunger"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"The pressure chain to the heart","lines":["Trapped air + collapsed airways + stiff walls → pulmonary vessels narrowed","Thin RV must push high pressure → hypertrophies → fails = cor pulmonale (right HF from lung disease)","Normal pulmonary pressure 15–18 mm Hg; hypertension > 25"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Why CO₂ retention is the late sign","lines":["CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate","ABGs near-normal until advanced","Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Lower airway = millions of parallel paths → decline over months–years, not minutes","So: trend > single finding, the patient's own baseline is the reference","Work of breathing, not SpO₂, shows if the disease is winning"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ On exam Intro. Below the larynx","lines":["Below the larynx the airway becomes a tree (bronchi → bronchioles → alveoli)","Nothing closes in minutes → this chapter is chronic, progressive loss","Six ways it fails: narrowed tube (asthma, chronic bronchitis) · destroyed sac (emphysema) · thick membrane (fibrosis) · mucus plugs (CF) · squeezed vessel (PAH) · tumor (lung cancer)","Name which one → findings, drug class and priority follow"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ ICS → local immune suppression →","lines":["ICS → local immune suppression → oral thrush (rinse + spit)","LABA alone in asthma → masks symptoms while inflammation worsens → always with ICS","Epoprostenol half-life minutes → interruption = rebound PAH"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"¶ On exam Proteases versus elastin —","lines":["Proteases normally destroy inhaled particles","Smoke drives excess proteases → they digest elastin → emphysema","Alpha₁-antitrypsin is the brake; inherited AAT deficiency removes it","Emphysema in a non-smoker or before 40 → think genetics (AAT)"]},{"note":"24-overview","title":"Elastic recoil","lines":["Recoil empties the lung (exhalation is passive)","Elastin destroyed → inflates but won't deflate → trapped air, bullae, ↓ surface area"]}]},{"id":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"COPD — the gas exchange concept exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"What it is","lines":["Emphysema + chronic bronchitis: separate diseases, most patients have both","~15.7 million Americans; ~3 million deaths/yr worldwide"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Emphysema — the sac","lines":["Elastin destroyed → no recoil → hyperinflation, air trapping, flat diaphragm, accessory muscles","Primary = AAT deficiency (non-smoker or onset < 40) · secondary = smoke, pollution, work exposure, childhood infections","Late: CO₂ retention + respiratory acidosis, low PaO₂, ↓ diffusion capacity"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Chronic bronchitis — the tube","lines":["Inflamed bronchi + ↑ mucus; productive cough ≥ 3 months/yr × 2 years","Irritants → edema, congestion, bronchospasm; mucus glands grow; walls thicken","Mucus → chronic infection","Diffusion capacity usually normal (bronchitis alone)"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Risk","lines":["Smoking #1; ≥ 20 pack-years usually already shows PFT changes; ask about vaping","Asthma → 12× COPD risk (asthma-COPD overlap syndrome)","More in women and ≥ 65"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Complications","lines":["Hypoxemia + acidosis, infection, cor pulmonale, dysrhythmias, respiratory failure","Cor pulmonale: dyspnea, fatigue, enlarged tender liver, warm cyanotic hands/feet + bounding pulses, cyanotic lips, JVD, RVH, pulsations below sternum, nausea, dependent edema, acidosis, pulmonary HTN"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"What the patient looks like","lines":["Thin, wasted limbs but big neck muscles, stooped, tripod position","Rapid shallow, jerky breathing; 40–50/min in a flare = act now","↓ fremitus, hyperresonance, ↓ breath sounds, wheezes in + out, crackles usually absent","Silent chest = serious obstruction or pneumothorax","Bronchitis adds: dusky cyanosis, lots of sputum, slow cap refill, clubbing"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Other labs","lines":["ABGs, then oximetry for trend","Polycythemia (↑ Hgb/Hct) from chronic hypoxia","↓ PO₄, K⁺, Ca²⁺, Mg²⁺ → weak muscles","WBC + sputum culture if infected; serum AAT if family history","SpO₂ often < 90% — change from their own baseline is what matters"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Gas exchange · 2. Weight loss · 3. Fatigue · 4. Infection risk"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Interventions — airway maintenance first","lines":["Assess rate, rhythm, depth, accessory muscles first; look for infection","Same drugs as asthma, more long-acting + combos (fluticasone/vilanterol; + umeclidinium) + mucolytics (saline, acetylcysteine, guaifenesin)","Stepped: up in flares, down when stable · inhaler demo every visit","Hospitalized COPD patient: assess ≥ q2 h whatever the admission","Sharp CO₂ ↑ or PaO₂ ↓ → escalate; NIV preferred over intubation"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"The techniques","lines":["Diaphragmatic: supine, knees bent, weight on abdomen, chest still","Pursed-lip: in nose, out through pursed lips, exhale ≥ 2× longer, no breath holding; inhale before effort, exhale during","Teach when not dyspneic","Upright, arms supported; chair 1 h, 2–3×/day","Controlled cough: feet flat, lean in, hug pillow → 3–5 pursed-lip breaths → deep breath → 2–3 mini-coughs, repeat ≥ 2× · on waking, before meals, before bed"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Oxygen, exercise, hydration, nutrition","lines":["O₂ 2–4 L/min or ≤ 40% Venturi, titrate slowly to SpO₂ 88–92% with ABGs — treating hypoxemia still comes first; no flames","Rehab: walk to symptom limit, rest, 20 min total, ≥ 2–3×/wk","Fluids ≥ 2 L/day unless contraindicated","Nutrition: weight within 10% of ideal, 4–6 small meals, biggest when rested, bronchodilator 30 min before eating, high-calorie high-protein, no gas-forming/dry foods or caffeine, no fluids with meals if full early; track weight + prealbumin"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Energy conservation","lines":["Don't rush mornings; split tasks, rest between, items in reach","Sit leaning on elbows; avoid arms raised (accessory muscles busy)","Don't talk while walking; never hold breath during effort"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Surgery","lines":["Lung volume reduction: removes hyperinflated tissue","Candidates: < 75, smoke-free ≥ 6 months, end-stage emphysema, minimal bronchitis, stable heart; usually bilateral VATS","After: incentive spirometry 10×/h awake, chest PT day 1, hourly lung checks","Transplant when nothing else remains"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Transition to home","lines":["Green/yellow/red zone action plan before discharge + appointments booked","Non-adherence = cost, misunderstanding, transport","Red zone → 911: dyspnea at rest, no activity, fever/chills, confusion/drowsy, chest pain, hemoptysis; plan tells paramedics CO₂ retainer → SpO₂ 88–92%"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["88–92% is not undertreatment: retainer's drive = hypoxemia → flooding O₂ lets CO₂ climb","But withholding O₂ kills faster → give it, titrate slowly, watch CO₂","Pursed lips add resistance → hold bronchioles open → trapped air empties"]},{"note":"24-copd-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemplar","title":"GOLD classification — by FEV₁ % predicted","lines":["FEV₁ % predicted: 1 mild ≥ 80 · 2 moderate 50–79 · 3 severe 30–49 · 4 very severe < 30","PFTs before + after bronchodilator; residual volume most affected; FEV₁/FVC falls","CAT (8 items, 0–40) → A–D symptom/risk group (GOLD 4 can be A; GOLD 1 can be D)"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"copd:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which COPD client needs attention first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Change from baseline beats abnormal-but-baseline.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"SpO₂ 89% on 2 L/min, baseline 88–90%","correct":false,"why":"At baseline."},{"id":"o1","text":"Barrel chest and pursed-lip breathing","correct":false,"why":"Expected chronic findings."},{"id":"o2","text":"New confusion and PaCO₂ rising from 50 to 68 mm Hg","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Worsening hypercapnia with change in mentation — respiratory failure."},{"id":"o3","text":"Weight loss of 1 kg this month","correct":false,"why":"Important, not urgent."}]},{"id":"copd:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why co₂ retention is the late sign?","answer":"CO₂ diffuses easier than O₂ → hypoxemia first, patient ↑ rate to compensate; ABGs near-normal until advanced; Then CO₂ made faster than blown off → chronic hypercapnia + respiratory acidosis; kidney keeps HCO₃⁻","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"copd:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about priority problems?","answer":"1. Gas exchange · 2. Weight loss · 3. Fatigue · 4. Infection risk","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses · Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping · Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)"},{"title":"Warning signals to teach","text":"Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough · Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis · Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis · Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing"},{"title":"Diagnosis and treatment","text":"CXR finds → CT defines/guides biopsy → biopsy/pleural fluid cells = diagnosis; PET, MRI stage · Chemo first choice (esp. SCLC) · Targeted needs the target: EGFR (afatinib, erlotinib), ALK (alectinib, crizotinib) → test cells first · Immunotherapy (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab): not curative, not first-line alone"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Know the chest tube escalation list.","task":"Which findings indicate a chest tube needs immediate provider notification? Select all that apply.","cue":"Air or blood accumulating under pressure displaces the mediastinum.","rule":"Know the chest tube escalation list.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"chest-tubes","ch":"24","chapters":["24"],"title":"Chest tubes and other lower-airway problems","notes":["24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 24; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses; Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping; Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)","Warning signals to teach: Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough; Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis; Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis; Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing","Diagnosis and treatment: CXR finds → CT defines/guides biopsy → biopsy/pleural fluid cells = diagnosis; PET, MRI stage; Chemo first choice (esp. SCLC); Targeted needs the target: EGFR (afatinib, erlotinib), ALK (alectinib, crizotinib) → test cells first; Immunotherapy (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab): not curative, not first-line alone; Radiation: skin irritation, fatigue, nausea, taste change, esophagitis → bland high-cal food; no sun on the field during + ≥ 1 yr after; Photodynamic: drug collects in cancer cells 48–72 h → laser; Surgery = main treatment for stage I–II NSCLC: wedge/segment, lobectomy, sleeve, pneumonectomy; open or VATS","Why: Everything is downstream of water: Cl⁻ can't move → water doesn't follow → all secretions too thick; So therapy is mechanical (vest, drainage, mucolytics), and new drugs target the channel itself"],"terms":["pneumothorax","hemothorax","atelectasis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Lung cancer and the chest tube","blocks":[{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"The disease","lines":["2nd most common cancer (men + women); 5-yr survival NSCLC ~26%, SCLC ~7%","Bronchogenic: small cell vs non-small cell","Spreads by extension, blood, lymph → nodes, bone, liver, brain, adrenals; staged by TNM"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Risk","lines":["Smoking → ~81% of deaths; 2nd/3rd-hand smoke too; vaping unknown","Asbestos, beryllium, chromium, coal products, cobalt, iron oxide, mustard gas, petroleum, radiation, tar, nickel, uranium, air pollution","Non-smoker cases rising · annual CT for high-risk adults"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Warning signals to teach","lines":["Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough","Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis","Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis","Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Assessment","lines":["Symptoms nonspecific + late","Pain: local, one-sided, pleuritic, or down the arm (nerve plexus)","↑ fremitus = tumor/fluid · ↓/absent = bronchus blocked · trachea may shift","Wheeze = partial · absent sounds = complete obstruction · rub = inflammation","Muffled heart sounds (tamponade), dysrhythmias, clubbing; late fatigue, weight loss, dysphagia","SVC syndrome = emergency · confusion/personality change → brain mets"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Paraneoplastic syndromes","lines":["Tumor makes hormones (mostly SCLC):","ACTH → Cushing · ADH → SIADH (weight gain, edema, dilute lytes) · FSH → gynecomastia","PTH → hypercalcemia · insulin → hypoglycemia"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Diagnosis and treatment","lines":["CXR finds → CT defines/guides biopsy → biopsy/pleural fluid cells = diagnosis; PET, MRI stage","Chemo first choice (esp. SCLC)","Targeted needs the target: EGFR (afatinib, erlotinib), ALK (alectinib, crizotinib) → test cells first","Immunotherapy (atezolizumab, durvalumab, nivolumab, pembrolizumab): not curative, not first-line alone","Radiation: skin irritation, fatigue, nausea, taste change, esophagitis → bland high-cal food; no sun on the field during + ≥ 1 yr after","Photodynamic: drug collects in cancer cells 48–72 h → laser","Surgery = main treatment for stage I–II NSCLC: wedge/segment, lobectomy, sleeve, pneumonectomy; open or VATS"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Chest tube — what the three chambers do","lines":["Air tube high in front (apex); fluid tube low at the base; airtight dressing","Drainage system below chest level","1. Collection: measure hourly × 24 h; must never reach the tubes → else tension pneumothorax","2. Water seal: one-way valve, ≥ 2 cm water; bubbles as air leaves, stops when lung re-expands","3. Suction: wet (gentle bubbling) or dry (dialed)"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Reading the system","lines":["Tidaling (water ↑ 2–4 in on inhale, ↓ on exhale) = normal; gone = lung re-expanded or tube blocked","Bubbling on cough/forced exhale = OK · continuous bubbling = air leak → check connections, notify","No kinks/dependent loops; coil loosely; tape connections; clamp only briefly","Sterile gauze + padded clamps at bedside","Don't strip; milk only if ordered","After removal: assess hourly (lung may collapse again)"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Notify the surgeon or Rapid Response Team immediately for","lines":["Tracheal deviation, sudden/↑ dyspnea, SpO₂ < 90%","Drainage > 70 mL/h; drainage stops in first 24 h; eyelets visible","Tube out → cover with dry sterile gauze first","Disconnected → put end in sterile water below chest level first"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"After thoracic surgery","lines":["Assess ≥ q2 h: tracheal alignment, SpO₂, rate/depth, crackles on non-op side","O₂ × 2 days, semi-Fowler/chair early, incentive spirometry hourly awake, splint to cough, tube not pulling","PCA taught early; VS before/after opioids","Pneumonectomy: no chest tube; risks empyema + bronchopleural fistula; position per surgeon (non-op side protects stump / op side lets fluid fill)"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Palliation","lines":["O₂ for hypoxemia + dyspnea sensation","Short high-dose radiation: hemoptysis, airway/SVC/esophageal compression, bone pain","Thoracentesis (fluid re-forms)","Morphine + positioning for dyspnea (often recliner); around-the-clock + PRN analgesia; hospice"]},{"note":"24-lung-cancer-and-the-chest-tube","title":"Why","lines":["Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses","Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping","Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)"]}]},{"id":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Cystic fibrosis in the adult","blocks":[{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"One defect, every organ","lines":["Autosomal recessive CFTR gene → chloride can't move → dry, thick mucus","Plugs lungs + pancreas, liver, salivary glands, testes","> 2000 mutations, ~25 most common → severity varies","~half of US patients are adults; life expectancy ~46; death = respiratory failure"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Diagnosis","lines":["Sweat chloride ≥ 60 mmol/L = positive · 30–59 = possible","Genetic test names the mutation; usually diagnosed by age 2"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Pulmonary picture","lines":["Congestion, ↓ exercise tolerance, cough, sputum, accessory muscles, ↓ FVC + FEV₁, infiltrate, ↑ AP diameter","Chronic infection, bronchitis, bronchiectasis, abscesses","Complications: pneumothorax, hemorrhage, respiratory failure"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Non-pulmonary","lines":["Distention, GERD, rectal prolapse, foul fatty stools (steatorrhea)","Fat-soluble vitamin (A, D, E, K) deficiency, malnutrition","Pancreatic insufficiency, male sterility, cirrhosis, osteoporosis, diabetes in > 50% of adults"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Exacerbation is defined","lines":["↑ congestion, ↓ activity, new crackles, FEV₁ ↓ 10%, more/bloody sputum, cough, weight loss, fatigue, ↓ SpO₂","Treat: airway clearance 4×/day, bronchodilators, mucolytics, steroids, O₂, antibiotics 14–21 days (IV if severe)","Avoid mechanical ventilation; BiPAP may be daily in advanced disease"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Airway clearance","lines":["PEP, active cycle breathing, exercise, daily chest PT + postural drainage","HFCWO vest: up to 25×/s → mini-coughs loosen mucus"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"The two organisms","lines":["Pseudomonas = most common","Burkholderia cepacia = dangerous: resistant, spread by casual contact between CF patients","→ separate CF patients (units, clinic days), CF-approved cleaning, no handshakes, meticulous hand hygiene","Add co-trimoxazole"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Modulators","lines":["Ivacaftor (potentiator) holds the channel open","Ivacaftor/lumacaftor (corrector) for F508del (most common)","Triple: ivacaftor + tezacaftor + elexacaftor → up to 90%","Recurrent hemoptysis → arterial embolization","Transplant fixes lungs only; antirejection ↑ infection risk"]},{"note":"24-cystic-fibrosis-in-the-adult","title":"Why","lines":["Everything is downstream of water: Cl⁻ can't move → water doesn't follow → all secretions too thick","So therapy is mechanical (vest, drainage, mucolytics), and new drugs target the channel itself"]}]},{"id":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension and idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","blocks":[{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"PAH — the numbers","lines":["Pulmonary pressure > 25 mm Hg (normal 15–18)","Thin RV fails → cor pulmonale (can be fatal)","Secondary → after COPD/fibrosis (treat cause) · Primary/idiopathic → unknown, no other lung disease"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"Who and how fast","lines":["Women 30–60, runs in families (autosomal dominant); BMPR2 mutation in 15–20%","Appetite suppressants (fen-phen, dexfenfluramine) ↑ risk","Untreated → death in ~2 years (heart failure); often diagnosed late","Women with an affected first-degree relative → regular checks"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"Presentation and diagnosis","lines":["Earliest: dyspnea + fatigue in a healthy adult, maybe angina-like pain","Diagnosis = right heart catheterization; V/Q, PFTs, CT support","Class I no limit → II mild-moderate activity → III < ordinary activity → IV at rest + right HF"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"Drugs and the teaching that goes with them","lines":["Endothelin antagonists (bosentan, ambrisentan, macitentan): full glass of water, don't crush; hypotension; liver toxicity (jaundice, dark urine, RUQ pain, no alcohol); pregnancy contraindicated → 2 contraceptives + monthly tests","Epoprostenol IV continuous central line → never stop abruptly (rebound) · treprostinil SC/IV/inhaled/oral","Sildenafil, tadalafil (PDE-5) → exercise capacity; postural hypotension","O₂ to SpO₂ > 90%, diuretics + daily weights; specialist center","Surgery: lung transplant, or heart-lung once cor pulmonale"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"IPF — excessive healing","lines":["Restrictive, unknown cause; men 50–70; smoking/irritants","Healing doesn't stop → fibrosis thickens alveolar walls","Death in 3–5 years"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"IPF findings and care","lines":["Slow exertional dyspnea, ↓ FVC, honeycomb on CT; later hypoxemia resistant to high-flow O₂","Nintedanib, pirfenidone slow it (start early); steroids, NAC, immunosuppressants","Transplant = only cure","Nursing: prevent infection, home O₂, pacing; late morphine for dyspnea; hospice if < 6 months"]},{"note":"24-pulmonary-arterial-hypertension-and-idio","title":"Why","lines":["\"Lung problem\" ≠ \"airway problem\"","PAH = vessel disease → vasodilators; the right heart fails","IPF = membrane disease → bronchodilators don't help; O₂ eventually fails (distance, not tube width)"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"chest-tubes:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client receiving epoprostenol has the infusion stop due to a pump failure. Which action first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Never interrupt continuous prostacyclin.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Wait for pharmacy to send a new bag","correct":false,"why":"Delay can be fatal."},{"id":"o1","text":"Restart the infusion immediately using the backup pump/line per protocol","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Half-life is minutes; rebound PAH can occur."},{"id":"o2","text":"Give an oral PAH drug","correct":false,"why":"Not an equivalent substitute."},{"id":"o3","text":"Document the event","correct":false,"why":"Care first."}]},{"id":"chest-tubes:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Pleural space is negative pressure → open it and the lung collapses; Everything protects a one-way exit: seal > 2 cm, below chest, no kinks, no long clamping; Tube out of patient → occlusive gauze · tube disconnected → sterile water (rebuild the valve)","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"chest-tubes:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about warning signals to teach?","answer":"Hoarseness, breathing change, new/changed cough; Blood-streaked, rust, purulent sputum; hemoptysis; Chest/shoulder/arm pain; recurring effusion, pneumonia, bronchitis; Dyspnea, fever + other signs, wheeze, weight loss, clubbing","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Pneumonia: infection, oxygenation and deterioration","cards":[{"title":"Mechanism","text":"Microbes in the alveoli trigger inflammation that fills the air spaces with exudate. Blood passes unventilated alveoli → hypoxemia."},{"title":"Observe","text":"Fever, productive cough · Crackles, ↓ breath sounds · Dull percussion, ↑ tactile fremitus · Pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea"},{"title":"Act and reassess","text":"Cultures first, then antibiotics fast · O₂, HOB up · Cough, deep breathe, incentive spirometer · Fluids 2–3 L unless restricted; ambulate"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Instability and change in mental status first.","task":"Which client with a respiratory infection should the nurse see first?","cue":"Systemic infection causes vasodilation and poor perfusion.","rule":"Instability and change in mental status first.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"pneumonia","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Pneumonia: infection, oxygenation and deterioration","notes":["25-overview","25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","25-adpie"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 25; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Microbes in the alveoli trigger inflammation that fills the air spaces with exudate. Blood passes unventilated alveoli → hypoxemia.","Findings: Fever, productive cough; Crackles, ↓ breath sounds; Dull percussion, ↑ tactile fremitus; Pleuritic chest pain, tachypnea; Older adult: confusion, maybe no fever","Nursing actions: Cultures first, then antibiotics fast; O₂, HOB up; Cough, deep breathe, incentive spirometer; Fluids 2–3 L unless restricted; ambulate","Low BP, falling SpO₂, new confusion, lactate >2 = sepsis."],"terms":["pneumonia","consolidation","sepsis"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"25-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"25-overview","title":"How infection destroys diffusion","lines":["Pathogens multiply in alveoli → WBCs move in, capillaries leak, edema + exudate, walls thicken, RBCs + fibrin follow","Gas must cross a thick wet wall → hypoxemia"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"Then the lung stiffens","lines":["Fibrin + edema → ↓ compliance + vital capacity; atelectasis takes more units out","Leak spreads infection sideways; blood past unventilated alveoli = shunt"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"Why immunity is the whole prevention story","lines":["Viral strains change a little every year → old antibodies partly work → several colds/yr, annual flu shot","Pandemic = organism with no human immunity (jumped from birds/animals): 1918 flu, 2009 H1N1, COVID-19"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Most tested idea: older adults present atypically","Fever, cough may be absent; WBC normal until severe; but hypoxemia usually present","→ most common sign = new confusion → treat as a respiratory assessment; waiting ↑ sepsis + death"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"¶ On exam Intro. Chapter 24 was","lines":["Ch 24 = structure failing slowly; ch 25 = an organism filling the alveoli","Two questions every time: how bad is diffusion? and who else is at risk?","Big four: influenza, pneumonia, COVID-19, TB","Nursing work: prevent transmission → restore gas exchange → finish the drug course"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"¶ On exam Two spread patterns —","lines":["Lobar pneumonia: a segment or whole lobe consolidated (solid with exudate)","Bronchopneumonia: scattered patches around the bronchi","Spread depends on host defenses","Abscess through the bronchial wall → necrosis"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"¶ On exam Where it goes next","lines":["Into the blood → septicemia, sepsis","Into the pleural space → empyema (pus)","Pneumonia is a common cause of sepsis → sepsis screen + SpO₂ with every set of vitals"]},{"note":"25-overview","title":"¶ On exam Why the findings are","lines":["Fluid conducts sound better than air","Pneumonia: ↑ fremitus, dull percussion, bronchial sounds, crackles","Emphysema: ↓ fremitus, hyperresonance","Same tools, opposite findings — one lung too full, the other too empty"]}]},{"id":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Pneumonia — the gas exchange concept exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"What it is","lines":["Excess lung fluid from inflammation; infection adds thick exudate → ↓ gas exchange","In interstitium, alveoli, often bronchioles","Any organism (bacteria, virus, mycoplasma, fungi, rickettsiae, protozoa, helminths)","Non-infectious: toxic gases, fumes, smoke, aspiration (water, food, saliva, vomit)"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"The organisms to name","lines":["Bacterial #1: Streptococcus pneumoniae · viral: influenza A/B, COVID-19","Drug-resistant S. pneumoniae rising → local patterns pick the antibiotic"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Three categories","lines":["CAP: most common, late fall/winter, often after flu; empirical antibiotics ≥ 5 days; in ED first dose within 4 h","HAP: onset ≥ 48 h after admission","VAP: ≥ 48 h after intubation (tube bypasses defenses → aspiration; dental plaque ↑ risk)","HCAP no longer used"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Preventing HAP and VAP","lines":["Pulmonary hygiene, ambulation, hydration, aspiration screening tool, early sepsis watch, vigorous oral care, hand hygiene","VAP bundle: HOB ≥ 30°, daily wean check, DVT prophylaxis, oral care, stress ulcer prophylaxis, suction PRN/subglottal"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Prevention teaching","lines":["Annual flu; pneumococcal (PPSV23 + PCV15, or PCV20 alone) for > 65 and 19–64 with risk factors; COVID series + boosters","Avoid crowds in flu season; cough, turn, deep breathe if immobile; clean equipment; no smoke/vape","Rest, eat well, ≥ 3 L fluid/day unless restricted","Cold/flu → be seen if fever > 24 h, illness > 1 week, or worse"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Assessment","lines":["Flushed, anxious; chest pain, myalgia, headache, chills, fever, cough, tachycardia, dyspnea, sputum, hemoptysis; tripod","Crackles, ↓ breath sounds, wheeze; bronchial sounds over consolidation, ↑ fremitus, dull percussion, unequal expansion","vs baseline: orthostatic hypotension (vasodilation + dehydration); rapid weak pulse = hypoxemia, dehydration, or early sepsis/shock; dysrhythmias"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Why each sign happens","lines":["↑ RR + dyspnea → chemoreceptors, J-receptors, ↓ compliance, anxiety, pain","Hypoxemia → consolidation + shunt","Rust/bloody/purulent sputum → fluid + RBCs into alveoli","Fever → pyrogens reset the hypothalamus","Pleuritic pain → inflamed parietal pleura on inspiration"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Labs and imaging","lines":["Sputum Gram stain + C&S (often no organism found; sputum trap if very ill)","CBC: ↑ WBC — often normal in older adults","Blood cultures, ABGs if severe, lactate (sepsis), flu test","↑ BUN + hypernatremia = dehydration","CXR most common but may lag ≥ 2 days; essential in older adults","SpO₂; thoracentesis if effusion"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Gas exchange (↓ diffusion) · 2. Airway obstruction (secretions, fatigue, weakness) · 3. Sepsis"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Interventions","lines":["O₂ (confused patient may not tolerate a mask; check skin under device/ears)","Incentive spirometry: sit up, exhale, seal, slow deep breath, hold 2–4 s, 5–10 breaths/h awake, record volume","Cough + deep breathe ≥ q2 h; ≥ 2 L fluid/day (watch I&O, mucosa, turgor)","Bronchodilators, steroids, guaifenesin","Antibiotics for all except viral: 5–7 days uncomplicated CAP, up to 21 days HAP/immunocompromised","Acid aspiration → ARDS, permanent damage"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Transition and evaluation","lines":["Finish the whole antibiotic course","Fatigue, weakness, cough for weeks = expected; full recovery will come; one floor, bedside commode","Report: returning chills/fever, cough, dyspnea, wheeze, hemoptysis, more sputum, chest pain, ↑ fatigue","Avoid crowds/irritants; vaccinate after recovery","Goals: SaO₂ ≥ 95% (or their normal), clear lungs, afebrile, normal WBC","High 30-day readmission, esp. older adults"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Why","lines":["Interventions = O₂, spirometry, coughing, fluids, finishing antibiotics → 3 of 5 are the patient's job → teaching is the treatment","Aspiration is often silent (no cough, no gag) → screen routinely"]},{"note":"25-pneumonia-the-gas-exchange-concept-exemp","title":"Risk factors — CAP","lines":["CAP: older adult; no pneumococcal vaccine or > 5 yr ago; no flu shot last year; COVID not current; low immunity; recent viral exposure; tobacco/alcohol/secondhand smoke","Hospital: older, lung disease, gram-negative colonization of mouth/throat/stomach, ↓ LOC, recent aspiration, ET/NG tube or trach, poor nutrition, low immunity","Drugs that ↑ gastric pH (H₂ blockers, antacids), alkaline tube feeds, mechanical ventilation"]}]},{"id":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Seasonal influenza and pandemic infection","blocks":[{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Seasonal influenza","lines":["Highly contagious viral infection; types A, B, C","Deadly for older adults, HF, chronic lung disease, immunocompromised","Most treated at home"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"The picture and the window","lines":["Sudden: severe headache, aches, fever, chills, fatigue; sore throat, cough, runny nose","B can add N/V/diarrhea","Contagious 24 h before symptoms → 5 days after","Fatigue 1–2 weeks after"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Vaccination","lines":["Annual (rebuilt each year); IM or intranasal; high-dose for > 65","Everyone ≥ 6 months, no preferred product","Nurses = key info source; your vaccine protects patients too"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Containment teaching","lines":["Handwashing (after nose blowing, coughing, touching face); stay home; tissues → trash","Cough/sneeze into upper sleeve, not hand"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Diagnosis and drugs","lines":["Usually clinical when flu is around","Rapid test: many false negatives → treat if suspected; preferred RT-PCR (A vs B)","No antibiotics for viral","Oseltamivir, zanamivir, peramivir, baloxavir: start within 24–48 h; ↓ hospitalization in high-risk","Rest, fluids, saline gargles, antihistamines"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Pandemic respiratory infection","lines":["Organism with no human immunity, spreads globally (animal virus mutated)","Surveillance, early recognition, quarantine, distancing, stockpiled vaccine","Stop gatherings, schools, travel","Households: ≥ 2 weeks of food, water, meds per person"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Care priorities","lines":["Supportive + stop spread","Ask travel in last 14 days → mask + isolate until assessed","Unknown route → Airborne + Droplet + Contact together","Home: rest, fluids, acetaminophen; oseltamivir within 48 h","O₂ for hypoxia/dyspnea/cognition change; ventilation if refractory; antibiotics only for bacterial pneumonia on top"]},{"note":"25-seasonal-influenza-and-pandemic-infectio","title":"Why","lines":["24–48 h antiviral window → a \"flu\" call is triage","By day 5 oseltamivir offers little → argument for next year's vaccine","Contagious a day before symptoms → teach containment before diagnosis"]}]},{"id":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"COVID-19 — the infection concept exemplar","blocks":[{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"The virus and the receptor","lines":["SARS-CoV-2 enters via ACE2 receptors (vessels, lungs, heart, brain, kidneys, gut, liver, pharynx) → any organ","Inflammation, vasoconstriction, hypercoagulability, endothelial damage, edema","Severe = cytokine storm (type 2 alveolar cells infected)","Earlier: SARS 2002–03, MERS 2012; variants evade immunity"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Vaccination","lines":["From 6 months","Pfizer/Moderna = mRNA; Novavax = protein subunit; Janssen used less (clots + low platelets)","Same brand for primary series; Pfizer 3–8 wk, Moderna 4–8 wk apart; fully vaccinated 2 wk after last dose","Booster ~5 months; 2nd booster 50+ ≥ 4 months later","Expected: sore arm, headache, aches, chills, fever, fatigue, nausea · rare: anaphylaxis, myocarditis → observe ≥ 15 min","Vaccinate after isolation ends; after infection wait 3 months"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Equity","lines":["Black, Hispanic, American Indian/Alaska Native adults → higher cases + deaths, even after adjustment","Distancing had unequal economic/social costs","Nursing: address misinformation + rebuild trust"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"History","lines":["URI symptoms, cough, fever, chills, GI upset, new loss of taste/smell","Ask: travel, sick household, hospital/LTC in last 30 days, home tests, vaccination (vaccinated people get it too)","Symptoms 2–14 days after exposure"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"The five dyspnea questions","lines":["Breathing harder/faster at rest? Hard to catch breath? Dizzy when walking? What brings it on? Worse over 1–2 days?","Often by telehealth — home care preferred"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Key features","lines":["Fever/chills, cough, dyspnea, fatigue, aches, headache, taste/smell loss, sore throat, N/V, diarrhea, abdominal pain","Unique: conjunctivitis, clots (venous + arterial), encephalopathy/delirium, red nodules on fingertips (young adults)","Emergency: trouble breathing, persistent chest pain, new confusion, can't wake/stay awake, blue lips/face"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Testing","lines":["Preferred: NAAT (RT-PCR); rapid antigen in minutes; home antigen","NAAT + antigen = current infection · antibody = past infection only","Nasal swab (sometimes saliva); imaging if dyspnea","SpO₂ ≤ 94% on room air → in-person evaluation; normal SpO₂ doesn't dismiss worsening dyspnea"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Risk factors for severe disease","lines":["≥ 65, cancer, stroke disease, chronic kidney/lung/liver disease, CF, diabetes, heart disease, disabilities, HIV, dementia, mental illness, obesity, smoking, TB, immunosuppressants"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Transmission · 2. ↓ oxygenation · 3. Progression to severe/critical"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Isolation and quarantine","lines":["Exposure = < 6 ft for ≥ 15 min total in 24 h","Not up to date → quarantine 5 days, mask, test day 5 · up to date → no quarantine, test day 5","Isolation (symptoms or positive, any vaccine status): 5 days, mask, no travel","Ends after 5 days if fever-free 24 h without antipyretics; ≥ 10 days if very sick/immunocompromised"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Protecting everyone else","lines":["Inpatient: mask, single room + own bathroom, door closed; AIIR for aerosol procedures","Staff: N95, goggles/face shield (glasses not enough), gown, gloves","MDI + spacer, not nebulizer","Don: hand hygiene → gown → respirator → shield → gloves over cuffs","Doff: gloves → gown (in room) → exit → goggles/shield → respirator outside → wash hands","Home: own room/bathroom, 6 ft, masks, few (vaccinated) caregivers, no shared dishes/bedding, disinfect surfaces"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Care","lines":["Acetaminophen preferred (NSAIDs OK)","Rest + mobility; reposition, walk, self-proning helps breathing","Cough + deep breathe ≥ q2 h; dextromethorphan/benzonatate; fluids","Needs O₂ → usually hospitalized"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Progression and specific therapy","lines":["Dyspnea at rest / increasing = progression; can reach ARDS within 2 days","Symptomatic/at-risk: nirmatrelvir-ritonavir (Paxlovid) preferred; alternatives remdesivir (3 days IV), bebtelovimab; convalescent plasma","Asthma/COPD: don't blame their disease for a change","Report chest pain, edema, hemoptysis, orthopnea → PE, HF, pericarditis"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Duration and after","lines":["Acute: up to 4 weeks","Post-COVID condition: symptoms ≥ 2 months (fatigue, dyspnea, cough, taste/smell, brain fog, anxiety, depression), sometimes > 1 yr","Goals: no transmission, SpO₂ ≥ 95%, no progression, back to baseline"]},{"note":"25-covid-19-the-infection-concept-exemplar","title":"Why","lines":["Doffing: most contaminated first (gloves, gown) in the room; respirator last, outside — it protects your airway while the air is contaminated","Nebulizer aerosolizes virus → MDI + spacer"]}]},{"id":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Rhinosinusitis, peritonsillar abscess, anthrax and endemic infection","blocks":[{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Rhinosinusitis","lines":["Inflamed sinus mucosa, usually with a cold","Blocked drainage: deviated septum, polyps, pollutants, cocaine, allergies, trauma, dental infection → trapped secretions get infected","Mostly viral; maxillary + frontal","Complications: cellulitis, abscess, meningitis"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Recognising and treating it","lines":["Cheek pain → teeth, sinus tenderness, pain to temple/back of head, worse bending forward","Bacterial: purulent drainage, postnasal drip, fever, swelling, dental + ear pain","Decongestants, antihistamines, nasal steroid, analgesics; antibiotics if bacterial","Humidify, nasal irrigation, moist heat, rest, fluids, HOB up, no smoke","Finish antibiotics even when better"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"First-generation antihistamines in older adults","lines":["↓ clearance → confusion, dry mouth, constipation (anticholinergic)","Avoid chlorpheniramine, diphenhydramine, hydroxyzine; no self-medicating"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Peritonsillar abscess","lines":["Complication of bacterial tonsillitis → can obstruct the airway","One-sided swelling, uvula pushed to the other side, pain to ear/teeth, muffled \"hot potato\" voice, fever, dysphagia, trismus, bad breath, nodes","Antibiotics ± steroids ± drainage","Drooling or stridor → ED now"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Inhalation anthrax","lines":["Bacillus anthracis spores in soil; inhaled = ~100% fatal untreated","Occupational: vets, farmers, wool/hides workers → no occupational risk = possible bioterrorism → report now","Spores → toxins → edema, hemorrhage, lung destruction → bacteremia, sepsis, meningitis; toxins kill"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Anthrax's two stages","lines":["Symptoms up to 8 weeks after exposure","Prodromal: low fever, fatigue, mild chest pain, dry cough, no runny nose or sore throat; wide mediastinum on x-ray; antibiotics now → likely survival","Feels better 2–4 days, then fulminant: sudden respiratory distress, stridor, cyanosis, high fever, hemorrhagic mediastinitis, shock, meningitis → death in 24–36 h even with antibiotics","Treat: ciprofloxacin, doxycycline, levofloxacin, moxifloxacin, clindamycin + antitoxins (obiltoxaximab, raxibacumab)"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Endemic and geographic infection","lines":["Common in one region (mostly spore-forming fungi); locals partly immune","Hantavirus: rodent urine/droppings, SW US → supportive, 38% fatal","Aspergillosis: mold · Blastomycosis: Great Lakes, Ohio/Mississippi valleys","Coccidioidomycosis: SW/far west US · Cryptococcosis: Pacific coast trees","Histoplasmosis: bird + bat droppings, central/eastern US"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Why they are missed, and how they are treated","lines":["Look like flu/pneumonia → misdiagnosed; not contagious person to person","Exposure: digging, farming, construction, dust storms, floods, demolition","Always ask about travel to endemic areas","Worse in older, pregnant, immunocompromised","Oral antifungals weeks–months; severe → IV amphotericin then oral"]},{"note":"25-rhinosinusitis-peritonsillar-abscess-ant","title":"Why","lines":["Anthrax lesson generalizes: better, then sudden setback = danger → seek care if breathlessness starts","Geography is diagnostic data → always ask travel/exposure"]}]},{"id":"25-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Exposure history goes first → the only assessment that protects other people","Travel question at the door decides seat, mask, precautions"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Why#2","lines":["Sepsis ranks 3rd but kills","Gas exchange + secretions = act on continuously; sepsis = screen relentlessly (tool + SpO₂ with every VS)"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Why#3","lines":["\"Does not transmit\" is measured in other people's charts (contacts tested, staff uninfected)","Achieved by nursing: precautions, technique, teaching, timing"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Why#4","lines":["Prevention is nurse-owned: oral care, ambulation, HOB 30°, hand hygiene, aspiration screening, spirometry","VAP/HAP are prevented by the bundle"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Why#5","lines":["Tell patients weeks of fatigue are expected — and exactly which findings aren't","Prevents despair, dropped follow-up, and missed relapse"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"¶ Every plan here carries two aims","lines":["Two aims, always together: restore gas exchange in this client","and stop the organism reaching anyone else — you, family, the next client"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Exposure history — ask first","lines":["Travel (last 14 days); time in high-TB countries; country of origin","Prior TB tests, BCG","Sick household; hospital or long-term care in the last 30 days","Endemic regions; animal hides/wool/bone meal (anthrax)","Vaccines: flu, pneumococcal, COVID-19","Pneumonia risks: swallowing problems, NG tube, alcohol, drugs, home equipment cleaning"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Gas exchange and effort","lines":["Look: flushed, anxious, tripod, broken sentences","Rate, pattern, accessory muscles; sputum amount, color, consistency, odor","Consolidation: crackles, bronchial sounds, ↑ fremitus, dull percussion, unequal expansion","SpO₂ with every set of vitals","COVID-19: ≤ 94% on room air → in-person evaluation; a normal number never dismisses worsening dyspnea"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"The atypical presentations","lines":["Older adults: weakness, falls, acute confusion (most common), poor appetite — often no fever, no cough, normal WBC, but hypoxemic","Sepsis: fast weak pulse, hypotension, orthostatic change — use the screening tool","TB: weeks–months of fatigue, weight loss, night sweats, low fever, blood-streaked sputum","Anthrax: no URI symptoms; improves 2–4 days, then collapses","Aspiration is often silent"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Diagnostics and psychosocial","lines":["Sputum Gram stain + culture (trap during suction if needed); CBC, blood cultures, ABGs","BUN + sodium for dehydration; lactate for sepsis","Chest x-ray can lag symptoms by ≥ 2 days","RT-PCR for flu/COVID; TST read at 48–72 h, IGRA, sputum culture","Dyspnea → anxiety; keep interviews short; PPE makes you hard to hear → isolation; TB stigma"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Independent","lines":["Cough + deep breathe ≥ q2h; incentive spirometer 5–10 breaths hourly while awake, hold 2–4 s","Upright; fluids ≥ 2 L/day, watch I&O and membranes","Oral care + walking prevent hospital-acquired pneumonia","HOB ≥ 30°, aspiration screening; skin under O₂ devices","COVID-19: self-proning; sepsis screen + SpO₂ every vitals"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Dependent","lines":["O₂ by cannula or mask","Antibiotics for every non-viral pneumonia: 5–7 days uncomplicated CAP, up to 21 days HAP; first ED dose within 4 h","Bronchodilators, steroids, guaifenesin","Oseltamivir within 24–48 h of flu onset; nirmatrelvir-ritonavir or remdesivir for at-risk COVID-19","TB: four drugs for 6–9 months (or 4-month regimen); bedaquiline + ECG for MDR TB","Fungal: oral azoles or IV amphotericin; anthrax: antitoxin + antibiotics"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Infection control","lines":["TB: Airborne, negative pressure, N95 for every worker; home: no precautions but test the household; 3 negative cultures ends infectiousness","COVID-19: single room, door closed, client masked, AIIR for aerosol procedures, N95 + eye protection + gown + gloves; MDI with spacer, not nebulizer","Doffing: gloves → gown → exit → eye protection → respirator outside → hand hygiene","Unknown organism: Airborne + Droplet + Contact until known","Cough into the sleeve, bag tissues, mask in crowds"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Teaching and collaborative","lines":["Finish the whole antibiotic/TB course","INH: empty stomach + B vitamins · rifampin: orange fluids, birth-control failure · PZA: uric acid, sun · ethambutol: vision · no alcohol with any","Directly observed therapy (DOT/VDOT)","Vaccines after recovery; isolation timing; warn about setback after improvement","Team: dietitian, pharmacist, social work, community health worker, RT, case management, public health for contacts"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"¶ Gas exchange is adequate with SaO₂","lines":["SaO₂ ≥ 95% or the client’s usual; clear breath sounds","No fever, normal WBC; no one else infected","Back to pre-illness health","TB: 3 negative sputum cultures (usually after ~3 months), all contacts tested"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Pneumonia .","lines":["1. Gas exchange (↓ diffusion) · 2. Airway obstruction (secretions, fatigue, weakness) · 3. Sepsis (vascular area + low immunity)"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Tuberculosis.","lines":["Airborne transmission · gas exchange · nutrition/weight loss","Non-adherence to 6–9 months → drug resistance in patient + community"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Unmet looks like:","lines":["New confusion in an older adult","Rapid weak pulse, hypotension, ↑ lactate = sepsis","COVID: dyspnea at rest/worsening (ARDS within 2 days)","Chest pain, edema, hemoptysis, orthopnea → PE, HF, pericarditis","Setback after improvement; persistent fever/cough","Drooling/stridor (peritonsillar abscess); vision or liver signs on TB drugs; 30-day readmission"]},{"note":"25-adpie","title":"Set expectations so recovery is not read as failure:","lines":["Pneumonia: fatigue, weakness, cough for weeks","Flu fatigue 1–2 weeks · acute COVID up to 4 weeks · post-COVID ≥ 2 months","Tell them the course + that full recovery happens"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"pneumonia:case","kind":"choice","scene":"Which client with a respiratory infection should the nurse see first?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Instability and change in mental status first.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Pneumonia, T 38.2°C, SpO₂ 93% on room air","correct":false,"why":"Stable."},{"id":"o1","text":"TB, asking when the mask can come off","correct":false,"why":"Teaching need."},{"id":"o2","text":"Pneumonia, new confusion, RR 30, BP 88/54","correct":true,"why":"Correct. Signs of sepsis and hypoperfusion."},{"id":"o3","text":"Influenza, myalgia 6/10","correct":false,"why":"Comfort need."}]},{"id":"pneumonia:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why immunity is the whole prevention story?","answer":"Viral strains change a little every year → old antibodies partly work → several colds/yr, annual flu shot; Pandemic = organism with no human immunity (jumped from birds/animals): 1918 flu, 2009 H1N1, COVID-19","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"pneumonia:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about ¶ on exam why the findings are?","answer":"Fluid conducts sound better than air; Pneumonia: ↑ fremitus, dull percussion, bronchial sounds, crackles; Emphysema: ↓ fremitus, hyperresonance; Same tools, opposite findings — one lung too full, the other too empty","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Tuberculosis: airborne precautions and a complete drug plan","cards":[{"title":"Why","text":"Adherence is a clinical intervention · 4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early · Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team"},{"title":"What happens in the lung","text":"Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis · Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve · Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test · Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge"},{"title":"Latent, active, secondary","text":"Latent: no symptoms for years–decades, not contagious · Few ever get active TB · Secondary = reactivation when immunity falls (older, chronic disease, HIV)"}],"caption":"Connect the mechanism with the findings, actions and reassessment. Open the chapter detail below for the full authored notes."},"strategy":{"title":"Know the drug-specific countermeasure.","task":"A client taking isoniazid reports numbness in the feet. Which prescription should the nurse expect?","cue":"INH interferes with B6 metabolism needed for nerve function.","rule":"Know the drug-specific countermeasure.","trap":"A related fact can still answer a different task. Check the actual instructions and patient data."},"id":"tuberculosis","ch":"25","chapters":["25"],"title":"Tuberculosis: airborne precautions and a complete drug plan","notes":["25-tuberculosis"],"book":"Exam 2 · Ignatavicius chapter 25; existing authored chapter notes and question bank.","why":["Why: Adherence is a clinical intervention; 4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early; Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team","What happens in the lung: Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis; Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve; Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test; Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge","Latent, active, secondary: Latent: no symptoms for years–decades, not contagious; Few ever get active TB; Secondary = reactivation when immunity falls (older, chronic disease, HIV)"],"terms":["tuberculosis","isoniazid","rifampin"],"termDefinitions":{},"patient":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Tuberculosis","blocks":[{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"The organism and the route","lines":["Mycobacterium tuberculosis, airborne (cough, laugh, sneeze, sing)","Was #1 single-agent infectious killer before COVID; 95% in resource-limited countries (Africa, India, SE Asia)"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"What happens in the lung","lines":["Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis","Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve","Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test","Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Latent, active, secondary","lines":["Latent: no symptoms for years–decades, not contagious","Few ever get active TB","Secondary = reactivation when immunity falls (older, chronic disease, HIV)"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Presentation","lines":["Slow onset; detection depends on what they report","Fatigue, lethargy, nausea, anorexia, weight loss, low-grade fever, night sweats","Cough with mucopurulent, blood-streaked sputum; chest tightness, dull pain; weeks–months","Exam: dull percussion, bronchial sounds, crackles, ↑ whispered sounds, local wheeze"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Skin testing","lines":["Mantoux (TST): PPD intradermal, read at 48–72 h","Measure induration, not redness: ≥ 10 mm = exposure; ≥ 5 mm if immunocompromised","Positive = exposure/dormant, not active disease","BCG → positive for up to 10 yr","Negative doesn't rule out in very old/immunocompromised (anergy)"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Blood, sputum, x-ray","lines":["IGRA (QuantiFERON, T-SPOT): infection, but can't tell latent from active","Xpert MTB/RIF detects resistance","Sputum culture confirms + tracks treatment: up to 4 weeks, repeat ~q4 wk, usually negative after 3 months","CXR can't tell active from inactive","Screen yearly: shelters, prisons; regularly: HIV, communal living, drug use"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Drug therapy","lines":["Combination therapy kills fast + limits resistance","Standard 6–9 months: isoniazid, rifampin, pyrazinamide, ethambutol (RIPE)","4-month option: isoniazid, rifapentine, moxifloxacin, pyrazinamide","Nausea → take at night, antiemetic, small carb snack if allowed"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"The four drugs, one teaching point each","lines":["Isoniazid: empty stomach (1 h before/2 h after meals) + B-complex vitamin","Rifampin: orange-red urine/secretions, stains soft contacts; ↓ birth control → backup during + 1 month after; many interactions","Pyrazinamide: ↑ uric acid → gout; ≥ 8 oz water; photosensitivity","Ethambutol: report any vision change (optic neuritis → blindness); ↑ uric acid; alcohol → severe N/V","All 4 = liver toxic → no alcohol whole course; report dark urine, jaundice, bruising"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Resistance","lines":["MDR = resists isoniazid + rifampin · XDR = + second-line (fluoroquinolones, aminoglycoside)","Main cause: misuse of drug therapy; caught from a resistant case = resistant from the start; AIDS ↑ risk","Higher doses, longer","Bedaquiline (MDR): QT prolongation → ECGs + lytes","XDR: absolute adherence + DOT"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Infection control","lines":["Hospital: Airborne Precautions, ventilated room (≥ 6 air exchanges), personal respirator for staff","Home: no airborne precautions (already exposed) but test every household member","Tissues, bag them, mask in crowds","3 consecutive negative sputum cultures = not infectious","DOT (in person or video) → more cures, fewer relapses, less resistance"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"The person","lines":["Frightening, isolating diagnosis → explain disease, hygiene, testing contacts","DOT = support, not surveillance","Team: dietitian, pharmacist, social worker, navigator, CHW; delay discharge if unsafe","Positive outlook + clear: stopping drugs → resistant infection","Culturally sensitive screening ↑ participation"]},{"note":"25-tuberculosis","title":"Why","lines":["Adherence is a clinical intervention","4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early","Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team"]}]}],"clinicalUpdate":"","sources":[],"steps":[{"id":"tuberculosis:case","kind":"choice","scene":"A client taking isoniazid reports numbness in the feet. Which prescription should the nurse expect?","ex":[],"task":"Which response fits?","hint":"Know the drug-specific countermeasure.","options":[{"id":"o0","text":"Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)","correct":true,"why":"Correct. INH depletes B6 causing peripheral neuropathy."},{"id":"o1","text":"Vitamin K","correct":false,"why":"Not relevant."},{"id":"o2","text":"Increase isoniazid","correct":false,"why":"Worsens toxicity."},{"id":"o3","text":"Stop all TB drugs","correct":false,"why":"Not without provider order; therapy continues."}]},{"id":"tuberculosis:r0","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about why?","answer":"Adherence is a clinical intervention; 4 drugs × 6–9 months with side effects, and they feel better early; Stopping early → resistant organism that spreads → hence DOT + whole team","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]},{"id":"tuberculosis:r1","kind":"reflect","scene":"Explain the idea before revealing the worked response.","task":"What do you remember about what happens in the lung?","answer":"Multiplies in bronchi/alveoli → pneumonitis; Immunity controls it in most → lesions resolve; Cell-mediated immunity 2–10 weeks → positive skin test; Primary often not on x-ray; upper lobes; nodes enlarge","hint":"Retrieve the explanation first, then compare it honestly with the worked response.","options":[{"id":"covered","text":"I covered the key idea before revealing","correct":true,"why":"Self-check recorded for this rehearsal. The independent checkpoint comes later."},{"id":"again","text":"I need another look","correct":false,"why":"Read the explanation, hide it, and try to retrieve it again."}]}],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Three problems need three different checks","cards":[{"title":"Volume loss","text":"Osmotic diuresis and vomiting reduce circulating volume. Begin prescribed isotonic fluids and assess perfusion."},{"title":"Potassium safety","text":"Serum K⁺ can mask depleted stores. If K⁺ <3.5 mmol/L, replace potassium and delay insulin until K⁺ >3.5 under the adult crisis protocol."},{"title":"Ketones and acidosis","text":"Insulin treats ketone production after potassium is safe. Add dextrose as directed when glucose falls; glucose alone does not establish resolution."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"An adult with DKA has received initial fluids. K⁺ is 3.2 mmol/L; an insulin infusion is prescribed.","task":"Which safety issue changes the immediate sequence?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Replace potassium and delay insulin under the crisis protocol.","why":"Insulin shifts potassium into cells and could worsen this already low serum potassium.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Start insulin immediately because the glucose is high.","why":"The glucose does not override the potassium hazard.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Stop monitoring potassium once fluids begin.","why":"Potassium can fall during treatment and needs continued monitoring.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate the glucose problem from the electrolyte risk.","id":"dka:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"During DKA treatment, glucose is 230 mg/dL, but ketones remain elevated and acidosis persists.","task":"Why may prescribed dextrose be added?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"To prove insulin is no longer necessary.","why":"Dextrose can support continued insulin; it is not proof of resolution.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Because a glucose of 230 mg/dL establishes hypoglycemia.","why":"That value is not hypoglycemia.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"To permit continued insulin while ketones and acidosis resolve.","why":"Glucose can improve before ketoacidosis resolves.","correct":true}],"hint":"Ask what insulin still needs to treat.","id":"dka:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The client’s glucose has improved and the team is considering transition from IV treatment.","task":"Which information is still essential?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Only whether the client feels hungry.","why":"Appetite does not replace biochemical and clinical assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Ketones, pH/bicarbonate, clinical recovery and the prescribed insulin transition plan.","why":"Resolution and safe insulin overlap require more than a glucose value.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only the latest glucose.","why":"A single glucose result cannot establish resolution.","correct":false}],"hint":"Evaluation should match the original problem, not one improved number.","id":"dka:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Do not let the highest number hide the safety constraint","task":"Identify whether the question asks for treatment sequence or evidence of resolution.","cue":"K⁺ 3.2 changes insulin safety; persistent ketones change the meaning of an improved glucose.","rule":"Use the limiting safety finding before selecting the next treatment.","trap":"“Glucose improved” is a tempting but incomplete end point."},"terms":["ketoacidosis","hypokalemia","dextrose"],"sources":[{"title":"2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00125-024-06183-8"}],"id":"dka","ch":"56","guide":"56:2","title":"DKA: fluids, potassium, then insulin","notes":["56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie","56-overview","56-adpie"],"book":"Ch 56: DKA and HHS, Table 56.9; safety update: 2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus.","why":["Too little effective insulin permits fat breakdown and ketone production. Ketones cause metabolic acidosis; glucose in urine pulls water and electrolytes out. Vomiting adds losses.","A serum potassium that looks normal or high can hide depleted body stores. Insulin moves potassium into cells, so treatment can make the serum value fall rapidly.","Restore perfusion with prescribed isotonic fluids, assess potassium and renal status, and monitor. Current adult consensus: if K⁺ is below 3.5 mmol/L, replace it and delay insulin until above 3.5. When glucose falls below 250 mg/dL, dextrose can allow insulin to continue until ketoacidosis resolves. Glucose improvement alone does not prove resolution."],"caseId":"dka","sourceSections":[{"id":"56-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"56-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["No insulin (type 1) or resistance → beta cells wear out (type 2)","Glucose first, then fat + protein metabolism","Two timescales: minutes–days (hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS) and years (vessels: heart, brain, eyes, kidneys, nerves, feet)","Job: glucose in range, catch emergencies early, teach the self-manager"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Insulin — the hormone of plenty","lines":["Glucose ↑ → beta cells release insulin → binds receptors → glucose enters cells","Liver: stores glycogen, stops glycogen breakdown, stops gluconeogenesis + ketogenesis","Basal (steady) + prandial burst within 10 min of eating"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Glucagon and the balancing hormones","lines":["Glucagon = hormone of starvation → liver releases glucose","Also raise glucose: epinephrine, norepinephrine, GH, cortisol","No liver glucose left → fat + muscle protein burned","Fast ≥ 8 h / NPO: insulin ↓ → glycogenolysis + gluconeogenesis prevent a low","Glycogenesis, lipogenesis store fuel · ketogenesis = acid fuel, not glucose"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Incretins","lines":["Food → gut releases GLP-1 → ↑ insulin, ↓ glucagon, slower stomach emptying","Drugs copy (GLP-1 agonists) or prolong (DPP-4 inhibitors) them"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Without insulin (Box 56.2)","lines":["↓ glycogen storage · ↑ glycogen breakdown, ↑ gluconeogenesis","↑ lipolysis, ↑ ketogenesis, ↑ protein breakdown","Body \"starves\" while glucose piles up"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"The three Ps","lines":["Glucose in urine pulls water → polyuria (+ electrolytes lost)","Dehydration → polydipsia","Starving cells → polyphagia, still starving"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Ketones, acid and Kussmaul breathing","lines":["Fat burned → ketones (acids); dehydration adds lactic acid","Anion-gap metabolic acidosis","Kussmaul (deep, fast) blows off CO₂; fruity acetone breath","ABG: ↓ pH, ↓ HCO₃⁻, ↓ PaCO₂ (compensation)"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Potassium can be low, normal or high","lines":["Urine losses → body K⁺ depleted","Acidosis shifts K⁺ out of cells → serum may look high/normal","Insulin + fluids + fixing acidosis → K⁺ back into cells → serum falls fast"]},{"note":"56-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Starvation in the middle of plenty: glucose everywhere except inside cells","Body burns fat (ketones), muscle (weight loss, ↑ BUN), releases more glucose","Insulin ends it — and K⁺ follows glucose into cells"]}]},{"id":"56-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"56-adpie","title":"¶ Diabetes care runs on two clocks at","lines":["Fast clock: hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS → minutes + right first step","Slow clock: eyes, kidneys, nerves, feet, heart → daily habits","Plan covers both; patient runs the slow one"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"History","lines":["Family history, weight change, 3 Ps, fatigue","Yeast infections, slow healing, vision/touch changes","Big babies / GDM; steroids, alcohol, smoking","Regimen, meals, activity, past lows/highs, cost"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Physical","lines":["VS + orthostatics, weight, waist","Skin + injection sites","Feet: pulses, monofilament, skin, nails, deformity","Hydration, Kussmaul / fruity breath, mental status"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Labs","lines":["Glucose, A1C, ketones","K⁺, pH, HCO₃⁻, anion gap, osmolarity","BUN/creatinine, urine albumin, lipids; ECG with K⁺ shifts"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Slow healing + yeast infections are often the first signs of type 2","Patients rarely link them to blood sugar → ask on purpose"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["1. Injury from hyperglycemia","2. Surgical complications","3. Injury from neuropathy (feet)","4. Kidney disease","5. Acute emergencies: hypoglycemia, DKA, HHS"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Prioritizing in the moment","lines":["Glucose < 70 → treat first","DKA/HHS: airway → fluids → insulin → K⁺","Unexpected rise → infection"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Glucose in target, A1C < 7%, no significant lows","Surgery without complications · intact feet","Kidneys + UO OK · no DKA/HHS/severe lows","Meets Box 56.15 criteria"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Glucose control","lines":["Check ≤ 30 min before meals; rapid insulin just before eating","Technique, rotation, storage","NPO → basal, hold meal insulin","Diet plan; exercise rules; sick days; alcohol with food"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Emergencies","lines":["Low: 15-15, glucagon/IV dextrose if can't swallow, recheck q15","DKA: fluids 15–20 mL/kg, IV insulin, D5½NS at 250, K⁺ < 5.0 if UO ≥ 30","HHS: fluids first, hourly neuro, insulin after, lytes q1–2h"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Surgery","lines":["Sulfonylureas −1 day, metformin held per order (book: ≥ 24 h)","Pre-op < 200; intra-op 140–180 hourly","Post-op ECG, K⁺, I&O, 200 g carb/day liquids, PCA"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Feet, kidneys and teaching","lines":["Monofilament yearly; daily foot checks; shoes + white socks; offload ulcers","Yearly albumin, BP < 140/80, ACEI/ARB + K⁺","Avoid nephrotoxins, hydrate for contrast","Teach-back, survival skills first"]},{"note":"56-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Target glucose/A1C without frequent lows","DKA resolved: 18, pH > 7.30, gap < 12","HHS: steady neuro improvement","Feet intact, albumin stable","Patient demonstrates injection, testing, treating a low, sick days, foot care"]}]}]},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Compare the baseline with a new change","cards":[{"title":"Assessment considerations","text":"Slower motility, medication effects, intake and activity can contribute to constipation. Review the person’s baseline."},{"title":"Changes needing follow-up","text":"New bleeding, severe pain, persistent vomiting or a new bowel-pattern change warrant assessment rather than attribution to age."},{"title":"Medication handling","text":"Reduced hepatic metabolism can increase exposure to some medicines. Review doses, effects and adverse findings."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older adult reports a new black, tarry stool and weakness.","task":"Which interpretation is safest?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Assess possible bleeding and the patient’s stability promptly.","why":"A new bleeding pattern must be investigated.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Reassure the patient that black stool is an expected age change.","why":"Age does not establish the cause of new black stool.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Ignore the symptom until a constipation diary is complete.","why":"A diary does not address possible bleeding and instability.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate a new warning sign from a common age-associated pattern.","id":"gi-aging:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older adult becomes unusually drowsy after a medication that is metabolized by the liver.","task":"What should guide the nursing response?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Give an extra dose because older adults clear all medicines faster.","why":"Clearance is not uniformly faster with age.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Treat the drowsiness as a normal requirement of aging.","why":"A new functional change should not be dismissed.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Assess the change and review the medicine and possible accumulation with the team.","why":"Medication handling and new sedation deserve assessment.","correct":true}],"hint":"Connect a mechanism to a new clinical finding.","id":"gi-aging:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Constipation developed after reduced mobility and a medication change.","task":"Which approach best fits the assessment?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Prescribe unrestricted fluids despite an existing fluid restriction.","why":"Prevention must respect the individual treatment plan.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Review intake, activity, medicines and the prescribed fluid plan.","why":"Several modifiable factors can contribute.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume age is the only cause.","why":"The history supplies other plausible contributors.","correct":false}],"hint":"Choose an assessment that uses the actual history.","id":"gi-aging:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Expected pattern versus unexpected change","task":"Look for a new finding or a change from baseline.","cue":"A new tarry stool is different from a gradual motility change.","rule":"Assess the specific finding and stability; age alone does not explain it.","trap":"A familiar age-related fact can distract from the new problem."},"terms":["motility","atrophy","melena"],"sources":[{"title":"MedlinePlus: aging changes in hormone production","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/004000.htm"}],"id":"gi-aging","ch":"45","guide":"45:0","title":"An aging gut: what changes, what needs assessment","notes":["45-gi-changes-with-aging"],"book":"Ch 45: GI changes associated with aging.","why":["The chapter connects aging with reduced GI motility and a weaker defecation signal. Intake, activity, medications and access to a toilet also influence constipation. Compare with the person’s baseline.","Reduced hepatic metabolism can allow some drugs to accumulate. Atrophic gastric changes and reduced acid can affect nutrient handling, including iron and vitamin B₁₂. These patterns guide assessment; they do not explain every new symptom.","Assess new bleeding, severe pain, persistent vomiting or a new bowel-pattern change. Review nutrition, hydration and medications rather than dismissing the problem as normal aging."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"quadrants","title":"Locate structures from the patient’s perspective","cards":[{"title":"RUQ","text":"Most of the liver, gallbladder, duodenum and pancreatic head."},{"title":"LUQ","text":"Stomach, spleen and pancreatic body/tail."},{"title":"RLQ","text":"Cecum and appendix."},{"title":"LLQ","text":"Descending and sigmoid colon."}],"caption":"Front view: the patient’s right is on your left. Organs can span regions; location supports assessment rather than proving a diagnosis."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient points to the right upper abdomen.","task":"Which organ pair belongs most closely to this area?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Liver and gallbladder.","why":"Most of the liver and the gallbladder are associated with the RUQ.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Spleen and stomach.","why":"These are mainly LUQ structures.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Appendix and cecum.","why":"These are RLQ structures.","correct":false}],"hint":"Name the patient’s side before selecting the quadrant.","id":"quadrants:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After abdominal trauma, a patient has left upper abdominal pain.","task":"Which structure is relevant to the location?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Appendix.","why":"The appendix is mainly in the RLQ.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Sigmoid colon.","why":"The sigmoid colon is mainly in the LLQ.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Spleen.","why":"The spleen is in the LUQ; assess the mechanism and stability as well.","correct":true}],"hint":"Locate first, then interpret the whole clinical picture.","id":"quadrants:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient indicates midline suprapubic discomfort with a distended bladder.","task":"What does this show about quadrant questions?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Location alone confirms a diagnosis.","why":"Additional findings are needed.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Some structures are midline or span regions; use the full assessment.","why":"A quadrant map is an aid rather than a diagnostic rule.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Every organ fits entirely in exactly one quadrant.","why":"Organs and symptoms do not obey that absolute rule.","correct":false}],"hint":"Check whether the answer makes an unjustified absolute claim.","id":"quadrants:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Translate orientation before recalling anatomy","task":"Identify the patient’s right/left and upper/lower location.","cue":"Frontal diagrams reverse your viewing side relative to the patient.","rule":"Match the named structure to the patient’s quadrant, then consider the rest of the scenario.","trap":"Do not confuse your right with the patient’s right or diagnose from location alone."},"terms":["duodenum","cecum","suprapubic"],"sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: anatomical terminology and regions","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/1-6-anatomical-terminology"}],"id":"quadrants","ch":"45","guide":"45:1","title":"Locate the organ before interpreting the symptom","notes":["45-gi-history-and-the-abdominal-exam","45-overview"],"book":"Ch 45: Table 45.1, abdominal quadrants and associated organs.","why":["RUQ: most of the liver, gallbladder, duodenum and pancreatic head. LUQ: stomach, spleen and pancreatic body/tail. RLQ: cecum and appendix. LLQ: descending and sigmoid colon.","The aorta and a distended bladder are midline landmarks. Some organs span regions. Location narrows an assessment; it does not establish a diagnosis. Use the full Table 45.1 notes to practise every listed structure."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Protect tissue, preserve intake, check swallowing","cards":[{"title":"Inspect and assess","text":"Look for lesions, pain, bleeding, infection concerns and impaired intake."},{"title":"Gentle care","text":"Use appropriate soft cleaning, prescribed nonirritating rinses and soft bland food within the treatment plan."},{"title":"After an oral anesthetic","text":"Reduced sensation can hide heat injury and impair protective swallowing. Follow product-specific eating precautions."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with mucositis says that mouth care is too painful to attempt.","task":"Which response supports both comfort and tissue care?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Assess pain and use prescribed comfort measures with gentle oral care.","why":"Comfort measures can make necessary gentle cleaning and intake more feasible.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Use vigorous brushing to remove all painful tissue.","why":"Traumatizing fragile mucosa can worsen injury.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Stop all oral assessment for the rest of treatment.","why":"Lesions, intake and infection risks still need monitoring.","correct":false}],"hint":"Look for care that protects fragile tissue rather than creating more injury.","id":"mouth:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The mouth and throat remain numb after a prescribed topical anesthetic. The patient requests hot soup.","task":"What is the safety check?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Use a straw to eliminate aspiration risk.","why":"A straw does not make impaired swallowing safe.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Give the hot soup because pain relief proves swallowing is safe.","why":"Pain relief does not establish protective sensation.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Follow eating precautions and assess safe swallowing before intake.","why":"Numb tissue can increase burn and aspiration risk.","correct":true}],"hint":"Distinguish comfort from airway protection.","id":"mouth:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Oral pain has improved, but the patient is eating very little.","task":"What should be evaluated next?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Whether the patient can tolerate a harsher mouthwash.","why":"More irritating care does not address the intake problem.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Actual intake, hydration, swallowing and barriers to eating.","why":"Improved pain alone does not establish an adequate nutrition outcome.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only the pain score.","why":"The goal also includes safe, sufficient intake.","correct":false}],"hint":"Match the outcome to the functional goal.","id":"mouth:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"A comfortable patient can still have a safety problem","task":"Decide whether the stem asks about comfort, tissue protection or safe intake.","cue":"Persistent numbness changes the eating decision.","rule":"Use the relevant function—swallowing or actual intake—rather than one improved symptom.","trap":"Do not treat pain relief as proof that eating is safe."},"terms":["mucositis","stomatitis","aspiration"],"sources":[{"title":"NCI: oral complications and supportive care","url":"https://www.cancer.gov/about-cancer/treatment/side-effects/mouth-throat/oral-complications-pdq"}],"id":"mouth","ch":"46","guide":"46:0","title":"Protect the mouth and keep eating safe","notes":["46-stomatitis-the-tissue-integrity-exemplar"],"book":"Ch 46: Boxes 46.1–46.2 and stomatitis nursing care.","why":["Healthy-mouth prevention includes brushing, appropriate flossing, dental follow-up, oral self-examination and tobacco avoidance. Adapt care to bleeding risk, discomfort and the treatment plan.","With mucositis, fragile tissue needs gentle cleaning: a soft brush, prescribed nonirritating rinses, pain management and soft bland foods. Inspect the mouth, assess intake and swallowing, and report lesions or infection concerns.","A topical anesthetic can remove protective sensation as well as pain. Follow the product’s directions about eating and drinking; a numb mouth or throat increases aspiration and burn risk."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Reflux explains some findings, not every new symptom","cards":[{"title":"Barrier fails","text":"An ineffective lower esophageal sphincter allows stomach contents into the esophagus."},{"title":"Reduce exposure","text":"Meal timing, individualized trigger management, head-of-bed elevation and prescribed acid suppression reduce reflux-related injury."},{"title":"Change the priority","text":"New chest pain, dysphagia, bleeding or weight loss needs assessment; a GERD history does not rule out another cause."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with known GERD develops new chest pressure with sweating.","task":"What should happen before attributing it to reflux?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Prompt assessment for a potentially serious cause, including a cardiac cause.","why":"The new presentation requires assessment rather than diagnostic anchoring.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume all chest symptoms are GERD once the diagnosis exists.","why":"A prior diagnosis does not exclude another acute problem.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Delay assessment until a food diary is completed.","why":"A diary is not the priority for this change.","correct":false}],"hint":"Do not let a familiar diagnosis replace assessment of new cues.","id":"gerd:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient eats a large meal immediately before lying down at bedtime.","task":"Which teaching targets the mechanism?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Lie flat sooner to keep acid in the stomach.","why":"Lying flat after eating can worsen reflux.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume acid medicine removes the need for any lifestyle assessment.","why":"Medication and tailored lifestyle measures can serve different roles.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Avoid meals near bedtime and use the individualized reflux plan.","why":"Meal timing and position can influence reflux exposure.","correct":true}],"hint":"Connect the behavior to reflux rather than choosing a generic healthy habit.","id":"gerd:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Heartburn has become less frequent, but swallowing is progressively difficult.","task":"Which finding drives follow-up?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"A preference for smaller meals without any swallowing problem.","why":"The actual alarm finding is worsening swallowing.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Progressive dysphagia.","why":"An alarm symptom still needs evaluation despite less heartburn.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only the reduced frequency of heartburn.","why":"One improved symptom does not cancel a new alarm finding.","correct":false}],"hint":"Prioritize the new warning sign over a reassuring trend elsewhere.","id":"gerd:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Avoid anchoring on the diagnosis in the first sentence","task":"Read the final task, then identify any new alarm finding.","cue":"New chest pressure or progressive dysphagia changes the question.","rule":"Assess the current threat; use reflux teaching when the scenario is appropriate for teaching.","trap":"A true reflux intervention may answer the wrong task."},"terms":["reflux","dysphagia","esophagitis"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: GERD treatment","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/acid-reflux-ger-gerd-adults/treatment"}],"id":"gerd","ch":"46","guide":"46:1","title":"GERD: reduce reflux and recognize warning signs","notes":["46-gerd-the-nutrition-exemplar","46-treating-gerd-diet-lifestyle-drugs-proce"],"book":"Ch 46: GERD, Box 46.6 and nonsurgical management.","why":["An ineffective lower esophageal sphincter lets stomach contents enter the esophagus. Heartburn and regurgitation are common; chronic cough or hoarseness can also occur.","Smaller meals, avoiding meals near bedtime, head-of-bed elevation and individualized trigger avoidance reduce reflux exposure. Acid suppression reduces acidity and injury. PPIs inhibit the acid pump; H₂ blockers reduce histamine-stimulated acid secretion.","Persistent injury can lead to esophagitis, stricture or Barrett changes. Dysphagia, bleeding and weight loss need follow-up. New chest pain requires assessment for a cardiac cause, even in someone with known reflux."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Movement before repair; protection after repair","cards":[{"title":"Sliding hernia","text":"The gastroesophageal junction moves above the diaphragm; reflux is common."},{"title":"Paraesophageal hernia","text":"Stomach moves beside the esophagus; obstruction or strangulation is a concern."},{"title":"After fundoplication","text":"A tighter junction can contribute to early dysphagia and gas-bloat. Follow diet orders and protect the surgical repair."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient asks why sliding and paraesophageal hernias are not the same.","task":"Which distinction is useful?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"The position of the junction and herniated stomach differs, changing potential complications.","why":"The anatomical difference helps explain reflux versus obstruction concerns.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Both names describe exactly the same anatomy.","why":"They represent different patterns.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"A paraesophageal hernia makes obstruction impossible.","why":"Obstruction and strangulation are important concerns.","correct":false}],"hint":"Identify what moved and where it moved.","id":"hernia:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After fundoplication, a patient reports difficulty belching and gas-bloat.","task":"Which teaching fits the prescribed recovery plan?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Use large carbonated drinks to force the junction open.","why":"This can worsen gas-bloat and is not a safe repair strategy.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Advance the diet independently without checking orders.","why":"Postoperative diet progression must follow the surgical plan.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Small meals, thorough chewing and avoiding carbonation as directed.","why":"The tighter junction can limit gas escape.","correct":true}],"hint":"Use the altered anatomy to explain the instruction.","id":"hernia:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A postoperative NG tube appears displaced.","task":"Which action protects the repair?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Ask the patient to pull it out and replace it later.","why":"Unplanned removal/replacement can create harm.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Contact the surgical team and follow its instructions before advancing or repositioning it.","why":"Manipulation can disrupt the operative repair.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Advance it immediately as a routine adjustment.","why":"Routine manipulation is not appropriate after this repair without direction.","correct":false}],"hint":"The recent surgery changes what would otherwise seem routine.","id":"hernia:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Let the procedure change the usual rule","task":"Identify whether the patient is before or after surgical repair.","cue":"A displaced postoperative tube is not just a routine tube-placement problem.","rule":"Choose the action that protects the repair and follows the surgical plan.","trap":"Do not generalize a routine bedside maneuver to every postoperative patient."},"terms":["hiatal hernia","fundoplication","strangulation"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: reflux treatment and fundoplication","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/acid-reflux-ger-gerd-adults/treatment"}],"id":"hernia","ch":"46","guide":"46:2","title":"Hiatal hernia and the tighter junction after surgery","notes":["46-hiatal-hernia-and-fundoplication-care"],"book":"Ch 46: hiatal hernia; Boxes 46.8–46.10.","why":["In a sliding hernia, the gastroesophageal junction moves above the diaphragm and reflux is common. In a paraesophageal hernia, stomach herniates beside the esophagus; obstruction or strangulation is a concern.","Fundoplication reinforces the junction. Swelling and the tighter wrap explain early dysphagia, difficulty belching and gas-bloat. Follow the ordered diet, use small meals, chew well and avoid carbonated drinks.","Report severe pain, persistent vomiting or worsening swallowing problems. Do not advance or reposition a postoperative NG tube without the surgeon’s direction; disturbing the repair can cause harm."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Treat the cause as well as the acid","cards":[{"title":"H. pylori","text":"Infection contributes to mucosal injury. Acid suppression alone does not eradicate it."},{"title":"NSAIDs","text":"Reduced prostaglandin-mediated protection weakens the mucosal defense. Review exposure and the prescribed protection plan."},{"title":"After eradication therapy","text":"Complete the regimen and confirm cure at the recommended interval; medication holds for testing require the clinician’s plan."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"Ulcer pain improves during acid suppression, but H. pylori treatment is incomplete.","task":"Which teaching remains essential?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Complete the prescribed eradication regimen and follow the test-of-cure plan.","why":"Symptom relief does not prove eradication.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Stop antibiotics as soon as pain improves.","why":"Incomplete treatment can leave infection unresolved.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume a PPI by itself eradicates H. pylori.","why":"Acid suppression and eradication do different jobs.","correct":false}],"hint":"Ask whether the outcome concerns symptoms or elimination of infection.","id":"pud:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient asks about testing after finishing H. pylori antibiotics.","task":"Which plan matches the current guidance?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Test the next morning while continuing all medicines without review.","why":"Timing and medication effects can compromise the result.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Never test if symptoms disappear.","why":"Symptoms alone do not establish cure.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Confirm eradication at least 4 weeks after antibiotics, with PPI/PCAB withholding for 2 weeks under clinician direction.","why":"The interval and acid-suppressant hold help avoid a misleading test result.","correct":true}],"hint":"Separate the test-of-cure clock from symptom improvement.","id":"pud:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking regular NSAIDs develops ulcer symptoms.","task":"Which history matters?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Assume NSAIDs protect the stomach from acid.","why":"NSAIDs can impair mucosal defense.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Dose, frequency and concurrent ulcer-risk medicines or prior bleeding.","why":"Medication exposure can affect mucosal protection and risk.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether symptoms occur before or after meals.","why":"Pain timing alone cannot reliably identify the cause or location.","correct":false}],"hint":"Choose the history that explains the mechanism and changes risk.","id":"pud:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Symptom relief is not the same as cure","task":"Find whether the question asks about comfort, eradication or complications.","cue":"Incomplete antibiotics or a premature test changes the response.","rule":"Select evidence that addresses the actual treatment goal.","trap":"Do not infer infection cure from an improved pain score."},"terms":["gastritis","peptic ulcer","eradication"],"sources":[{"title":"ACG 2024: H. pylori treatment and confirmation of cure","url":"https://gi.org/journals-publications/ebgi/schoenfeld_sep2024/"},{"title":"NIDDK: peptic ulcers","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/peptic-ulcers-stomach-ulcers"}],"id":"pud","ch":"47","guide":"47:0","title":"Gastritis and ulcers: repair the defense and treat the cause","notes":["47-gastritis","47-peptic-ulcer-disease-the-infection-exemp","47-overview","47-adpie"],"book":"Ch 47: gastritis, PUD, Tables 47.1–47.2; ACG 2024 treatment update.","why":["Gastritis is inflammation of the stomach lining; an ulcer is deeper tissue injury. H. pylori and NSAIDs weaken mucosal defense by different routes. Assess medication use, alcohol, symptoms, bleeding and barriers to treatment.","Acid suppression supports healing but does not eradicate H. pylori. The current ACG update recommends 14-day optimized bismuth quadruple therapy as a principal empiric regimen when appropriate; clarithromycin triple therapy requires demonstrated susceptibility.","Teach completion and confirmation of eradication. The test of cure is at least 4 weeks after antibiotics, with PPIs/PCABs withheld for 2 weeks under the clinician’s plan. Pain timing can suggest a pattern but cannot reliably locate an ulcer by itself."],"caseId":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"47-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"47-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Stomach is protected by a mucosal barrier kept up by prostaglandins","Break it (NSAIDs, alcohol, smoking, H. pylori) → gastritis (inflamed) or PUD (eroded; infection exemplar)","Emergencies: upper GI bleeding, perforation/peritonitis, outlet obstruction","Then gastric cancer, gastrectomy and dumping syndrome"]},{"note":"47-overview","title":"The mucosal barrier","lines":["Prostaglandins keep the barrier → stomach doesn't digest itself","Barrier broken → histamine + vagus worsen it","Acid back-diffuses → edema, bleeding, erosion"]},{"note":"47-overview","title":"Why NSAIDs injure","lines":["NSAIDs block prostaglandins (COX) → barrier thins","Steroids, aldosterone antagonists, SSRIs also contribute"]},{"note":"47-overview","title":"How H. pylori survives acid","lines":["Spread oral-oral or fecal-oral","Urease → ammonia neutralizes acid around it","Spiral shape burrows under mucus, hides from immune cells","Inflammation damages the lining → ulcers, cancer"]},{"note":"47-overview","title":"Chronic gastritis and B₁₂","lines":["Chronic inflammation → atrophy","Parietal cells fail → ↓ acid + no intrinsic factor → B₁₂ not absorbed → pernicious anemia","Most common cause: H. pylori"]},{"note":"47-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Three drug jobs: lower acid (PPI, H₂, antacid) · rebuild barrier (sucralfate, misoprostol, bismuth) · remove cause (antibiotics, stop NSAIDs)","Heal without killing H. pylori → ulcer comes back"]}]},{"id":"47-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"47-adpie","title":"¶ Stomach care is about acid, blood and","lines":["Acid, blood, barrier: find what broke it (NSAIDs, H. pylori, alcohol, smoking)","Guard against bleeding + perforation; heal with the right drug at the right time","After gastrectomy: rebuild eating around no reservoir"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"History","lines":["NSAIDs, steroids, H. pylori, alcohol, tobacco, stress","Diet risks + food insecurity; past surgery","Pain vs meals/night; change in pain → perforation?","Psychosocial: effect on work/family, support, barriers to adherence (housing, money, mood)"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Physical and labs","lines":["Epigastric tenderness; rigidity, rebound","Orthostatics; vomit + stool for blood","Hgb/Hct, BUN, K⁺, Na⁺, WBC; H. pylori","Beefy tongue (B₁₂)"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["↑ BUN with normal creatinine → digested blood (protein load) + dehydration → supports upper GI bleed"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Priority problems","lines":["PUD: pain + bleeding risk","Active bleeding: hypovolemia first","Gastrectomy: dumping, B₁₂/iron/folate/Ca deficits","Cancer: grief, coping, nutrition"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Pain ≤ 3/10; H. pylori gone, ulcer healed","No bleeding (or controlled, VS normal)","After gastrectomy: no dumping, normal B₁₂/CBC"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Drugs, timed right","lines":["Finish triple/quadruple therapy","PPI 30 min before main meal; taper","Antacids 1–2 h apart; sucralfate before meals","Bismuth: no aspirin · metronidazole: no alcohol · misoprostol: no pregnancy"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Bleeding","lines":["ABCs, O₂, 2 large-bore IVs, NS/LR, blood","NG, VS, Hct, SpO₂, I&O; replace K⁺","EGD: NPO 4–6 h; after: gag reflex first","PPIs; watch for perforation"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Diet and lifestyle","lines":["Bland in flares; avoid caffeine, tomato, citrus, peppers, onions, spicy; no bedtime snacks; no alcohol/tobacco; stress care","Gastrectomy: small meals, no fluids with meals, lie down after, B₁₂"]},{"note":"47-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Pain controlled; no blood in stool/vomit","Hgb/Hct, BP, pulse stable; no orthostatics","H. pylori negative; no dumping, weight stable","States emergency signs"]}]}]},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Four drug jobs, four different precautions","cards":[{"title":"Suppress secretion","text":"PPIs inhibit the proton pump; H₂ blockers reduce histamine-stimulated secretion."},{"title":"Neutralize or protect","text":"Antacids act on acid already present; sucralfate forms a barrier and has administration/interactions to check."},{"title":"Replace a protective effect","text":"Misoprostol supports prostaglandin-mediated protection; pregnancy precautions are critical."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient says an antacid and a PPI must work by the same mechanism.","task":"Which correction is accurate?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"An antacid neutralizes existing acid; a PPI reduces acid secretion.","why":"A shared symptom benefit does not imply the same mechanism.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Both eradicate H. pylori as single agents.","why":"Neither statement describes eradication therapy.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Both form the same sucralfate barrier.","why":"The mucosal barrier is a different mechanism.","correct":false}],"hint":"Sort by mechanism rather than by the symptom treated.","id":"ulcer-drugs:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Sucralfate is scheduled with several other oral medicines.","task":"What must the nurse check?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Give every oral medicine together to avoid interactions.","why":"Co-administration can create interactions.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume timing is irrelevant for all mucosal protectants.","why":"The exact product directions matter.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Empty-stomach directions and product-specific spacing from interacting medicines and antacids.","why":"Binding and administration timing can affect treatment.","correct":true}],"hint":"A medication-timing question requires the exact drug instructions.","id":"ulcer-drugs:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient prescribed misoprostol reports a possible pregnancy.","task":"Which concern needs prompt clarification?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Assume all stomach-protection medicines are safe in pregnancy.","why":"Drug classes have different precautions.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The pregnancy contraindication/precautions and the prescriber’s plan.","why":"Misoprostol has important pregnancy risks in this ulcer-prevention context.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether the tablet is a pleasant flavor.","why":"That does not address the safety issue.","correct":false}],"hint":"A new contraindication outranks routine administration convenience.","id":"ulcer-drugs:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Translate the drug name into its job","task":"Identify whether the stem asks mechanism, administration or a contraindication.","cue":"Sucralfate suggests spacing; misoprostol plus possible pregnancy suggests safety.","rule":"Use the exact drug’s role and directions rather than a class-wide shortcut.","trap":"Medicines used for one condition are not interchangeable."},"terms":["sucralfate","misoprostol","proton pump inhibitor"],"sources":[{"title":"DailyMed: official prescribing information for the exact product","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/"}],"id":"ulcer-drugs","ch":"47","guide":"47:0","title":"Acid drugs: match the mechanism to the timing","notes":["47-ulcer-and-gastritis-drugs-table-47-1"],"book":"Ch 47: Table 47.1, ulcer and gastritis drugs.","why":["PPIs inhibit the final proton pump step in acid secretion; H₂ blockers reduce histamine-stimulated secretion. Antacids neutralize acid already present. These mechanisms are different, even when symptoms improve with each.","Sucralfate forms a protective barrier over injured tissue. It is generally given on an empty stomach and separated from interacting medications and antacids according to the prescription and product directions.","Misoprostol replaces a prostaglandin effect to protect the mucosa during NSAID use; pregnancy is a major safety concern. Use the linked drug table for adverse effects, interactions and the full prescribed regimen."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Read the perfusion pattern","cards":[{"title":"Blood loss","text":"Hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis or melena can signal bleeding. Appearance alone does not quantify stability."},{"title":"Circulation changes","text":"Tachycardia, hypotension, confusion and reduced urine output can indicate impaired perfusion. An early hemoglobin can be misleading."},{"title":"Response to treatment","text":"Assess improving perfusion and stable trends while preparing prescribed resuscitation and definitive treatment. A rigid, acutely painful abdomen raises a different emergency concern."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with hematemesis has BP 84/48, pulse 124 and new confusion.","task":"What has priority?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Activate help and assess/support airway and perfusion while preparing prescribed emergency care.","why":"These findings suggest hemodynamic instability.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Complete routine diet teaching before reassessing the pressure.","why":"Teaching is lower priority than this instability.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Wait for the next hemoglobin before escalating.","why":"An early hemoglobin may not reflect the full acute loss.","correct":false}],"hint":"Group the pressure, pulse and mental-status cues.","id":"gi-bleed:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Drainage changes from red to coffee-ground material, but pressure remains low and urine output falls.","task":"Which interpretation is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"The color change proves all bleeding has stopped.","why":"Appearance alone cannot establish that outcome.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Low urine output is unrelated to circulation.","why":"Reduced renal perfusion can reduce output.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The perfusion findings still require urgent attention.","why":"A change in drainage appearance does not establish recovery.","correct":true}],"hint":"Choose a direct outcome measure for circulation.","id":"gi-bleed:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with an ulcer develops sudden severe abdominal pain and a rigid abdomen.","task":"What new complication must be considered?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"A reason to offer a large meal before assessment.","why":"Urgent evaluation and the prescribed intake restrictions take priority.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Perforation requiring urgent evaluation.","why":"This abrupt presentation is a serious warning pattern.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Expected relief as the ulcer heals.","why":"Rigidity and sudden severe pain are not healing indicators.","correct":false}],"hint":"A new pattern can require a different emergency response.","id":"gi-bleed:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Prioritize the unstable pattern","task":"Determine whether the task is priority action or evaluation.","cue":"Low pressure plus tachycardia and altered mentation points to impaired perfusion.","rule":"Use the immediate clinical threat; reassess with outcomes that measure perfusion.","trap":"A reassuring drainage description or single lab does not outweigh unstable findings."},"terms":["hematemesis","melena","perfusion"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: treatment for GI bleeding","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/gastrointestinal-bleeding/treatment"}],"id":"gi-bleed","ch":"47","guide":"47:1","title":"Upper GI bleeding: perfusion before reassurance","notes":["47-upper-gi-bleeding-and-ulcer-emergencies"],"book":"Ch 47: upper GI bleeding, ulcer emergencies and nursing priorities.","why":["Hematemesis, coffee-ground emesis and melena suggest GI bleeding. Blood loss can reduce venous return and cardiac output; tachycardia, hypotension, confusion and reduced urine output suggest impaired perfusion.","Assess airway and hemodynamic stability, activate help for instability, keep NPO and prepare prescribed IV access, fluids, labs, blood products and endoscopic treatment. An early hemoglobin alone can be misleading during acute loss.","Sudden severe pain with a rigid abdomen suggests perforation and demands urgent evaluation. Improvement means recovering perfusion and stable trends, not simply a change from red to coffee-ground drainage."],"caseId":"ugib"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Link the operation to the later problem","cards":[{"title":"Persistent symptoms","text":"Early satiety, unexplained weight loss or anemia can require evaluation even when symptoms seem vague."},{"title":"Intrinsic factor lost","text":"After total gastrectomy, follow the lifelong vitamin B₁₂ replacement and monitoring plan. The formulation/route is individualized."},{"title":"Faster emptying","text":"Early dumping involves fluid/vasomotor effects; later dumping can involve insulin-related hypoglycemia. Tailor meal size, simple sugars and fluid timing."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient reports persistent early satiety and unexplained weight loss.","task":"Which response is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Arrange assessment rather than dismissing the symptoms as minor indigestion.","why":"Persistent symptoms and weight loss require evaluation.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Reassure the patient that vague symptoms exclude serious disease.","why":"Vagueness does not rule out disease.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Use symptom timing alone to diagnose a specific cancer stage.","why":"Evaluation, not symptom timing alone, establishes the diagnosis and stage.","correct":false}],"hint":"Persistent change and weight loss are important qualifiers.","id":"gastric-cancer:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After total gastrectomy, a patient asks why vitamin B₁₂ follow-up is needed.","task":"Which mechanism explains the plan?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"B₁₂ follow-up ends once the incision heals.","why":"The absorption change outlasts wound healing.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Every patient must use the same replacement route without individualized review.","why":"The replacement regimen is individualized; the ongoing need is the key point.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Loss of intrinsic-factor production changes B₁₂ absorption and requires ongoing replacement planning.","why":"The operation can remove cells necessary for normal intrinsic-factor-mediated absorption.","correct":true}],"hint":"Connect the removed function to the long-term requirement.","id":"gastric-cancer:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After stomach surgery, cramping and dizziness occur shortly after a large sugary meal.","task":"Which teaching targets dumping risk?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Assume all symptoms occurring hours later have the same mechanism as early dumping.","why":"Later insulin-related hypoglycemia differs from early fluid shifts.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Follow the individualized plan for smaller meals, limited simple sugars and separating fluids from meals.","why":"These measures can reduce rapid-emptying-related symptoms.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Use larger sugary meals to prevent every episode.","why":"This can aggravate dumping.","correct":false}],"hint":"Use the time after eating to distinguish mechanisms, not to dismiss symptoms.","id":"gastric-cancer:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Match the time course and the missing function","task":"Ask whether the problem is cancer evaluation, nutrient replacement or dumping.","cue":"Total gastrectomy suggests loss of intrinsic factor; meal timing helps separate dumping patterns.","rule":"Choose the explanation that fits the operation and timing.","trap":"Do not replace a long-term absorption issue with a short-term wound-care answer."},"terms":["gastrectomy","intrinsic factor","hypoglycemia"],"sources":[{"title":"NCI: nutrition after gastrectomy","url":"https://ccr.cancer.gov/surgical-oncology-program/clinical-team/diet-and-nutrition-after-gastrectomy-what-you-should-know"},{"title":"NIDDK: dumping syndrome treatment","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/digestive-diseases/dumping-syndrome/treatment"}],"id":"gastric-cancer","ch":"47","guide":"47:1","title":"Gastric cancer and life after gastrectomy","notes":["47-gastric-cancer-and-life-after-gastrectom"],"book":"Ch 47: gastric cancer, gastrectomy, dumping and discharge teaching.","why":["Persistent indigestion, early satiety, weight loss or anemia warrant assessment; early symptoms can be vague. H. pylori and dietary exposures are among the chapter’s risk factors. Support nutrition and access to evaluation.","After gastrectomy, assess for bleeding, anastomotic complications, intake problems and altered nutrient absorption. Loss of intrinsic-factor production after total gastrectomy requires a lifelong vitamin B₁₂ replacement plan.","Rapid emptying can cause early dumping: fluid shifts bring cramping, diarrhea and vasomotor symptoms soon after eating. Later, an insulin response may cause hypoglycemia. Small frequent meals, limiting simple sugars and separating fluids from meals are part of the individualized plan."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Three hormone controls to keep separate","cards":[{"title":"Pancreas","text":"Insulin favors uptake/storage and lowers liver glucose output. Glucagon helps the liver release glucose."},{"title":"Thyroid feedback","text":"Pituitary TSH stimulates T₃/T₄. Rising thyroid hormone normally feeds back to reduce TSH."},{"title":"Parathyroids","text":"PTH helps raise serum calcium. Low calcium can increase neuromuscular excitability."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A fasting patient asks what helps maintain circulating glucose.","task":"Which hormone action fits?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Glucagon promotes hepatic glucose release.","why":"Glucagon supports glucose availability when intake is low.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Insulin normally increases hepatic glucose output during fasting.","why":"Insulin generally suppresses hepatic output.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"TSH directly supplies dietary glucose.","why":"TSH signals the thyroid; it does not supply glucose.","correct":false}],"hint":"Identify the target organ and the direction of the effect.","id":"glands:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Free T₄ is elevated from primary thyroid overactivity; pituitary feedback is intact.","task":"What TSH direction is expected?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Higher TSH because T₄ stimulates its release.","why":"That reverses the normal feedback direction.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"TSH must always remain unchanged.","why":"TSH responds to thyroid-hormone feedback.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Lower TSH.","why":"Higher thyroid hormone normally suppresses pituitary TSH.","correct":true}],"hint":"Read both hormones as a feedback pair.","id":"glands:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"PTH secretion is low and a patient develops tingling and muscle spasms.","task":"Which connection is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Muscle spasms prove that calcium cannot be involved.","why":"Low calcium can produce spasms and tetany.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Low calcium can increase neuromuscular excitability.","why":"Reduced PTH can contribute to low calcium.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Low PTH must cause high calcium in every case.","why":"That reverses the usual effect being tested.","correct":false}],"hint":"Translate the hormone effect before choosing the symptom explanation.","id":"glands:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Keep the signal separate from the product","task":"Identify which gland, hormone and target the stem names.","cue":"TSH is a pituitary signal; T₄ is a thyroid product.","rule":"Use the direction of feedback or the hormone’s target action.","trap":"A true statement about another gland can still answer the wrong question."},"terms":["glucagon","thyrotropin","parathyroid"],"sources":[{"title":"OpenStax: the endocrine system","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/17-introduction"}],"id":"glands","ch":"53","guide":"53:0","title":"The pancreas, thyroid and parathyroids set the controls","notes":["53-pituitary-thyroid-parathyroid-and-pancre"],"book":"Ch 53: pancreas, thyroid and parathyroid anatomy and physiology (assigned portion).","why":["Pancreatic beta cells release insulin: glucose uptake and fuel storage increase, and hepatic glucose output and ketone production decrease. Alpha-cell glucagon promotes hepatic glucose release when fuel is needed.","The pituitary’s TSH stimulates thyroid T₃/T₄. Thyroid hormones affect metabolic rate. Negative feedback means rising thyroid hormone normally suppresses TSH; interpret the pair rather than one result alone.","Parathyroid hormone helps raise serum calcium through bone, kidney and vitamin D-related intestinal effects. Low calcium increases neuromuscular excitability. Calcitonin has an opposing calcium-lowering effect."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Assess risk without dismissing new illness","cards":[{"title":"Fluids","text":"Changes in thirst and regulation can increase vulnerability. Follow any cardiac/renal fluid restrictions."},{"title":"Bone and function","text":"Hormonal changes can affect bone risk. Assess nutrition, mobility and falls prevention."},{"title":"Glucose and healing","text":"Declining glucose tolerance is an assessment consideration. New infection, poor healing or confusion still needs evaluation."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older adult with a prescribed fluid restriction has reduced thirst.","task":"Which prevention plan fits?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Monitor intake and hydration within the individualized restriction and clinical plan.","why":"Both dehydration risk and the restriction matter.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Offer unlimited fluids regardless of the restriction.","why":"A generic hydration rule can conflict with cardiac or renal needs.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume reduced thirst means fluids are unnecessary.","why":"Thirst may not reliably reflect need.","correct":false}],"hint":"Choose the answer that respects both findings in the stem.","id":"endocrine-aging:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older adult has new confusion and weakness.","task":"What should guide the response?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Document it as inevitable hormonal aging without assessment.","why":"Age does not explain every new symptom.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Wait for several more episodes before checking the patient.","why":"The current change warrants assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Assess the change and investigate reversible causes.","why":"A new functional change should not be dismissed as aging.","correct":true}],"hint":"The word “new” changes the interpretation.","id":"endocrine-aging:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient has slow wound healing and recurrent infections.","task":"Which action fits the lesson’s risk assessment?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Use age as a reason to omit further assessment.","why":"These findings still require evaluation.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Assess glucose status, nutrition and other possible contributors.","why":"Several factors may explain the findings; assess rather than assume.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Conclude that aging alone establishes the diagnosis.","why":"A risk factor supports assessment; it does not establish the cause of these new findings.","correct":false}],"hint":"Do not turn a risk factor into a confirmed cause.","id":"endocrine-aging:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Use all the qualifiers","task":"Find the new change and any treatment restriction.","cue":"Reduced thirst plus a fluid restriction needs an individualized plan.","rule":"Assessment and prevention must fit this patient.","trap":"Avoid answers that apply one generic age-related rule to everyone."},"terms":["osteoporosis","hyperglycemia","homeostasis"],"sources":[{"title":"MedlinePlus: aging changes in hormone production","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/004000.htm"},{"title":"OpenStax: the endocrine system","url":"https://openstax.org/books/anatomy-and-physiology-2e/pages/17-introduction"}],"id":"endocrine-aging","ch":"53","guide":"53:1","title":"Endocrine aging: fluids, bone and glucose","notes":["53-aging-and-covid-19-what-changes-in-the-g"],"book":"Ch 53: endocrine changes associated with aging (assigned portion; not the COVID section).","why":["The chapter connects aging with changes in fluid regulation and thirst, estrogen-related bone loss and declining glucose tolerance. These are assessment considerations, not reasons to dismiss new illness.","Offer and monitor fluids within the person’s prescribed plan, assess nutrition and falls risk, and watch for hyperglycemia clues such as recurrent infection or slow healing. Heart or kidney disease can require fluid restrictions.","New confusion, weakness or functional decline needs assessment. Compare with baseline, review medications and investigate reversible causes."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"timeline","title":"A complete timed interval, not a partial container","cards":[{"title":"Start time","text":"Void, discard that urine, and record the exact start time."},{"title":"During the interval","text":"Collect every subsequent void in the specified container; use the lab’s preservative and storage instructions."},{"title":"Exact end time","text":"Include the final void at the exact end of the 24 hours, then submit as directed."},{"title":"If a specimen is missed","text":"Notify the team/lab and follow restart instructions. Do not estimate the missing analyte."}],"caption":"Timing, completeness and test-specific storage all affect validity."},"steps":[{"kind":"order","scene":"A 24-hour urine collection is ordered. The lab has supplied the container and storage instructions.","task":"Arrange the collection sequence.","items":["Void and discard the urine; record the exact start time.","Collect every subsequent void using the lab’s container and storage instructions.","At the exact 24-hour end time, collect the final void.","Submit the completed collection as directed."],"why":"The first discarded void establishes an empty-bladder start; the final included void completes the interval.","hint":"Distinguish the start void from the end void.","id":"urine:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient accidentally voids into the toilet midway through the collection.","task":"What is the next step?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Notify the team/lab and follow its restart instructions.","why":"A missing specimen can invalidate the collection.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Estimate the volume and mark the specimen complete.","why":"Estimated volume cannot replace the missing analyte.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Add an extra hour without consulting the lab.","why":"A different interval does not reliably replace the missed specimen.","correct":false}],"hint":"Ask whether the test still represents the full ordered interval.","id":"urine:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Two different 24-hour urine tests have different storage requirements.","task":"Which instruction controls?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"One universal room-temperature rule for all urine tests.","why":"Storage conditions are test-specific.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"The patient’s preference even when it conflicts with the lab requirements.","why":"Convenience does not establish specimen validity.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The laboratory directions for the specific ordered test.","why":"Container, preservative and temperature can vary.","correct":true}],"hint":"Specimen handling is specific to the test, not just the sample type.","id":"urine:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Sequence the beginning and end explicitly","task":"Read whether the item asks about starting, finishing or an interrupted collection.","cue":"Discard the start void; include the end void.","rule":"Preserve the exact full interval and required handling.","trap":"Do not “repair” a missing sample by inventing a volume or extra time."},"terms":["specimen","analyte","24-hour urine"],"sources":[{"title":"MedlinePlus: 24-hour urine collection","url":"https://medlineplus.gov/ency/article/003425.htm"}],"id":"urine","ch":"53","guide":"53:2","title":"A 24-hour urine result is only as good as the collection","notes":["53-diagnostic-tests-and-the-nurse-s-job"],"book":"Ch 53: 24-hour urine specimen collection, assigned diagnostic-test portion.","why":["At the exact start time, have the patient void and discard that urine. Collect every subsequent void throughout the interval, including the final void at the exact end time.","Use the correct container, preservative and storage conditions for the ordered test; follow lab instructions. Explain the plan to the patient and everyone helping with toileting.","A missed or discarded specimen can invalidate the result. Notify the appropriate team and follow the lab’s restart instructions; do not silently estimate the missing volume."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Low hormone → slower function → careful replacement","cards":[{"title":"Primary gland failure","text":"Low free T₄ with elevated TSH is a typical primary-hypothyroid pattern. Pituitary causes differ."},{"title":"Clinical pattern","text":"Cold intolerance, fatigue, constipation, dry skin and bradycardia can reflect reduced metabolic activity."},{"title":"Replacement safety","text":"Give levothyroxine consistently as prescribed; check empty-stomach instructions, interactions and cardiac tolerance."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient has low free T₄ and high TSH with primary thyroid failure.","task":"Which interpretation fits?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"TSH and T₄ are the same hormone.","why":"They come from different glands and serve different roles.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The pituitary is increasing its signal in response to low thyroid hormone.","why":"This fits primary gland failure with intact feedback.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"The lab pair proves primary hyperthyroidism.","why":"Primary overactivity usually produces the opposite feedback pattern.","correct":false}],"hint":"Use the feedback pair rather than one value alone.","id":"hypothyroid:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient takes levothyroxine together with calcium and iron supplements.","task":"Which teaching matters?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Follow the prescribed timing and separation from interacting products.","why":"Calcium and iron can reduce levothyroxine absorption.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume supplements never affect prescription medicines.","why":"They can affect absorption.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Change the prescribed dose independently to compensate.","why":"Dose changes require the treatment team’s plan.","correct":false}],"hint":"A timing problem is not automatically a reason to alter the dose.","id":"hypothyroid:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older patient with coronary disease is beginning thyroid replacement.","task":"Why is careful titration important?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Replacement has no cardiovascular effect.","why":"Thyroid hormone affects metabolic and cardiovascular demand.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Every patient should begin at the same high dose.","why":"Dosing is individualized, especially with cardiac disease.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Increasing metabolic demand can stress the heart.","why":"Symptoms and cardiac tolerance need monitoring during replacement.","correct":true}],"hint":"Match the precaution to the comorbidity in the stem.","id":"hypothyroid:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Pair the labs and identify the interaction","task":"Determine whether the task is interpretation or safe administration.","cue":"Low T₄/high TSH and simultaneous calcium use answer different questions.","rule":"Use feedback for labs and product directions for administration.","trap":"Do not solve an absorption interaction by choosing an unprescribed dose change."},"terms":["hypothyroidism","levothyroxine","bradycardia"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: hypothyroidism","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hypothyroidism"},{"title":"DailyMed: official prescribing information for the exact product","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/"},{"title":"DailyMed: Synthroid interactions and administration","url":"https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=b9aea421-ae9b-439a-90a3-07a0a50e835d"}],"id":"hypothyroid","ch":"55","guide":"55:0","title":"Hypothyroidism: slower metabolism and careful replacement","notes":["55-hypothyroidism-the-cellular-regulation-e","55-overview","55-adpie"],"book":"Ch 55: hypothyroidism, levothyroxine and nursing safety.","why":["Low thyroid hormone slows metabolic activity: fatigue, cold intolerance, constipation, dry skin and bradycardia can result. Primary gland failure usually produces low free T₄ with high TSH; pituitary causes have a different pattern.","Levothyroxine replaces hormone. Teach consistent administration on an empty stomach as prescribed and separation from calcium and iron according to directions. Follow symptoms and thyroid labs over time.","Older adults and people with coronary disease need careful titration: increased metabolic demand can stress the heart. Severe hypothyroidism also increases sensitivity to sedating medications."],"caseId":"","sourceSections":[{"id":"55-overview","title":"Overview & how it fits together","blocks":[{"note":"55-overview","title":"Intro.","lines":["Thyroid hormone = speed of every cell · PTH = guardian of serum calcium","Hypothyroid slows everything (exemplar) · hyperthyroid speeds everything","Parathyroid = calcium too low or too high","Danger points: myxedema coma, thyroid storm, airway + calcium after neck surgery, tetany"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"Why a goiter appears in both diseases","lines":["Hypo: low hormone → TSH ↑ → cells grow → goiter, no more hormone","Graves: antibodies stimulate the TSH receptor → grows and overproduces","Goiter = thyroid problem, not which direction"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"Myxedema — what the swelling is","lines":["Low metabolism → glycosaminoglycans collect → hold water","Nonpitting edema (shins, tops of feet), big thick tongue, puffy face and eyes"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"The heart in each disease","lines":["Hypo: flabby heart, big chambers → ↓ cardiac output","Hyper: ↑ rate + stroke volume → ↑ CO + ↑ systolic, ↓ diastolic (wide pulse pressure), A-fib"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"Metabolism in hyperthyroidism","lines":["Protein breakdown > buildup · glucose tolerance ↓ (hyperglycemia) · fat ↓","Weight loss despite ↑ appetite → nutrition deficits"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"Calcium and PTH","lines":["PTH: kidney keeps Ca²⁺, dumps phosphorus; bone + gut release Ca²⁺","Ca²⁺ and phosphorus move opposite: low PTH = ↓ Ca + ↑ P; high PTH = ↑ Ca + ↓ P"]},{"note":"55-overview","title":"Why","lines":["Calcium steadies nerve membranes → low Ca²⁺ = too excitable: tingling, twitching, tetany, laryngospasm","High Ca²⁺ = the reverse: weak muscles, slow gut, cloudy brain"]}]},{"id":"55-adpie","title":"Nursing process (ADPIE)","blocks":[{"note":"55-adpie","title":"¶ Every plan in this chapter starts by","lines":["Name the direction first (slow/fast, Ca low/high)","Guard the organ that fails first: breathing + cardiac output (slow) · heart + temperature (fast) · airway + calcium (neck surgery)"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Thyroid history","lines":["Activity + sleep vs a year ago; heat vs cold intolerance","Weight vs appetite, bowels, palpitations, chest pain","Eyes, mood/memory (family), menses, libido","Lithium, amiodarone, thyroid drugs, past surgery/RAI/radiation, COVID-19"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Thyroid exam","lines":["Apical pulse, BP + pulse pressure, temp, RR, SpO₂","Skin, hair, eyebrows, nails, tongue, voice","Eyes: exophthalmos, lid/globe lag, closure","Goiter by sight only, bruit, reflexes, tremor, edema, mental status"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Calcium checks","lines":["Ask tingling (mouth, fingers, toes); watch twitching","Chvostek + Trousseau","Every Ca²⁺ after neck surgery; stones, fractures, GI, confusion if high"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Why","lines":["Hypothyroid change is so slow that only then vs now reveals it"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Hypothyroidism","lines":["1. ↓ Gas exchange · 2. ↓ Perfusion · 3. Myxedema coma"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Hyperthyroidism and surgery","lines":["Thyroid storm, dysrhythmias, weight loss, eye injury","After thyroidectomy: airway, bleeding, hypocalcemia, nerve injury"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Parathyroid","lines":["Hypo: tetany, laryngospasm, seizures","Hyper: fractures, stones, ulcer, confusion; after surgery low Ca crisis"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Outcomes","lines":["Hypo: pulse > 60, SpO₂ > 93%, cognition ↑, no coma","Hyper: HR controlled, no temp rise, weight stable, eyes safe","Neck surgery: airway patent, no bleeding, Ca²⁺ normal","Parathyroid: Ca low-normal, no stones, no fractures"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Hypothyroidism#2","lines":["Breathing, SpO₂, O₂, no/low sedatives; BP, HR, rhythm","Levothyroxine empty stomach, 4 h from iron/Ca/antacids, start low","Warmth, fiber + fluids","q8h coma check → Box 55.3 care"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Hyperthyroidism","lines":["Pulse, BP, temp q4h; ↑ 1°F → report","Quiet, rest, cool, ice water, dry linens; eye care","Methimazole, SSKI diluted, beta blockers; RAI precautions","Never palpate; storm → Box 55.6"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"After neck surgery","lines":["VS q15 until stable; semi-Fowler, no neck extension","Dressing + behind neck; trach kit, O₂, suction","Voice q2h; tingling + Ca²⁺; IV calcium gluconate ready","Stridor/drooling/↓ SpO₂ → Rapid Response"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Parathyroid#2","lines":["Hypo: IV Ca gluconate, calcitriol + Ca carbonate, seizure precautions","Hyper: fluids, mobility, lift sheet, cinacalcet/bisphosphonates, no lithium","After surgery: every Ca²⁺, watch tetany"]},{"note":"55-adpie","title":"Evaluate","lines":["Hypo: pulse > 60, SpO₂ > 93%, clearer thinking","Hyper: HR + temp controlled, weight stable","Surgery: airway open, no bleeding, Ca²⁺ normal","Patient states levothyroxine rules, signs of too much/too little, RAI precautions, tingling = low Ca"]}]}]},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Slow breathing changes the priority","cards":[{"title":"Recognize decompensation","text":"Severe hypothyroidism can combine hypothermia, hypoventilation, hypotension and altered mentation. Coma is not required."},{"title":"Support vital function","text":"Activate emergency help; assess/support airway, ventilation and perfusion and prepare prescribed therapy."},{"title":"Prevent further depression","text":"Sedating medicines can worsen breathing. Monitor rhythm, glucose, sodium, temperature and the precipitating illness."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with severe hypothyroidism has RR 7/min, T 34.7°C and reduced responsiveness.","task":"What has priority?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Give a sedative for restlessness before checking breathing.","why":"Further respiratory depression can worsen the threat.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Activate emergency help and support airway/ventilation.","why":"Severely depressed breathing is an immediate threat.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Arrange a routine thyroid follow-up in a month.","why":"This presentation is unstable.","correct":false}],"hint":"Prioritization comes from the immediate physiologic problem.","id":"myxedema:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient becomes harder to arouse after an opioid.","task":"Which assessment is urgent?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Respiratory rate, ventilation and oxygenation.","why":"Severe hypothyroidism can increase vulnerability to respiratory depression.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether constipation has improved.","why":"Bowel symptoms are lower priority than this change.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Only the time of the next routine TSH test.","why":"A scheduled lab does not address the breathing threat.","correct":false}],"hint":"A new medication effect can make an existing risk more urgent.","id":"myxedema:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient is confused and hypothermic but is not fully comatose.","task":"Does the lack of coma remove concern?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Yes; a patient must be fully comatose for severe disease to be considered.","why":"That is an unsafe interpretation of the terminology.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Yes; hypothermia alone proves stable recovery.","why":"Temperature must be interpreted with breathing and mental status.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"No; the decompensated clinical pattern still requires urgent evaluation.","why":"The name does not require complete coma.","correct":true}],"hint":"Do not let the diagnostic label override the actual findings.","id":"myxedema:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Treat the immediate threat described","task":"Identify the finding that threatens vital function now.","cue":"RR 7 with reduced responsiveness outranks routine follow-up.","rule":"Use the observed instability; do not wait for every feature of a named syndrome.","trap":"“Assess first” does not mean delaying emergency help for a complete history."},"terms":["myxedema","hypoventilation","hypothermia"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: hypothyroidism","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hypothyroidism"}],"id":"myxedema","ch":"55","guide":"55:0","title":"Myxedema crisis: slow breathing is an emergency","notes":["55-myxedema-coma"],"book":"Ch 55: myxedema coma and emergency nursing care.","why":["Severe decompensated hypothyroidism can cause hypoventilation, hypothermia, bradycardia, hypotension and altered mental status. The term myxedema coma does not require the patient to be fully comatose.","Activate emergency help, support airway and ventilation, and prepare prescribed thyroid-hormone and other supportive therapy. Monitor cardiac rhythm, glucose, sodium, temperature and perfusion.","Avoid actions that further depress breathing. Use ordered, cautious warming and treat the precipitating illness; this is not a routine follow-up for fatigue."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Different treatments act at different points","cards":[{"title":"Excess hormone effects","text":"Heat intolerance, tremor, weight loss and tachycardia can reflect increased metabolic/adrenergic activity."},{"title":"Reduce synthesis","text":"Antithyroid medicines reduce new thyroid hormone production. Fever or sore throat during treatment requires urgent attention."},{"title":"Reduce symptoms / definitive treatment","text":"Beta blockers target adrenergic symptoms. Radioactive iodine and surgery have different indications and precautions."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient asks whether a beta blocker removes the cause of Graves disease.","task":"Which explanation is accurate?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Every hyperthyroidism medicine has exactly the same role.","why":"Treatment mechanisms differ.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"It controls adrenergic symptoms; hormone-directed treatment has a different role.","why":"Symptom control and reduction of hormone synthesis are not the same.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"It instantly eliminates all thyroid hormone production.","why":"That is not its mechanism.","correct":false}],"hint":"Sort symptom control from treatment of hormone production.","id":"hyperthyroid:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking methimazole develops a fever and sore throat.","task":"Which response fits the safety concern?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Promptly contact the treatment team for evaluation and medication/CBC instructions.","why":"These findings can signal a serious blood-cell adverse effect.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Take extra methimazole without contacting anyone.","why":"An extra dose does not address the possible adverse effect.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Wait until the routine appointment because sore throat is irrelevant.","why":"The warning requires prompt evaluation.","correct":false}],"hint":"A medication warning changes the priority from routine adherence teaching.","id":"hyperthyroid:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Radioactive iodine treatment is being considered and the patient may be pregnant.","task":"What needs clarification?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether the patient prefers morning appointments.","why":"That does not address the safety issue.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume radioactive iodine is safe in every pregnancy.","why":"That generalization is unsafe.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Pregnancy status and treatment-specific contraindications/precautions.","why":"Radioactive iodine has important pregnancy restrictions.","correct":true}],"hint":"Look for the contraindication attached to this treatment.","id":"hyperthyroid:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Classify the treatment before answering","task":"Identify whether the question asks mechanism, symptom control or a drug warning.","cue":"Beta blocker, methimazole and radioactive iodine point to different decisions.","rule":"Apply the specific drug or procedure’s purpose and precautions.","trap":"A true effect of one treatment is not an effect of every treatment."},"terms":["hyperthyroidism","methimazole","agranulocytosis"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: hyperthyroidism","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hyperthyroidism"},{"title":"DailyMed: official prescribing information for the exact product","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/"},{"title":"DailyMed: methimazole warnings","url":"https://www.dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=490f168f-c703-5497-e063-6294a90ade87"}],"id":"hyperthyroid","ch":"55","guide":"55:1","title":"Hyperthyroidism: excess drive and targeted treatments","notes":["55-hyperthyroidism-and-graves-disease","55-treating-hyperthyroidism-drugs-radioacti"],"book":"Ch 55: Graves disease, antithyroid drugs, radioactive iodine and comfort/eye care.","why":["Excess thyroid hormone increases metabolic activity: heat intolerance, weight loss, tachycardia, tremor and anxiety can result. Primary hyperthyroidism typically has suppressed TSH and elevated thyroid hormone; Graves is an autoimmune cause.","Antithyroid drugs reduce new hormone synthesis; beta blockers control adrenergic symptoms. Teach urgent reporting of fever or sore throat with methimazole because of agranulocytosis risk. Radioactive iodine has pregnancy and radiation precautions.","Support rest, nutrition and coping. With Graves eye disease, protect exposed corneas and follow the eye-care plan. Emotional symptoms deserve assessment and support."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"After neck surgery: airway before routine recovery","cards":[{"title":"Airway or bleeding warning","text":"Stridor, expanding neck swelling/tightness or rapidly worsening breathing requires immediate help. Assess bleeding, including behind the neck."},{"title":"Voice changes","text":"A stable mild voice change still needs assessment and trending; it is different from an airway emergency."},{"title":"Calcium warning","text":"Tingling, tetany or spasms can reflect parathyroid disruption. Laryngospasm is an airway emergency."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"After thyroidectomy, a patient develops stridor and rapidly increasing neck swelling.","task":"Which action has priority?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Reassure the patient that all voice and breathing changes are expected.","why":"Stridor is not simply mild postoperative hoarseness.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Activate immediate help and support airway assessment/management.","why":"The new neck swelling and stridor can threaten the airway.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Finish routine discharge teaching before calling for help.","why":"The airway concern outranks routine teaching.","correct":false}],"hint":"Distinguish an airway sound from a stable voice change.","id":"thyroidectomy:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The patient reports perioral tingling and hand spasms after surgery.","task":"What should be assessed?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Calcium status, symptoms and the prescribed treatment plan.","why":"Parathyroid disruption can lower calcium.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether the dressing color matches the gown.","why":"That does not address the neuromuscular findings.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume high calcium is the only possible postoperative pattern.","why":"Calcium can fall after parathyroid disruption.","correct":false}],"hint":"Connect the surgery to the nearby gland’s function.","id":"thyroidectomy:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient has stable mild hoarseness without stridor or expanding swelling.","task":"Which response fits?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Treat it as proof that an expanding hematoma is present.","why":"The available findings do not establish that diagnosis.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Ignore all future respiratory changes because hoarseness is common.","why":"New respiratory findings still require reassessment.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Assess and trend voice, breathing and other findings; report concerns according to the plan.","why":"Stable hoarseness still needs assessment, while findings determine urgency.","correct":true}],"hint":"Use the severity and trend rather than the symptom name alone.","id":"thyroidectomy:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Rank the actual airway finding","task":"Compare new stridor or expanding swelling with a stable mild voice change.","cue":"Breathing difficulty is different from isolated stable hoarseness.","rule":"Respond to the immediate threat, then reassess and monitor other postoperative risks.","trap":"Do not classify every postoperative neck symptom as either harmless or equally urgent."},"terms":["thyroidectomy","stridor","hypocalcemia"],"sources":[{"title":"ATA: surgical management of Graves disease","url":"https://www.thyroid.org/surgical-management-graves-disease/"}],"id":"thyroidectomy","ch":"55","guide":"55:1","title":"After thyroidectomy: airway, bleeding and calcium","notes":["55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm"],"book":"Ch 55: postoperative thyroidectomy assessment and complications.","why":["After neck surgery, swelling, hematoma or laryngeal dysfunction can threaten the airway. Assess breathing, neck swelling/tightness, bleeding including behind the neck, and changes in the voice.","Stridor or rapidly worsening swelling requires immediate help and airway support. Maintain prescribed positioning and the unit’s emergency equipment plan. Stable mild hoarseness still needs assessment and trending.","Parathyroid disruption can lower calcium. Perioral/finger tingling, tetany or spasms need prompt assessment, calcium review and prescribed treatment; laryngospasm is an airway emergency."],"caseId":"thyroidectomy"},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Excess thyroid activity plus systemic instability","cards":[{"title":"Trigger and pattern","text":"Illness or another stressor can precipitate severe thyrotoxicosis with marked fever, tachycardia and altered mentation."},{"title":"Emergency response","text":"Activate help and assess airway, circulation, temperature and rhythm while preparing prescribed treatment."},{"title":"Monitor the response","text":"Follow mentation, pulse, temperature, perfusion and the precipitating illness; routine symptom teaching is insufficient."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with hyperthyroidism develops a very high fever, HR 160 and delirium during an infection.","task":"Which interpretation changes the priority?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Proof that the patient should simply exercise to lower stress.","why":"Exercise does not address the instability.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Possible thyroid storm needing urgent monitored treatment.","why":"The systemic instability is a dangerous pattern.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Routine anxiety that can wait for the next visit.","why":"The vital-sign and mental-status changes exceed routine anxiety.","correct":false}],"hint":"Cluster the fever, circulation and brain-function cues.","id":"thyroid-storm:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The team is evaluating suspected thyroid storm and confirmatory labs are pending.","task":"What should the nurse do meanwhile?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Continue urgent assessment, monitoring and preparation for prescribed emergency care.","why":"Emergency support should not be delayed for routine confirmation.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Stop monitoring until a lab proves every diagnostic feature.","why":"The current instability requires action.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Offer only routine medication-adherence teaching.","why":"Teaching alone does not address the acute threat.","correct":false}],"hint":"Do not let a pending test replace management of present instability.","id":"thyroid-storm:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After treatment, temperature and heart rate improve but the patient becomes harder to arouse.","task":"Which finding drives reassessment?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Only the lower temperature.","why":"Evaluation must consider the whole patient.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"The assumption that improvement in one vital sign proves complete recovery.","why":"Recovery is not established by one measure.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The new deterioration in mental status.","why":"One improved trend does not cancel a new warning sign.","correct":true}],"hint":"Look for discordant trends when judging outcomes.","id":"thyroid-storm:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Cluster cues before applying a familiar label","task":"Decide whether the stem describes routine hyperthyroidism or systemic decompensation.","cue":"Marked fever, extreme tachycardia and delirium together raise urgency.","rule":"Use instability to prioritize support and monitored treatment.","trap":"A label such as anxiety should not explain away objective deterioration."},"terms":["thyrotoxicosis","tachycardia","delirium"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: hyperthyroidism","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/hyperthyroidism"}],"id":"thyroid-storm","ch":"55","guide":"55:1","title":"Thyroid storm: fever plus cardiovascular and brain changes","notes":["55-thyroidectomy-and-thyroid-storm"],"book":"Ch 55: thyroid storm, precipitating factors and emergency management.","why":["Severe thyrotoxicosis with systemic decompensation can produce marked fever, tachycardia, agitation/delirium and cardiovascular instability. Infection, surgery or interrupted treatment can precipitate it.","Activate emergency care, assess airway, breathing and circulation, monitor continuously, and prepare the ordered antithyroid, cardiovascular and supportive therapies. Cooling, fluids and treatment of the trigger are important.","When ordered for storm, iodine follows a thionamide so hormone synthesis is blocked before iodine is supplied. Monitor response across temperature, heart rate, mentation and perfusion rather than using a single number."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Inflammation, nodules and replacement follow different paths","cards":[{"title":"Thyroiditis over time","text":"Release of stored hormone can cause an early hyperthyroid phase, followed by low-hormone findings. Follow symptoms and labs."},{"title":"Nodule or local change","text":"A new nodule, persistent local change or progressive swallowing/voice issue needs evaluation. Cancer types and stages behave differently."},{"title":"After total thyroidectomy","text":"Plan lifelong thyroid hormone replacement and follow-up; add any treatment-specific precautions."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with thyroiditis first had palpitations and later develops fatigue and cold intolerance.","task":"Which explanation is possible?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"The symptom change removes any need for repeat labs.","why":"Follow-up helps identify the current phase.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Hormone patterns can change from an early excess phase to a low-hormone phase.","why":"Inflammation can release stored hormone and later leave a low-hormone state.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Symptoms cannot change once thyroiditis is diagnosed.","why":"The process can evolve over time.","correct":false}],"hint":"Use the time course instead of assuming one fixed phase.","id":"thyroiditis-cancer:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient notices a persistent new thyroid-area nodule.","task":"Which response is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Arrange evaluation rather than assume its cause or behavior.","why":"A nodule needs assessment; symptoms alone do not establish type or stage.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume every nodule is harmless and needs no follow-up.","why":"That conclusion is unsupported.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume every thyroid cancer has the same prognosis.","why":"Cancer type and stage affect treatment and prognosis; a nodule alone does not establish either.","correct":false}],"hint":"Evaluation comes before predicting a diagnosis or prognosis.","id":"thyroiditis-cancer:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"After total thyroidectomy, a patient asks whether hormone replacement stops after wound healing.","task":"Which explanation fits?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"All replacement ends when the dressing is removed.","why":"Wound recovery and endocrine function are different outcomes.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"The patient should stop and restart hormone whenever energy varies.","why":"Changes require the prescribed treatment and lab plan.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Replacement and thyroid follow-up are long-term needs after total removal.","why":"The lost hormone-producing tissue is not restored by incision healing.","correct":true}],"hint":"Link the permanent anatomical change to the continuing function needed.","id":"thyroiditis-cancer:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Follow the time course and the extent of surgery","task":"Identify whether the item concerns evolving thyroiditis, evaluation or total removal.","cue":"A changing symptom pattern and the word “total” are meaningful qualifiers.","rule":"Match the follow-up need to the process described.","trap":"Do not infer cancer type or permanent hormone status from one symptom alone."},"terms":["thyroiditis","thyroid nodule","thyroidectomy"],"sources":[{"title":"ATA: thyroiditis","url":"https://www.thyroid.org/thyroiditis/"},{"title":"ATA: surgical management of Graves disease","url":"https://www.thyroid.org/surgical-management-graves-disease/"}],"id":"thyroiditis-cancer","ch":"55","guide":"55:1","title":"Thyroiditis, cancer and ongoing replacement","notes":["55-thyroiditis-and-thyroid-cancer"],"book":"Ch 55: thyroiditis, thyroid cancer and postoperative transition teaching.","why":["An inflamed thyroid can release stored hormone, causing a temporary hyperthyroid phase, then a hypothyroid phase. Painful subacute thyroiditis may follow a viral illness; follow symptoms and labs over time.","A thyroid nodule or a persistent local change needs evaluation. Thyroid cancer treatment and prognosis depend on type and stage; do not assume every thyroid cancer behaves the same way.","After total thyroidectomy, lifelong hormone replacement and follow-up are necessary. Teach wound and airway concerns, medication use, any prescribed radioactive-iodine precautions, and when to report symptoms."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Low calcium excites; excess PTH can weaken bone","cards":[{"title":"Low calcium","text":"Tingling, cramps, tetany, seizures or laryngospasm can reflect increased neuromuscular excitability."},{"title":"Excess PTH / high calcium","text":"Bone loss, renal stones, constipation, weakness and cognitive changes may occur. Follow the fluid and treatment plan."},{"title":"After parathyroid surgery","text":"Calcium can fall; monitor symptoms and trends and give prescribed calcium/active vitamin D when indicated."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"After neck surgery, a patient has tingling, hand spasms and new stridor.","task":"What makes this urgent?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Stridor is unrelated to breathing safety.","why":"It can signal an airway threat.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Possible low-calcium-related airway spasm requires immediate help and assessment.","why":"Airway involvement changes the urgency of hypocalcemic symptoms.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Tingling always proves a harmless anxiety episode.","why":"The surgical history and airway finding require assessment.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate the biochemical explanation from the immediate airway priority.","id":"calcium:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with excess PTH has renal stones, constipation and weakness.","task":"Which mechanism helps explain the pattern?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"PTH-related calcium changes and bone/renal effects.","why":"Excess PTH can raise serum calcium while weakening bone.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"A lack of PTH always increases serum calcium.","why":"That reverses the usual relationship.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"High calcium always makes muscles more excitable in the same way as low calcium.","why":"The clinical patterns differ.","correct":false}],"hint":"Translate the direction of calcium change before matching findings.","id":"calcium:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"Calcium falls after parathyroidectomy and replacement is prescribed.","task":"What should be evaluated?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Only whether the neck incision looks dry.","why":"The incision does not measure calcium recovery.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Replace calcium and then omit all reassessment.","why":"Treatment requires evaluation.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Symptoms, calcium trends and the response to the prescribed regimen.","why":"Monitoring guides safe replacement and recovery.","correct":true}],"hint":"Choose outcomes that measure the problem being treated.","id":"calcium:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Direction first, priority second","task":"Determine whether calcium is low or high, then assess severity.","cue":"Tetany and stridor point to a different response from stable constipation.","rule":"Use both the mechanism and the immediate physiologic threat.","trap":"A correct biochemical label alone is not a complete priority action."},"terms":["hypocalcemia","hypercalcemia","tetany"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: primary hyperparathyroidism","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/endocrine-diseases/primary-hyperparathyroidism"}],"id":"calcium","ch":"55","guide":"55:2","title":"Parathyroids: low calcium excites, high calcium weakens","notes":["55-hypoparathyroidism-and-hyperparathyroidi"],"book":"Ch 55: hypoparathyroidism, hyperparathyroidism and parathyroidectomy.","why":["Too little PTH can lower calcium: tingling, cramps, tetany, seizures and laryngospasm reflect increased excitability. Assess symptoms and calcium trends; severe symptoms require urgent care and prescribed calcium treatment.","Excess PTH can raise serum calcium while weakening bones. Stones, constipation, weakness and cognitive changes may occur. Protect against falls and fractures and follow the fluid and treatment plan.","After parathyroidectomy, calcium can fall as bone takes it up and hormone effects change. Monitor closely. Long-term treatment may include calcium and active vitamin D for low calcium, or other therapies for high calcium; these are not interchangeable."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Different mechanisms can produce high glucose","cards":[{"title":"Type 1","text":"Usually autoimmune beta-cell destruction with severe insulin deficiency. Basal insulin remains necessary during fasting, with team-directed adjustments."},{"title":"Type 2","text":"Insulin resistance combines with progressive beta-cell dysfunction. Treatment follows the individual’s needs."},{"title":"Osmotic symptoms","text":"Urinary glucose draws water: polyuria contributes to dehydration and thirst. Age and body size alone do not establish the type."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient asks why type 1 diabetes requires insulin even when no meal is eaten.","task":"Which explanation is accurate?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Any unprescribed oral medicine can replace basal insulin.","why":"That does not replace the basal requirement in type 1 diabetes.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Basal insulin restrains glucose output and ketone production between meals.","why":"The body needs background insulin as well as meal coverage.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"All insulin is only for food, so none is needed while fasting.","why":"Omission can create severe insulin deficiency and DKA risk.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate background metabolic need from meal coverage.","id":"diabetes-types:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient has polyuria and marked thirst with high glucose.","task":"Which mechanism connects the findings?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Glucose in urine draws water, increasing fluid loss and thirst.","why":"Osmotic diuresis explains the link.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"High glucose prevents all urine formation.","why":"That reverses the osmotic effect.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Thirst by itself proves the diabetes type.","why":"Symptoms do not establish type on their own.","correct":false}],"hint":"Find the cause-and-effect chain, not just a memorized symptom pair.","id":"diabetes-types:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"An adult’s body size seems atypical for the assumed diabetes type.","task":"What should guide classification and care?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Body size alone is sufficient for every diagnosis.","why":"That shortcut can misclassify the condition.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Ignore possible monogenic or other forms because there are only two types.","why":"Other forms of diabetes exist and need type-specific assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Appropriate history, testing and individualized assessment.","why":"Type cannot be assigned reliably by age or body size alone.","correct":true}],"hint":"Avoid a stereotype when the question asks for assessment.","id":"diabetes-types:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Mechanism beats demographic shortcuts","task":"Identify whether the question asks type, symptom mechanism or treatment need.","cue":"Insulin deficiency and insulin resistance are different mechanisms.","rule":"Use the history and testing rather than age or body size alone.","trap":"Do not turn a common demographic association into a diagnostic rule."},"terms":["polyuria","polydipsia","insulin resistance"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: types of diabetes","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/what-is-diabetes"},{"title":"ADA 2026: diabetes care in hospital","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s016"}],"id":"diabetes-types","ch":"56","guide":"56:0","title":"Diabetes types: different routes to ineffective insulin","notes":["56-types-risk-and-screening"],"book":"Ch 56: types, risk factors, prevention and screening.","why":["Type 1 usually involves autoimmune beta-cell destruction and severe insulin deficiency. Type 2 combines insulin resistance with progressive beta-cell dysfunction. Both require individualized care; body size or age alone does not determine type.","Glucose above the kidney’s reabsorption capacity spills into urine and draws water: polyuria causes dehydration and thirst. Insufficient effective insulin also alters fuel storage and use.","Risk assessment, appropriate screening, nutrition, activity and weight-management support can reduce or delay type 2 risk. Other forms, including monogenic diabetes, need type-specific assessment and treatment."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Choose the measurement that answers the question","cards":[{"title":"Diagnosis","text":"A1C ≥6.5%, fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL or 2-hour OGTT ≥200 mg/dL are diagnostic thresholds; confirmation rules apply."},{"title":"Now versus longer term","text":"A meter gives a point-in-time reading. A1C reflects longer-term glycemia and can have interpretation limitations."},{"title":"CGM","text":"Interstitial glucose and trends may differ from symptoms or a meter. Follow the device’s confirmation/calibration directions; treat suspected symptomatic lows promptly."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient without unequivocal hyperglycemia has one abnormal diagnostic test.","task":"What is the key next consideration?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Use a single CGM value alone as every diagnostic criterion.","why":"Diagnostic testing and monitoring are different uses.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Appropriate confirmatory testing under the diagnostic plan.","why":"An isolated abnormal result may require confirmation.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume confirmation is never needed.","why":"Confirmation rules depend on the presentation.","correct":false}],"hint":"Distinguish diagnosis from day-to-day monitoring.","id":"monitoring:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The team asks whether glucose control has changed over the past several months.","task":"Which measure addresses that time scale?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"A1C, interpreted with the patient’s clinical context.","why":"A1C reflects longer-term glycemia rather than one moment.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Only one pre-lunch meter value.","why":"That is a point-in-time measurement.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"The number of remaining sensor stickers.","why":"Supplies do not measure glycemia.","correct":false}],"hint":"Read the time frame in the stem.","id":"monitoring:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient feels shaky and sweaty, but the CGM reading does not fit the symptoms.","task":"What is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Assume the sensor makes hypoglycemia impossible.","why":"Symptoms still require assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Apply one universal fingerstick-calibration schedule to every device.","why":"Calibration requirements differ by device.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Use available meter confirmation and the device/clinical plan without delaying necessary low-glucose treatment.","why":"Lag or sensor problems can cause disagreement; assess the patient.","correct":true}],"hint":"A mismatch requires patient assessment and device-specific instructions.","id":"monitoring:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Match the test to the time frame","task":"Decide whether the item asks diagnosis, immediate safety or longer-term control.","cue":"“Right now,” “months” and “confirm diagnosis” point to different tools.","rule":"Use the measurement for its intended purpose and check contradictory findings.","trap":"Do not use a longer-term average to dismiss an immediate symptomatic low."},"terms":["glycated hemoglobin","interstitial","glucometer"],"sources":[{"title":"ADA 2026: diagnosis and classification","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s002"},{"title":"ADA 2026: diabetes technology","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s007"}],"id":"monitoring","ch":"56","guide":"56:0","title":"Glucose, A1C and CGM answer different questions","notes":["56-diagnosing-and-monitoring-glucose-a1c-cg"],"book":"Ch 56: diagnosis and monitoring; current device-specific CGM clarification in source review.","why":["Diagnostic thresholds include A1C ≥6.5%, fasting plasma glucose ≥126 mg/dL, or 2-hour OGTT ≥200 mg/dL. Random plasma glucose ≥200 mg/dL with classic symptoms or crisis can establish diabetes; otherwise confirm abnormal testing as appropriate.","A1C reflects longer-term glycemia, while a meter gives a point-in-time glucose. CGM measures interstitial glucose and shows trends; lag or sensor issues can cause disagreement with symptoms or a meter.","Calibration and confirmatory meter requirements depend on the specific device. Keep BGM available and follow its instructions when readings do not match expectations. For symptomatic suspected hypoglycemia, do not delay necessary treatment."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Background, meals and delivery are different jobs","cards":[{"title":"Basal","text":"Background coverage between meals and overnight. Type 1 diabetes still requires basal insulin during fasting under an adjusted plan."},{"title":"Meal / correction","text":"Prandial insulin covers food; correction dosing follows the ordered plan for elevated glucose. Coordinate timing with food and the exact product."},{"title":"Delivery safety","text":"Verify product, concentration, dose, route, storage and mixing rules. Pump interruption can rapidly remove insulin delivery."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient is about to receive rapid-acting meal insulin, but the meal has not arrived.","task":"What should be checked?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Assume all insulin products have identical onset and timing.","why":"Insulin products differ in onset and duration; the exact product and orders control timing.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Food availability, glucose, the insulin product and the prescribed timing plan.","why":"Meal-insulin timing must fit intake and orders.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Give an extra meal dose to cover the delay.","why":"That increases mismatch and low-glucose risk.","correct":false}],"hint":"Match the role of this dose to its immediate context.","id":"insulin:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient asks whether insulin glargine can be mixed with another insulin in the same syringe.","task":"Which response is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Follow the exact product label; glargine should not be mixed with other insulins.","why":"Mixing rules are product-specific.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Mix every insulin together to reduce injections.","why":"Some products cannot be mixed.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume concentration and product names are interchangeable.","why":"Product and concentration errors can cause harm.","correct":false}],"hint":"The exact product name matters in an administration question.","id":"insulin:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A pump reports an occlusion and glucose is unexpectedly rising.","task":"Which response fits?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Wait until the next routine visit without further checks.","why":"Delivery failure needs prompt assessment.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Stop monitoring until the pump repairs itself.","why":"Monitoring is important during suspected interruption.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Assess delivery, check glucose/ketones as directed and use the prescribed backup/contact plan promptly.","why":"Interrupted insulin delivery can lead to insulin deficiency and DKA.","correct":true}],"hint":"Identify a device failure that can change the medication actually delivered.","id":"insulin:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Name the purpose of the dose","task":"Determine whether insulin is basal, meal, correction or interrupted delivery.","cue":"A delayed meal, a specific product or a pump alarm changes the action.","rule":"Use product instructions and the individualized plan; do not improvise dose changes.","trap":"“Not eating” does not mean that all insulin has no purpose."},"terms":["basal insulin","insulin glargine","subcutaneous"],"sources":[{"title":"ADA 2026: diabetes care in hospital","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s016"},{"title":"DailyMed: official prescribing information for the exact product","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/"},{"title":"DailyMed: Lantus prescribing information","url":"https://dailymed-beta.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/drugInfo.cfm?setid=d5e07a0c-7e14-4756-9152-9fea485d654a"}],"id":"insulin","ch":"56","guide":"56:1","title":"Insulin: basal coverage, meals and safe delivery","notes":["56-insulin-types-timing-injection-and-stora"],"book":"Ch 56: insulin types, timing, injection, storage and pump care.","why":["Basal insulin covers ongoing needs between meals and overnight. Prandial insulin covers meals; correction doses address elevations under the plan. Match rapid/short-acting insulin timing to food and orders, and know the product’s onset and peak.","Check the correct product, concentration, dose and route. Rotate sites within an area and avoid damaged tissue. Storage, mixing rules and in-use discard times depend on the insulin product; use the exact label and pharmacy instructions.","A pump interruption can rapidly remove insulin delivery. Persistent unexpected hyperglycemia needs assessment of the delivery system, ketones when indicated, and the prescribed backup plan. A person with type 1 diabetes needs basal insulin even when not eating, with dose adjustment by the team."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Predict the risk from the mechanism","cards":[{"title":"Metformin / secretagogues","text":"Metformin reduces hepatic glucose output. Sulfonylureas increase insulin secretion and can cause hypoglycemia."},{"title":"GLP-1 / SGLT2","text":"GLP-1 receptor agonists affect glucose-dependent secretion and gastric emptying. SGLT2 inhibitors increase urinary glucose loss; DKA can occur without marked hyperglycemia."},{"title":"Thiazolidinediones","text":"Fluid retention can cause edema, weight gain and heart-failure concerns. Drug-specific contraindications and holds matter."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking a sulfonylurea has eaten very little and becomes shaky.","task":"Which medication-related problem needs assessment?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Use the absence of an insulin injection to rule out a low.","why":"The medication class also matters.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Hypoglycemia.","why":"Insulin secretion can continue despite inadequate intake.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Assume oral diabetes drugs cannot cause low glucose.","why":"Some non-insulin drugs can cause hypoglycemia.","correct":false}],"hint":"Identify whether the drug stimulates insulin secretion.","id":"diabetes-drugs:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking an SGLT2 inhibitor is ill with nausea and ketones, but glucose is not dramatically elevated.","task":"Which interpretation is safest?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Evaluate possible ketoacidosis despite the glucose level.","why":"SGLT2-associated DKA can occur without marked hyperglycemia.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Rule out ketoacidosis from glucose alone.","why":"That can miss this presentation.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Give an unprescribed extra dose to clear ketones.","why":"Extra dosing does not address the emergency concern.","correct":false}],"hint":"Look for an exception to the familiar glucose pattern.","id":"diabetes-drugs:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking a thiazolidinedione develops edema and rapid weight gain.","task":"What should be assessed?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Treat the change as proof that glucose control is excellent.","why":"Weight gain and edema are not glucose-control measures.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Ignore breathing symptoms if the glucose is in range.","why":"A normal glucose does not remove a fluid-related threat.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Fluid retention and possible heart-failure findings.","why":"This drug class can retain fluid.","correct":true}],"hint":"The adverse-effect mechanism may outrank the target glucose value.","id":"diabetes-drugs:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Classify the mechanism before the adverse effect","task":"Identify the drug class and the symptom the question wants interpreted.","cue":"A secretagogue, SGLT2 inhibitor and thiazolidinedione carry different warning patterns.","rule":"Use the mechanism and the actual findings, not the label “diabetes pill.”","trap":"An acceptable glucose does not rule out ketoacidosis or another drug complication."},"terms":["metformin","sulfonylurea","empagliflozin"],"sources":[{"title":"2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00125-024-06183-8"},{"title":"DailyMed: official prescribing information for the exact product","url":"https://dailymed.nlm.nih.gov/dailymed/"},{"title":"ADA 2026: pharmacologic approaches","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s009"}],"id":"diabetes-drugs","ch":"56","guide":"56:1","title":"Non-insulin drugs: predict the risk from the mechanism","notes":["56-antidiabetic-drugs-non-insulin"],"book":"Ch 56: antidiabetic drugs (non-insulin).","why":["Metformin reduces hepatic glucose output; sulfonylureas increase insulin secretion and can cause hypoglycemia. Risk changes with renal function, intake and other medications.","GLP-1 receptor agonists affect glucose-dependent insulin secretion and gastric emptying; SGLT2 inhibitors increase urinary glucose loss. SGLT2-associated ketoacidosis can occur without a marked glucose rise. Follow drug-specific sick-day and perioperative instructions.","Thiazolidinediones can cause fluid retention; watch for edema, rapid weight gain or heart-failure symptoms. Use the full drug cards for contraindications, administration, other classes and adverse effects rather than treating all diabetes pills alike."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Swallowing safety decides the route","cards":[{"title":"Oral route","text":"For an alert adult with safe swallowing and permitted oral intake, follow the usual 15 g fast carbohydrate / 15-minute recheck plan."},{"title":"Nonoral rescue","text":"Unsafe swallowing excludes oral food, liquids and gel. Obtain emergency help and use prescribed IV dextrose or glucagon as appropriate."},{"title":"Reassess and prevent recurrence","text":"Repeat checks/treatment as directed; reassess the patient and review the medication–intake mismatch."}],"caption":"The detailed pilot diagram and six-decision rehearsal remain available in this lesson."},"steps":[],"strategy":{"title":"Find the route-changing cue","task":"Determine whether the patient can safely swallow and whether oral intake is permitted.","cue":"Unresponsiveness or unsafe swallowing excludes oral treatment.","rule":"Choose an available safe route, then reassess glucose and the patient.","trap":"The 15–15 rule is not a reason to give oral carbohydrate to an unresponsive person."},"terms":["hypoglycemia","glucagon","dextrose"],"sources":[{"title":"ADA 2026: glycemic goals and hypoglycemia","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s006"},{"title":"ADA 2026: diabetes care in hospital","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s016"}],"id":"hypoglycemia","ch":"56","guide":"56:2","title":"Hypoglycemia: swallowing decides the route","notes":["56-hypoglycemia-the-15-15-rule"],"book":"Ch 56: hypoglycemia; ADA 2026 Standards of Care, sections 6 and 16.","why":["Glucose below 70 mg/dL calls for action. Sweating, tremor and palpitations can precede confusion, seizures or loss of consciousness. Symptoms vary; beta blockers and impaired awareness can hide warnings.","For a conscious adult who can safely swallow and is permitted oral intake, the common 15–15 approach is 15 g fast-acting carbohydrate, recheck after 15 minutes, and repeat if still low under the treatment plan.","If swallowing is unsafe or the person is unresponsive, do not give oral food or liquid. Get emergency help and give prescribed glucagon or IV dextrose as appropriate. Recheck and investigate the cause; long-acting insulin or sulfonylureas can cause recurrence."],"caseId":"hypo"},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"High osmolality can be dangerous without major ketoacidosis","cards":[{"title":"Water loss","text":"Very high glucose drives osmotic diuresis and profound dehydration."},{"title":"Brain and circulation","text":"Confusion, reduced responsiveness and impaired perfusion require urgent assessment. Infection or reduced intake may contribute."},{"title":"Monitored correction","text":"Prescribed fluids, electrolytes and insulin require close reassessment. DKA and HHS can overlap; follow the actual labs and clinical state."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"An older adult has glucose 820 mg/dL, marked dehydration and confusion without major ketoacidosis.","task":"Which interpretation fits?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Normal aging with no need for a glucose assessment.","why":"The findings are not normal age changes.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Possible HHS requiring urgent monitored treatment.","why":"Severe dehydration and hyperosmolality can be dangerous without significant ketosis.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"No emergency because ketones are not prominent.","why":"Absent ketones do not make the presentation safe.","correct":false}],"hint":"Do not let a missing DKA feature hide a different emergency.","id":"hhs:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"During treatment, a patient develops a new seizure and worsening responsiveness.","task":"What has priority?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Immediate assessment and escalation of the neurologic deterioration.","why":"New neurologic changes can indicate a serious complication.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Document improvement because the glucose is lower.","why":"A lower glucose does not cancel neurologic deterioration.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Wait until the next routine electrolyte test before responding.","why":"The new clinical finding requires prompt action.","correct":false}],"hint":"Evaluate the patient’s trend as well as the glucose trend.","id":"hhs:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The team is planning ongoing monitoring during HHS treatment.","task":"Which set is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Only a single glucose at the end of the day.","why":"That does not support safe monitored correction.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Only whether ketones are negative.","why":"Multiple clinical and biochemical goals matter.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Glucose, electrolytes, osmolality as ordered, perfusion, neurologic status and fluid balance.","why":"Controlled correction needs metabolic and clinical monitoring.","correct":true}],"hint":"The original problems include water, electrolytes and brain function.","id":"hhs:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Absence of one finding does not exclude the whole emergency","task":"Decide whether the task asks diagnosis, priority or response to treatment.","cue":"Extreme dehydration and neurologic change matter even without major ketones.","rule":"Use the full pattern and new deterioration to guide priority.","trap":"Do not force every hyperglycemic crisis into an identical DKA presentation."},"terms":["hyperosmolar","osmotic diuresis","dehydration"],"sources":[{"title":"2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/s00125-024-06183-8"}],"id":"hhs","ch":"56","guide":"56:2","title":"HHS: extreme water loss and neurologic change","notes":["56-dka-and-hhs-the-hyperglycemic-emergencie"],"book":"Ch 56: Table 56.9; 2024 adult hyperglycemic-crisis consensus.","why":["In HHS, enough insulin remains to limit major ketone production, but not enough to prevent severe hyperglycemia. Prolonged osmotic diuresis produces profound dehydration and hyperosmolality.","An older adult with type 2 diabetes, infection, reduced intake and new confusion may fit the pattern. Assess glucose, osmolality, electrolytes, ketones, acid–base status, perfusion and mentation. DKA and HHS can overlap.","Urgent treatment includes prescribed fluids, electrolyte management and insulin, with close monitoring and controlled correction. Neurologic status and fluid balance matter throughout; absent significant ketones does not make this safe."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"compare","title":"Silent damage needs deliberate screening and protection","cards":[{"title":"Small vessels","text":"Retinopathy, nephropathy and neuropathy can develop with few early symptoms. Screening remains important."},{"title":"Feet","text":"Loss of protective sensation plus poor circulation can hide injury and impair healing. Inspect daily and protect skin."},{"title":"Larger vessels","text":"Cardiovascular and cerebrovascular risk needs ongoing management; new acute symptoms still require assessment."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with foot numbness says an injury would always hurt enough to be noticed.","task":"Which teaching is important?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Use a heating pad to test sensation.","why":"A numb foot can be burned without warning.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Inspect the feet daily because reduced sensation can hide injury.","why":"Pain is not a reliable warning when protective sensation is impaired.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Skip inspections when the foot does not hurt.","why":"Reduced protective sensation can make an injury painless, so relying on pain can miss damage.","correct":false}],"hint":"Identify the safety function lost with neuropathy.","id":"diabetes-complications:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient has no visual complaints and asks whether diabetes eye follow-up is unnecessary.","task":"Which response fits?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Follow the recommended eye screening plan even without symptoms.","why":"Early complications can be silent.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Wait until vision loss occurs before any evaluation.","why":"Symptoms are not the only trigger for screening.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Assume a normal foot examination proves the eyes are unaffected.","why":"Different organ complications need their own assessment.","correct":false}],"hint":"Screening addresses disease before symptoms make it obvious.","id":"diabetes-complications:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with diabetes and neuropathy develops new chest pressure.","task":"What should guide priority?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Attribute every new symptom to chronic neuropathy.","why":"That can miss an acute threat.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Delay assessment until the next annual screening visit.","why":"New symptoms require current evaluation.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Prompt assessment for a potentially serious acute cause.","why":"A chronic complication does not explain away new chest symptoms.","correct":true}],"hint":"Separate a chronic diagnosis from a new acute presentation.","id":"diabetes-complications:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Do not wait for pain or symptoms to validate risk","task":"Identify whether the question asks prevention/screening or a new acute concern.","cue":"Numbness reduces warning sensation; new chest pressure changes urgency.","rule":"Use screening for silent risk and immediate assessment for acute change.","trap":"One normal organ assessment does not establish safety in every organ."},"terms":["neuropathy","retinopathy","nephropathy"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: preventing diabetes problems","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/preventing-problems"}],"id":"diabetes-complications","ch":"56","guide":"56:3","title":"Eyes, kidneys, nerves and feet: damage can be silent","notes":["56-chronic-complications-vessels-eyes-nerve","56-foot-care-kidneys-and-preventing-injury"],"book":"Ch 56: chronic complications and foot/renal care.","why":["Long-term vascular and nerve injury can affect the eyes, kidneys, heart, brain and feet. Follow the screening plan: a lack of symptoms does not rule out early damage.","Loss of protective sensation means a patient may not notice trauma. Combine daily inspection, appropriate footwear, safe nail/skin care and timely referral for a new wound, redness or ischemic change. Avoid burns from heating pads or hot water.","Declining renal function can reduce insulin clearance and increase hypoglycemia risk. Review glucose trends, kidney monitoring and medication plans. Foot risk assessment includes both sensation and circulation."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"flow","title":"Make the plan usable in daily life","cards":[{"title":"Connect the parts","text":"Food, activity, medication timing and glucose interact. Prepare for activity-related lows when relevant."},{"title":"Plan for illness","text":"Monitor as directed, check ketones when indicated and follow medication-specific sick-day instructions. Type 1 basal insulin remains necessary."},{"title":"Check feasibility","text":"Use teach-back and demonstration. Assess access to food, medicines, supplies, follow-up and support."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient can repeat an injection instruction but cannot obtain the prescribed supplies.","task":"What should happen next?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"Assume lack of supplies proves unwillingness.","why":"Assess barriers without unsupported judgments.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Address access barriers and confirm a workable plan with the team.","why":"Knowledge alone does not make treatment feasible.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Record successful teaching and ignore the missing supplies.","why":"The patient cannot implement the plan without access.","correct":false}],"hint":"Look for the obstacle that prevents the instruction from becoming an action.","id":"self-management:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient with type 1 diabetes is ill and eating poorly.","task":"Which teaching fits a sick-day plan?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"Continue needed basal coverage under the team’s plan, monitor and follow ketone/contact instructions.","why":"Illness and fasting require an individualized plan rather than stopping all insulin.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Stop every insulin dose until normal eating resumes.","why":"Severe insulin deficiency can lead to DKA.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Use the same unreviewed plan for every diabetes medication.","why":"Medication-specific holds and precautions differ.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate poor intake from the need for background insulin.","id":"self-management:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"A patient taking insulin plans exercise after previously having activity-related lows.","task":"What belongs in preparation?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Assume exercise has no relationship to glucose.","why":"Activity can change glucose needs.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Make an arbitrary large insulin increase before every walk.","why":"Medication changes should follow the individualized plan.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Glucose monitoring, access to fast carbohydrate and individualized food/medication guidance.","why":"Planning can reduce activity-related hypoglycemia risk.","correct":true}],"hint":"Link the teaching to the person’s actual prior problem.","id":"self-management:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Teach-back should show usable understanding","task":"Determine whether the item asks knowledge, performance or feasibility.","cue":"Missing supplies and prior exercise-related lows require different interventions.","rule":"Choose an action that addresses the actual barrier and checks understanding.","trap":"Repeating the nurse’s words is not the same as demonstrating a workable plan."},"terms":["ketones","self-management","hypoglycemia"],"sources":[{"title":"NIDDK: managing diabetes","url":"https://www.niddk.nih.gov/health-information/diabetes/overview/managing-diabetes"}],"id":"self-management","ch":"56","guide":"56:3","title":"Make the diabetes plan workable at home","notes":["56-eating-and-exercise-with-diabetes","56-teaching-self-management"],"book":"Ch 56: nutrition, activity, sick days, self-management and health equity.","why":["Use an individualized eating and activity plan. Coordinate carbohydrate intake, medications and exercise to prevent highs and lows. Carry a rapid glucose source and know when ketones or illness make exercise unsafe.","Sick-day planning includes continued essential basal insulin for type 1 diabetes, closer glucose/ketone checks, hydration and clear contact thresholds. Other drugs may need temporary holds; use the medication-specific plan.","Use teach-back and demonstration, then check whether the patient can obtain insulin, supplies, food and follow-up. Cost, health literacy, work, distress and support affect whether the plan can actually be followed."],"caseId":""},{"visual":{"type":"decision","title":"Fasting and illness change the plan, not the need to assess","cards":[{"title":"Stress and intake","text":"Illness can raise glucose; reduced intake, renal dysfunction and treatment mismatch can lower it."},{"title":"Adjust roles of therapy","text":"Basal, meal, correction and non-insulin medicines need specific plans. Type 1 diabetes continues to need basal coverage during fasting."},{"title":"Delegate safely","text":"Routine tasks may be delegated within jurisdiction, policy and competency for an appropriate patient. RN assessment, interpretation and response remain distinct."}],"caption":"Use the patient’s findings and the prescribed plan to choose the next action."},"steps":[{"kind":"choice","scene":"A fasting inpatient with type 1 diabetes asks whether all insulin is stopped for surgery.","task":"Which response is appropriate?","options":[{"id":"w2","text":"All insulin can be stopped solely because the patient is NPO.","why":"That can create dangerous insulin deficiency.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"The team adjusts therapy and monitoring; basal insulin remains necessary.","why":"Fasting changes meal coverage and dosing needs, not the underlying basal requirement.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Every diabetes drug is automatically continued at its usual dose.","why":"Perioperative medication plans are drug-specific.","correct":false}],"hint":"A blanket stop/continue rule ignores the different medication roles.","id":"hospital-diabetes:0"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"An appropriately trained assistive person may check routine glucose under local policy for a stable patient.","task":"What should the RN communicate?","options":[{"id":"c","text":"The task, timing, reporting thresholds and when to obtain immediate RN help.","why":"Safe delegation requires clear communication and supervision within policy.","correct":true},{"id":"w1","text":"Independently diagnose and change the insulin plan if a result is abnormal.","why":"Clinical interpretation and treatment decisions are different from the routine measurement.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Do not report any results until the end of the shift.","why":"Abnormal results can need prompt assessment.","correct":false}],"hint":"Separate collecting a value from interpreting and treating it.","id":"hospital-diabetes:1"},{"kind":"choice","scene":"The delegated check shows a low glucose and the patient is newly confused.","task":"What is the RN’s responsibility?","options":[{"id":"w1","text":"Leave all interpretation and treatment to the assistive person.","why":"Delegating a measurement does not transfer these nursing responsibilities.","correct":false},{"id":"w2","text":"Ignore the result because the earlier patient was stable.","why":"The new confusion and low glucose change the circumstances and require prompt RN reassessment.","correct":false},{"id":"c","text":"Promptly assess and respond under the hypoglycemia/emergency plan.","why":"A new abnormal result and clinical change require RN judgment and action.","correct":true}],"hint":"Reassess whether the original circumstances for delegation still apply.","id":"hospital-diabetes:2"}],"strategy":{"title":"Delegate the task, not the clinical judgment","task":"Identify the patient’s stability, the task and the applicable scope/policy.","cue":"A routine measurement differs from interpreting a new abnormal result and changing therapy.","rule":"Use clear reporting and supervision; retain the required nursing assessment and response.","trap":"Delegation rules vary by jurisdiction and policy; a job title alone is not enough."},"terms":["basal insulin","delegation","perfusion"],"sources":[{"title":"ADA 2026: diabetes care in hospital","url":"https://doi.org/10.2337/dc26-s016"},{"title":"NCSBN: delegation and jurisdiction-specific rules","url":"https://www.ncsbn.org/delegation"}],"id":"hospital-diabetes","ch":"56","guide":"56:3","title":"In hospital: fasting, illness and a changing insulin need","notes":["56-diabetes-in-the-hospital-and-around-surg"],"book":"Ch 56: hospital and perioperative diabetes care.","why":["Illness, surgery, infection and some medications raise stress hormones and glucose. Reduced intake, kidney dysfunction or a treatment mismatch can cause hypoglycemia. Reassess when the pattern changes.","NPO does not mean that every diabetes medication is automatically stopped or continued. The team adjusts basal, meal and correction therapy, glucose checks and drug-specific holds. Type 1 diabetes still requires basal coverage.","The RN assesses and interprets findings and teaches the patient. Assistive personnel may perform routine tasks within policy for a stable patient, but abnormal results require prompt reporting and RN assessment."],"caseId":""}]}};
window.LEARN_CFG = window.LEARN_DATA.config;
window.LEARN_STUDIOS = window.LEARN_DATA.studios;
